var e={notes:[{id:`monseigneur`,term:`Monseigneur`,kind:`title`,chapter:1,text:`The French honorific for a bishop, roughly 'My Lord'. Madame Magloire uses it with relish; the Bishop himself prefers the nickname the poor give him. Hapgood sometimes translates it as 'Monsignor' or simply leaves it.`,quote:`housekeeper to Monseigneur`},{id:`parliament-of-aix`,term:`Parliament of Aix`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Under the old monarchy a 'parliament' was not a legislature but a sovereign law court. Aix-en-Provence had one, and its councillors formed the 'nobility of the bar' (noblesse de robe), a hereditary caste of judges. M. Myriel was born into that world, which the Revolution destroyed.`,quote:`the nobility of the bar`},{id:`emigrated`,term:`emigrated`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`To emigrate, in a French context, means to have fled the Revolution: the émigrés were the nobles and clergy who left France after 1789, many of them ruined. Returning émigrés, often ruined, were a familiar type under the Restoration.`,quote:`emigrated to Italy`,seeAlso:[98]},{id:`93`,term:`’93`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Shorthand for 1793, the year of the Terror: the execution of Louis XVI, the guillotine in permanent session, civil war in the Vendée. For royalists it is the Revolution's unforgivable year; for republicans, a thunderbolt that answered fifteen centuries of oppression.`,quote:`the tragic spectacles of ’93`,seeAlso:[10]},{id:`cure`,term:`curé`,kind:`title`,chapter:1,text:`A parish priest. A curé might be assisted by a vicar (vicaire), and village curés were often poor and little educated. Hugo keeps the French word throughout.`,quote:`the Curé of Brignolles`},{id:`cardinal-fesch`,term:`Cardinal Fesch`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Joseph Fesch (1763–1839), half-brother of Napoleon's mother and so the Emperor's uncle, Archbishop of Lyons and a cardinal. He was the natural channel for a provincial priest seeking favours in Paris. Hugo's anecdote of the Emperor meeting Myriel in Fesch's anteroom is invented.`,quote:`M. le Cardinal Fesch`,seeAlso:[11]},{id:`the-coronation`,term:`the coronation`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Napoleon crowned himself Emperor in Notre-Dame on 2 December 1804, with Pope Pius VII present. The 'epoch of the coronation' is therefore late 1804, when the Empire was new and bishoprics were in the Emperor's gift.`,quote:`the epoch of the coronation`},{id:`prefect`,term:`prefect`,kind:`title`,chapter:1,text:`The head of a département, appointed by the central government in Paris, created by Napoleon in 1800. A prefect ran the local administration and police and was the most powerful official in a provincial town, outranking the mayor.`,quote:`the general and the prefect`},{id:`livres`,term:`livres`,kind:`money`,chapter:2,text:`The livre was the old unit of account, replaced by the franc in 1795 at the same value; old-fashioned people like the Bishop went on reckoning in livres. 15,000 a year was a handsome salary when a labourer earned perhaps 500 francs a year, so the 1,500 the Bishop and his sister keep is genuine poverty.`,quote:`15,000 livres`},{id:`lazarists`,term:`Lazarists`,kind:`reference`,chapter:2,text:`Priests of the Congregation of the Mission, founded by Saint Vincent de Paul in 1625 and nicknamed after their Paris house of Saint-Lazare. They preached in the countryside and trained clergy. Vincent also founded the Sisters of Charity, the nursing nuns of French hospitals.`,quote:`the Lazarists of Montdidier`,seeAlso:[55]},{id:`incarcerated-for-debt`,term:`incarcerated for debt`,kind:`custom`,chapter:2,text:`Imprisonment for debt was legal in France until 1867. A creditor could have a debtor locked up, at the creditor's expense, until the debt was paid, which for a poor family meant ruin. Paying to free such men was a recognised charity.`,quote:`incarcerated for debt`},{id:`the-18-brumaire`,term:`the 18 Brumaire`,kind:`history`,chapter:2,text:`The coup d'état of 9 November 1799 (18 Brumaire in the revolutionary calendar) by which Napoleon overthrew the Directory. The Council of the Five Hundred was the lower house that was dispersed at bayonet point. Men who supported the coup were rewarded, this senator among them.`,quote:`favored the 18 Brumaire`},{id:`senator-of-the-empire`,term:`senator of the Empire`,kind:`title`,chapter:2,text:`Napoleon's Senate was an appointed body of well-paid dignitaries, and a senator might also be given a 'sénatorerie', a country estate with revenues. The office carried prestige and little work, which is why Hugo's senator can be a cheerful epicurean.`,quote:`a senator of the Empire`,seeAlso:[8]},{id:`bienvenu`,term:`Bienvenu`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:2,text:`French for 'welcome'. It was one of the Bishop's baptismal names, and the poor chose it as the one that meant something. Hapgood often translates it, so the same man appears as 'Monseigneur Bienvenu' and 'Monseigneur Welcome'.`,quote:`Monseigneur Bienvenu`},{id:`marriage-bans-dispensations`,term:`marriage bans, dispensations`,kind:`custom`,chapter:2,text:`A bishop's 'perquisites' were the fees paid for church services beyond the sacraments themselves: publishing marriage banns, granting dispensations (for instance to marry a cousin or in Lent), private baptisms and blessings. The Bishop charged the rich and gave the proceeds away.`,quote:`the fees for marriage bans, dispensations`},{id:`canton`,term:`canton`,kind:`word`,chapter:3,text:`A small administrative district grouping a handful of villages, the level below the arrondissement and the département. When the Bishop scolds 'the cantons where they were harsh to the poor', he means whole clusters of parishes.`,quote:`In the cantons where they were harsh`},{id:`briancon-embrun-queyras-devolny`,term:`Briançon, Embrun, Queyras, Devolny`,kind:`place`,chapter:3,text:`Mountain districts of the Hautes-Alpes, the high Alps north of Digne. Briançon is Europe's highest fortified town; Embrun was an ancient archbishopric; the Queyras is a remote valley on the Italian border. The Bishop holds them up as models of neighbourly life.`,quote:`the valley of Queyras`},{id:`stamped-paper`,term:`stamped paper`,kind:`custom`,chapter:3,text:`Legal documents had to be written on paper bearing a government stamp, bought at a price: a tax on every lawsuit, contract and petition. Peasants who 'ruined themselves in stamped paper' were litigants paying for endless proceedings.`,quote:`ruined themselves in stamped paper`,seeAlso:[52]},{id:`quill-pens-in-the-hat`,term:`quill pens in the hat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:3,text:`Itinerant schoolmasters hired themselves out at fairs in the Alps, and advertised their qualifications by the feathers in their hatbands: one for reading, two for reading and arithmetic, three for Latin as well. Hugo took the detail from a real Dauphiné custom.`,quote:`the quill pens which they wear in the cord of their hat`},{id:`your-grace`,term:`Your Grace`,kind:`title`,chapter:4,text:`Hapgood's rendering of 'Votre Grandeur', the formal address for a bishop, literally 'Your Greatness'. The Bishop's joke about his greatness not reaching the top shelf depends on that literal sense.`,quote:`call him Your Grace`},{id:`heir-by-entail`,term:`heir by entail`,kind:`custom`,chapter:4,text:`An entail (substitution) fixed the descent of a title or estate in advance through a line of heirs, so that a nephew could know he would one day be a duke. Madame de Lô's 'expectations' are these future inheritances; the Bishop quietly finds them indecent.`,quote:`the heir by entail to the title of the Duke`},{id:`saint-augustine`,term:`Saint Augustine`,kind:`reference`,chapter:4,text:`Bishop of Hippo (354–430), the most influential of the Latin Church Fathers. The saying the Bishop attributes to him, to put your hopes in the man from whom you will not inherit, is not in Augustine; it is Hugo's own.`,quote:`to be found, I believe, in St. Augustine`},{id:`galloons`,term:`galloons`,kind:`word`,chapter:4,text:`Narrow woven braid or ribbon used to trim uniforms, liveries and furniture. M. Géborand made his two millions in coarse cloth and galloon, the unglamorous end of the textile trade.`,quote:`serges, and woollen galloons`},{id:`ultra-royalist-and-ultra-voltairian`,term:`ultra-royalist and ultra-Voltairian`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`The 'Ultras' were the extreme royalist party of the Restoration, more royalist than the king, allied with the Church. A Voltairian was a sceptic who mocked religion in the manner of Voltaire. The two should be opposites, which is Hugo's point about the Marquis de Champtercier.`,quote:`an ultra-royalist and an ultra-Voltairian`,seeAlso:[91]},{id:`the-tax-on-doors-and-windows`,term:`the tax on doors and windows`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`A tax introduced in 1798 and assessed on the number of openings in a dwelling, on the theory that more windows meant more wealth. The poor responded by bricking up windows, which is why the Bishop says the law sells air. It lasted until 1926.`,quote:`the tax on doors and windows`,seeAlso:[101]},{id:`dauphine-isere-var-the-two-alpes`,term:`Dauphiné, Isère, Var, the two Alpes`,kind:`place`,chapter:4,text:`The mountainous south-east of France. Dauphiné is the old province around Grenoble; the Isère, the Var, the Hautes-Alpes and the Basses-Alpes are the départements carved out of it and Provence after 1790. Digne is the chief town of the Basses-Alpes.`,quote:`the Alpes, the Hautes, and the Basses`},{id:`the-dialect-of-the-south`,term:`the dialect of the south`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:4,text:`Provençal and the related tongues of Languedoc and Dauphiné, varieties of the langue d'oc that most southern peasants spoke instead of French in 1815. The Bishop's scraps of patois mean roughly 'Well, sir, are you wise?', 'Where will you pass?' and 'Bring a good mutton with a good fat cheese'.`,quote:`the dialect of the south`},{id:`counterfeiting-was-still-punishable-with-death`,term:`Counterfeiting was still punishable with death`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`Under the Penal Code of 1810, coining false money was a capital crime, as it had been under the old monarchy. The death penalty for it was abolished in 1832, which is why Hugo writes 'still'.`,quote:`Counterfeiting was still punishable with death`},{id:`the-attorney-for-the-crown`,term:`the attorney for the crown`,kind:`title`,chapter:4,text:`The public prosecutor, in French the procureur du roi, or at the Assizes the avocat général. Hapgood translates the office variously as 'attorney for the crown', 'advocate of the crown', 'district-attorney' and 'attorney-general'; they are all the same prosecuting magistrate. The Bishop's question, where will the prosecutor himself be tried, is the first of Hugo's many attacks on judicial zeal.`,quote:`the attorney for the crown`,seeAlso:[61,63]},{id:`court-of-assizes`,term:`Court of Assizes`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`The criminal court that tried serious crimes before a jury, sitting periodically in each département's chief town. Lesser offences went to the 'correctional police' courts without a jury. Hugo's trial scenes all take place at the Assizes.`,quote:`At the Court of Assizes`,seeAlso:[54,63]},{id:`mountebank`,term:`mountebank`,kind:`word`,chapter:4,text:`A fairground performer, juggler or quack who harangued crowds from a platform. The condemned man had also been a 'writer for the public', a scribe who penned letters for the illiterate, so he was half showman, half clerk.`,quote:`a mountebank at fairs`},{id:`tumbril`,term:`tumbril`,kind:`word`,chapter:4,text:`The open cart that carried the condemned to the scaffold, infamous from the Terror. In 1815 the guillotine was still a public spectacle in the town square, which is why the Bishop's presence beside the criminal was so visible.`,quote:`He mounted the tumbril with him`},{id:`camail`,term:`camail`,kind:`word`,chapter:4,text:`A short cape covering the shoulders, worn by bishops and canons over the cassock; a bishop's is purple. Wearing it with the pectoral cross meant the Bishop went to the scaffold in full episcopal dress.`,quote:`his purple camail`},{id:`de-maistre-and-beccaria`,term:`de Maistre and Beccaria`,kind:`reference`,chapter:4,text:`Joseph de Maistre (1753–1821), the Catholic reactionary philosopher, defended the executioner as the cornerstone of society. Cesare Beccaria's 'On Crimes and Punishments' (1764) was the Enlightenment's great argument against torture and the death penalty. Hugo, who campaigned against the guillotine all his life, sets them as the two poles.`,quote:`like de Maistre; others execrate it, like Beccaria`,seeAlso:[44]},{id:`vindicte`,term:`vindicte`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:4,text:`French legal term for the public vengeance exacted by the law (vindicte publique). Hugo uses the word to insist that the guillotine is not neutral machinery but society's act of revenge.`,quote:`it is called vindicte`},{id:`sou`,term:`sou`,kind:`money`,chapter:4,text:`The smallest everyday coin, one twentieth of a franc, five centimes. People still counted in sous for small sums: a loaf cost a few sous, a labourer earned twenty or thirty a day. M. Géborand's single sou shared among six beggars is Hugo's measure of a miser.`,quote:`he gave a sou every Sunday`,seeAlso:[15,20,27,48]},{id:`vicars-general`,term:`vicars-general`,kind:`title`,chapter:5,text:`A bishop's chief deputies, priests who administered the diocese on his behalf. A canon was a member of the cathedral chapter, a comfortable clerical post. Hugo lists them to show how much routine business a bishop had.`,quote:`nearly every day his vicars-general`},{id:`breviary`,term:`breviary`,kind:`word`,chapter:5,text:`The book of daily prayers (the Divine Office) that every priest was bound to recite. 'His offices and his breviary' are the Bishop's fixed devotional duties, before any time for the poor.`,quote:`his offices and his breviary`},{id:`trappist`,term:`trappist`,kind:`reference`,chapter:5,text:`A monk of the strictest Cistercian observance, founded at La Trappe, vowed to silence and a diet without meat. The town's joke is that the Bishop eats like a Trappist except when a visiting curé gives Madame Magloire an excuse for fish and game.`,quote:`the cheer of a trappist`},{id:`onkelos`,term:`Onkelos`,kind:`reference`,chapter:5,text:`The author of the Targum, the ancient Aramaic ('Chaldaic') paraphrase of the Hebrew Bible. Flavius Josephus was the first-century Jewish historian. The Bishop comparing their versions of Genesis 1:2 shows him as a scholar as well as a saint.`,quote:`the Chaldaic paraphrase of Onkelos`},{id:`great-grand-uncle-to-the-writer-of-this-book`,term:`great-grand-uncle to the writer of this book`,kind:`lore`,chapter:5,text:`Hugo is boasting of a (dubious) ancestor, a seventeenth-century bishop of Ptolemaïs named Hugo. The narrator of Les Misérables is openly Victor Hugo himself, who intrudes with family lore, politics and his own travels throughout.`,quote:`great-grand-uncle to the writer of this book`},{id:`tithes`,term:`tithes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:6,text:`The tenth of produce once owed to the Church, abolished in 1789. The Bishop's joke is that he pays tithes to the hospital, giving half his cows' milk, a reversal of the old flow from poor to clergy.`,quote:`I am paying my tithes`},{id:`prie-dieu`,term:`prie-Dieu`,kind:`word`,chapter:6,text:`A kneeling desk for prayer: a low step to kneel on with a sloping ledge for a book. The Bishop's are of straw, like all his furniture.`,quote:`two straw prie-Dieu`},{id:`bergere`,term:`bergère`,kind:`word`,chapter:6,text:`A deep, wide upholstered armchair of the eighteenth century, 'shepherdess' in French. Mademoiselle Baptistine's gilded bergère, hoisted through a window because the stair was too narrow, is the one relic of comfort in the house.`,quote:`to hoist this bergère up`},{id:`utrecht-velvet`,term:`Utrecht velvet`,kind:`word`,chapter:6,text:`A stamped mohair velvet, originally from the Dutch town, used for upholstery. A drawing-room set in it with a mahogany sofa was a modest bourgeois dream; Mademoiselle Baptistine saved forty-two francs toward it in five years.`,quote:`yellow Utrecht velvet`},{id:`order-of-citeaux`,term:`order of Cîteaux`,kind:`reference`,chapter:6,text:`The Cistercians, the reformed Benedictine order founded at Cîteaux in Burgundy in 1098. An 'abbé' of Grand-Champ held the revenues of a Cistercian abbey without necessarily living as a monk, a common arrangement before the Revolution.`,quote:`order of Cîteaux`},{id:`wafers`,term:`wafers`,kind:`word`,chapter:6,text:`Small discs of dried paste, moistened to seal letters or stick papers together before gummed envelopes existed. The note on the back of the portrait is fixed with four of them.`,quote:`with four wafers`},{id:`jussieu-against-linnaeus`,term:`Jussieu against Linnaeus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:6,text:`The great quarrel of eighteenth-century botany. Linnaeus classified plants by counting their sexual parts; Tournefort and then the Jussieu family grouped them by overall 'natural' affinity. The Bishop, says Hugo, took no side: he loved flowers rather than studying plants.`,quote:`nor with Jussieu against Linnaeus`},{id:`nisi-dominus-custodierit-domum`,term:`Nisi Dominus custodierit domum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:6,text:`Latin, from Psalm 127 (126 in the Vulgate): 'Unless the Lord keep the house, they watch in vain who guard it.' The Bishop's whole case for an unlocked door is this verse.`,quote:`Nisi Dominus custodierit domum`},{id:`colonel-of-dragoons`,term:`colonel of dragoons`,kind:`title`,chapter:6,text:`Dragoons were heavy cavalry, and their colonels a byword for dash and swagger. The Bishop sets the priest's quiet bravery beside the soldier's showy kind.`,quote:`a colonel of dragoons`},{id:`gaspard-bes`,term:`Gaspard Bès`,kind:`history`,chapter:7,text:`A real Provençal brigand, Gaspard de Besse (1757–1781), who robbed travellers in the gorges of Ollioules near Toulon and was broken on the wheel at Aix. He became a local Robin Hood figure; Cravatte, his 'lieutenant', is Hugo's invention.`,quote:`the band of Gaspard Bès`},{id:`gendarmes`,term:`gendarmes`,kind:`title`,chapter:7,text:`The gendarmerie was the military police force that patrolled the countryside and roads, organised in small brigades under a 'brigadier' (a corporal). They checked travellers' papers and hunted brigands. Town police were a separate, civilian body under the prefect.`,quote:`The gendarmes were set on his track`,seeAlso:[15,23,26]},{id:`te-deum`,term:`Te Deum`,kind:`reference`,chapter:7,text:`The ancient Latin hymn of thanksgiving, 'We praise thee, O God', sung on great occasions. To 'chant a Te Deum pontifically' is to celebrate it in full bishop's vestments, which the mountain parish does not possess.`,quote:`to chant a Te Deum pontifically`,seeAlso:[10,168]},{id:`cope-mitre-crosier`,term:`cope, mitre, crosier`,kind:`word`,chapter:7,text:`The vestments of a bishop: the cope is the long semicircular cloak worn for ceremonies, the mitre the tall pointed headdress, the crosier the shepherd's-crook staff. A chasuble is the poncho-like garment any priest wears to say mass. Cravatte's chest contains the full pontifical set.`,quote:`a cope of cloth of gold, a mitre`},{id:`presbytery`,term:`presbytery`,kind:`word`,chapter:7,text:`The parish priest's house, the French presbytère; Hapgood also uses 'parsonage' and 'the priest's house'. Visiting bishops lodged there.`,quote:`deposited in the presbytery`},{id:`epicurus-and-pigault-lebrun`,term:`Epicurus and Pigault-Lebrun`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`Epicurus was the Greek philosopher of tranquil pleasure, a respectable name for a materialist. Pigault-Lebrun (1753–1835) wrote racy, irreligious popular novels. Hugo's jab is that the senator thinks himself a philosopher when he is merely a reader of dirty books.`,quote:`only a product of Pigault-Lebrun`,seeAlso:[38]},{id:`two-augurs`,term:`two augurs`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`Roman augurs read omens in the flight of birds. Cato is said to have wondered how two augurs could pass each other without laughing, since both knew the business was a fraud. The senator means that he and the Bishop are both in on the trick of religion; the Bishop does not accept the compliment.`,quote:`We are two augurs`,seeAlso:[28]},{id:`the-marquis-d-argens-pyrrhon-hobbes-and-m-naigeon`,term:`the Marquis d’Argens, Pyrrhon, Hobbes, and M. Naigeon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`The senator's library of unbelievers: d'Argens, a freethinking friend of Frederick the Great; Pyrrho, the Greek sceptic; Hobbes, the English materialist; Naigeon, Diderot's atheist editor. He prefers them to Diderot and Voltaire, whom he finds too religious.`,quote:`the Marquis d’Argens, Pyrrhon, Hobbes, and M. Naigeon`},{id:`needham-s-eels`,term:`Needham’s eels`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`John Needham, an eighteenth-century priest-scientist, claimed to see tiny 'eels' generate spontaneously in flour paste left in vinegar, proof that life needs no creator. Voltaire ridiculed him. The senator takes Needham's side: if paste can make eels, God is superfluous.`,quote:`Needham’s eels prove that God is useless`},{id:`fiat-lux`,term:`fiat lux`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:8,text:`Latin, 'let there be light', God's first words in Genesis. The senator says a drop of vinegar in paste does the same job.`,quote:`supplies the fiat lux`,seeAlso:[157]},{id:`the-veil-of-isis`,term:`the veil of Isis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`The Egyptian goddess Isis was said to have an inscription at Sais: 'No mortal has lifted my veil.' To raise it became a figure for penetrating nature's secrets; the senator counts himself among the initiated who have.`,quote:`raised the veil of Isis`},{id:`the-moniteur`,term:`the Moniteur`,kind:`history`,chapter:8,text:`Le Moniteur universel, the official government newspaper where laws, appointments and speeches were published. To say something 'in the Moniteur' is to say it publicly and officially; the senator keeps his atheism for private company.`,quote:`in the Moniteur`,seeAlso:[41,173]},{id:`sardanapalus-and-vincent-de-paul`,term:`Sardanapalus and Vincent de Paul`,kind:`reference`,chapter:8,text:`Sardanapalus, the legendary last king of Assyria, stood for luxury and debauchery; Saint Vincent de Paul (1581–1660) for charity to the poor. The senator's creed is that since death ends everything, it makes no difference which you were.`,quote:`You have been Sardanapalus, you have been Vincent de Paul`},{id:`telemachus-being-knighted-by-minerva`,term:`Telemachus being knighted by Minerva`,kind:`reference`,chapter:9,text:`A scene from Fénelon's 'Télémaque' (1699), the moral novel about Odysseus's son guided by the goddess Minerva, standard reading for well-brought-up girls. Baptistine muddles it with 'Roman ladies', which tells us how little she minds about such things.`,quote:`Telemachus being knighted by Minerva`},{id:`crowns-of-six-francs`,term:`crowns of six francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:9,text:`The écu, the big silver coin of the old monarchy worth six livres, still in circulation and still used as a unit of reckoning: 'a hundred crowns' meant 300 francs, 'a thousand crowns' 3,000. Regilding two tables for two crowns each was a small luxury the household refused.`,quote:`two crowns of six francs each`,seeAlso:[56,102]},{id:`generalship-of-caen`,term:`generalship of Caen`,kind:`word`,chapter:9,text:`Hapgood's literal rendering of 'généralité', a fiscal district of the old monarchy administered by an intendant. Baptistine's genealogy of the Faux family is the kind of pedigree-tracing that still mattered in Restoration drawing-rooms.`,quote:`the generalship of Caen`},{id:`peer-of-france`,term:`peer of France`,kind:`title`,chapter:9,text:`A member of the highest nobility under the old monarchy, and after 1814 a member of the Chamber of Peers, the upper house of the Restoration parliament, appointed by the king. Peers wore a distinctive costume and sat as a court for political trials.`,quote:`peer of France`,seeAlso:[28]},{id:`member-of-the-convention`,term:`member of the Convention`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The National Convention was the assembly that governed France from September 1792 to 1795: it proclaimed the Republic, tried and condemned Louis XVI, and presided over the Terror. Its surviving members were pariahs under the Restoration, and those who had voted for the king's death ('regicides') were exiled by a law of 1816. G—— voted against the death and so could stay.`,quote:`a former member of the Convention`},{id:`thou-and-citizen`,term:`“thou” and “citizen”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:10,text:`The Revolution abolished 'Monsieur' in favour of 'citoyen' and encouraged everyone to use the familiar 'tu' as a mark of equality. By 1815 these were damning marks of an old Jacobin.`,quote:`people called each other “thou,” and when they said “citizen.”`,seeAlso:[17]},{id:`provost-s-court`,term:`provost’s court`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The cours prévôtales, special courts set up by the Restoration in 1815 to try political and seditious offences summarily, without appeal, during the 'White Terror' against Bonapartists and revolutionaries. Digne's gossips think G—— should have been hauled before one.`,quote:`brought before a provost’s court`},{id:`azrael`,term:`Azrael`,kind:`reference`,chapter:10,text:`In Islamic tradition, the angel of death who separates the soul from the body. Hugo says even Azrael would have turned back from so vigorous a dying man.`,quote:`Azrael, the Mohammedan angel of the sepulchre`},{id:`the-king-who-was-flesh-above-and-marble-below`,term:`the king who was flesh above and marble below`,kind:`reference`,chapter:10,text:`From the Arabian Nights: the young king of the Black Isles, whose sorceress wife turned the lower half of his body to stone while he lived on from the waist up. G——'s legs are dead while his head blazes.`,quote:`flesh above and marble below`},{id:`louis-xvii`,term:`Louis XVII`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The son of Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette, proclaimed king by royalists after his father's execution. He was kept in the Temple prison in Paris, neglected and abused, and died there in 1795 aged ten. For royalists he was the martyred child; G—— sets another child beside him.`,quote:`Louis XVII?`},{id:`the-brother-of-cartouche`,term:`the brother of Cartouche`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`Louis-Dominique Cartouche was the most famous bandit of Paris, broken on the wheel in 1721. His fifteen-year-old brother was sentenced to be hung by the armpits for two hours in the Place de Grève, the public square of executions, and died of it. G——'s point: an innocent child of the people, as pitiable as a royal one.`,quote:`the brother of Cartouche`},{id:`sinite-parvulos`,term:`Sinite parvulos`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:10,text:`Latin, 'Suffer the little children' (Mark 10:14), Christ's words when the disciples kept children away. G—— quotes it to say Christ made no distinction between a dauphin and a beggar's child.`,quote:`‘Sinite parvulos,’`},{id:`bossuet-chanting-the-te-deum-over-the-dragonnades`,term:`Bossuet chanting the Te Deum over the dragonnades`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The dragonnades were Louis XIV's method of forcing Protestants to convert: billeting brutal dragoons in their homes. Bossuet, the great court preacher and Bishop of Meaux, celebrated the Revocation of the Edict of Nantes (1685) that followed. G—— answers Marat's guillotine with the monarchy's own atrocities, and wounds the Bishop by touching his hero.`,quote:`the Te Deum over the dragonnades`},{id:`carrier-fouquier-tinville-maillard`,term:`Carrier, Fouquier-Tinville, Maillard`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`Men of the Terror: Carrier drowned prisoners in the Loire at Nantes; Fouquier-Tinville was the Revolutionary Tribunal's prosecutor; Maillard led the September Massacres; 'Père Duchesne' was Hébert's foul-mouthed paper; Jourdan 'Head-cutter' massacred at Avignon. G—— pairs each with a persecutor under the kings: Montrevel and Bâville against the Protestants, Saulx-Tavannes at Saint Bartholomew, Letellier and Louvois behind the dragonnades.`,quote:`Carrier is a bandit; but what name do you give to Montrevel?`},{id:`huguenot`,term:`Huguenot`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`A French Protestant. After Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes in 1685, Protestant worship was outlawed, ministers banished and those who would not abjure imprisoned, sent to the galleys or tortured; hundreds of thousands fled abroad.`,quote:`that poor Huguenot woman, who, in 1685`,seeAlso:[72]},{id:`vermis-sum`,term:`Vermis sum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:10,text:`Latin, 'I am a worm', from Psalm 22:6. The Bishop's answer when challenged to say who he is; G—— retorts that a worm does not travel by carriage.`,quote:`Vermis sum—I am a worm`},{id:`the-restoration`,term:`the Restoration`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The return of the Bourbon monarchy after Napoleon's fall: Louis XVIII, brother of the guillotined Louis XVI, entered Paris in 1814, fled during the Hundred Days, and returned after Waterloo in 1815. The Restoration lasted until 1830. Most of this novel is set in it, and Hugo, writing in 1862, judges it harshly; G—— calls 1814 'that fatal return of the past'.`,quote:`the past, which is called 1814`,seeAlso:[88]},{id:`the-red-cap`,term:`the red cap`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The bonnet rouge or Phrygian cap, the emblem of the Revolution worn by sans-culottes. The dowager's joke is that the Bishop is turning Jacobin; his reply plays on the red hat of a cardinal, which the same people would revere.`,quote:`receive the red cap`},{id:`g-d-the-marquise-de-r`,term:`G——, D——, the Marquise de R——`,kind:`lore`,chapter:10,text:`Hugo often prints only an initial and a dash for a name or town, in the old novelist's manner of pretending to protect real people. Digne becomes 'D——' in some passages; the conventionist and the charitable marquise are never named. Treat the dashes as part of the book's texture, not as puzzles.`,quote:`His name was G⸺`,seeAlso:[15]},{id:`baron-of-the-empire`,term:`baron of the Empire`,kind:`title`,chapter:11,text:`Napoleon created a new nobility in 1808, with titles of prince, duke, count, baron and knight bestowed for service, and gave many bishops the rank of baron. It sat oddly on a man who gave away his carriage money.`,quote:`a baron of the Empire`,seeAlso:[171]},{id:`the-arrest-of-the-pope`,term:`the arrest of the Pope`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`Pius VII excommunicated Napoleon in 1809 after the French annexed Rome; on the night of 5–6 July French troops seized him and carried him off to Savona and later Fontainebleau. Napoleon summoned a national council of bishops to Paris in 1811 to get round the Pope's refusal to confirm his bishops. It defied him, and he dissolved it.`,quote:`The arrest of the Pope took place`,seeAlso:[88]},{id:`ultramontane-rather-than-a-gallican`,term:`ultramontane rather than a gallican`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`The two parties in the French Church. Gallicans upheld the traditional independence of the French Church and the king's authority over it; ultramontanes looked 'beyond the mountains' to the Pope's supreme authority. Under Napoleon, gallicanism meant siding with the Emperor against the captive Pope.`,quote:`an ultramontane rather than a gallican`},{id:`the-hundred-days`,term:`the Hundred Days`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`Napoleon's last reign: he escaped from Elba, landed near Cannes on 1 March 1815, marched to Paris as Louis XVIII fled, and ruled until Waterloo in June. Everyone in France had to choose sides, and the Bishop chose the king.`,quote:`during the Hundred Days`,seeAlso:[15,32]},{id:`the-disembarkation-at-cannes`,term:`the disembarkation at Cannes`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`Napoleon landed from Elba at Golfe-Juan near Cannes on 1 March 1815 with about a thousand men and marched north through the Alps, by Digne and Grenoble, gathering troops sent to stop him. The Bishop's brother, a general in Provence, pursued him very half-heartedly.`,quote:`the disembarkation at Cannes`,seeAlso:[15,65]},{id:`waterloo`,term:`Waterloo`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`The battle of 18 June 1815 near Brussels in which Wellington and Blücher finally defeated Napoleon, ending the Hundred Days and the Empire. Here it is only glimpsed on the horizon of 1815, the year the Bishop's story reaches.`,quote:`when Waterloo could be dimly discerned`,seeAlso:[71,73]},{id:`legion-of-honor`,term:`Legion of Honor`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`The order Napoleon founded in 1802 to reward military and civil merit, its cross worn on a red ribbon. Under the Restoration the Emperor's profile on the medal was replaced by Henri IV's, and the imperial eagle by Bourbon fleurs-de-lys, which the old soldier calls 'three frogs'. A 'chevalier' is the lowest grade.`,quote:`a member of the Legion of Honor at Austerlitz`,seeAlso:[41,89,98]},{id:`bonapartist`,term:`Bonapartist`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`A supporter of Napoleon and his dynasty after 1815. Bonapartists were watched by the Restoration police, prosecuted for 'seditious speeches' and dismissed from posts; the cult of the exiled Emperor among old soldiers and the common people runs through the novel.`,quote:`as much of a Bonapartist as the eagle`,seeAlso:[70]},{id:`louis-xviii`,term:`Louis XVIII`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`The Bourbon king restored in 1814, younger brother of Louis XVI. He was elderly, enormously fat and crippled with gout, had spent his exile partly in England (hence the 'English gaiters'), and wore the old-fashioned powdered queue or pigtail. Bonapartists despised him; he reigned until 1824.`,quote:`to scoff aloud at Louis XVIII`,seeAlso:[28,98]},{id:`beadle`,term:`beadle`,kind:`word`,chapter:11,text:`A lay church officer who kept order during services and led processions, usually a dignified ex-soldier in a braided coat with a staff. The Bishop finds the dismissed Bonapartist porter this harmless post.`,quote:`appointed him beadle in the cathedral`,seeAlso:[108]},{id:`abbe`,term:`abbé`,kind:`title`,chapter:12,text:`Originally an abbot, but by the eighteenth century a courtesy title for any young secular priest or cleric. 'Little abbés' are the ambitious junior clergy who attach themselves to a rising bishop; the Bishop of Digne has none.`,quote:`a full squadron of little abbés`},{id:`saint-francois-de-sales`,term:`Saint François de Sales`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`Francis de Sales (1567–1622), Bishop of Geneva and author of the 'Introduction to the Devout Life', famous for gentleness and wit. 'Prêtres blancs-becs', callow priests, literally 'white-beaks', is his phrase for fledgling clergy.`,quote:`that charming Saint François de Sales`},{id:`prebends-archidiaconates-canonships`,term:`prebends, archidiaconates, canonships`,kind:`word`,chapter:12,text:`The paid positions of the Church: a prebend was the income attached to a cathedral stall, an archdeacon a bishop's regional deputy, a canon a member of the chapter. These were the 'nice little promotions' a well-connected bishop could scatter among favourites.`,quote:`prebends, archidiaconates, chaplaincies`},{id:`from-a-grace-to-an-eminence`,term:`from a Grace to an Eminence`,kind:`title`,chapter:12,text:`The ladder of Roman ecclesiastical titles. A bishop is 'Your Grace' (Grandeur), a cardinal 'Your Eminence', the Pope 'Your Holiness'. The pallium is the woollen band the Pope gives archbishops; a conclavist is a cardinal's attendant at a papal election; an auditor and a papal chamberlain are posts at the Roman court.`,quote:`from a Grace to an Eminence is only a step`},{id:`perrette-s-pot-of-milk`,term:`Perrette’s pot of milk`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`From La Fontaine's fable 'The Milkmaid and the Pot of Milk': Perrette carries milk to market dreaming of the eggs, chickens, pig and cow it will buy, skips, and spills it all. Any daydream of future riches is 'Perrette's pot'.`,quote:`Perrette’s pot of milk`},{id:`menaechmus`,term:`Menaechmus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`The identical twins of Plautus's comedy 'Menaechmi', the source of Shakespeare's 'Comedy of Errors'. Success is talent's twin, Hugo says: so alike that history mistakes one for the other.`,quote:`that Menaechmus of talent`},{id:`juvenal-and-tacitus`,term:`Juvenal and Tacitus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`The Roman satirist and the Roman historian, both merciless about the corruption and sycophancy of imperial Rome. Hugo claims them as the only writers who refuse to flatter success.`,quote:`Juvenal and Tacitus alone grumble at it`},{id:`the-army-of-the-sambre-and-meuse`,term:`the army of the Sambre-and-Meuse`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`One of the Republic's armies of the 1790s, fighting on the Belgian and Rhine frontier. Hugo's apothecary who got rich selling cardboard boot-soles to it stands for every war profiteer; the contractors who starved the revolutionary armies were a national scandal.`,quote:`the army of the Sambre-and-Meuse`},{id:`credo-in-patrem`,term:`Credo in Patrem`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:13,text:`Latin, 'I believe in the Father', the opening of the Creed. Hugo offers it as the sum of what can be known of the Bishop's orthodoxy.`,quote:`“Credo in Patrem,” he often exclaimed`},{id:`quia-multum-amavit`,term:`quia multum amavit`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:13,text:`Latin, 'because she loved much', Christ's words forgiving the sinful woman in Luke 7:47. Hugo applies it to the Bishop: his 'excess of love' is what 'serious men' hold against him.`,quote:`quia multum amavit`},{id:`who-knoweth-whither-the-soul-of-the-animal-goeth`,term:`“Who knoweth whither the soul of the animal goeth?”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:13,text:`Ecclesiastes 3:21, which doubts whether a beast's spirit goes down to the earth and man's upward. Hugo cites it for the Bishop's tenderness to spiders and ants, a sentiment he says is rare in priests.`,quote:`Who knoweth whither the soul of the animal goeth?`},{id:`gregory-xvi`,term:`Gregory XVI`,kind:`history`,chapter:13,text:`Pope from 1831 to 1846, a rigid reactionary who condemned liberalism and railways alike. Hugo's aside that he 'held himself erect' but was 'a bad bishop' is the exiled republican of 1862 settling a score.`,quote:`Gregory XVI, at the age of eighty`},{id:`pantheistical`,term:`pantheistical`,kind:`word`,chapter:14,text:`Pantheism identifies God with nature as a whole, a charge Catholic critics flung at Romantic writers, Hugo among them. He heads it off: the Bishop's night meditations under the stars are not a philosophy but a heart.`,quote:`a certain “pantheistical” physiognomy`},{id:`swedenborg-and-pascal`,term:`Swedenborg and Pascal`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`Emanuel Swedenborg, the Swedish scientist turned visionary who described conversations with angels; Blaise Pascal, the mathematician whose religious terror of the infinite spaces haunted his later years. Hugo names them as great minds whom contemplation of the absolute drove toward madness.`,quote:`like Swedenborg and Pascal`},{id:`elijah-s-mantle`,term:`Elijah’s mantle`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`The prophet Elijah's cloak, with which he parted the Jordan and which fell to Elisha when he was taken up to heaven (2 Kings 2). A chasuble with its folds would be a prophet's garment; the Bishop, Hugo says, had nothing of the prophet.`,quote:`the folds of Elijah’s mantle`},{id:`lucretius-manou-saint-paul-dante`,term:`Lucretius, Manou, Saint Paul, Dante`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`Four minds who stared into the abyss of being: the Roman poet of atoms and death, Manu the legendary Hindu lawgiver, the apostle, and the poet of Hell. Hugo sets them as the 'archangels of the human mind' whose questions the Bishop declined to ask.`,quote:`Lucretius, Manou, Saint Paul, Dante`},{id:`boulevard-gassendi`,term:`boulevard Gassendi`,kind:`place`,chapter:15,text:`Digne's tree-lined promenade, named for the philosopher Pierre Gassendi (1592–1655), a native of the district who was Descartes's great opponent. Hugo visited Digne and uses its real streets, fountains and inns.`,quote:`the boulevard Gassendi`},{id:`the-proclamation-of-the-gulf-juan`,term:`the proclamation of the Gulf Juan`,kind:`history`,chapter:15,text:`The manifesto Napoleon issued on landing from Elba on 1 March 1815, promising to reclaim his throne. General Drouot read it to the people of Digne on 4 March as the Emperor passed through; a Digne printer first printed the proclamations dictated on Elba. Jean Valjean enters the town by the same street seven months later.`,quote:`the proclamation of the Gulf Juan`},{id:`the-three-dauphins`,term:`the Three Dauphins`,kind:`history`,chapter:15,text:`The Grenoble inn where Napoleon lodged on 7 March 1815 rather than at the prefecture, saying he was going to a brave man's house. The Guides were his personal escort cavalry; General Bertrand, his Grand Marshal, followed him to Elba and Saint Helena. The Digne innkeeper Labarre borrows glory from his Grenoble cousin.`,quote:`the inn of the Three Dauphins in Grenoble`},{id:`leagues`,term:`leagues`,kind:`word`,chapter:15,text:`The French league (lieue) was about four kilometres, two and a half miles. Twelve leagues is nearly fifty kilometres, a brutal day's walk.`,quote:`I have travelled twelve leagues`,seeAlso:[56]},{id:`tso-maraude`,term:`Tso-maraude`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:15,text:`Provençal patois, roughly 'it's the prowler' (ço es lo maraud): the word that has gone round Digne for the stranger. It shows how fast the innkeeper's note to the town hall has travelled.`,quote:`“Tso-maraude.”`},{id:`la-rose-couverte`,term:`la rose couverte`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:15,text:`'The covered rose', a term from French stick-fencing (bâton): a whirling guard that keeps the staff moving around the body. Jean Valjean knows it, and uses it to back out of a dog's kennel.`,quote:`la rose couverte`},{id:`the-wars-of-religion`,term:`the wars of religion`,kind:`history`,chapter:15,text:`The civil wars between Catholics and Protestants that tore France apart from 1562 to 1598. Digne, in a region with many Protestants, was besieged more than once, and in 1815 still had its sixteenth-century walls and towers.`,quote:`sieges during the wars of religion`},{id:`jeannette-cross`,term:`Jeannette cross`,kind:`word`,chapter:16,text:`A small gold cross hung on a black velvet ribbon round the neck, the traditional ornament of French peasant and servant women. It is Madame Magloire's one piece of jewellery.`,quote:`a gold Jeannette cross on a velvet ribbon`},{id:`fichu-and-stomacher`,term:`fichu and stomacher`,kind:`word`,chapter:16,text:`A fichu is a triangular kerchief worn over the shoulders and tucked into the bodice; a stomacher is the decorative panel covering the front of the bodice. Madame Magloire dresses like a Marseilles working woman; Mademoiselle Baptistine like a lady of 1806, in a high-waisted Empire gown worn for ten years.`,quote:`a very white fichu`},{id:`the-baby-wig`,term:`the “baby wig”`,kind:`word`,chapter:16,text:`A small frizzed wig (perruque à l'enfant) worn by elderly ladies to cover grey hair, a fashion of the late eighteenth century. Baptistine's wig and 1806 gown mark her as gently out of date.`,quote:`known as the “baby wig.”`},{id:`a-bohemian`,term:`a Bohemian`,kind:`word`,chapter:16,text:`A gypsy, and by extension any rootless vagrant. Madame Magloire's gossip turns a tired traveller into 'a Bohemian, a barefooted vagabond', the standard picture of a dangerous outsider.`,quote:`a Bohemian, a barefooted vagabond`},{id:`the-galleys`,term:`the galleys`,kind:`history`,chapter:17,text:`The bagne, the prison for men sentenced to hard labour. The name survived from the oared galleys of Louis XIV, long gone; by 1815 'galley-slaves' worked in chains in the naval dockyards of Toulon, Brest and Rochefort. Sentences were counted in years of the galleys or 'for life', and the word carried a lifelong stain.`,quote:`a convict from the galleys`,seeAlso:[20,21,92]},{id:`yellow-passport`,term:`yellow passport`,kind:`history`,chapter:17,text:`A liberated convict received a passport printed on yellow paper, recording his crime and sentence and marking him as under police surveillance. He had to show it in every town and follow a prescribed route to an assigned residence. It made honest work and lodging almost impossible, which is Hugo's point.`,quote:`my yellow passport`,seeAlso:[23,91]},{id:`pontarlier`,term:`Pontarlier`,kind:`place`,chapter:17,text:`A town in the Jura mountains near the Swiss border, in Franche-Comté. It is the place of residence assigned to Jean Valjean on his passport, the far end of a forced march across France.`,quote:`on my way to Pontarlier`,seeAlso:[18]},{id:`the-bishop-of-majore`,term:`the Bishop of Majore`,kind:`word`,chapter:17,text:`Hapgood's rendering of 'la Major', the old cathedral of Marseilles. Bishops sometimes said mass for the convicts in the Toulon yard, with the men ranged in lines under loaded cannon: Jean Valjean's only idea of what a bishop is.`,quote:`the Bishop of Majore at Marseilles`},{id:`the-red-coat-the-ball-on-the-ankle`,term:`the red coat, the ball on the ankle`,kind:`lore`,chapter:17,text:`Convict dress and irons: a red woollen jacket or smock, a numbered cap, and a chain riveted to an iron ring on the ankle, often ending in a cannonball or linking two men together. The 'double chain' was a punishment, the 'cell' solitary confinement. Jean Valjean's catalogue is the galleys in a sentence.`,quote:`the red coat, the ball on the ankle`,seeAlso:[57,64]},{id:`the-shipwrecked-of-the-medusa`,term:`the shipwrecked of the Medusa`,kind:`history`,chapter:17,text:`The frigate Méduse ran aground off Africa in July 1816; 150 people were set adrift on a raft and only fifteen survived after thirteen days of thirst, murder and cannibalism. Géricault's huge painting made it the scandal of the Restoration. 'A glass of water to one of the shipwrecked' is Hugo's image of what 'Monsieur' means to a convict.`,quote:`the shipwrecked of the Medusa`,seeAlso:[28]},{id:`mauves-wine`,term:`Mauves wine`,kind:`word`,chapter:17,text:`Wine from Mauves on the Rhône, in the Saint-Joseph vineyards, a good local red. The Bishop keeps a bottle for guests and never drinks it himself, because wine is dear.`,quote:`his old Mauves wine`},{id:`thou`,term:`“thou”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:17,text:`French distinguishes the familiar 'tu' from the respectful 'vous'. Convicts, servants and beggars were addressed as 'tu' by their superiors as a matter of course, so when the Bishop says 'Monsieur' and 'vous' to Jean Valjean it is almost unbearable to him. Hapgood marks the contemptuous form with 'thou'.`,quote:`You do not address me as thou?`,seeAlso:[26,69]},{id:`more-joy-in-heaven`,term:`more joy in heaven`,kind:`reference`,chapter:17,text:`The Bishop paraphrases Luke 15:7, from the parable of the lost sheep: more rejoicing over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine just men. It is the only sermon he allows himself.`,quote:`There will be more joy in heaven`},{id:`franche-comte`,term:`Franche-Comté`,kind:`place`,chapter:18,text:`The old province on the Swiss border around Besançon and Pontarlier, mountainous, forested, with iron foundries, watchmaking and dairies. The Bishop took refuge there during the Revolution and worked with his hands, which is why he can describe its industries to a man who needs a trade.`,quote:`I took refuge in Franche-Comté`},{id:`fruitieres`,term:`fruitières`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:18,text:`The cooperative cheese-dairies of the Jura, where peasants pooled their cows' milk to make the great wheels of Comté (Gruyère) cheese. The grurin is the hired cheesemaker who records each member's milk on a tally stick. The Bishop dwells on the trade as a quiet hint of an honest living.`,quote:`which they call fruitières`},{id:`the-old-regime`,term:`the old regime`,kind:`history`,chapter:18,text:`The ancien régime: France before 1789, with its king, nobility, provinces and venal offices. A 'captain of the gates' was one of its small military sinecures. Baptistine's relative held one; the Revolution swept such things away along with the family's fortune.`,quote:`captain of the gates at Pontarlier under the old regime`},{id:`snuffing-out-his-candle-with-his-nostrils`,term:`snuffing out his candle with his nostrils`,kind:`custom`,chapter:19,text:`Convicts had no snuffers and learned to put out a candle by blowing sharply down through the nose. Hugo gives the detail as a reflex the galleys have left in Jean Valjean's body, along with sleeping in his clothes.`,quote:`Snuffing out his candle with his nostrils after the manner of convicts`},{id:`brie-and-faverolles`,term:`Brie and Faverolles`,kind:`place`,chapter:20,text:`Brie is the flat farming country east of Paris, known for its cheese. Faverolles, in the Aisne on its edge, is the village where Jean Valjean was a tree-pruner (élagueur), a seasonal labourer who trimmed orchards and roadside trees.`,quote:`a tree-pruner at Faverolles`,seeAlso:[54]},{id:`milk-fever`,term:`milk fever`,kind:`word`,chapter:20,text:`Puerperal fever, the often fatal infection that killed many women after childbirth, blamed at the time on the onset of the milk. Jean Valjean's mother died of it for want of care.`,quote:`died of a milk fever`},{id:`eighteen-sous-a-day`,term:`eighteen sous a day`,kind:`money`,chapter:20,text:`A pruner's wage, less than a franc, in a season when the work existed at all. A four-pound loaf cost around twelve to fifteen sous in a bad year, so feeding eight people on this was impossible, which is the arithmetic behind the theft.`,quote:`he earned eighteen sous a day`},{id:`the-terms-of-the-code`,term:`the terms of the Code`,kind:`history`,chapter:20,text:`The Penal Code of 1791 punished theft committed at night, by breaking into an inhabited house, with years in irons regardless of the value stolen. Hugo's charge is that the law was 'explicit' and therefore blind: a loaf and a broken pane carried five years.`,quote:`The terms of the Code were explicit`},{id:`floreal-year-iv`,term:`Floréal, year IV`,kind:`history`,chapter:20,text:`The revolutionary calendar renamed the months and counted years from the Republic's founding in 1792; 2 Floréal, year IV is 21 April 1796. 'Buona-Parte' is the Italian spelling of the young general's name, which the Directory's message still used. Hugo sets Napoleon's first victory at Montenotte against Jean Valjean's chaining on the same day.`,quote:`the 2nd of Floréal, year IV`},{id:`bicetre`,term:`Bicêtre`,kind:`place`,chapter:20,text:`A vast old hospital-prison south of Paris where convicts from all over northern France were gathered twice a year into the 'chain', riveted together by the neck in the courtyard, then marched or carted to the galleys. Jean Valjean's twenty-seven-day journey to Toulon began there.`,quote:`put in chains at Bicêtre`},{id:`number-24-601`,term:`number 24,601`,kind:`lore`,chapter:20,text:`Each convict was known by his register number and nothing else. Jean Valjean's number at Toulon is 24,601. The number is Hugo's way of saying the man himself has been erased.`,quote:`he was number 24,601`,seeAlso:[90]},{id:`the-maritime-tribunal`,term:`the maritime tribunal`,kind:`title`,chapter:20,text:`Because the galleys belonged to the Navy, offences inside them, escapes above all, were judged by a special naval court that could add years to a sentence without a jury. Four escape attempts turned Jean Valjean's five years into nineteen.`,quote:`The maritime tribunal condemned him`},{id:`claude-gaux`,term:`Claude Gaux`,kind:`reference`,chapter:20,text:`Hugo means Claude Gueux, the hero of his own 1834 story of that name: a real Troyes workman who stole bread to feed his family, killed a prison warder and was guillotined. Hugo cites his earlier book as evidence that hunger is where the law's disasters begin.`,quote:`Claude Gaux had stolen a loaf`},{id:`the-ignorantin-friars`,term:`the Ignorantin friars`,kind:`reference`,chapter:21,text:`The Brothers of the Christian Schools, the teaching order founded by Jean-Baptiste de La Salle, nicknamed 'Ignorantins' because they taught the poor and took no holy orders. They ran the school in the Toulon galleys where Jean Valjean learned to read at forty.`,quote:`kept by the Ignorantin friars`},{id:`dante-at-the-portals-of-hell`,term:`Dante at the portals of hell`,kind:`reference`,chapter:21,text:`The inscription over the gate of Hell in Dante's Inferno ends 'Abandon all hope, you who enter.' Hugo's physiologist would do the same to Jean Valjean's soul: erase the word hope.`,quote:`like Dante at the portals of hell`,seeAlso:[57]},{id:`jean-the-jackscrew`,term:`Jean the Jackscrew`,kind:`word`,chapter:21,text:`A jackscrew (cric) is the screw-jack used to lift carts and heavy loads. Hugo adds that the old French word for it was 'orgueil', pride, whence the Rue Montorgueil in Paris. Jean Valjean's nickname in the galleys is for a man who can lift what a machine lifts.`,quote:`nicknamed him Jean the Jackscrew`,seeAlso:[45]},{id:`the-caryatids-of-puget`,term:`the caryatids of Puget`,kind:`reference`,chapter:21,text:`Pierre Puget (1620–1694), the great Baroque sculptor of Provence, carved the two straining stone atlantes that hold up the balcony of Toulon's town hall, a landmark of the port. Jean Valjean is said to have shouldered one when it came loose.`,quote:`those admirable caryatids of Puget`},{id:`galley-sergeant`,term:`galley-sergeant`,kind:`title`,chapter:21,text:`The garde-chiourme, the warder of a gang of convicts, armed with a cudgel and a whip. In Jean Valjean's vision of society he is the nearest tier of the pyramid that crushes him, with the gendarme and the archbishop above and the Emperor at the top.`,quote:`the galley-sergeant and his cudgel`},{id:`grasse`,term:`Grasse`,kind:`place`,chapter:23,text:`The perfume town in the hills above Cannes, surrounded by fields of orange trees, roses and jasmine distilled for scent. Jean Valjean's first day's work as a free man, unloading bales at an orange-flower distillery, earns him half the agreed wage.`,quote:`an orange-flower distillery`},{id:`a-miner-s-candlestick`,term:`a miner’s candlestick`,kind:`word`,chapter:24,text:`A heavy iron spike with a socket for a candle, driven into rock to light a quarry face. Convicts quarried stone in the hills round Toulon and could get hold of such tools. In the dark it is simply a short pointed iron bar.`,quote:`a miner’s candlestick`},{id:`the-statue-of-salt`,term:`the statue of salt`,kind:`reference`,chapter:25,text:`Lot's wife, turned to a pillar of salt when she looked back at Sodom (Genesis 19:26). Jean Valjean freezes like her at the shriek of the Bishop's door hinge.`,quote:`petrified like the statue of salt`},{id:`the-pastoral-ring`,term:`the pastoral ring`,kind:`word`,chapter:25,text:`The ring a bishop wears as a sign of his office, kissed by the faithful. It is on the hand that hangs over the edge of the bed as the Bishop sleeps.`,quote:`adorned with the pastoral ring`},{id:`brigadier-of-gendarmes`,term:`brigadier of gendarmes`,kind:`title`,chapter:26,text:`The non-commissioned officer commanding a small gendarmerie post, equivalent to a corporal, not a general as in English. He salutes the Bishop as a superior and takes his word without question.`,quote:`A brigadier of gendarmes`},{id:`savoyard`,term:`Savoyard`,kind:`word`,chapter:27,text:`Boys from the poor Alpine duchy of Savoy, then outside France, came down each winter to earn pennies as chimney-sweeps, street musicians and showmen, with a hurdy-gurdy and a trained marmot in a box. They were the type of the homeless child, and Hugo's Little Gervais is one.`,quote:`a little Savoyard`,seeAlso:[43,90]},{id:`forty-sou-piece`,term:`forty-sou piece`,kind:`money`,chapter:27,text:`A silver two-franc coin, which the child calls his 'white piece'. It is a day's wages for a labourer and the boy's whole fortune.`,quote:`one forty-sou piece`,seeAlso:[57,70,100]},{id:`five-franc-pieces`,term:`five-franc pieces`,kind:`money`,chapter:27,text:`The big silver coin of the period, also called an écu or 'a hundred sous'; working people nicknamed it a 'hind wheel' for its size. Jean Valjean presses two, then two more, on the priest, a sum a peasant family might live on for a fortnight.`,quote:`two five-franc pieces`,seeAlso:[100,101]},{id:`the-twenty-second-of-his-reign`,term:`the twenty-second of his reign`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`Louis XVIII dated his reign from 1795, when the boy Louis XVII died in prison, as if the Republic and the Empire had never happened. So 1817 was officially his twenty-second year, though he had sat on the throne for barely three. Hugo opens the Book with this royal fiction.`,quote:`entitled the twenty-second of his reign`},{id:`fleurs-de-lys`,term:`fleurs-de-lys`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`The stylised lily, emblem of the French kings. After 1814 it replaced the imperial eagle and bees everywhere, on coins, shop signs and buttonholes, and loyal citizens wore a silver lily on a white ribbon. Scratching off Napoleon's N's from the Louvre was the same campaign in reverse.`,quote:`decked with fleurs-de-lys`,seeAlso:[32]},{id:`st-helena`,term:`St. Helena`,kind:`place`,chapter:28,text:`The remote British island in the South Atlantic where Napoleon was sent after Waterloo and kept under guard. He was there from October 1815 until his death in 1821. Hugo's detail that he had his old coats turned, for want of green cloth, is a jab at his English jailers.`,quote:`Napoleon was at St. Helena`},{id:`champ-de-mai`,term:`Champ de Mai`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`The grand ceremony Napoleon staged on the Champ de Mars in Paris on 1 June 1815, during the Hundred Days, to swear in a new constitution before the army. Hugo enjoys the absurdity: the 'Field of May' was held in June on the 'Field of March'. Two years later its painted columns lie rotting in the grass.`,quote:`the Emperor’s platform in the Champ de Mai`},{id:`talleyrand-and-the-abbe-louis`,term:`Talleyrand and the Abbé Louis`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`Talleyrand, a bishop under the old monarchy, served the Revolution, Napoleon and then the Bourbons in turn; the Abbé Louis was the Restoration's finance minister. Both had officiated at the patriotic mass of the Festival of Federation on 14 July 1790. Hugo reuses the 'two augurs' who cannot look at each other without laughing.`,quote:`laughed as they looked at each other, with the laugh of the two augurs`},{id:`the-charter`,term:`the Charter`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`The constitution Louis XVIII 'granted' in 1814: a king with real power, two chambers elected by a tiny wealthy electorate, and guarantees of some revolutionary gains. It was the Restoration's compromise between the old monarchy and the Revolution, so 'the snuffbox à la Charter' and the king's title 'Immortal Author of the Charter' were political fashions.`,quote:`the snuffbox à la Charter`},{id:`duc-de-berri-and-louvel`,term:`Duc de Berri and Louvel`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`The Duc de Berry, the king's nephew, was the Bourbons' hope for an heir. In February 1820 a saddler named Louvel stabbed him to death outside the Opéra, to end the dynasty. In 1817 Hugo can show him newly married and 'surveyed from the shadow' by his future killer.`,quote:`already surveyed from the shadow by Louvel`},{id:`cafe-lemblin-and-cafe-valois`,term:`Café Lemblin and Café Valois`,kind:`place`,chapter:28,text:`Two cafés in the Palais-Royal that served as political clubs: Lemblin was the haunt of Bonapartist half-pay officers, the Valois of old royalists. Under the Restoration even where one drank one's coffee declared one's side.`,quote:`The Café Lemblin stood up for the Emperor`},{id:`regicides`,term:`regicides`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`The members of the Convention who had voted for the death of Louis XVI in 1793. In 1816 those who had rallied to Napoleon in the Hundred Days were banished from France. Whether one said 'the regicides' or 'the voters', 'Napoleon' or 'Bonaparte', 'the allies' or 'the enemies', placed one instantly.`,quote:`the regicides, or to say the voters`},{id:`chateaubriand`,term:`Chateaubriand`,kind:`reference`,chapter:28,text:`François-René de Chateaubriand (1768–1848), the great Romantic writer and royalist statesman, the literary idol of Hugo's youth. 'The Monarchy According to the Charter' (1816) was his political pamphlet. Hugo's picture of him cleaning his teeth while dictating is affectionate mockery.`,quote:`Chateaubriand stood every morning at his window`},{id:`monsieur`,term:`Monsieur`,kind:`title`,chapter:28,text:`Used alone and capitalised, 'Monsieur' was the title of the king's eldest brother: here the Comte d'Artois, leader of the Ultras. 'Madame' likewise meant the senior royal lady, the Duchesse d'Angoulême, daughter of Louis XVI. The Duc d'Angoulême was the heir's elder cousin, the Duc d'Orléans the head of a rival branch.`,quote:`with Monsieur’s approval`},{id:`the-king-of-rome`,term:`the King of Rome`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`Napoleon's only legitimate son (1811–1832), given the title at birth. After 1815 he lived in Vienna with his mother's Austrian family. For Bonapartists he was 'Napoleon II'; schoolboys wearing the Bourbon lily fought over him.`,quote:`apropos of the King of Rome`},{id:`steamboat`,term:`steamboat`,kind:`history`,chapter:28,text:`Steam navigation was being tried on the Seine in the late 1810s, and Parisians treated it as a toy. Hugo, writing in 1862 when steam had transformed travel, savours the shortsightedness. He uses the same device for the obscure Fourier, the socialist thinker, overshadowed by the famous mathematician of the same name.`,quote:`an utopia—a steamboat`},{id:`the-invalides`,term:`the Invalides`,kind:`place`,chapter:28,text:`The Hôtel des Invalides, Louis XIV's great home for old and wounded soldiers, with its gilded dome, where Napoleon's veterans lived out their days. Its pensioners were issued large blue cotton handkerchiefs, a familiar sight in Paris.`,quote:`having the dome of the Invalides regilded`,seeAlso:[35,37]},{id:`student`,term:`student`,kind:`custom`,chapter:29,text:`Provincial young men of good family went to Paris to study law or medicine, lived in the Latin Quarter on allowances from home, and often took years over their degrees. Tholomyès, an 'antique old student' of thirty with four thousand francs a year, is the type at its most comfortable.`,quote:`when one says student, one says Parisian`},{id:`oscars`,term:`Oscars`,kind:`reference`,chapter:29,text:`Ossian, the Scottish bard of James Macpherson's forged 'ancient' poems (1760s), was a European craze, and Napoleon loved him. Fashionable parents named their sons Oscar after Ossian's son. Hugo's joke is that the four students belong to a generation of Oscars, before Wellington made 'Arthur' the smart name.`,quote:`They were four Oscars`},{id:`grisettes`,term:`grisettes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:29,text:`Young working women of Paris, seamstresses, milliners and shop girls, named for the cheap grey cloth they wore. The grisette who lived with a student for a few years, without marriage, was a stock figure of Parisian life and literature, treated lightly by everyone except Hugo.`,quote:`throngs of students and grisettes`},{id:`mount-sainte-genevieve`,term:`Mount Sainte-Geneviève`,kind:`place`,chapter:29,text:`The hill on the left bank crowned by the Panthéon, heart of the Latin Quarter, where the schools and colleges of the University stood. To have four thousand francs a year there was riches beside the usual student's pinched allowance.`,quote:`a splendid scandal on Mount Sainte-Geneviève`},{id:`the-vaudeville`,term:`the Vaudeville`,kind:`place`,chapter:29,text:`The Théâtre du Vaudeville in Paris specialised in light comedies with songs. Having a piece rejected there marks Tholomyès as a dabbler in wit, not a writer.`,quote:`He had had a piece rejected at the Vaudeville`},{id:`forty-five-years-ago`,term:`forty-five years ago`,kind:`lore`,chapter:30,text:`Hugo writes from 1862, looking back to 1817, and often reminds the reader of the gap: the railway and the steamboat have since replaced the coach and the rowing-boat, and Paris has swallowed its villages. The past in this book is always seen from the narrator's present.`,quote:`forty-five years ago`},{id:`saint-cloud`,term:`Saint-Cloud`,kind:`place`,chapter:30,text:`A royal château and park on the Seine west of Paris, open to the public, with famous cascades and fountains. It was the classic Sunday outing for Parisians of modest means, reached by coach or by boat.`,quote:`they went to Saint-Cloud by the coach`},{id:`canezou`,term:`canezou`,kind:`word`,chapter:30,text:`A light muslin bodice or sleeveless jacket worn over a gown, fashionable in the 1810s. Hugo's derivation from 'quinze août' (15 August) spoken in a Marseilles accent is a playful folk etymology. With her mauve barège gown, a light gauzy wool, it is Fantine's whole summer finery.`,quote:`whose name, canezou, a corruption of the words quinze août`},{id:`nankeen`,term:`nankeen`,kind:`word`,chapter:30,text:`A sturdy yellowish cotton cloth, originally from Nanjing, used for summer trousers. Tholomyès's wide 'elephant-leg' nankeen trousers and his cigar, still a novelty, are the dandy's uniform of 1817.`,quote:`elephant-leg pattern of nankeen`},{id:`the-departure-for-cythera`,term:`The departure for Cythera`,kind:`reference`,chapter:31,text:`Watteau's painting 'Pilgrimage to Cythera' (1717) shows elegant couples on the island of Venus, the classic image of a lovers' outing. Hugo calls on Watteau, Lancret, Diderot and d'Urfé, author of the pastoral romance 'L'Astrée', as connoisseurs of such idylls.`,quote:`The departure for Cythera! exclaims Watteau`},{id:`russian-mountains`,term:`Russian mountains`,kind:`custom`,chapter:31,text:`The 'montagnes russes', an early roller coaster: cars running on rails down an undulating wooden track, imported from the ice slides of Russia. The one in the Beaujon gardens near the Champs-Élysées was a sensation of 1817.`,quote:`sliding down the Russian mountains`},{id:`barrier-of-l-etoile`,term:`barrier of l’Étoile`,kind:`place`,chapter:31,text:`Paris was ringed by the customs wall of the Farmers-General, with gates called barriers where duty (the octroi) was paid on goods entering the city. The Étoile barrier stood at the top of the Champs-Élysées, where the Arc de Triomphe was then unfinished.`,quote:`they reached the barrier of l’Étoile`},{id:`the-white-flag`,term:`the white flag`,kind:`history`,chapter:32,text:`The plain white banner of the Bourbon monarchy, restored in 1814 in place of the revolutionary tricolour. To fly it over the Tuileries palace was to say that the Revolution was over; Bonapartists kept the tricolour as a forbidden emblem.`,quote:`the white flag, showing faintly rosy`},{id:`rendez-nous-notre-pere-de-gand`,term:`Rendez-nous notre père de Gand`,kind:`history`,chapter:32,text:`'Give us back our father from Ghent': a royalist song of 1815, when Louis XVIII had fled to Ghent during the Hundred Days. Opponents turned it into a pun on 'gant' (glove) to mock a king who returned behind foreign armies.`,quote:`Rendez-nous notre père de Gand`},{id:`place-louis-xv`,term:`Place Louis XV`,kind:`place`,chapter:32,text:`The great square at the foot of the Champs-Élysées, renamed Place de la Révolution in 1792, when the guillotine stood there and Louis XVI was beheaded. It became Place de la Concorde in 1795 and was given back its royal name at the Restoration, until 1830.`,quote:`which had become the Place Louis XV once more`},{id:`angles`,term:`Anglès`,kind:`history`,chapter:32,text:`Count Jules Anglès, prefect of police of Paris from 1815 to 1821. His reassuring report on the 'amiable rabble' of the suburbs gives Hugo the cue for a warning about what the Paris populace can do.`,quote:`Chief of Police Anglès`},{id:`the-10th-of-august`,term:`the 10th of August`,kind:`history`,chapter:32,text:`On 10 August 1792 the armed people of the Paris faubourgs stormed the Tuileries palace and overthrew the monarchy. Hugo pairs it with Austerlitz, Napoleon's greatest victory, as proof that the 'little man of the faubourgs' becomes a giant when roused.`,quote:`he will produce the 10th of August`},{id:`la-carmagnole-and-the-marseillaise`,term:`la Carmagnole and the Marseillaise`,kind:`history`,chapter:32,text:`Two songs of the Revolution. 'La Carmagnole' was a jeering dance-song against the king and queen; the 'Marseillaise', written in 1792 and sung by volunteers from Marseilles, became the anthem of the Republic and was banned under the Restoration. Hugo's point: one topples a king, the other frees the world.`,quote:`nothing but “la Carmagnole,”`},{id:`caudine-forks`,term:`Caudine Forks`,kind:`reference`,chapter:32,text:`The mountain pass where the Samnites trapped a Roman army in 321 BC and forced it to march out under a yoke of spears in humiliation. Hugo says the Paris workman will make any narrow street a trap of the same kind.`,quote:`Caudine Forks`},{id:`horses-of-marly`,term:`horses of Marly`,kind:`place`,chapter:32,text:`Guillaume Coustou's two great marble groups of rearing horses held by grooms, carved for the royal château of Marly and set at the entrance of the Champs-Élysées in 1795. They were a Paris landmark.`,quote:`The horses of Marly`},{id:`the-spleen`,term:`the spleen`,kind:`word`,chapter:33,text:`Melancholy or low spirits, thought to come from the spleen; the French borrowed the English word in the eighteenth century, and to 'have the spleen' was fashionably English. Favorite's version is a grisette's grumble about rain, stingy lovers and the price of butter.`,quote:`I have the spleen, as the English say`},{id:`precentor`,term:`precentor`,kind:`title`,chapter:33,text:`The church officer who leads the singing in a parish, a modest lay or clerical post. Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas is a Latin Quarter church; Favorite's young actor is a precentor's son who copies documents for an attorney at twenty sous a day.`,quote:`a former precentor of Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas`},{id:`est-modus-in-rebus`,term:`Est modus in rebus`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:34,text:`Latin, 'there is a measure in things', from Horace's Satires. Tholomyès stuffs his mock sermon on moderation with Latin tags, puns and pedantry, the showing-off of a law student who has stayed too long at school.`,quote:`Est modus in rebus`},{id:`quirites`,term:`Quirites`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:34,text:`The formal name of the citizens of ancient Rome, used in solemn address. Tholomyès mixes it with 'caballeros' and 'gentlemen' in a parody of oratory; his 'Sylla' is Sulla, the Roman dictator who unexpectedly laid down his power.`,quote:`Quirites, gentlemen, caballeros, my friends`},{id:`grimod-de-la-reyniere`,term:`Grimod de la Reynière`,kind:`reference`,chapter:34,text:`Alexandre Grimod de la Reynière (1758–1837), the first great French food writer, author of the 'Almanach des Gourmands'. Tholomyès claims him and Talleyrand, a famous host, as authorities for eating slowly.`,quote:`Grimod de la Reynière agrees with Talleyrand`},{id:`pardieu`,term:`Pardieu`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:34,text:`'By God', a mild French oath. Hapgood leaves many such exclamations in French (Pardieu, Mon Dieu, Parbleu); they are everyday expletives, not blasphemies.`,quote:`“Pardieu,” said Tholomyès.`},{id:`elleviou`,term:`Elleviou`,kind:`reference`,chapter:34,text:`Jean Elleviou (1769–1842), the celebrated tenor of the Opéra-Comique. To call the nightingale 'a gratuitous Elleviou' is to say it gives a star's performance for free.`,quote:`a gratuitous Elleviou`},{id:`descartes-or-spinoza`,term:`Descartes or Spinoza`,kind:`reference`,chapter:35,text:`The two great rationalist philosophers of the seventeenth century, a serious student's question. Tholomyès answers 'Désaugiers', a writer of drinking songs, and then turns 'Berquin', a children's author, into the pun 'Ber-Choux'. Every reply is a dodge.`,quote:`Which do you prefer, Descartes or Spinoza?`},{id:`nil-sub-sole-novum`,term:`Nil sub sole novum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:35,text:`Latin, 'nothing new under the sun', from Ecclesiastes 1:9; 'amor omnibus idem', 'love is the same for all', is from Virgil's Georgics. Tholomyès's punning 'Carabine' and 'Carabin' is student slang for a grisette and a medical student.`,quote:`Nil sub sole novum`},{id:`aspasia`,term:`Aspasia`,kind:`reference`,chapter:35,text:`The brilliant mistress of Pericles in fifth-century Athens, admired by Socrates. Tholomyès calls her 'Socrates plus Manon Lescaut', joining the philosopher to the heroine of Prévost's novel of a fatally charming kept woman.`,quote:`Do you know what Aspasia was, ladies?`},{id:`matin`,term:`Mâtin`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:35,text:`A carter's oath, here glossed as 'the jade'. Tholomyès makes a pun on Malherbe's famous elegy, in which a girl lived 'the space of a morning' (matin); his horse lived 'the space of a mâtin'. It is a callous joke over a dying animal.`,quote:`the sacramental word, Mâtin`},{id:`diligences`,term:`diligences`,kind:`custom`,chapter:36,text:`The big public stagecoaches that carried passengers and mail between towns, with several compartments and luggage piled on the roof under a tarpaulin. They travelled day and night and kept to timetables, stopping only at fixed posts, which is why Fantine is surprised to see one halt.`,quote:`the departure of the mail-coaches and diligences`},{id:`laffitte-and-caillard`,term:`Laffitte and Caillard`,kind:`history`,chapter:36,text:`Laffitte, Caillard et Compagnie ran the great coaching service of the day, rivals of the Messageries royales. The students' joke is that they flee 'in the arms of Laffitte and on the wings of Caillard', that is, by stagecoach, at three leagues an hour.`,quote:`in the arms of Laffitte and on the wings of Caillard`},{id:`prodigal-sons`,term:`prodigal sons`,kind:`reference`,chapter:36,text:`The parable in Luke 15 of the son who wastes his inheritance and is welcomed home with a fatted calf. The students' letter turns it inside out: they go back to their families, and to careers as prefects and magistrates, as if returning were a sacrifice.`,quote:`call us prodigal sons`},{id:`montfermeil`,term:`Montfermeil`,kind:`place`,chapter:37,text:`A village on a wooded plateau about fifteen kilometres east of Paris, beyond Livry and Chelles, in the forest of Bondy. In 1818 it was quiet countryside, a day's walk from the city, reached by the little suburban coaches.`,quote:`There was, at Montfermeil, near Paris`},{id:`sergeant-of-waterloo`,term:`Sergeant of Waterloo`,kind:`lore`,chapter:37,text:`Inns were known by their painted signboards. This one shows a soldier carrying a wounded general off a smoky battlefield, a scene of heroism at Waterloo, the battle of 1815 that ended Napoleon's reign. Its spelling 'Sargent' is part of the sign's roughness.`,quote:`At the sign of Sergeant of Waterloo`,seeAlso:[38,94]},{id:`valenciennes-lace`,term:`Valenciennes lace`,kind:`word`,chapter:37,text:`A fine, costly bobbin lace from Valenciennes in northern France. Fantine has dressed her child in it while she herself wears coarse linen, the one vanity she has kept.`,quote:`Valenciennes lace on her cap`},{id:`put-out-to-nurse`,term:`put out to nurse`,kind:`custom`,chapter:37,text:`It was common, among workers and the middle class alike, to send a baby or small child to be boarded by a woman in the country for a monthly fee. A working mother with no husband had little choice. Seven francs a month, with six months in advance, is a steep but plausible rate.`,quote:`I will give you six francs a month`},{id:`louis-philippe`,term:`Louis Philippe`,kind:`history`,chapter:37,text:`Louis-Philippe, Duc d'Orléans, of the younger branch of the royal family, became King of the French after the revolution of July 1830 and reigned until 1848. Hugo uses his reign as a later fixed point when he dismisses Tholomyès from the story.`,quote:`under King Louis Philippe`,seeAlso:[224]},{id:`a-bailiff-and-a-protest`,term:`a bailiff and a protest`,kind:`custom`,chapter:37,text:`An unpaid promissory note was formally 'protested' by a bailiff (huissier), the first step to seizure of goods and prison for debt. Thénardier's note for 110 francs falls due the next day, which is why the stranger's 57 francs come so pat.`,quote:`I should have had a bailiff and a protest after me`},{id:`clelie-and-lodoiska`,term:`Clélie and Lodoïska`,kind:`reference`,chapter:38,text:`Clélie was Mademoiselle de Scudéry's vast heroic romance of the 1650s; Lodoïska a sentimental tale of the 1790s. Hugo traces the long slide of the romance from the salons to the porters' lodges, down to the cheap novels Madame Thénardier devours.`,quote:`after having been Clélie, was no longer anything but Lodoïska`},{id:`eponine`,term:`Éponine`,kind:`lore`,chapter:38,text:`Epponina was a Gaulish heroine of the first century who hid her rebel husband for nine years and died with him under Vespasian, a favourite of romances. Gulnare is the heroine of Byron's 'The Corsair'; Ducray-Duminil wrote popular novels. The Thénardier girls are named out of their mother's reading.`,quote:`her eldest daughter was named Éponine`},{id:`megaera-and-pamela`,term:`Megaera and Pamela`,kind:`reference`,chapter:38,text:`Megaera is one of the Furies of Greek myth, a byword for a shrew; Pamela the virtuous servant-girl heroine of Richardson's novel. Hugo sums up Madame Thénardier's whole development in the passage from one to the other.`,quote:`when the Megaera began to be developed from the Pamela`},{id:`pawnbroker-s`,term:`pawnbroker’s`,kind:`custom`,chapter:39,text:`The Mont-de-Piété, the state pawnshop of Paris, lent money against goods at interest. Pawning was a routine part of poor people's budgets. Madame Thénardier gets sixty francs for the outfit Fantine was so proud of.`,quote:`pawned it at the pawnbroker’s for sixty francs`},{id:`dumollard`,term:`Dumollard`,kind:`history`,chapter:39,text:`Martin Dumollard, a murderer of servant girls near Lyons, tried and guillotined in 1862 as Hugo was finishing the novel. Hugo cites the court record that he had been 'working for his living' alone since the age of five.`,quote:`a man named Dumollard`},{id:`montreuil-sur-mer`,term:`Montreuil-sur-Mer`,kind:`place`,chapter:40,text:`A small walled hill town in the Pas-de-Calais, in northern France near the Channel coast, with an upper and a lower town; despite its name it is some way inland. Hapgood often abbreviates it, as Hugo does, to M. sur M.`,quote:`had reached Montreuil-sur-Mer`},{id:`english-jet-and-black-glass-trinkets`,term:`English jet and black glass trinkets`,kind:`word`,chapter:40,text:`Jet is a black fossil stone, polished for mourning jewellery and beads; it came chiefly from Whitby in England. Cheap imitations were made of black glass. Replacing costly resin with gum-lac (shellac) is the kind of small technical trick that made a provincial fortune in the early industrial age.`,quote:`the imitation of English jet and the black glass trinkets of Germany`},{id:`father-madeleine`,term:`Father Madeleine`,kind:`custom`,chapter:40,text:`'Father' (le père) before a surname was the familiar way of naming a working man or an older man of the people, like 'old so-and-so' in English. The narrator tracks the stranger's rise through his names: Father Madeleine, then Monsieur Madeleine, then Monsieur le Maire.`,quote:`He was called Father Madeleine`},{id:`laffitte`,term:`Laffitte`,kind:`history`,chapter:41,text:`Jacques Laffitte (1767–1844), the most famous Paris banker of the Restoration, governor of the Bank of France and a leading liberal. Having money 'with Laffitte' was the period's shorthand for a fortune safely banked in Paris.`,quote:`lodged in his name with Laffitte`,seeAlso:[42,346]},{id:`mayor`,term:`mayor`,kind:`title`,chapter:41,text:`Under the Restoration mayors were not elected: the king appointed them, on the prefect's recommendation, and the appointment appeared in the Moniteur. The mayor was head of the town's administration and its municipal police. Refusing the office, as Father Madeleine first does, was a rare snub to royal favour.`,quote:`to be appointed by the King, mayor of Montreuil-sur-Mer`,seeAlso:[52]},{id:`low-mass`,term:`low mass`,kind:`custom`,chapter:41,text:`A short, spoken mass said early in the morning without music, attended by servants and working people. High mass, with singing and full ceremony, and vespers in the afternoon were the services where the respectable were seen. The deputy's piety is for show.`,quote:`He went regularly to low mass every Sunday`},{id:`fouche-duc-d-otrante`,term:`Fouché, Duc d’Otrante`,kind:`history`,chapter:41,text:`Joseph Fouché (1759–1820), once a teacher in the Oratorian order, then a regicide of the Convention and Napoleon's dreaded minister of police; he served Louis XVIII briefly in 1815. Hugo's irony is that the deputy shares 'the religious ideas' of this famous unbeliever.`,quote:`Fouché, Duc d’Otrante`},{id:`infant-school`,term:`infant school`,kind:`history`,chapter:41,text:`The salle d'asile, a day nursery and school for the small children of working mothers, was just being imported from England in the 1820s; the first in Paris opened in 1826. Madeleine's is ahead of its time, like his dispensary and workers' fund.`,quote:`an infant school, a thing then almost unknown in France`},{id:`orviot`,term:`orviot`,kind:`word`,chapter:42,text:`Hapgood's spelling of 'orvale', clary sage, a strong-smelling herb hung up to drive off weevils. Madeleine's farming 'recipes', from salted water for wheat blight to nettles as fodder and cloth, mark him as someone who knows country work at first hand.`,quote:`hanging up orviot in bloom everywhere`},{id:`hall-marked`,term:`hall-marked`,kind:`word`,chapter:42,text:`Silver plate was stamped by the state assay office to guarantee its purity, so a hallmark proved that an object was real silver and not plated. The ladies of the town take their wit from noticing it.`,quote:`“for they were hall-marked,”`},{id:`in-the-odor-of-sanctity`,term:`in the odor of sanctity`,kind:`word`,chapter:43,text:`An old phrase for dying with a reputation for holiness, from the belief that saints' bodies gave off a sweet scent. It is the language of the newspaper notice, not a formal title.`,quote:`died in the odor of sanctity`},{id:`crape-on-his-hat`,term:`crape on his hat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:43,text:`Mourning was strictly codified: black clothes, and a band of black crape round the hat for a man, worn for a set period according to how close the dead person had been. Public mourning for someone was a claim of connection, which is why the town gossips read it as one.`,quote:`with crape on his hat`,seeAlso:[171]},{id:`faubourg-saint-germain`,term:`Faubourg Saint-Germain`,kind:`place`,chapter:43,text:`The aristocratic quarter of the Left Bank in Paris, home of the old nobility after the Restoration and a byword for exclusive royalist society. Every provincial town had its 'microscopic Faubourg Saint-Germain' of local gentry.`,quote:`The microscopic Faubourg Saint-Germain`,seeAlso:[166]},{id:`inspector`,term:`inspector`,kind:`title`,chapter:44,text:`An inspector of police was a mid-ranking officer who carried out the investigations and arrests; in a small town he was the police. Javert owes his post to the patronage of the prefect of police in Paris, Comte Anglès.`,quote:`the unpleasant but useful functions of an inspector`},{id:`broken-her-ban`,term:`broken her ban`,kind:`custom`,chapter:44,text:`A released convict was placed under surveillance and forbidden to live outside his assigned place; to 'break one's ban' (rompre son ban) was to leave it, a crime that sent a man back to the galleys. Hugo's sentence shows how Javert ranks duty above blood.`,quote:`if she had broken her ban`},{id:`brutus-in-vidocq`,term:`Brutus in Vidocq`,kind:`reference`,chapter:44,text:`Vidocq (1775–1857) was the escaped convict who became chief of the Paris detective police; Brutus the Roman consul who condemned his own sons for treason. Hugo means a policeman with a Roman's pitiless virtue.`,quote:`Brutus in Vidocq`},{id:`styx`,term:`Styx`,kind:`reference`,chapter:44,text:`In Greek myth, the river of the underworld that the dead cross and cannot recross. Hugo's image is of a law that places such a river at the bottom of society, so that those who fall can never come back.`,quote:`who place a Styx at the base of society`},{id:`louis-d-or`,term:`louis d’or`,kind:`money`,chapter:45,text:`A gold coin. Strictly the louis was the old royal coin, but by the 1820s people used 'louis' for any twenty-franc gold piece, the same coin as the napoleon. Twenty louis, the four hundred francs Madeleine offers for lifting the cart, is more than a labourer earned in a year.`,quote:`There are five louis d’or to be earned!`,seeAlso:[49]},{id:`notary`,term:`notary`,kind:`title`,chapter:45,text:`A notaire was a public lawyer who drew up contracts, wills and property deeds and kept them on record; every small town had one, and he was a man of standing. An ex-notary reduced to driving a cart has fallen a long way.`,quote:`an ex-notary and a peasant who was almost educated`,seeAlso:[138]},{id:`farrier`,term:`farrier`,kind:`word`,chapter:45,text:`A blacksmith who shod horses and did rough ironwork. He was the likeliest person in a village to own a jackscrew, the screw-jack for lifting a cart.`,quote:`where there is a farrier`},{id:`sisters-of-charity`,term:`sisters of charity`,kind:`custom`,chapter:46,text:`The Daughters of Charity, founded by Saint Vincent de Paul in 1633, were nuns who lived in the world rather than behind convent walls, nursing in hospitals and serving the poor in their grey-blue habit and great white cornette. Most French hospitals and infirmaries were staffed by them.`,quote:`served by two sisters of charity`,seeAlso:[55]},{id:`thousand-franc-banknote`,term:`thousand-franc banknote`,kind:`money`,chapter:46,text:`Bank of France notes came only in large denominations, 500 and 1,000 francs, and ordinary people rarely saw one. A thousand francs was two or three years' wages for a working man.`,quote:`a thousand-franc banknote on his night-stand`},{id:`the-scarf`,term:`the scarf`,kind:`custom`,chapter:46,text:`A French mayor's badge of office is a sash worn across the chest or round the waist, put on for ceremonies and acts of authority. Seeing Madeleine in it is what makes Javert shudder.`,quote:`clothed in the scarf which gave him authority`},{id:`m-de-villele`,term:`M. de Villèle`,kind:`history`,chapter:46,text:`Joseph de Villèle, the Ultra-royalist leader who was finance minister from 1821 and prime minister from 1822 to 1828. He was a careful administrator, and Hugo has him cite the arrondissement's cheap tax collection as a model.`,quote:`M. de Villèle, then Minister of Finance`,seeAlso:[170]},{id:`public-letter-writer`,term:`public letter-writer`,kind:`custom`,chapter:47,text:`The écrivain public sat at a stall or in a booth and wrote letters, petitions and love notes for the many who could not write. He knew everyone's secrets, which is why the gossips stand him drinks.`,quote:`she was obliged to write through a public letter-writer`},{id:`paid-the-carriage-on-the-letter`,term:`paid the carriage on the letter`,kind:`custom`,chapter:47,text:`Before postage stamps, which France adopted only in 1849, postage was charged by distance and usually paid by the person who received the letter. Paying it in advance yourself was a courtesy, and an expense, which the workroom notices.`,quote:`she paid the carriage on the letter`},{id:`from-the-bernardines-to-the-jacobins`,term:`from the Bernardines to the Jacobins`,kind:`history`,chapter:47,text:`A pun. The Bernardines were Cistercian monks; the 'Jacobins' were Dominican friars, but also the radical revolutionary club named after their old convent in Paris. Madame Victurnien's husband left his monastery in 1793 for revolutionary politics.`,quote:`passed from the Bernardines to the Jacobins`},{id:`bigot`,term:`bigot`,kind:`word`,chapter:47,text:`In older English and in French (bigote), a person of showy, narrow and censorious piety, not specifically one prejudiced against a group. Madame Victurnien turned devout at the Restoration, when piety was in fashion and in favour.`,quote:`At the Restoration she had turned bigot`},{id:`twelve-sous-a-day`,term:`twelve sous a day`,kind:`money`,chapter:48,text:`Sewing coarse shirts for the garrison brought in twelve sous, barely half a franc, for a long day; Cosette's board alone cost ten. When prison workshops undercut the trade, the rate falls to nine sous for seventeen hours. Hugo gives the arithmetic so the reader can see there is no way out.`,quote:`earned twelve sous a day`},{id:`farthing`,term:`farthing`,kind:`money`,chapter:48,text:`Hapgood's English for the liard, a tiny old copper coin worth a quarter of a sou. Half a farthing's worth of millet every two days is the cost of keeping a bird, and Fantine gives it up.`,quote:`half a farthing’s worth of millet`},{id:`napoleon`,term:`napoleon`,kind:`money`,chapter:49,text:`The gold twenty-franc piece first struck under Napoleon and still called by his name. Two napoleons make forty francs, the sum the Thénardiers demand and the dentist offers for two teeth.`,quote:`That makes two napoleons!`},{id:`miliary-fever`,term:`miliary fever`,kind:`word`,chapter:49,text:`An old diagnosis for a feverish illness with a rash of tiny blisters like millet seeds, often fatal and much feared. Doctors of the time used it loosely; for the Thénardiers it is simply a frightening name.`,quote:`A miliary fever, they call it`},{id:`quack-dentist`,term:`quack dentist`,kind:`custom`,chapter:49,text:`Itinerant tooth-pullers worked fairs and market squares from a showy cart, selling elixirs and extracting teeth in public. Real human teeth were bought from the poor to make dentures for the rich, which is why he will pay for healthy front teeth.`,quote:`He was a quack dentist on his rounds`},{id:`woman-of-the-town`,term:`woman of the town`,kind:`word`,chapter:49,text:`A euphemism for a prostitute (fille publique). Prostitution was legal but regulated: women were registered with the police and subject to their discretion. Hugo's next chapter calls it a form of slavery.`,quote:`The unfortunate girl became a woman of the town.`},{id:`christus-nos-liberavit`,term:`Christus Nos Liberavit`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:50,text:`Latin, 'Christ has set us free', echoing Galatians 5:1. Hugo gives the title to his short sermon on prostitution as the slavery Christian Europe has not abolished.`},{id:`dandies`,term:`dandies`,kind:`custom`,chapter:51,text:`Hugo's portrait of the provincial idler of 1823: three waistcoats, a short olive coat with a 'codfish tail', spurs without a horse and a fierce moustache. Pont-à-Mousson, a small town in Lorraine, stands for the provinces copying Paris copying London.`,quote:`They are dandies`},{id:`morillos-and-bolivars`,term:`morillos and bolivars`,kind:`history`,chapter:51,text:`Hats named after the two sides in the Spanish American wars of independence: Morillo was the royalist Spanish general, Bolívar the liberator. In France the narrow-brimmed 'morillo' marked a royalist, the broad 'bolivar' a liberal.`,quote:`and were called morillos`},{id:`right-thinker`,term:`right thinker`,kind:`word`,chapter:51,text:`Hapgood's rendering of 'bien pensant': conventionally right-minded, loyal to throne and altar. Hugo uses it with sarcasm for the respectable man who torments Fantine.`,quote:`a “right thinker,”`},{id:`under-the-rods`,term:`under the rods`,kind:`custom`,chapter:51,text:`Running the gauntlet: a soldier condemned to pass between two lines of comrades who beat him with rods, a punishment of the old armies. Fantine's silent circuit past the dandy is that ordeal repeated every five minutes.`,quote:`like the condemned soldier who returns under the rods`},{id:`elector`,term:`elector`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`Under the Restoration only men paying at least 300 francs a year in direct taxes could vote, under a hundred thousand in all France. To Javert, M. Bamatabois as 'a freeholder and an elector' is society itself.`,quote:`in the person of a freeholder and an elector`,seeAlso:[203]},{id:`the-code-of-criminal-examination`,term:`the code of criminal examination`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`The Code d'instruction criminelle of 1808, Napoleon's code of criminal procedure. Madeleine cites its articles, and a law of December 1799 against arbitrary detention, to show that minor street offences belong to the mayor's municipal police, so the decision is his, not the inspector's.`,quote:`of the code of criminal examination`},{id:`like-a-russian-soldier`,term:`like a Russian soldier`,kind:`reference`,chapter:52,text:`Russian infantry were famous in the Napoleonic wars for standing and taking fire without flinching or breaking. Javert absorbs the mayor's order the same way.`,quote:`like a Russian soldier`},{id:`laennec`,term:`Laënnec`,kind:`history`,chapter:53,text:`René Laënnec (1781–1826), the French physician who invented the stethoscope in 1816 and described the signs of tuberculosis of the lungs, then called consumption or phthisis. Sounding a patient's chest was new medicine in 1823.`,quote:`the fine Laënnec’s fine suggestions`},{id:`wise-virgins-and-foolish-virgins`,term:`wise virgins and foolish virgins`,kind:`reference`,chapter:53,text:`Christ's parable in Matthew 25 of the ten bridesmaids, five who kept their lamps filled and five who did not. Medieval sculptors carved them on cathedral portals, as at Reims. The 'ambubajae' were Syrian flute girls of ancient Rome, a byword for loose women.`,quote:`the wise virgins as they survey the foolish virgins`},{id:`prefecture-of-police`,term:`Prefecture of Police`,kind:`place`,chapter:54,text:`The headquarters of the Paris police, under the prefect of police, which also kept the national registers of convicts and wanted men. A provincial inspector's report on a suspect went there.`,quote:`At the Prefecture of Police in Paris`},{id:`adjutant-guard`,term:`adjutant-guard`,kind:`title`,chapter:54,text:`An assistant warder (adjudant) in the convict prison, a junior officer over the galley-sergeants. It means Javert knew the convicts of Toulon by sight, year after year.`,quote:`when I was adjutant-guard of convicts at Toulon`},{id:`arras`,term:`Arras`,kind:`place`,chapter:54,text:`The chief town of the Pas-de-Calais, about twenty leagues east of Montreuil-sur-Mer, seat of the département's prefecture, its prison and its Court of Assizes. Robespierre came from there.`,quote:`where the departmental prison is situated`,seeAlso:[61]},{id:`fagot`,term:`fagot`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:54,text:`Convict slang for a convict or ex-convict, from the bundles of sticks that were tied together as the chained men were. Brevet, a former convict made turnkey, uses the galleys' own word.`,quote:`He is a fagot!`},{id:`examining-magistrate`,term:`examining magistrate`,kind:`title`,chapter:54,text:`The juge d'instruction, the judge who investigates a criminal case before trial, questions the accused and witnesses, and decides whether to send it to court. He arranges the confrontations with men who claim to recognise the prisoner.`,quote:`the examining magistrate finds it convenient`},{id:`previous-conviction`,term:`previous conviction`,kind:`custom`,chapter:54,text:`French law punished a repeat offender far more heavily than a first offender. A theft of apples that would bring a few days in prison would, from a released convict, be housebreaking before the Assizes, punishable by the galleys for life.`,quote:`he has his previous conviction against him`},{id:`auvergne`,term:`Auvergne`,kind:`place`,chapter:54,text:`The mountainous province of central France, whose people were known for migrating to Paris as water-carriers and labourers, and for an accent that turns 'Jean' into 'Chan'. Javert's theory of the name 'Champmathieu' depends on it.`,quote:`The local pronunciation turns Jean into Chan`},{id:`capuchin-ursuline-carmelite`,term:`Capuchin, Ursuline, Carmelite`,kind:`reference`,chapter:55,text:`Religious orders: Capuchins were barefoot, bearded Franciscan friars; Ursulines teaching nuns; Carmelites strictly enclosed contemplative nuns. Hugo lists them to say that the cloister easily absorbs a peasant's piety.`,quote:`a Capuchin or an Ursuline`},{id:`the-abbe-sicard-and-massieu`,term:`the Abbé Sicard and Massieu`,kind:`reference`,chapter:55,text:`Abbé Sicard (1742–1822) directed the Paris institute for the deaf; Jean Massieu was his famous deaf pupil and assistant. Hugo's mention of a letter praising Sister Simplice is a piece of invented authority, of the kind he enjoys.`,quote:`The Abbé Sicard speaks of Sister Simplice`},{id:`simplice-of-sicily`,term:`Simplice of Sicily`,kind:`reference`,chapter:55,text:`The saint Hugo describes, martyred rather than tell a small lie about her birthplace, is not in the calendar of saints; he invented or adapted her to fit Sister Simplice's one rule, never to lie.`,quote:`Simplice of Sicily, as we know`},{id:`fleming`,term:`Fleming`,kind:`word`,chapter:56,text:`A Flemish-speaking native of Flanders, the region straddling northern France and Belgium. Montreuil-sur-Mer is near enough for a Flemish horse-hirer; his Dutch-sounding name Scaufflaer is Frenchified as Scaufflaire.`,quote:`to a Fleming named Master Scaufflaer`},{id:`cabriolet-and-tilbury`,term:`cabriolet and tilbury`,kind:`word`,chapter:56,text:`Light two-wheeled carriages pulled by one horse. A cabriolet had a folding hood; a tilbury, named after its London maker, was lighter and faster still but open to the weather. In winter that is a real hardship.`,quote:`a little tilbury that I own`},{id:`posting-relays`,term:`posting relays`,kind:`custom`,chapter:56,text:`Main roads were divided into stages between post-houses, where travellers could change horses. Distances were reckoned in leagues between relays: here five to Hesdin, six to Saint-Pol, eight and a half to Arras.`,quote:`That must designate the posting relays`},{id:`hesdin-and-saint-pol`,term:`Hesdin and Saint-Pol`,kind:`place`,chapter:56,text:`Small towns of the Pas-de-Calais on the road east from Montreuil-sur-Mer toward Arras. Hesdin was a fortified town on the Canche; Saint-Pol-sur-Ternoise a market town on the plateau.`,quote:`It is five leagues from here to Hesdin`},{id:`alighieri`,term:`Alighieri`,kind:`reference`,chapter:57,text:`Dante Alighieri, whose Inferno begins with the poet lost in a dark wood and brought to the gate of Hell. Hugo opens his chapter on a single night of conscience by setting it beside Homer's battles, Milton's demons and Dante's circles.`,quote:`Alighieri one day met with a sinister-looking door`},{id:`the-elections`,term:`the elections`,kind:`history`,chapter:57,text:`Restoration elections were held by small colleges of wealthy voters who travelled to the chief town of the arrondissement or département to vote, often with a passport for the journey. That the mayor went 'to the elections' shows he counted among the few with the vote.`,quote:`when he went to the elections`},{id:`think-march-on`,term:`Think! … March on!`,kind:`reference`,chapter:57,text:`The legend of the Wandering Jew, Ahasuerus, who mocked Christ on the way to Calvary and was condemned to walk the earth until the Second Coming, driven on by the command 'Walk!' Hugo likens the inner voice that will not let his man stop thinking to that curse.`,quote:`as it said to another condemned man, two thousand years ago, “March on!”`},{id:`the-iron-necklet-the-green-cap`,term:`the iron necklet, the green cap`,kind:`lore`,chapter:57,text:`Galley dress distinguished terms of sentence: a red cap for a fixed term, a green cap for life. The iron collar (carcan) and the 'post' were the public exposure in the pillory that preceded a convict's departure for the galleys, abolished in 1832.`,quote:`the iron necklet, the green cap`,seeAlso:[64]},{id:`penal-servitude-for-life`,term:`penal servitude for life`,kind:`history`,chapter:57,text:`Travaux forcés à perpétuité, hard labour for life in the bagne. Under the Penal Code a man already sentenced to hard labour who committed a new crime could receive it, which is why a second theft by an ex-convict, however small, is so dangerous.`,quote:`penal servitude for life`,seeAlso:[63]},{id:`romainville`,term:`Romainville`,kind:`place`,chapter:57,text:`A village with a wood on the heights east of Paris, a favourite Sunday spot for lovers gathering lilac in spring. The name surfaces in the man's exhausted mind from a clock-maker's name and an old song, the sort of stray memory a mind seizes on in extremity.`,quote:`Romainville was a little grove near Paris`},{id:`the-terrible-cup`,term:`the terrible cup`,kind:`reference`,chapter:57,text:`Christ's agony in the garden of Gethsemane among the olive trees, where he prayed that 'this cup' of suffering might pass from him (Matthew 26:39). Hugo ends the chapter by setting the night's struggle against that one.`,quote:`the terrible cup which appeared to Him`},{id:`papers-in-his-own-handwriting`,term:`papers in his own handwriting`,kind:`lore`,chapter:58,text:`Hugo's narrator presents himself as a historian working from documents, here a written account of a dream left by the man himself. It is a novelist's device for authority and intimacy, like his references to witnesses still alive.`,quote:`It is one of the papers in his own handwriting`},{id:`mail-wagons`,term:`mail-wagons`,kind:`custom`,chapter:59,text:`The malle-poste, the light, fast government coach that carried the mails at night between towns, with one or two seats for passengers. Hugo describes the old two-wheeled Empire model, yellow with a black mail-box behind.`,quote:`small mail-wagons of the time of the Empire`},{id:`calash`,term:`calash`,kind:`word`,chapter:59,text:`A calèche, a low four-wheeled carriage with a folding hood, needing two horses. A wheelwright in a poor town is keeping one for a townsman who uses it 'on the thirty-sixth of the month', a French joke for never.`,quote:`an old calash under the shed yonder`},{id:`post-horses`,term:`post-horses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:59,text:`Fresh horses hired at each posting station along a main road, with a postilion to bring them back. They were the fastest way to travel, but on a crossroad in ploughing season the relays might have no horses to give, and a passport had to be shown.`,quote:`I will take two post-horses`},{id:`postilion`,term:`postilion`,kind:`word`,chapter:59,text:`The rider who sits on one of the horses, or here on the shaft, to drive and guide, then takes the horses back to their relay. The stable-boy calls himself one to sound grander.`,quote:`who called himself a postilion`},{id:`whiffletree`,term:`whiffletree`,kind:`word`,chapter:59,text:`The swinging crossbar to which a horse's traces are hitched, so that its pull reaches the cart evenly. If it breaks, the horse cannot draw at all; a cut branch can stand in for it.`,quote:`the whiffletree broke`},{id:`tinques-and-mont-saint-eloy`,term:`Tinques and Mont-Saint-Éloy`,kind:`place`,chapter:59,text:`Villages of the Artois plain on the way to Arras. Mont-Saint-Éloy is marked by the ruined towers of its old abbey on a hill north-west of the city. The detour by Carency and Camblin is through real country lanes.`,quote:`towards Tinques, which is only five leagues from Arras`},{id:`tester`,term:`tester`,kind:`word`,chapter:60,text:`The canopy over a curtained bed. Hospital beds, like domestic ones, were often hung with curtains for warmth and privacy, which is why Fantine has to push her head through them to listen.`,quote:`staring at the tester of her bed`},{id:`chinchona`,term:`chinchona`,kind:`word`,chapter:60,text:`Cinchona or Peruvian bark, the source of quinine, the standard remedy for fevers. An infusion of it was among the few things a doctor of 1823 could prescribe for a consumptive's fever.`,quote:`an infusion of pure chinchona`},{id:`the-cradle-song`,term:`the cradle song`,kind:`reference`,chapter:60,text:`Fantine's lullaby, with its refrain of pink roses and blue cornflowers and the Virgin bringing linen for a baby, is Hugo's own pastiche of an old French folk romance. Its last turn, from baby clothes to a winding-sheet, is in the folk tradition.`,quote:`This song was an old cradle romance`},{id:`porte-cochere`,term:`porte-cochère`,kind:`word`,chapter:61,text:`A carriage gateway: a large door opening from the street into an inner courtyard, wide enough for a coach. Inns and town houses had them.`,quote:`entered the porte-cochère of the Hotel de la Poste`,seeAlso:[196]},{id:`return-horse`,term:`return horse`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:61,text:`Cheval de retour, slang for a convict who has come back, a repeat offender. It was originally a post-horse being taken back to its home stable. The lawyers in the hall use it as casually as 'old lag'.`,quote:`the “return horse.”`},{id:`the-limosin-woman`,term:`the Limosin woman`,kind:`custom`,chapter:61,text:`A woman from the Limousin, in central France. Infanticide by poor unmarried mothers was one of the commonest capital cases at the Assizes; juries often refused to find premeditation, which brought the sentence down from death to hard labour for life.`,quote:`The Limosin woman`},{id:`the-prefecture`,term:`the prefecture`,kind:`place`,chapter:61,text:`The prefect's official residence and offices. At Arras it occupied the former bishop's palace, confiscated in the Revolution, and while the courthouse was repaired the Assizes sat in its great hall, under the old episcopal ceilings.`,quote:`the prefecture of today was the bishop’s palace before the Revolution`},{id:`president`,term:`President`,kind:`title`,chapter:61,text:`The presiding judge of the Court of Assizes, a councillor of the Royal Court (the appeal court) sent to preside over the session. He questioned the accused and witnesses himself and summed up for the jury; places behind his chair were for privileged spectators.`,quote:`behind Monsieur le President`},{id:`usher`,term:`usher`,kind:`title`,chapter:61,text:`The huissier audiencier, the court officer who kept the doors, called witnesses and carried messages to the bench. His sudden deference shows what a mayor's name is worth.`,quote:`An usher stood at the door`},{id:`royal-court-of-douai`,term:`Royal Court of Douai`,kind:`history`,chapter:62,text:`The cour royale was the regional appeal court; Douai's covered the Nord and the Pas-de-Calais. One of its councillors presided over each quarterly session of the Assizes in the département.`,quote:`The Councillor of the Royal Court of Douai`},{id:`communes`,term:`communes`,kind:`word`,chapter:62,text:`The commune is the basic unit of French local government, a town or village with its own mayor. The arrondissement of Montreuil-sur-Mer had about a hundred and forty of them.`,quote:`the hundred and forty communes`},{id:`pache-year-ii`,term:`Pache, year II`,kind:`history`,chapter:62,text:`Jean-Nicolas Pache was minister of war and then mayor of Paris during the Terror. The framed letter, dated in the revolutionary calendar, is about the arrest of deputies in 1793: a relic of the Revolution's own justice hanging in the judges' room.`,quote:`Jean Nicolas Pache, mayor of Paris`},{id:`article-383-of-the-penal-code`,term:`article 383 of the Penal Code`,kind:`history`,chapter:63,text:`The article of the 1810 Penal Code that punished theft on a public highway with hard labour for life, and with death in aggravated cases. That is why the prosecutor 'reserves' the earlier robbery of the boy for a later trial.`,quote:`article 383 of the Penal Code`},{id:`romorantin-or-montbrison`,term:`Romorantin or Montbrison`,kind:`place`,chapter:63,text:`Two small, sleepy provincial towns, used here as Hugo uses Pont-à-Mousson: anywhere far from Paris. He mocks the stately periphrases of courtroom oratory, where a theatre is 'the temple of Melpomene', the Muse of tragedy.`,quote:`at Romorantin or at Montbrison`},{id:`bossuet-s-chicken`,term:`Bossuet’s chicken`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`In his funeral oration for Anne of Gonzaga, the great preacher Bossuet had to describe a dream in which a hen protected her chicks, and did it in grand style. Hugo cites it to excuse the defence lawyer's lofty treatment of stolen apples.`,quote:`Bénigne Bossuet himself was obliged to allude to a chicken`},{id:`the-satanic-school`,term:`The Satanic School`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`The poet Robert Southey's sneer (1821) at Byron and Shelley, taken up by French conservative critics against the young Romantics, Hugo among them. The Quotidienne and the Oriflamme were royalist papers. The prosecutor blames the crime on Romantic literature, a fashionable charge.`,quote:`The Satanic School`},{id:`the-tale-of-theramene`,term:`the tale of Théramène`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`In Racine's tragedy 'Phèdre', Théramène delivers a long, famous speech describing the sea monster that kills Hippolyte. Its set-piece horror, Hugo says, is the model for prosecutors' portraits of the accused as monsters.`,quote:`the tale of Théramène`},{id:`you-cannot-take-an-oath`,term:`you cannot take an oath`,kind:`custom`,chapter:64,text:`A man sentenced to an 'ignominious' penalty, such as hard labour, lost his civil rights, including the right to testify under oath. Convicts could be heard only 'for information', which is why the President appeals to their conscience instead.`,quote:`you cannot take an oath`},{id:`central-prisons`,term:`central prisons`,kind:`history`,chapter:64,text:`The maisons centrales, large prisons created under Napoleon for men serving long terms of 'reclusion' rather than hard labour, with their own grey-and-black uniform. Their workshops produced goods under contract, the 'prison work' that undercut seamstresses' wages.`,quote:`the black and gray waistcoat of the central prisons`},{id:`laundress-at-the-river`,term:`laundress at the river`,kind:`custom`,chapter:64,text:`Paris washerwomen worked on floating laundry-boats on the Seine or in covered washhouses like that of the Enfants-Rouges, standing in tubs or bending over running water all day in every season. It was one of the hardest and worst-paid trades for women.`,quote:`my daughter, who was a laundress at the river`},{id:`shrove-tuesday`,term:`Shrove-Tuesday`,kind:`custom`,chapter:64,text:`Mardi Gras, the last day of Carnival before Lent, when Paris went masked to balls and street festivities. A working girl who went to bed at eight that night was very sober indeed.`,quote:`one Shrove-Tuesday`},{id:`t-f-p`,term:`T. F. P.`,kind:`lore`,chapter:65,text:`Travaux Forcés à Perpétuité, hard labour for life. Until 1832 convicts were branded on the shoulder with a hot iron bearing the letters of their sentence, and some tried to burn the mark away. The brand stayed for life.`,quote:`the three letters T. F. P.`},{id:`nearly-forty-years-ago`,term:`nearly forty years ago`,kind:`lore`,chapter:65,text:`Hugo, writing around 1860, claims that the speech was written down by a witness and still rings in the ears of those who heard it. The narrator's pose as a chronicler of real events with surviving witnesses gives the scene the weight of testimony.`,quote:`nearly forty years ago`},{id:`the-little-glass`,term:`the little glass`,kind:`custom`,chapter:66,text:`Doctors held a small mirror to a patient's lips to see whether it misted, the simplest test of whether breath, and so life, remained. It is the only looking-glass in the sisters' infirmary.`,quote:`the little glass which the doctor of the infirmary used to see whether a patient was dead`},{id:`first-communion`,term:`first communion`,kind:`custom`,chapter:67,text:`French Catholic children made their first communion at about twelve, after catechism classes, girls dressed in white with a veil like little brides. It was a great family occasion and a mark of respectability. Fantine counts the years until her daughter's.`,quote:`Then she will take her first communion`},{id:`epiphonemas`,term:`epiphonemas`,kind:`word`,chapter:68,text:`An epiphonema is a rhetorical flourish, a sententious exclamation that rounds off an argument. Hugo means that the defence lawyer closed with stock lines about judicial error.`,quote:`some epiphonemas, not very fresh`},{id:`the-emperor-and-not-bonaparte`,term:`the Emperor, and not Bonaparte`,kind:`history`,chapter:68,text:`Under the Restoration, calling Napoleon 'the Emperor' implied that his reign had been legitimate; loyal royalists said 'Bonaparte' or 'the usurper'. A single word in a speech could betray a man's politics, and here it shocks the royalist President.`,quote:`say the Emperor, and not Bonaparte`},{id:`leather-stock`,term:`leather stock`,kind:`word`,chapter:68,text:`A stiff band of leather or cloth worn round the neck in place of a cravat, buckled at the back, part of military and police dress. A buckle slipped round under the ear is, for a man as exact as Javert, a sign of inner upheaval.`,quote:`The buckle of his leather stock was under his left ear`},{id:`saint-michael`,term:`Saint Michael`,kind:`reference`,chapter:68,text:`The archangel who leads the armies of heaven and is shown with his foot on the dragon, sword raised, weighing souls. Hugo's 'monstrous Saint Michael' is righteousness without mercy.`,quote:`this monstrous Saint Michael`},{id:`thumbscrews`,term:`thumbscrews`,kind:`word`,chapter:69,text:`The poucettes were small screw-clamps fastened on a prisoner's thumbs to control him under arrest, a police tool rather than a torture here. Javert threatens them to hurry his man away.`,quote:`or you’ll get the thumbscrews!`},{id:`drapeau-blanc`,term:`Drapeau Blanc`,kind:`history`,chapter:70,text:`'The White Flag', an Ultra-royalist newspaper of the Restoration, named after the Bourbon flag. Its old lady subscriber turns the mayor's fall into a lesson for Bonapartists, though nothing connects them.`,quote:`a subscriber to the Drapeau Blanc`},{id:`blouse`,term:`blouse`,kind:`word`,chapter:70,text:`The loose smock of blue or grey linen worn over other clothes by French workmen, carters and peasants. It was the uniform of the labouring class, and a perfect disguise for a man leaving a town on foot.`,quote:`he was dressed in a blouse`},{id:`the-public-grave`,term:`the public grave`,kind:`custom`,chapter:70,text:`The fosse commune, a shared trench in the cemetery where the poor were buried free, without a marked plot, the bodies laid side by side and the ground reused after some years. A paid concession was needed for a grave of one's own.`,quote:`She was thrown into the public grave.`},{id:`last-year-1861`,term:`Last year (1861)`,kind:`lore`,chapter:71,text:`The narrator steps out of the story as himself: Hugo really did visit the battlefield in May 1861, staying at Mont-Saint-Jean while he finished the novel. Volume II opens with a traveller's walk, not with the characters.`,quote:`Last year (1861), on a beautiful May morning`},{id:`nivelles-and-braine-l-alleud`,term:`Nivelles and Braine-l’Alleud`,kind:`place`,chapter:71,text:`Towns of Belgian Brabant just south of Brussels, at the western edge of the battlefield. In 1815 Belgium was part of the new Kingdom of the Netherlands; the field lies along the roads from Nivelles and from Genappe to Brussels.`,quote:`was coming from Nivelles`},{id:`hougomont`,term:`Hougomont`,kind:`place`,chapter:71,text:`A walled château-farm with a garden and orchard on the western side of the field of Waterloo. The French attacked it all day and never took it from its British Guards and German defenders. Hugo walks into it as a working farm, still scarred by shot.`,quote:`“Hougomont,” said the peasant woman.`,seeAlso:[72,75]},{id:`the-lion`,term:`the lion`,kind:`place`,chapter:71,text:`The Lion's Mound, an artificial hill about forty metres high topped by a cast-iron lion, raised in 1826 by the Dutch king where his son the Prince of Orange was wounded. Building it reshaped the ground, as Hugo complains: the field is no longer as it was.`,quote:`something which at that distance resembled a lion`,seeAlso:[74,78,86]},{id:`the-village-of-waterloo`,term:`the village of Waterloo`,kind:`place`,chapter:71,text:`A village about fifteen kilometres south of Brussels, where Wellington had his headquarters and dated his dispatch. The fighting took place a few kilometres further south, around Mont-Saint-Jean, but the battle took the village's name.`,quote:`He was on the battlefield of Waterloo.`,seeAlso:[89]},{id:`cooke-s-four-companies-of-guards`,term:`Cooke’s four companies of guards`,kind:`history`,chapter:72,text:`Light companies of the British Foot Guards under General Cooke, who held Hougomont with Nassau and Hanoverian troops against repeated French assaults. The defence of its north gate, forced and then shut again, became a British legend.`,quote:`Cooke’s four companies of guards`},{id:`jerome`,term:`Jérôme`,kind:`history`,chapter:72,text:`Jérôme Bonaparte, the Emperor's youngest brother and former King of Westphalia, who commanded a division in Reille's corps. His attack on Hougomont, meant as a diversion, swallowed thousands of men. Foy, Bachelu, Guilleminot and Kellermann were French generals in the same sector.`,quote:`Napoleon sent his brother Jérôme against Hougomont`},{id:`hanoverian-nassau-brunswick`,term:`Hanoverian, Nassau, Brunswick`,kind:`history`,chapter:72,text:`Wellington's army was barely a third British: the rest were Dutch and Belgian troops and contingents from German states, Hanover (ruled by the British king), the duchy of Nassau and the duchy of Brunswick, whose black-uniformed soldiers had lost their duke two days earlier.`,quote:`the Hanoverian lieutenant, Wilda`},{id:`biscayan`,term:`biscayan`,kind:`word`,chapter:72,text:`A heavy musket ball, or a large lead ball fired from a cannon in a case of grapeshot, named after the long Biscay muskets. Grapeshot and canister were clusters of small balls that scattered from the gun's mouth like a giant shotgun.`,quote:`its bullet or its biscayan`},{id:`lenotre`,term:`Lenôtre`,kind:`reference`,chapter:72,text:`André Le Nôtre (1613–1700), the gardener of Versailles, master of the formal French garden. Hougomont's terraced garden is in the older style before him, and in 1861 ruins and briars.`,quote:`the first French style which preceded Lenôtre`},{id:`the-edict-of-nantes`,term:`the Edict of Nantes`,kind:`history`,chapter:72,text:`Henri IV's edict of 1598 granting French Protestants freedom of worship. Louis XIV revoked it in 1685 and hundreds of thousands of Huguenots fled abroad, which is how the German general Duplat came to have a French name.`,quote:`the revocation of the Edict of Nantes`},{id:`blucher`,term:`Blücher`,kind:`history`,chapter:73,text:`Gebhard von Blücher (1742–1819), the seventy-two-year-old Prussian field marshal, beaten by Napoleon at Ligny two days before Waterloo but able to march to Wellington's aid. Hugo makes the late start of the battle, caused by the rain, the reason he arrived in time.`,quote:`that gave Blücher time to come up`},{id:`austerlitz`,term:`Austerlitz`,kind:`history`,chapter:73,text:`Napoleon's greatest victory, over the Austrians and Russians on 2 December 1805, where the morning 'sun of Austerlitz' broke through the fog. Hugo uses it as Waterloo's opposite: the beginning and the end of the Empire's glory.`,quote:`make Waterloo the end of Austerlitz`},{id:`napoleon-was-an-artillery-officer`,term:`Napoleon was an artillery officer`,kind:`history`,chapter:73,text:`Napoleon trained as a gunner and first made his name directing the guns at the siege of Toulon in 1793. Massed artillery was the key to his battles, so ground too wet for the guns to move was a serious handicap.`,quote:`Napoleon was an artillery officer`},{id:`hal-and-tongres`,term:`Hal and Tongres`,kind:`place`,chapter:73,text:`Towns west and east of Brussels, the directions of the British and Prussian lines of retreat. Napoleon's plan was to split the two allied armies and drive them apart toward their own bases.`,quote:`to drive the British half back on Hal`},{id:`mont-saint-jean`,term:`Mont-Saint-Jean`,kind:`place`,chapter:74,text:`A hamlet and farm on the Brussels road where the ground rises to a plateau. Wellington's army stood along its crest, partly hidden behind it, which is why the French called the battle after it. Hugo's capital A puts it at the apex.`,quote:`The top of the A is Mont-Saint-Jean`},{id:`la-belle-alliance`,term:`La Belle-Alliance`,kind:`place`,chapter:74,text:`An inn on the Brussels road south of the plateau, near Napoleon's position. Its name, 'the fine alliance', struck Hugo as an omen, since Wellington and Blücher met there after the battle; the Prussians wanted the battle named after it.`,quote:`the right tip is the Belle-Alliance, where Napoleon was`},{id:`the-hollow-road-to-ohain`,term:`the hollow road to Ohain`,kind:`place`,chapter:74,text:`A sunken lane running along the crest of the plateau from Braine-l'Alleud to Ohain, cut deep between banks, invisible from the French side. The building of the Lion's Mound later used its banks and levelled it. Hugo describes it with dread.`,quote:`the hollow road to Ohain from Braine-l’Alleud`,seeAlso:[78,79,89]},{id:`the-forest-of-soignes`,term:`the forest of Soignes`,kind:`place`,chapter:74,text:`The great beech forest that runs between the battlefield and Brussels. It lay at Wellington's back, so a defeat would have meant a disorderly retreat through its tracks and ponds.`,quote:`is the forest of Soignes`},{id:`brienne`,term:`Brienne`,kind:`history`,chapter:74,text:`The military school at Brienne-le-Château in Champagne where the young Bonaparte studied from 1779 to 1784. Hugo dresses the Emperor in his famous costume: little cocked hat, green chasseur's coat, the sword he carried at Marengo.`,quote:`the little three-cornered hat of the school of Brienne`},{id:`picton-and-hill`,term:`Picton and Hill`,kind:`history`,chapter:74,text:`Sir Thomas Picton and Lord Hill were Wellington's veteran generals from the Spanish campaigns; Picton was killed in the battle. D'Erlon and Reille were the French corps commanders facing them.`,quote:`d’Erlon facing Picton, Reille facing Hill`},{id:`babylon-violated-lessens-alexander`,term:`Babylon violated lessens Alexander`,kind:`reference`,chapter:74,text:`Hugo lists conquerors whose glory is diminished by what they did: Alexander the Great at Babylon, Caesar enslaving Rome, Titus destroying Jerusalem in AD 70. History, he says, sets the despot against the hero in the same man.`,quote:`Babylon violated lessens Alexander`},{id:`la-haie-sainte`,term:`La Haie-Sainte`,kind:`place`,chapter:75,text:`A walled farm in front of the centre of Wellington's line, on the Brussels road, held by the King's German Legion until they ran out of ammunition and it fell in the late afternoon. It was the key to the British centre.`,quote:`hurling Quiot’s brigade on La Haie-Sainte`,seeAlso:[76]},{id:`ney`,term:`Ney`,kind:`history`,chapter:75,text:`Michel Ney (1769–1815), marshal of France, 'the bravest of the brave', who directed the French attacks at Waterloo. He had promised Louis XVIII to bring Napoleon back 'in an iron cage' and then went over to him in March 1815.`,quote:`Ney pushed forward the right wing`,seeAlso:[79,82]},{id:`quid-obscurum-quid-divinum`,term:`Quid obscurum, quid divinum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:75,text:`Latin, 'something obscure, something divine', Hugo's tag for the element in a battle that escapes plans and maps. The chapter's title calls it the 'quid obscurum' of battles.`,quote:`Quid obscurum, quid divinum.`},{id:`salvator-rosa-and-gribeauval`,term:`Salvator Rosa and Gribeauval`,kind:`reference`,chapter:75,text:`Salvator Rosa was a seventeenth-century painter of wild battle scenes; Gribeauval the eighteenth-century engineer who standardised French artillery. Rembrandt and Van der Meulen, Folard and Polybius are paired the same way: the painter of chaos against the exact recorder.`,quote:`what Salvator Rosa requires, not what is suited to the needs of Gribeauval`},{id:`scotch-grays`,term:`Scotch Grays`,kind:`history`,chapter:76,text:`The Royal Scots Greys, a heavy cavalry regiment on grey horses, part of Ponsonby's Union Brigade. Their charge broke d'Erlon's infantry early in the afternoon, then ran on too far and was cut up by French lancers and cuirassiers.`,quote:`The Scotch Grays no longer existed`},{id:`prince-of-orange`,term:`Prince of Orange`,kind:`history`,chapter:76,text:`William, heir to the Dutch throne, aged twenty-two, who commanded Wellington's Dutch-Belgian corps and was wounded in the battle. The Lion's Mound marks the spot.`,quote:`The Prince of Orange was in command of the centre`},{id:`talavera-vittoria-salamanca`,term:`Talavera, Vittoria, Salamanca`,kind:`history`,chapter:76,text:`Wellington's victories over the French in Spain during the Peninsular War (1808–1814). His 'old companions' were the veterans of those campaigns.`,quote:`his old companions of Talavera, of Vittoria, of Salamanca`},{id:`ridet-caesar-pompeius-flebit`,term:`Ridet Caesar, Pompeius flebit`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:77,text:`Latin, 'Caesar laughs, Pompey will weep', a soldiers' saying before Pharsalus, where Caesar beat Pompey. Hugo applies it to Napoleon's high spirits on the morning of Waterloo, then reverses it.`,quote:`Ridet Caesar, Pompeius flebit`},{id:`the-duchess-of-richmond-s-ball`,term:`the Duchess of Richmond’s ball`,kind:`history`,chapter:77,text:`The famous ball in Brussels on the night of 15 June 1815, where Wellington and his officers learned that Napoleon had crossed the frontier; many went straight from the dance to battle. Soult's joke at breakfast turns on it.`,quote:`at the Duchess of Richmond’s`},{id:`soult`,term:`Soult`,kind:`history`,chapter:77,text:`Nicolas Soult, marshal of France and Duke of Dalmatia, Napoleon's chief of staff in 1815. He had fought Wellington for years in Spain and warned the Emperor not to underrate him.`,quote:`saying to Soult as he did so`},{id:`grumblers`,term:`grumblers`,kind:`history`,chapter:77,text:`The grognards, Napoleon's nickname for the veteran grenadiers of the Old Guard, who grumbled on the march but followed him anywhere. It became the name for any old soldier of the Empire.`,quote:`his grenadiers “his grumblers;”`},{id:`elba`,term:`Elba`,kind:`place`,chapter:77,text:`The small Mediterranean island off Tuscany given to Napoleon as a sovereign principality after his first abdication in 1814. He escaped from it in February 1815 on the brig L'Inconstant and landed in France on 1 March.`,quote:`trip from the island of Elba to France`},{id:`scabra-rubigine`,term:`Scabra rubigine`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:77,text:`Latin, 'with rough rust', from Virgil's Georgics, where the farmer of the future ploughs up rusted spears and helmets on an old battlefield. Hugo, turning up shell fragments on the knoll, is that farmer.`,quote:`Scabra rubigine`},{id:`grouchy`,term:`Grouchy`,kind:`history`,chapter:78,text:`Emmanuel de Grouchy, the marshal sent with some 30,000 men to pursue the Prussians after Ligny. He followed his orders too literally, ignored the sound of the guns and never reached the field: the absent army of Waterloo.`,quote:`Grouchy’s delay`,seeAlso:[81]},{id:`beresina-leipzig-and-fontainebleau`,term:`Bérésina, Leipzig, and Fontainebleau`,kind:`history`,chapter:78,text:`The disasters of Napoleon's decline: the river crossing during the retreat from Moscow in 1812, the 'Battle of the Nations' lost in 1813, and the palace where he abdicated in April 1814. Crécy, Poitiers, Agincourt, Malplaquet and Ramillies are old English victories over France.`,quote:`when one has Bérésina, Leipzig, and Fontainebleau behind one`},{id:`marengo`,term:`Marengo`,kind:`history`,chapter:78,text:`Napoleon's victory over the Austrians in northern Italy in June 1800, which secured his power as First Consul. 'The man of Marengo' is the young Bonaparte of the glory years.`,quote:`The man of Marengo was wiping out Agincourt`},{id:`cuirassiers`,term:`cuirassiers`,kind:`title`,chapter:78,text:`Heavy cavalry who wore a steel breastplate (cuirass) and helmet, on big horses, used to break enemy lines by sheer weight. Milhaud's and Kellermann's cuirassiers made the great charges of the afternoon.`,quote:`He gave orders to Milhaud’s cuirassiers`,seeAlso:[79,169]},{id:`the-muskowa`,term:`the Muskowa`,kind:`history`,chapter:79,text:`The battle of Borodino, near Moscow, on 7 September 1812, which the French call the Moskova. The heavy cavalry took the great redoubt there, under Murat, Napoleon's flamboyant cavalry marshal and brother-in-law.`,quote:`the great redoubt of the Muskowa`},{id:`squares`,term:`squares`,kind:`custom`,chapter:79,text:`Against cavalry, infantry formed hollow squares several ranks deep, bayonets outward on every side, the front rank kneeling. Horses would not charge into a steady wall of bayonets, and the guns sheltered inside. Wellington's squares stood all afternoon.`,quote:`the English infantry, formed into thirteen squares`},{id:`vive-l-empereur`,term:`Vive l’Empereur!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:79,text:`'Long live the Emperor!', the battle-cry of Napoleon's armies. After 1815 shouting it in France was a 'seditious cry' that could bring a prison sentence.`,quote:`shouting, “Vive l’Empereur!”`},{id:`pibroch`,term:`pibroch`,kind:`word`,chapter:80,text:`Strictly the classical music of the Highland bagpipe, but here the pipes themselves. Scottish regiments marched and fought to them. 'Ben Lothian' is Hugo's invented Scottish mountain.`,quote:`with his pibroch under his arm`},{id:`the-iron-duke`,term:`the Iron Duke`,kind:`history`,chapter:80,text:`The Duke of Wellington (1769–1852), Arthur Wellesley, commander of the allied army. 'Blücher, or night!' is his famous prayer: either the Prussians arrive or darkness ends the fighting.`,quote:`The Iron Duke remained calm`},{id:`louis-xviii-at-ghent`,term:`Louis XVIII at Ghent`,kind:`history`,chapter:80,text:`During the Hundred Days the king and his court took refuge in Ghent, in Belgium, waiting on the outcome. The panic among fugitives from the battlefield reached them there; the Prince de Condé was at Mechlin.`,quote:`the Prince de Condé at Mechlin, and Louis XVIII at Ghent`},{id:`bulow`,term:`Bülow`,kind:`history`,chapter:81,text:`Friedrich Wilhelm von Bülow, commander of Blücher's IV Corps, the first Prussian troops to reach the field. His attack on Plancenoit, behind Napoleon's right, drew off the French reserves.`,quote:`Bülow, Blücher’s lieutenant`},{id:`plancenoit`,term:`Plancenoit`,kind:`place`,chapter:81,text:`A village behind the French right, where the arriving Prussians struck. It changed hands several times in savage fighting between Prussians and the Young Guard, and burned.`,quote:`instead of below Plancenoit`},{id:`the-guard`,term:`the Guard`,kind:`history`,chapter:82,text:`The Imperial Guard, Napoleon's elite reserve of veterans in tall bearskin caps, his last card at Waterloo. Its repulse on the slope of Mont-Saint-Jean in the evening, the first time it had been beaten, set off the French collapse.`,quote:`the Guard entering the line`},{id:`marshal-of-france`,term:`Marshal of France`,kind:`title`,chapter:82,text:`The highest military dignity in France, revived by Napoleon. Hugo's 'reserved for French bullets' refers to Ney's fate: tried by the Chamber of Peers and shot by a firing squad in Paris in December 1815 for rallying to Napoleon.`,quote:`how a Marshal of France dies on the field of battle!`},{id:`uhlans`,term:`Uhlans`,kind:`word`,chapter:83,text:`Lancers of the Prussian and other German armies. In the pursuit after the battle they, and the Prussian 'death's-head' hussars with skulls on their caps, gave no quarter.`,quote:`the swords of the Uhlans`},{id:`the-grand-army`,term:`the Grand Army`,kind:`history`,chapter:83,text:`La Grande Armée, the name of Napoleon's main army from 1805, which had marched from Madrid to Moscow. Hugo ends the rout with the name, to measure the fall.`,quote:`The Grand Army.`},{id:`hoc-erat-in-fatis`,term:`Hoc erat in fatis`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:83,text:`Latin, 'this was in the fates': it was destined. Hugo's view is that Waterloo was decided by Providence, not by generals.`,quote:`Hoc erat in fatis.`},{id:`ulm-wagram-jena-friedland`,term:`Ulm, Wagram, Jéna, Friedland`,kind:`history`,chapter:84,text:`Napoleon's victories of 1805–1809, over Austria (Ulm, Wagram), Prussia (Jena) and Russia (Friedland). The last squares of the Guard, Hugo says, carried those names down with them.`,quote:`Ulm, Wagram, Jéna, Friedland, died with them`},{id:`cambronne`,term:`Cambronne`,kind:`history`,chapter:84,text:`General Pierre Cambronne (1770–1842), who commanded a battalion of the Old Guard in the last squares. Called on to surrender, he is said to have answered either 'The Guard dies but does not surrender' or the single word Hugo prints. He was wounded, captured, and survived.`,quote:`an obscure officer named Cambronne`,seeAlso:[85]},{id:`merde`,term:`Merde!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:84,text:`'Shit!', the coarsest common French word, which Hugo was the first to print in a serious novel, and defends at length in the next chapter. French speakers still call it 'le mot de Cambronne', Cambronne's word.`,quote:`Cambronne replied, “Merde!”`},{id:`leonidas-with-rabelais`,term:`Leonidas with Rabelais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:85,text:`Leonidas was the Spartan king who died holding the pass of Thermopylae; Rabelais the earthy comic genius of the French Renaissance. To finish one with the other is Hugo's summary of Cambronne's answer: heroism and obscenity in one breath.`,quote:`to finish Leonidas with Rabellais`},{id:`rouget`,term:`Rouget`,kind:`reference`,chapter:85,text:`Claude-Joseph Rouget de Lisle, the army officer who wrote the 'Marseillaise' in a single night in 1792. Danton, the great orator of the Revolution, and Kléber, a republican general, are Hugo's other examples of that spirit.`,quote:`as Rouget invents the “Marseillaise,”`},{id:`charras`,term:`Charras`,kind:`reference`,chapter:86,text:`Jean-Baptiste Charras, a republican colonel exiled after Louis-Napoleon's coup of 1851, like Hugo, and author of a careful history of the 1815 campaign that Hugo used. Jomini and Müffling were earlier military historians of the battle.`,quote:`Charras alone`},{id:`quine`,term:`quine`,kind:`word`,chapter:86,text:`In the old French state lottery, a 'quine' was the jackpot won by drawing all five numbers. Waterloo, for Hugo, was a lucky draw: 'won by Europe, paid by France'.`,quote:`The quine won by Europe, paid by France.`},{id:`bareme`,term:`Barême`,kind:`reference`,chapter:86,text:`François Barrême, a seventeenth-century mathematician whose book of ready-reckoned tables gave French the word 'barème', a scale of figures. Calling Wellington the Barrême of war and Napoleon its Michelangelo sets calculation against genius.`,quote:`Wellington was the Barême of war`},{id:`lodi-montebello-montenotte-mantua-arcola`,term:`Lodi, Montebello, Montenotte, Mantua, Arcola`,kind:`history`,chapter:86,text:`Bonaparte's victories in his first Italian campaign of 1796–1797, when at twenty-six he beat a series of elderly Austrian generals: Beaulieu, Wurmser, Alvinczy. Hugo's quip is that Wellington is simply a Wurmser who won.`,quote:`beneath Lodi, Montebello, Montenotte, Mantua, Arcola`},{id:`the-20th-of-march`,term:`the 20th of March`,kind:`history`,chapter:87,text:`On 20 March 1815 Napoleon re-entered Paris and the Tuileries, the night after Louis XVIII fled. With the 14th of July 1789, the storming of the Bastille, it stands for Hugo as revolutionary France, which the allies at Waterloo were fighting.`,quote:`attacked through the 20th of March, 1815`},{id:`a-postilion-on-the-throne-of-naples`,term:`a postilion on the throne of Naples`,kind:`history`,chapter:87,text:`Napoleon made Joachim Murat, an innkeeper's son, King of Naples, and his marshal Bernadotte, once a sergeant, became Crown Prince and then King of Sweden. Hugo's point is that the Empire, despotic as it was, spread the Revolution's equality.`,quote:`Bonaparte places a postilion on the throne of Naples`},{id:`foy`,term:`Foy`,kind:`history`,chapter:87,text:`General Maximilien Foy, wounded at Waterloo, became the great liberal orator of the Chamber of Deputies under the Restoration; his funeral in 1825 was a huge opposition demonstration. Hugo's example of a soldier turned into a voice for liberty.`,quote:`Foy falls at Hougomont and rises again in the tribune`},{id:`the-bearnese`,term:`the Bearnese`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`Henri IV (reigned 1589–1610), the first Bourbon king, born in Béarn, a popular hero of the monarchy. The Restoration set him up as the anti-Napoleon: 'the Corsican' against 'the Bearnese'.`,quote:`The Corsican became the antithesis of the Bearnese`},{id:`hartwell`,term:`Hartwell`,kind:`place`,chapter:88,text:`Hartwell House in Buckinghamshire, England, where Louis XVIII lived in exile from 1808 to 1814. Hugo pictures the exile's simple pine table carried into the throne room of Louis XIV.`,quote:`Hartwell’s pine table`},{id:`trestaillon`,term:`Trestaillon`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`The nickname of Jacques Dupont, leader of royalist gangs who murdered Protestants and Bonapartists around Nîmes in the 'White Terror' of 1815. That he 'was celebrated' says what the new order tolerated.`,quote:`Trestaillon was celebrated.`},{id:`non-pluribus-impar`,term:`non pluribus impar`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:88,text:`Latin, 'not unequal to many', the motto of Louis XIV, set under his emblem of the sun. Its return on barrack walls marks the Restoration's revival of the old monarchy's symbols.`,quote:`The device non pluribus impar reappeared`},{id:`the-duc-d-enghien`,term:`the Duc d’Enghien`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`A young Bourbon prince kidnapped from German territory on Napoleon's orders and shot in the moat of the château of Vincennes in March 1804, the year of the coronation. The Restoration raised a monument on the spot.`,quote:`the Duc d’Enghien had perished`},{id:`article-14`,term:`Article 14`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`The article of the Charter of 1814 that let the king issue ordinances 'for the security of the State'. Liberals feared it as a loophole for absolute rule; Hugo calls it the serpent hidden in the constitution.`,quote:`with Article 14 in the heart`,seeAlso:[179]},{id:`holy-alliance`,term:`Holy Alliance`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`The pact signed in September 1815 by the rulers of Russia, Austria and Prussia to govern by Christian principles, which in practice meant suppressing revolution in Europe. Hugo sets it against 'Belle-Alliance', the inn on the battlefield.`,quote:`There was a Holy Alliance`},{id:`hudson-lowe`,term:`Hudson Lowe`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`Sir Hudson Lowe, the British governor of St Helena and Napoleon's jailer, whose petty strictness became notorious. Montchenu was the French commissioner on the island; Longwood the damp house where Napoleon lived and died in 1821.`,quote:`England had him guarded by Hudson Lowe`},{id:`the-congress-of-vienna`,term:`the Congress of Vienna`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`The conference of European powers (1814–1815) that redrew the map after Napoleon's wars and restored the old dynasties. Hugo makes it, more than the return of the king, the true Restoration.`,quote:`The Congress of Vienna made the treaties in 1815`},{id:`sic-vos-non-vobis`,term:`sic vos non vobis`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:89,text:`Latin, 'thus you, but not for yourselves', from a verse attributed to Virgil about a poet whose work another claimed. The village of Waterloo, which took no part, gets the glory of the fighting at Mont-Saint-Jean and Hougomont.`,quote:`the sic vos non vobis`},{id:`stragglers`,term:`stragglers`,kind:`word`,chapter:89,text:`The camp followers, deserters and hangers-on who trailed every army: sutlers selling drink and food from carts, servants, beggars, marauders. They stripped and robbed the dead and wounded after a battle, and soldiers caught doing the same could be shot.`,quote:`they are called “stragglers.”`},{id:`sutler-s-wagon`,term:`sutler’s wagon`,kind:`word`,chapter:89,text:`A sutler (vivandier) sold provisions and drink to soldiers on campaign from a covered cart, often run by a man and his wife. The little wagon hidden by the Nivelles road is the kind that followed every army.`,quote:`a sort of little sutler’s wagon`},{id:`turenne`,term:`Turenne`,kind:`history`,chapter:89,text:`The Vicomte de Turenne (1611–1675), Louis XIV's great marshal, loved by his men, who allowed them to pillage and devastated the Palatinate in 1674. Hoche and Marceau, young generals of the Republic, kept strict discipline.`,quote:`Turenne was adored by his soldiers`},{id:`alleurs`,term:`Alleurs`,kind:`lore`,chapter:89,text:`Hugo's name for night-wandering larvae or spirits of Norman folklore, creatures that haunt ruins. The image fits the furtive figure moving among the dead.`,quote:`which ancient Norman legends call the Alleurs`},{id:`gazette-des-tribunaux`,term:`Gazette des Tribunaux`,kind:`history`,chapter:90,text:`A Paris daily devoted to court reports, founded in 1825, which made trials a popular reading matter. Before it, news of a provincial case reached the public only in brief paragraphs like the two Hugo 'transcribes' from the Drapeau Blanc and the Journal de Paris.`,quote:`the Gazette des Tribunaux was not yet in existence`},{id:`the-patriarch-of-ferney`,term:`the patriarch of Ferney`,kind:`reference`,chapter:90,text:`Voltaire, who spent his last twenty years at his château of Ferney near Geneva and was revered there as 'the patriarch'. The newspaper quotes his verse on the little Savoyards who come every year to sweep chimneys.`,quote:`the patriarch of Ferney`},{id:`commute-his-penalty`,term:`commute his penalty`,kind:`custom`,chapter:90,text:`Only the king could pardon or commute a sentence. A death sentence reduced to hard labour for life was a common act of royal clemency, and the Constitutionnel, a liberal paper, read it as favour to a man known for his piety.`,quote:`has deigned to commute his penalty`},{id:`the-death-of-alexander`,term:`the death of Alexander`,kind:`reference`,chapter:90,text:`When Alexander the Great died in 323 BC his generals carved his empire into rival kingdoms. Hugo compares the break-up of Madeleine's industry among squabbling former foremen to that partition.`,quote:`because it occurred after the death of Alexander`},{id:`the-devil-s-treasure`,term:`the devil’s treasure`,kind:`custom`,chapter:91,text:`Hugo's account of a Montfermeil superstition: a horned black man digs holes in the forest at night to hide the devil's treasure, and whoever meets him dies within a week, a month or a year depending on what he does. Tryphon's Latin couplet lists what the digger finds: 'copper, coins, stones, a corpse, ghosts, and nothing'.`,quote:`the devil, from time immemorial, has selected the forest as a hiding-place`},{id:`roger-bacon-and-charles-vi`,term:`Roger Bacon and Charles VI`,kind:`history`,chapter:91,text:`Roger Bacon, the thirteenth-century English friar, was credited with the first Western recipe for gunpowder; playing cards were said to have been invented to amuse the mad French king Charles VI around 1392. Hugo uses them to date the devil's later finds.`,quote:`before Roger Bacon’s time, and cards before the time of Charles VI`},{id:`road-mender`,term:`road-mender`,kind:`custom`,chapter:91,text:`Cantonniers kept a stretch of road in repair, breaking stones by the roadside. The administration gave such work, at reduced pay, to released convicts under police supervision who could find nothing else, which is how Boulatruelle lives.`,quote:`employed him at reduced rates as a road-mender`},{id:`the-water-test`,term:`the water test`,kind:`history`,chapter:91,text:`Under the old monarchy, judicial torture ('the question') was used to extract confessions; one form forced quantities of water into the accused. It was abolished in 1788. Thénardier's 'wine test' is the tavern version.`,quote:`the water test, for example`},{id:`voltairians`,term:`Voltairians`,kind:`word`,chapter:91,text:`Sceptics and anticlericals who mocked religion in Voltaire's manner, a familiar village type of the Restoration alongside the devout. Their jibe about catching the devil turns the superstition into a joke.`,quote:`The Voltairians added`},{id:`the-spanish-war`,term:`the Spanish war`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`In 1823 a French army under the Duc d'Angoulême invaded Spain at the request of the Holy Alliance to restore the absolute power of Ferdinand VII against the liberal constitution. It was quick and almost bloodless. Hugo calls it a war by Napoleon's generals for Louis XIV's heirs against liberty.`,quote:`“the epoch of the Spanish war.”`,seeAlso:[93]},{id:`the-hero-of-andujar`,term:`the hero of Andujar`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`The Duc d'Angoulême, nephew of Louis XVIII, issued an ordinance at Andújar curbing royalist reprisals in Spain; the liberal papers gave him the title with irony. The Trocadéro was a fort at Cadiz whose capture ended the war, and gave its name to a square in Paris.`,quote:`“the hero of Andujar,”`},{id:`the-holy-office`,term:`the Holy Office`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`The Spanish Inquisition, the church tribunal for heresy, abolished by the liberals and the symbol of the absolute monarchy that France was restoring. The 'descamisados', the shirtless, were the Spanish radicals, as the sans-culottes had been the French.`,quote:`the Holy Office`},{id:`coblentz`,term:`Coblentz`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`The Rhineland town where émigré nobles gathered an army against the Revolution in 1791–1792 under the white flag. In 1823 a few French liberals in Spain did the same under the tricolour, against their own country's army.`,quote:`thirty years earlier at Coblentz`},{id:`saragossa-and-palafox`,term:`Saragossa and Palafox`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`The Spanish city whose people, led by General Palafox, held out against two terrible French sieges in 1808–1809. Rostopchin was the Moscow governor blamed for burning the city in 1812; Ballesteros a Spanish general who surrendered in 1823. France, Hugo says, prefers enemies who fight.`,quote:`Soldiers of the war of 1808, on whom Saragossa had fallen`},{id:`el-rey-netto`,term:`el rey netto`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:92,text:`Spanish, 'the king pure and simple': an absolute king without a constitution, the royalists' cry for Ferdinand VII. Hugo's warning is that a French government able to impose one in Madrid might be tempted to try at home.`,quote:`el rey netto`},{id:`machineel-tree`,term:`machineel tree`,kind:`reference`,chapter:92,text:`The manchineel, a tropical tree whose sap and fruit are poisonous; legend held that sleeping in its shade was fatal. Hugo's image for a regime trusting the loyalty of its army.`,quote:`in the shadow of a machineel tree`},{id:`ship-of-the-line`,term:`ship of the line`,kind:`word`,chapter:92,text:`The largest class of sailing warship, with two or three gun decks and up to 120 cannon, built to fight in the line of battle. Convicts from the bagne worked in Toulon's naval arsenal and were sent aboard ships under repair.`,quote:`A ship of the line is one of the most magnificent combinations`},{id:`yard-and-foot-rope`,term:`yard and foot-rope`,kind:`word`,chapter:92,text:`A yard is the long horizontal spar from which a square sail hangs; sailors working the sail stood on a foot-rope slung beneath it. The topman is the sailor stationed aloft on the tops of the masts.`,quote:`he seized the footrope`},{id:`livry-and-chelles`,term:`Livry and Chelles`,kind:`place`,chapter:93,text:`Villages on either side of Montfermeil east of Paris, Chelles in the Marne valley with the remains of a royal abbey. The plateau between the Ourcq and the Marne was wooded and dry, so water had to be fetched from springs and ponds.`,quote:`Montfermeil is situated between Livry and Chelles`},{id:`christmas-eve`,term:`Christmas eve`,kind:`custom`,chapter:93,text:`Christmas in rural France was a church feast with a midnight mass, a late supper and a fair, not a day of family presents; children set out a shoe by the hearth for a small gift. A travelling fair has set up its booths in the village for the feast.`,quote:`On Christmas eve itself`},{id:`kaleidoscope`,term:`kaleidoscope`,kind:`history`,chapter:93,text:`The optical toy of mirrors and coloured glass, invented by the Scot David Brewster in 1816 and a craze across Europe in the following years. With a ribbed tin lamp it dates the Thénardiers' room to 1823.`,quote:`a kaleidoscope and a lamp of ribbed tin`},{id:`cat-o-nine-tails`,term:`cat-o’-nine-tails`,kind:`word`,chapter:93,text:`A whip of several knotted cords on a handle, used in armies and navies for flogging. Hanging on a nail in the chimney-corner, it is the household's instrument for one child only.`,quote:`a cat-o’-nine-tails was hanging on a nail`},{id:`wooden-shoes`,term:`wooden shoes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:93,text:`Sabots, shoes carved from a single block of wood, worn by peasants and the poor, often without stockings. Cosette wears them on bare feet in winter while she knits woollen stockings for the Thénardier girls.`,quote:`her bare feet were thrust into wooden shoes`,seeAlso:[100]},{id:`abbe-delille`,term:`Abbé Delille`,kind:`reference`,chapter:94,text:`Jacques Delille (1738–1813), a fashionable poet and translator of Virgil, admired in his day and soon ridiculed. Thénardier looks like his portraits; his heroes Voltaire, Raynal and Parny were all freethinking writers of the eighteenth century.`,quote:`the portraits of the Abbé Delille`},{id:`filousophe`,term:`filousophe`,kind:`word`,chapter:94,text:`A pun on 'filou' (swindler) and 'philosophe', the eighteenth-century name for the freethinking men of letters. Hugo's coinage for a cheat who talks like a philosopher.`,quote:`A filousophe, a scientific thief.`},{id:`the-champ-d-asile`,term:`the Champ d’Asile`,kind:`history`,chapter:94,text:`'Field of Refuge', a colony founded in Texas in 1818 by exiled Bonapartist officers. Liberals and Bonapartists in France subscribed money for it, a safe political gesture; it collapsed within months.`,quote:`He had subscribed for the Champ d’Asile.`},{id:`quibus`,term:`quibus`,kind:`word`,chapter:94,text:`Slang for money, from a Latin legal phrase; 'to have the quibus' is to be in funds. Hugo names exactly where Thénardier's came from.`,quote:`“some quibus,”`},{id:`exposing-the-crown`,term:`exposing the crown`,kind:`history`,chapter:94,text:`A parliamentary phrase: to drag the monarch's name into public debate, which ministers were bound to avoid. Madame Thénardier never contradicts her husband 'before strangers'.`,quote:`“exposing the crown.”`},{id:`fifteen-sou-piece`,term:`fifteen-sou piece`,kind:`money`,chapter:95,text:`A small silver coin worth three-quarters of a franc, enough for a large loaf. For Cosette it is a sum she is responsible for and cannot replace.`,quote:`Here’s a fifteen-sou piece.`},{id:`midnight-mass`,term:`midnight mass`,kind:`custom`,chapter:96,text:`The first mass of Christmas, celebrated at midnight on Christmas eve, to which villagers walked carrying candles. The fair's booths stay lit for them.`,quote:`on their way to the midnight mass`},{id:`werewolf-child`,term:`werewolf child`,kind:`lore`,chapter:97,text:`Country people believed in the loup-garou, a person turned wolf at night who roamed the fields. A small figure out alone in the dark on Christmas eve is, for a moment, uncanny.`,quote:`Is it a werewolf child?`},{id:`henry-iv-s-frills`,term:`Henry IV’s frills`,kind:`word`,chapter:97,text:`A country name for a crimped wild grass, likened to the stiff pleated ruff worn at the court of Henri IV. Hugo gives the spring's plants their local names.`,quote:`those tall, crimped grasses which are called Henry IV’s frills`},{id:`boulevard-de-l-hopital`,term:`Boulevard de l’Hôpital`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`A wide, half-empty boulevard on the south-east edge of Paris, running past the vast Salpêtrière hospital for poor and insane women. The faubourg Saint-Marceau around it was one of the poorest quarters of the city.`,quote:`the Boulevard de l’Hôpital in Paris`,seeAlso:[103]},{id:`choisy-le-roi`,term:`Choisy-le-Roi`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`A town on the Seine south of Paris with a royal château. Louis XVIII, too lame to walk, drove there almost daily at a gallop, so the poor women of the quarter told the time by his carriage.`,quote:`King Louis XVIII went nearly every day to Choisy-le-Roi`},{id:`the-golden-fleece-the-cross-of-saint-louis`,term:`the Golden Fleece, the cross of Saint Louis`,kind:`history`,chapter:98,text:`Royal orders of chivalry worn by Louis XVIII: the Habsburg and Spanish Order of the Golden Fleece, the military Order of Saint Louis, and the Order of the Holy Spirit with its wide blue ribbon, the highest order of the French monarchy. His hair is powdered in the style called 'royal bird'.`,quote:`the Golden Fleece, the cross of Saint Louis`},{id:`the-bodyguard`,term:`the bodyguard`,kind:`history`,chapter:98,text:`The Gardes du Corps, the king's mounted household troops in silver-laced uniforms, restored in 1814. The Duc de Havré was one of their captains, riding in the royal carriage.`,quote:`surrounded by a squadron of the bodyguard`},{id:`porte-saint-martin`,term:`Porte Saint-Martin`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`A triumphal arch of Louis XIV on the boulevards, and the popular theatre beside it, home of melodrama. From the boulevard the coaches for the eastern suburbs left from inns in the nearby alleys.`,quote:`the theatre of the Porte Saint-Martin`},{id:`twenty-sou-piece`,term:`twenty-sou piece`,kind:`money`,chapter:100,text:`A silver one-franc coin, worth more than the lost fifteen sous; Madame Thénardier notices and keeps quiet. A night's lodging at the inn was normally twenty sous.`,quote:`it was a twenty-sou piece`},{id:`brave-homme`,term:`brave homme`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:100,text:`'Good fellow' or 'my good man', the patronising way of speaking to a working man or a beggar. The switch to 'Monsieur' marks Madame Thénardier's change of mind about her guest's purse.`,quote:`brave homme or bonhomme`},{id:`corpus-delicti`,term:`corpus delicti`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:100,text:`Latin legal term, 'the body of the crime': the object or evidence proving that an offence was committed. Hugo applies it with mock solemnity to the Thénardier girls' doll.`,quote:`the corpus delicti`},{id:`the-duchesse-de-berry`,term:`the Duchesse de Berry`,kind:`history`,chapter:100,text:`The widow of the murdered Duc de Berry and mother of the Bourbons' young heir, a celebrated figure at court. Saying 'Your Majesty' to her is Madame Thénardier's idea of absurd deference.`,quote:`as though to the Duchesse de Berry!`},{id:`courrier-francais`,term:`Courrier Français`,kind:`history`,chapter:100,text:`A liberal opposition newspaper of the Restoration. Thénardier reading it, three times over, fits his pose as a Bonapartist and liberal.`,quote:`began to read the Courrier Français`},{id:`shoes-in-the-chimney`,term:`shoes in the chimney`,kind:`custom`,chapter:100,text:`On Christmas eve French children set a shoe on the hearth for the Christ Child, or a 'good fairy', to fill with a small present or coin by morning. A ten-sou piece was a modest gift; a gold louis would be a fortune.`,quote:`children place their shoes in the chimney on Christmas eve`},{id:`orange-flowers`,term:`orange flowers`,kind:`custom`,chapter:100,text:`Brides wore a wreath of orange blossom, real or wax, a symbol of purity, and kept it afterwards under a glass globe on the mantelpiece. Thénardier has bought one second-hand for show.`,quote:`a woman’s headdress in silver wire and orange flowers`},{id:`the-hundredths`,term:`the hundredths`,kind:`history`,chapter:101,text:`The 'centimes additionnels', surcharges added to direct taxes to fund local government, alongside the trade licence (patente) and the door and window tax. Madame Thénardier lists them all to plead poverty.`,quote:`the door and window tax, the hundredths!`},{id:`castlereagh`,term:`Castlereagh`,kind:`history`,chapter:101,text:`Lord Castlereagh, the British foreign secretary who negotiated the peace settlement and the indemnity France had to pay the allies after 1815. Hugo compares Thénardier's dissatisfaction with his bill to his.`,quote:`the accent of Castlereagh auditing France’s bill`},{id:`twenty-franc-pieces-of-the-restoration`,term:`twenty-franc pieces of the Restoration`,kind:`money`,chapter:101,text:`Gold twenty-franc coins struck under Louis XVIII bore his bare head with a short tied-back queue in place of Napoleon's laurel wreath. Hugo calls it 'Prussian', a sneer at a king restored by foreign armies.`,quote:`perfectly new twenty-franc pieces of the Restoration`},{id:`dressed-in-mourning`,term:`dressed in mourning`,kind:`custom`,chapter:101,text:`Black clothes were worn for a set period after a death in the family, by children as well as adults. A child walking out of the village all in black is plainly mourning someone.`,quote:`a little girl dressed in mourning`},{id:`livry-and-bondy`,term:`Livry and Bondy`,kind:`place`,chapter:102,text:`Villages north of Montfermeil on the way back toward Paris, at the edge of the great forest of Bondy, long known for its highwaymen. The aqueduct of the old Abbey of Chelles crossed the woods there.`,quote:`in the direction of Livry and Bondy`},{id:`balaguier`,term:`Balaguier`,kind:`place`,chapter:103,text:`A headland across the harbour from Toulon, with Cape Brun on the other side of the roadstead. Hugo names real places along an escaped convict's route, and a house that served as a 'dressing-room' where fugitives exchanged their convict red for ordinary clothes.`,quote:`in the neighborhood of Balaguier`},{id:`barrier-monceaux`,term:`Barrier Monceaux`,kind:`place`,chapter:103,text:`One of the toll gates in the customs wall round Paris, on the north-west side. Entering the city there and crossing it by cab to the Observatoire, then on foot through the poor streets of the Ourcine and the Glacière, made the route hard to follow.`,quote:`by way of the Barrier Monceaux`},{id:`the-salpetriere-and-bicetre`,term:`The Salpêtrière and Bicêtre`,kind:`place`,chapter:104,text:`Two vast hospital-asylums on the southern edge of Paris. The Salpêtrière, built on an old gunpowder (saltpetre) works, held poor, mad and 'fallen' women; Bicêtre, just outside the wall, was its male counterpart and also a prison. Hugo places the Gorbeau house between them, 'between the madness of women and the madness of men'.`,quote:`between the Salpêtrière, a glimpse of whose dome could be seen, and Bicêtre`},{id:`barriere`,term:`Barrière`,kind:`place`,chapter:104,text:`One of the toll gates in the wall of the Farmers-General, the customs wall that still ringed Paris in 1823. Goods entering the city paid duty there, so the barriers were cheap, rough districts of wine-shops and waste ground. The Barrière d'Italie and the Barrière Saint-Jacques are on the road out to Bicêtre; the latter became the site of public executions.`,quote:`In 1823 the city wall was still in existence.`},{id:`place-de-greve`,term:`Place de Grève`,kind:`place`,chapter:104,text:`The square in front of the Paris town hall where public executions had been carried out for centuries. Hugo calls the gallows set up by the elms of the Barrière Saint-Jacques a 'shop-keeping and bourgeois' Grève: a society that would neither abolish the death penalty nor own it openly moved the scaffold out of sight.`,quote:`that miserable and shameful Place de Grève of a shop-keeping and bourgeois society`},{id:`the-chatelet`,term:`The Châtelet`,kind:`history`,chapter:104,text:`The old fortress on the right bank that housed the chief law courts and a prison of royal Paris; it was demolished early in the century. The attorneys Corbeau and Renard who gave the house its name practised there about 1770.`,quote:`two attorneys at the Châtelet named, one Corbeau (Raven), the other Renard (Fox)`},{id:`maitre-corbeau-sur-un-arbre-perche`,term:`'Maître Corbeau, sur un arbre perché'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:104,text:`The opening of La Fontaine's fable 'The Crow and the Fox', in which a flattering fox talks a crow out of its cheese: every French schoolchild knew it by heart. The courthouse wags replaced the tree with a legal file and the cheese with a writ of seizure, and the two lawyers petitioned the king to change their names.`,quote:`Maître Corbeau, sur un dossier perché`},{id:`madame-du-barry`,term:`Madame du Barry`,kind:`history`,chapter:104,text:`The last mistress of Louis XV, a former shop-girl raised to the court in 1769. Hugo's anecdote of a papal nuncio and a cardinal putting slippers on her bare feet is a jab at the servility of the old regime's clergy, slipped in while explaining a house name.`,quote:`putting on, in his Majesty’s presence, a slipper on the bare feet of Madame du Barry`},{id:`the-orleans-railway`,term:`The Orleans railway`,kind:`history`,chapter:104,text:`The Paris–Orléans line, whose terminus (today the Gare d'Austerlitz) opened in 1840 beside the Salpêtrière. Hugo, writing in the 1860s, keeps stepping out of 1823 to say what has become of these places since; the railway is his emblem of the suburb being swallowed by the city.`,quote:`a railway station is the death of a suburb and the birth of a city`},{id:`faubourg-saint-marceau`,term:`Faubourg Saint-Marceau`,kind:`place`,chapter:104,text:`The poor working quarter of the left bank around the Rue Mouffetard and the Bièvre tanneries, long a byword for misery. Hugo's 'suburb of Saint-Marceau' is where paved streets only arrived, he says, in July 1845.`,quote:`Paris had entered the suburb of Saint-Marceau`},{id:`dressing-room-cabinet`,term:`Dressing-room (cabinet)`,kind:`word`,chapter:105,text:`Hapgood's rendering of the French cabinet: a small closet-room off a main chamber, big enough for a folding bed. Cheap Paris lodgings were described by their rooms and cabinets; a cabinet alone, without a fireplace, could be let as a dwelling.`,quote:`At the extreme end there was a dressing-room with a folding bed`},{id:`spanish-bonds`,term:`Spanish bonds`,kind:`history`,chapter:106,text:`Loans floated by the Spanish liberal government of 1820–23 and repudiated after the French army restored King Ferdinand VII in 1823, ruining many French holders. 'A gentleman ruined by Spanish bonds' was therefore a plausible story in December 1823, and an excuse for living poorly on hidden money.`,quote:`a gentleman of means who had been ruined by Spanish bonds`},{id:`principal-lodger`,term:`Principal lodger`,kind:`custom`,chapter:106,text:`In a Paris house the principale locataire rented the whole building from the owner and sublet the rooms, usually serving as portress and cleaner as well. Hugo mocks the grand title; she is the only neighbour and the only eyes in the Gorbeau house.`,quote:`ornamented with the name of the “principal lodger,” and in reality entrusted with the functions of portress`},{id:`chilblains`,term:`Chilblains`,kind:`word`,chapter:106,text:`Red, cracked, itching swellings on the hands and feet caused by repeated exposure to cold: the mark of a child who has worked outdoors and slept unheated. Cosette's hands still carry Montfermeil.`,quote:`her tiny red hand, all cracked with chilblains`},{id:`mourning`,term:`Mourning`,kind:`custom`,chapter:106,text:`Black clothes worn for a dead relative; even small children were dressed in black. The 'little black gown' Valjean bought at Montfermeil puts Cosette in mourning for her mother.`,quote:`Cosette was no longer in rags; she was in mourning.`},{id:`saint-medard`,term:`Saint-Médard`,kind:`place`,chapter:107,text:`The parish church of the Faubourg Saint-Marceau, at the foot of the Rue Mouffetard, a few minutes from the Gorbeau house. Its churchyard was notorious in the 1730s for the 'convulsionaries', Jansenist devotees who fell into fits at a deacon's tomb, a story Hugo alludes to elsewhere.`,quote:`He liked to go to Saint-Médard, which is the nearest church.`},{id:`thousand-franc-bank-bill`,term:`Thousand-franc bank-bill`,kind:`money`,chapter:107,text:`The Bank of France printed notes in large denominations only; a thousand-franc note was a year's wages for a labourer and something a poor woman might see two or three times in her life. Sewn into the lining of a shabby coat it is the stuff of neighbourhood legend.`,quote:`a bank-bill for a thousand francs. It was the second or third only that she had seen`},{id:`the-beggar-who-gives-alms`,term:`'The beggar who gives alms'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:107,text:`The nickname the Faubourg Saint-Marceau gives the shabby old man who accepts a sou from kind women and slips silver to beggars when he thinks no one is looking. Valjean's disguise works too well: a contradiction gets talked about.`,quote:`under the name of “the beggar who gives alms.”`},{id:`hundred-sou-piece`,term:`Hundred-sou piece`,kind:`money`,chapter:108,text:`The large silver five-franc coin, still counted in sous by most people (the chapter title calls it a five-franc piece). Valjean is making up a roll of a hundred francs in these coins when one escapes and rings on the floor, a sound a listening house can hear.`,quote:`a hundred-sou piece escaped from his hands and rolled noisily on the floor`},{id:`the-author-absent-from-paris`,term:`The author absent from Paris`,kind:`history`,chapter:109,text:`Hugo had been in exile since Louis-Napoléon's coup of December 1851, writing on Guernsey while Haussmann rebuilt Paris. This aside explains why he describes the city of his youth in the present tense and warns the reader that the streets he names may no longer exist.`,quote:`has been absent from Paris for many years`},{id:`venery`,term:`Venery`,kind:`word`,chapter:109,text:`The art and vocabulary of the hunt. Hugo narrates the whole night pursuit in hunting terms: the stag doubling back on its track (which he calls 'false re-imbushment'), the hounds, the scent, the 'knowing dog'. Valjean is the hunted animal, Javert the huntsman.`,quote:`In venery this is called “false re-imbushment.”`},{id:`the-mouffetard-quarter`,term:`The Mouffetard quarter`,kind:`place`,chapter:109,text:`The maze of old streets around the Rue Mouffetard on the left bank, between the Gorbeau house and the Panthéon. Hugo lists its lanes (Censier, Copeau, Puits-l'Ermite) like a map; Saint-Étienne-du-Mont, whose clock strikes eleven, is the church beside the Panthéon.`,quote:`many and varied labyrinths in the Mouffetard quarter`},{id:`commissary-of-police`,term:`Commissary of police`,kind:`title`,chapter:109,text:`The commissaire de police, the salaried official in charge of a Paris district's police post, under the Prefect of Police. The lantern over his office in the Rue de Pontoise is what betrays the men following Valjean.`,quote:`in front of the office of the commissary of police, situated at No. 14`},{id:`pont-d-austerlitz`,term:`Pont d'Austerlitz`,kind:`place`,chapter:110,text:`An iron bridge across the Seine by the Jardin des Plantes, finished in 1807 and named for Napoleon's victory. Like other new bridges it was built by a company that charged a toll, a sou a head, for decades; a man carrying a child who can walk pays for two.`,quote:`Tolls were still collected there at that epoch.`},{id:`the-goblet-potteries`,term:`The Goblet potteries`,kind:`reference`,chapter:110,text:`A real pottery in the Rue de la Clef whose rhymed sign Hugo quotes: 'Here is the factory of Goblet the son; come choose jugs and pitchers, flower-pots, pipes and brick; to all comers the Heart sells Diamonds (tiles).' The last line is a pun on playing-card suits and roof tiles. Hugo copies it as one of the small, real sights Valjean passes in the night.`,quote:`A tout venant le Coeur vend des Carreaux`},{id:`jardin-des-plantes`,term:`Jardin des Plantes`,kind:`place`,chapter:110,text:`The royal botanical garden and natural-history museum on the left bank near the river, with a small zoo. Valjean skirts it by the lower streets to reach the quay and the bridge.`,quote:`skirted the Jardin des Plantes by the lower streets, and reached the quay`},{id:`the-petit-picpus-quarter`,term:`The Petit-Picpus quarter`,kind:`place`,chapter:111,text:`Hugo's name for a half-built district of walls, gardens and convents between the Faubourg Saint-Antoine and the river port of La Râpée, so obscure that, he says, no existing map shows it and only the 1727 plan of Denis Thierry does. Its Y of streets (Chemin-Vert, Petit-Picpus, Polonceau) joined by the Rue Droit-Mur and ending in the blind alley Genrot is the trap Valjean has walked into.`,quote:`Le Petit-Picpus, which, moreover, hardly ever had any existence`},{id:`mazas`,term:`Mazas`,kind:`history`,chapter:111,text:`A great cellular prison built in the 1840s near what is now the Gare de Lyon, on the ground Hugo is describing. He lists it among the 'improvements' of the new quarter as progress 'with its antidote'.`,quote:`railway stations, and a prison, Mazas, there`},{id:`les-quatre-nations-feydeau`,term:`Les Quatre-Nations, Feydeau`,kind:`place`,chapter:111,text:`Examples of Paris's habit of keeping old names: the Collège des Quatre-Nations was the building that became the Institut de France, and the Opéra-Comique had played in the Rue Feydeau. Hugo lists such survivals (Porte Saint-Jacques, la Bourbe, la Petite-Pologne) as the people's memory of a vanished city.`,quote:`persists in calling the Institut les Quatre-Nations, and the Opera-Comique Feydeau`},{id:`espalier`,term:`Espalier`,kind:`word`,chapter:112,text:`A fruit tree trained flat against a wall on a frame of branches. The old lead water-pipes branching across the convent façade look to Valjean like a leafless espalier, and for a moment like a ladder.`,quote:`This odd espalier, with its branches of lead and iron`},{id:`the-conciergerie-and-battemolle`,term:`The Conciergerie and Battemolle`,kind:`history`,chapter:113,text:`The Conciergerie is the medieval prison on the Île de la Cité where the condemned waited for the scaffold. Battemolle, a man under sentence of death, is said to have escaped by climbing a corner of its wall by sheer muscle; Hugo cites him to show that Valjean's feat has precedents.`,quote:`that corner of the wall of the Conciergerie of Paris by which Battemolle, condemned to death, made his escape`},{id:`street-lanterns-on-ropes`,term:`Street lanterns on ropes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:113,text:`Before gas (which came to Paris streets from the late 1820s), oil lanterns hung over the roadway from a rope stretched between walls and were lowered and raised with a pulley; the lamplighter kept the key to the little box that locked the rope. Not lit on moonlit nights, such a lantern is simply a rope waiting to be cut.`,quote:`they were ascended and descended by means of a rope, which traversed the street from side to side`},{id:`would-have-given-it-for-a-rope`,term:`'Would have given it for a rope'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:113,text:`An echo of Shakespeare's Richard III crying 'My kingdom for a horse!' on Bosworth field. Hugo turns the king's cry into a convict's: the one thing he lacks at midnight is a rope.`,quote:`if Jean Valjean had had a kingdom, he would have given it for a rope at that moment`},{id:`swallow-knot`,term:`Swallow knot`,kind:`word`,chapter:113,text:`A sailor's knot (the French noeud d'hirondelle) that can be tied fast in a moment and will not slip. Valjean, who learned such things in the port of Toulon, uses it to fasten his cravat round Cosette to the lantern rope.`,quote:`which seafaring men call a “swallow knot,”`},{id:`cravat`,term:`Cravat`,kind:`word`,chapter:113,text:`A long strip of linen or silk wound round the neck and knotted, worn by every man who was not in rags; a coat without a cravat looked half-dressed. Valjean's becomes a harness to lift Cosette over the wall.`,quote:`he undid his cravat, passed it round Cosette’s body under the armpits`},{id:`deaf-stones-of-which-the-scriptures-speak`,term:`'Deaf stones of which the Scriptures speak'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:114,text:`The Bible repeatedly mocks idols of wood and stone that cannot hear or speak (Habakkuk 2:18–19, Psalm 115). Hugo borrows the image for the convent walls, which seem not to hear the uproar of the search outside them.`,quote:`those walls had been built of the deaf stones of which the Scriptures speak`},{id:`winding-sheet`,term:`Winding-sheet`,kind:`word`,chapter:115,text:`The cloth in which a corpse is wrapped for burial: a shroud. Hugo's first glimpse into the convent is deliberately a vision of the grave, a form lying face down under what looks like a shroud, with a rope round its neck.`,quote:`something which appeared to be covered with a winding-sheet, and which resembled a human form`},{id:`bell-glasses`,term:`Bell-glasses`,kind:`word`,chapter:116,text:`Glass domes (cloches) set over young melons and other tender plants to protect them from frost. Spreading straw mats over the melon bed on a bright, freezing night is exactly what a gardener would be doing at two in the morning.`,quote:`walking amid the bell-glasses of the melon beds`},{id:`the-gardener-s-bell`,term:`The gardener's bell`,kind:`lore`,chapter:117,text:`A bell buckled to the gardener's knee so that the nuns hear him coming and keep away; in a house of women no other man may enter but the archbishop. The 'tinkling like cattle bells' Valjean heard in the garden was this. Fauchelevent wears it with a wink.`,quote:`a leather kneecap, whence hung a moderately large bell`},{id:`pardieu-pardine`,term:`Pardieu, pardine`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:117,text:`Mild rustic oaths, 'by God' with the blasphemy softened by mispronunciation, like 'parbleu' and 'morbleu'. Fauchelevent's speech is full of them; he is a peasant who was once a notary, and Hugo gives him a countryman's voluble tongue.`,quote:`“Ah! pardieu, this is too much!”`},{id:`the-prefecture-of-police`,term:`The Prefecture of Police`,kind:`title`,chapter:118,text:`The headquarters of the Paris police, under a prefect appointed by the government; the Comte Anglès was prefect from 1815 to 1821, and M. Chabouillet his secretary. Being 'attached to the police force of Paris' was a promotion for a provincial inspector like Javert.`,quote:`M. Chabouillet, secretary of the Prefecture under Comte Anglès`},{id:`the-prince-generalissimo-at-bayonne`,term:`The Prince Generalissimo at Bayonne`,kind:`history`,chapter:118,text:`The Duc d'Angoulême, nephew of Louis XVIII, commanded the French army that invaded Spain in 1823 to restore King Ferdinand VII's absolute power, and returned in triumph that December. A royalist like Javert would read a newspaper for this and nothing else.`,quote:`the triumphal entry of the Prince Generalissimo into Bayonne`},{id:`seine-et-oise`,term:`Seine-et-Oise`,kind:`place`,chapter:118,text:`The département that surrounded Paris on all sides, including Montfermeil; its prefecture was at Versailles. A report of a child stolen there would be forwarded to the Paris police as a matter of routine.`,quote:`a police report was transmitted from the prefecture of the Seine-et-Oise`},{id:`the-free-press-and-the-chambers`,term:`The free press and the Chambers`,kind:`history`,chapter:118,text:`In the early 1820s newspapers could still denounce police abuses, and deputies could raise them in the two Chambers (Deputies and Peers) of the constitutional monarchy. Hugo explains Javert's hesitation partly by this: an arrest of a respectable white-haired 'grandfather' that turned out wrong would be in twenty papers and cost the agent his place.`,quote:`the free press embarrassed it; several arbitrary arrests denounced by the newspapers`},{id:`emigre-preceptor`,term:`Émigré-preceptor`,kind:`word`,chapter:118,text:`The look of a returned exile of the Revolution reduced to earning his bread as a tutor: shabby, genteel, elderly, harmless. Valjean's yellow coat and careful manners read that way to Javert, and the altered gait of a man pacing himself to a child's steps completes the picture.`,quote:`his costume of an émigré-preceptor`},{id:`attila-hannibal-at-capua-danton-at-arcis`,term:`Attila, Hannibal at Capua, Danton at Arcis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:118,text:`Three famous hesitations: Attila wavering between the eastern and western Roman emperors, Hannibal idling at Capua instead of marching on Rome, Danton lingering at his home at Arcis-sur-Aube in 1794 while his enemies prepared his arrest. Hugo ranks Javert's lost quarter-hour at the Carrefour Rollin with them.`,quote:`it is Hannibal tarrying at Capua; it is Danton falling asleep at Arcis-sur-Aube`},{id:`duvivier-ligniville-desprez-artonge`,term:`Duvivier, Ligniville, Desprez, Artonge`,kind:`reference`,chapter:118,text:`Old authorities on stag-hunting whom Hugo cites as if they were generals. When a stag escapes with the pack at its heels, the hunters, he says, are left saying 'it was not a stag, but a sorcerer'.`,quote:`Duvivier, Ligniville, and Desprez halt short.`},{id:`open-sesame`,term:`Open Sesame`,kind:`reference`,chapter:119,text:`The magic words that open the robbers' cave in 'Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves' from the Arabian Nights. Getting past the convent's porter, and then past the voice behind the grating, needs passwords that only the initiated know.`,quote:`there was an Open Sesame! which it was necessary to know`},{id:`nankin-paper-at-fifteen-sous-the-roll`,term:`Nankin paper at fifteen sous the roll`,kind:`money`,chapter:119,text:`Cheap wallpaper in the buff colour of nankeen cotton, sold at three-quarters of a franc a roll. Hugo prices it to make the point: the visitors' room of this convent is as bare and poor as a servant's attic.`,quote:`hung with nankin paper with green flowers, at fifteen sous the roll`},{id:`baignoire`,term:`Baignoire`,kind:`word`,chapter:119,text:`A ground-floor theatre box, so called because its shape recalls a bathtub, closed off from the house by a grille until the chandelier is lit. Hugo uses it to make the reader feel the convent parlour: a dark box facing a stage that stays shuttered.`,quote:`when one enters at the theatre into a grated baignoire`},{id:`guimpe`,term:`Guimpe`,kind:`word`,chapter:119,text:`The wimple: the piece of linen or wool covering a nun's neck and breast up to the chin, here black. With the veil it leaves only the mouth and chin visible, which is all a visitor to this parlour ever sees of a nun.`,quote:`One caught a glimpse of a black guimpe`},{id:`parlor-parloir`,term:`Parlor (parloir)`,kind:`custom`,chapter:119,text:`The one room of a closed convent where nuns may speak to outsiders, always through a grating; in strict houses a shutter behind the grating is opened only for close relatives. The Petit-Picpus version, with its double grille, perforated tin plate and black shutters, is the harshest Hugo knows.`,quote:`The box in which you stood was the parlor.`},{id:`bernardines-benedictines-of-martin-verga`,term:`Bernardines-Benedictines of Martin Verga`,kind:`history`,chapter:120,text:`The order of the Petit-Picpus nuns as Hugo describes it: Benedictines following a Spanish reform he dates to 1425, dependent on Cîteaux and so 'Bernardine' in name, under the rule of Saint Benedict made harsher. Historians have not found Martin Verga; the real nuns of the Rue Picpus were Benedictines of the Blessed Sacrament, and Hugo seems to have combined and disguised his sources.`,quote:`a community of Bernardines of the obedience of Martin Verga`},{id:`saint-benoit-benedict-and-saint-bernard`,term:`Saint Benoît (Benedict) and Saint Bernard`,kind:`reference`,chapter:120,text:`Benedict of Nursia (c. 480–547) wrote the rule that governs Western monks and founded Monte Cassino; Hugo repeats the legend of him driving the devil from a temple of Apollo at Subiaco. Bernard of Clairvaux (1090–1153) led the Cistercian reform of that rule. The chapter's tangle of orders (Cluny, Cîteaux, Carthusians and the rest) all descend from Benedict.`,quote:`they were the subjects, not of Saint Bernard, but of Saint Benoît`},{id:`carmelites`,term:`Carmelites`,kind:`reference`,chapter:120,text:`An order of nuns reformed by Teresa of Ávila in the 1560s, famous for its severity: barefoot (hence 'Discalced'), fasting, silent. Hugo ranks only their rule above that of the Petit-Picpus.`,quote:`After the rule of the Carmelites, who go barefoot`},{id:`perpetual-adoration`,term:`Perpetual Adoration`,kind:`custom`,chapter:120,text:`The devotion of keeping at least one nun kneeling before the consecrated Host, day and night, without interruption, the sisters relieving one another hourly 'like soldiers on guard'. It gives the convent its name and sets its clock: the bell that interrupts everything on the hour, the twelve-hour 'reparation', the night office.`,quote:`there is always a sister kneeling before the Holy Sacrament`},{id:`reparation-at-the-post`,term:`Reparation; 'at the post'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:120,text:`The Petit-Picpus practice by which one nun in turn kneels for twelve hours on the stone before the altar, a rope round her neck, praying for all the sins of the world, lying flat with arms outstretched when she can no longer kneel. Because it is done before a post holding a candle, the nuns say 'to be at the post', preferring the phrase that sounds like a punishment.`,quote:`with hands clasped, a rope around her neck`},{id:`vocal-mothers-prioress`,term:`Vocal mothers; prioress`,kind:`title`,chapter:120,text:`The mères vocales are the senior professed nuns who have a voice in the chapter, the governing assembly; they elect the prioress, who rules the house for a three-year term and may be re-elected twice. Below them come the professed sisters, lay-sisters who do manual work, novices and postulants.`,quote:`the mothers, who are called mères vocales because they have a voice in the chapter`},{id:`ladies-of-the-holy-sacrament`,term:`Ladies of the Holy Sacrament`,kind:`history`,chapter:120,text:`Benedictine nuns of the Perpetual Adoration who had two Paris houses at the start of the century, at the Temple and in the Rue Neuve-Sainte-Geneviève, wearing a white wimple and a gilt emblem of the Host. Hugo insists they are not the Petit-Picpus order, though both practise the same devotion.`,quote:`the Benedictines called Ladies of the Holy Sacrament`},{id:`the-oratory-philip-neri-and-berulle`,term:`The Oratory: Philip Neri and Bérulle`,kind:`history`,chapter:120,text:`Two societies of priests of the same name: the Italian Oratory founded by Philip Neri in Rome in the 1560s and the French Oratory founded by Cardinal Pierre de Bérulle in 1611. Hugo's joke is that the French one claimed precedence because a cardinal outranks a saint.`,quote:`Philip de Neri was only a saint, while Bérulle was a cardinal`},{id:`breviary-and-matins`,term:`Breviary and matins`,kind:`word`,chapter:120,text:`The breviary is the book of the daily round of psalms and prayers (the 'office') that religious are bound to recite; matins is its night portion. The Petit-Picpus nuns rise between one and three in the morning to chant it, which is why singing is heard from a dark convent at that hour.`,quote:`rise from their first sleep, from one to three o’clock in the morning, to read their breviary and chant matins`},{id:`drugget-and-serge`,term:`Drugget and serge`,kind:`word`,chapter:120,text:`Coarse, scratchy woollen cloths. The nuns sleep between serge sheets and wear a drugget shift against the skin from 14 September (the feast of the Exaltation of the Holy Cross) until Easter, a penance the rule once demanded all year until it made them ill.`,quote:`wear drugget chemises for six months in the year`},{id:`saint-therese`,term:`Saint Thérèse`,kind:`reference`,chapter:120,text:`Teresa of Ávila (1515–1582), the Spanish mystic who reformed the Carmelites. The story Hugo tells, of her refusing a would-be nun who was 'attached' to her Bible, is quoted for the rule against owning or loving any object, even a holy one.`,quote:`“Ah, you are attached to something! In that case, do not enter our order!”`},{id:`visitandines`,term:`Visitandines`,kind:`reference`,chapter:120,text:`Nuns of the Order of the Visitation, founded in 1610 by Francis de Sales and Jeanne de Chantal, a gentler order than the Benedictines. Their greeting 'Ave Maria' / 'Gratia plena' ('Hail Mary' / 'full of grace') is contrasted with the Petit-Picpus 'Forever!'.`,quote:`Among the Visitandines the one who enters says: “Ave Maria,”`},{id:`the-vaugirard-cemetery`,term:`The Vaugirard cemetery`,kind:`place`,chapter:120,text:`An old burial ground on the south-western edge of Paris, on land once belonging to the convent, where the nuns had leave to be buried in a corner of their own at nightfall. Since the city forbids burials inside the convent, this is their only concession.`,quote:`a special corner in the ancient Vaugirard cemetery, which was made of land which had formerly belonged to their community`},{id:`chapter`,term:`Chapter`,kind:`word`,chapter:120,text:`The weekly assembly of a religious house, presided over by the prioress, where business is decided and faults confessed aloud. 'Chapter' also names the hall where it meets.`,quote:`Once a week the chapter assembles: the prioress presides`},{id:`coulpe`,term:`Coulpe`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:120,text:`From the Latin culpa, fault: the penance of lying prostrate before the prioress during the office for a trivial offence (a broken glass, a wrong note) until she taps her foot. The nun imposes it on herself; Hugo also calls it the peccavi, 'I have sinned'.`,quote:`To make one’s coulpe means to prostrate one’s self flat on one’s face`},{id:`postulant-novice-professed`,term:`Postulant, novice, professed`,kind:`title`,chapter:121,text:`The three stages of entering a convent: a postulant asks admission and lives under the rule on trial (here two to four years), a novice wears the habit in white and is trained (four years), and a professed nun has taken final vows, rarely before twenty-three or twenty-four. Each step could be refused, so a girl might live years in the house before being finally bound to it.`,quote:`One is a postulant for two years at least, often for four; a novice for four.`},{id:`our-sister-is-dead`,term:`'Our sister is dead'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:121,text:`The profession ceremony as the Petit-Picpus kept it: the novice comes crowned with roses in her finest dress, prostrates herself, is covered with a black pall while the office of the dead is sung, and the nuns answer one another that she is dead and alive in Christ. Taking the veil is staged as a funeral.`,quote:`“Our sister is dead;” and the other file responds in a voice of ecstasy, “Our sister is alive in Jesus Christ!”`},{id:`saint-aulaire-belissen-talbot`,term:`Saint-Aulaire, Bélissen, Talbot`,kind:`history`,chapter:121,text:`Names of the boarding-school's pupils meant to signal rank: old French nobility, and Talbot, the family of the Earls of Shrewsbury, the most prominent Catholic house in England. The school took girls of 'noble and mostly wealthy families' and raised them to dread the world.`,quote:`an English girl bearing the illustrious Catholic name of Talbot`},{id:`holy-spirit-of-silver-gilt`,term:`Holy Spirit of silver gilt`,kind:`custom`,chapter:121,text:`A pendant in the shape of a dove, emblem of the Holy Spirit, worn on the breast of the pupils' blue uniform. Convent schools dressed their girls in a distinctive colour with a devotional badge, as the Legion of Honour school at Écouen did.`,quote:`a Holy Spirit of silver gilt or of copper on their breast`},{id:`saint-martha-s-day`,term:`Saint Martha's day`,kind:`custom`,chapter:121,text:`The feast of Martha of Bethany, 29 July, patron of housekeepers and servants. On this and a few other great days the pupils were allowed to dress as nuns and keep the whole office, which they treated as a holiday.`,quote:`particularly Saint Martha’s day`},{id:`homer-and-perrault-hecuba-and-la-mere-grand`,term:`Homer and Perrault; Hecuba and la Mère-Grand`,kind:`reference`,chapter:122,text:`Homer's epics and Charles Perrault's fairy tales (1697) stand for the grand and the homely; Hecuba is the grieving queen of Troy and la Mère-Grand the grandmother of Little Red Riding Hood. Hugo says the girls' laughter would have smoothed the wrinkles of all these ancestresses.`,quote:`Homer would have come thither to laugh with Perrault`},{id:`agathocles`,term:`Agathocles`,kind:`reference`,chapter:122,text:`Tyrant of Syracuse in the fourth century BC, a name every schoolgirl who studied ancient history knew. The 'big big girls' turn the stout portress Sister Agatha into Agathocles.`,quote:`called her Agathocles`},{id:`the-four-nations-of-the-college-mazarin`,term:`The four nations of the Collège Mazarin`,kind:`history`,chapter:122,text:`Cardinal Mazarin's college (the 'Quatre-Nations') was founded in 1661 for students from four provinces newly joined to France, and sorted them accordingly. The pupils borrow the idea: each belongs to a 'nation' named after the corner of the refectory she eats in, Spider, Caterpillar, Woodlouse or Cricket.`,quote:`as in the old College Mazarin to distinguish four nations`},{id:`ecouen-and-madame-campan`,term:`Écouen and Madame Campan`,kind:`history`,chapter:122,text:`Napoleon founded a school at the château of Écouen in 1807 for daughters of members of the Legion of Honour and put it under Madame Campan, who had been first lady of the bedchamber to Marie-Antoinette and ran a famous girls' school. Hugo cites its procession ranks ('virgins', 'florists') as another convent-school custom.`,quote:`At the beginning of this century Écouen was one of those strict and graceful places`},{id:`punchinello`,term:`Punchinello`,kind:`reference`,chapter:122,text:`Polichinelle, the hunch-backed, stick-wielding puppet of French fairground shows (the ancestor of Punch). A small girl's 'poem' about Punchinello beating the cat and being put in prison is one of Hugo's specimens of children's talk in the cloister.`,quote:`It was Punchinello who bestowed it on the cat.`},{id:`the-white-paternoster`,term:`The white Paternoster`,kind:`custom`,chapter:123,text:`A folk prayer in rhythmic peasant French, half charm and half creed, promising paradise to whoever says it three times night and morning; versions of it were recited across rural France for centuries. Painted over the refectory door, it vanished under whitewash in 1827.`,quote:`this prayer, which was called the “white Paternoster,”`},{id:`nemo-regulas-externis-communicabit`,term:`Nemo regulas ... externis communicabit`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:123,text:`Latin: 'No one shall communicate our rules or constitutions to outsiders.' The convent's rule book was a single unprinted copy the pupils were forbidden to read, which is why stealing a look at it was such an adventure.`,quote:`Nemo regulas, seu constitutiones nostras, externis communicabit.`},{id:`ronds-d-eau`,term:`Ronds d'eau`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:123,text:`'Rounds of water': the large varnished bowls on the bare refectory tables in which the pupils rinsed their own cups and cutlery. Throwing a scrap of bad meat into them was punished.`,quote:`These bowls were called ronds d’eau.`},{id:`m-de-quelen`,term:`M. de Quélen`,kind:`history`,chapter:123,text:`Hyacinthe-Louis de Quélen, Archbishop of Paris from 1821 to 1839, a nobleman and a favourite of the Restoration court. Only the archbishop may enter the convent, so his word over-rides the prioress, and a bold pupil's three days' leave cannot be refused.`,quote:`Quélen smiled and said, “What, my dear child, a day’s leave of absence! Three days if you like.`},{id:`duc-de-rohan`,term:`Duc de Rohan`,kind:`history`,chapter:123,text:`Auguste de Rohan-Chabot, Prince de Léon, a young widower of one of France's greatest houses who became a priest, vicar-general of Paris, then Archbishop of Besançon and a cardinal. His elegance made him a hero to the schoolgirls, and the mad Madame Albertine's cry of 'Ah! Auguste!' set the convent gossiping about who she had been.`,quote:`M. Le Duc de Rohan, peer of France, officer of the Red Musketeers in 1815`},{id:`red-musketeers`,term:`Red Musketeers`,kind:`history`,chapter:123,text:`One of the two companies of mounted musketeers of the king's household, named for the colour of their horses, revived by Louis XVIII in 1814 for young nobles and disbanded in 1815. A former musketeer in a cassock is Hugo's shorthand for the Restoration's mixing of gallantry and devotion.`,quote:`He had been a mousquetaire`},{id:`magnates-mulieres`,term:`Magnates mulieres`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:123,text:`Latin, 'great ladies': the privilege by which women of the highest rank could enter a closed convent that admitted no one else. Two severe duchesses used it to terrify the boarding-school.`,quote:`in virtue of the privilege Magnates mulieres`},{id:`my-zetulbe`,term:`'My Zétulbé'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:123,text:`An air from Boieldieu's comic opera 'The Caliph of Baghdad' (1800), in which Zétulbé is the heroine. Played every day on a flute somewhere in the Rue Droit-Mur, it gave sixteen-year-old recluses an imaginary lover; the player turned out to be a blind, penniless old émigré.`,quote:`“My Zétulbé, come reign o’er my soul,”`},{id:`the-little-convent`,term:`The Little Convent`,kind:`lore`,chapter:124,text:`The third building of the Petit-Picpus enclosure, where aged nuns of orders dissolved by the Revolution lived out their days on a small state pension, each keeping her own rule. Hugo calls the medley of black, grey and white habits a 'harlequin convent'.`,quote:`a sort of harlequin convent`},{id:`sainte-aure`,term:`Sainte-Aure`,kind:`history`,chapter:124,text:`A small order of nuns whose convent had occupied the Petit-Picpus house early in the eighteenth century; by 1824 one old nun of the order survived, too poor to wear its white robe and scarlet scapular, which she displayed on a doll. Hugo's note that 'today there remains only a doll' is the whole chapter in miniature.`,quote:`a nun of Sainte-Aure, the only one of her order who had survived`},{id:`madame-de-genlis`,term:`Madame de Genlis`,kind:`history`,chapter:124,text:`Stéphanie-Félicité de Genlis (1746–1830), governess of the Orléans princes and a prolific novelist and moralist, in old age a fierce devotee. The Duc d'Orléans (her former pupil, who became King Louis-Philippe in 1830) got her a cell in the convent about 1820; she left because the garden had no shade.`,quote:`Madame de Genlis had made romances.`},{id:`dismas-and-gestas`,term:`Dismas and Gestas`,kind:`reference`,chapter:124,text:`The traditional names of the two thieves crucified beside Christ, the penitent and the impenitent. Madame de Genlis's Latin verses naming them (with the variant Gesmas) were pasted in her cupboard as a charm against burglars, a superstition Hugo says the Knights Hospitaller shared.`,quote:`Dismas et Gesmas, media est divina potestas`},{id:`lazaretto`,term:`Lazaretto`,kind:`word`,chapter:124,text:`A quarantine station where travellers and goods suspected of plague were isolated. Hugo calls the public door of the convent church a 'lazaretto entrance' because it admits outsiders without letting them come near, or even see, the nuns behind the curtain.`,quote:`The public was even admitted by a sort of lazaretto entrance on the street.`},{id:`marguerite-de-blemeur`,term:`Marguerite de Blemeur`,kind:`history`,chapter:125,text:`A seventeenth-century Benedictine nun and scholar who wrote lives of the saints of her order ('L'Année bénédictine'). The prioress, Mademoiselle de Blemeur in the world and Mother Innocente in religion, comes of the same family and shares the learning.`,quote:`author of Lives of the Saints of the Order of Saint-Benoît`},{id:`names-in-religion`,term:`Names in religion`,kind:`custom`,chapter:125,text:`A nun gives up her family name on profession and takes a new one, here often a mystery of Christ's life (Nativity, Presentation, Assumption) or a saint. Hugo prints both names for each mother, the Mademoiselle she was and the Mother she became, which is why the list reads like a roll of two worlds.`,quote:`Mother Sainte-Mechtilde (Mademoiselle Gauvain)`},{id:`isle-de-bourbon`,term:`Isle de Bourbon`,kind:`place`,chapter:125,text:`The French sugar island in the Indian Ocean now called Réunion. A young nun from there, descended from the Chevalier Roze who fought the Marseilles plague of 1720, is among the 'prettiest' in the house.`,quote:`a charming girl of three and twenty, who was from the Isle de Bourbon`},{id:`ceracchi`,term:`Ceracchi`,kind:`history`,chapter:125,text:`Giuseppe Ceracchi, an Italian sculptor who made busts of Washington and Bonaparte and was guillotined in 1801 for a plot to kill the First Consul. That his sister is a Petit-Picpus nun who went mad is one of Hugo's glimpses of the Revolution's wreckage inside the cloister.`,quote:`Mother Sainte-Céligne (sister of the sculptor Ceracchi), who went mad`},{id:`the-acoustic-telegraph-of-bells`,term:`The acoustic telegraph of bells`,kind:`custom`,chapter:125,text:`Because the rule of silence forbids speech, a loud bell by the portress calls every person and announces every event by a code of strokes: one-one for the prioress, six-five for lessons, nineteen for the opening of the iron 'door of seclusion' when the archbishop comes. The pupils say 'to go to six-five', not 'to go to lessons'.`,quote:`a sort of acoustic telegraph`},{id:`c-est-le-diable-a-quatre`,term:`'C'est le diable à quatre'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:125,text:`A French idiom, 'it's the devil by fours', meaning a great to-do. Madame de Genlis's bell signal was four-four, so the uncharitable had their pun ready whenever it rang.`,quote:`“C’est le diable a quatre,”—it’s the very deuce—said the uncharitable`},{id:`tennis-ground-of-the-eleven-thousand-devils`,term:`Tennis-ground of the eleven thousand devils`,kind:`history`,chapter:126,text:`A jeu de paume, the walled court of the old French game from which tennis descends, that stood on the convent's site from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century. Hugo likes the irony that a house of perpetual prayer was built on the 'eleven thousand devils'.`,quote:`the “tennis-ground of the eleven thousand devils.”`},{id:`a-gibbet-laid-flat`,term:`A gibbet laid flat`,kind:`word`,chapter:126,text:`Hugo describes the convent's ground plan as the shape of a gallows (the French potence, an L or T) lying on the ground: the long arm along the Rue Droit-Mur, the short arm on the Rue Petit-Picpus. The image is chosen; the convent is a place of voluntary execution of the self.`,quote:`outlined, with considerable exactness, a gibbet laid flat on the ground`},{id:`abbey-of-fontevrault`,term:`Abbey of Fontevrault`,kind:`place`,chapter:127,text:`A great medieval abbey in Anjou, unusual in that an abbess ruled both monks and nuns; it was so large that, as the centenarian says, it had streets. Suppressed in the Revolution, it became a prison under Napoleon.`,quote:`She told wonders of the Abbey of Fontevrault—that it was like a city`},{id:`m-de-miromesnil-a-presidentess`,term:`M. de Miromesnil; a Presidentess`,kind:`title`,chapter:127,text:`Miromesnil was Keeper of the Seals (minister of justice) under Louis XVI from 1774 to 1787. A 'Presidentess' (présidente) was the wife of a président of one of the old parlements, the sovereign law courts. Dropping these names is the old nun's way of saying she was once in society.`,quote:`M. de Miromesnil, Keeper of the Seals under Louis XVI and of a Presidentess Duplat`},{id:`the-four-wines`,term:`The four wines`,kind:`custom`,chapter:127,text:`A pre-Revolutionary ceremony of Burgundy and Champagne: a visiting grandee was offered four silver cups labelled monkey wine, lion wine, sheep wine and hog wine, the four stages of drunkenness (merry, quarrelsome, dull, brutish). The centenarian's stories are Hugo's way of letting the eighteenth century speak inside the cloister.`,quote:`monkey wine; on the second, lion wine; on the third, sheep wine; on the fourth, hog wine`},{id:`faenza-platter`,term:`Faenza platter`,kind:`word`,chapter:127,text:`A dish of painted tin-glazed earthenware from Faenza in Italy (the origin of the word 'faience'). The centenarian's secret treasure is a comic one showing cupids chased by apothecary boys with enema syringes, the sort of joke Molière built a play on.`,quote:`It was a Faenza platter representing little Loves flitting away pursued by apothecary lads armed with enormous syringes.`},{id:`the-temple`,term:`The Temple`,kind:`place`,chapter:128,text:`The walled enclosure of the medieval Knights Templar in north-east Paris, which gave its name to the quarter. Its convent of Benedictines of the Perpetual Adoration, with its famous chestnut tree and a comfortable parlour, is Hugo's foil to the grim Petit-Picpus; its tower, where Louis XVI was imprisoned, had been pulled down in 1808.`,quote:`At the convent of the Rue du Temple, in particular`},{id:`catherine-de-bar`,term:`Catherine de Bar`,kind:`history`,chapter:128,text:`Mother Mechtilde of the Blessed Sacrament (1614–1698), the Benedictine nun who founded the order of the Perpetual Adoration in Paris in 1653 after two sacrilegious thefts of the consecrated Host in 1649 scandalised the city. Two noblewomen endowed the first house in the Rue Cassette.`,quote:`Mother Catherine de Bar, called of the Holy Sacrament`},{id:`three-hundred-livres-income`,term:`Three hundred livres income`,kind:`money`,chapter:128,text:`The dowry a woman needed to enter the new order: an income of 300 livres a year, meaning a capital of 6,000 livres at five per cent. The livre was the pre-Revolutionary unit replaced by the franc of roughly equal value; religious houses lived on such dowries.`,quote:`unless she contributed three hundred livres income, which amounts to six thousand livres, to the principal`},{id:`letters-patent-chamber-of-accounts-parliament`,term:`Letters-patent, Chamber of Accounts, Parliament`,kind:`title`,chapter:128,text:`The paperwork of founding a convent under the old monarchy: the king's open letters granting permission, registered by the Chamber of Accounts (which audited royal finances) and the Parlement of Paris (the sovereign court). Hugo traces the order's 'legal consecration' step by step.`,quote:`all the rest, abbatial charter, and royal letters, was confirmed in 1654 by the Chamber of Accounts and the Parliament`},{id:`a-papal-brief`,term:`A papal brief`,kind:`word`,chapter:128,text:`A short letter of the Pope, less formal than a bull, granting a favour or permission. Alexander VII's brief of 1657 allowed the Petit-Picpus nuns to adopt the Perpetual Adoration while remaining a separate order.`,quote:`Pope Alexander VII had authorized, by a special brief`},{id:`volaverunt`,term:`Volaverunt`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:129,text:`Latin, 'they have flown away'. Hugo uses it for the vanished schoolgirls of the Petit-Picpus; it is also the title of one of Goya's Caprichos, a woman borne off through the air.`,quote:`the latter had taken their departure. Volaverunt.`},{id:`hic-jaceo-vixi-annos-viginti-et-tres`,term:`'Hic jaceo. Vixi annos viginti et tres'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:129,text:`'Here I lie. I lived twenty-three years': the epitaph of Julia Alpinula, a young Roman priestess said to have died of grief after failing to save her father. The inscription was a Renaissance forgery, but it was famous, and Hugo gives it to a nun dead at twenty-three.`,quote:`“Hic jaceo. Vixi annos viginti et tres.”`},{id:`hortus-conclusus`,term:`Hortus conclusus`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:129,text:`Latin, 'a garden enclosed', from the Song of Songs ('A garden enclosed is my sister, my spouse'), long applied to the Virgin and to the cloister. Hugo uses it to name the closed world he has just described.`,quote:`It is the closed garden, hortus conclusus.`},{id:`joseph-de-maistre`,term:`Joseph de Maistre`,kind:`history`,chapter:129,text:`The Savoyard counter-revolutionary philosopher (1753–1821) whose 'Soirées de Saint-Pétersbourg' praises the executioner as the keystone of social order. Hugo sets himself between de Maistre's 'hosanna' and Voltaire's sneer: respecting the convent without approving it.`,quote:`the hosanna of Joseph de Maistre, who wound up by anointing the executioner`},{id:`voltaire-and-calas`,term:`Voltaire and Calas`,kind:`history`,chapter:129,text:`Jean Calas, a Protestant merchant of Toulouse, was broken on the wheel in 1762 on a false charge of murdering his son; Voltaire's campaign cleared his name and made him the emblem of judicial fanaticism. Hugo's point is that the defender of Calas would have defended Jesus, 'the assassinated sage'.`,quote:`Voltaire would have defended Jesus as he defended Calas`},{id:`kislar-aga-odalisque-seraglio`,term:`Kislar-aga, odalisque, seraglio`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:131,text:`Terms of the Ottoman harem: the seraglio is the sultan's palace of women, an odalisque a woman kept in it, the kislar-aga the chief black eunuch who guards it. Hugo uses them for the Spanish convent, with the archbishop as eunuch-guardian of 'souls reserved for God'.`,quote:`The archbishop, the kislar-aga of heaven, locked up and kept watch over this seraglio of souls`},{id:`in-pace`,term:`In pace`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:131,text:`A monastic dungeon, from the formula vade in pace ('go in peace') said over a monk or nun walled up for life. Hugo describes the ones he saw himself at the ruined abbey of Villers near Brussels: stone boxes half under water, too short to lie in and too low to stand in.`,quote:`The in pace replaced the leather sack.`},{id:`the-leather-sack`,term:`The leather sack`,kind:`custom`,chapter:131,text:`The Ottoman punishment of sewing a condemned woman of the harem into a sack and throwing her into the Bosphorus. Hugo's 'monstrous parallel' is that the East drowned what the West buried alive.`,quote:`That which was cast into the sea in the East was thrown into the ground in the West.`},{id:`calas-labarre-sirven`,term:`Calas, Labarre, Sirven`,kind:`history`,chapter:131,text:`The three great judicial causes Voltaire fought in the 1760s: Calas executed for a murder he did not commit, the Chevalier de La Barre beheaded at nineteen for impiety, the Protestant Sirven condemned for his daughter's death. Hugo names them against those who call such protests mere 'declamations'.`,quote:`Voltaire on Calas, Labarre, and Sirven, declaimers`},{id:`that-poor-holofernes`,term:`'That poor Holofernes'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:131,text:`The Assyrian general beheaded by Judith in the Book of Judith. Pitying him, like calling Nero a victim or Tacitus a declaimer, is Hugo's parody of the fashionable rehabilitation of tyrants.`,quote:`pity is decidedly due to “that poor Holofernes.”`},{id:`phthisis`,term:`Phthisis`,kind:`word`,chapter:132,text:`The old medical name for consumption, tuberculosis of the lungs, a wasting disease. Hugo calls monasticism 'a sort of phthisis for civilization': it depopulates and stops life short.`,quote:`is a sort of phthisis for civilization`},{id:`bos-cretatus`,term:`Bos cretatus`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:132,text:`Latin, 'the chalked ox'. Roman soothsayers who lacked a white victim rubbed a black heifer with chalk; Hugo's image for restorers who whitewash the past under names like 'social order' and 'legitimacy'.`,quote:`“She is white, Bos cretatus.”`},{id:`fakirs-bonzes-santons-marabouts-talapoins-dervishes`,term:`Fakirs, bonzes, santons, marabouts, talapoins, dervishes`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:132,text:`Holy men of other religions: Hindu and Muslim ascetics (fakirs), Buddhist monks of China and Japan (bonzes), Muslim hermits of Turkey and North Africa (santons, marabouts), Buddhist monks of Siam (talapoins), Sufi mendicants (dervishes). Hugo's quarrel is with ascetic seclusion everywhere, not with Catholic convents alone.`,quote:`fakirs, bonzes, santons, Greek monks, marabouts, talapoins, and dervishes`},{id:`the-city-of-89-and-of-1830-and-of-1848`,term:`The city of '89 and of 1830 and of 1848`,kind:`history`,chapter:132,text:`Paris named by its three revolutions: 1789, the July Revolution of 1830 that replaced Charles X with Louis-Philippe, and February 1848 that made the Second Republic. Hugo writes from after all three; a cloister in such a city is, he says, an anachronism.`,quote:`in the very heart of the city of ’89 and of 1830 and of 1848`},{id:`equality-fraternity-liberty`,term:`Equality, Fraternity ... Liberty`,kind:`reference`,chapter:133,text:`The three words of the republican motto. Hugo argues that a monastery already realises two of them (all equal, all brothers) and lacks only the third; add liberty, that is, make entry and exit free, and the cloister becomes a republic.`,quote:`The monastery is the product of the formula: Equality, Fraternity. Oh! how grand is liberty!`},{id:`a-metaphysical-school-of-the-north`,term:`A metaphysical school of the North`,kind:`reference`,chapter:135,text:`German idealist philosophy, which put 'Will' at the root of things (Schopenhauer's 'The World as Will and Idea' is the obvious target). Hugo's objection is that a school willing to say 'the plant wills' while denying a will in the universe has its logic backwards.`,quote:`replacing the word Force with the word Will`},{id:`nihilism`,term:`Nihilism`,kind:`word`,chapter:135,text:`The doctrine that nothing is real or knowable, so that everything is 'a mental conception'. The word was new in 1862, popularised that year by Turgenev's 'Fathers and Sons'; Hugo answers it with 'Zero does not exist. Everything is something.'`,quote:`The negation of the infinite leads straight to nihilism.`},{id:`eden-into-a-lyceum`,term:`Eden into a Lyceum`,kind:`reference`,chapter:135,text:`The Lyceum was Aristotle's school in Athens. Hugo wants philosophy to turn man from a creature of happiness (Adam, Eden) into one of wisdom (Socrates, Marcus Aurelius): the garden of innocence should become a school.`,quote:`Eden should be changed into a Lyceum.`},{id:`caiphas-draco-trimalcion-tiberius`,term:`Caiphas, Draco, Trimalcion, Tiberius`,kind:`reference`,chapter:136,text:`Four types of bad authority: Caiaphas the high priest who condemned Jesus, Draco the Athenian whose laws were 'written in blood', Trimalchio the vulgar millionaire of Petronius's Satyricon (here made a legislator), and the tyrant-emperor Tiberius. Fighting these, Hugo says, is the plain duty of history and philosophy.`,quote:`to combat Caiphas the High-priest, Draco the Lawgiver, Trimalcion the Legislator, Tiberius the Emperor`},{id:`cenobitism`,term:`Cenobitism`,kind:`word`,chapter:136,text:`Life in a religious community under a common rule, as opposed to the hermit's solitude; from the Greek for 'common life'. Hugo treats the right to live apart in this way as a real human problem, not a joke.`,quote:`Cenobitism is a human problem.`},{id:`post-obit`,term:`Post obit`,kind:`word`,chapter:136,text:`A loan repayable only after someone's death, usually against an expected inheritance. Hugo's financial metaphors (a bill drawn on death, hell accepted as a post-obit on paradise) describe the convent as a bargain that pays out only beyond the grave.`,quote:`hell is accepted in advance as a post obit on paradise`},{id:`thales`,term:`Thales`,kind:`reference`,chapter:137,text:`The Greek thinker of the sixth century BC counted as the first philosopher. The legend that he stood motionless for four years is Hugo's proof that contemplation is labour: 'folded arms toil, clasped hands work'.`,quote:`Thales remained motionless for four years. He founded philosophy.`},{id:`the-abbe-de-la-trappe-replies-to-horace`,term:`The Abbé de la Trappe replies to Horace`,kind:`reference`,chapter:137,text:`Rancé, the seventeenth-century reformer of the Trappist monks, whose rule meditates constantly on death, set against the Roman poet of 'seize the day'. Hugo's 'We must die' is the monk's answer to the poet, and he says sage and ascetic agree on it.`,quote:`“We must die.” The Abbé de la Trappe replies to Horace.`},{id:`deo-erexit-voltaire`,term:`Deo erexit Voltaire`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:137,text:`'Voltaire erected this to God', the inscription the great sceptic put on the church he built at Ferney in 1761, pointedly dedicating it to God rather than to a saint. For Hugo it shows that even the mocker of religions could adore.`,quote:`Leibnitz praying is grand, Voltaire adoring is fine. Deo erexit Voltaire.`},{id:`bonapartism-in-the-northern-peasantry`,term:`Bonapartism in the northern peasantry`,kind:`history`,chapter:138,text:`Hugo's remark that peasants of Picardy and the north kept 'an old fund of Bonapartism': loyalty to the Emperor who had ended feudal dues and filled the armies with their sons. Fauchelevent is pleased rather than shocked to think M. Madeleine may be in hiding for a political affair.`,quote:`had an old fund of Bonapartism about him`},{id:`the-prayers-of-the-forty-hours`,term:`The prayers of the forty hours`,kind:`custom`,chapter:138,text:`A devotion of continuous prayer before the exposed Host for forty hours, the time Christ's body lay in the tomb, here offered for a dying nun. With the whole community absorbed in it, no one is looking toward the gardener's hut.`,quote:`The prayers of the forty hours are being said.`},{id:`the-knell`,term:`The knell`,kind:`custom`,chapter:138,text:`The convent's death-bell: one stroke a minute for twenty-four hours, from the death until the body leaves the church. Fauchelevent reads every peal of the house, and interprets for Valjean each stage of 'the ceremony of dying' as it rings.`,quote:`The bell will continue to strike once a minute for twenty-four hours`},{id:`doctor-of-the-dead`,term:`Doctor of the dead`,kind:`title`,chapter:138,text:`The médecin des morts, a physician employed by the municipality to view every corpse and certify death before burial was permitted. He is the one man besides the gardener who may enter the convent's dead-room, and the nuns dislike his visit because 'he lifts the veil'.`,quote:`the dead-doctor is to come here and view a corpse`},{id:`de-profundis`,term:`De profundis`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:138,text:`'Out of the depths', the opening of Psalm 130, the psalm sung at burials. Fauchelevent ends his account of how a nun goes to heaven in a hearse with the words a priest says over a grave.`,quote:`That’s what a burial is like. De profundis.`},{id:`dom-mabillon-and-merlonus-horstius`,term:`Dom Mabillon and Merlonus Horstius`,kind:`history`,chapter:140,text:`Jean Mabillon (1632–1707), the great Benedictine scholar who founded the critical study of documents and edited Saint Bernard, and Horstius, an earlier editor who printed fewer of Bernard's letters. The prioress's point, that one does not despise the lesser worker, is her way of telling Fauchelevent to manage with a woman's help.`,quote:`Dom Mabillon gives four hundred and seventeen epistles of Saint Bernard, while Merlonus Horstius only gives three hundred and sixty-seven`},{id:`jansenist`,term:`Jansenist`,kind:`history`,chapter:140,text:`A follower of the austere Catholic movement, centred on Port-Royal, that stressed predestination and was condemned by Rome; by 1824 it was a stubborn minority. A Jansenist lady 'turned orthodox' merely by seeing Mother Crucifixion at prayer is offered as proof of the dead nun's sanctity.`,quote:`Madame de Béthune, a Jansenist, turned orthodox`},{id:`cardinal-de-berulle-dying-at-mass`,term:`Cardinal de Bérulle dying at mass`,kind:`reference`,chapter:140,text:`Pierre de Bérulle, founder of the French Oratory, died in 1629 while saying mass, at the words 'Hanc igitur oblationem' ('This offering, therefore'). The prioress ranks Mother Crucifixion's death just below this ideal.`,quote:`while pronouncing these words: Hanc igitur oblationem`},{id:`pius-vii-who-crowned-the-emp-bonaparte`,term:`Pius VII, 'who crowned the Emp—Bonaparte'`,kind:`history`,chapter:140,text:`Pope from 1800 to 1823, Pius VII came to Paris to crown Napoleon in 1804 and was later held prisoner by him. Fauchelevent's slip, catching 'Emperor' and correcting to 'Bonaparte', is a Bonapartist's reflex in a royalist house.`,quote:`“The one who crowned the Emp—Bonaparte.”`},{id:`acarus-mezzocane-terentius-bernard-guidonis`,term:`Acarus, Mezzocane, Terentius, Bernard Guidonis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:140,text:`The prioress's precedents for obeying a dead saint's wish about burial: a bishop whose tomb says only 'worm', an abbot buried under the gallows, a bishop who asked to be spat on, and Bernard Gui the Dominican inquisitor carried from Spain to Limoges. Real and doubtful lore mixed, cited from obscure chroniclers; Hugo is enjoying her erudition and her obstinacy at once.`,quote:`Acarus, which signifies, a worm of the earth`},{id:`stat-crux-dum-volvitur-orbis`,term:`Stat crux dum volvitur orbis`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:140,text:`'The cross stands while the world turns', the motto of the Carthusian order. The prioress answers every mention of the police inspector with a Latin tag, and Fauchelevent answers every Latin tag with 'Amen'.`,quote:`Stat crux dum volvitur orbis.`},{id:`saint-bernard-s-career`,term:`Saint Bernard's career`,kind:`reference`,chapter:140,text:`The prioress's torrent is accurate: Bernard of Clairvaux founded scores of monasteries, defeated the philosopher Abelard at the council of Sens in 1140, denounced the monk Raoul who incited massacres of Jews, advised popes and kings, wrote the rule of the Templars and preached the Second Crusade. 'Two hundred and fifty miracles' is her own addition.`,quote:`he overthrew Abeilard at the council of Sens in 1140`},{id:`father-coton-and-henri-iv-s-oath`,term:`Father Coton and Henri IV's oath`,kind:`reference`,chapter:140,text:`Pierre Coton, Jesuit confessor to Henri IV, is remembered because the king, told not to swear 'je renie Dieu' (I deny God), substituted 'jarnicoton', a nonsense oath on the priest's name. The prioress complains that people know this and not that Coton helped found the Oratory.`,quote:`because he furnished Henri IV, the Huguenot king, with the material for an oath`},{id:`saint-martin-s-cloak`,term:`Saint Martin's cloak`,kind:`reference`,chapter:140,text:`Martin of Tours, the fourth-century soldier-saint, cut his cloak in two to clothe a beggar at Amiens, the best-known act of charity in French hagiography. The prioress sets it against the scandal of bad bishops.`,quote:`Martin de Tours from being a saint, and giving half of his cloak to a beggar`},{id:`voltaire-and-cesar-de-bus`,term:`Voltaire and César de Bus`,kind:`history`,chapter:140,text:`César de Bus (1544–1607) founded the Fathers of Christian Doctrine to teach catechism to the poor; Voltaire needs no introduction. That the world knows the mocker and not the teacher is, for the prioress, the whole indictment of the age.`,quote:`The name of Voltaire is known, but not the name of César de Bus.`},{id:`the-emperor-henry-ii-the-saint-and-the-lame`,term:`The Emperor Henry II, the Saint and the Lame`,kind:`history`,chapter:140,text:`Henry II of Germany (died 1024), the only German emperor canonised, who fought an antipope and restored Benedict VIII; he was also lame. The prioress offers him as comfort to the limping gardener, who hears 'two surtouts' (overcoats) and agrees.`,quote:`has two surnames, the Saint and the Lame`},{id:`the-sanitary-commission`,term:`The sanitary commission`,kind:`title`,chapter:140,text:`The municipal public-health authority that, with the commissary of police and the prefecture, regulated burials in Paris and forbade interment inside churches and convents. Fauchelevent raises each in turn; the prioress dismisses them all as 'a revolutionary invention'.`,quote:`Your sanitary department is a revolutionary invention.`},{id:`austin-castillejo-and-charles-v`,term:`Austin Castillejo and Charles V`,kind:`reference`,chapter:141,text:`Hugo cites a monk Castillejo for the tale that the Emperor Charles V, after abdicating in 1556 to the monastery of Yuste, had a woman he loved ('the Plombes') smuggled in and out in a coffin. No such source is known; the point is that Valjean's convict's trick has, Hugo claims, an imperial precedent.`,quote:`If we are to credit the monk Austin Castillejo`},{id:`gimlet`,term:`Gimlet`,kind:`word`,chapter:141,text:`A small hand tool for boring holes in wood. Valjean asks for a few gimlet holes near his mouth and the lid nailed on loosely, the practical details of being buried alive on purpose.`,quote:`You surely must have a gimlet, you will make a few holes here and there`},{id:`au-bon-coing`,term:`Au Bon Coing`,kind:`place`,chapter:141,text:`'The Good Quince', the wine-shop beside the Vaugirard cemetery with a quince painted on its sign, one wall on the drinkers' tables and the other on the tombs. Fauchelevent's whole plan rests on getting the gravedigger inside it before the gates close.`,quote:`‘Come and drink a bout while the Bon Coing is open.’`},{id:`the-gravedigger-s-card`,term:`The gravedigger's card`,kind:`lore`,chapter:142,text:`A permit issued by the department of public funerals that let a gravedigger out of a Paris cemetery after the gates shut at sunset: he dropped it in the porter's box and the small gate opened. Without it he must be identified and fined fifteen francs. The Vaugirard, where the nuns were buried at nightfall, ran on this card.`,quote:`his gravedigger’s card furnished by the department of public funerals`},{id:`porte-cavaliere-porte-pietonne`,term:`Porte cavalière, porte piétonne`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:142,text:`The carriage gate and the foot gate, the old names the quarter kept for the cemetery's two entrances beside the keeper's lodge built by Perronet, the engineer of the Pont de la Concorde. The names matter because the gates shut at sunset, and getting out afterwards needs a permit.`,quote:`still called the porte cavalière and the porte piétonne`},{id:`pere-lachaise`,term:`Père-Lachaise`,kind:`place`,chapter:142,text:`The great landscaped cemetery opened in 1804 on the eastern heights of Paris, which by the 1820s was the fashionable place to be buried. To be buried at the Vaugirard 'hinted at poverty'; Père-Lachaise 'is equivalent to having furniture of mahogany'.`,quote:`to be buried in Père-Lachaise is equivalent to having furniture of mahogany`},{id:`after-napoleon-louis-xviii`,term:`'After Napoleon, Louis XVIII'`,kind:`history`,chapter:142,text:`Gribier's grim joke on succession: as the king replaced the emperor in 1814, so he replaces Father Mestienne. It is the first blow to Fauchelevent, whose plan assumed a drunkard he knew.`,quote:`“After Napoleon, Louis XVIII. After Mestienne, Gribier.`},{id:`surene-and-argenteuil-wine`,term:`Surêne and Argenteuil wine`,kind:`word`,chapter:142,text:`Cheap, sour red wines from villages just outside Paris, the drink of the barriers; Fauchelevent calls a jug of Suresnes 'little Father Lenoir' and offers Argenteuil at six sous. Gribier, who never drinks, is unmoved by either.`,quote:`It is a jug of Surêne, morbigou!`},{id:`public-writer`,term:`Public writer`,kind:`title`,chapter:142,text:`An écrivain public kept a stall where he wrote letters for people who could not; Gribier's is in the Umbrella Market in the Rue de Sèvres, where the cooks of the Croix-Rouge quarter dictate love letters to soldiers. 'In the morning I write love letters; in the evening I dig graves.'`,quote:`But I am still a public writer.`},{id:`the-prytaneum`,term:`The Prytaneum`,kind:`history`,chapter:142,text:`The Prytanée, the military college founded under the Revolution and Napoleon (at Saint-Cyr, then La Flèche) for sons of officers. Gribier's father was its porter and 'destined him for literature' before losing his money on the exchange.`,quote:`My father was a porter at the Prytaneum.`},{id:`the-quarter-of-an-hour-of-rabelais`,term:`The quarter of an hour of Rabelais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:142,text:`The moment of paying the bill, from the story that Rabelais, penniless at an inn, got himself arrested as a poisoner to be carried to Paris free. Fauchelevent offers drinks but leaves the question of who pays carefully dark.`,quote:`the old gardener left the proverbial quarter of an hour named after Rabelais in the dark`},{id:`the-burial-prayers`,term:`The burial prayers`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:143,text:`What Valjean hears through the planks: 'Those who sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life and some to shame' (Daniel 12:2); 'Eternal rest give unto him, O Lord' / 'And let perpetual light shine upon him'; 'May he rest in peace'. The holy water pattering on the lid follows.`,quote:`Requiem aeternam dona ei, Domine.`},{id:`conscript`,term:`Conscript`,kind:`word`,chapter:144,text:`A raw recruit; Fauchelevent's name for the new gravedigger once he has the upper hand, as Gribier called him 'peasant' and 'villager'. The fine of fifteen francs, 'three pieces of a hundred sous', is more than a labourer earned in a week.`,quote:`“Ah, come now, conscript,” said Fauchelevent, “none of this despair.`},{id:`picquigny`,term:`Picquigny`,kind:`place`,chapter:145,text:`A small town on the Somme near Amiens, in Picardy, Fauchelevent's own country. He gives it as the birthplace of his 'brother' Ultime, borrowing the name of a real brother who had died.`,quote:`“From Picquigny, near Amiens.”`},{id:`ultime-fauchelevent`,term:`Ultime Fauchelevent`,kind:`lore`,chapter:145,text:`The name under which Valjean is admitted to the convent as assistant gardener: that of Fauchelevent's dead brother, so the papers would hold if anyone looked. The nuns never use it, calling him 'the other Fauvent'.`,quote:`His name was Ultime Fauchelevent.`},{id:`eluded-caesar-and-satisfied-god`,term:`'Eluded Caesar and satisfied God'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:145,text:`From 'Render unto Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and unto God the things that are God's' (Matthew 22:21). Hugo's verdict on the double burial: the state's rules were dodged, the dead nun's wish fulfilled, and 'no one was aware of it'.`,quote:`eluded Caesar and satisfied God`},{id:`m-de-latil-monsieur-s-confessor`,term:`M. de Latil, Monsieur's confessor`,kind:`history`,chapter:145,text:`'Monsieur' is the title of the king's eldest brother, here the Comte d'Artois, who became Charles X in 1824. Jean-Baptiste de Latil, his confessor, became Archbishop of Reims and crowned him there in 1825, and was made a cardinal.`,quote:`M. de Latil, Monsieur’s confessor, afterwards Archbishop of Reims and Cardinal`},{id:`leo-xii-della-genga`,term:`Leo XII (Della Genga)`,kind:`history`,chapter:145,text:`Pope from 1823 to 1829, Annibale della Genga, a conservative who favoured the religious orders. Hugo's 'note' in which the Pope praises 'an excellent gardener, who is also a holy man, named Fauvent' is a comic invention capping the chapter.`,quote:`the then reigning Pope, Leo XII`},{id:`the-illustrated-london-news`,term:`The Illustrated London News`,kind:`reference`,chapter:145,text:`The English pictorial weekly, founded in 1842, which printed engravings of prize cattle. Fauchelevent knowing nothing of his fame in Rome is like a Durham bull ignorant of its own portrait; the comparison is deliberately out of its time.`,quote:`whose portrait is published in the London Illustrated News`},{id:`charity-pupil`,term:`Charity pupil`,kind:`custom`,chapter:145,text:`A girl taken into a fee-paying convent school without payment, as an act of charity and in hope of a vocation. The prioress's judgement that Cosette 'will grow up ugly' decides it: plain girls, Hugo observes, make nuns more readily than pretty ones.`,quote:`gave her a place in the school as a charity pupil`},{id:`vendean-paper-money-a-chouan`,term:`Vendean paper money; a Chouan`,kind:`history`,chapter:146,text:`In 1793 the royalist peasants of the Vendée, in western France, rose against the Republic and issued their own notes 'in the king's name'; the Chouans were their guerrilla allies in Brittany and Maine. The old gardener who nailed such a note over the chimney was one of these royalist rebels, ending his days in a convent.`,quote:`This specimen of Vendean paper money had been nailed to the wall by the preceding gardener, an old Chouan`},{id:`camphor`,term:`Camphor`,kind:`word`,chapter:146,text:`A strong-smelling resin used to keep moths from stored cloth. Valjean packs Cosette's little mourning suit, stockings and shoes in camphor and convent aromatics in a locked valise; she asks what is in the box that smells so good.`,quote:`with a quantity of camphor and all the aromatics in which convents abound`},{id:`grafting-and-budding`,term:`Grafting and budding`,kind:`word`,chapter:146,text:`Joining a shoot or bud of a good fruit variety onto a wild rootstock so that the tree bears the better fruit. Valjean, once a tree-pruner in Faverolles, grafts the convent's wild orchard; it is honest skilled work and his way of being useful.`,quote:`He budded them and made them produce excellent fruit.`},{id:`expiation-for-others`,term:`Expiation for others`,kind:`reference`,chapter:146,text:`The Christian idea that the innocent may take on suffering to atone for the guilty, as Christ did; the nuns' 'reparation' for the sins of the world is its daily practice. Valjean, who understands a convict's expiation for himself, is stopped short by this other kind, and it humbles him.`,quote:`“The most divine of human generosities, the expiation for others.”`},{id:`gamin`,term:`Gamin`,kind:`word`,chapter:147,text:`The Paris street boy: a child of seven to thirteen who lives more in the street than at home, ragged, cheeky, quick and free. The word was still new and slangy when Hugo wrote, and Hapgood keeps it, sometimes adding 'street arab', the English equivalent of the day. Book I of this volume is a portrait of the type rather than of any one child.`,quote:`the child is called the gamin`},{id:`homuncio`,term:`Homuncio`,kind:`reference`,chapter:147,text:`Latin, 'little man', a word used by the Roman comic playwright Plautus. Hugo likes to dress the gutter child in classical tags: the effect is half mock-heroic and half tender. He keeps calling the gamin 'the homuncio' in the chapters that follow.`,quote:`Homuncio, Plautus would say.`},{id:`calls-gay-women-thou`,term:`Calls gay women 'thou'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:147,text:`French has two words for 'you': the polite vous and the familiar tu ('thou'), kept for family, close friends, children and inferiors. A small boy who says tu to prostitutes ('gay women' in Victorian English) is claiming to be their equal and their pal. Hapgood often renders tu as 'thou' to keep the nuance.`,quote:`calls gay women “thou,”`},{id:`street-arab`,term:`Street arab`,kind:`word`,chapter:148,text:`Victorian English for a homeless or half-wild city child, from the idea of the wandering Bedouin. Hapgood uses it as a gloss on gamin; to Hugo's readers the French word carried more cheek and less pity.`,quote:`The gamin—the street arab—of Paris is the dwarf of the giant.`},{id:`to-eat-dandelions-by-the-root`,term:`'To eat dandelions by the root'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:148,text:`A translation of the French slang manger les pissenlits par la racine, 'to be dead and buried', still in use today, much like English 'pushing up daisies'. It is the first of the many street sayings Hugo collects in this book.`,quote:`to be dead is “to eat dandelions by the root;”`},{id:`making-the-bridge-of-arts`,term:`'Making the bridge of arts'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:148,text:`A pun on the Pont des Arts, the footbridge across the Seine by the Louvre, which charged a toll. In heavy rain gamins laid planks across flooded gutters and charged passers-by a sou to cross: their own toll bridge.`,quote:`which he calls “making the bridge of arts,”`},{id:`loques`,term:`Loques`,kind:`word`,chapter:148,text:`French for 'rags'. Here it is the gamins' private currency, scraps of worked copper picked up in the street, which pass from hand to hand at a fixed value among the children.`,quote:`This curious money, which receives the name of loques—rags`},{id:`talleyrand`,term:`Talleyrand`,kind:`history`,chapter:148,text:`Charles-Maurice de Talleyrand (1754–1838), the bishop turned diplomat who served the Revolution, Napoleon, the Bourbons and Louis-Philippe in turn. He was famous for cynical one-liners. Hugo gives the gamin as many sayings as Talleyrand, and more honesty.`,quote:`this child has as many of them as Talleyrand`},{id:`titi`,term:`Titi`,kind:`word`,chapter:149,text:`Parisian slang for a gamin at the theatre, perched in the cheapest seats under the roof. Hugo makes him the winged form of the street boy, as the moth is of the larva. The word survives as the name of the cheeky Paris urchin type.`,quote:`he was the street arab, he becomes the titi`},{id:`paradise-the-theatre-gallery`,term:`Paradise (the theatre gallery)`,kind:`custom`,chapter:149,text:`The highest, cheapest gallery of a theatre, under the roof, was called le paradis in French, as 'the gods' in English. Hugo plays on the name: the stuffy, filthy 'keel' of the upturned ship becomes heaven because the children are happy there.`,quote:`the name of Paradise`},{id:`mademoiselle-mars`,term:`Mademoiselle Mars`,kind:`history`,chapter:149,text:`Anne Boutet, called Mademoiselle Mars (1779–1847), the leading comic actress of the Comédie-Française for thirty years, admired for her elegance and diction. The gamins' nickname 'Muche' plays on se musser (to hide), mocking her as she aged in young roles.`,quote:`the popularity of Mademoiselle Mars`},{id:`matanturlurette`,term:`Matanturlurette`,kind:`word`,chapter:149,text:`A nonsense refrain from old popular songs, like 'fol-de-rol'. Setting it beside 'Alleluia', as the Jack-pudding (the fairground clown) beside the De Profundis, is Hugo's way of saying the gamin mixes the sacred and the comic without blinking.`,quote:`tempers Alleluia with Matanturlurette`},{id:`adamastor`,term:`Adamastor`,kind:`reference`,chapter:149,text:`The giant spirit of the Cape of Good Hope in Camões's Portuguese epic 'The Lusiads' (1572), who rises from the storm to warn Vasco da Gama. Hugo's point: faced with an epic monster, the street boy would just call it a bogeyman.`,quote:`If Adamastor were to appear to him`},{id:`prudhomme-and-fouillou`,term:`Prudhomme and Fouillou`,kind:`reference`,chapter:150,text:`Joseph Prudhomme is the pompous, platitudinous bourgeois invented by the caricaturist Henry Monnier in the 1830s, a byword for smug respectability. Fouillou stands for the poking, rummaging street child (fouiller means to rummage). Hugo pairs them as the two poles of Paris: the lounger who looks on and the gamin who acts.`,quote:`Prudhomme and Fouillou`},{id:`currit-rota-the-latin-potter`,term:`Currit rota; the Latin potter`,kind:`reference`,chapter:150,text:`From Horace's 'Art of Poetry': a potter sets out to make an amphora, but as the wheel turns (currit rota) out comes a mere jug. Hugo reverses the line: Paris starts with a jug of common clay and turns out an amphora. Ionian and Boeotian are the old Greek types of the refined and the dull.`,quote:`currit rota, the Spirit of Paris`},{id:`urbis-amator-ruris-amator`,term:`Urbis amator, ruris amator`,kind:`reference`,chapter:151,text:`Horace opens one of his Epistles by greeting his friend Fuscus, 'lover of the city', from himself, Flaccus, 'lover of the country'. Hugo gives the gamin both loves, which leads him into the waste ground around Paris.`,quote:`Urbis amator, like Fuscus; ruris amator, like Flaccus`},{id:`banlieue`,term:`Banlieue`,kind:`word`,chapter:151,text:`The ring of half-rural land and villages just outside a French city's limits, beyond the toll barriers. Hugo calls it the 'limbo' of Paris, neither town nor country: quarries, market gardens, waste ground and cheap tea-gardens, where runaway children play.`,quote:`the banlieue of Paris is another`},{id:`the-wall-of-grenelle-speckled-with-balls`,term:`The wall of Grenelle 'speckled with balls'`,kind:`place`,chapter:151,text:`The plain of Grenelle, south-west of the city by the Champ-de-Mars, was where military executions were carried out by firing squad; the wall behind the condemned was pocked with bullet marks. Hugo lists it among the bleak landmarks of the outskirts he roamed as a young man.`,quote:`the hideous wall of Grenelle all speckled with balls`},{id:`half-farthings`,term:`Half-farthings`,kind:`money`,chapter:151,text:`Hapgood's English for the liard, the smallest copper coin of old France, worth a quarter of a sou. It was already out of use by the 1820s. The children quarrelling over them are quarrelling over almost nothing.`,quote:`quarrelling over half-farthings`},{id:`the-swallows-of-the-bridge-of-arcola`,term:`'The swallows of the bridge of Arcola'`,kind:`history`,chapter:152,text:`A notorious band of homeless children who slept under the arches of the Pont d'Arcole, by the Hôtel de Ville, and lived by petty theft; their case caused a scandal in the 1840s. Hugo cites them as the darker side of the Paris street child.`,quote:`“the swallows of the bridge of Arcola.”`},{id:`colbert-and-the-galley-slaves`,term:`Colbert and the galley-slaves`,kind:`history`,chapter:152,text:`Jean-Baptiste Colbert, Louis XIV's great minister, built up the French navy in the 1660s, and the Mediterranean galleys needed rowers. Courts were urged to sentence as many men to the oar as possible: Protestants (Huguenots) for refusing a Catholic gesture such as baring the head to a procession, and vagrant boys of fifteen.`,quote:`Colbert had the commissioners of provinces and the parliaments make as many convicts as possible.`},{id:`the-king-s-baths-of-purple`,term:`The king's baths of purple`,kind:`history`,chapter:152,text:`In 1750 a rumour ran through Paris that police were kidnapping children so that a sick Louis XV could bathe in their blood; riots broke out and several people were hanged. The lawyer Edmond Barbier recorded it in his journal. The exempts were police officers of the old regime.`,quote:`the king’s baths of purple. Barbier speaks ingenuously of these things`},{id:`claude-gueux`,term:`Claude Gueux`,kind:`reference`,chapter:153,text:`A short story Hugo himself published in 1834, about a starving workman jailed for theft who kills a cruel prison director and is guillotined. Hugo is drily claiming that the word gamin first got into print through his own pen.`,quote:`It is in a little work entitled “Claude Gueux” that this word made its appearance.`},{id:`samson-and-the-abbe-montes`,term:`Samson and the Abbé Montès`,kind:`history`,chapter:153,text:`Samson was the family of hereditary Paris executioners who worked the guillotine from Louis XVI to the 1840s; the Abbé Montès was the prison chaplain who rode with the condemned in the cart. To the gamin they are celebrities, the stars of the show at the Place de Grève.`,quote:`Samson and the Abbé Montès are the truly popular names`},{id:`lacenaire-papavoine-and-the-condemned`,term:`Lacenaire, Papavoine and the condemned`,kind:`history`,chapter:153,text:`Hugo's roll-call of men guillotined in Paris between about 1820 and 1840, as the street boys remember them. Lacenaire was a poet-murderer who became a romantic celebrity; Papavoine killed two children in 1824; Bories was one of the 'four sergeants of La Rochelle' executed for a political plot in 1822, hence the confusion of 'politicians' with assassins.`,quote:`In the brotherhood of gamins Voltaire is not known, but Papavoine is.`},{id:`epiphonema`,term:`Epiphonema`,kind:`word`,chapter:153,text:`A rhetorical term for a striking exclamation that sums up what came before. Hugo uses the learned word, tongue in cheek, for a street boy's wish to see someone fall from a fifth-floor window.`,quote:`a profound epiphonema`},{id:`louis-philippe-and-the-pear`,term:`Louis-Philippe and the pear`,kind:`history`,chapter:154,text:`In 1831 the caricaturist Charles Philipon drew King Louis-Philippe's heavy, jowly face turning by stages into a pear, and the pear became the opposition's emblem; street boys chalked pears on every wall. In Hugo's anecdote the king himself finishes a child's pear and pays him a louis, with a pun on the king's face being on the coin too.`,quote:`from 1830 to 1848, he scrawled pears on the walls`},{id:`evohe`,term:`Evohe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:154,text:`Evoe, the ecstatic cry of the worshippers of Bacchus in Greek ritual. Hugo mock-solemnly hears it, and the chants of the Panathenaea (Athens's great festival), in the gamins' warning cry when the police come for the boys bathing in the Seine.`,quote:`one encounters again the ancient Evohe`},{id:`tantalus`,term:`Tantalus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:154,text:`In Greek myth, a king punished in the underworld by standing in water that drains away when he stoops to drink, under fruit that the wind lifts out of reach. 'To play Tantalus' is to long for something forever just out of reach: here, overthrowing the government and getting one's trousers mended.`,quote:`There are two things to which he plays Tantalus`},{id:`the-papal-nuncio`,term:`The Papal Nuncio`,kind:`title`,chapter:154,text:`The Pope's ambassador to a foreign court, resident in Paris in the Rue de l'Université. The anticlerical gamin thumbs his nose at the gate simply because 'there is a curé there'.`,quote:`It was there, in fact, that the Papal Nuncio lived.`},{id:`poquelin-and-beaumarchais`,term:`Poquelin and Beaumarchais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:155,text:`Jean-Baptiste Poquelin is Molière (1622–1673), France's great comic playwright, son of a Paris upholsterer near the market of Les Halles. Beaumarchais (1732–1799), the watchmaker's son who wrote 'The Barber of Seville' and 'The Marriage of Figaro', mocked the nobility with street-wise cheek. Hugo claims both for the gamin's line.`,quote:`There was something of that boy in Poquelin`},{id:`camille-desmoulins-and-championnet`,term:`Camille Desmoulins and Championnet`,kind:`history`,chapter:155,text:`Desmoulins was the young journalist who roused the Paris crowd at the Palais-Royal two days before the fall of the Bastille in 1789. General Championnet, a Revolutionary soldier who took Naples in 1799, was said to have forced the clergy there to make the blood of Saint Januarius liquefy on schedule: Hugo's 'treated miracles brutally'.`,quote:`Camille Desmoulins was a native of the faubourgs`},{id:`barra-the-drummer-boy`,term:`Barra the drummer-boy`,kind:`history`,chapter:155,text:`Joseph Bara, a boy of thirteen or fourteen serving with the Republican army, killed by Vendéan rebels in 1793; legend says he died crying 'Vive la République' rather than surrender horses. The Republic made him a hero and painters made him an icon. Hugo makes him the gamin turned hero.`,quote:`Barra the drummer-boy was a gamin of Paris`},{id:`the-horse-of-scripture-says-vah`,term:`The horse of Scripture says 'Vah!'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:155,text:`The war-horse of the Book of Job (39:25), which 'saith among the trumpets, Ha, ha; and he smelleth the battle afar off'. Hugo sets the boy's 'Forward!' beside it. The 'little Theban' who shakes off the lion's skin is the young Hercules.`,quote:`as the horse of Scripture says “Vah!”`},{id:`graeculus`,term:`Graeculus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:156,text:`Latin, 'little Greek': the Roman sneer for the quick-witted, rootless Greeks who lived by their wits in imperial Rome, as tutors, flatterers and jacks-of-all-trades (Juvenal's satire is the classic portrait). Hugo makes the gamin the Paris equivalent: the child of an old civilization, born knowing.`,quote:`like the graeculus of Rome in days gone by`},{id:`paris-as-rome`,term:`Paris as Rome`,kind:`lore`,chapter:156,text:`This chapter is a dizzying catalogue matching every piece of ancient Rome and Greece with something in modern Paris: the Faubourg Saint-Antoine is the Aventine, the hill of the Roman plebs; the Gemonian stairs, where the bodies of the executed were thrown, are replaced by ridicule. Most of the Latin names come from Plautus, Horace and Juvenal. The reader needs none of them to follow the point: everything that ever existed elsewhere exists in Paris.`,quote:`Paris has a capital, the Town-Hall, a Parthenon, Notre-Dame, a Mount Aventine, the Faubourg Saint-Antoine`},{id:`pegre`,term:`Pègre`,kind:`word`,chapter:156,text:`Paris slang for the underworld of petty thieves, set here beside the lazzarone, the idle street poor of Naples. Faraud, in the same list, is a swaggering dandy of the lower classes, the Paris version of the Spanish majo.`,quote:`its lazzarone is the pègre`},{id:`mesmer-s-tub-cagliostro-saint-germain`,term:`Mesmer's tub, Cagliostro, Saint-Germain`,kind:`history`,chapter:156,text:`Three famous impostors or wonder-workers of the late eighteenth century in Paris. Franz Mesmer treated patients round a tub of 'magnetised' water; Cagliostro was a Sicilian adventurer who sold elixirs and séances; the Comte de Saint-Germain claimed to be centuries old. Hugo matches them with the oracles and magicians of antiquity.`,quote:`The vat of Trophonius contains nothing that is not in Mesmer’s tub`},{id:`the-miracles-of-saint-medard`,term:`The miracles of Saint-Médard`,kind:`history`,chapter:156,text:`In the 1720s and 1730s crowds of Jansenist devotees gathered at the tomb of the deacon Pâris in the Saint-Médard churchyard, falling into convulsions and claiming miraculous cures, until the king had the cemetery closed. A wit chalked on the gate: 'By order of the King, God is forbidden to work miracles here.'`,quote:`the cemetery of Saint-Médard works quite as good miracles`},{id:`mayeux-and-mademoiselle-lenormand`,term:`Mayeux and Mademoiselle Lenormand`,kind:`history`,chapter:156,text:`Mayeux was a lewd, boastful hunchback, a stock figure of 1830s caricature and street songs. Marie-Anne Lenormand was the most celebrated fortune-teller of Paris, said to have read the cards for Josephine and Napoleon. Hugo casts them as Paris's Aesop and its witch Canidia.`,quote:`Paris has an Aesop-Mayeux, and a Canidia, Mademoiselle Lenormand`},{id:`table-turning`,term:`Table-turning`,kind:`history`,chapter:156,text:`The spiritualist craze that reached Paris from America in 1853, when people sat round tables waiting for spirits to tilt them and rap out messages. Hugo himself held séances of this kind in exile on Jersey, so his comparison with the oracle of Dodona is not entirely mocking.`,quote:`it makes tables turn as Dodona did tripods`},{id:`chodruc-duclos`,term:`Chodruc Duclos`,kind:`history`,chapter:156,text:`A Bordeaux gentleman who, after quarrelling with the Bourbons he had served, paraded the arcades of the Palais-Royal for decades in filthy rags, unshaven, refusing all help, until his death in 1842. He was one of the famous sights of Restoration Paris: a beggar with the bearing of a lord.`,quote:`makes Chodruc Duclos`},{id:`basile-and-tartuffe`,term:`Basile and Tartuffe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:156,text:`Two stage hypocrites. Tartuffe is Molière's sanctimonious impostor (1664), who uses piety to swindle his host; Basile is the oily music-master in Beaumarchais's 'Barber of Seville', famous for his speech on how slander grows. Paris, says Hugo, enjoys them both too much to be shocked.`,quote:`it does not hold its nose before Basile`},{id:`the-bal-mabile-and-the-lorette`,term:`The bal Mabile and the lorette`,kind:`custom`,chapter:156,text:`The bal Mabille was an open-air dance hall off the Champs-Élysées, fashionable from the 1840s. A lorette was a kept woman of the new quarter round the church of Notre-Dame-de-Lorette, a step up from the working grisette. The Barrière du Combat was where animal fights were staged for paying crowds.`,quote:`the dealer in ladies’ wearing apparel there devours the lorette with her eyes`},{id:`the-oath-of-tennis-the-night-of-the-4th-of-august`,term:`The oath of tennis; the night of the 4th of August`,kind:`history`,chapter:157,text:`Two founding moments of the Revolution of 1789. On 20 June the deputies of the Third Estate, locked out of their hall at Versailles, swore in a royal tennis court not to separate until France had a constitution. On the night of 4 August the Assembly abolished feudal privileges in a single sitting.`,quote:`it forces all nations to take the oath of tennis`},{id:`washington-kosciusko-bolivar-bozzaris-riego`,term:`Washington, Kosciusko, Bolivar, Bozzaris, Riego…`,kind:`history`,chapter:157,text:`Hugo's roll of liberators lit by the French example: the American, Polish and South American independence leaders; Markos Botsaris and Kanaris of the Greek war against the Turks; Rafael del Riego, whose rising at the Isle de Léon near Cádiz in 1820 forced a constitution on Spain; the revolutionaries of 1848 at Pest and Venice; Garibaldi at Palermo in 1860; and John Brown at Harper's Ferry in 1859. Byron died at Missolonghi in 1824 while helping the Greeks.`,quote:`Washington, Kosciusko, Bolivar, Bozzaris, Riego, Bem, Manin, Lopez, John Brown, Garibaldi`},{id:`bouginier-s-nose-and-credeville-the-thief`,term:`Bouginier's nose and 'Credeville the thief'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:157,text:`Two jokes of Paris art students: the painter Bouginier had an enormous nose, and his fellow students caricatured it on walls wherever they went, reportedly as far as the ruins of Greece; 'Crédeville voleur' was a similar running gag scrawled on monuments. Even when Paris is conquering the world with ideas, says Hugo, it is still a gamin with a stick of charcoal.`,quote:`sketches in charcoal Bouginier’s nose on the wall of the temple of Theseus`},{id:`arouet`,term:`Arouet`,kind:`reference`,chapter:157,text:`François-Marie Arouet was Voltaire's real name. The list of thinkers who 'prepared' the Revolution (Montesquieu, Diderot, Beaumarchais, Condorcet, Voltaire, Rousseau) ends with Danton, the orator who 'dared' it: Hugo's praise of audacity over theory.`,quote:`that Arouet should prepare it`},{id:`fex-urbis-burke-s-mob`,term:`Fex urbis; Burke's 'mob'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:158,text:`Cicero's contemptuous name for the Roman poor, 'the dregs of the city', and Edmund Burke, the British statesman whose 'Reflections on the Revolution in France' (1790) spoke of the 'swinish multitude'. Hugo throws back the insults and asks why the barefoot poor should be abandoned for not knowing how to read.`,quote:`“Fex urbis,” exclaims Cicero; “mob,” adds Burke`},{id:`montfaucon`,term:`Montfaucon`,kind:`place`,chapter:158,text:`A hill north-east of Paris where the great medieval gibbet stood, and which later held the city's horse-knackers' yards and refuse dumps, notorious for stench and rats. The 'knacker of Montfaucon' and the porter of La Râpée, the wine and timber wharf on the Seine, are the two ends of the working poor.`,quote:`the knacker of Montfaucon`},{id:`the-marseillaise`,term:`The Marseillaise`,kind:`history`,chapter:158,text:`The marching song written in 1792 and carried to Paris by volunteers from Marseilles, adopted as the national anthem of the Republic. Under the Restoration it was banned as seditious; to sing it was to declare for revolution.`,quote:`sing the Marseillaises`},{id:`boulevard-du-temple`,term:`Boulevard du Temple`,kind:`place`,chapter:159,text:`The boulevard on the north-eastern side of Paris lined with popular theatres, circuses and puppet shows, nicknamed the 'Boulevard of Crime' for the melodramas played there. The Cirque (Franconi's equestrian circus) and the Porte Saint-Martin theatre stood nearby, and the Château-d'Eau was a big fountain at the end of the boulevard. It is the natural home of a theatre-loving gamin.`,quote:`people noticed on the Boulevard du Temple, and in the regions of the Château-d’Eau`},{id:`eight-or-nine-years-after`,term:`Eight or nine years after`,kind:`lore`,chapter:159,text:`Hugo's way of moving the clock: the second part ended with events of 1823–24, so the story now stands in about 1831–32. He has just spent the whole book on the gamin as a type before introducing one particular boy.`,quote:`Eight or nine years after the events narrated in the second part of this story`},{id:`a-pole-or-an-italian-or-even-a-spaniard`,term:`'A Pole or an Italian, or even a Spaniard'`,kind:`history`,chapter:159,text:`After the failed risings in Poland, Italy and Spain around 1830–31, political refugees poured into Paris, and charities and the government gave them relief. A destitute lodger who says he will answer to any of these nationalities is telling the portress he means to beg under whatever story pays best.`,quote:`if anyone should chance to come and inquire for a Pole or an Italian, or even a Spaniard, perchance, it is I`},{id:`sewer-man-and-ragpicker`,term:`Sewer-man and ragpicker`,kind:`custom`,chapter:159,text:`The two lowest trades of Paris. The égoutier cleaned out the city's drains; the chiffonnier went through the rubbish at night with a basket and a hook, collecting rags for the paper mills, bones and scraps to sell. Hugo puts them at the very bottom of the social ladder the Gorbeau tenants are sliding down.`,quote:`the sewer-man who sweeps up the mud, and the ragpicker who collects scraps`},{id:`streets-named-for-provinces-and-capitals`,term:`Streets named for provinces and capitals`,kind:`place`,chapter:160,text:`The streets round the Temple were laid out under Louis XIV and named after French provinces (Normandie, Saintonge, Poitou, Bretagne). In Hugo's own day the new 'Tivoli' or Europe quarter near the Gare Saint-Lazare got streets named after European capitals (Rome, Londres, Vienne). His 'progression in which progress is visible' is a small joke about a nation widening its horizons.`,quote:`the streets of the new Tivoli quarter have received the names of all the capitals of Europe`},{id:`fifteen-thousand-francs-a-year`,term:`Fifteen thousand francs a year`,kind:`money`,chapter:160,text:`M. Gillenormand calls himself ruined on an income of 15,000 francs, when a labourer earned perhaps 500 a year and a clerk 1,500: he is comfortably rich by any ordinary measure. His dream of 'a hundred thousand livres income' is the fortune of a great lord. The old man still counts in livres, the money of the old regime, roughly equal to the franc.`,quote:`All he had left, in fact, was an income of about fifteen thousand francs.`},{id:`the-great-century`,term:`'The great century'`,kind:`history`,chapter:160,text:`Le Grand Siècle: the reign of Louis XIV (1643–1715), the age of Versailles, when a gentleman might beat a servant or an impudent commoner with his cane. M. Gillenormand, born in the 1740s, keeps its manners into the 1830s.`,quote:`he beat people as he had done in the great century`},{id:`carogne`,term:`'Carogne'`,kind:`word`,chapter:160,text:`An old French insult, 'carrion', used to abuse women and servants: roughly 'you hussy' or 'you wretch'. His oath 'by the pantoufloche of the pantouflochade' is nonsense built on pantoufle, slipper: an old man's comic swearing.`,quote:`“Ah! carogne!”`},{id:`the-marais`,term:`The Marais`,kind:`place`,chapter:161,text:`The old aristocratic quarter of the right bank around the Place Royale (now Place des Vosges) and the Temple, full of seventeenth-century mansions. By the 1820s the nobility had moved west to the Faubourg Saint-Germain and the Marais had become a quiet backwater of old bourgeois and retired families, a fitting home for a relic like M. Gillenormand.`,quote:`He lived in the Marais, Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire, No. 6.`,seeAlso:[272]},{id:`gobelins-beauvais-and-coromandel`,term:`Gobelins, Beauvais and Coromandel`,kind:`custom`,chapter:161,text:`The Gobelins in Paris and the Beauvais works were the royal tapestry manufactories, whose pastoral scenes covered the walls and chairs of rich eighteenth-century houses. Coromandel lacquer was carved, painted Chinese lacquer named after the Indian coast through which it was traded. M. Gillenormand's rooms are a museum of his youth.`,quote:`furnished to the very ceilings with great Gobelins and Beauvais tapestries representing pastoral scenes`},{id:`m-de-vivonne`,term:`M. de Vivonne`,kind:`history`,chapter:161,text:`Louis-Victor de Rochechouart, Duc de Vivonne (1636–1688), General of the Galleys under Louis XIV and brother of the king's mistress Madame de Montespan. Hugo's detail that the straw hangings of the boudoir were woven by galley-slaves to order for a nobleman's mistress is pointed.`,quote:`made on the galleys of Louis XIV and ordered of his convicts by M. de Vivonne for his mistress`},{id:`jordaens`,term:`Jordaens`,kind:`reference`,chapter:161,text:`Jacob Jordaens (1593–1678), the Flemish painter of Antwerp, Rubens's follower, known for big, boisterous, fleshy canvases of feasts and peasants. A Jordaens portrait marks M. Gillenormand as a real connoisseur.`,quote:`painted by Jordaens`},{id:`incroyables-of-the-directory`,term:`Incroyables of the Directory`,kind:`history`,chapter:161,text:`The dandies of 1795–99, after the Terror, who wore huge lapels, tight breeches, high cravats and swallowtail coats, and affected a lisp that dropped the letter r ('incoyable!'). M. Gillenormand stopped following fashion then, so in the 1830s he still dresses like a fop of thirty-five years before.`,quote:`it was that of the Incroyables of the Directory`},{id:`fob`,term:`Fob`,kind:`word`,chapter:161,text:`A small pocket in the waistband of breeches, made for a watch. A man who 'always thrust his hands into his fobs' strikes the pose of a self-satisfied old beau.`,quote:`He always thrust his hands into his fobs.`},{id:`the-camargo-the-salle-the-guimard`,term:`The Camargo, the Sallé, the Guimard`,kind:`history`,chapter:162,text:`The great ballerinas of the eighteenth-century Paris Opéra. Marie Camargo and Marie Sallé were rivals of the 1730s, both praised in Voltaire's verses; Marie-Madeleine Guimard was the star of the 1770s and 80s, famous for her lovers and her mansion. M. Gillenormand's memories run from one to the other.`,quote:`the Camargo and the Sallé`},{id:`longchamps`,term:`Longchamps`,kind:`custom`,chapter:162,text:`Every Holy Week in the eighteenth century, fashionable Paris drove out to the abbey of Longchamp in the Bois de Boulogne, nominally for the services, in fact to show off carriages and the season's clothes. M. Gillenormand's description of the Guimard there is a string of comic fashion names of the day.`,quote:`the last time I saw her at Longchamps`},{id:`madame-de-boufflers`,term:`Madame de Boufflers`,kind:`history`,chapter:162,text:`Probably the Comtesse de Boufflers (1725–1800), the brilliant hostess of the Temple circle and friend of Rousseau and Hume, whose verdict could make or mar a young man in society. 'A charming fool' is a very eighteenth-century compliment.`,quote:`Madame de Boufflers, having seen him by chance when he was twenty`},{id:`corbiere-humann-casimir-perier`,term:`Corbière, Humann, Casimir Périer`,kind:`history`,chapter:162,text:`Politicians of the Restoration and the July Monarchy: Corbière was Charles X's ultra-royalist interior minister, Humann a finance minister from Alsace, and Casimir Périer the banker who led Louis-Philippe's government in 1831–32. To M. Gillenormand, all are vulgar upstarts, whatever their party.`,quote:`Corbière! Humann! Casimir Périer!`},{id:`the-age-of-periphrase-in-verse`,term:`'The age of periphrase in verse'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:162,text:`Eighteenth-century French poetry avoided plain words for genteel circumlocutions (a cow was 'the heifer of the plain', a fiddle 'the sonorous wood'). Hugo, who had led the Romantic revolt against that style, notes that the same age was frankly coarse in conversation.`,quote:`the age of periphrase in verse was the age of crudities in prose`},{id:`luc-esprit`,term:`Luc-Esprit`,kind:`lore`,chapter:162,text:`M. Gillenormand's Christian names: Luc (Luke) and Esprit, which in French means both 'spirit' and 'wit'. His godfather chose them as a prophecy that he would be clever; Hugo calls them 'significant'.`,quote:`these two significant names: Luc-Esprit`},{id:`the-duc-de-nivernais-and-the-blue-ribbon`,term:`The Duc de Nivernais and the blue ribbon`,kind:`history`,chapter:163,text:`Louis-Jules Mancini-Mazarini, Duc de Nivernais (1716–1798), diplomat, minor poet and the model of a polished grand seigneur, who handed out school prizes in his duchy around Nevers. The blue ribbon was the sash of the Order of the Holy Spirit, the highest order of the French monarchy. M. Gillenormand has treasured his prize-giving for seventy years.`,quote:`he had been crowned by the hand of the Duc de Nivernais`},{id:`the-elixir-of-gold`,term:`The elixir of gold`,kind:`history`,chapter:163,text:`A fashionable eighteenth-century nostrum, sold as 'General Lamotte's drops' and the 'yellow tincture' of the Russian chancellor Bestuzhev, and supposed to cure venereal disease ('the catastrophes of love'). At a louis the half-ounce it was a luxury remedy. Hugo deflates it: it was iron chloride.`,quote:`the elixir of gold, from Bestucheff`},{id:`ninety-three-twice`,term:`'Ninety-three twice'`,kind:`history`,chapter:163,text:`A pun on 1793, the year of the Terror, when the Revolution guillotined the king and thousands of suspects. M. Gillenormand, who boasts of having slipped through it, means he hopes to reach the age of ninety-three without living through another such year.`,quote:`“I hope that I shall not see ninety-three twice.”`},{id:`national-property`,term:`'National property'`,kind:`history`,chapter:164,text:`During the Revolution the lands of the Church and of émigré nobles were confiscated as biens nationaux and sold off. M. Gillenormand has seen inherited estates vanish that way, which is one reason he prefers to sink his capital in an annuity that dies with him.`,quote:`become “national property;”`},{id:`three-percents-and-the-great-book`,term:`Three percents and the Great Book`,kind:`money`,chapter:164,text:`The 'consolidated three percent' was French government stock paying three per cent a year, recorded in the Grand Livre de la Dette Publique (the Great Book of the Public Debt). Its value rose and fell with every change of regime, and in 1797 the Directory wrote off two-thirds of the debt at a stroke: hence the old man's distrust.`,quote:`he had been present at the avatars of consolidated three percents`},{id:`the-rue-quincampois`,term:`The Rue Quincampois`,kind:`history`,chapter:164,text:`The narrow Paris street where the Scottish financier John Law ran his Mississippi Company bank in 1719–20, scene of a wild speculative frenzy and then a crash that ruined thousands. 'That's the Rue Quincampoix!' means 'that's a swindle, paper wealth'.`,quote:`“All that’s the Rue Quincampois!”`},{id:`servants-renamed`,term:`Servants renamed`,kind:`custom`,chapter:164,text:`Old-regime masters often called a servant not by his own name but by his province of origin (Picard, Poitevin, Basque) or gave every maid the same name, as English houses called footmen 'James'. M. Gillenormand keeps the habit: every man is named for his province and every woman is 'Nicolette', even if he must raise her wages to make it stick.`,quote:`When a servant entered his establishment, M. Gillenormand re-baptized him.`},{id:`cordon-bleu`,term:`Cordon bleu`,kind:`word`,chapter:164,text:`'Blue ribbon': a first-class cook. The phrase borrowed the name of the royal Order of the Holy Spirit, whose blue sash was the highest honour of the old monarchy. Thirty francs a month was a good wage for a cook, and fifty a very generous one.`,quote:`a cordon bleu, of the lofty race of porters`},{id:`silvae-sint-consule-dignae`,term:`Silvae sint consule dignae`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:165,text:`Latin from Virgil's Fourth Eclogue: 'let the woods be worthy of a consul'. M. Gillenormand twists it into a complaint that if he must be robbed 'as though in a forest', the robbery should at least be worthy of a man of his standing.`,quote:`Silvae sint consule dignae!`},{id:`the-duc-d-angouleme-bastard-of-charles-ix`,term:`The Duc d'Angoulême, bastard of Charles IX`,kind:`history`,chapter:165,text:`Charles de Valois (1573–1650), the illegitimate son of King Charles IX, who in his old age married a girl of about twenty and had children by her. Not to be confused with the Restoration Duc d'Angoulême. M. Gillenormand reels off such precedents to show he is quite flattered that a baby is laid at his door at eighty-four.`,quote:`M. le Duc d’Angoulême, the bastard of his Majesty Charles IX`},{id:`eighty-francs-a-month`,term:`Eighty francs a month`,kind:`money`,chapter:165,text:`What M. Gillenormand agrees to pay Magnon for the keep of the two boys she says are his: nearly a thousand francs a year, about two years of a labourer's wages. It is a generous sum for a man who calls himself ruined.`,quote:`promising to pay eighty francs a month for their maintenance`},{id:`demonetized-sous`,term:`Demonetized sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:165,text:`Copper coins withdrawn from circulation and no longer legal tender, worthless in a shop. The miserly priest-brother gave them as alms, keeping the form of charity while giving nothing, 'going to hell by way of paradise'.`,quote:`he never gave them anything except bad or demonetized sous`},{id:`dog-s-ears`,term:`'Dog's ears'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:166,text:`Oreilles de chien: long locks of hair hanging over the ears on either side of the face, a style of the Directory dandies of the 1790s. M. Gillenormand keeps it, like his clothes, from the last fashion he followed.`,quote:`always dressed in “dog’s ears.”`},{id:`receiving-only-in-the-evening`,term:`Receiving only in the evening`,kind:`custom`,chapter:166,text:`Eighteenth-century society dined early in the afternoon and paid and received calls in the evening by candlelight; the daytime was for business and for people who worked. M. Gillenormand's rule (dinner at five, door open after) is a deliberately antique habit.`,quote:`never to receive anyone whatever except in the evening`},{id:`madame-la-prefete`,term:`Madame la Préfète`,kind:`title`,chapter:167,text:`The wife of a prefect, the government's chief official in a département, who presided over official receptions and balls in the provincial capital. In French a wife took her husband's title as a courtesy, so to marry a prefect was to become 'Madame la Préfète'.`,quote:`to be “Madame la Préfète,”`},{id:`lancers`,term:`Lancers`,kind:`title`,chapter:167,text:`Light cavalry armed with lances, a fashionable arm revived in the French army after Napoleon's Polish lancers. An officer of Lancers had a dashing uniform and a reputation to match.`,quote:`an officer of the Lancers, her grand nephew, named Théodule`},{id:`society-of-the-virgin-the-sacred-heart`,term:`Society of the Virgin; the sacred heart`,kind:`custom`,chapter:167,text:`Under the Restoration, pious lay associations ('congregations') flourished under Jesuit guidance, and the devotions to the Sacred Heart of Jesus and the Precious Blood were their badges. Liberals mocked them as political as well as religious: piety went with ultra-royalism. Mademoiselle Gillenormand practises the full set.`,quote:`She belonged to the society of the Virgin`},{id:`rococo-jesuit-altar`,term:`Rococo-Jesuit altar`,kind:`reference`,chapter:167,text:`The ornate eighteenth-century style of church decoration favoured by the Jesuits: gilded wooden rays, marble clouds and cherubs. Hugo, a lover of Gothic, uses it as a sign of showy, shallow devotion.`,quote:`before a rococo-Jesuit altar`},{id:`agnus-dei-and-ave-maria`,term:`Agnus Dei and Ave Maria`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:167,text:`Latin prayers: 'Lamb of God', said at mass before communion, and 'Hail Mary'. Mademoiselle Vaubois knows these and how to make jam, and nothing else.`,quote:`Beyond the Agnus Dei and Ave Maria`},{id:`m-de-bonald`,term:`M. de Bonald`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`Louis de Bonald (1754–1840), the leading philosopher of the counter-revolution, who argued that society rests on God, the king and the father, and who led the ultra-royalists in the Chamber after 1815. To hold one's own against Bonald was a feat in a royalist salon.`,quote:`against M. de Bonald`},{id:`ultra-royalist-salons`,term:`Ultra-royalist salons`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`Under the Restoration, the 'ultras' wanted the old monarchy back whole and thought even Louis XVIII, with his constitutional Charter and moderate ministers, a crypto-Jacobin. They pinned their hopes on the king's brother, the Comte d'Artois. Madame de T.'s salon is a small, poor, fierce outpost of that party.`,quote:`the Jacobinism of Louis XVIII`},{id:`napoleon-called-nicolas-the-federates`,term:`Napoleon called 'Nicolas'; the federates`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`In royalist street songs of 1814–15 Napoleon was mocked as 'Nicolas', a name for a simpleton. The fédérés were the volunteer workers' battalions raised in Paris during the Hundred Days to defend the Emperor; the duchesses' song tells them to tuck their shirt-tails in, a jeer at the white flag of surrender.`,quote:`Napoleon was called “Nicolas,”`},{id:`the-dessolles-ministry-pun`,term:`The Dessolles ministry pun`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`The moderate cabinet of 1818–19 under General Dessolles included Decazes, Louis XVIII's liberal-leaning favourite, and De Serre. The royalist couplet says that to steady the throne one must change 'sol, serre and case', the soil, greenhouse and hut, which sound like the ministers' names. This is the salon's idea of a deadly epigram.`,quote:`Il faut changer de sol, et de serre et de case.`},{id:`ca-ira`,term:`Ça ira`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`'It'll be fine': the revolutionary song of 1790 whose refrain called for aristocrats to be strung up 'à la lanterne', from the street lamp-posts. The royalists sing it with Bonapartists in place of aristocrats. Hugo's comment: songs, like the guillotine, cut any head.`,quote:`Les Bonapartistes à la lanterne!`},{id:`the-fualdes-affair`,term:`The Fualdès affair`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`The sensational murder of Antoine Fualdès, a former magistrate, in Rodez in 1817 (Hugo writes 1816); Bastide and Jausion were tried and executed for it. Because Fualdès had served the Empire, the royalist salons sided with the accused.`,quote:`In the Fualdès affair`},{id:`lamothe-valois-and-the-affair-of-the-necklace`,term:`Lamothe-Valois and the affair of the necklace`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`In 1785 the adventuress Jeanne de La Motte-Valois, who claimed descent from the Valois kings, tricked Cardinal de Rohan into buying a fabulous diamond necklace supposedly for Marie-Antoinette, then had it broken up and sold. The scandal stained the queen's name on the eve of the Revolution. Her husband, the Comte de La Motte, escaped with the diamonds; in Madame de T.'s salon he is received with respect.`,quote:`“Do you know? That is the Lamothe of the affair of the necklace.”`},{id:`marigny-du-barry-guemenee`,term:`Marigny, Du Barry, Guémenée`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`Examples of the old nobility's indifference to scandal: Marigny was the brother of Madame de Pompadour, Louis XV's mistress; Jean du Barry was the pimp who launched Jeanne Bécu ('the Vaubernier') as the future Madame du Barry; the Prince de Guémenée went spectacularly bankrupt in 1782. All were welcome in the best houses. Mercury was the god of thieves.`,quote:`Marigny, the brother of the Pompadour, had his entry with M. le Prince de Soubise`},{id:`the-count-de-ruppin`,term:`The Count de Ruppin`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`The incognito under which King Frederick William III of Prussia visited Paris after the allies restored Louis XVIII. Louis treated him like a provincial marquis, as if the house of Bourbon still outranked every other crown, which M. Gillenormand applauds.`,quote:`under the name of the Count de Ruppin`},{id:`sus-is-superfluous`,term:`'Sus is superfluous'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:168,text:`A pun: a newspaper editor 'suspendu' (suspended) should simply be 'pendu', hanged. The Courrier Français was a leading liberal paper of the Restoration, frequently prosecuted.`,quote:`“Sus is superfluous,” observed M. Gillenormand.`},{id:`brigand-of-the-loire`,term:`'Brigand of the Loire'`,kind:`history`,chapter:168,text:`After Waterloo the remains of Napoleon's army withdrew behind the Loire and were disbanded there in the autumn of 1815. Royalists called its officers 'the brigands of the Loire', and many were put on half-pay and watched by the police. In the Gillenormand household it is the name for the boy's father.`,quote:`he had for a father “a brigand of the Loire.”`},{id:`vernon`,term:`Vernon`,kind:`place`,chapter:169,text:`A small Norman town on the Seine about seventy kilometres down-river from Paris, on the road to Rouen, with an old stone bridge and walled gardens running down to the water. Far enough from the capital for an officer under surveillance, close enough to slip into Paris.`,quote:`the little town of Vernon`},{id:`half-pay-and-residence`,term:`Half-pay and 'residence'`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`The Restoration put thousands of Napoleon's officers on demi-solde, half pay without duties, and sent the suspect ones to live in an assigned town under police watch. The 'demi-soldes', bitter and poor, were a byword for Bonapartist discontent.`,quote:`The Restoration had placed him on half-pay, then had sent him into residence`},{id:`ranks-granted-in-the-hundred-days`,term:`Ranks granted in the Hundred Days`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`Louis XVIII treated everything Napoleon did between his return from Elba and Waterloo as void, so promotions and titles given in those months were not recognised. A colonel and baron made on the field of Waterloo became officially a chef d'escadron again, and his Legion of Honour officer's rosette 'illegal'.`,quote:`did not recognize his quality as an officer of the Legion of Honor, nor his grade of colonel, nor his title of baron`},{id:`the-colonel-s-campaigns`,term:`The colonel's campaigns`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`Hugo's list runs through the whole revolutionary and Napoleonic saga: the Rhine campaigns of 1792–93, Italy and Lodi (1796), Novi (1799), Ulm and Austerlitz (1805), Eylau and Friedland (1807), Moscow and the Berezina (1812), Leipzig (1813), the defence of France (1814), Elba and Waterloo. A soldier who saw all of these had lived the Empire from end to end.`,quote:`Pontmercy fought at Spire, at Worms, at Neustadt`},{id:`captain-louis-hugo-at-eylau`,term:`Captain Louis Hugo at Eylau`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`Victor Hugo's uncle, who held the cemetery at Eylau with a single company through the snowstorm battle of February 1807. Hugo's father was also a Napoleonic general, and this personal aside is a sign of how close the Empire's legend was to his own family.`,quote:`the heroic Captain Louis Hugo, the uncle of the author of this book`},{id:`sir-hudson-lowe`,term:`Sir Hudson Lowe`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`The British governor of Saint Helena, Napoleon's jailer from 1816 to his death in 1821. He addressed him only as 'General Bonaparte', refusing the imperial title, and Napoleon sent such letters back unopened.`,quote:`the missives of Sir Hudson Lowe addressed to “General Bonaparte.”`},{id:`carthaginian-prisoners-and-flaminius`,term:`Carthaginian prisoners and Flaminius`,kind:`reference`,chapter:169,text:`An image from Roman history: Carthaginian captives in Rome who refused to salute a Roman consul, keeping the pride of Hannibal, their general. Flaminius was in fact the consul Hannibal destroyed at Lake Trasimene.`,quote:`there were at Rome Carthaginian prisoners who refused to salute Flaminius`},{id:`soulange-bodin`,term:`Soulange Bodin`,kind:`history`,chapter:169,text:`Étienne Soulange-Bodin (1774–1846), a former soldier who became one of France's great horticulturists and founded the Paris horticultural society. Hugo says the colonel anticipated his beds of heath-mould for rare American and Chinese shrubs.`,quote:`he had forestalled Soulange Bodin`},{id:`warden-marguillier`,term:`Warden (marguillier)`,kind:`title`,chapter:169,text:`A churchwarden: a layman of the parish who sat on the council (the fabrique) managing a church's property and accounts, with a reserved pew. Saint-Sulpice was the great church of the Faubourg Saint-Germain.`,quote:`That worthy priest was the brother of a warden of Saint-Sulpice`},{id:`st-george-s-day`,term:`St. George's day`,kind:`custom`,chapter:169,text:`23 April, the feast of Saint George. In Catholic France a person celebrated the feast of his patron saint, his 'name-day', more than his birthday, so the boy's letters went on New Year's Day and on the name-day of his father, Georges.`,quote:`on the first of January and on St. George’s day`},{id:`mathan-noe-levis-cambis`,term:`Mathan, Noé, Lévis, Cambis`,kind:`lore`,chapter:170,text:`Real old noble family names that happen to sound biblical: Mathan is a priest of Baal in the Book of Kings, Noé is Noah, Lévis was pronounced like Levi, and Cambis like Cambyses, the Persian king. To a small boy learning the Old Testament by heart, the old ladies round the fire become patriarchs and magi.`,quote:`some very noble ladies named Mathan, Noé, Lévis—which was pronounced Lévi—Cambis, pronounced Cambyse`},{id:`madame-de-berry`,term:`Madame de Berry`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Marie-Caroline, Duchesse de Berry, wife of the king's nephew the Duc de Berry, whose assassination in 1820 left her the mother of the posthumous Bourbon heir. Her household was a centre of ultra-royalist hopes.`,quote:`private secretary to Madame de Berry`},{id:`refractory`,term:`'Refractory'`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Under the Revolution a 'refractory' priest was one who refused the oath to the Civil Constitution of the Clergy (1790), and a refractory soldier one who refused to serve the Republic. Both were hunted. The chevalier's story of being chained at Toulon with an old bishop and made to carry away the guillotined is a typical salon horror tale.`,quote:`he had been put in the galleys as refractory and chained with an octogenarian, the Bishop of Mirepoix`},{id:`marat-and-trestaillon`,term:`Marat and Trestaillon`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Jean-Paul Marat was the journalist of the Terror who called for heads in his paper 'L'Ami du peuple' until Charlotte Corday stabbed him in 1793. Trestaillon was the nickname of Jacques Dupont, leader of royalist gangs that murdered Protestants and Bonapartists around Nîmes in the White Terror of 1815. The salon curses one and cheers the other.`,quote:`by dint of cursing Marat, they applauded Trestaillon`},{id:`deputies-of-the-undiscoverable-variety`,term:`Deputies 'of the undiscoverable variety'`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`The Chamber of Deputies elected in August 1815 was so ultra-royalist that Louis XVIII called it la Chambre introuvable, the 'chamber one could never have hoped to find'. Hugo's deputies at the whist table are members of it.`,quote:`Some deputies of the undiscoverable variety played their whist there`},{id:`the-abbe-frayssinous`,term:`The Abbé Frayssinous`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Denis Frayssinous (1765–1841), a famous preacher who became Grand Master of the University and Minister of Ecclesiastical Affairs under Charles X, and a count, bishop and peer. Hugo shows him earlier, in a cassock with buttons missing.`,quote:`the Abbé Frayssinous, who was not, as yet, either count, or bishop, or minister, or peer`},{id:`bishop-in-partibus`,term:`Bishop in partibus`,kind:`title`,chapter:170,text:`Short for in partibus infidelium, 'in the lands of the unbelievers': a bishop given the title of an ancient see now in Muslim territory (here Carystus in Greece), so that he has the rank without a diocese. Such titles went to papal diplomats and assistants like the cardinal's conclavist.`,quote:`Bishop in partibus of Caryste`},{id:`one-of-the-forty`,term:`One of the Forty`,kind:`title`,chapter:170,text:`A member of the Académie française, which has forty seats held for life. In those years it met in the Institut building on the Quai Conti; on Tuesdays passers-by could watch the academicians through the glass door.`,quote:`and one of the Forty`},{id:`peers-of-france`,term:`Peers of France`,kind:`title`,chapter:170,text:`Members of the hereditary upper house created by Louis XVIII's Charter of 1814, on the English model of the House of Lords. The Duc de Valentinois, also reigning Prince of Monaco, thought being a French peer so much grander that he described cardinals as 'peers of France of Rome'.`,quote:`accentuated by five peers of France`},{id:`pere-duchene`,term:`Père Duchêne`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`'Le Père Duchesne' was the foul-mouthed revolutionary newspaper of Jacques Hébert in the 1790s, written in the voice of a swearing stove-maker. Even Chateaubriand, the great royalist writer, would have shocked this salon as much as Hébert.`,quote:`Chateaubriand, had he entered there, would have produced the effect of Père Duchêne.`},{id:`reeks-of-the-fagot`,term:`Reeks of the fagot`,kind:`word`,chapter:170,text:`A fagot is a bundle of sticks, the fuel for burning heretics at the stake; to 'smell of the fagot' meant to be suspected of heresy. Hugo turns it round: the aristocratic quarter still smells of the stake, of persecution.`,quote:`The Faubourg Saint-Germain reeks of the fagot even now.`},{id:`madame-la-generale-madame-la-colonelle`,term:`Madame la Générale, Madame la Colonelle`,kind:`custom`,chapter:170,text:`The old custom of giving a wife her husband's military rank as a title. By the 1820s it sounded provincial to outsiders, but in this salon it was simply the manners of the old court surviving.`,quote:`A woman was called Madame la Générale.`},{id:`soiled-by-the-usurper`,term:`'Soiled by the usurper'`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Napoleon had been addressed as 'Your Majesty', so the purest royalists stopped using the phrase for Louis XVIII and spoke to him in the third person as 'the King'. For them the 'usurper' was Bonaparte.`,quote:`the designation of “Your Majesty” having been “soiled by the usurper.”`},{id:`coblentz-and-the-twenty-fifth-year-of-the-reign`,term:`Coblentz and the twenty-fifth year of the reign`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Coblenz on the Rhine was where the émigré princes gathered their army in 1791–92. Louis XVIII dated his reign from 1795, the death of his young nephew Louis XVII in prison, so his Charter of 1814 was given 'in the nineteenth year of our reign', as though the Republic and the Empire had never happened. Hugo mocks the émigrés for counting the same way.`,quote:`They declared that the time which had elapsed since Coblentz had not existed.`},{id:`methuselah-and-epimenides`,term:`Methuselah and Epimenides`,kind:`reference`,chapter:170,text:`Methuselah is the man in Genesis who lived 969 years; Epimenides the Cretan sage said to have slept for fifty-seven years in a cave. One very old man brings the news to another who has slept through it all: Hugo's picture of the salon.`,quote:`Methuselah bestowed information on Epimenides`},{id:`ultra`,term:`Ultra`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Short for ultra-royaliste: those who wanted to be more royalist than the king, rejecting the Charter and every compromise with the Revolution. Hugo defines the word as 'so strongly for, as to be against' and says the ultra spirit marks the years 1814–1820.`,quote:`What did they do in Madame de T.’s salon? They were ultra.`},{id:`fontenoy-and-marengo`,term:`Fontenoy and Marengo`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Fontenoy (1745) was a celebrated victory of Louis XV's army, Marengo (1800) one of Bonaparte's. The old nobility sneered at the Empire's soldiers and the Empire's soldiers sneered back; each side despised the other's swords.`,quote:`the sword of Fontenoy was laughable and nothing but a scrap of rusty iron; the sword of Marengo was odious and was only a sabre`},{id:`scapin`,term:`Scapin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:170,text:`The scheming, quick-witted valet of Molière's comedy 'Les Fourberies de Scapin' (1671). To call Bonaparte Scapin was to reduce the conqueror of Europe to a clever lackey.`,quote:`There was someone who called Bonaparte Scapin.`},{id:`martainville-fievee-colnet`,term:`Martainville, Fiévée, Colnet`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Royalist journalists and pamphleteers of the early Restoration, much read in their own circle and soon forgotten. Martainville edited the ultra paper 'Le Drapeau blanc'. That he 'had more wit than Voltaire' is Hugo's irony.`,quote:`when M. Martainville had more wit than Voltaire`},{id:`the-corsican-ogre-m-le-marquis-de-bonaparte`,term:`The Corsican Ogre; M. le Marquis de Bonaparte`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`Royalist propaganda painted Napoleon as an ogre from Corsica who devoured France's young men. A Jesuit school textbook by Father Loriquet was popularly believed to have called him 'the Marquis de Bonaparte, lieutenant-general of the King's armies', erasing the Empire altogether; Hugo cites it as a 'concession' to the times.`,quote:`Napoleon was to them thoroughly the Corsican Ogre`},{id:`doctrinarians`,term:`Doctrinarians`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`The doctrinaires were a small group of liberal royalists (Royer-Collard and Guizot the best known) who wanted a constitutional monarchy reconciling the Bourbons with the gains of the Revolution. Hugo lets them speak at length: their plea to 'love the whole of France', old and new, is close to his own.`,quote:`Beginning with 1818, doctrinarians began to spring up in them`},{id:`the-5th-of-september-and-the-n-s-of-the-bridge-of-jena`,term:`The 5th of September and the N's of the bridge of Jéna`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`On 5 September 1816 Louis XVIII dissolved the ultra 'Chambre introuvable', a defeat for the old nobility; in July 1815 it had been the Empire's people who were purged. M. de Vaublanc, an ultra minister, had Napoleon's N's chiselled off the Pont d'Iéna. Bouvines (1214) was a great victory of the medieval French crown.`,quote:`We scoff at M. de Vaublanc for erasing the N’s from the bridge of Jéna!`},{id:`the-congregation`,term:`The Congregation`,kind:`history`,chapter:170,text:`A Catholic lay society under Jesuit direction, founded under the Empire and believed by liberals to be the hidden power behind the Restoration's later years, placing its members in office. 'Congregation' became shorthand for clerical influence in politics.`,quote:`congregation characterized the second`},{id:`souvenirs-that-touch-his-mother`,term:`Souvenirs that touch his mother`,kind:`lore`,chapter:170,text:`Hugo's mother, Sophie Trébuchet, was a royalist from the Vendée region, and he himself was a fervent young royalist poet under the Restoration. His affectionate sketch of a world he has since rejected is partly a family memory.`,quote:`Souvenirs both respectful and affectionate, for they touch his mother`},{id:`coaches-from-the-cour-des-fontaines`,term:`Coaches from the Cour des Fontaines`,kind:`custom`,chapter:171,text:`Before railways, coaches for Normandy left from coaching yards in central Paris, such as the Cour des Fontaines by the Palais-Royal and the Rue du Bouloi nearby. A day coach took twelve hours to Vernon; a night diligence to Rouen would have got Marius there by morning, and nobody looked into it.`,quote:`there is a coach which leaves the Cour des Fontaines at six o’clock`},{id:`the-slasher`,term:`'The slasher'`,kind:`word`,chapter:171,text:`Le sabreur: a soldier good only at swinging a sabre, a sneer at Napoleon's officers as brutes without breeding. M. Gillenormand uses it 'on his amiable days', when he is not saying 'brigand'.`,quote:`“the slasher,” as M. Gillenormand called him on his amiable days`},{id:`brain-fever`,term:`Brain fever`,kind:`word`,chapter:171,text:`A nineteenth-century catch-all for an illness with high fever and delirium: meningitis, encephalitis, or typhoid in its worst stage. It killed quickly, which is why the colonel wrote at once.`,quote:`The colonel had been attacked by brain fever three days previously.`},{id:`chairs-in-church`,term:`Chairs in church`,kind:`custom`,chapter:172,text:`French churches had no fixed pews; worshippers hired straw or velvet chairs from a chair-woman at each service, and regulars and officials had their own chairs with their names on the back. A churchwarden also had a right to a stall. Utrecht velvet was a thick, stamped upholstery velvet.`,quote:`upon a chair of Utrecht velvet, on the back of which was inscribed this name: “Monsieur Mabeuf, warden.”`},{id:`mon-dieu`,term:`Mon Dieu`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:172,text:`'My God': a mild, everyday French exclamation, no stronger than 'good heavens'. Hapgood often leaves it in French.`,quote:`Mon Dieu! a man is not a monster because he was at Waterloo`},{id:`the-memorial-de-sainte-helene`,term:`The Memorial de Sainte-Hélène`,kind:`reference`,chapter:173,text:`Las Cases's record of Napoleon's conversations in exile on Saint Helena, published in 1823. It shaped the legend of the Emperor as a liberal hero martyred by the kings, and was the best-seller that made a generation of young men Bonapartists.`,quote:`the Memorial de Sainte-Hélène`},{id:`bulletins-of-the-grand-army`,term:`Bulletins of the grand army`,kind:`history`,chapter:173,text:`Napoleon's official dispatches from the field, written or dictated by himself, published in the Moniteur and read aloud across France. Vivid and self-glorifying, they named regiments and officers who had distinguished themselves; Hugo calls them 'heroic strophes'.`,quote:`the bulletins of the grand army`},{id:`mirabeau-vergniaud-saint-just`,term:`Mirabeau, Vergniaud, Saint-Just`,kind:`history`,chapter:173,text:`Great figures of the Revolution: Mirabeau, the thunderous orator of 1789; Vergniaud, leader of the Girondins, guillotined in 1793; Saint-Just, Robespierre's young ally, guillotined with him in 1794. Hugo lists them with Robespierre, Desmoulins and Danton as the 'stars' Marius discovers.`,quote:`Mirabeau, Vergniaud, Saint-Just, Robespierre, Camille, Desmoulins, Danton`},{id:`comte-h`,term:`Comte H.`,kind:`lore`,chapter:173,text:`One of the generals Marius visits. The initial is a private nod: Hugo's own father, Léopold Hugo, was a Napoleonic general and count.`,quote:`among others, Comte H.`},{id:`the-black-legend-of-napoleon`,term:`The black legend of Napoleon`,kind:`history`,chapter:173,text:`The slanders royalist pamphlets spread about Bonaparte: incest with his sisters, lessons in posing from the actor Talma, and the poisoning of his own plague-stricken soldiers at Jaffa during the Syrian campaign of 1799. Marius had swallowed them all as a boy.`,quote:`the monster who was the lover of his own sisters—the actor who took lessons of Talma—the poisoner of Jaffa`},{id:`committee-of-public-safety`,term:`Committee of Public Safety`,kind:`history`,chapter:173,text:`The twelve-man executive of the Revolutionary government in 1793–94, dominated by Robespierre, which ran the war and the Terror. That Marius puts it in a line of nation-builders from Charlemagne and Louis XI to Richelieu and Louis XIV shows how far he has swung.`,quote:`of Louis XIV, and of the Committee of Public Safety`},{id:`the-great-nation`,term:`'The great nation'`,kind:`history`,chapter:173,text:`La Grande Nation: the name revolutionary and Napoleonic France gave itself as the liberator and leader of Europe. To Marius, Napoleon is the man who made the other nations say it.`,quote:`who had forced all nations to say: “The great nation!”`},{id:`jacobite`,term:`Jacobite`,kind:`word`,chapter:173,text:`A supporter of the exiled Stuart kings of Britain after 1688; by extension, a diehard legitimist loyal to a deposed dynasty. It is not the same as Jacobin, the radical revolutionary. Marius sheds both his royalism and this die-hard loyalty to the old line.`,quote:`the aristocrat, the Jacobite and the Royalist`},{id:`visiting-cards`,term:`Visiting cards`,kind:`custom`,chapter:173,text:`Engraved cards left at the door or with the porter when calling on someone, the basic tool of social life. The Quai des Orfèvres, the goldsmiths' quay on the Île de la Cité, was where engravers worked. A hundred cards for 'Le Baron Marius Pontmercy', claiming his father's disputed title, and no one to leave them with.`,quote:`he went to an engraver on the Quai des Orfévres and ordered a hundred cards`},{id:`geronte-and-werther`,term:`Géronte and Werther`,kind:`reference`,chapter:173,text:`Géronte is the stock foolish old father of French comedy (in Molière's 'Scapin' among others); Werther is the melancholy young hero of Goethe's 'The Sorrows of Young Werther' (1774). Hugo uses the pair for a frivolous grandfather and a brooding grandson.`,quote:`The gayety of Géronte shocks and exasperates the melancholy of Werther.`},{id:`jupiter`,term:`Jupiter`,kind:`reference`,chapter:173,text:`The largest planet, bright in the night sky. Hugo's figure is generous: Jupiter's volume is about 1,300 times the Earth's. The cosmic scale sets up Marius's midnight cry from the window.`,quote:`one beholds Jupiter, which is twelve hundred times larger than the earth`},{id:`coupe-and-imperial`,term:`Coupé and imperial`,kind:`custom`,chapter:174,text:`The compartments of a French diligence, or stage-coach. The coupé was the closed front compartment with a window onto the road, the dearest seats; the impériale was the bench on the roof under a hood, the cheapest. The officer rides inside in comfort; the student rides on top.`,quote:`He in the imperial, I in the coupé.`},{id:`ten-louis`,term:`Ten louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:174,text:`The louis was the gold coin worth twenty francs, so the aunt's present is two hundred francs: several months' pay for a junior officer's pocket, and plenty to make Théodule obliging. Mademoiselle Gillenormand keeps such sums in her writing-desk for her favourite.`,quote:`And she put ten louis into his hand.`},{id:`duenna`,term:`Duenna`,kind:`word`,chapter:174,text:`From Spanish dueña: an older woman employed to chaperone a young lady and keep watch on her suitors. A dashing lancer sent to spy on a young man's love affair feels he has been demoted to a governess.`,quote:`“Here I am a duenna.”`},{id:`cartouche`,term:`Cartouche`,kind:`history`,chapter:174,text:`Louis-Dominique Cartouche, the most famous Paris highwayman and gang leader of the early eighteenth century, broken on the wheel in 1721 and turned into a folk hero. The grimace of 'Cartouche praised for his probity' is a rogue being complimented on his honesty.`,quote:`The lancer made the pleased grimace of Cartouche when praised for his probity.`,seeAlso:[313]},{id:`argus`,term:`Argus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:174,text:`The hundred-eyed giant of Greek myth set by Hera to watch Io, whose eyes never all closed at once until Hermes lulled him to sleep. The spy who snores all night is a comic Argus.`,quote:`Argus snored all night long.`},{id:`lass-fillette`,term:`Lass (fillette)`,kind:`word`,chapter:174,text:`Fillette, 'little girl', used by men of M. Gillenormand's and Théodule's kind for a working girl taken as a mistress. The aunt is convinced because she hears her father's very word and tone in the lieutenant's mouth.`,quote:`at that word fillette`},{id:`i-would-rather-be-called-alfred`,term:`'I would rather be called Alfred'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:174,text:`Théodule is a pious old saint's name; Alfred was the fashionable romantic name of the 1820s and 30s (Alfred de Musset, Alfred de Vigny). The lieutenant would rather be in fashion than in the calendar of saints.`,quote:`“I would rather be called Alfred,” said the officer.`},{id:`relay-and-postilion`,term:`Relay and postilion`,kind:`custom`,chapter:174,text:`Coaches changed horses every few leagues at post-houses, the 'relays'; the postilion rode one of the leading horses to guide the team. Villagers sold flowers and food to passengers during the change.`,quote:`“Vernon! relay of Vernon! Travellers for Vernon!”`},{id:`the-swimming-school`,term:`The swimming-school`,kind:`custom`,chapter:175,text:`Paris had floating bath-houses moored on the Seine, with enclosed pools where men swam and bathed; most homes had no bathroom. After two nights on a coach roof, Marius goes for an hour's swim.`,quote:`by an hour at the swimming-school`},{id:`shagreen`,term:`Shagreen`,kind:`word`,chapter:175,text:`A rough, grainy leather made from the skin of a horse or shark, often dyed black or green and used for small cases. The little black case on a ribbon looks to M. Gillenormand like a locket portrait of a mistress.`,quote:`a case of black shagreen, resembling a medallion portrait`},{id:`billet-doux`,term:`Billet-doux`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:175,text:`French, 'sweet note': a love letter. 'From the same to the same' is M. Gillenormand's knowing joke that a man keeps his lady's letters next to her picture.`,quote:`“From the same to the same,” said M. Gillenormand, bursting with laughter. “I know what it is. A billet-doux.”`},{id:`redcaps`,term:`Redcaps`,kind:`history`,chapter:175,text:`The red Phrygian cap, the bonnet rouge, was the emblem of the radical sans-culottes of 1792–94 and of liberty in general. To an old royalist, 'redcap' means a Terrorist.`,quote:`assassins, redcaps, thieves!`},{id:`b-u-o-naparte`,term:`B-u-o-naparté`,kind:`history`,chapter:175,text:`Royalists pointedly used the Italian spelling of Napoleon's family name, Buonaparte, and gave it an Italian sound, to stress that he was a Corsican foreigner, not a French sovereign. M. Gillenormand spells it out syllable by syllable for emphasis.`,quote:`All who served B-u-o-naparté were brigands!`},{id:`the-duc-de-berry`,term:`The Duc de Berry`,kind:`history`,chapter:175,text:`Charles-Ferdinand, Duc de Berry (1778–1820), the king's nephew and the hope of the dynasty, stabbed outside the Opéra in 1820 by a Bonapartist workman, Louvel. His bust on a royalist mantelpiece was a shrine; M. Gillenormand bows to it as if apologising for what has just been said.`,quote:`a bust of M. le Duc de Berry`,seeAlso:[262]},{id:`louis-xviii-had-been-dead-for-four-years`,term:`'Louis XVIII had been dead for four years'`,kind:`history`,chapter:175,text:`Louis XVIII died in September 1824 and was succeeded by his brother, Charles X, so the scene is in 1828. Marius's insult is political rather than accurate.`,quote:`Louis XVIII had been dead for four years; but it was all the same to him.`},{id:`sixty-pistoles`,term:`Sixty pistoles`,kind:`money`,chapter:175,text:`The pistole was an old name for a gold coin worth about ten livres, still used in speech by people of M. Gillenormand's age. Sixty pistoles every six months is about six hundred francs, so 1,200 francs a year: a modest student allowance, sent with a curse.`,quote:`You will send sixty pistoles every six months to that blood-drinker`},{id:`you-instead-of-thou`,term:`'You' instead of 'thou'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:175,text:`A French father said tu ('thou') to his children. Calling his daughter vous, the formal 'you', for three months is M. Gillenormand's way of keeping his anger cold: an icy, polite distance.`,quote:`he continued to address his daughter as “you” instead of “thou” for the next three months`},{id:`hackney-coach-by-the-hour`,term:`Hackney-coach by the hour`,kind:`custom`,chapter:175,text:`A fiacre, the Paris cab, could be hired by the trip or by the hour. Marius, with thirty francs and no plan, takes one by the hour and drives at random.`,quote:`He had entered a hackney-coach, had engaged it by the hour`},{id:`the-latin-quarter`,term:`The Latin quarter`,kind:`place`,chapter:175,text:`The left-bank district round the Sorbonne, the law and medical schools and the Panthéon, home of students, cheap lodgings, cafés and printing shops, so called because Latin was once the language of its schools. It is the natural refuge for a penniless law student.`,quote:`towards the Latin quarter`},{id:`tugendbund-and-carbonarism`,term:`Tugendbund and Carbonarism`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`The Tugendbund ('League of Virtue') was a Prussian patriotic society of 1808 aimed at throwing off Napoleon; the Carbonari ('charcoal-burners') were the Italian secret society that organised risings against Austrian and Bourbon rule from about 1815, with a French offshoot, the Charbonnerie, in the early 1820s. Hugo's point is that France had nothing so large, only small circles.`,quote:`like the German tugendbund and Italian Carbonarism`},{id:`the-cougourde`,term:`The Cougourde`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`A republican secret society of Provence; the name is Provençal for a gourd. Hugo places its beginnings at Aix in these years. He mentions it alongside the Paris group as one of several shoots of the same underground growth.`,quote:`The Cougourde was being outlined at Aix`},{id:`the-friends-of-the-a-b-c`,term:`The Friends of the A.B.C.`,kind:`lore`,chapter:176,text:`A pun: in French the letters A, B, C are pronounced 'ah-bay-say', the same sound as abaissé, 'brought low', 'debased'. The name passes for a society to teach children their letters while meaning 'Friends of the downtrodden'. Hugo's examples show that puns have made history before.`,quote:`They declared themselves the Friends of the A.B.C.—the Abaissé—the debased—that is to say, the people.`},{id:`castratus-ad-castra-barbari-et-barberini-tu-es-petrus`,term:`Castratus ad castra; Barbari et Barberini; Tu es Petrus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`Three famous puns. Narses, the Byzantine general, was a eunuch, so 'the castrated man to the camp'; Pasquino's Roman jibe 'what the barbarians did not do, the Barberini did' mocked Pope Urban VIII's family for stripping the Pantheon's bronze; and Christ's 'Thou art Peter (Petrus), and upon this rock (petram) I will build my church' (Matthew 16:18) is the pun on which the papacy rests.`,quote:`witness the Castratus ad castra, which made a general of the army of Narses`},{id:`cafe-musain-and-corinthe`,term:`Café Musain and Corinthe`,kind:`place`,chapter:176,text:`The two haunts of the group. The Café Musain was a small café near the Panthéon with a back room reached by a long corridor and a private stair onto the Rue des Grès; Corinthe was a wine-shop near the old fish-market of Les Halles on the right bank. One is close to the students, the other to the workmen.`,quote:`in a little café in the Rue Saint-Michel called the Café Musain, now torn down`},{id:`a-map-of-france-under-the-republic`,term:`A map of France under the Republic`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`A map showing the départements of the Republic, or the frontiers of 1792–1799, instead of the kingdom: under the Restoration, hanging one was a quiet political statement, enough to draw a police spy's eye. The police watched cafés where students met.`,quote:`An old map of France under the Republic was nailed to the wall`},{id:`antinous`,term:`Antinous`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`The beautiful youth loved by the Emperor Hadrian, drowned in the Nile in AD 130 and deified; his statues became the classic type of young male beauty. A 'savage Antinous' is beauty with a fierce edge.`,quote:`He was a savage Antinous.`},{id:`gracchus-on-the-aventine`,term:`Gracchus on the Aventine`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Gaius Gracchus, the Roman tribune who championed land reform for the poor, made his last stand with his followers on the Aventine hill in 121 BC and died there. Saint-Just was the youngest and most uncompromising of the Jacobins in the Convention.`,quote:`On Mount Aventine, he would have been Gracchus; in the Convention, he would have been Saint-Just.`},{id:`harmodius-and-aristogeiton`,term:`Harmodius and Aristogeiton`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`The two Athenians who in 514 BC killed the tyrant's brother Hipparchus, hiding their daggers in myrtle branches at a festival, and were honoured as liberators. Evadne stands for any beautiful woman. To Enjolras, as to them, flowers are good only to hide a sword.`,quote:`he, like Harmodius, thought flowers good for nothing except to conceal the sword`},{id:`the-cherub-of-ezekiel-and-the-cherubino-of-beaumarchais`,term:`The cherub of Ezekiel and the Cherubino of Beaumarchais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`The cherubim in Ezekiel's vision are terrible, four-faced, winged beings of fire. Chérubin is the amorous teenage page of Beaumarchais's 'Marriage of Figaro', in love with every woman. A girl who took Enjolras for the second would meet the first.`,quote:`not to confound the mighty cherub of Ezekiel with the gallant Cherubino of Beaumarchais`},{id:`condorcet`,term:`Condorcet`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`The Marquis de Condorcet (1743–1794), mathematician and philosopher of the Revolution, who believed in the indefinite progress of humanity through reason and education, and wrote his 'Sketch' of that progress while hiding from the Terror. He died in prison. Robespierre is the model for Enjolras, Condorcet for Combeferre.`,quote:`The first attached himself to Robespierre; the second confined himself to Condorcet.`},{id:`homo-and-vir`,term:`Homo and vir`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:176,text:`Two Latin words for 'man': homo, the human being, and vir, the male, the man of courage (the root of 'virile' and 'virtue'). Combeferre is the humane one, Enjolras the virile one.`,quote:`Homo and vir, that was the exact effect of their different shades.`},{id:`arago-geoffroy-saint-hilaire`,term:`Arago, Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Leading Paris scientists of the 1820s whose public lectures drew students: François Arago, the physicist and astronomer who worked on the polarization of light, and Étienne Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire, the naturalist of the Jardin des Plantes who argued for a unity of plan across animal bodies. Combeferre follows them both.`,quote:`learned the polarization of light from Arago`},{id:`saint-simon-and-fourier`,term:`Saint-Simon and Fourier`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`The two founders of French socialism. Henri de Saint-Simon (1760–1825) wanted society run by industrialists and scientists for the good of the poorest; Charles Fourier (1772–1837) planned ideal communities, 'phalansteries', where work would be made attractive. Comparing them was what a serious young reformer did in 1828.`,quote:`compared Saint-Simon with Fourier`},{id:`puysegur-and-deleuze`,term:`Puységur and Deleuze`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Followers of Mesmer who studied 'animal magnetism' and the trance state called magnetic sleep, an early form of hypnotism. Combeferre reads them with an open mind: he affirms nothing and denies nothing.`,quote:`studied Puységur and Deleuze`},{id:`the-polytechnic`,term:`The Polytechnic`,kind:`place`,chapter:176,text:`The École Polytechnique, founded in 1794 on the Montagne Sainte-Geneviève, France's elite school of engineering and science, run on military lines. Its students had a strong republican tradition.`,quote:`a graduate of the Polytechnic`,seeAlso:[228]},{id:`combeferre-s-dreams`,term:`Combeferre's dreams`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Hugo lists inventions that were still dreams in 1828 and realities when he wrote in the 1860s: railways, anaesthesia for surgery (ether, 1846), photography (images fixed in the camera obscura, 1839), the electric telegraph. Only the steerable balloon was still to come.`,quote:`railroads, the suppression of suffering in chirurgical operations, the fixing of images in the dark chamber, the electric telegraph, the steering of balloons`},{id:`jehan`,term:`Jehan`,kind:`lore`,chapter:176,text:`The medieval spelling of Jean. Hugo's own 'Notre-Dame de Paris' (1831) had helped launch the Romantic craze for the Middle Ages, and some young men took to old-fashioned spellings of their names. Prouvaire's 'Jehan' marks him as a poet of that school.`,quote:`His name was Jehan`},{id:`andre-chenier`,term:`André Chénier`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`The finest French poet of the late eighteenth century, guillotined in July 1794, two days before the fall of Robespierre. Hugo calls him 'a royal head', royal in poetry; Prouvaire holds his death against the Revolution.`,quote:`that of André Chénier`,seeAlso:[344]},{id:`agrippa-d-aubigne`,term:`Agrippa d'Aubigné`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`Théodore Agrippa d'Aubigné (1552–1630), Protestant soldier and poet whose epic 'Les Tragiques' rages against the persecution of the Huguenots. Preferring Corneille's grandeur to Racine's refinement, and d'Aubigné's fury to both, marks Prouvaire's taste for the 'immense'.`,quote:`he preferred Corneille to Racine, and Agrippa d’Aubigné to Corneille`},{id:`fan-maker-at-three-francs-a-day`,term:`Fan-maker at three francs a day`,kind:`custom`,chapter:176,text:`Fan-making was a skilled Paris craft (painting and mounting the leaves of fans). Three francs a day was a decent wage for a workman, enough to live on alone but not to buy books, which Feuilly does anyway.`,quote:`Feuilly was a workingman, a fan-maker, orphaned both of father and mother, who earned with difficulty three francs a day`},{id:`the-partition-of-poland-1772`,term:`The partition of Poland, 1772`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`In 1772 Russia, Prussia and Austria each seized a slice of Poland; two more partitions in 1793 and 1795 wiped it off the map. The Congress of Vienna in 1815 confirmed most of it, putting Warsaw under the Tsar. To Feuilly this 'three-sided crime' is the pattern for every later suppression of a nation.`,quote:`Above all things, the great violence of 1772 aroused him.`},{id:`ne-variatur`,term:`Ne variatur`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:176,text:`Latin legal phrase, 'let it not be altered', stamped on a document to certify it as the fixed, authentic text. Every despot since, says Feuilly, has signed a copy of the partition of Poland without changing a word.`,quote:`ne variatur`},{id:`the-particle`,term:`The particle`,kind:`custom`,chapter:176,text:`The little 'de' before a surname (de Courfeyrac, de Lafayette), widely taken as a sign of nobility, though it proves nothing. Under the Restoration liberal gentlemen dropped it to show their politics: Lafayette, Benjamin Constant. La Minerve was the leading liberal review of 1818–1820.`,quote:`But the bourgeois of the epoch of la Minerve estimated so highly that poor de, that they thought themselves bound to abdicate it.`},{id:`beaute-du-diable`,term:`Beauté du diable`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:176,text:`'The devil's beauty': the bloom of youth, attractive whatever the features, that fades with age. Hugo applies it to the mind: Courfeyrac's sparkle is the same student wit as Tholomyès's, passed down the generations.`,quote:`the beauté du diable of the mind`},{id:`quasi-cursores`,term:`Quasi cursores`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`From Lucretius: 'and like runners they pass on the torch of life'. The generations of students hand on the same jokes and the same style of wit as relay runners hand on a torch.`,quote:`quasi cursores`},{id:`lallemand-s-funeral`,term:`Lallemand's funeral`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Nicolas Lallemand, a law student, was shot by a soldier of the royal guard during demonstrations over a new electoral law in June 1820 (Hugo writes 1822); his funeral turned into a mass liberal protest broken up by troops. It was the first time Restoration students fought in the streets.`,quote:`the burial of young Lallemand`},{id:`the-square-cap-in-the-nightstand`,term:`The square cap in the nightstand`,kind:`lore`,chapter:176,text:`The square cap (bonnet carré) was the cap of a lawyer or judge; a nightstand was the bedside cupboard that held the chamber pot. Bahorel's mock coat of arms puts the one in the other: that is his opinion of the law.`,quote:`for his armorial bearings a nightstand in which was visible a square cap`},{id:`paletot`,term:`Paletot`,kind:`word`,chapter:176,text:`A loose, short overcoat that became the fashion in the 1830s and 40s. Hugo's aside 'the paletot had not yet been invented' dates the scene: in 1828 a student still buttoned up a fitted frock-coat.`,quote:`the paletot had not yet been invented`},{id:`the-marquis-d-avaray`,term:`The Marquis d'Avaray`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Antoine de Béziade, Comte (later Duc) d'Avaray, the friend who smuggled the future Louis XVIII out of Paris in a cab in June 1791, when Louis XVI's own flight failed at Varennes. The king rewarded him lavishly; Hugo says it was for helping him into a hackney-coach.`,quote:`The Marquis d’Avaray, whom Louis XVIII made a duke for having assisted him to enter a hackney-coach on the day when he emigrated`},{id:`non-bonaparte-orthography`,term:`'Non-Bonaparte orthography'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:176,text:`L'Aigle means 'the eagle', Napoleon's emblem, which Louis XVIII would not want to see on a petition. Spelt 'Lesgle', and explained as a contraction of the comic Lesgueules ('the gobs'), it becomes harmless, and the king laughs.`,quote:`This non-Bonaparte orthography touched the King and he began to smile.`},{id:`bossuet`,term:`Bossuet`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet (1627–1704), Bishop of Meaux, the grandest preacher of Louis XIV's reign, nicknamed 'the Eagle of Meaux'. Since Lesgle (l'Aigle, 'the eagle') signs himself 'de Meaux', his friends naturally call him Bossuet.`,quote:`As an abbreviation, his companions called him Bossuet.`},{id:`guignon`,term:`Guignon`,kind:`word`,chapter:176,text:`French for persistent bad luck, a jinx. Bossuet greets misfortune by its nickname like an old friend: 'Good day, Guignon.'`,quote:`“Good day, Guignon,” he said to it.`},{id:`eat-a-hundred-francs`,term:`'Eat a hundred francs'`,kind:`money`,chapter:176,text:`A hundred francs, five gold louis, was a month's income for a modest clerk; spending it on one supper is 'unbridled extravagance' for a penniless student. Hence his joke calling his companion a 'five-louis jade'.`,quote:`“Pull off my boots, you five-louis jade.”`},{id:`le-malade-imaginaire`,term:`Le Malade imaginaire`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`'The Imaginary Invalid', Molière's last comedy (1673), about a hypochondriac who lives for doctors and purges. Joly, a medical student who checks his tongue in the mirror and lines his bed up with the earth's magnetism, is a junior version.`,quote:`Joly was the “malade imaginaire” junior.`},{id:`grantaire-s-rebus-r`,term:`Grantaire's rebus: R`,kind:`lore`,chapter:176,text:`A letter-puzzle: the capital letter R is a 'grand R', pronounced in French exactly like 'Grantaire'. He signs his name with it.`,quote:`he was in the habit of signing himself with this rebus: R`},{id:`grantaire-s-paris`,term:`Grantaire's Paris`,kind:`place`,chapter:176,text:`Real student haunts of the 1820s: the Café Lemblin at the Palais-Royal, a Bonapartist officers' café; the Café Voltaire near the Odéon; the Ermitage dance-garden on the Boulevard du Maine; and the barrier taverns of La Cunette and Le Combat. Grantaire's learning is a guide to where to eat, drink and dance.`,quote:`he knew that the best coffee was to be had at the Café Lemblin`},{id:`foot-fencing-and-singlestick`,term:`Foot-fencing and singlestick`,kind:`custom`,chapter:176,text:`Foot-fencing is savate, the French art of fighting with kicks as well as fists, popular among Paris workmen and students; singlestick (la canne) is fencing with a walking stick. Grantaire is a sceptic who can look after himself.`,quote:`boxing and foot-fencing and some dances; and he was a thorough singlestick player`},{id:`robespierre-junior-and-loizerolles`,term:`Robespierre junior and Loizerolles`,kind:`history`,chapter:176,text:`Two famous acts of devotion in the Terror: Augustin Robespierre insisted on sharing his brother's arrest and death, and old Loizerolles answered to his son's name at the prison roll-call and was guillotined in his place. Grantaire sneers at both.`,quote:`Robespierre junior as well as Loizerolles`},{id:`vive-henri-iv`,term:`'Vive Henri IV'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`An old popular song in honour of Henry IV, the good king who loved wine and women; its tune carried many drinking songs, including Grantaire's 'J'aimons les filles et j'aimons le bon vin' ('We love the girls and we love good wine') in mock-peasant grammar. Under the Restoration it was practically a royalist anthem.`,quote:`“J’aimons les filles, et j’aimons le bon vin.” Air: Vive Henri IV.`},{id:`pollux-patroclus-nisus-orestes-and-pylades`,term:`Pollux, Patroclus, Nisus… Orestes and Pylades`,kind:`reference`,chapter:176,text:`The great pairs of devoted friends: Pollux and Castor, Patroclus and Achilles, Nisus and Euryalus (in the 'Aeneid'), Hephaestion and Alexander, and Orestes and his faithful Pylades. Eudamidas left his friends his debts in his will, and Pechméja was a minor writer devoted to his friend. Grantaire belongs among the men who exist as the second half of someone else.`,quote:`They are Pollux, Patrocles, Nisus, Eudamidas, Ephestion, Pechmeja.`},{id:`caryatid`,term:`Caryatid`,kind:`reference`,chapter:177,text:`A column carved as a standing woman holding up a roof, as on the Erechtheum in Athens. Bossuet propped against the café doorpost looks like one that has taken a day off.`,quote:`He had the air of a caryatid on a vacation`},{id:`cabriolet`,term:`Cabriolet`,kind:`custom`,chapter:177,text:`A light two-wheeled hired cab with a folding hood, the driver sitting beside the passenger. Cheaper and faster than the four-wheeled fiacre, it was the young man's cab of the 1820s (the English 'cab' comes from it).`,quote:`a two-wheeled vehicle proceeding through the place, at a foot pace and apparently in indecision`},{id:`the-roll-call-at-the-law-school`,term:`The roll-call at the law-school`,kind:`custom`,chapter:177,text:`Paris law students paid fees for each term's inscription, and a professor could strike off a student who missed the roll three times, so the fees ('sixty francs in the gulf') were lost and the term did not count towards the degree. Answering for an absent friend was an old student trick.`,quote:`At the third call, unanswered, your name is erased from the list. Sixty francs in the gulf.`},{id:`boileau-s-malicious-nose`,term:`Boileau's malicious nose`,kind:`reference`,chapter:177,text:`Nicolas Boileau (1636–1711), the satirist and lawgiver of French classical poetry, whose satires mock pedants. Bossuet credits him with the phrase to sharpen his portrait of the roll-calling Blondeau.`,quote:`who must be the malicious nose alluded to by Boileau`},{id:`erudimini-qui-judicatis-terram`,term:`Erudimini qui judicatis terram`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:177,text:`Latin from Psalm 2:10: 'Be instructed, ye that judge the earth'. The line was famous as the text of Bossuet's funeral orations, so this Bossuet uses it for his mock oration over the living Blondeau. Nasica (the 'big-nosed', a surname of the Scipios) and bos disciplinae ('ox of discipline') complete the parody.`,quote:`Erudimini qui judicatis terram`},{id:`defend-the-widow-attack-the-orphan`,term:`'Defend the widow, attack the orphan'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:177,text:`The lawyer's traditional boast was to 'defend the widow and the orphan'. Bossuet's twist (defending the widow, attacking the orphan) is a student joke about what lawyers actually do.`,quote:`I shall not defend the widow, and I shall not attack the orphan`},{id:`a-rent-of-nine-thousand-francs`,term:`'A rent of nine thousand francs'`,kind:`money`,chapter:177,text:`A cab hired by the hour cost about a franc an hour, so living in one all year round would come to some nine thousand francs: the rent of a grand apartment. Bossuet congratulates Marius on his 'opulence'.`,quote:`You have there a rent of nine thousand francs per annum.`},{id:`hotel-de-la-porte-saint-jacques`,term:`Hôtel de la Porte-Saint-Jacques`,kind:`place`,chapter:177,text:`A cheap furnished lodging-house (hôtel garni) in the Latin Quarter near the old site of the Saint-Jacques gate, where students rented rooms by the month. In French 'hôtel' could mean any lodging house, as well as a private mansion.`,quote:`“Coachman,” said he, “hotel de la Porte-Saint-Jacques.”`},{id:`the-gray-hue-of-a-reassured-rat`,term:`'The gray hue of a reassured rat'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:178,text:`A joke on the fanciful names of fashionable colours of the time ('mouse-grey', 'frightened-mouse', 'dying-monkey'). Courfeyrac means Marius's 'democrat-Bonapartist' is a timid in-between shade, neither one colour nor the other.`,quote:`“The gray hue of a reassured rat,” said Courfeyrac.`},{id:`classic-tragedy`,term:`Classic tragedy`,kind:`reference`,chapter:178,text:`Seventeenth- and eighteenth-century French verse tragedy on the ancient model (Racine, Voltaire), with its rules of unity and noble alexandrines, still staged in the 1820s. The Romantics, Hugo at their head, attacked it as 'bewigged'; Combeferre defends its right to exist as the duck exists beside the bird.`,quote:`“Down with tragedy dear to the bourgeois!” cried Bahorel.`},{id:`jean-jacques-and-therese`,term:`Jean-Jacques and Thérèse`,kind:`history`,chapter:178,text:`Jean-Jacques Rousseau lived in the Rue Plâtrière (renamed after him in 1791) with Thérèse Levasseur, and admitted in his 'Confessions' that he left their five children at the Foundling Hospital. That the prophet of education and natural feeling did this was a favourite jibe of his enemies; Enjolras answers that he 'adopted the people'.`,quote:`Thérèse gave birth to them, Jean-Jacques represented them as foundlings.`},{id:`bonaparte-not-the-emperor`,term:`'Bonaparte', not 'the Emperor'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:178,text:`What one called Napoleon was a political badge. Bonapartists said 'the Emperor'; republicans said 'Bonaparte', the general who had betrayed the Republic; royalists said 'Buonaparte', the Corsican. The Friends' refusal of the title surprises Marius.`,quote:`Not one of these young men articulated the word: The Emperor.`},{id:`initium-sapientiae`,term:`Initium sapientiae`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:178,text:`Latin, 'the beginning of wisdom', from Psalm 111: 'The fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom'. Hugo uses it for Marius's surprise: being shaken is where learning starts.`,quote:`Marius was vaguely surprised. Initium sapientiae.`},{id:`argand-lamp`,term:`Argand lamp`,kind:`custom`,chapter:179,text:`An oil lamp with a hollow circular wick and a glass chimney, invented by Aimé Argand in the 1780s, which gave a far brighter, steadier light than candles. Lighting it 'solemnly' marks the start of the evening's meeting.`,quote:`The argand lamp was solemnly lighted.`},{id:`the-tun-of-heidelberg`,term:`The tun of Heidelberg`,kind:`place`,chapter:179,text:`The enormous wine cask in the cellar of Heidelberg castle, built in the eighteenth century to hold over 200,000 litres, a famous sight for travellers. Grantaire dreams of being one of the leeches applied to it.`,quote:`that the tun of Heidelberg has an attack of apoplexy`,seeAlso:[250]},{id:`all-is-vanity`,term:`'All is vanity'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`The refrain of the Book of Ecclesiastes ('Vanity of vanities, saith the Preacher; all is vanity'), traditionally ascribed to Solomon. Grantaire takes it as his text for a drunken sermon on the emptiness of everything.`,quote:`Ecclesiastes says: ‘All is vanity.’`},{id:`consul-incitatus-and-baronet-roastbeef`,term:`Consul Incitatus and Baronet Roastbeef`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`The Roman emperor Caligula was said to have planned to make his horse Incitatus a consul. The English story that a king knighted a loin of beef, making it 'Sir Loin', is a popular folk etymology for 'sirloin', usually told of James I or Charles II. Grantaire's point: honours are toys.`,quote:`Caligula made a horse a consul; Charles II made a knight of a sirloin.`},{id:`pupil-of-gros-rapin`,term:`Pupil of Gros; rapin`,kind:`word`,chapter:179,text:`Antoine-Jean Gros was the painter of Napoleon's battles and ran the most popular studio in Paris in the 1820s. A rapin was a studio apprentice, the lowest of the art students, who ground colours and ran errands. Grantaire's pun links rapin and rapine, plunder: he stole apples.`,quote:`rapin is the masculine of rapine`},{id:`brutus-nero-and-strongylion`,term:`Brutus, Nero and Strongylion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`Pliny the Elder records that Brutus, Caesar's assassin, doted on a statue of a boy by the Greek sculptor Strongylion, and that Nero took the same artist's Amazon 'Eucnemos' ('of the beautiful leg') on his travels. Grantaire uses it to show the noble republican and the monster emperor liking the same things.`,quote:`The Brutus who killed Caesar was in love with the statue of a little boy.`},{id:`pydna-and-tolbiac`,term:`Pydna and Tolbiac`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`Pydna (168 BC) was the Roman victory that ended the kingdom of Macedon; Tolbiac (c. 496) the battle in which the Frankish king Clovis beat the Alemanni and, by tradition, vowed to become a Christian. Grantaire pairs them with Marengo and Austerlitz: history copies itself.`,quote:`The battle of Marengo copies the battle of Pydna`},{id:`si-volet-usus`,term:`Si volet usus`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:179,text:`From Horace's 'Art of Poetry': words live or die 'if usage wills it', for usage is the judge and law of speech. Grantaire uses it to say that everything obeys success, even grammar.`,quote:`Si volet usus, says Horace.`},{id:`phocion-and-coligny`,term:`Phocion and Coligny`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`Phocion was the upright Athenian general and statesman condemned to drink hemlock by his fellow citizens in 318 BC. Admiral Coligny, leader of the French Protestants, was murdered in Paris at the start of the Saint Bartholomew's Day Massacre in 1572. Both were killed by their own city.`,quote:`slew Phocion, as we might say Coligny`},{id:`john-bull-and-brother-jonathan`,term:`John Bull and Brother Jonathan`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`The cartoon figures of England and of the United States: John Bull the stout farmer, Brother Jonathan the lanky Yankee who preceded Uncle Sam. Grantaire rejects both: England for its 'Time is money', the America of 1828–1860 for its slavery and 'Cotton is king'.`,quote:`If I do not admire John Bull, shall I admire Brother Jonathan?`},{id:`murdered-tsars`,term:`Murdered tsars`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`Grantaire's grim list of violent deaths in the Russian royal house: the Tsarevich Alexis, who died after torture on his father Peter the Great's orders (1718); Peter III, killed in 1762 after his wife Catherine took power; Paul I, strangled and beaten to death by officers in 1801. Hence the palace's 'flagrant insalubrity'.`,quote:`A decapitated Alexis, a poniarded Peter, a strangled Paul`},{id:`the-grand-lama`,term:`The Grand Lama`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`The Dalai Lama of Tibet, a sacred ruler whose body and person were objects of veneration; Europeans told grotesque tales about it. Grantaire's retort is that Western courts made just as much ceremony over the queen's dirty shift and the dauphin's close-stool.`,quote:`what you find to laugh at in the Grand Lama`},{id:`bibines-goguettes-bastringues`,term:`Bibines, goguettes, bastringues…`,kind:`word`,chapter:179,text:`Paris slang for low drinking places: a bibine was a ragpickers' dive, a goguette a singing club in a tavern back room, a bastringue a cheap dance hall, a mastroquet a wine-seller, and caboulot and boui-boui both mean a dingy café. Grantaire rattles off the names like a connoisseur.`,quote:`the wineshops of the ragpickers are called ‘bibines;’`},{id:`richard-s-at-forty-sous`,term:`Richard's at forty sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:179,text:`A student eating-house where a fixed-price dinner cost two francs a head, cheap but respectable. That is Grantaire's idea of the sybarite life, in the same breath as Persian carpets for Cleopatra.`,quote:`I eat at Richard’s at forty sous a head`},{id:`hippocrates-refusing-artaxerxes`,term:`Hippocrates refusing Artaxerxes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`The Greek father of medicine was said to have refused the Persian king Artaxerxes' rich gifts and an invitation to cure his army, rather than serve an enemy of Greece; the scene was a favourite of painters. Bossuet's restraining gesture reminds Grantaire of it.`,quote:`your gesture of Hippocrates refusing Artaxerxes’ bric-a-brac`},{id:`spleen`,term:`Spleen`,kind:`word`,chapter:179,text:`In the nineteenth century 'spleen' meant a gloomy, bored melancholy, thought to be an English disease; the French borrowed the English word. Grantaire lists it with homesickness and hypochondria as his ailments.`,quote:`I have the spleen, complicated with melancholy`},{id:`emphyteuses`,term:`Emphyteuses`,kind:`word`,chapter:179,text:`An emphyteusis is a very long lease, from the Roman law, by which a tenant held land for decades or forever on condition of improving it and paying a fixed rent. Bossuet's tangle of old Norman feudal law is meant to sound absurdly obscure.`,quote:`for all emphyteuses, leases, freeholds, contracts of domain, mortgages`},{id:`vaudeville`,term:`Vaudeville`,kind:`custom`,chapter:179,text:`A light comedy with songs set to popular tunes, the staple of the Paris boulevard theatres. Students and clerks often tried to write them for quick money; the stock names (Dorimon, Célestine, Colonel Sainval) show the formula.`,quote:`announced that a vaudeville was being sketched out`},{id:`bigre`,term:`Bigre`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:179,text:`A mild French oath, 'heck!' or 'by Jove!', a softened form of a cruder word. The duel advice is in fencing jargon: attack, feints, parade (parry).`,quote:`bigre! and he is left-handed`},{id:`a-little-holy-alliance-vaud-and-gex`,term:`A little Holy Alliance; Vaud and Gex`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`The Holy Alliance was the pact of Russia, Austria and Prussia after 1815 to defend thrones and religion. Bahorel's lovers' treaty has frontiers like the Congress of Vienna's: the Swiss canton of Vaud and the French Pays de Gex sit on either side of Lake Geneva, which became a treaty border. It is a joke about dividing a bed.`,quote:`On forming our little Holy Alliance we assigned ourselves each our frontier, which we never cross.`},{id:`staub-s`,term:`Staub's`,kind:`place`,chapter:179,text:`Staub was the fashionable Paris tailor of the 1820s and 30s, known for his trousers. Advising Joly to buy a pair there is advising him to dress like a dandy.`,quote:`Buy a good pair of trousers of double-milled cloth at Staub’s.`},{id:`the-vignemale-and-cybele-pissevache`,term:`The Vignemale and Cybele; Pissevache`,kind:`reference`,chapter:179,text:`The Vignemale is a high peak of the Pyrenees, and Cybele the Phrygian mother-goddess, shown crowned with towers; Pan played pipes of reed; Io was the nymph turned into a heifer. The Pissevache is a waterfall in the Swiss Valais whose name means 'pissing cow'. Prouvaire's defence of the pagan gods ends in a schoolboy joke.`,quote:`I have always believed that Io had something to do with the cascade of Pissevache`},{id:`the-touquet-charter`,term:`The Touquet Charter`,kind:`history`,chapter:179,text:`A cheap edition of Louis XVIII's constitutional Charter of 1814, sold by the liberal publisher Touquet and bound with snuffboxes and handkerchiefs in the 1820s. To liberals the Charter was a guarantee; to republicans like Courfeyrac it was a gift the king could take back.`,quote:`On the table lay an unfortunate copy of the famous Touquet Charter.`},{id:`entrain`,term:`Entrain`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:179,text:`French for liveliness, go, high spirits that carry a group along. Hugo sets it against English 'humour' as two national kinds of wit, both on show in the room.`,quote:`that French thing which is called entrain`},{id:`bonaparte-s-number-18`,term:`Bonaparte's number 18`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`Courfeyrac's numerology: on 18 Brumaire of the Year VIII (9 November 1799) General Bonaparte overthrew the Directory in a coup and took power; on 18 June 1815 he lost at Waterloo and Louis XVIII came back. Brumaire was the 'month of mists' in the revolutionary calendar. Enjolras turns the pair into 'the crime and the expiation'.`,quote:`Place Louis in front and Brumaire behind, you have the whole destiny of the man`},{id:`quia-nominor-leo`,term:`Quia nominor leo`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:180,text:`Latin, 'because I am called lion', from Phaedrus's fable of the lion's share: the lion claims the first portion of the spoil for no reason but his name. Enjolras means France needs no Napoleon to be great; it is great because it is France. (The text prints 'nomina'.)`,quote:`Quia nomina leo.`},{id:`justinian-laplace-merlin`,term:`Justinian, Laplace, Merlin`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`Marius's comparisons: the Emperor Justinian, who codified Roman law, as Napoleon did French law in the Civil Code of 1804; the mathematician Pierre-Simon Laplace, a fellow member of the Academy of Sciences; and Merlin de Douai, the leading jurist of the Council of State. Napoleon was at home with lawyers and astronomers alike.`,quote:`He made codes like Justinian`},{id:`tilsit`,term:`Tilsit`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`The town on the river Niemen where in July 1807 Napoleon met Tsar Alexander I on a raft in midstream and dictated peace to Russia and Prussia: the high point of his power in Europe. Napoleon's bearing there impressed even the sovereigns he had beaten.`,quote:`at Tilsit he taught Emperors majesty`},{id:`cromwell-blowing-out-one-of-two-candles`,term:`Cromwell blowing out one of two candles`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`An anecdote of Oliver Cromwell's thrift: he put out one of two candles burning on his table to save tallow. Marius cites Napoleon haggling over a curtain tassel in the same spirit: the master of Europe watched the small expenses too.`,quote:`like Cromwell blowing out one of two candles`},{id:`the-cannon-of-the-invalides`,term:`The cannon of the Invalides`,kind:`custom`,chapter:180,text:`Guns at the Hôtel des Invalides, the veterans' hospital in Paris, fired salutes for great events: births in the ruling family, and, under the Empire, the news of a victory. Parisians woke to the cannon and knew a battle had been won.`,quote:`to have the cannon of the Invalides to rouse you in the morning`},{id:`marengo-arcola-austerlitz-jena-wagram`,term:`Marengo, Arcola, Austerlitz, Jéna, Wagram`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`Napoleon's great victories: Arcola (1796) in Italy, where he led the charge across a bridge himself; Marengo (1800) against Austria; Austerlitz (1805) over Austria and Russia; Jena (1806) over Prussia; Wagram (1809) over Austria again. Paris named bridges and streets after them.`,quote:`Marengo, Arcola, Austerlitz, Jéna, Wagram!`},{id:`si-cesar-m-avait-donne`,term:`'Si César m'avait donné'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:180,text:`Combeferre's adaptation of the old song Alceste quotes in Molière's 'The Misanthrope' (1666): 'If the king had given me Paris, his great city, and I had to give up my sweetheart's love, I would say to King Henry: take back your Paris, I prefer my love.' Combeferre sings 'Caesar', 'glory and war' and 'my mother': freedom over glory. 'Ô gué' is a nonsense refrain.`,quote:`J’aime mieux ma mère, ô gué!`},{id:`citizen`,term:`'Citizen'`,kind:`history`,chapter:180,text:`Citoyen, the form of address the Revolution imposed in place of 'Monsieur' in 1792, to make all men equal. Under the Restoration only a republican would use it, so Enjolras's 'Citizen' is a declaration in itself.`,quote:`“Citizen,” said Enjolras to him, “my mother is the Republic.”`},{id:`res-angusta`,term:`Res angusta`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:181,text:`The chapter title, from Juvenal's line that men rise with difficulty when 'res angusta domi', narrow means at home, hold them back. It means straitened circumstances: Marius's money runs out.`,quote:`The realities of life do not allow themselves to be forgotten.`},{id:`selling-clothes-and-watch`,term:`Selling clothes and watch`,kind:`money`,chapter:181,text:`The ordinary way a young man of good family without money raised cash: sell spare clothes to a second-hand dealer and the watch to a watchmaker. Twenty francs for a frock-coat and trousers, forty-five for a gold watch, against a hotel bill of seventy: Marius is left with ten francs.`,quote:`He paid twenty francs for the cast-off garments. They went to the watchmaker’s. He bought the watch for forty-five francs.`},{id:`swallowing-a-tongue`,term:`'Swallowing a tongue'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:181,text:`Courfeyrac's pun: in French langue means both 'tongue' and 'language'. Eating five francs while learning English means swallowing a language very fast, or a hundred sous (the same five francs) very slowly.`,quote:`That will be swallowing a tongue very fast, or a hundred sous very slowly.`},{id:`thirty-louis`,term:`Thirty louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:181,text:`The sixty pistoles M. Gillenormand ordered sent: six hundred francs in gold, which is also thirty louis of twenty francs each. It would keep a frugal student for half a year, and Marius sends it back with three francs in his pocket.`,quote:`Marius sent back the thirty louis to his aunt`},{id:`de-la-vache-enragee`,term:`De la vache enragée`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:182,text:`'Eating mad cow': the French idiom for going through hard times, living in want and privation. Hugo spells out the menu: days without bread, nights without a candle, the porter's insolence.`,quote:`He ate of that terrible, inexpressible thing that is called de la vache enragé`},{id:`a-mutton-cutlet-for-six-or-seven-sous`,term:`A mutton cutlet for six or seven sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:182,text:`About a third of a franc, the price of a few rolls. Marius makes one cutlet last three days, meat, fat and bone, and buys his sou's worth of Brie at the fruiterer's: the arithmetic of a student with nothing.`,quote:`asked for a mutton cutlet, paid six or seven sous for it`},{id:`turning-a-coat`,term:`Turning a coat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:182,text:`A worn coat could be unpicked and resewn inside out, so the unfaded underside of the cloth showed; it was a poor man's way of getting a 'new' coat. Marius's comes out green, not black, so he goes out only after dark to stay in mourning.`,quote:`For thirty sous, Marius got it turned by some porter or other, and it was a new coat.`},{id:`admitted-to-practice-as-a-lawyer`,term:`Admitted to practice as a lawyer`,kind:`custom`,chapter:182,text:`After the law degree, a young man took the oath and was enrolled as an avocat, at first on probation; the rules required a respectable address and a library of law books. Marius borrows Courfeyrac's room and shelves to satisfy them.`,quote:`In spite of all this, he got admitted to practice as a lawyer.`},{id:`a-baron-and-a-lawyer-at-the-same-time`,term:`'A baron and a lawyer at the same time'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:182,text:`Under the old regime a nobleman who took up a trade or certain professions could lose his status ('derogate'), and old-fashioned people still felt a title and a lawyer's gown did not go together. The grandfather's mutter sneers at both the title and the profession, and shows he has not stopped thinking about Marius.`,quote:`“If you were not a fool, you would know that one cannot be a baron and a lawyer at the same time.”`},{id:`utility-man`,term:`Utility man`,kind:`word`,chapter:183,text:`A theatre term for an actor who plays whatever small part is needed. Marius is the publisher's literary odd-job man: prospectuses, translations, notes, short biographies.`,quote:`Marius filled the modest post of utility man in the literature of the publishing house`},{id:`marius-s-budget`,term:`Marius's budget`,kind:`money`,chapter:183,text:`Hugo's careful accounts: 700 francs a year earned, 650 spent. Twenty sous (one franc) a day for food, 30 francs a year rent for an attic room, 36 to the old woman for service, 100 for clothes, 50 each for linen and washing. It is close to what an unskilled labourer earned, and it is enough to feel 'rich'.`,quote:`Thus, breakfast four sous, dinner sixteen sous; his food cost him twenty sous a day`},{id:`rousseau-the-aquatic`,term:`Rousseau the Aquatic`,kind:`place`,chapter:183,text:`A cheap student restaurant in the Rue Saint-Jacques at the corner of the Rue des Mathurins, where a dinner of meat, vegetables, dessert and unlimited bread cost fifteen sous and most diners drank water. Hence the owner's nickname.`,quote:`The proprietor had a fine nickname: he was called “Rousseau the Aquatic.”`},{id:`rue-saint-jacques`,term:`Rue Saint-Jacques`,kind:`place`,chapter:183,text:`The ancient main street of the Latin Quarter, running south from the Seine past the Sorbonne and the Val-de-Grâce towards the Barrière Saint-Jacques. From the Gorbeau house Marius walks down it into the student quarter.`,quote:`he descended the Rue Saint-Jacques to dine at Rousseau’s`},{id:`commode`,term:`Commode`,kind:`word`,chapter:183,text:`A low chest of drawers. Marius's three shirts are one on his back, one in the commode and one at the laundress's.`,quote:`the second in the commode`,seeAlso:[205]},{id:`the-brass-vase-and-the-iron-pot`,term:`The brass vase and the iron pot`,kind:`reference`,chapter:184,text:`From La Fontaine's fable of the earthen pot and the iron pot, which travel together until the iron one breaks the other with a friendly bump. Two hard heads, neither willing to give way: grandson and grandfather keep apart.`,quote:`Marius was the brass vase, while Father Gillenormand was the iron pot.`},{id:`septembrist`,term:`Septembrist`,kind:`history`,chapter:184,text:`One of the mobs who murdered more than a thousand prisoners, priests and nobles among them, in the Paris jails in the September Massacres of 1792. To M. Gillenormand it is the worst name for a revolutionary, and he heaps it on his grandson with 'Jacobin' and 'terrorist'.`,quote:`this Buonapartist, this Jacobin, this terrorist, this Septembrist`},{id:`furnaces-which-consume-their-own-smoke`,term:`Furnaces which consume their own smoke`,kind:`history`,chapter:184,text:`Smokeless furnaces, an industrial novelty of Hugo's day designed to burn the gases that otherwise poured out of factory chimneys. The old man's grief burns inside without showing.`,quote:`His sorrow was like those recently invented furnaces which consume their own smoke.`},{id:`pettifogging`,term:`Pettifogging`,kind:`word`,chapter:184,text:`Practising petty, quibbling law for small fees; a pettifogger is a lawyer of the lowest kind. M. Gillenormand's sneer at 'Monsieur le Baron de Pontmercy' mocks both the title and the trade.`,quote:`“Monsieur le Baron de Pontmercy is practising pettifogging in some corner or other.”`,seeAlso:[347]},{id:`fifteen-hundred-francs-and-a-room`,term:`Fifteen hundred francs and a room`,kind:`money`,chapter:184,text:`The publisher's offer would have doubled Marius's income and housed him, roughly a junior clerk's salary. He refuses it for his freedom. Hugo names the publisher, a real firm, with an 'I think'.`,quote:`to give him fifteen hundred francs a year`},{id:`the-eumenides`,term:`The Eumenides`,kind:`reference`,chapter:185,text:`'The Kindly Ones': the Greeks' flattering name for the Furies, the avenging spirits, used so as not to provoke them. M. Mabeuf approves all political opinions in the same spirit, so that they will leave him alone.`,quote:`as the Greeks called the Furies “the beautiful, the good, the charming,” the Eumenides`},{id:`bouquinist`,term:`Bouquinist`,kind:`word`,chapter:185,text:`From bouquin, an old book: a lover and collector of old books (today the word also means the second-hand booksellers along the Seine). Hugo's joke is that in an age of Royalists, Bonapartists and Orleanists, M. Mabeuf's only '-ist' is this one.`,quote:`he was a bouquinist, a collector of old books`},{id:`folios-and-32mos`,term:`Folios and 32mos`,kind:`word`,chapter:185,text:`Book sizes named by how many times the printed sheet was folded: a folio once (big volumes), a 32mo into thirty-two leaves (pocket books). A collector loves both ends of the scale.`,quote:`heaps of folios, and even of 32mos`},{id:`elzevir`,term:`Elzevir`,kind:`reference`,chapter:185,text:`A small, beautifully printed book from the Elzevier family press of Leiden and Amsterdam in the seventeenth century, prized by collectors. M. Mabeuf loves no man as much as an Elzevir.`,quote:`nor any man as much as an Elzevir`},{id:`st-germain-pears-the-mirabelle`,term:`St. Germain pears; the Mirabelle`,kind:`custom`,chapter:185,text:`The Saint-Germain was a famous old French dessert pear; the mirabelle is a small, sweet yellow plum of Lorraine. Raising new fruit varieties from seed was a serious amateur pursuit, and Hugo credits M. Mabeuf with a late-season mirabelle.`,quote:`M. Mabeuf had succeeded in producing seedling pears as savory as the pears of St. Germain`},{id:`cauteretz`,term:`Cauteretz`,kind:`place`,chapter:185,text:`Cauterets, a spa town in the high Pyrenees, famous for its thermal waters and mountain flora. A botanical guide with coloured plates to its environs would sell steadily to the visitors who came to take the cure.`,quote:`a Flora of the Environs of Cauteretz, with colored plates`},{id:`rue-mesieres`,term:`Rue Mésières`,kind:`place`,chapter:185,text:`The Rue Mézières, a quiet street beside Saint-Sulpice in the Faubourg Saint-Germain, where M. Mabeuf lived while he was a churchwarden there.`,quote:`People rang his bell, in the Rue Mésières, two or three times a day, to ask for it.`},{id:`indigo`,term:`Indigo`,kind:`custom`,chapter:185,text:`The blue dye plant of India and the tropics, all imported. Napoleon's blockade had set off efforts to grow dye plants in France; to naturalise indigo is M. Mabeuf's harmless botanical dream.`,quote:`His dream was to naturalize indigo in France.`,seeAlso:[231]},{id:`allegri-s-miserere`,term:`Allegri's Miserere`,kind:`reference`,chapter:185,text:`The setting of Psalm 51 by Gregorio Allegri, sung by the papal choir in the Sistine Chapel during Holy Week and long kept secret (the boy Mozart famously wrote it down from memory). Hugo's joke is that the old servant's cat had a voice fit for it.`,quote:`which might have mewed Allegri’s miserere in the Sixtine Chapel`},{id:`mother-plutarque`,term:`Mother Plutarque`,kind:`lore`,chapter:185,text:`M. Mabeuf's nickname for his servant, after Plutarch, the Greek author of the 'Parallel Lives'. It is a gentle joke: she 'knew how to read', which was not common for a woman of her class.`,quote:`M. Mabeuf had nicknamed her Mother Plutarque.`},{id:`ten-thousand-francs-lost-to-a-notary`,term:`Ten thousand francs lost to a notary`,kind:`money`,chapter:185,text:`Notaries held clients' savings and invested them, and when one went bankrupt or absconded, the savings went with him. Ten thousand francs at five per cent would have brought M. Mabeuf five hundred a year; with the July Revolution of 1830 killing book sales, his income collapses.`,quote:`A notary’s failure deprived him of the sum of ten thousand francs`},{id:`fifty-crowns`,term:`Fifty crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:185,text:`An écu, or crown, was the old silver coin of three livres, still used in counting by older people; fifty crowns is about 150 francs a year. The rent of the cottage at Austerlitz is half that of the Rue Montparnasse house he could not afford.`,quote:`where, for fifty crowns a year, he got three rooms and a garden`},{id:`the-village-of-austerlitz`,term:`The village of Austerlitz`,kind:`place`,chapter:185,text:`A straggling suburb near the Salpêtrière and the Jardin des Plantes, by the Austerlitz bridge, still half-rural in the 1830s with gardens and thatched cottages. The warlike name annoys the gentle old man, who fled the Rue Montparnasse partly because of the pistol-shots from a shooting-gallery.`,quote:`in a sort of thatched cottage of the village of Austerlitz`,seeAlso:[242]},{id:`buddha-and-the-dragon`,term:`Buddha and the Dragon`,kind:`lore`,chapter:185,text:`A pun lost in English: in French la belle bouda et le dragon ('the beauty pouted, and the dragoon') sounds like 'Bouddha et le dragon'. Half-listening, the old scholar hears a Buddhist legend in a cheap romance.`,quote:`“Buddha and the Dragon,” struck in M. Mabeuf in a low voice.`},{id:`the-outer-boulevards`,term:`The outer boulevards`,kind:`place`,chapter:186,text:`The ring of broad, tree-lined roads running just inside the customs wall around Paris, as opposed to the 'grands boulevards' of the old city centre. They were half-empty, edged with waste ground, market gardens and barrier taverns: a walker's solitude.`,quote:`taking long walks alone on the outer boulevards`},{id:`comte-pajol-general-fririon`,term:`Comte Pajol, General Fririon`,kind:`history`,chapter:186,text:`Real officers of the Empire still living in Paris in the 1830s: Claude-Pierre Pajol, a cavalry general who fought at Waterloo's sister battle of Wavre, and François-Nicolas Fririon; Bellavesne commanded the military school of Saint-Germain. Through them Marius hears about his father and goes to their balls in his one good coat.`,quote:`to Comte Pajol, to General Bellavesne, to General Fririon, to the Invalides`},{id:`the-revolution-of-1830`,term:`The Revolution of 1830`,kind:`history`,chapter:186,text:`The 'Three Glorious Days' of 27–29 July 1830, when Paris rose against Charles X's ordinances suspending press freedom and changing the electoral law. Charles fled, and the liberal deputies made his cousin Louis-Philippe, Duc d'Orléans, 'King of the French'. To a young liberal it looked like victory, which is why it calms Marius.`,quote:`The Revolution of 1830 assisted in the process, by satisfying and calming him.`},{id:`truth-at-the-bottom-of-her-well`,term:`Truth at the bottom of her well`,kind:`reference`,chapter:186,text:`A saying ascribed to the Greek philosopher Democritus: 'Truth lies at the bottom of a well.' Hugo turns it round: from the bottom of the well, the only thing Truth can see is the sky.`,quote:`the only thing which Truth can perceive from the bottom of her well`},{id:`two-quarters-rent`,term:`Two quarters' rent`,kind:`custom`,chapter:186,text:`Paris rents were paid by the quarter, on fixed term-days (January, April, July and October). Owing two quarters, twenty francs for a garret, was enough to be thrown out. Marius pays it from his thirty francs of savings, anonymously.`,quote:`“Because they do not pay their rent; they owe for two quarters.”`},{id:`la-quotidienne`,term:`La Quotidienne`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`A leading ultra-royalist and then legitimist daily paper, loyal to the exiled elder Bourbons after 1830. It is naturally M. Gillenormand's paper, and it still makes him angry.`,quote:`about to read something in the Quotidienne`},{id:`the-cannon-of-the-national-guard`,term:`The cannon of the National Guard`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`The National Guard was the citizen militia of property-owners, revived in 1830 as the 'citizen king's' support. In 1831 its artillery companies, full of republicans, quarrelled with the government over the guns parked in the Louvre courtyard, and the government disbanded them; students held meetings on the Place du Panthéon in protest.`,quote:`the artillery of the National Guard, and a conflict between the Minister of War and “the citizen’s militia,”`},{id:`life-pension`,term:`Life pension`,kind:`money`,chapter:187,text:`A rente viagère, an annuity bought with capital that pays an income for life and dies with the buyer. Théodule calculates that the 'old druid' may still have money left outside it to leave, and dresses as a civilian to court him.`,quote:`“The old druid has not sunk all his money in a life pension.`},{id:`two-watches-in-two-fobs`,term:`Two watches in two fobs`,kind:`custom`,chapter:187,text:`Wearing two watches, one in each breeches pocket with their chains and seals dangling, was a dandy's fashion of the 1780s and the Directory. M. Gillenormand still does it.`,quote:`twitching, with his irritated old fingers, at the two watches which he wore in his two fobs`},{id:`descamisados`,term:`Descamisados`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:187,text:`Spanish, 'shirtless ones': the name given to the radical liberals of the Spanish revolution of 1820, much as the French radicals had been sans-culottes. M. Gillenormand uses it for revolutionaries in general.`,quote:`That is what the descamisados have brought us to!`},{id:`carnot-and-fouche`,term:`Carnot and Fouché`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`Lazare Carnot, the incorruptible 'organiser of victory' of the Revolution, and Joseph Fouché, Napoleon's slippery police minister who betrayed every master; both were regicides. After Waterloo, as Fouché arranged the return of the Bourbons, Carnot is supposed to have asked where he could go, and Fouché answered: wherever you like.`,quote:`Carnot used to say: ‘Where would you have me go, traitor?’ Fouché replied: ‘Wherever you please, imbecile!’`},{id:`hernani`,term:`Hernani`,kind:`reference`,chapter:187,text:`Victor Hugo's own verse drama, whose first nights at the Comédie-Française in February 1830 turned into a running battle between young Romantics and defenders of classical tragedy. M. Gillenormand's verdict ('antitheses! abominations which are not even written in French!') is the classical party's, and the author's private joke.`,quote:`A year ago, they ran to Hernani.`},{id:`apollo-belvedere-and-venus-de-medici`,term:`Apollo Belvedere and Venus de Medici`,kind:`reference`,chapter:187,text:`Two of the most admired ancient statues, the Apollo from the Vatican and the Venus from Florence, both carried off to the Louvre by Napoleon and returned in 1815. The old man is fifteen years out of date, but the point stands: cannon in the courtyard of a museum.`,quote:`Do you want to fire grapeshot at the Apollo Belvedere?`},{id:`tiercelin-and-potier`,term:`Tiercelin and Potier`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`Popular comic actors of the boulevard theatres of the 1820s, whose catchphrases and puns young men repeated. To M. Gillenormand it proves the young have no wit of their own.`,quote:`they repeat the puns of Tiercelin and Potier`},{id:`ogibewas-and-cadodaches`,term:`Ogibewas and Cadodaches`,kind:`reference`,chapter:187,text:`The Ojibwa and the Caddo, Native American peoples. In M. Gillenormand's rant they stand for 'savages', and even they, he says, do not let students deliberate on the National Guard.`,quote:`such a thing could not be seen among the Ogibewas nor the Cadodaches!`},{id:`newspapers-under-the-arcades-of-the-odeon`,term:`Newspapers under the arcades of the Odéon`,kind:`custom`,chapter:187,text:`Under the arcades of the Odéon theatre in the Latin Quarter, booksellers and reading-rooms rented newspapers for a sou a read, since a subscription was beyond most students. It was where opinions were caught.`,quote:`so long as they go and read the newspapers under the arcades of the Odéon`},{id:`the-drapeau-blanc-and-the-annuaire-militaire`,term:`The Drapeau Blanc and the Annuaire Militaire`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`Le Drapeau blanc ('The White Flag') was Martainville's ultra-royalist paper of the 1820s; the Annuaire Militaire was the army list of officers and regiments. Théodule's idea of a library is the army list and the official gazette.`,quote:`“There should be no other newspaper than the Moniteur, and no other book than the Annuaire Militaire.”`},{id:`sieyes`,term:`Sieyès`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`The Abbé Sieyès, author of the famous 1789 pamphlet 'What is the Third Estate?', voted for the death of Louis XVI, helped Bonaparte to power in 1799 and ended a count and senator of the Empire. M. Gillenormand's example of the republican who ends with a title.`,quote:`It is like their Sieyès! A regicide ending in a senator`},{id:`senators-in-violet-velvet-sown-with-bees`,term:`Senators in violet velvet sown with bees`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`The ceremonial dress of Napoleon's Senate: violet velvet mantles embroidered with golden bees, the imperial emblem, and plumed hats in a sixteenth-century style. To the old royalist they looked like a monkey court.`,quote:`in mantles of violet velvet sown with bees, with hats à la Henri IV`},{id:`the-grimacer-of-tivoli`,term:`The grimacer of Tivoli`,kind:`history`,chapter:187,text:`Tivoli was a popular Paris pleasure garden of the Empire and Restoration, with fireworks, side-shows and entertainers; one of its attractions was a man who pulled funny faces through a pair of comic spectacles. M. Gillenormand rates philosophers no higher.`,quote:`than of the spectacles of the grimacer of Tivoli`},{id:`sicambres`,term:`Sicambres`,kind:`history`,chapter:188,text:`The Sicambri, a Germanic tribe of the lower Rhine later merged into the Franks. When Clovis, king of the Franks, was baptised, the bishop famously told him: 'Bow thy head, proud Sicambrian.' Hugo uses them for a rounded, 'leonine' northern face as against the Roman 'aquiline' profile.`,quote:`that complete absence of angles which rendered the Sicambres so easily recognizable among the Romans`},{id:`monsieur-l-abbe`,term:`'Monsieur l'Abbé'`,kind:`title`,chapter:188,text:`The ordinary title of a secular priest or a young cleric. Courfeyrac's greeting teases Marius for living like a seminarian, studious and afraid of girls.`,quote:`“Good morning, Monsieur l’Abbé!”`},{id:`the-luxembourg-and-the-pepiniere`,term:`The Luxembourg and the Pépinière`,kind:`place`,chapter:188,text:`The Luxembourg garden on the left bank, laid out for Marie de Médicis's palace (then the Chamber of Peers), was the park of the Latin Quarter: students, nursemaids, old gentlemen. The Pépinière was its old nursery garden of fruit trees on the south side, towards the Rue de l'Ouest, a quiet corner later swallowed by Haussmann's street-building.`,quote:`in one of the walks of the Luxembourg, the one which skirts the parapet of the Pépinière`},{id:`quaker-shirt`,term:`Quaker shirt`,kind:`lore`,chapter:188,text:`Hugo's phrase for a shirt of plain coarse linen kept spotlessly white, like the plain, neat dress the Quakers were known for. Combined with the always-new hat and sober blue, it gives the impression of a man of modest means and careful habits.`,quote:`a quaker shirt, that is to say, it was dazzlingly white, but of coarse linen`},{id:`merino`,term:`Merino`,kind:`word`,chapter:188,text:`A fine woollen cloth made from the wool of merino sheep, hard-wearing and plain; black merino was a common stuff for schoolgirls' and mourning dresses. The badly cut black gown is what makes the students call the girl 'Mademoiselle Lanoire'.`,quote:`it consisted of a badly cut gown of black merino`},{id:`monsieur-leblanc-and-mademoiselle-lanoire`,term:`Monsieur Leblanc and Mademoiselle Lanoire`,kind:`lore`,chapter:188,text:`Nicknames Courfeyrac made up for strangers whose names nobody knew: 'Mister White' for the man's white hair and 'Miss Black' for the girl's black dress. Hugo adopts the students' nickname for the man for convenience. It is not his name.`,quote:`he had dubbed the daughter Mademoiselle Lanoire, and the father, Monsieur Leblanc`},{id:`parthian-dart`,term:`Parthian dart`,kind:`reference`,chapter:188,text:`The horsemen of ancient Parthia were famous for pretending to flee and then shooting arrows backwards over their horses' tails; hence a 'Parthian shot', a cutting remark made on the way out. Courfeyrac flings his nickname as he leaves.`,quote:`He had fled, discharging at them a sobriquet, like a Parthian dart.`},{id:`lux-facta-est`,term:`Lux facta est`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:189,text:`Latin, 'and there was light', from God's creation of light in Genesis (1:3). Hugo makes it the title of the chapter in which Marius sees the thin schoolgirl of the year before turned, in six months, into a beautiful young woman.`,quote:`This child had not only grown, she had become idealized.`},{id:`raphael-and-jean-goujon`,term:`Raphael and Jean Goujon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:189,text:`Raphael (1483–1520), the Italian painter of serene, perfect Madonnas; Jean Goujon (c. 1510–1567), the French Renaissance sculptor of the slender nymphs of the Fountain of the Innocents in Paris. Hugo borrows one for the face and the other for the neck.`,quote:`a head such as Raphael would have given to Mary, set upon a neck that Jean Goujon would have attributed to a Venus`},{id:`black-damask-and-white-crape`,term:`Black damask and white crape`,kind:`custom`,chapter:189,text:`Damask is a rich woven silk with a pattern visible in the weave; crape a fine, crinkled silk gauze. With white gloves, silk shoes and an ivory-handled parasol, the girl is now dressed with expensive simplicity, quite unlike the schoolgirl's merino of the year before.`,quote:`She wore a dress of black damask, a cape of the same material, and a bonnet of white crape.`},{id:`equal-distance-from-despotism-and-from-anarchy`,term:`'Equal distance from despotism and from anarchy'`,kind:`history`,chapter:191,text:`The juste milieu, the 'happy medium', was the slogan of Louis-Philippe's July Monarchy: neither the absolutism of Charles X nor the republic of the radicals. The pot-bellied bourgeois teaching it to a child of five is Hugo's portrait of the regime's ideal citizen.`,quote:`“Shun excess, my son, keep at an equal distance from despotism and from anarchy.”`},{id:`the-manuel-du-baccalaureat`,term:`The Manuel du Baccalauréat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:191,text:`A cram book for the baccalauréat, the school-leaving examination needed for university and the professions, with set analyses of the classics. Marius's complaint (three Racine tragedies and one Molière comedy) is the Romantic generation's quarrel with official taste.`,quote:`he was thinking that the Manuel du Baccalauréat was a stupid book`},{id:`under-the-cannon-of-the-place`,term:`'Under the cannon of the place'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:191,text:`A military phrase: to come within range of a fortress's guns. Hugo keeps up the mock-heroic campaign language ('Hannibal marched on Rome') for a young man walking past a bench.`,quote:`At the moment when he passed—under the cannon of the place—he felt his heart beat wildly.`},{id:`marcos-obregon-and-gil-blas`,term:`Marcos Obregón and Gil Blas`,kind:`reference`,chapter:191,text:`'Gil Blas' (1715–35) is Lesage's picaresque French novel of a young Spaniard's adventures; it borrowed from the Spanish 'Marcos de Obregón' by Vicente Espinel. François de Neufchâteau, a former minister and academician, published an edition with an essay on those borrowings. Hugo hints that Marius ghost-wrote it: the hack work of a poor scholar.`,quote:`the dissertation on Marcos Obrégon de la Ronde, which M. François de Neufchâteau put, as though it were his own, at the head of his edition of Gil Blas`},{id:`ma-am-bougon`,term:`Ma'am Bougon`,kind:`lore`,chapter:192,text:`Courfeyrac's nickname for the old portress of the Gorbeau house: bougon is French for grumbling, grumpy. Hugo notes that her real name was Burgon, which Courfeyrac 'iconoclast' that he is, would not respect.`,quote:`Ma’am Bougon, as Courfeyrac styled the old portress-principal-tenant`},{id:`the-closing-of-the-luxembourg`,term:`The closing of the Luxembourg`,kind:`custom`,chapter:192,text:`The public gardens of Paris were locked at nightfall; at the Luxembourg a drum or bell warned strollers and the keepers cleared the walks. Marius stays until he is shut out.`,quote:`only went home when the gates of the Luxembourg closed`},{id:`petrarch-and-dante`,term:`Petrarch and Dante`,kind:`reference`,chapter:193,text:`The two great Italian poets of idealised love: Petrarch wrote hundreds of sonnets to Laura, whom he saw in a church at Avignon, and Dante made Beatrice, glimpsed in the streets of Florence, his guide to Paradise. Both loves began with a glance.`,quote:`which would have made Petrarch sing and Dante kneel`},{id:`audry-de-puyraveau`,term:`Audry de Puyraveau`,kind:`history`,chapter:193,text:`Pierre-François Audry de Puyraveau, a radical deputy of the left in the 1830s, whose speeches in the Chamber were reported at length. That Marius suddenly admires one shows a lover finding everything wonderful.`,quote:`What a fine discourse Audry de Puyraveau delivered!`},{id:`frederick-in-l-auberge-des-adrets`,term:`Frédérick in L'Auberge des Adrets`,kind:`history`,chapter:193,text:`Frédérick Lemaître, the great actor of the boulevard theatres, made his name in 1823 at the Porte-Saint-Martin's rival, the Ambigu, by playing the murderous bandit Robert Macaire in the melodrama 'The Inn of Les Adrets' as a comic, swaggering rogue. The part became a sensation and a type.`,quote:`They went to the Porte-Sainte-Martin to see Frédérick in L’Auberge des Adrets.`},{id:`modiste`,term:`Modiste`,kind:`word`,chapter:193,text:`A milliner, a maker of women's hats and trimmings; Paris modistes were young working women with a reputation for coquetry, like grisettes. Courfeyrac would like to 'collect' this one; Marius, newly in love, will not even look.`,quote:`he refused to look at the garter of a modiste who was stepping across a gutter`},{id:`quicherat-s-dictionaries`,term:`Quicherat's dictionaries`,kind:`reference`,chapter:193,text:`Louis Quicherat (printed 'Guicherat') was the compiler of standard Latin dictionaries and grammars used in French schools. Students arguing over their errors is ordinary café talk, which Marius interrupts with a non sequitur about the cross of the Legion of Honour.`,quote:`the faults and omissions in Guicherat’s dictionaries and grammars`},{id:`leonidas-spartacus-the-gladiator`,term:`Leonidas, Spartacus, the Gladiator`,kind:`place`,chapter:194,text:`Statues in the Luxembourg garden, mostly copies after the antique, which Marius uses as cover: Leonidas, the Spartan king who held Thermopylae; Spartacus, the slave who led a revolt against Rome; and a copy of the famous Borghese Gladiator. A lover hides behind heroes.`,quote:`in the shade of a Leonidas or a Spartacus`},{id:`virgil-s-nymphs-and-theocritus-s-fawns`,term:`Virgil's nymphs and Theocritus's fawns`,kind:`reference`,chapter:195,text:`Theocritus, the Greek poet of Sicily, and Virgil after him in the 'Eclogues', wrote pastoral poetry full of nymphs, shepherds and fauns in a world of innocent sensuality. Hugo's gust of spring wind belongs in that world.`,quote:`worthy of Virgil’s nymphs, and the fawns of Theocritus`},{id:`the-robe-of-isis`,term:`The robe of Isis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:195,text:`The veiled statue of the Egyptian goddess at Saïs bore the inscription 'No mortal has lifted my veil': her robe stood for the mystery of nature that must not be uncovered. Hugo calls the girl's dress more sacred still.`,quote:`the robe more sacred than that of Isis`},{id:`the-bartholo-who-exists-in-cherubin`,term:`The Bartholo who exists in Cherubin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:195,text:`In Beaumarchais's plays, Bartholo is the suspicious old guardian who locks up his ward Rosine in 'The Barber of Seville', and Chérubin the adolescent page in love with every woman. Every young lover, says Hugo, has a jealous old guardian inside him.`,quote:`Marius, in whom quivered the Bartholo who exists in Cherubin`},{id:`the-soldier-s-cross-of-saint-louis`,term:`The soldier's cross of Saint-Louis`,kind:`history`,chapter:195,text:`The Order of Saint-Louis was the old monarchy's military order for officers; long-serving ordinary soldiers got a 'veteran's badge', an oval of red cloth with crossed swords. The old man in a Louis XV-style uniform with an empty sleeve and a wooden leg is a pensioner of the Invalides, a 'relic of Mars'.`,quote:`the little oval plaque of red cloth, with the crossed swords, the soldier’s cross of Saint-Louis`},{id:`first-floor-third-floor`,term:`First floor, third floor`,kind:`custom`,chapter:196,text:`French floors are counted from above the ground floor, so the 'first floor' is one flight up. In older Paris houses the first floor was the best and dearest; the higher one climbed, the cheaper and humbler the lodging. A gentleman on the third floor is of modest means.`,quote:`“No,” replied the porter. “He is the gentleman on the third floor.”`},{id:`a-gentleman-of-property`,term:`'A gentleman of property'`,kind:`title`,chapter:196,text:`The porter's phrase translates rentier: a man who lives on income from investments or property rather than from work or a profession. It was a respectable, if vague, social category, and the porter will say no more.`,quote:`“He is a gentleman of property, sir.`},{id:`the-third-lower-floor`,term:`The third lower floor`,kind:`word`,chapter:197,text:`Le troisième dessous: the lowest of the three cellar levels under a theatre stage, where the machinery for trapdoors and scene changes was worked. To 'fall into the third dessous' meant to sink to the very bottom. Hugo uses it for the deepest layer of society, beneath all the honest underground work of new ideas.`,quote:`Human societies all have what is called in theatrical parlance, “a third lower floor.”`},{id:`the-encyclopedia`,term:`The Encyclopedia`,kind:`history`,chapter:197,text:`The great 'Encyclopédie' edited by Diderot and d'Alembert (1751–72), a summary of all knowledge written in a spirit of reason that challenged the Church and absolute monarchy; it was banned and published anyway. Hugo calls it a mine 'almost open to the sky' under the old regime.`,quote:`The Encyclopedia, in the last century, was a mine that was almost open to the sky.`},{id:`the-catacombs`,term:`The catacombs`,kind:`history`,chapter:197,text:`The underground burial galleries of ancient Rome, where, by tradition, early Christians met and said mass in hiding under persecution. For Hugo they are the model of an underground that changes the world above.`,quote:`The catacombs, in which the first mass was said, were not alone the cellar of Rome, they were the vaults of the world.`},{id:`calvin-and-socinus`,term:`Calvin and Socinus`,kind:`history`,chapter:197,text:`John Calvin, the Protestant reformer of Geneva, and Lelio and Fausto Sozzini (Socinus), Italian reformers who denied the Trinity. Calvin was a fierce opponent of anti-Trinitarians and had one of them, Servetus, burned. Reformers underground sometimes fight each other.`,quote:`Calvin seizes Socinius by the hair.`},{id:`from-huss-to-babeuf`,term:`From Huss to Babeuf`,kind:`history`,chapter:197,text:`Hugo's descending ladder of 'miners' who undermined the established order: Jan Hus, the Czech reformer burned in 1415; Luther; Descartes, who founded philosophy on doubt; Voltaire; Condorcet; Robespierre; Marat; and Gracchus Babeuf, guillotined in 1797 for a 'conspiracy of equals' to abolish private property. Saint-Simon, Fourier and the British reformer Robert Owen work in 'lateral galleries'.`,quote:`Below John Huss, there is Luther; below Luther, there is Descartes`},{id:`inferi`,term:`Inferi`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:197,text:`Latin, 'those below': the dead, the underworld. Hugo uses it for the bottom layer of society, cut off from the light, below even the most violent revolutionaries, where there is no idea at all.`,quote:`It is the cellar of the blind. Inferi.`},{id:`the-social-ugolino`,term:`The social Ugolino`,kind:`reference`,chapter:198,text:`Count Ugolino of Pisa, starved to death in a tower with his sons in 1289; in Dante's 'Inferno' he is frozen in the ice of the lowest circle, gnawing the skull of the archbishop who imprisoned him. Hugo's image for hunger turned into savagery at the bottom of society.`,quote:`The social Ugolino is in this gulf.`},{id:`schinderhannes`,term:`Schinderhannes`,kind:`history`,chapter:198,text:`Johannes Bückler, called Schinderhannes, a robber chief of the Rhineland executed at Mainz in 1803 and made a folk hero. Hugo sets him with Cartouche: to such pure criminals even Marat looks like an aristocrat and Babeuf like a speculator.`,quote:`Marat is an aristocrat to Schinderhannes`},{id:`book-fourth`,term:`Book Fourth`,kind:`lore`,chapter:198,text:`Hugo's cross-reference to the Book of this volume devoted to the Friends of the A.B.C. ('The Friends of the A B C'). He is contrasting the 'upper mine' of idealists with the 'cavern of evil' he is about to describe.`,quote:`We have just seen, in Book Fourth, one of the compartments of the upper mine`},{id:`the-sewer-of-the-arche-marion`,term:`The sewer of the Arche-Marion`,kind:`place`,chapter:199,text:`An old covered drain opening onto the Seine near the Pont-Neuf on the right bank, under an arch named after a long-vanished Marion. The vaulted outlets of Paris sewers were known hiding-places for vagrants and criminals.`,quote:`For his lair he had the sewer of the Arche-Marion.`},{id:`the-farnese-hercules`,term:`The Farnese Hercules`,kind:`reference`,chapter:199,text:`A colossal ancient marble statue of Hercules resting on his club, with enormous muscles, found in Rome and kept in the Farnese collection (now in Naples). Hugo dresses it in a labourer's duck trousers (heavy cotton canvas) and a velvet waistcoat.`,quote:`One thought one beheld the Farnese Hercules clad in duck trousers and a cotton velvet waistcoat.`},{id:`marshal-brune`,term:`Marshal Brune`,kind:`history`,chapter:199,text:`Guillaume Brune, one of Napoleon's marshals, murdered at Avignon in August 1815 by a royalist mob during the White Terror; his body was thrown into the Rhône. That Gueulemer, then a porter at Avignon, had 'somewhat to do with' it places him in that mob.`,quote:`He had, probably, somewhat to do with Marshal Brune, having been a porter at Avignon in 1815.`},{id:`creole`,term:`Creole`,kind:`word`,chapter:199,text:`In French usage, a person of European descent born in the colonies, especially the West Indies, though it was also used loosely of people of mixed ancestry. 'Thought to be a creole' says no one knew where Gueulemer came from.`,quote:`He was thought to be a creole.`},{id:`babet-s-tooth-pulling`,term:`Babet's tooth-pulling`,kind:`money`,chapter:199,text:`Fairground tooth-pullers worked from booths with trumpets and grand titles. Babet's prices fall the more teeth one has out (one franc fifty for one, two francs fifty for three), a bargain that ruins the customer's mouth: hence 'take advantage of this opportunity'.`,quote:`Price: one tooth, one franc, fifty centimes; two teeth, two francs; three teeth, two francs, fifty.`},{id:`the-head-of-the-state`,term:`'The head of the State'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:199,text:`Babet sold plaster busts of whoever was ruling: Hugo's careful phrase points out that the head changed often enough (Napoleon, Louis XVIII, Charles X, Louis-Philippe) for a pedlar to keep his options open. Between 1814 and 1830 France had four.`,quote:`selling, in the open air, plaster busts and portraits of “the head of the State.”`},{id:`a-nocturne-for-two-voices`,term:`'A nocturne for two voices'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:199,text:`Babet's joke on Claquesous, who appears only at night and is a ventriloquist: a nocturne is a piece of night music, and he speaks with two voices, his own and his stomach's. The name Claquesous is itself only a nickname.`,quote:`Babet said: “Claquesous is a nocturne for two voices.”`},{id:`garroter`,term:`Garroter`,kind:`word`,chapter:199,text:`A street robber who seizes his victim from behind by the throat, with an arm or a cord, to choke him into submission. Montparnasse has risen, or sunk, from gamin to pickpocket to garroter.`,quote:`It was the street boy turned pickpocket, and a pickpocket turned garroter.`},{id:`the-style-of-1829`,term:`The style of 1829`,kind:`custom`,chapter:199,text:`Young dandies of the late 1820s wore the hat brim turned up on one side to show off a curl of hair, with a nipped-in waist, padded chest and a flower in the buttonhole. Montparnasse wears the fashion of the elegant boulevards with a bludgeon in his pocket.`,quote:`after the style of 1829`},{id:`cain-and-abel`,term:`Cain and Abel`,kind:`reference`,chapter:199,text:`The sons of Adam in Genesis: Cain, the elder, killed his brother Abel out of jealousy, the first murder. Hugo says the first girl who called Montparnasse handsome turned an Abel into a Cain.`,quote:`had made a Cain of this Abel`,seeAlso:[355]},{id:`proteus`,term:`Proteus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:200,text:`The old sea-god of Greek myth who could change shape at will to escape anyone who tried to hold him; Virgil's 'Georgics' describe him turning into a tree, a flame, a stream. Hugo's four ruffians shift identities the same way, lending each other names and hiding-places.`,quote:`These four ruffians formed a sort of Proteus`},{id:`vidocq-and-coco-latour`,term:`Vidocq and Coco-Latour`,kind:`history`,chapter:200,text:`Eugène-François Vidocq (1775–1857), a former convict who became head of the Paris detective brigade, the Sûreté, from 1811 to 1827 and again in 1832, staffed with ex-criminals; his ghost-written memoirs were a best-seller. Coco-Lacour, one of his former-thief agents, succeeded him. Both knew the underworld from inside.`,quote:`striving to escape Vidocq’s indiscreet glances`},{id:`patron-minette`,term:`Patron-Minette`,kind:`lore`,chapter:200,text:`The underworld name of the four men's association. In old popular Paris speech it meant the very early morning, the hour when the night's work ends; Hugo sets it beside 'between dog and wolf' for dusk. A robber's working day runs from one to the other.`,quote:`Patron-Minette—such was the name which was bestowed in the subterranean circulation on the association of these four men.`},{id:`entre-chien-et-loup`,term:`Entre chien et loup`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:200,text:`'Between dog and wolf': the French idiom for dusk, the hour when the light is too dim to tell a dog from a wolf. It is still in common use.`,quote:`entre chien et loup—between dog and wolf—signifies the evening`},{id:`president-of-the-assizes`,term:`President of the Assizes`,kind:`title`,chapter:200,text:`The senior judge presiding over the Cour d'assises, the criminal court that tried serious crimes before a jury. Lacenaire, the poet-murderer tried in 1835, used the gang's name to the judge as a private joke the police would understand.`,quote:`When the President of the Assizes visited Lacenaire in his prison`},{id:`the-names-of-patron-minette`,term:`The names of Patron-Minette`,kind:`lore`,chapter:200,text:`Underworld nicknames, many of them jokes: Mardisoir ('Tuesday evening'), Dépêche ('make haste'), Mangedentelle ('lace-eater'), Les-pieds-en-l'Air ('feet in the air', a hanged man's posture), and Demi-Liard ('half-farthing') called Deux-Milliards ('two billion'). Hugo says each name stands for a type of criminal, not just a man.`,quote:`Demi-Liard, called Deux-Milliards.`},{id:`quarries-of-montmartre-and-montrouge`,term:`Quarries of Montmartre and Montrouge`,kind:`place`,chapter:200,text:`Paris was built of stone and gypsum quarried from under and around the city, and the abandoned galleries at Montmartre in the north and Montrouge in the south, with the lime-kilns beside them, were sleeping places for vagrants and hideouts for criminals. They 'ran to earth' by day.`,quote:`sometimes in the abandoned quarries of Montmatre or Montrouge`},{id:`ambubaiarum-collegia`,term:`Ambubaiarum collegia`,kind:`reference`,chapter:200,text:`From Horace's 'Satires': 'the guilds of Syrian flute-girls, quack-druggists, beggars, actresses', the riff-raff mourning a dead singer in Rome. Hugo's point: the same underworld existed under Augustus and will exist as long as society stays as it is.`,quote:`Horace speaks of them: Ambubaiarum collegia, pharmacopolae, mendici, mimae`},{id:`the-chaumiere`,term:`The Chaumière`,kind:`place`,chapter:201,text:`A popular open-air dance garden on the Boulevard du Montparnasse, haunt of students and grisettes in the 1820s and 30s. Courfeyrac's cure for Marius's gloom is the standard student one: go dancing.`,quote:`Here, come to the Chaumière.`},{id:`the-ball-at-sceaux`,term:`The ball at Sceaux`,kind:`custom`,chapter:201,text:`Sceaux, a village a few miles south of Paris, held a famous Sunday dance in its park, reached by crowded carriages and carts. Grantaire's sneer that this is where 'all lost women' turn up reflects its reputation as a place of easy pleasure.`,quote:`the ball at Sceaux`},{id:`candlemas-and-mathieu-laensberg`,term:`Candlemas and Mathieu Laensberg`,kind:`custom`,chapter:202,text:`Candlemas, 2 February, was folk weather-day: if the sun shone, the bear went back into its den for six more weeks of winter (the origin of Groundhog Day). Mathieu Laensberg was the supposed author of a cheap almanac from Liège, full of such sayings. Hugo uses the rhyme to date the snow that falls on the Gorbeau house.`,quote:`that ancient Candlemas day`},{id:`the-bobbies`,term:`The bobbies`,kind:`word`,chapter:202,text:`Hapgood's English for a slang name for the police (in French, 'les cognes'). The girls on the boulevard use it, and Marius only half understands; it is the first taste in this part of the book of the underworld's own language.`,quote:`The bobbies have come.`},{id:`begging-letter-writing`,term:`Begging-letter writing`,kind:`custom`,chapter:203,text:`Writing false petitions to the rich under invented identities was a recognised Paris trade. Each letter here plays to its reader: a Spanish royalist exile for a legitimist marquise, an abandoned mother for a countess, a playwright for a shopkeeper with literary pretensions, a 'dramatic artist' for a churchgoer. The spelling mistakes repeated across all four give the game away.`,quote:`all four were written by the same hand`},{id:`spanish-victim-of-legitimacy`,term:`Spanish victim of legitimacy`,kind:`history`,chapter:203,text:`After France crushed the Spanish liberals in 1823 and restored Ferdinand VII, Spanish exiles of both camps drifted through Paris and appealed to French royalists for help. 'Legitimacy' was the royalist watchword: the right of the old dynasty against usurpers. A sham 'Don Alvarès' is calculated to touch a marquise who mourns the fallen Bourbons.`,quote:`the sacred cause of legitimacy`},{id:`the-theatre-francais`,term:`The Théâtre-Français`,kind:`place`,chapter:203,text:`The Comédie-Française, the state theatre in the Rue de Richelieu, home of classical tragedy and comedy. Getting a play accepted there was the summit of a playwright's hopes; the begging 'man of letters' claims to have sent it a drama.`,quote:`the Théâtre-Français`},{id:`saint-jacques-du-haut-pas`,term:`Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas`,kind:`place`,chapter:203,text:`A parish church in the Rue Saint-Jacques, on the slope of the Latin Quarter south of the Luxembourg. The 'benevolent gentleman' who hears mass there every day is the old man the begging letter targets.`,quote:`the church of Saint-Jacques-du-haut-Pas`},{id:`vaudevilles`,term:`Vaudevilles`,kind:`word`,chapter:204,text:`Light comic plays with popular songs set to well-known tunes; their refrains circulated in the streets like hit songs. The girl hums scraps of them as she prowls Marius's room.`,quote:`she hummed scraps of vaudevilles`},{id:`j-ai-faim-mon-pere-pas-de-fricot`,term:`'J'ai faim, mon père. Pas de fricot.'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:204,text:`A mock-cheerful street rhyme: 'I'm hungry, father. No stew. I'm cold, mother. No knitting.' The jaunty tune and the grim words together are the girl's whole manner.`,quote:`J’ai faim, mon père.`},{id:`the-shiner-a-monarch`,term:`'The shiner! a monarch!'`,kind:`word`,chapter:204,text:`Slang names for a five-franc piece, the big silver coin. Five francs was several days' food for a family like this one, which is why the coin melts 'the avalanches of slang' in her head.`,quote:`Five francs! the shiner! a monarch!`},{id:`lavater`,term:`Lavater`,kind:`reference`,chapter:206,text:`Johann Kaspar Lavater, Swiss pastor whose books on physiognomy (1770s) claimed to read character in the face. Hugo borrows the idea for his portrait of Jondrette: vulture and pettifogger in one face.`,quote:`If Lavater had studied this visage`},{id:`the-dream-and-maringo-austerlits-iena-wagramme-elot`,term:`'The Dream' and 'Maringo Austerlits Iena Wagramme Elot'`,kind:`history`,chapter:206,text:`A cheap coloured print of Bonapartist sentiment: Napoleon in glory, and a sleeping child, Napoleon's son the King of Rome, whose crown a woman pushes away. The column lists Napoleon's victories, Marengo, Austerlitz, Jena, Wagram, Eylau, comically misspelled. The family who hang it call themselves 'fine Bonapartists'.`,quote:`Maringo Austerlits Iena Wagramme Elot`},{id:`ducray-duminil`,term:`Ducray-Duminil`,kind:`reference`,chapter:206,text:`François Ducray-Duminil (Hugo spells him 'Duchray'), author of tearful, wildly popular sentimental novels around 1800. The volumes lying about the garret are the reading-room romances that also gave Jondrette's daughters their high-flown names.`,quote:`by Duchray Duminil 1814`},{id:`canaille`,term:`Canaille`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:206,text:`French for rabble, scum. Hugo calls Jondrette's 'everybody is canaille!' a variant of Solomon's 'Vanity of vanities, all is vanity' (Ecclesiastes): the cynic's version of the preacher's despair.`,quote:`Canaille! canaille! everybody is canaille!`},{id:`fiacre`,term:`Fiacre`,kind:`word`,chapter:207,text:`A hackney cab hired in the street, named after the Hôtel Saint-Fiacre where the first cab stand stood. Arriving in one marks a visitor as comfortably off; hence Jondrette's 'He is Rothschild', the name of the great banking family.`,quote:`He is coming in a fiacre.`},{id:`i-suppress-the-liberty-of-the-press`,term:`'I suppress the liberty of the press'`,kind:`history`,chapter:207,text:`Jondrette's joke when his wife protests. Charles X's attempt to muzzle the press by decree was one of the July Ordinances of 1830 that brought down his throne; the phrase had become a catchword for tyranny.`,quote:`I suppress the liberty of the press.`},{id:`talma-and-mademoiselle-mars`,term:`Talma and Mademoiselle Mars`,kind:`reference`,chapter:209,text:`François-Joseph Talma (died 1826) was the great tragic actor of the Empire, Napoleon's favourite; Mademoiselle Mars was the leading comic actress of the Comédie-Française. Jondrette, posing as the actor Fabantou, claims Talma as his teacher and Mars as his friend.`,quote:`I am a pupil of Talma!`},{id:`celimene-elmire-belisaire`,term:`Célimène, Elmire, Bélisaire`,kind:`reference`,chapter:209,text:`Célimène and Elmire are heroines of Molière (The Misanthrope, Tartuffe), roles Mademoiselle Mars made famous; Belisarius was the Byzantine general said to have ended as a blind beggar. Jondrette casts himself as the ruined old soldier begging from the actress.`,quote:`Elmire would bestow alms on Bélisaire!`},{id:`cab-tariff-two-francs-an-hour`,term:`Cab tariff: two francs an hour`,kind:`money`,chapter:210,text:`Paris cabs had fixed rates; by the hour, cheaper by the trip. Two francs was a day's pay for many workers. Marius, with sixteen sous in his pocket after giving away his five francs, cannot pay in advance, and the driver whistles and leaves.`,quote:`“Pay in advance,” said the coachman.`},{id:`the-air-of-la-palisse`,term:`The air of La Palisse`,kind:`reference`,chapter:210,text:`The tune of a comic song about Jacques de la Palice, whose verses state the absurdly obvious ('a quarter of an hour before his death, he was still alive'). The cabman's whistling is a mocking dismissal.`,quote:`the air of La Palisse`},{id:`la-force`,term:`La Force`,kind:`place`,chapter:210,text:`A prison in the Marais, in the old town house of the dukes of La Force, used from the Revolution for debtors and criminals awaiting trial. The 'Fosse-aux-Lions', the Lions' Den, was its yard for the most dangerous inmates.`,quote:`he was discussed at La Force in the Fosse-aux-Lions`},{id:`croesus`,term:`Croesus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:212,text:`The proverbially rich king of ancient Lydia. Jondrette's name for the old gentleman shows how he sees him: not as a benefactor but as a fortune walking into reach.`,quote:`The Croesus is caught`},{id:`bushels-of-charcoal`,term:`Bushels of charcoal`,kind:`custom`,chapter:212,text:`The poor of Paris heated and cooked with charcoal bought by the bushel and burned in a brazier, a portable iron pan. Two bushels for thirty sous is an odd extravagance in a household that has just doused its fire to look poor.`,quote:`“Two good ones.”`},{id:`solus-cum-solo-in-loco-remoto`,term:`'Solus cum solo, in loco remoto…'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:213,text:`Latin: 'Alone with one alone, in a remote place, they will not be thought to be saying the Lord's Prayer', an old moralist's saying about suspicious meetings. Hugo uses it for the two men talking in the snow behind the wall.`},{id:`five-hundred-balls`,term:`Five hundred balls`,kind:`word`,chapter:213,text:`Thieves' slang: 'balles' were francs. The man in the snow means the job is worth five hundred francs a head, against a risk of five to ten years.`,quote:`five hundred balls`},{id:`the-gaite-theatre`,term:`The Gaîté Theatre`,kind:`place`,chapter:213,text:`One of the boulevard theatres on the Boulevard du Temple, the 'Boulevard of Crime', famous for melodrama full of murders and rescues. The two plotters chat about last night's melodrama while planning a real one.`,quote:`the Gaîté Theatre`},{id:`knock-me-downs`,term:`Knock-me-downs`,kind:`word`,chapter:214,text:`Hapgood's rendering of 'coups-de-poing': small pocket pistols, short-barrelled and meant for close range, the kind a police officer could carry unseen.`,quote:`of the sort called “knock-me-downs.”`},{id:`gorbeau`,term:`Gorbeau`,kind:`lore`,chapter:214,text:`The house on the Boulevard de l'Hôpital is named after a former owner, which is why the inspector, holding Marius's key, mutters 'Former property of Gorbeau': he knows the building well.`,quote:`Former property of Gorbeau.`},{id:`cold-chisel`,term:`Cold chisel`,kind:`word`,chapter:215,text:`A heavy all-steel chisel for cutting metal, struck with a hammer; the name means it works on cold iron, without a forge. Jondrette hides the one he buys under his greatcoat.`,quote:`a huge cold chisel with a white wood handle`},{id:`dine-like-charles-the-tenth`,term:`'Dine like Charles the Tenth'`,kind:`history`,chapter:216,text:`Charles X, last Bourbon king of France, reigned 1824–30 and was overthrown in the July Revolution. To dine like him is to dine like a king; by 1832 he is a king in exile.`,quote:`You shall dine like Charles the Tenth`},{id:`nos-amours-ont-dure-toute-une-semaine`,term:`'Nos amours ont duré toute une semaine'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:216,text:`'Our love lasted a whole week, but how short are the moments of happiness! Adoring each other for eight days, was it worth the trouble?' A sentimental song of the day the girl sings to Marius's mirror; the second, 'You leave me to go to glory', is another romance of parted lovers.`,quote:`Nos amours ont duré toute une semaine`},{id:`diogenes-turned-cartouche`,term:`Diogenes turned Cartouche`,kind:`reference`,chapter:217,text:`Diogenes the Cynic was said to search Athens with a lantern for an honest man; Cartouche was the notorious Paris robber chief broken on the wheel in 1721. A thieves' dark-lantern is Diogenes's lamp put to the opposite use.`,quote:`worthy of Diogenes turned Cartouche`},{id:`louis`,term:`Louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:218,text:`A gold coin of twenty francs, still called a louis after the old royal coins whatever king was on it. Four louis, eighty francs, is far more than the sixty the 'actor' asked for.`,quote:`He laid four louis on the table.`},{id:`the-bourbe`,term:`The Bourbe`,kind:`place`,chapter:219,text:`The popular name ('the Mire') for the Port-Royal maternity hospital and dispensary near the Gorbeau quarter, where the poor went for free treatment. Jondrette's story is that the elder girl has taken her sister there.`,quote:`taken her to the Bourbe`},{id:`jacobin-bousingot`,term:`Jacobin, bousingot`,kind:`history`,chapter:219,text:`A Jacobin was a radical of the 1790s, and by extension any republican; a 'bousingot' was a young republican hothead of the early 1830s, known by his varnished sailor's hat. Jondrette protests he is neither, flattering his visitor's supposed respectability.`,quote:`I am not Jacobin, sir, I am not a bousingot.`},{id:`new-year-s-boxes`,term:`New Year's boxes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:219,text:`The French gave presents (étrennes) on New Year's Day, and making the fancy cardboard boxes for sweets and gifts was a small trade open to poor girls. Jondrette claims he meant to apprentice his daughters to it.`,quote:`the making of boxes for New Year’s gifts`},{id:`philippes`,term:`Philippes`,kind:`money`,chapter:220,text:`Gold twenty-franc pieces struck under Louis-Philippe, the king since 1830, nicknamed after him as earlier coins were called louis or napoleons. The four the visitor lays down are eighty francs, two years' rent for the garret, which lets for forty francs a year.`,quote:`the four miserable Philippes`},{id:`david-at-bruqueselles`,term:`David at 'Bruqueselles'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:220,text:`Jacques-Louis David, the great painter of the Revolution and the Empire, died in exile at Brussels in 1825 after the Restoration banished the regicides. Claiming the daub is 'by David' is Thénardier's absurd attempt to put a price on a tavern signboard.`,quote:`painted by David at Bruqueselles`},{id:`chimney-builders`,term:`Chimney-builders`,kind:`word`,chapter:220,text:`The men with blackened faces: Hugo calls them 'fumistes', stove-and-chimney workers, whose trade left them sooty. Soot was the cheap mask of the barrière bandit.`,quote:`the “chimney-builders,”`},{id:`benvenuto-cellini-and-villon`,term:`Benvenuto Cellini and Villon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:220,text:`Cellini was the Renaissance goldsmith and sculptor; François Villon the fifteenth-century poet who was also a thief. Hugo's point: the galleys hold artists as fine as these, like the convict who can hollow out a sou to hide a watch-spring saw.`,quote:`There are Benvenuto Cellinis in the galleys`},{id:`frederick-ii-at-potsdam`,term:`Frederick II at Potsdam`,kind:`reference`,chapter:221,text:`Frederick the Great of Prussia reviewed his drilled regiments at Potsdam with a famously cold and complete eye. Javert names each ruffian as he passes them in review, like a general who knows every soldier.`,quote:`a glance of a Frederick II at a Potsdam parade`},{id:`like-men-of-auvergne`,term:`'Like men of Auvergne'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:221,text:`Auvergnats, migrants from the poor mountains of central France, did much of Paris's heavy work as water-carriers, coal-heavers and porters, and had a name for settling things with their fists. The phrase means a rough-and-tumble brawl.`,quote:`collaring each other like men of Auvergne`},{id:`saint-lazare-and-the-madelonettes`,term:`Saint-Lazare and the Madelonettes`,kind:`place`,chapter:222,text:`Two Paris prisons for women. Saint-Lazare, an old leper-house in the Faubourg Saint-Denis, held prostitutes and women awaiting trial; the Madelonettes, a former convent for repentant women in the Marais, held others. With the father at La Force, the whole family is now behind bars.`,quote:`“At Saint-Lazare.”`},{id:`le-roi-coupdesabot`,term:`'Le roi Coupdesabot'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:222,text:`A nonsense song, 'King Clog-chop went a-hunting, a-hunting for crows', sung by the gamin as he goes. It sets the tone of the scene: a child told where his whole family is jailed answers 'Ah!' and walks off singing.`,quote:`Le roi Coupdesabot`},{id:`the-revolution-of-july`,term:`The Revolution of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`The 'Three Glorious Days', 27–29 July 1830, when Paris rose against Charles X, built barricades and drove the last Bourbon king into exile. It ended not in a republic but in a new king, Louis-Philippe. Hugo opens Volume IV with it because 1831–32, the years of this part of the story, are its aftershock.`,quote:`the Revolution of July`},{id:`movement-and-resistance`,term:`Movement and resistance`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`The two camps of the early July Monarchy: the 'Movement' wanted the 1830 revolution carried further, toward wider suffrage and support for liberal causes abroad; the 'Resistance' wanted it stopped and order kept. Hugo borrows the labels to describe the whole epoch.`,quote:`movement and resistance`},{id:`the-king-of-yvetot`,term:`The King of Yvetot`,kind:`reference`,chapter:223,text:`A song by Béranger (1813) about a tiny, lazy, contented king who 'rose late, went to bed early' and made no wars. Hugo uses it for what a France exhausted by Napoleon wanted: a small, quiet king. Prusias, a minor king of Bithynia, plays the same role against Caesar.`,quote:`Napoleon for the King of Yvetot`},{id:`the-charter-of-1814`,term:`The Charter of 1814`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`The constitution Louis XVIII issued on his return, guaranteeing a parliament, equality before the law and press freedom. The Bourbons treated it as a gift 'granted' by the king, which he could take back. Hugo's point is that these were rights won, not favours.`,quote:`the charter of Louis XVIII`},{id:`the-ordinances-of-july`,term:`The ordinances of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`Four decrees signed by Charles X on 25 July 1830: they suspended press freedom, dissolved the newly elected Chamber, and cut the electorate to favour great landowners. Paris answered with barricades within two days.`,quote:`the ordinances of July`},{id:`the-stuarts-and-the-protector`,term:`The Stuarts and the Protector`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`Hugo's English parallel. After Cromwell's Protectorate, Charles II was restored in 1660 and the Stuarts, like the Bourbons, overreached; James II was driven out in 1688 and replaced by a more pliable branch. France in 1830 repeated the pattern.`,quote:`what England demanded of the Stuarts after the Protector`},{id:`charles-x-s-round-table`,term:`Charles X's round table`,kind:`history`,chapter:223,text:`Leaving France from Cherbourg in August 1830, the fallen king is said to have had a round table cut square on board ship so that court precedence could still be observed at meals. Hugo uses it to show the Bourbons minding etiquette while losing a crown.`,quote:`causing a round table to be cut over into a square table`},{id:`guillaume-du-vair`,term:`Guillaume du Vair`,kind:`reference`,chapter:223,text:`A magistrate and moralist of the late sixteenth century. The 'day of the Barricades' he refers to was 12 May 1588, when Paris first barricaded its streets, against Henri III. His words of loyalty to fallen kings are what Hugo says France practised in 1830.`,quote:`Guillaume du Vair after the day of the Barricades`},{id:`iturbide`,term:`Iturbide`,kind:`history`,chapter:224,text:`Agustín de Iturbide, a Mexican officer who had himself crowned Emperor of Mexico in 1822 and was overthrown within a year. Hugo's example of the 'first man of fortune' turned into a king, as against Napoleon, the man of genius.`,quote:`in the second, Iturbide`},{id:`the-house-of-brunswick-and-1688`,term:`The House of Brunswick and 1688`,kind:`history`,chapter:224,text:`England's 'Glorious Revolution' of 1688 replaced James II with William and Mary, and later the crown passed to the German House of Hanover (Brunswick-Lüneburg). For Hugo, England had already shown how to halt a revolution by finding a new branch of royalty, which 1830 copied with the House of Orléans.`,quote:`the House of Brunswick or the House of Orleans`},{id:`the-bourgeoisie`,term:`The bourgeoisie`,kind:`word`,chapter:224,text:`The middle class of property, trade and the professions, which ran France after 1830: only men of means could vote. Hugo refuses to call it a class or caste: 'The bourgeois is the man who now has time to sit down.'`,quote:`A chair is not a caste.`},{id:`the-221`,term:`The 221`,kind:`history`,chapter:224,text:`The 221 deputies who in March 1830 voted an address of no confidence in Charles X's ministry. When the king answered with the July Ordinances, the same Chamber offered the crown to Louis-Philippe. Hugo calls them a half-Parliament that elected a half-king.`,quote:`The 221 made Louis Philippe King.`},{id:`lafayette-at-the-hotel-de-ville`,term:`Lafayette at the Hôtel de Ville`,kind:`history`,chapter:224,text:`The Marquis de Lafayette, hero of the American and French Revolutions, commanded the National Guard in 1830. On 31 July he embraced Louis-Philippe on the balcony of the town hall wrapped in a tricolour flag, which won the crowd; the new regime was called 'the best of republics'. The town hall replaced Reims cathedral, where French kings had been crowned.`,quote:`Lafayette undertook the coronation.`},{id:`the-july-monarchy`,term:`The July Monarchy`,kind:`history`,chapter:224,text:`The regime of 1830–48 under Louis-Philippe: a constitutional monarchy of property-owners, with a narrow electorate, the tricolour flag and a 'citizen king'. Hugo calls it 'the halt', a revolution stopped halfway; writing in the 1860s, he knows it lasted until the revolution of 1848.`,quote:`1830 is a revolution arrested midway.`},{id:`philippe-egalite`,term:`Philippe Égalité`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Louis-Philippe's father, the Duke of Orléans, who took the name 'Equality' in the Revolution and as a deputy voted for the death of his cousin Louis XVI, before going to the guillotine himself in 1793. Hence Hugo's 'a father to whom history will accord certain attenuating circumstances', and the jibe 'Prince Equality' at the son.`,quote:`To be Prince Equality`},{id:`valmy-and-jemappes`,term:`Valmy and Jemappes`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`The first victories of the French revolutionary armies in 1792. The young Louis-Philippe, then Duke of Chartres, fought in both, which let the July King claim a revolutionary past.`,quote:`a general at Valmy; a soldier at Jemappes`},{id:`ancona-antwerp-pritchard`,term:`Ancona, Antwerp, Pritchard`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Episodes of Louis-Philippe's foreign policy: French troops occupied Ancona in 1832 to check Austria in Italy; a French army besieged Antwerp in 1832 to secure Belgian independence; and in 1844 France paid compensation to George Pritchard, a British missionary expelled from Tahiti, to avoid war with England, which many French saw as humiliating.`,quote:`bombarding Antwerp, and paying off Pritchard`},{id:`attacked-eight-times-by-regicides`,term:`Attacked eight times by regicides`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Louis-Philippe survived repeated assassination attempts. The worst was Fieschi's 'infernal machine', a rack of gun barrels fired at the king's procession on the Boulevard du Temple in July 1835, which killed eighteen people.`,quote:`attacked eight times by regicides and always smiling`},{id:`the-rue-transnonain`,term:`The Rue Transnonain`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`During the Paris rising of April 1834, soldiers fired on from a house at 12 Rue Transnonain stormed it and killed the residents, men, women and children. Daumier's lithograph made it the emblem of the regime's repression.`,quote:`the Rue Transnonain`,seeAlso:[320]},{id:`the-legal-country`,term:`The legal country`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`The 'pays légal': the small body of voters, fewer than 200,000 out of over thirty million, as against the 'pays réel', the real country. Hugo's 'three hundred thousand privileged persons' is the July Monarchy's whole electorate.`,quote:`the absorption of the real country by the legal country`},{id:`abd-el-kader-blaye-deutz`,term:`Abd-el-Kader, Blaye, Deutz`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Charges against the reign. Abd-el-Kader led Algerian resistance to the French conquest and was imprisoned despite a promise of free exile. The Duchesse de Berry, who tried to raise the west for her son in 1832, was betrayed for money by Simon Deutz and held in the fortress of Blaye.`,quote:`Abd-el-Kader, Blaye, Deutz bought`},{id:`the-iron-cage-of-mont-saint-michel`,term:`The iron cage of Mont-Saint-Michel`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`A wooden-and-iron cage in the abbey prison of Mont-Saint-Michel, said to date from Louis XI, in which state prisoners were confined. The young Louis-Philippe helped break it up in 1788, a gesture the liberal bourgeoisie remembered.`,quote:`the iron cage of Mont-Saint-Michel`},{id:`the-laws-of-september`,term:`The laws of September`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Laws passed in September 1835 after the Fieschi attempt, which tightened press censorship and made it easier to convict for political offences. Hugo concedes they existed even as he calls Louis-Philippe 'a king of the broad daylight'.`,quote:`The laws of September are open to sight.`},{id:`casimir-perier`,term:`Casimir Périer`,kind:`history`,chapter:225,text:`Banker and Louis-Philippe's chief minister from March 1831, the strong man of the 'Resistance', who crushed the Lyons rising and kept order at home. Hugo makes him stand for the narrow side of the bourgeoisie, against the king's liberal side.`,quote:`one of the victories of Casimir Périer`},{id:`two-tombs-in-exile`,term:`Two tombs in exile`,kind:`lore`,chapter:225,text:`Louis-Philippe was overthrown in 1848 and died in England in 1850; Hugo wrote this chapter in exile on Guernsey, having refused to live under Napoleon III. So he writes of himself as a ghost testifying for another ghost: 'one shade may console another shade.'`,quote:`two tombs in exile`},{id:`legitimists`,term:`Legitimists`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`Supporters of the elder Bourbon line, who regarded Charles X and his grandson as the only lawful kings and Louis-Philippe as a usurper. With the republicans they attacked the July throne from the opposite side.`,quote:`the old legitimist parties`},{id:`socialists`,term:`Socialists`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`In 1832 a new word for thinkers such as Saint-Simon, Fourier and their followers, who turned from political rights to the organisation of work and wealth. Hugo sums their programme up as two problems: to produce wealth, and to share it.`,quote:`the generic title of socialists`},{id:`armed-peace`,term:`Armed peace`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`Europe after 1830: no open war, but standing armies on alert and the great monarchies, led by Austria's chancellor Metternich, watching revolutionary France. Hugo calls it a 'ruinous expedient'.`,quote:`armed peace`},{id:`the-omens-of-1832`,term:`The omens of 1832`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`Hugo's catalogue of the year: the last Prince de Condé found hanged (1830); Belgium's revolt against the Dutch Nassaus and its crown given to Leopold of Saxe-Coburg; Tsar Nicholas I crushing Poland (Warsaw fell in 1831); absolutist Ferdinand VII in Spain and Dom Miguel in Portugal; Austria in Bologna. At home, the banker Laffitte ruined, Benjamin Constant dead poor, Casimir Périer dead.`,quote:`the last Prince de Condé engulfed in the shadows`},{id:`the-cross-torn-from-notre-dame`,term:`The cross torn from Notre Dame`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`In February 1831 a legitimist memorial mass at Saint-Germain-l'Auxerrois set off a riot; crowds sacked the church and the archbishop's palace, and crosses were pulled from churches, Notre-Dame included. In the same mood the royal lilies were scraped off the king's carriages.`,quote:`the cross torn from Notre Dame`},{id:`lyons-and-servile-war`,term:`Lyons and 'servile war'`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`In November 1831 the silk-weavers (canuts) of Lyons, starving on falling piece-rates, rose under the banner 'Live working or die fighting' and held the city for days. Hugo calls it a servile war, a war of the labouring class, beside the political civil war in Paris.`,quote:`at Lyons servile war`},{id:`the-duchesse-de-berry-in-la-vendee`,term:`The Duchesse de Berry in la Vendée`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`The widowed daughter-in-law of Charles X landed in the south in spring 1832 and tried to raise the royalist west for her son, the 'Henri V' of the legitimists. The rising failed within weeks.`,quote:`the Duchesse de Berry in la Vendée`},{id:`the-cholera-of-1832`,term:`The cholera of 1832`,kind:`history`,chapter:226,text:`Asiatic cholera reached Paris in March 1832 and killed some 18,000 people in six months, mostly the poor of crowded old quarters; Casimir Périer died of it in May. Rumours that the poor were being poisoned added to the anger in the faubourgs.`,quote:`cholera`},{id:`the-faubourg-saint-antoine`,term:`The Faubourg Saint-Antoine`,kind:`place`,chapter:227,text:`The workers' district east of the Bastille, home of furniture-makers and artisans, which had stormed the Bastille in 1789 and risen again in 1830. Hugo calls it the 'powder-house of suffering and ideas', the place Paris watches whenever revolt is in the air.`,quote:`Paris kept an eye on the Faubourg Saint-Antoine`},{id:`babouvist-and-gisquet`,term:`Babouvist and Gisquet`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`A Babouvist followed Gracchus Babeuf, executed in 1797 for plotting a communist 'Conspiracy of the Equals'. Henri Gisquet was Prefect of Police from 1831. The crowd suspects the loud 'Babouvist' on the corner post of being a police agent provocateur.`,quote:`beneath Babeuf, the people scented Gisquet`},{id:`jacquerie`,term:`Jacquerie`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`The great peasant rising of 1358 in northern France, named after 'Jacques Bonhomme', the nickname for a peasant. It came to mean any revolt of the poor against the rich. Hugo insists he does not use it as an insult: 'The Jacques were the poor.'`,quote:`The Jacques were the poor.`},{id:`quinturions-centurions-decurions`,term:`Quinturions, centurions, decurions`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`Ranks in the cell structure of the secret societies, borrowed from Roman army units of five, a hundred and ten men, with éclaireurs, scouts. The lists Hugo reproduces are from real police finds of 1832.`,quote:`quinturions, centurions, decurions, éclaireurs`},{id:`the-society-of-the-rights-of-man`,term:`The Society of the Rights of Man`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`The chief republican secret society of the early 1830s, grown out of the Friends of the People, organised in small sections with names like 'Robespierre' or 'Phrygian cap'. It dated its orders by the revolutionary calendar ('Pluviôse, Year 40') and was broken after the risings of 1834.`,quote:`the Society of the Rights of Man`},{id:`ca-ira-and-january-21`,term:`Ça Ira and January 21`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`Section names chosen to frighten: 'Ça ira' ('it'll be fine') was the revolutionary song of 1790 that promised to hang aristocrats from lamp-posts; 21 January 1793 was the day Louis XVI was guillotined.`,quote:`Ça Ira`},{id:`the-court-of-peers`,term:`The Court of Peers`,kind:`history`,chapter:227,text:`The upper chamber of parliament sitting as a high court to try crimes against the state. It tried the rebels of these years in mass trials, and the defiant answers Hugo quotes come from its records.`,quote:`a man accused before the Court of Peers`},{id:`jeu-de-siam`,term:`Jeu de Siam`,kind:`custom`,chapter:227,text:`A wine-shop game, a kind of skittles in which a weighted disc was rolled to knock down pins. It is a detail Hugo takes from the police reports.`,quote:`a Jeu de Siam`},{id:`the-sibylline-wine`,term:`The sibylline wine`,kind:`reference`,chapter:227,text:`The Sibyl was the prophetess of ancient Italy who spoke in a frenzy; Ennius, an early Roman poet, gives the phrase. Hugo makes the faubourg's wine-shops into oracle-taverns where men grow drunk 'more on words than on wine'. 'Ingens', huge, is Virgil's word for the blind Cyclops.`,quote:`the “sibylline wine.”`},{id:`dupuytren`,term:`Dupuytren`,kind:`reference`,chapter:228,text:`Guillaume Dupuytren, the most celebrated surgeon of the day, chief at the Hôtel-Dieu, whose clinical lectures drew medical students in crowds. Joly, the medical student, is sent to sound them.`,quote:`Dupuytren’s clinical lecture`},{id:`the-barriere-du-maine-and-richefeu-s`,term:`The Barrière du Maine and Richefeu's`,kind:`place`,chapter:228,text:`The barrier on the southern edge of Paris beyond Montparnasse, outside the toll wall, where wine was cheap; Richefeu's was a wine-shop there. Its marble-cutters and sculptors' workmen are the lukewarm recruits Grantaire offers to warm up.`,quote:`The Barrière du Maine`},{id:`robespierre-waistcoat`,term:`Robespierre waistcoat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:228,text:`A waistcoat with wide lapels in the style of 1793, worn by young republicans of the 1830s as a political badge; Grantaire's is red. Prudhomme's revolutionary newspaper, Rousseau's Social Contract and the constitution of Year Two (1793) are the reading he boasts of.`,quote:`a Robespierre waistcoat`},{id:`hebertist`,term:`Hébertist`,kind:`history`,chapter:228,text:`A follower of Jacques Hébert, the most violent and foul-mouthed of the Terror's journalists, guillotined by Robespierre in 1794. Grantaire calls himself 'a bit of a Hébertist' as a boast of being further left than the left.`,quote:`I am even a bit of a Hébertist.`},{id:`absinthe`,term:`Absinthe`,kind:`word`,chapter:228,text:`A strong green wormwood spirit, then becoming fashionable in Paris. Enjolras tells Grantaire to sleep it off.`,quote:`Sleep yourself sober from your absinthe.`},{id:`the-quarries-of-issy`,term:`The quarries of Issy`,kind:`place`,chapter:228,text:`The south-west edge of Paris was honeycombed with old stone quarries, abandoned workings that made good secret meeting-places. The Cougourde's members in Paris meet in one.`,quote:`one of the abandoned quarries`},{id:`the-rue-de-la-verrerie`,term:`The Rue de la Verrerie`,kind:`place`,chapter:229,text:`A street of the old Marais quarter near the Hôtel de Ville, in the tangle of narrow streets where the risings of the 1830s were fought. Courfeyrac's move there 'for political reasons' is a radical student going where the fighting is likely to be.`,quote:`he had gone to live in the Rue de la Verrerie “for political reasons;”`},{id:`escousse-and-lebras`,term:`Escousse and Lebras`,kind:`history`,chapter:229,text:`Victor Escousse and Auguste Lebras, two very young poets who, after their play failed, suffocated themselves together with charcoal fumes in February 1832. Their suicide was much discussed as a symptom of romantic despair; Hugo cites them as a warning of where pure reverie can lead.`,quote:`Excess of reverie breeds men like Escousse and Lebras.`},{id:`the-gobelins-river-and-the-lark-s-meadow`,term:`The Gobelins river and the Lark's meadow`,kind:`place`,chapter:229,text:`The Bièvre, a small polluted stream that ran through the tanneries and dye-works of the Gobelins quarter to the Seine, here called the 'river of the Gobelins'. Near the Rue Croulebarbe it still crossed open meadows hung with washing. A passer-by gives one of them its local name, the Lark's meadow, and the word catches Marius.`,quote:`“It is the Lark’s meadow.”`},{id:`ulbach-and-the-shepherdess-of-ivry`,term:`Ulbach and the shepherdess of Ivry`,kind:`history`,chapter:229,text:`A notorious crime of 1827: Honoré Ulbach, a young wine-shop servant, stabbed Aimée Millot, a young goat-girl from Ivry who had rejected him, and was guillotined; Hugo's passer-by places the murder in this very field. The passer-by's gruesome footnote is lost on Marius, who hears only 'Lark'.`,quote:`It was here that Ulbach killed the shepherdess of Ivry.`},{id:`val-de-grace-and-the-pantheon`,term:`Val-de-Grâce and the Pantheon`,kind:`place`,chapter:229,text:`Two domes on the southern skyline of Paris seen from the Gobelins: the Val-de-Grâce, a seventeenth-century convent church turned military hospital, and the Pantheon, the former church of Sainte-Geneviève made into a temple to France's great men. The 'pole of the Deaf-Mutes' is the tower of the Institution for the Deaf in the Rue Saint-Jacques.`,quote:`the Pantheon, the pole of the Deaf-Mutes, the Val-de-Grâce`},{id:`nemorin-and-schinderhannes`,term:`Nemorin and Schinderhannes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:230,text:`Némorin is the sighing young lover of Florian's pastoral romance Estelle et Némorin; Schinderhannes was a famous German robber chief executed in 1803. Montparnasse prefers playing the swain with the daughter to playing the brigand with the father, so he leads the girl off her post.`,quote:`preferring to play Nemorin with the daughter rather than Schinderhannes with the father`},{id:`the-fosse-aux-lions-and-the-charlemagne-yard`,term:`The Fosse-aux-Lions and the Charlemagne yard`,kind:`place`,chapter:230,text:`Courtyards of La Force prison. The official name of the worst yard was the Court Saint-Bernard; the inmates called it the Lions' Ditch. Brujon is left loose in another yard, the Charlemagne, so the guards can watch whom he talks to.`,quote:`which the robbers called the Fosse-aux-Lions`},{id:`a-postilion-sent-into-ireland`,term:`A 'postilion' sent 'into Ireland'`,kind:`word`,chapter:230,text:`Prison slang: a 'postilion' is a pellet of moulded bread with a note inside, flung over the roofs from one courtyard to another; to send it 'into Ireland' is to send it over England, from one land to another. Hugo glosses it himself; it is the first of many argot terms in this part.`,quote:`What prisoners call a “postilion”`},{id:`biscuit`,term:`Biscuit`,kind:`word`,chapter:230,text:`Prison code: a ship's biscuit sent back about a proposed job meant 'nothing to be done here'. The girl sent to look over the Rue Plumet house returns one, and Brujon's scheme dies in a word.`,quote:`A biscuit, in the shady symbolism of prisons, signifies: Nothing to be done.`},{id:`delancre-and-the-devils-of-vauvert`,term:`Delancre and the devils of Vauvert`,kind:`reference`,chapter:231,text:`Mabeuf's two treasured books on demonology. Pierre de Lancre was a seventeenth-century witch-hunting judge whose treatise on 'the inconstancy of demons' is a classic of the genre; the devils of Vauvert haunted a ruined château near the Luxembourg in Paris legend, hence the phrase 'au diable Vauvert', far away. 'Gobelins' is also an old word for goblins, which is why the old man half believes in a sprite of the Bièvre.`,quote:`De l’Inconstance des Démons`},{id:`the-gans-and-savigny-controversy`,term:`The Gans and Savigny controversy`,kind:`reference`,chapter:232,text:`A bitter dispute between two German jurists in the 1820s: Savigny, head of the 'historical school', held that law grows out of a people's history; Gans, a disciple of Hegel, defended reason and codification. Marius earns his bread translating such learned German into French.`,quote:`the Gans and Savigny controversy`},{id:`the-quotidienne`,term:`The Quotidienne`,kind:`reference`,chapter:232,text:`A royalist daily newspaper of the Restoration and July Monarchy, read by old legitimists. Éponine's picture of 'barons' as old men reading it in the sun at the Luxembourg is not far from the truth.`,quote:`they read the Quotidienne for a sou`},{id:`laundresses-of-the-gobelins`,term:`Laundresses of the Gobelins`,kind:`custom`,chapter:232,text:`Washerwomen knelt on the banks of the Bièvre and beat linen with wooden paddles, one of the sounds of the quarter. Paris then had no piped water for most of the poor, and riverside washing-places were busy all day.`,quote:`the laundresses of the Gobelins beating their linen`},{id:`the-rue-plumet-and-the-rue-de-babylone`,term:`The Rue Plumet and the Rue de Babylone`,kind:`place`,chapter:233,text:`Two quiet streets of the Faubourg Saint-Germain near the Invalides, among the great town houses of the nobility. The Rue Plumet (today Rue Oudinot) was almost empty; the house's back lane comes out a long way off in the Rue de Babylone, so that its tenant can come and go unseen.`,quote:`the lane which ended in the Rue de Babylone`},{id:`a-chief-justice-in-the-parliament`,term:`A chief justice in the Parliament`,kind:`history`,chapter:233,text:`A 'président à mortier', one of the senior judges of the Parlement of Paris, the great law court of the old monarchy. Such men were expected to be grave; Hugo's magistrate builds a hidden love-nest for his mistress, a 'little house', as eighteenth-century fashion had it.`,quote:`a chief justice in the Parliament of Paris`},{id:`mansard-watteau-rocaille`,term:`Mansard, Watteau, rocaille`,kind:`reference`,chapter:233,text:`The pavilion's styles: a steep 'mansard' roof after the architect François Mansart; interior decoration in the manner of Watteau, painter of graceful parties in parks; and rocaille, the shell-and-rock ornament of the rococo. Together they say: an eighteenth-century pleasure house.`,quote:`in the taste of Mansard`},{id:`the-rue-de-l-ouest-and-the-rue-de-l-homme-arme`,term:`The Rue de l'Ouest and the Rue de l'Homme Armé`,kind:`place`,chapter:233,text:`The two cheap lodgings kept as bolt-holes: the Rue de l'Ouest near the Luxembourg on the left bank, and the Rue de l'Homme-Armé ('the Armed Man') in the Marais on the right bank, far apart on purpose.`,quote:`the one in the Rue de l’Ouest, the other in the Rue de l’Homme Armé`},{id:`little-houses`,term:`Little Houses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:234,text:`'Petites maisons': discreet villas on the edges of eighteenth-century Paris where noblemen and rich men kept mistresses and gave suppers. The grotto in the Rue Plumet garden belongs to that world.`,quote:`the epoch of follies and “Little Houses”`},{id:`the-national-guard`,term:`The National Guard`,kind:`history`,chapter:234,text:`The citizen militia of Paris, revived in 1830 as the backbone of the July Monarchy. Every man who paid direct taxes, from about twenty to sixty, had to serve, buy his own uniform and mount guard several times a year; the shopkeeper in his bearskin became the symbol of the regime. Serving is the perfect disguise for a man who wants to look like any taxpayer.`,quote:`M. Fauchelevent, independent gentleman, belonged to the national guard`},{id:`the-comte-de-lobau`,term:`The Comte de Lobau`,kind:`history`,chapter:234,text:`Marshal Georges Mouton, Comte de Lobau, a hero of Napoleon's wars, commanded the Paris National Guard from 1830. Disputing with him over exemption is what a man with nothing to hide would do.`,quote:`nor to quibble with Comte de Lobau`},{id:`floreal`,term:`Floréal`,kind:`word`,chapter:235,text:`'The month of flowers', April–May in the revolutionary calendar. Hugo uses the old republican name for spring as the abandoned garden comes to life.`,quote:`In Floréal this enormous thicket`},{id:`the-faubourg-saint-germain`,term:`The Faubourg Saint-Germain`,kind:`place`,chapter:235,text:`The aristocratic quarter of the left bank, with the 'classic and splendid hotels' (town houses) of the Rue de Varenne, the Rue de Bourgogne and the Rue Saint-Dominique, and the dome of the Invalides close by. A wild garden there is a wilderness in the most formal part of Paris.`,quote:`the classic and splendid hotels of the Rue de Varennes`},{id:`omnibuses`,term:`Omnibuses`,kind:`word`,chapter:235,text:`Horse-drawn public coaches with fixed routes and fares, new in Paris since 1828 and painted in company colours, yellow, brown, white or red. Hugo lists them among the city noises that fail to reach the garden.`,quote:`the yellow, brown, white, and red omnibuses`},{id:`paphos-made-over-into-eden`,term:`Paphos made over into Eden`,kind:`reference`,chapter:236,text:`Paphos in Cyprus was the shrine of Venus, goddess of love. The magistrate's garden of gallantry becomes an Eden of innocence. Lamoignon was a great family of magistrates; Le Nôtre, the gardener of Versailles: the garden was laid out by a judge playing at one and a gardener playing at the other.`,quote:`Paphos had been made over into Eden.`},{id:`black-bread-and-white-bread`,term:`Black bread and white bread`,kind:`custom`,chapter:236,text:`White wheat bread was a mark of comfort; the poor ate coarse dark bread of rye or mixed grain. Cosette's protest at her father's 'horrible bread' is a protest at his self-imposed poverty.`,quote:`why do you eat horrible bread like that`},{id:`a-thebaid`,term:`A Thebaid`,kind:`reference`,chapter:237,text:`The desert of the Thebaid in Upper Egypt, where the first Christian hermits lived. Hugo uses it for the hidden, solitary life in the Rue de Babylone.`,quote:`in that Thebaid of the Rue de Babylone`},{id:`pity-is-not-a-pandour`,term:`'Pity is not a pandour'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:238,text:`In songbooks allowed into the convent, the nuns replaced 'amour' (love) with 'tambour' (drum) or 'pandour' (a Croatian irregular soldier), which rhyme with it. The older girls puzzled over the nonsense that resulted. Cosette left too young even for that, so she has no word for what she feels.`,quote:`amour was replaced by tambour or pandour`},{id:`comte-coutard-commandant-of-paris`,term:`Comte Coutard, commandant of Paris`,kind:`history`,chapter:240,text:`General Louis-François Coutard commanded the Paris military division in the early 1830s. The sight of him in full uniform is what sets Jean Valjean dreaming of a gilded uniform of his own for Cosette's sake.`,quote:`Comte Coutard, the commandant of Paris`},{id:`the-chain-gang`,term:`The chain-gang`,kind:`history`,chapter:240,text:`Until 1836, convicts sentenced to the galleys were sent from Bicêtre to the ports of Brest, Rochefort or Toulon by road in a 'chain': iron collars riveted on in the prison yard, then weeks on open carts, chained together, guarded by the 'argousins'. Its departures drew crowds. In 1836 it was replaced by closed cell-wagons.`,quote:`It was the chain-gang, in fact`},{id:`desaugiers-and-the-vestal`,term:`Désaugiers and 'The Vestal'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:240,text:`Marc-Antoine Désaugiers was the most popular writer of comic songs and vaudevilles of the Restoration. The convicts bellow a medley of his on the road; Hugo sets the bawdy cheerful tune against the procession of the damned.`,quote:`a potpourri by Désaugiers, then famous, called “The Vestal;”`},{id:`fetes-on-the-champ-de-mars`,term:`Fêtes on the Champ de Mars`,kind:`custom`,chapter:240,text:`Public holidays under the July Monarchy, notably the anniversary of July 1830 and the King's name-day, meant a review of troops and National Guard on the Champ de Mars, boat jousts on the Seine, free shows in the Champs-Élysées and fireworks at the Arc de Triomphe.`,quote:`a review in the Champ de Mars, jousts on the Seine`},{id:`the-dog-doctor`,term:`The dog-doctor`,kind:`word`,chapter:241,text:`A sarcastic name for a vet, or a quack. Jean Valjean will not see a real doctor about his burned arm; a doctor's questions are the last thing he wants.`,quote:`“Call the dog-doctor,” said he.`},{id:`a-lieutenant-of-lancers`,term:`A lieutenant of lancers`,kind:`title`,chapter:243,text:`Lancers were light cavalry armed with lances, with a plumed shapska helmet and spurs that rang on the pavement: the most showy uniform in the army. A cavalry barracks stood in the Rue de Babylone, so a handsome officer passing the gate every day was quite natural.`,quote:`the regiment in barracks in the Rue de Babylone`},{id:`euryanthe`,term:`Euryanthe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:244,text:`An opera by Carl Maria von Weber (1823), a German romantic tale of knights and wronged love; its hunters' chorus was famous. Hugo calls it 'probably the most beautiful thing in all the sphere of music', and lets its forest mood colour Cosette's fear of the dark garden.`,quote:`the chorus from Euryanthe`},{id:`the-gladiator`,term:`The Gladiator`,kind:`place`,chapter:248,text:`A marble copy of the ancient 'Borghese Gladiator' that stood in the Luxembourg garden. The young man's 'near the Gladiator' fixes the spot where their eyes first met.`,quote:`near the Gladiator`},{id:`the-arcade-of-the-odeon`,term:`The arcade of the Odéon`,kind:`place`,chapter:248,text:`The covered galleries round the Odéon theatre, by the Luxembourg, where booksellers' stalls stood and students read the newspapers. A natural place for a poor law graduate to linger.`,quote:`under the arcade of the Odéon`},{id:`the-marechale-de-la-mothe-houdancourt`,term:`The Maréchale de La Mothe-Houdancourt`,kind:`reference`,chapter:249,text:`The wife of a seventeenth-century Marshal of France, known (Hugo says) for loving her daughters and hating her sons. He uses her as precedent for a mother whose maternity stops at her girls.`,quote:`Like the Maréchale de La Mothe-Houdancourt`},{id:`the-petit-musc`,term:`The Petit-Musc`,kind:`place`,chapter:249,text:`An old street off the Quai des Célestins in the Marais. Its name ('little musk', a corruption of an earthier medieval name) lets Hugo joke that Magnon could turn her bad 'odour' into good.`,quote:`this ancient street of the Petit-Musc`},{id:`the-croup-epidemic`,term:`The croup epidemic`,kind:`history`,chapter:249,text:`Croup, here meaning diphtheria, killed young children in waves along the crowded Seine quarters; doctors tried alum inhalations, later replaced by iodine. Writing in the 1860s, Hugo dates the epidemic 'thirty-five years ago', in the late 1820s.`,quote:`the great epidemic of croup`},{id:`jean-jacques-rousseau-did-even-better`,term:`'Jean Jacques Rousseau did even better!'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:249,text:`Rousseau, the philosopher of natural education, left all five of his children at the Paris Foundling Hospital, as he admitted in his Confessions. Thénardier's quip silences his wife's scruple about giving up her own boys.`,quote:`“Jean Jacques Rousseau did even better!”`},{id:`retired-tipstaff`,term:`Retired tipstaff`,kind:`title`,chapter:249,text:`Hapgood's English for a 'huissier', a bailiff or court officer who served writs and seized goods. M. Barge's address is on the paper the little boys lose.`,quote:`a retired tipstaff`},{id:`the-medals-and-mademoiselle-mars-s-diamonds`,term:`The medals and Mademoiselle Mars's diamonds`,kind:`history`,chapter:249,text:`Two celebrated thefts: in 1831 thieves stole ancient gold medals from the Royal Library's coin cabinet; in 1827 the jewels of the actress Mademoiselle Mars were stolen and partly recovered in a famous trial. Magnon's lodger, 'Mamselle Miss', is said to be mixed up in both.`,quote:`the medals in the Library and Mademoiselle Mar’s diamonds`},{id:`shaving-barbers`,term:`'Shaving barbers'`,kind:`word`,chapter:250,text:`Gavroche's name for his own sideline: smashing the windows of barbers' shops. The barber who has just thrown out two small children has earned his attention.`,quote:`“shaving barbers.”`},{id:`the-abbey-of-ascend-with-regret`,term:`'The Abbey of Ascend-with-Regret'`,kind:`word`,chapter:250,text:`Argot for the scaffold ('l'abbaye de Monte-à-Regret'): the steps one climbs unwillingly. Gavroche's joking answer to 'Do you know where I'm going?' is 'to the gallows'.`,quote:`“To the Abbey of Ascend-with-Regret,”`},{id:`kekcaa-and-tanflute`,term:`Kekçaa and tanflûte`,kind:`word`,chapter:250,text:`Paris street-talk. 'Kekçaa?' is 'Qu'est-ce que ça a?', 'What's wrong with that?', squashed into one word; Hugo calls it 'the language which no one writes, and which everyone speaks'. 'Tanflûte!' is a scornful exclamation, roughly 'Rubbish!'.`,quote:`Kekçaa signifies`},{id:`mon-dogue-ma-dague-et-ma-digue`,term:`'Mon dogue, ma dague et ma digue'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:250,text:`Old slang of the Temple quarter, 'my dog, my knife and my wife', a showman's formula from the age of Molière and the engraver Callot. Montparnasse uses it to let Gavroche know, without saying so, that he cannot talk freely.`,quote:`mon dogue, ma dague et ma digue`},{id:`the-elephant-of-the-bastille`,term:`The elephant of the Bastille`,kind:`place`,chapter:250,text:`Napoleon planned a colossal bronze elephant fountain, with a tower on its back, for the site of the Bastille. Only a full-size plaster-and-timber model was built, in 1814; it stood decaying in a corner of the square, full of rats, until 1846. Gavroche lives inside it.`,quote:`a singular monument`},{id:`the-column-of-july`,term:`The Column of July`,kind:`place`,chapter:250,text:`The bronze column on the Place de la Bastille honouring those killed in the July Revolution, begun in 1831 and finished in 1840. In 1832 it was still in its wooden scaffolding. Hugo calls it a 'stovepipe', and 'this monument of a revolution that miscarried'.`,quote:`the column of July`},{id:`the-fumade-steel`,term:`The Fumade steel`,kind:`word`,chapter:250,text:`A patent tinder-lighter sold by a man named Fumade, a quick way to make a flame in the days before friction matches were common. Gavroche lights his candle-end with one.`,quote:`the Fumade steel represented progress`},{id:`the-tuileries`,term:`The Tuileries`,kind:`place`,chapter:250,text:`The royal palace on the Seine, Louis-Philippe's residence. 'Do you want the Tuileries?' is Gavroche asking his guests whether they expect a palace.`,quote:`Do you want the Tuileries?`},{id:`frederick-lemaitre-and-the-ambigu`,term:`Frédérick Lemaître and the Ambigu`,kind:`reference`,chapter:250,text:`Frédérick Lemaître was the great actor of the boulevard melodrama, idol of the popular audience; the Ambigu-Comique was one of the boulevard theatres where such shows played. Gavroche's world of free amusements includes both, and the claque, paid applauders at the Opera.`,quote:`I’ll take you to see Frédérick Lemaître.`},{id:`monsieur-sanson`,term:`Monsieur Sanson`,kind:`history`,chapter:250,text:`Sanson was the name of the hereditary family of Paris executioners; Henri Sanson held the post in 1832 and lived in the Rue des Marais. To Gavroche, watching the guillotine is one more free show.`,quote:`Monsieur Sanson.`},{id:`crib-and-nut`,term:`'Crib' and 'nut'`,kind:`word`,chapter:250,text:`Gavroche corrects the little boys' speech into street slang: not 'lodging' but 'crib' (piaule), not 'head' but 'nut' (tronche). It is his way of making them at home.`,quote:`you don’t say ‘lodgings,’ you say ‘crib.’`},{id:`fresh-meat`,term:`'Fresh meat'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:250,text:`The ogre's cry in Perrault's Tom Thumb (Le Petit Poucet), 'I smell fresh meat!'. Hugo gives it to the rats of the elephant, swarming at the netting round the children.`,quote:`what the good storyteller Perrault calls “fresh meat,”`},{id:`the-bel-air-of-la-force`,term:`The Bel-Air of La Force`,kind:`place`,chapter:251,text:`The top floor of the 'New Building' at La Force prison, called 'Fine-Air' with prison irony: a garret hall of iron-barred cells under the roof, used for solitary confinement. An old kitchen flue of the dukes' mansion runs up through every storey to it.`,quote:`a top story which was called the Bel-Air (Fine-Air)`},{id:`milton-viewed-through-berquin`,term:`Milton viewed through Berquin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:251,text:`Milton is the sublime poet of Paradise Lost; Arnaud Berquin wrote sweet little moral tales for children. A grim prison wall next to a flower-filled bathhouse yard is the sublime and the twee side by side.`,quote:`Milton viewed through Berquin`},{id:`desrues-and-the-sleep-compellers`,term:`Desrues and the Sleep-compellers`,kind:`history`,chapter:251,text:`Antoine-François Desrues was a poisoner executed in 1777. The 'Endormeurs' were a gang who drugged their victims with doctored wine before robbing them. Thénardier drugs his guard the same way.`,quote:`the band of the Endormeurs, or “Sleep-compellers,”`},{id:`icigo-and-icicaille`,term:`Icigo and icicaille`,kind:`word`,chapter:251,text:`Two slang words for 'ici', here: icigo from the barriers, icicaille from the Temple old-clothes market. Each tells the man on the wall who is speaking below, before he sees anyone.`,quote:`These two words, icigo and icicaille`},{id:`poulailler-and-cartouche`,term:`Poulailler and Cartouche`,kind:`reference`,chapter:251,text:`Two famous robber chiefs of the eighteenth century. Their old-fashioned thieves' cant is to modern argot, Hugo says, what Racine's French is to André Chénier's: classic against new.`,quote:`spoken by Poulailler and Cartouche`},{id:`whom-are-we-to-eat`,term:`'Whom are we to eat?'`,kind:`word`,chapter:251,text:`In slang, to 'eat' a man is to kill or rob him. The narrator calls the remark 'frightfully transparent'.`,quote:`“Now, whom are we to eat?”`},{id:`argot`,term:`Argot`,kind:`word`,chapter:252,text:`The secret slang of the Paris underworld: thieves, convicts, beggars and prowlers of the barriers. Hugo uses 'argot' and 'slang' interchangeably, and opens a three-chapter digression to defend writing it. For him it is not quaint jargon but 'the language of wretchedness', a tongue invented for war on society.`,quote:`It is the language of wretchedness.`},{id:`la-pegre`,term:`La pègre`,kind:`word`,chapter:252,text:`Argot for the underworld as a whole, the tribe of thieves; 'la pègrenne', hunger. Hugo derives both from Latin 'pigritia', idleness, and builds a little genealogy: idleness the mother, theft the son, hunger the daughter.`,quote:`la pègre, for which read “theft,”`},{id:`the-last-day-of-a-condemned-man`,term:`The Last Day of a Condemned Man`,kind:`reference`,chapter:252,text:`Hugo's own short novel of 1829 (he says 1828), the inner monologue of a man waiting for the guillotine, which included a thief who talks argot and caused an outcry. 'The narrator of this grave and sombre history' is Hugo speaking of himself.`,quote:`the author of The Last Day of a Condemned Man`},{id:`balzac-and-eugene-sue`,term:`Balzac and Eugène Sue`,kind:`reference`,chapter:252,text:`The two novelists who followed Hugo in letting criminals speak their own language: Balzac in the stories of the master criminal Vautrin, Sue in The Mysteries of Paris (1842–43), a hugely popular serial about the Paris underworld.`,quote:`Balzac and Eugène Sue`},{id:`the-hotel-rambouillet-and-the-court-of-miracles`,term:`The Hôtel Rambouillet and the Court of Miracles`,kind:`place`,chapter:252,text:`The Marquise de Rambouillet's salon was the cradle of the précieuses, ladies of the seventeenth century who invented an affected refined language; the Court of Miracles was the beggars' enclave of old Paris, where cripples 'miraculously' stood up at night. Hugo puts them side by side: every class has its private language.`,quote:`The Hotel Rambouillet nearly adjoins the Cour des Miracles.`},{id:`plautus-and-phoenician`,term:`Plautus and Phoenician`,kind:`reference`,chapter:252,text:`The Roman comic playwright Plautus wrote passages in Punic, the Phoenician tongue of Carthage, for a character in his Poenulus; they are among the few surviving texts of that language. Hugo's point: by writing down a despised speech, a writer may be the one who preserves it.`,quote:`Plautus rendered`},{id:`jean-bart-duquesne-suffren-duperre`,term:`Jean Bart, Duquesne, Suffren, Duperré`,kind:`history`,chapter:252,text:`Four great French admirals from the seventeenth century to Hugo's day. The language of the sea they spoke is, for Hugo, the heroic slang, 'to the fierce slang of the thieves what the lion is to the jackal'.`,quote:`Jean Bart, Duquesne, Suffren, and Duperré`},{id:`mais-ou-sont-les-neiges-d-antan`,term:`'Mais où sont les neiges d'antan?'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:253,text:`'But where are the snows of yesteryear?', the famous refrain of François Villon's Ballade of the Ladies of Bygone Times (1460s). Hugo claims 'antan', from Latin ante annum, 'the year before', as a word of beggars' slang that the poet-thief lifted into poetry.`,quote:`Mais où sont les neiges d’antan?`},{id:`the-kingdom-of-thunes`,term:`The kingdom of Thunes`,kind:`lore`,chapter:253,text:`In the lore of old Paris the beggars and vagabonds were a mock kingdom with their own king (the 'roi de Thunes', or the 'grand Coësre') and their own language. A condemned 'king' scratched on a Bicêtre wall that kings of old always went to be 'anointed' at the Coësre's stone: the galleys.`,quote:`a king of Thunes condemned to the galleys`},{id:`mandrin`,term:`Mandrin`,kind:`history`,chapter:253,text:`Louis Mandrin, a smuggler of the 1750s who fought pitched battles with the tax-farmers' men and was broken on the wheel at Valence; a folk hero. Hugo pairs him with Villon: argot was chewed by the bandit and spoken by the poet.`,quote:`it was chewed by Mandrin`},{id:`argot-s-borrowings`,term:`Argot's borrowings`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`Hugo digs argot like a geologist and finds layers from every language of the poor: Spanish ('gat', cat, from gato), Italian ('spade', sword, from spada), English ('raille', spy, from rascal), German ('caleur', waiter, from Kellner), Latin ('cadene', chain, from catena), Basque and Breton. Crowns are 'maltèses', after the coins used on the galleys of Malta.`,quote:`Do you want Spanish?`},{id:`larbin-rabouin-tronche-sorgue`,term:`Larbin, rabouin, tronche, sorgue`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`Samples of argot coined from nothing or by metaphor: 'le larbin', the lackey (a word that passed into ordinary French); 'le rabouin', the devil, later 'le boulanger', the baker who puts bread into the oven; 'la sorgue', night, and 'l'orgue', man, so that man is 'a derivative of the night'; 'la tronche', the head on the block, beside 'la sorbonne', the head that plots.`,quote:`the lackey, le larbin`},{id:`il-lansquine`,term:`'Il lansquine'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:253,text:`Argot for 'it is raining': slanting lines of rain likened to the lances of the lansquenets, German mercenary pikemen of the sixteenth century. Hugo admires it as an old figure that 'bears its date about it'.`,quote:`il lansquine, it rains`},{id:`railles-roussins-cognes`,term:`Railles, roussins, cognes`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`The argot names for the police changed again and again as each became known: railles, roussins, rousses, 'dealers in stay-laces', coquers, cognes. The girls' 'bobbies' on the boulevard was one of these. Argot, Hugo says, flees understanding as its speakers flee the law.`,quote:`the police are railles, then roussins`},{id:`cartouche-and-lacenaire`,term:`Cartouche and Lacenaire`,kind:`history`,chapter:253,text:`Cartouche, the robber chief of the 1720s, and Pierre-François Lacenaire, the dandy murderer and poet guillotined in 1836, a celebrity of the July Monarchy. 'Cartouche's talk would have been Hebrew to Lacenaire': a century had changed the whole language.`,quote:`Cartouche’s talk would have been Hebrew to Lacenaire.`},{id:`bastringue-castus-the-college`,term:`Bastringue, castus, the college`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`Argot words Hugo reads as moral lessons. The file that cuts a fetter is the 'bastringue', a dance hall, because free feet can dance; the dungeon is the 'castus', for its forced chastity; the convict is a 'fagot', firewood for hell; and the prison itself is 'the college'.`,quote:`he calls the saw the bastringue`},{id:`the-cellar-of-the-chatelet`,term:`The cellar of the Châtelet`,kind:`history`,chapter:253,text:`Before the Revolution, men sentenced to the galleys waited in a cellar of the Grand-Châtelet prison below the level of the Seine, chained by the neck to a beam, unable to lie down, sometimes for months. Hugo says most of the galley songs, the 'lirlonfa', were born there.`,quote:`There existed at the Châtelet in Paris a large and long cellar.`},{id:`to-eat-the-bit`,term:`'To eat the bit'`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`Argot for informing on one's comrades: the informer feeds on a little of each man's flesh. For a community whose only bond is the secret, it is the worst crime.`,quote:`“to eat the bit.”`},{id:`camoufle-and-camouflet`,term:`Camoufle and camouflet`,kind:`word`,chapter:253,text:`In argot a candle is a 'camoufle'; from 'seeing thirty-six candles' after a blow comes 'camouflet', a slap, which reached Voltaire's pen. Hugo's example of slang climbing 'from the cavern to the Academy'.`,quote:`Candle, camoufle.`},{id:`andromeda`,term:`Andromeda`,kind:`reference`,chapter:253,text:`In Greek myth the princess chained naked to a rock as an offering to a sea-monster, until Perseus came on the winged horse. Hugo ends the chapter with the human soul of the wretched as an Andromeda in the dark, waiting for a rescuer.`,quote:`a sombre Andromeda white and naked amid the shadows`},{id:`malure-and-larifla`,term:`Maluré and larifla`,kind:`word`,chapter:254,text:`Two kinds of refrain in prison songs: the 'maluré', a plaintive moan (malheur, misfortune), and the 'larifla', a jaunty nonsense chorus. Hugo dates the shift from the first to the second to the mid-eighteenth century, and reads it as the underworld losing its sense of guilt.`,quote:`The plaintive maluré was replaced by the larifla.`},{id:`the-marquis-de-pantin`,term:`The Marquis de Pantin`,kind:`word`,chapter:254,text:`'Pantin' was argot for Paris, and also means a jumping-jack puppet. Calling Louis XV 'the Marquis of Pantin' is the new mocking gaiety of the eighteenth-century underworld toward the king.`,quote:`“le Marquis de Pantin.”`},{id:`encyclopedists-physiocrats-philosophers-utopians`,term:`Encyclopedists, physiocrats, philosophers, utopians`,kind:`history`,chapter:254,text:`The four 'sacred legions' of the eighteenth-century Enlightenment: Diderot's Encyclopédie, Turgot's economists, Voltaire, and Rousseau. Hugo absolves them, and blames the gutter writers 'patronised by a prince' whose books ended in the Secret Library, the locked collection of forbidden books.`,quote:`these are four sacred legions`},{id:`restif-de-la-bretonne`,term:`Restif de La Bretonne`,kind:`reference`,chapter:254,text:`Nicolas-Edme Restif (or Rétif) de La Bretonne, prolific author of the 1770s–90s, whose novels of Paris night life and sexual frankness shocked his age. Hugo names him as the writer who dug 'the most unhealthy gallery' among the masses.`,quote:`Restif de La Bretonne`},{id:`schiller-s-the-robbers`,term:`Schiller's The Robbers`,kind:`reference`,chapter:254,text:`Friedrich Schiller's first play (1781), in which a wronged nobleman becomes the chief of a band of outlaws in revolt against a corrupt society. Hugo takes it as the emblem of a moment when theft dressed itself up as protest.`,quote:`Schiller in his famous drama The Robbers`},{id:`the-crown-jewels-in-1848`,term:`The crown jewels in 1848`,kind:`history`,chapter:254,text:`When the Tuileries were invaded in the February Revolution of 1848, the crown and its diamonds, including the Regent diamond, were carried to safety under escort of ragged men from the faubourg. Hugo uses it to argue that the Revolution had made a jacquerie impossible.`,quote:`By whom were the wagons containing the wealth of the Tuileries escorted in 1848?`},{id:`the-14th-of-july-and-the-10th-of-august`,term:`The 14th of July and the 10th of August`,kind:`history`,chapter:254,text:`The great 'days' of the Revolution: the storming of the Bastille on 14 July 1789, and the storming of the Tuileries that overthrew the monarchy on 10 August 1792. On such days, Hugo says, 'there is no longer any populace': the crowd shoots thieves.`,quote:`a 14th of July, a 10th of August`},{id:`selling-the-field-where-hannibal-camped`,term:`Selling the field where Hannibal camped`,kind:`reference`,chapter:255,text:`When Hannibal was encamped before Rome, the Romans showed their confidence by selling the very field he occupied at full price. Hugo urges the same calm faith in progress against the fear of disaster.`,quote:`Let us sell the field on which Hannibal is encamped.`},{id:`two-swans-meeting-on-the-jungfrau`,term:`Two swans meeting on the Jungfrau`,kind:`reference`,chapter:256,text:`The Jungfrau is one of the highest peaks of the Swiss Alps, a byword for untouched snow. Hugo's image for two chaste lovers is two white birds meeting on a white summit.`,quote:`Two swans meeting on the Jungfrau.`},{id:`the-cholera-in-the-lovers-may`,term:`The cholera in the lovers' May`,kind:`history`,chapter:257,text:`The epidemic that reached Paris in March 1832 was still killing through the spring, with carts of coffins in the poor quarters. Hugo notes that the two lovers in the garden do not even notice it.`,quote:`They did not notice the cholera which decimated Paris`},{id:`none-of-that-lisette`,term:`'None of that, Lisette!'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:259,text:`The refrain of a Béranger song, roughly 'Not so fast, my girl!'. Éponine mutters it to herself as she takes up her post by the loosened bar of the gate.`,quote:`“None of that, Lisette!”`},{id:`nous-n-sommes-pas-le-jour-de-l-an`,term:`'Nous n'sommes pas le jour de l'an'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:259,text:`'It isn't New Year's Day, for kissing papa and mamma': the masked ventriloquist's sneer at the girl who throws her arms round her father. New Year's was the day of family visits and embraces.`,quote:`Nous n’ sommes pas le jour de l’an`},{id:`brujon-s-omens`,term:`Brujon's omens`,kind:`custom`,chapter:259,text:`Thieves were notoriously superstitious. Brujon calls off the job because he has seen two sparrows fighting and jostled a quarrelling woman: bad signs. Hugo lets a superstition do what threats could not.`,quote:`this morning I came across two sparrows fighting`},{id:`under-pantin`,term:`'Under Pantin'`,kind:`word`,chapter:259,text:`Argot: Pantin is Paris itself (also a village just outside it), so 'Where shall we sleep?' 'Under Pantin' means anywhere in town, or nowhere.`,quote:`“Under Pantin.”`},{id:`faublas-and-the-prudhommes`,term:`Faublas and the Prudhommes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:261,text:`Faublas, the young rake hero of Louvet de Couvray's eighteenth-century novel of seductions; Monsieur Prudhomme, Henry Monnier's caricature of the pompous bourgeois. Both kinds of men, Hugo says, deny that love has a soul.`,quote:`the Faublas and the Prudhommes add`},{id:`busiris-tiberius-henry-viii`,term:`Busiris, Tiberius, Henry VIII`,kind:`reference`,chapter:261,text:`Tyrants of legend and history: Busiris, the Egyptian king who sacrificed strangers; Tiberius, the suspicious Roman emperor; Henry VIII, who executed two of his wives. To Marius, a journey to England is a crime worse than theirs.`,quote:`no action of Busiris, of Tiberius, or of Henry VIII`},{id:`humblot-conte-peer-of-france`,term:`Humblot-Conté, peer of France`,kind:`history`,chapter:262,text:`Arnould Humblot-Conté, a pencil manufacturer and deputy, made a peer by Louis-Philippe. After 1830 the peerage was no longer hereditary and was opened to industrialists; seeing a pencil-maker in the House of Peers would once have outraged M. Gillenormand.`,quote:`M. Humblot-Conté, peer of France`},{id:`decorated-by-july`,term:`Decorated by July`,kind:`history`,chapter:262,text:`Fighters of the 1830 revolution received a special decoration, the 'July Cross', and the storming of the Louvre on 29 July was one of the famous episodes. Bullets and cannonballs lodged in house walls were proudly labelled with the date. M. Gillenormand jeers at all of it.`,quote:`Are you one of those decorated by July?`},{id:`incroyable`,term:`Incroyable`,kind:`word`,chapter:262,text:`The extravagant dandies of the Directory (1795–99), with huge cravats, tight coats and mannered speech; the name mocks their habit of saying 'incroyable!' ('incredible!'). M. Gillenormand still dresses like one in 1832. Garat was a fashionable singer of that time.`,quote:`dressed, according to his wont, like an incroyable`},{id:`the-rue-blomet-and-pamela`,term:`The Rue Blomet and Pamela`,kind:`reference`,chapter:262,text:`M. Gillenormand confuses the Rue Plumet with the Rue Blomet, a street of his youth, and pictures barracks and pretty girls. Pamela, heroine of Richardson's novel, is the virtuous servant-girl courted by her master; for the old man, 'a Pamela in a garden' means a pretty mistress.`,quote:`In a garden, a Pamela.`},{id:`the-champ-de-mars`,term:`The Champ-de-Mars`,kind:`place`,chapter:263,text:`The great parade ground in front of the École Militaire on the left bank, used for reviews and festivals, and empty on ordinary days. Its slopes are where Jean Valjean goes to sit alone and think.`,quote:`the most solitary slopes in the Champ-de-Mars`},{id:`passport`,term:`Passport`,kind:`custom`,chapter:263,text:`French subjects needed a passport issued by the authorities to travel inside France or abroad, with name, description and place of residence. For a man living under another name, getting one meant questions he could not safely answer.`,quote:`the difficulty of procuring a passport`},{id:`pepin-and-morey`,term:`Pépin and Morey`,kind:`history`,chapter:263,text:`Two accomplices of Fieschi in the 1835 'infernal machine' attempt on Louis-Philippe, both guillotined. Hugo names them as the kind of conspirator the police were hunting, any of whose nets might catch a man hiding a past.`,quote:`a man like Pépin or Morey`},{id:`general-lamarque`,term:`General Lamarque`,kind:`history`,chapter:264,text:`Jean-Maximilien Lamarque (1770–1832), one of Napoleon's generals, then a deputy of the opposition: a patriot who spoke for Poland and against the treaties of 1815, and popular with republicans and Bonapartists alike. He died of cholera on 1 June 1832; his funeral on 5 June was the chance the secret societies were waiting for.`,quote:`“Are you coming to General Lamarque’s funeral?”`},{id:`othello-and-candide`,term:`Othello and Candide`,kind:`reference`,chapter:264,text:`Shakespeare's Othello is destroyed by jealousy; Voltaire's Candide is too innocent to suspect anyone. Marius, Hugo says, is a Candide in this: the lancer passing the garden never makes him jealous.`,quote:`That which overwhelmed Othello glides innocuous over Candide.`},{id:`the-rue-de-la-chanvrerie`,term:`The Rue de la Chanvrerie`,kind:`place`,chapter:264,text:`A short street in the old Halles quarter, between the Rue Saint-Denis and the market, named after the hemp-dealers (chanvriers) who once lived there. It disappeared in the rebuilding of Paris after 1850.`,quote:`the barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie`},{id:`estienne-aldus-elzevir`,term:`Estienne, Aldus, Elzevir`,kind:`reference`,chapter:265,text:`The great printing houses of the Renaissance and after: Aldus Manutius in Venice ('in aedibus Manutianis', 'in the house of the Manutii'), the Estiennes in Paris, the Elzevirs in Holland. Their editions are a book-lover's treasures, and Mabeuf sells them one by one for bread.`,quote:`a Robert Estienne`},{id:`diogenes-laertius`,term:`Diogenes Laertius`,kind:`reference`,chapter:265,text:`A Greek writer of the third century AD whose Lives of the Eminent Philosophers is the chief source on ancient thinkers. Mabeuf's copy, printed at Lyons in 1644 with rare manuscript variants, is his last and greatest treasure.`,quote:`a Diogenes Laertius, printed at Lyons in 1644`},{id:`the-quai-malaquais-bookstalls`,term:`The Quai Malaquais bookstalls`,kind:`place`,chapter:265,text:`The second-hand booksellers whose boxes lined the Seine embankments opposite the Louvre, and the shops of the Rue des Grès by the Sorbonne, were where scholars bought and sold old books for a few sous.`,quote:`under the Quai Malaquais`},{id:`the-arsenal`,term:`The Arsenal`,kind:`place`,chapter:265,text:`The old artillery arsenal and its library on the right bank, near the Bastille and the Austerlitz bridge. 'Fighting near the Arsenal' places the trouble where Lamarque's funeral procession was headed.`,quote:`“In the neighborhood of the Arsenal.”`},{id:`philintus-against-alcestis`,term:`Philintus against Alcestis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:266,text:`The two friends of Molière's The Misanthrope: Alceste, who will not bend the truth for anyone, and Philinte, who makes polite allowances for everything. Hugo uses Philinte for the 'good sense' that always splits the difference.`,quote:`Philintus against Alcestis`},{id:`the-golden-mean`,term:`The golden mean`,kind:`history`,chapter:266,text:`The 'juste milieu', Louis-Philippe's own phrase for his policy: a middle way between reaction and revolution. Hugo mocks it as 'the lukewarm water party', the school that tallies what a riot costs and never asks what caused it.`,quote:`A whole political school called “the golden mean”`},{id:`jean-chouan-and-jeanne`,term:`Jean Chouan and Jeanne`,kind:`history`,chapter:266,text:`Jean Chouan led the royalist peasant guerrillas of the west in the 1790s, 'the war of thickets'; Jeanne was the leader of the republican barricade at Saint-Merry in June 1832, 'the war of the pavements'. Hugo pairs the two kinds of hero.`,quote:`the one has Jean Chouan, the other has a Jeanne`},{id:`insurrection-and-revolt`,term:`Insurrection and revolt`,kind:`lore`,chapter:267,text:`Hugo's own distinction, central to this part of the book. Insurrection is the whole people rising against a fraction that has usurped its rights, and it is in the right; revolt (or riot, uprising) is a fraction rising against the whole, and it is in the wrong. The same cannon is wrong at the Tuileries on 10 August 1792 and right in Bonaparte's hands in Vendémiaire 1795.`,quote:`the war of the whole against the fraction is insurrection`},{id:`terray-and-turgot`,term:`Terray and Turgot`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`Two finance ministers of Louis XVI's era: the Abbé Terray, hated for arbitrary taxes and bankruptcies, and Turgot, the reformer who freed the grain trade and was met with riots in 1775, the 'Flour War'. The same fury, Hugo says, is just against one and absurd against the other.`,quote:`legitimate when directed against Terray and absurd when directed against Turgot`},{id:`salt-smugglers-and-chouannerie`,term:`Salt-smugglers and chouannerie`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`The 'gabelle', the royal salt tax, bred a whole population of smugglers (faux-sauniers) in the west who fought the king's men for generations. In 1793 many of them joined the royalist Chouan rising: rebels against the throne turned into its defenders.`,quote:`that long and bloody protest of dealers in contraband salt`},{id:`the-white-terror-s-killers`,term:`The White Terror's killers`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`Hugo's list of massacres he calls mere 'uprisings': Saint Bartholomew's Day (1572), the September Massacres (1792), the Glacière killings at Avignon, the Princesse de Lamballe torn apart by a mob, and Marshal Brune lynched at Avignon in 1815 by royalists. Miquelets, Verdets, Cadenettes and the Companions of Jehu were bands of the royalist White Terror in the south.`,quote:`the assassins of Brune, Miquelets, Verdets, Cadenettes`},{id:`polignac-is-a-rioter`,term:`'Polignac is a rioter'`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`Prince Jules de Polignac was Charles X's chief minister and author of the July Ordinances; Camille Desmoulins was the journalist whose street speech set off the taking of the Bastille. Hugo's paradox: the minister was the rebel, the man in the street the lawful power.`,quote:`Polignac is a rioter; Camille Desmoulins is one of the governing powers.`},{id:`facit-indignatio`,term:`Facit indignatio`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:267,text:`From Juvenal's first Satire: 'facit indignatio versum', indignation makes the verse. Under the Caesars there could be no insurrection, Hugo says, so protest went into the satire of Juvenal, exiled to Syene in Egypt, and the Annals of Tacitus.`,quote:`The facit indignatio replaces the Gracchi.`},{id:`masaniello-and-spartacus`,term:`Masaniello and Spartacus`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`Masaniello was the Neapolitan fishmonger who led a bread-and-tax riot against Spanish rule in 1647; Spartacus led the great slave rising against Rome. Riot is the stomach, insurrection the mind. 'Gaster' is the belly, from the Greek.`,quote:`Riot is Masaniello; insurrection, Spartacus.`},{id:`buzancais`,term:`Buzançais`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`A town in the Indre where, in the famine winter of 1847, hungry villagers attacked grain stores and killed a landowner; several were guillotined. Hugo's example of a riot that was right at bottom and wrong in form.`,quote:`Buzançais, for example`},{id:`universal-suffrage`,term:`Universal suffrage`,kind:`history`,chapter:267,text:`The vote for all adult men, introduced in France in 1848, after this story's time but before Hugo wrote it. He argues that once every man can vote, street insurrection loses its justification: 'by giving the vote to insurrection, it deprives it of its arms.'`,quote:`Universal suffrage has this admirable property`},{id:`we-have-seen-this`,term:`'We have seen this'`,kind:`lore`,chapter:267,text:`Hugo was in Paris in June 1832 and saw part of the fighting himself; in the next chapters he places 'the author of this book' under fire in the Passage du Saumon. He warns that he alters names but that the episode he tells is real.`,quote:`“We have seen this.”`},{id:`the-funeral-of-general-lamarque`,term:`The funeral of General Lamarque`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`On 5 June 1832 Lamarque's coffin was taken in procession from his house along the boulevards to the Austerlitz bridge, to be sent on to his native Landes. Ten thousand National Guards escorted it, followed by students, workers, secret societies and foreign exiles with Polish, Italian, German and Spanish flags, while the government kept troops ready all round.`,quote:`General Lamarque’s funeral procession traversed Paris`},{id:`general-foy`,term:`General Foy`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`Maximilien Foy, another of Napoleon's generals, became the great liberal orator of the Restoration Chamber; his funeral in 1825 drew a hundred thousand mourners. Lamarque was his successor in both roles.`,quote:`Like Foy, his predecessor`},{id:`marshals-in-petto`,term:`Marshals in petto`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:268,text:`'In petto', in the breast, is used of cardinals the Pope has named secretly. Lamarque, Gérard and Drouet were the generals Napoleon would have made marshals had the Empire lasted.`,quote:`one of Napoleon’s marshals in petto`},{id:`the-municipal-guard`,term:`The Municipal Guard`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`The paid, professional police troops of Paris created after 1830, foot and horse, distinct from the citizen National Guard and from the army. They were hated in the working quarters as the regime's street enforcers, and they were the first sent in against barricades.`,quote:`the Municipal Guard echelonned from street to street`},{id:`the-place-louis-xv`,term:`The Place Louis XV`,kind:`place`,chapter:268,text:`Today's Place de la Concorde, where Louis XVI was guillotined. Its name changed with every regime (Louis XV, Revolution, Concord); in 1832 cavalry with loaded muskets waited there during the funeral.`,quote:`the Place Louis XV`},{id:`the-duc-de-reichstadt`,term:`The Duc de Reichstadt`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`Napoleon's only legitimate son, once King of Rome, brought up in Austria under this title. Bonapartists dreamed of him as Napoleon II; in June 1832 he was dying of tuberculosis in Vienna, aged twenty-one.`,quote:`the Duc de Reichstadt`},{id:`the-vendome-column`,term:`The Vendôme column`,kind:`place`,chapter:268,text:`The bronze column in the Place Vendôme cast from cannon captured at Austerlitz, with Napoleon on top. Carrying the old soldier's coffin round it was a Bonapartist homage.`,quote:`the coffin borne round the Vendome column`},{id:`the-gallic-cock`,term:`The Gallic cock`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`The cockerel was the emblem the July Monarchy put on its flags in place of the Bourbon lilies and the Napoleonic eagle. Tearing it off a banner and dragging it in the mud was an insult to the regime.`,quote:`the Gallic cock torn from a popular flag`},{id:`the-red-flag-at-the-austerlitz-bridge`,term:`The red flag at the Austerlitz bridge`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`During Lafayette's farewell speech a man in black on horseback appeared with a red flag, or a pike capped with a red liberty-cap. Red was the colour of the most radical republicans; Lafayette turned away, and General Exelmans left the procession. Within minutes shots were fired and the riot began.`,quote:`a man on horseback, clad in black, made his appearance in the middle of the group with a red flag`},{id:`lamarque-to-the-pantheon`,term:`Lamarque to the Pantheon!`,kind:`history`,chapter:268,text:`The Pantheon was where the nation buried its great men; the Town-hall, the Hôtel de Ville, was where Lafayette had launched Louis-Philippe in 1830. The crowd's two shouts meant: honour the hero as the Revolution would, and start 1830 over again.`,quote:`“Lamarque to the Pantheon!—Lafayette to the Town-hall!”`},{id:`dragoons-and-carabineers`,term:`Dragoons and carabineers`,kind:`word`,chapter:268,text:`Dragoons were mounted troops who could fight on horse or foot; carabineers were heavy cavalry in steel breastplates. Both were held ready around the procession, the dragoons in the Célestins barracks by the river.`,quote:`the dragoons emerged from the Célestins`},{id:`the-ile-louviers`,term:`The Île Louviers`,kind:`place`,chapter:268,text:`A small island in the Seine near the Arsenal, joined to the right bank in 1843 when the channel was filled in. In 1832 it was a vast timber-yard, and its stacks of wood made a ready-made fortress.`,quote:`the timber-yards of the Isle Louviers`},{id:`the-saint-merry-rising`,term:`The Saint-Merry rising`,kind:`history`,chapter:269,text:`The heart of the June rising: around the church of Saint-Merry in the Rue Saint-Martin, insurgents led by Charles Jeanne made house No. 50 and the barricades on either side of it their fortress. It became the best-known stronghold of the June days, and its tocsin is the sound of this part of the book.`,quote:`that famous house No. 50, which was the fortress of Jeanne and her six hundred companions`},{id:`the-halles-quarter`,term:`The Halles quarter`,kind:`place`,chapter:269,text:`The old central market district of Paris, between the Rue Saint-Denis and the Rue Saint-Martin: a maze of narrow, crooked medieval streets 'more intricate than a forest', perfect for barricades. Twenty-seven rose there in under an hour.`,quote:`twenty-seven barricades sprang out of the earth in the quarter of the Halles alone`},{id:`ecossais`,term:`Écossais`,kind:`word`,chapter:269,text:`A type of light horse-drawn public coach, one of the omnibus lines' vehicles. Overturned, any big carriage made the backbone of a barricade.`,quote:`made with an “écossais,”`},{id:`the-passage-du-saumon`,term:`The Passage du Saumon`,kind:`place`,chapter:269,text:`A covered shopping arcade off the Rue Montmartre. Hugo tells us that he himself, 'an observer, a dreamer, the author of this book', was trapped there between the two fires for half an hour on the evening of 5 June.`,quote:`the Passage du Saumon became the field of battle`},{id:`lobau-bugeaud-soult`,term:`Lobau, Bugeaud, Soult`,kind:`history`,chapter:269,text:`The commanders against the rising: Marshal Lobau, head of the Paris National Guard; General Bugeaud, a hard soldier later blamed for the Rue Transnonain and notorious in Algeria; and Marshal Soult, Minister of War, a veteran of Austerlitz, who hesitated that evening.`,quote:`Two intrepid men, tried in great wars, the Marshal Lobau and General Bugeaud`},{id:`the-53rd-of-the-line`,term:`The 53rd of the Line`,kind:`history`,chapter:269,text:`In July 1830 the 53rd infantry regiment refused to fire on the people and was cheered by the crowds. In June 1832 everyone remembered it, and no one was sure the soldiers would shoot.`,quote:`the neutrality of the 53rd of the Line in July, 1830`},{id:`the-tocsin`,term:`The tocsin`,kind:`word`,chapter:269,text:`The alarm bell, rung fast and without stopping to call people out in fire, war or revolt. The insurgents rang the bell of Saint-Merry all through the night of 5 June, and all Paris heard it.`,quote:`the Saint-Merry tocsin began to make itself heard`},{id:`the-insurrection-of-1839`,term:`The insurrection of 1839`,kind:`history`,chapter:270,text:`The rising of the Society of the Seasons led by Barbès and Blanqui on 12 May 1839, fought in the same Rue Saint-Martin quarter and crushed in a day. Hugo cites it for the old cocoa-seller serving both sides.`,quote:`At the time of the insurrection of 1839`},{id:`armand-carrel-and-marshal-clausel`,term:`Armand Carrel and Marshal Clausel`,kind:`history`,chapter:270,text:`Carrel was the editor of the liberal paper Le National, the leading opposition journalist; Clausel, a Napoleonic marshal. The rumour that Carrel asked Clausel to lead the rising and was told 'Get a regiment first' is among the stories flying round frightened Paris.`,quote:`Armand Carrel had been to see Marshal Clausel`},{id:`anne-radcliffe`,term:`Anne Radcliffe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:270,text:`Ann Radcliffe, the English novelist of Gothic terror (The Mysteries of Udolpho). Hugo jokes that the police's lurid rumours of night pillage sound like one of her novels.`,quote:`that Anne Radcliffe mixed up with the Government`},{id:`the-rue-de-paris-of-the-conciergerie`,term:`The 'Rue de Paris' of the Conciergerie`,kind:`place`,chapter:270,text:`The long vaulted hall of the medieval Conciergerie prison, nicknamed the Rue de Paris. On the night of 5 June it was strewn with straw and packed with arrested men, among them Lagrange, a leader of the Lyons rising.`,quote:`the long vault which is called the Rue de Paris`},{id:`a-holster-pistol`,term:`A holster-pistol`,kind:`word`,chapter:271,text:`A big horse-pistol, made to be carried in a holster on the saddle. The one Gavroche 'borrows' from a junk shop in the Rue Ménilmontant turns out to have no trigger.`,quote:`an old holster-pistol`},{id:`belleville`,term:`Belleville`,kind:`place`,chapter:271,text:`A hill village of vineyards and cheap open-air taverns just outside the north-east wall of Paris, where working people went on Sundays. Gavroche comes down from it with a branch of laburnum.`,quote:`on the heights of Belleville`},{id:`baour-lormian-one-of-the-forty`,term:`Baour-Lormian, one of the Forty`,kind:`reference`,chapter:271,text:`Pierre Baour-Lormian, a now-forgotten poet and translator of Ossian, member of the French Academy, whose members are 'the Forty' (immortals). Gavroche once ran an errand for him, which makes him, Hugo jokes, 'a gamin of letters'.`,quote:`M. Baour-Lormian, one of the Forty`},{id:`thou-shalt-be-king`,term:`'Thou shalt be King'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:272,text:`The witches' prophecy to Macbeth on the heath in Shakespeare. Hugo says three old crones of a Paris crossroads could have greeted Bonaparte with it as well as any weird sisters.`,quote:`the “Thou shalt be King” could be quite as mournfully hurled at Bonaparte`},{id:`the-king-of-rome-and-the-duc-de-bordeaux`,term:`The King of Rome and the Duc de Bordeaux`,kind:`history`,chapter:272,text:`The gossips' royal nostalgia: Napoleon's baby son, the King of Rome, who was driven round the Tuileries in a little carriage drawn by sheep; and the Duc de Bordeaux, Charles X's grandson, the 'miracle child' born after his father's murder in 1820, now in exile.`,quote:`the little carriage of the King of Rome`},{id:`ragpicker`,term:`Ragpicker`,kind:`word`,chapter:272,text:`The 'chiffonnier', who walked the streets at night with basket and hook, picking rags, bones and paper out of the rubbish to sell. They were the lowest recognised trade in Paris, and proud of it: 'I have a profession.'`,quote:`the ragpicker interposed`},{id:`l-orme-saint-gervais`,term:`L'Orme-Saint-Gervais`,kind:`place`,chapter:272,text:`The square before the church of Saint-Gervais behind the Hôtel de Ville, named after an ancient elm under which, by tradition, justice was done and debts paid. Gavroche is heading back into the riot's centre.`,quote:`l’Orme-Saint-Gervais`},{id:`veteran-of-the-emperor-s-wars`,term:`Veteran of the Emperor's wars`,kind:`history`,chapter:273,text:`The old soldier's wounds read like a list of Napoleon's battles: Marengo, Austerlitz, Jena, Friedland, the Moskowa (Borodino), Lützen, Waterloo. A 'biscaïen' was a heavy musket-ball or grapeshot. Napoleon was wounded once, in the foot, at Ratisbon in 1809.`,quote:`a ball from a biscaïen in the thigh`},{id:`dialogue-between-the-razor-and-the-sword`,term:`'Dialogue between the razor and the sword'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:273,text:`Hugo imagines Monsieur Prudhomme, Henry Monnier's pompous bourgeois, giving the barber's talk a grand title. The shopkeeper wishing for a hero's cannonball, and the soldier's dry answer, are the comedy of the scene.`,quote:`“Dialogue between the razor and the sword.”`},{id:`the-marche-saint-jean`,term:`The Marché Saint-Jean`,kind:`place`,chapter:274,text:`A small market square behind the Hôtel de Ville, with a guard-post the insurgents disarm. It is where the students' band forms up.`,quote:`in the Marché Saint-Jean, where the post had already been disarmed`},{id:`long-live-poland`,term:`'Long live Poland!'`,kind:`history`,chapter:274,text:`Poland's rising against Russia had been crushed in 1831, and Polish exiles filled Paris; their cause was a passion of French republicans and of General Lamarque himself. The students shout for it as they march.`,quote:`“Long live Poland!”`},{id:`a-lenten-mandate`,term:`A Lenten mandate`,kind:`custom`,chapter:274,text:`Each year the Archbishop of Paris posted a pastoral letter setting the rules for Lent, including permission to eat eggs on fast days. The archbishop in 1832 was the legitimist de Quélen, much hated since the sack of his palace; Bahorel tears his notice down.`,quote:`a permission to eat eggs, a Lenten admonition addressed by the Archbishop of Paris`},{id:`hercle-and-para-bellum`,term:`Hercle and para bellum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:274,text:`Latin. 'Hercle!' is 'by Hercules!', a Roman oath, which Bahorel glosses with student cheek as 'cursed name of a dog'. 'Para bellum', 'prepare for war', from the proverb 'if you want peace, prepare for war'. Gavroche feels he now understands Latin.`,quote:`“What does Hercle mean?”`},{id:`keksekca`,term:`'Keksekça?'`,kind:`word`,chapter:274,text:`Gavroche's street contraction of 'Qu'est-ce que c'est que ça?', 'What's that?'. He asks it about the very old man marching with the band.`,quote:`“Keksekça?” said he to Courfeyrac.`},{id:`a-regicide-of-the-convention`,term:`A regicide of the Convention`,kind:`history`,chapter:275,text:`A member of the revolutionary Convention who had voted in 1793 for the death of Louis XVI. To young republicans of 1832 such survivors were living relics of the great Revolution, which is why the rumour runs through the column about the old man.`,quote:`a former member of the Convention—an old regicide`},{id:`chatou-passy-meudon-pantin`,term:`Chatou, Passy, Meudon, Pantin`,kind:`place`,chapter:275,text:`Villages round Paris, rhymed for nonsense in Gavroche's song: 'Tou tou tou, for Chatou'. The song itself is about two drunken sparrows who have drunk the dew off the thyme.`,quote:`Pour Chatou.`},{id:`monsieur-de-courfeyrac`,term:`'Monsieur de Courfeyrac'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:276,text:`The particle 'de' marks a noble name. Courfeyrac, a republican, will not be called 'Monsieur de Courfeyrac' and threatens to make the concierge 'Mother de Veuvain' in revenge.`,quote:`if you call me Monsieur de Courfeyrac again`},{id:`concierge`,term:`Concierge`,kind:`word`,chapter:276,text:`The porter or portress who lived in a lodge by the street door of a Paris house, took in letters, watched comings and goings and knew everyone's business. Hapgood uses 'portress' and 'concierge' for the same job.`,quote:`I’m the concierge`},{id:`the-rue-rambuteau`,term:`The Rue Rambuteau`,kind:`place`,chapter:277,text:`A broad new street driven through the old Halles quarter in the late 1830s by the Prefect Rambuteau, the first of the great clearances of medieval Paris. It swallowed the Rue de la Chanvrerie and Corinthe; Hugo writes as one describing a vanished city.`,quote:`The Rue de la Chanvrerie and Corinthe have disappeared beneath the pavement of the Rue Rambuteau.`},{id:`hugo-s-n-of-streets`,term:`Hugo's N of streets`,kind:`lore`,chapter:277,text:`To map the spot Hugo uses the device he used for Waterloo: a letter. Picture an N with its top on the Rue Saint-Denis and its foot on the Halles: its two uprights are the Rue de la Grande-Truanderie and the Rue de la Chanvrerie, its crossbar the Rue de la Petite-Truanderie, and the crooked Rue Mondétour slices across all three.`,quote:`the simple means which we have already employed in the case of Waterloo`},{id:`mondetour-and-pirouette`,term:`Mondétour and Pirouette`,kind:`place`,chapter:277,text:`Street names of the old Halles: the Rue Mondétour ('my detour') and the Rue Pirouette, whose very names, Hugo says, describe their windings. The Pointe Saint-Eustache, by the great market church, was the corner they led to.`,quote:`The name of Mondétour paints marvellously well the sinuosities of that whole set of streets.`},{id:`theophile-and-regnier`,term:`Théophile and Régnier`,kind:`reference`,chapter:277,text:`Two poets of the early seventeenth century who drank in this quarter: Théophile de Viau, whose couplet describes the skeleton of a hanged lover swinging there, and the satirist Mathurin Régnier, who drank at the wine-shop when it was still the Pot-aux-Roses.`,quote:`old Theophilus described in the following couplet`},{id:`the-pot-aux-roses`,term:`The Pot-aux-Roses`,kind:`word`,chapter:277,text:`The wine-shop's old name, painted as a rebus: a post (poteau) coloured rose. 'Découvrir le pot aux roses' is also an idiom for uncovering a secret.`,quote:`this cabaret was called the Pot-aux-Roses`},{id:`corinthe`,term:`Corinthe`,kind:`place`,chapter:277,text:`The wine-shop at the corner of the Rue de la Chanvrerie, renamed after a bunch of Corinth grapes ('Au Raisin de Corinthe') that the painter Natoire, a regular, painted on its post. It became the A.B.C. students' second meeting-place.`,quote:`“Au Raisin de Corinthe.” Hence the name of Corinthe.`},{id:`carpe-ho-ras`,term:`'Carpe ho ras'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:277,text:`Hucheloup painted 'CARPES AU GRAS' ('carps cooked with meat', his speciality) on his wall; rain washed away letters and left 'Carpe horas', Latin for 'seize the hours', as in Horace's 'carpe diem'. The cook who knew no French had written a motto.`,quote:`Carpe ho ras`},{id:`matelote-and-gibelotte`,term:`Matelote and Gibelotte`,kind:`lore`,chapter:277,text:`The nicknames of Corinthe's two serving-maids. A matelote is a fish stew in wine, a gibelotte a rabbit stew: the students named them after dishes on the menu.`,quote:`Two serving-maids, named Matelote and Gibelotte`},{id:`regale-si-tu-peux-et-mange-si-tu-l-oses`,term:`'Régale si tu peux et mange si tu l'oses'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:277,text:`Courfeyrac's chalked motto on the door: 'Treat if you can, and eat if you dare', a parody of a line of classical verse. A fair warning about Corinthe's kitchen.`,quote:`Régale si tu peux et mange si tu l’oses.`},{id:`du-breul-sauval-lebeuf`,term:`Du Breul, Sauval, Lebeuf`,kind:`reference`,chapter:278,text:`Three antiquarian historians of Paris from the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, who catalogued its churches and convents. Grantaire cites them on the swarms of monks, Franciscans, Minims, Capuchins, Augustines, who once filled the quarter.`,quote:`Du Breul and Sauval give a list of them, and so does the Abbé Lebeuf.`},{id:`brennus-and-clusium`,term:`Brennus and Clusium`,kind:`history`,chapter:278,text:`In Livy, the Gauls under Brennus attacked the Etruscan town of Clusium; Rome protested, and the Gauls answered that Rome had done the same to Alba and Fidenae, then sacked Rome itself. Grantaire's drunken lesson: every conquest is a theft.`,quote:`Brennus took Rome.`},{id:`the-comet-of-1811`,term:`The comet of 1811`,kind:`history`,chapter:278,text:`The Great Comet of 1811, visible for months, was taken as an omen of Napoleon's war with Russia. Grantaire calls history a poster with 'the comet of 1811 at the head': heaven itself puts on a show.`,quote:`the comet of 1811 at the head of the poster`},{id:`joly-s-cold`,term:`Joly's cold`,kind:`lore`,chapter:278,text:`Joly, the medical student and hypochondriac, has a head cold, so his m's and n's come out as b's and d's: 'Barius' for Marius, 'dod't' for don't, 'abberent' for apparent. Hugo spells it out.`,quote:`it is decidedly abberent that Barius is in love`},{id:`mitron-skip-the-gutter-gamin`,term:`Mitron, Skip-the-Gutter, gamin`,kind:`word`,chapter:278,text:`Hugo lists the names of errand-boys by trade: the baker's lad is a 'mitron' (for his paper cap), the notary's a 'saute-ruisseau' (skip-the-gutter), the cook's a scullion, the sailor's a cabin-boy. The boy who brings a message from Enjolras is little Navet.`,quote:`the baker’s gamin is called a mitron`},{id:`down-with-polignac`,term:`'Down with Polignac!'`,kind:`history`,chapter:278,text:`Charles X's last minister, imprisoned since 1830. Navet's being 'the one to shout Down with Polignac' is a child's bit of borrowed politics, two years out of date.`,quote:`I’m the one to shout ‘Down with Polignac!’`},{id:`building-a-barricade`,term:`Building a barricade`,kind:`custom`,chapter:279,text:`Paris had barricaded its streets since 1588, and in 1830 and 1832 the method was well known: tear up the paving-stones (big sandstone cubes laid in sand) with bars and picks, overturn the heaviest vehicle at hand, and pile casks, furniture and stones round it. Here a lime-dealer's dray, the widow's casks and a hijacked omnibus make the core; the whole is done in under an hour.`,quote:`the dray of a lime-dealer named Anceau`},{id:`non-licet-omnibus-adire-corinthum`,term:`'Non licet omnibus adire Corinthum'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:279,text:`Bossuet's pun as he stops an omnibus: the Latin proverb 'Not everyone may go to Corinth' (because pleasure there was costly), where 'omnibus' means 'to everyone'. Here it means: omnibuses do not get past the Corinthe.`,quote:`Non licet omnibus adire Corinthum.`},{id:`a-gothic-pygmalion`,term:`A Gothic Pygmalion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:279,text:`Pygmalion, the sculptor in Ovid who fell in love with his statue and prayed Venus to bring it to life. Grantaire's version: a medieval mason fell in love with his ugliest gargoyle, and the result was Matelote.`,quote:`a Gothic Pygmalion`},{id:`thermopylae-and-drogheda`,term:`Thermopylae and Drogheda`,kind:`history`,chapter:279,text:`Leonidas and his three hundred Spartans died holding the pass of Thermopylae against Persia; Cromwell's Puritan army stormed Drogheda in Ireland in 1649 and burned defenders in a church steeple. Enjolras, Spartan and Puritan, would have fought at either.`,quote:`He would have perished at Thermopylae with Leonidas`},{id:`the-hundred-franc-fine`,term:`The hundred-franc fine`,kind:`custom`,chapter:280,text:`The police fined householders for anything that fell or was hung out of a window into the street. Mother Hucheloup still smarts at paying a hundred francs over a counterpane and a flowerpot, so Courfeyrac tells her the barricade is her revenge on the government.`,quote:`the government collected a fine of a hundred francs`},{id:`lint`,term:`Lint`,kind:`word`,chapter:280,text:`'Charpie': threads picked from old linen, used as dressing to pack wounds before cotton wool came in. Making lint was what women did on the eve of a battle; here the widow and her maids tear up dishcloths for it.`,quote:`tearing up old dishcloths and making lint`,seeAlso:[292]},{id:`fire-pots-from-the-king-s-festival`,term:`Fire-pots from the King's festival`,kind:`history`,chapter:280,text:`Louis-Philippe's name-day, Saint Philip's day on 1 May, was the July Monarchy's great holiday, with illuminations. Its leftover fire-pots and carnival torches now light the barricade; they are said to come from the grocer Pépin of the Faubourg Saint-Antoine.`,quote:`a basket filled with fire-pots, “left over from the King’s festival.”`},{id:`smashing-the-lanterns`,term:`Smashing the lanterns`,kind:`custom`,chapter:280,text:`Paris streets were lit by oil lanterns hung on ropes across the roadway. Insurgents broke them first, to darken the approaches and leave the troops blind; here every lantern in the surrounding streets goes.`,quote:`They smashed the only street lantern in the Rue de la Chanvrerie`,seeAlso:[296]},{id:`casting-bullets`,term:`Casting bullets`,kind:`custom`,chapter:280,text:`Insurgents had guns but few cartridges, so they melted lead, pewter or brass and poured it into bullet-moulds, then rolled the balls with powder in paper cartridges. At Corinthe the kitchen fire melts down the widow's mugs, spoons and forks.`,quote:`moulding into bullets, pewter mugs, spoons, forks`},{id:`mother-gibou-s-tea`,term:`'Mother Gibou's tea'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:280,text:`A proverbial catch-all mixture from an old comic sketch, 'Madame Gibou's tea' in which everything is thrown into the pot. Gavroche's recipe for a barricade: put everything in.`,quote:`A barricade is Mother Gibou’s tea.`},{id:`the-redoubt`,term:`The redoubt`,kind:`custom`,chapter:281,text:`A redoubt is a small closed fortification. Two barricades at a right angle, the high one across the Rue de la Chanvrerie and the low one across the Rue Mondétour, with the wine-shop at the corner, make one; steps of paving-stones inside let the defenders climb up to fire, a gap at one end lets a man out, and the Rue des Prêcheurs is left open as a line of retreat.`,quote:`The two barricades united formed a veritable redoubt.`},{id:`folard`,term:`Folard`,kind:`reference`,chapter:281,text:`Jean-Charles de Folard, an eighteenth-century soldier and writer on tactics, whose jargon Hugo borrows for the open line of retreat: a 'branch' of the works.`,quote:`what Folard in his strategical style would have termed a branch`},{id:`bearskin-caps`,term:`Bearskin caps`,kind:`word`,chapter:281,text:`The tall fur caps of grenadiers, worn by the élite companies of the National Guard and the line. 'Not a single bearskin cap or a single bayonet' means no troops at all.`,quote:`without this handful of bold men seeing a single bearskin cap`,seeAlso:[291]},{id:`thirty-cartridges`,term:`Thirty cartridges`,kind:`custom`,chapter:281,text:`A cartridge was a twist of paper holding a measured charge of powder and a ball, bitten open and rammed down a musket barrel. Thirty each, from Courfeyrac's coffer, is a soldier's allowance for a day's fighting.`,quote:`Each one received thirty cartridges.`},{id:`manuel-and-foy`,term:`Manuel and Foy`,kind:`history`,chapter:282,text:`Jacques-Antoine Manuel and General Foy, the great liberal orators of the Restoration Chamber. In 1823 Manuel was expelled from the Chamber by force. Prouvaire's poem dates his young love by them: 'Manuel was proud and wise, Foy hurled the thunderbolt.'`,quote:`Manuel était fier et sage`},{id:`the-prado`,term:`The Prado`,kind:`place`,chapter:282,text:`A dance hall on the Île de la Cité, opposite the Palais de Justice, popular with students and grisettes in winter. In the poem the penniless young lawyer takes his girl to dine there.`,quote:`Quand je vous menais au Prado dîner`},{id:`the-carte-du-tendre`,term:`The Carte du Tendre`,kind:`reference`,chapter:282,text:`The 'Map of Tenderness', an allegorical map of the country of love in Madeleine de Scudéry's seventeenth-century novel Clélie, with towns like Tender-on-Inclination. Prouvaire's poem lays it over the Latin Quarter.`,quote:`La carte du Tendre au pays Latin`},{id:`malebranche-and-lamennais`,term:`Malebranche and Lamennais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:282,text:`Two philosophers of God: Nicolas Malebranche, the seventeenth-century Oratorian metaphysician, and Félicité de Lamennais, the priest whose liberal Catholicism was condemned by Rome in 1832. The girl in the poem proves divine goodness better than either, with a flower.`,quote:`Mieux que Malebranche et que Lamennais`},{id:`la-courtille`,term:`La Courtille`,kind:`place`,chapter:282,text:`A district of cheap taverns at Belleville. On Ash Wednesday morning the carnival crowds came 'down from the Courtille' in a drunken procession of masks and torches; the barricade's torch is one of those carnival torches.`,quote:`on their way to la Courtille`},{id:`the-police-card`,term:`The police card`,kind:`custom`,chapter:283,text:`Paris police inspectors carried a small round card under glass with the royal arms and the motto 'Surveillance et vigilance', signed by the Prefect of Police, Henri Gisquet in 1832. Finding it on a man settles who he is.`,quote:`a little round card pasted between two pieces of glass`},{id:`the-post-in-the-taproom`,term:`The post in the taproom`,kind:`lore`,chapter:283,text:`The wine-shop's old name, the Pot-aux-Roses, came from a rose-painted post (poteau) that stood in the middle of the room. The prisoner is tied to that same post.`,quote:`that celebrated post in the middle of the room which had formerly given the wine-shop its name`},{id:`the-mouse-who-has-caught-the-cat`,term:`'The mouse who has caught the cat'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:283,text:`Gavroche turns round the usual order of things: in the gamin's world the police are the cats and street boys the mice.`,quote:`“It’s the mouse who has caught the cat.”`},{id:`themis`,term:`Themis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:284,text:`The Greek goddess of divine law and order, often shown stern and pure. Hugo gives Enjolras, judging the murderer, 'something of the antique Themis': justice with a woman's face.`,quote:`something of the antique Themis`},{id:`police-agents-among-the-insurgents`,term:`Police agents among the insurgents`,kind:`history`,chapter:284,text:`The July Monarchy's police planted informers and agents provocateurs in the secret societies, and some of the loudest men in a crowd were the police's own. Hugo says that in 1848, when the archives were opened, he held the 1832 report on this one.`,quote:`a police agent’s card was found on Le Cabuc`},{id:`the-morgue`,term:`The Morgue`,kind:`place`,chapter:284,text:`The Paris mortuary on the Île de la Cité, behind Notre-Dame, where unidentified bodies were laid out behind glass for the public to view and claim. The dead of the June fighting were taken there and searched.`,quote:`when the bodies were taken to the morgue`},{id:`the-rue-de-rivoli-under-gas`,term:`The Rue de Rivoli under gas`,kind:`place`,chapter:285,text:`The arcaded street along the Tuileries, built under Napoleon, the smartest shopping street in Paris, already lit by gas in 1832. A few hundred yards from the barricades, people are eating ices and cakes there; from the Hôtel Meurice, the English travellers' hotel, posting-chaises leave for the country.`,quote:`the gas was burning under the arcades`},{id:`the-palais-royal`,term:`The Palais-Royal`,kind:`place`,chapter:285,text:`The palace of the Orléans family by the Louvre, with arcades of shops, cafés and gaming houses round its garden. Louis-Philippe had lived there before becoming king; on 5 June 1832 cavalry stood in its square.`,quote:`There was cavalry on the Place du Palais-Royal.`},{id:`the-rue-du-contrat-social`,term:`The Rue du Contrat-Social`,kind:`place`,chapter:285,text:`A little street by the market pillars of the Halles, named in the Revolution after Rousseau's Social Contract. The barber's shaving-dish pierced by a stray bullet there, Hugo says, was still hanging in 1848.`,quote:`the Rue du Contrat-Social`},{id:`the-tower-of-saint-jacques`,term:`The Tower of Saint-Jacques`,kind:`place`,chapter:286,text:`The Gothic bell-tower of the vanished church of Saint-Jacques-de-la-Boucherie, near the Seine, the only part left standing after the Revolution. With Saint-Merry it is one of the dark shapes rising over the blacked-out quarter.`,quote:`the tower of Saint-Jacques`},{id:`montmirail-and-champaubert`,term:`Montmirail and Champaubert`,kind:`history`,chapter:287,text:`Two of Napoleon's last victories, in February 1814, fought on French soil against invading armies: the very image of a war in defence of the country. Marius, thinking of his father's sword, tells himself that the barricade is something different, a war for an idea.`,quote:`It is no longer Montmirail nor Champaubert`},{id:`washington-and-camille-desmoulins`,term:`Washington and Camille Desmoulins`,kind:`history`,chapter:287,text:`Marius's argument with himself: if George Washington's sword freed America, why should it disown the pike of Camille Desmoulins, who called Paris to arms in July 1789? He decides there is no such thing as foreign or civil war, 'there is only just and unjust war'.`,quote:`By what right should the sword of Washington disown the pike of Camille Desmoulins?`},{id:`ambiorix-artevelde-marnix-pelagius`,term:`Ambiorix, Artevelde, Marnix, Pelagius`,kind:`history`,chapter:287,text:`Leaders of resistance against foreign rule: Ambiorix of the Gauls against Caesar, Jacob van Artevelde of Ghent against France, Marnix for the Dutch against Spain, Pelagius of Asturias against the Moors. Marius sets beside them those who fought a native tyrant, such as Brutus, Étienne Marcel and Coligny.`,quote:`That was the war of Ambiorix, of Artevelde, of Marnix, of Pelagius.`},{id:`aristogeiton-and-thrasybulus`,term:`Aristogeiton and Thrasybulus`,kind:`history`,chapter:287,text:`Aristogeiton killed the Athenian tyrant Hipparchus; Thrasybulus overthrew the Thirty Tyrants. In Marius's reasoning, thinkers like Aeschylus and Diderot open men's minds, and fighters like Thrasybulus and Danton finish the work.`,quote:`After Aeschylus, Thrasybulus`},{id:`mon-nez-est-en-larmes`,term:`'Mon nez est en larmes'`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:288,text:`Gavroche's warning song to the tune of 'Au clair de la lune': 'My nose is in tears, my friend Bugeaud, lend me your gendarmes to have a word with them; in blue greatcoats, the hen in a shako, here come the suburbs!' The 'hen in a shako' is a soldier, and the 'banlieue' the troops brought in from outside Paris.`,quote:`La poule au shako`},{id:`the-statue-of-the-commander`,term:`The statue of the commander`,kind:`reference`,chapter:288,text:`In the Don Juan legend (Molière's play, Mozart's Don Giovanni), the stone statue of the murdered Commander walks to the libertine's supper with heavy, terrible steps. Hugo hears the same stony tread in the troops advancing from Saint-Leu.`,quote:`the statue of the commander`},{id:`who-goes-there`,term:`'Who goes there?'`,kind:`custom`,chapter:288,text:`The sentry's challenge, 'Qui vive?', which demands a side, not a name. Enjolras answers it with 'The French Revolution!'.`,quote:`“The French Revolution!”`},{id:`videttes`,term:`Videttes`,kind:`word`,chapter:288,text:`Mounted or foot sentries posted ahead of a position to give warning. Enjolras's videttes at the ends of the streets run back when the troops come.`,quote:`the vidette of the Rue de la Petite-Truanderie`},{id:`the-heart-of-a-brutus`,term:`'The heart of a Brutus'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:289,text:`Lucius Junius Brutus drove the kings out of Rome, and Marcus Brutus killed Caesar; both were heroes to the republicans of the Revolution. Enjolras's verdict on the old man: 'the head of a blockhead and the heart of a Brutus'.`,quote:`The head of a blockhead and the heart of a Brutus`},{id:`municipal-guards-on-the-barricade`,term:`Municipal guards on the barricade`,kind:`history`,chapter:290,text:`The Municipal Guard picked tall, seasoned men, many of them ex-soldiers, and sent them in first against barricades with the bayonet rather than firing from a distance. The insurgents hated them more than the line regiments, who were conscripts.`,quote:`Municipal guards of lofty stature were making their way in`},{id:`gorget`,term:`Gorget`,kind:`word`,chapter:291,text:`A small crescent of polished metal worn at the throat by infantry officers, the last survival of plate armour. By 1832 it was purely a badge of rank, so the man who calls on the insurgents to lay down their arms is marked out as the officer commanding the assault.`,quote:`an officer in a gorget extended his sword`},{id:`municipal-guards-soldiers-of-the-line-and-national-guards-from-the-suburbs`,term:`Municipal Guards, soldiers of the line and National Guards from the suburbs`,kind:`history`,chapter:291,text:`The three kinds of force sent against the barricades. The Municipal Guard was the professional armed police of Paris; the line regiments were the regular army; the National Guard was the citizen militia of property-owners, and the companies from the suburbs (the banlieue) had a name for being the most eager and the least disciplined. Hugo is careful throughout to say which is which.`,quote:`Municipal Guards, soldiers of the line and National Guards from the suburbs`},{id:`flag-of-truce`,term:`Flag of truce`,kind:`custom`,chapter:292,text:`A white cloth raised to ask for a parley; under the customs of war the bearer is not to be fired on. Combeferre means to improvise one with his handkerchief on his cane, treating the insurgents and the army as two belligerents who can exchange prisoners.`,quote:`go as a flag of truce`},{id:`en-voyant-lafayette-le-gendarme-repete-sauvons-nous`,term:`“En voyant Lafayette, le gendarme répète: Sauvons-nous!”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:293,text:`“Seeing Lafayette, the gendarme repeats: Let’s run for it!” A street song of the moment. The old general Lafayette, hero of 1789 and 1830, was the idol of the opposition, and the funeral of General Lamarque that set off this rising had been led by him; in the song, the police flee at the mere sight of him.`,quote:`En voyant Lafayette`},{id:`a-hundred-sous`,term:`A hundred sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:293,text:`A five-franc piece, the coin Marius once pressed on the starving girl by the washerwomen's boulevard and she refused. To a penniless student it was real money, several days' food; she remembers that he could not afford it.`,quote:`You gave me a hundred sous`},{id:`sealing-a-letter`,term:`Sealing a letter`,kind:`custom`,chapter:294,text:`Envelopes were not yet in common use: a letter was folded so that the outside carried the address, then closed with a wafer or a blob of wax. Cosette's note is “sealed with a woman’s elegant care”; at the barricade Marius has nothing to seal with and can only fold his paper in four, so anyone who holds it can read it.`,quote:`Having nothing wherewith to seal this letter`},{id:`to-monsieur-monsieur-marius-pontmercy`,term:`“To Monsieur, Monsieur Marius Pontmercy”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:294,text:`The formal French way of addressing a letter, repeating the title: the first “Monsieur” is the salutation, the second goes with the name. “At M. Courfeyrac’s” means care of Courfeyrac, with whom Marius had been lodging.`,quote:`To Monsieur, Monsieur Marius Pontmercy`},{id:`barneville`,term:`Barneville`,kind:`place`,chapter:295,text:`A village on the west coast of the Cotentin peninsula in Normandy. Toussaint's stammer and her country speech place her as a Norman peasant woman, and Hugo makes her lack of curiosity the whole of her value as a servant.`,quote:`her peasant dialect of Barneville`},{id:`blotting-book-and-the-symmetrical-image`,term:`Blotting-book and the symmetrical image`,kind:`word`,chapter:295,text:`Ink dried slowly, so writers pressed a fresh page against a sheet of absorbent blotting paper kept in a little book; the blotter took a reversed copy of the words. A mirror reverses them back: geometry's “symmetrical image”. It is the most ordinary of household objects that undoes Valjean.`,quote:`what is called in geometry, “the symmetrical image;”`},{id:`stone-post-borne`,term:`Stone post (borne)`,kind:`word`,chapter:295,text:`A short stone bollard set at the foot of a wall or the corner of a doorway to keep cartwheels from scraping the house. Parisians sat on them as on a bench. Valjean's post at his door in the Rue de l’Homme Armé becomes his place of vigil.`,quote:`he sat upon the stone post at the door of his house`},{id:`the-clock-of-saint-paul`,term:`The clock of Saint-Paul`,kind:`place`,chapter:296,text:`Saint-Paul-Saint-Louis, the domed Jesuit church on the Rue Saint-Antoine at the southern edge of the Marais, the parish church of the Rue de l’Homme Armé's neighbourhood. Hugo sets its calm striking of the hour against the tocsin: “the tocsin is man; the hour is God.”`,quote:`the clock of Saint-Paul struck eleven`},{id:`there-is-ninety-three-come-again`,term:`“There is ‘Ninety-three’ come again”`,kind:`history`,chapter:296,text:`1793, the year of the Terror, when the Revolution executed the king and then its own. For a frightened bourgeois of 1832, a smashed street-lamp in the night was enough to call up the guillotine and the mobs of that year.`,quote:`There is ‘Ninety-three’ come again.`},{id:`the-archives`,term:`The Archives`,kind:`place`,chapter:296,text:`The national archives of France, housed in the Hôtel de Soubise, a great mansion with a colonnaded court at the end of the Rue de l’Homme Armé. Gavroche sees its columns only as material for a barricade.`,quote:`It’s the Archives, isn’t it?`},{id:`the-tiger-with-five-claws`,term:`“The tiger” with five claws`,kind:`lore`,chapter:296,text:`Gavroche's name for the five-franc piece, a big silver coin worth a hundred sous: more money than a street boy would ever hold at once, roughly several days' wages for a labourer. Its five francs are its five “claws”; refusing it, he plays at being an incorruptible republican.`,quote:`Let us contemplate the tiger.`},{id:`it-comes-from-the-provisional-government`,term:`“It comes from the Provisional Government”`,kind:`history`,chapter:296,text:`Gavroche's joke. In 1830 a provisional government had been proclaimed at the Hôtel de Ville while the old one fell; every insurrection after it dreamed of naming one. A note from a barricade of thirty men becomes, in his mouth, an official despatch of the new Republic.`,quote:`It comes from the Provisional Government.`},{id:`brioches`,term:`Brioches`,kind:`word`,chapter:296,text:`A rich egg-and-butter bun. In Parisian slang “faire une brioche” meant to make a blunder, so Gavroche tells Valjean he is talking nonsense by saying he is baking brioches.`,quote:`bits of pastry vulgarly called brioches`},{id:`fumade-lighter`,term:`Fumade lighter`,kind:`word`,chapter:297,text:`An early chemical match-box of the 1820s and 1830s: a little bottle of phosphorus paste into which a sulphur-tipped match was dipped and then rubbed to catch fire. It was fiddly at the best of times, and Valjean needs three or four matches because his hand is shaking.`,quote:`the bottle of the Fumade lighter`},{id:`cartridge-box`,term:`Cartridge-box`,kind:`word`,chapter:297,text:`A leather pouch worn on a belt holding paper cartridges, each a ball and a measure of powder wrapped together, to be torn open with the teeth and rammed down the barrel. A National Guardsman's full kit included one, and the porter has no trouble finding the pieces in the neighbourhood.`,quote:`a cartridge-box filled with cartridges`},{id:`ou-vont-les-belles-filles-lon-la`,term:`“Où vont les belles filles, lon la”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:298,text:`“Where the pretty girls go, lon la”: the nonsense refrain of a song Gavroche makes up as he runs. The first verses are a string of girls' names out of novels and the stage (Atala is Chateaubriand's Indian heroine, Pamela Richardson's); the later ones turn political, with old Paris knocking down the Louvre “where monarchy displayed itself in furbelows”.`,quote:`Où vont les belles filles`},{id:`monsieur-orfila`,term:`Monsieur Orfila`,kind:`reference`,chapter:298,text:`Mathieu Orfila, dean of the Paris medical faculty and the most famous toxicologist of the day, the expert witness in every poisoning trial. In Gavroche's song the girls' poison would make even Orfila tipsy.`,quote:`Griserait Monsieur Orfila.`},{id:`le-roi-charles-dix-ce-jour-la-tenait-mal-et-se-decolla`,term:`“Le roi Charles-Dix ce jour-là, tenait mal et se décolla”`,kind:`history`,chapter:298,text:`“King Charles X that day held on badly and came unstuck.” Charles X, last of the elder Bourbon kings, was driven out by the three days of July 1830, when Parisians forced the railings of the Louvre and the Tuileries. Gavroche's song imagines doing it again.`,quote:`Le roi Charles-Dix ce jour-là`},{id:`auvergnat`,term:`Auvergnat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:298,text:`A man from the Auvergne, the poor mountain country of central France. Auvergnats came to Paris in numbers as water-carriers, coal-heavers and street-corner porters (commissionnaires) with handcarts; they were a stock figure of the city, thrifty, strong and slow.`,quote:`the Auvergnat’s head was supported against the front of the cart`},{id:`the-royal-printing-establishment`,term:`The Royal Printing Establishment`,kind:`place`,chapter:298,text:`The Imprimerie Royale, the state printing works that produced laws, decrees and official publications, housed in the Hôtel de Rohan in the Marais. As a government building it had its own guard post, here manned by suburban National Guards on camp beds.`,quote:`There was a post at the Royal Printing Establishment.`},{id:`gavroche-s-gesture`,term:`Gavroche's gesture`,kind:`custom`,chapter:298,text:`Hand to nose, fingers spread and waggled forward, while slapping the back of the head: the French “pied de nez”, the equivalent of thumbing one's nose. Hugo calls it the condensed irony of the Parisian street boy.`,quote:`lifted his left hand to a level with his nose and thrust it forward three times`},{id:`the-councils-of-war-and-the-public-ministry`,term:`The councils of war and the public ministry`,kind:`history`,chapter:298,text:`After June 1832 Paris was put under a state of siege and prisoners were tried by military courts (councils of war) until the highest court ruled this illegal. The “public ministry” is the state prosecutor. Hugo's joke is that the only captive of Gavroche's raid is a drunk Auvergnat, solemnly prosecuted as an accomplice.`,quote:`harassed before the councils of war as an accomplice`},{id:`the-insurrection-of-june-1848`,term:`The insurrection of June 1848`,kind:`history`,chapter:299,text:`Four months after the February revolution of 1848 set up the Second Republic, the government closed the National Workshops that employed the Paris unemployed. The workers of the eastern faubourgs rose, and in four days of street war (23–26 June) the army crushed them with thousands dead. Hugo, then a deputy, was on the side of the Assembly; this chapter is his attempt to honour the men he helped to fight.`,quote:`the fatal insurrection of June, 1848`},{id:`ochlocracy-and-demos`,term:`Ochlocracy and demos`,kind:`word`,chapter:299,text:`Greek terms: demos is the people as a political body; ochlocracy is rule by the mob. Hugo uses the pair to name what troubled him about June 1848: the poor in arms against a republic elected by universal suffrage, the people fighting itself.`,quote:`the ochlocracy rises against demos`},{id:`fex-urbis-lex-orbis`,term:`“Fex urbis, lex orbis”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:299,text:`Latin: “the dregs of the city, the law of the world.” Hugo gives the saying to Saint Jerome, the fourth-century translator of the Bible, as a thought about the poor from whom the apostles and martyrs came. The rhyme of fex and lex is the point: the lowest of the city become the rule of the earth.`,quote:`“Fex urbis, lex orbis,”`},{id:`the-faubourg-saint-antoine-barricade`,term:`The Faubourg Saint-Antoine barricade`,kind:`history`,chapter:299,text:`In June 1848 the great barricade at the mouth of the Faubourg Saint-Antoine stood on the Place de la Bastille, “the formidable place which had seen the 14th of July”. The working-class faubourg east of the Bastille had fought in every revolution since 1789, which is why Hugo piles the dates of the Revolution onto its heap of rubbish.`,quote:`the formidable place which had seen the 14th of July`},{id:`an-ossa-on-pelion`,term:`An Ossa on Pelion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:299,text:`In Greek myth the giants piled Mount Ossa on Mount Pelion to storm heaven. Hugo follows it with a pile of revolutionary dates: 1793 on 1789, Thermidor (the fall of Robespierre) on the 10th of August (the fall of the monarchy), Brumaire (Bonaparte's coup) and the Revolution's other turning-points, 1848 on 1830.`,quote:`an Ossa on Pelion of all revolutions`},{id:`the-carmagnole-and-the-marseillaise`,term:`The Carmagnole and the Marseillaise`,kind:`reference`,chapter:299,text:`Two songs of the Revolution. The Marseillaise was the anthem of the nation and of the Republic; the Carmagnole was a dancing song of the sans-culottes, mocking the king and queen. Hugo's line means the rabble of the Revolution defying the Republic it had made.`,quote:`it was the Carmagnole bidding defiance to the Marseillaise`},{id:`the-african-generals`,term:`The African generals`,kind:`history`,chapter:299,text:`Cavaignac, Lamoricière and the other commanders who put down June 1848 had made their careers in the conquest of Algeria, begun in 1830. Zaatcha and Constantine were Algerian strongholds taken by storm, the soldiers boring through houses and coming over the roofs; they used the same method on the Faubourg du Temple.`,quote:`the strategy of the African generals`},{id:`biscaien`,term:`Biscaïen`,kind:`word`,chapter:299,text:`A heavy musket ball or a small iron cannon-ball, the size of a walnut, of the kind fired as grapeshot. The defenders of the Temple barricade fired them from home-made guns of cast-iron gas-pipe.`,quote:`a biscaïen was seen to ensconce itself in some closed shutter`},{id:`cournet-and-barthelemy`,term:`Cournet and Barthélemy`,kind:`history`,chapter:299,text:`Frédéric Cournet, a former naval officer, and Emmanuel Barthélemy, a worker sent to the galleys as a youth for shooting a policeman, both real leaders of June 1848. Exiled in London, they quarrelled and fought a duel in 1852 in which Barthélemy killed Cournet; in 1855 Barthélemy was hanged in London for a separate double killing. Hugo knew the story from exile.`,quote:`Cournet made the Saint-Antoine barricade; Barthélemy the barricade of the Temple.`},{id:`colonel-monteynard`,term:`Colonel Monteynard`,kind:`history`,chapter:299,text:`An officer before the Temple barricade in June 1848, as Hugo tells it. His remark that the wall was made “of porcelain” is Hugo's emblem of that barricade's eerie precision; the cross the bullet breaks on his breast is his Legion of Honour.`,quote:`It is made of porcelain.`},{id:`ambulance`,term:`Ambulance`,kind:`word`,chapter:300,text:`In the military language of the time, not a vehicle but a field hospital or dressing-station. The insurgents turn Corinthe's kitchen into one, with mattresses and straw for the badly wounded, and tend the captured municipal guards first.`,quote:`the kitchen appropriated for the ambulance`},{id:`jeanne-and-the-barricade-saint-merry`,term:`Jeanne and the barricade Saint-Merry`,kind:`history`,chapter:300,text:`Charles Jeanne, a worker and former soldier, led the strongest real barricade of June 1832, by the church of Saint-Merry, which held out until the afternoon of the 6th. Hugo quotes his grim answer to the hungry men here, and in the next chapter the insurgents' cry as it was recorded in the trial papers. He sets his invented barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie beside this historical one.`,quote:`in the barricade Saint-Merry, Jeanne, surrounded by the insurgents`},{id:`harmodius-and-aristogiton-brutus-chereas-stephanus-charlotte-corday-sand`,term:`Harmodius and Aristogiton, Brutus, Chereas, Stephanus, Charlotte Corday, Sand`,kind:`reference`,chapter:300,text:`A roll of political assassins. Harmodius and Aristogiton killed the Athenian tyrant's brother; Brutus killed Caesar; Chaerea killed Caligula; Stephanus killed Domitian; Charlotte Corday killed Marat in his bath in 1793; the student Karl Sand killed the playwright Kotzebue in 1819. Combeferre is thinking of Enjolras's execution of Le Cabuc: each, he says, felt the remorse of having killed.`,quote:`Harmodius and Aristogiton, Brutus, Chereas, Stephanus, Cromwell, Charlotte Corday, Sand`},{id:`translators-of-the-georgics`,term:`Translators of the Georgics`,kind:`reference`,chapter:300,text:`Virgil's poem on farming, which ends its first book with the portents at Caesar's death. Delille's French verse translation (1770) was famous; Malfilâtre, Cournand and Raux made rival versions. That students on a barricade at dawn should argue about them is Hugo's picture of their education and their calm.`,quote:`comparing the translators of the Georgics`},{id:`zoilus-maevius-vise`,term:`Zoïlus, Maevius, Visé`,kind:`reference`,chapter:300,text:`Proverbial envious critics: Zoïlus attacked Homer, Maevius Virgil, and Donneau de Visé Molière. Combeferre sets them against Cicero, whose severity towards Caesar he counts as judgement, not envy.`,quote:`When Zoïlus insults Homer, when Maevius insults Virgil, when Visé insults Molière`},{id:`regia-ac-pene-tyrannica`,term:`“Regia ac pene tyrannica”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:300,text:`Latin: “kingly and almost tyrannical”, the verdict of the Roman historian Suetonius on Caesar's conduct after he crossed the Rubicon with his army and made himself master of Rome. Combeferre, a republican, holds that Caesar fell justly.`,quote:`regia ac pene tyrannica`},{id:`cydathenaeum-myrrhinus-probalinthus`,term:`Cydathenaeum, Myrrhinus, Probalinthus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:300,text:`Demes, or villages, of ancient Attica, and the Aeantides were men of the Athenian tribe of Ajax. Bossuet, standing on the paving-stones, shows off a scrap of learning: he wishes he could speak Homer's verse like a native Greek.`,quote:`Oh Cydathenaeum, Oh Myrrhinus, Oh Probalinthus`},{id:`the-fifth-of-the-line-and-the-sixth-legion`,term:`The fifth of the line and the sixth legion`,kind:`history`,chapter:301,text:`The 5th infantry regiment of the regular army, and the 6th legion of the National Guard. Each of the twelve arrondissements of Paris raised its own legion of citizen guards. Enjolras recognises them by their shakos and standards; the insurgents had hoped some regiment “labored with” by republican agitators would come over to them.`,quote:`I have picked out the shakos of the fifth of the line, and the standard-bearers of the sixth legion.`},{id:`blouse-wearer`,term:`Blouse-wearer`,kind:`custom`,chapter:301,text:`The blue linen smock was the working man's garment, and in political talk “the blouse” meant the working class, as “the coat” meant the bourgeois. Hugo makes the man who says the barricade's decisive word a nameless worker.`,quote:`he was some unknown blouse-wearer`},{id:`grand-guard`,term:`Grand guard`,kind:`word`,chapter:302,text:`A military term for a large outpost thrown forward to watch the approaches to a position. Round the barricade every way out leads into one, where a man in a blouse with powder-blackened hands would be shot.`,quote:`some grand guard of the line or the suburbs`},{id:`saint-lazare-and-the-dead-house`,term:`Saint-Lazare and the dead-house`,kind:`place`,chapter:302,text:`Saint-Lazare was the Paris prison and hospital for women, where prostitutes picked up by the police were held. The dead-house is the Morgue, on the island of the Cité, where unidentified bodies were laid out behind glass for families to recognise. Combeferre names the two places the insurgents' women will end up if the breadwinners die.`,quote:`Misery, prostitution, the police, Saint-Lazare`},{id:`necker-hospital-and-the-house-surgeon`,term:`Necker Hospital and the house-surgeon`,kind:`place`,chapter:302,text:`The Hôpital Necker, a charity hospital on the Rue de Sèvres founded by Madame Necker in 1778. A house-surgeon (interne) was a medical student who had won by competition the right to live in and treat patients; Combeferre, the medical student of the group, speaks from what he has seen on its dissecting table.`,quote:`He was taken to the Necker Hospital, where I saw him. I was house-surgeon in that hospital.`},{id:`abandoned-children`,term:`Abandoned children`,kind:`history`,chapter:302,text:`Thousands of infants were left each year at the Paris foundling hospital or farmed out to wet-nurses and keepers in the country, and most did not survive childhood. Combeferre's figure of fifty-five percent mortality is, if anything, mild for the period.`,quote:`the mortality among abandoned children is fifty-five percent`},{id:`saint-just-and-anacharsis-cloots`,term:`Saint-Just and Anacharsis Cloots`,kind:`history`,chapter:303,text:`Two men of the Revolution, both guillotined in 1794. Saint-Just was Robespierre's young lieutenant, beautiful, pitiless and absolute; Cloots, a Prussian baron who called himself “the orator of the human race”, preached a universal republic of all mankind. Hugo measures Enjolras between the two: too much of the first, not enough of the second.`,quote:`he had too much of Saint-Just about him, and not enough of Anacharsis Cloots`},{id:`eighty-three`,term:`“Eighty-three”`,kind:`history`,chapter:303,text:`A slip in the translation: Hugo wrote “quatre-vingt-treize”, Ninety-three, the year of the Terror (1793). The “epic and redoubtable school” Enjolras belongs to is that of the men who held that the Republic might use violence to save itself.`,quote:`“Eighty-three.”`},{id:`tripods`,term:`Tripods`,kind:`reference`,chapter:303,text:`The priestess of Apollo at Delphi sat on a bronze tripod over a cleft in the rock and prophesied in a trance. Hugo means that places of death make men prophesy; Enjolras's speech that follows is his oracle.`,quote:`places where death is have these effects of tripods`},{id:`the-hydra-the-dragon-and-the-griffin`,term:`The hydra, the dragon and the griffin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:303,text:`Monsters of ancient myth ruling water, fire and air. Enjolras reads them as the forces man feared and is now taming: the hydra breathing on the waters is the steam locomotive, the griffin of the air is the balloon. The first railways in France were barely a few years old in 1832.`,quote:`We have vanquished the hydra, and it is called the locomotive`},{id:`amphictyons`,term:`Amphictyons`,kind:`history`,chapter:303,text:`The Amphictyonic League was a council of Greek peoples that met twice a year, at Delphi and at Thermopylae, to guard the shrines and settle disputes. Enjolras sees in it the model of a future parliament of Europe and of the world.`,quote:`The amphictyons had two sittings a year, one at Delphos the seat of the gods, the other at Thermopylae`},{id:`social-contract`,term:`Social contract`,kind:`reference`,chapter:303,text:`Rousseau's Social Contract (1762) founded the state on an agreement by which each person gives up part of his freedom to the community on equal terms. Enjolras builds his definitions of Liberty, Equality and Fraternity on that idea, and prefers the word “bond”.`,quote:`Some say social contract`},{id:`gratuitous-and-obligatory-instruction`,term:`Gratuitous and obligatory instruction`,kind:`history`,chapter:303,text:`Free, compulsory primary schooling: in 1832 a radical demand. The Guizot law of 1833 required every commune to keep a boys' school, but it was neither free for all nor compulsory; France made primary school free and obligatory only in 1881–82. Hugo argued for it all his life.`,quote:`gratuitous and obligatory instruction`},{id:`a-partition-of-peoples-by-a-congress`,term:`A partition of peoples by a congress`,kind:`history`,chapter:303,text:`Enjolras's list of the old world's evils is concrete: wars over royal marriages and successions, and peoples carved up by diplomats, as Poland was partitioned and the map of Europe redrawn at the Congress of Vienna in 1815 without asking the inhabitants.`,quote:`a partition of peoples by a congress`},{id:`martingale`,term:`Martingale`,kind:`word`,chapter:304,text:`Borrowed from horse harness, where a martingale is the strap from girth to bridle that keeps a horse from tossing its head. In prisons it was a rope from the neck, forking over the stomach and through the legs to the bound hands, so that any struggle tightens it. The insurgents add it to Javert's bonds, with a hobble on his feet “as is done to men on the point of mounting the scaffold”.`,quote:`that sort of bond which is called in prisons a martingale`},{id:`the-avenue-of-sphinxes-at-thebes`,term:`The avenue of Sphinxes at Thebes`,kind:`place`,chapter:305,text:`The processional road between the temples of Karnak and Luxor in Egypt, lined with hundreds of stone sphinxes. French readers knew it from the engravings of Napoleon's Egyptian expedition. Hugo uses it for the dead silence of the Rue Saint-Denis at dawn.`,quote:`as dumb as the avenue of Sphinxes at Thebes`},{id:`despair-the-last-weapon-virgil-has-said-so`,term:`“Despair, the last weapon … Virgil has said so”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:305,text:`Aeneas's line to his comrades on the night Troy falls: “Una salus victis nullam sperare salutem”, the one safety of the defeated is to hope for no safety (Aeneid II). Hugo makes the hopelessness of the barricade its strength.`,quote:`despair—the last weapon, which sometimes gives victory; Virgil has said so`},{id:`fore-carriage-caisson-and-lint-stock`,term:`Fore-carriage, caisson and lint-stock`,kind:`word`,chapter:305,text:`Parts of a field gun's equipment. The fore-carriage (limber) is the two-wheeled front unit that tows the gun and is unhooked to fire; the caisson is the ammunition wagon; the lint-stock (linstock) holds the slow-burning match that is put to the vent to fire the charge. The burning match is what the barricade sees first.`,quote:`They could see the smoke of the burning lint-stock.`},{id:`a-piece-of-eight-and-gribeauval-s-movable-star`,term:`A piece of eight and Gribeauval's movable star`,kind:`history`,chapter:305,text:`An eight-pounder, a standard field gun firing an eight-pound ball. Gribeauval reformed French artillery under Louis XV; his “movable star” was a gauge with spring points pushed down the barrel to find flaws in the bore. Combeferre, the scientist, lectures on gun metallurgy while the gun is aimed at him, and ends with the speed of light: “the superiority of Jesus Christ over Napoleon.”`,quote:`It is a piece of eight, new model, brass`},{id:`epaulement`,term:`Epaulement`,kind:`word`,chapter:306,text:`A low breastwork thrown up to cover troops or guns from the side. The soldiers make theirs out of the same paving-stones as the barricade, so the street holds two small walls facing each other.`,quote:`this epaulement`},{id:`arms-piled`,term:`Arms piled`,kind:`custom`,chapter:306,text:`Infantry at rest stood their muskets in little pyramids, three or four locked together by the bayonets, ready to be snatched up. A battalion with arms piled is waiting, not attacking, but it closes the street.`,quote:`A battalion of the line whose arms were piled in the Rue de la Petite Truanderie`},{id:`mattress-at-the-window`,term:`Mattress at the window`,kind:`custom`,chapter:307,text:`Parisians in riot quarters stuffed mattresses into their windows against stray bullets, since packed wool or straw stops a musket ball that would go through a shutter. This one hangs from two cords over the poles where the household dried its linen, and the barricade needs it to deaden the grapeshot.`,quote:`The mattress, placed crosswise, supported at the bottom on two poles for drying linen, was upheld at the top by two ropes`},{id:`the-marksmanship-of-a-poacher`,term:`The marksmanship of a poacher`,kind:`lore`,chapter:307,text:`The chapter's title recalls what the reader learned of the young tree-pruner of Faverolles: he was a fine shot and something of a poacher, which counted against him at his trial in 1796. The same eye now cuts two cords at the top of a six-storey house.`,quote:`Jean Valjean took aim at the attic window and fired.`},{id:`crinoline-in-their-locks`,term:`Crinoline in their locks`,kind:`custom`,chapter:308,text:`Writing in the 1860s, the age of the hooped crinoline skirt and of hair built up over pads and horsehair, Hugo contrasts it with the plain smoothed bands and curls of 1832, when a girl could do her hair in a few minutes.`,quote:`when women did not swell out their curls and bands with cushions and puffs, and did not put crinoline in their locks`},{id:`the-martins-nest`,term:`The martins' nest`,kind:`word`,chapter:308,text:`House martins, small blue-black and white swallows, build cup-shaped mud nests under cornices and eaves and return to them every spring. Cosette, who cannot see the street, watches the parent birds feeding their brood.`,quote:`there was a martin’s nest`},{id:`fireman-s-helmet`,term:`Fireman's helmet`,kind:`custom`,chapter:309,text:`The Paris fire service, the sapeurs-pompiers, was a military corps under the army, wearing brass helmets. That is why a fireman is posted as a lookout on a roof, and why his helmet makes so easy a mark in the sun.`,quote:`A fireman had placed his back against a tall chimney`},{id:`pantin-des-vertus-or-la-cunette`,term:`Pantin des Vertus or la Cunette`,kind:`place`,chapter:310,text:`Places on the edge of Paris beyond the customs wall (Pantin lies in the old plain of the Vertus to the north-east), where wine paid no city duty and Parisians went out on Sundays to drink and dance in guinguettes and tea-gardens. Hugo's example of the suburban National Guard is a dance-hall keeper dying for the order that keeps his trade open.`,quote:`Pantin des Vertus or la Cunette`},{id:`lacedaemonian-enthusiasm`,term:`Lacedaemonian enthusiasm`,kind:`reference`,chapter:310,text:`Lacedaemon is Sparta, byword for patriotic courage. Hugo's irony: the shopkeepers of 1832 defended the shop, “that immense diminutive of the fatherland”, with the spirit of Thermopylae.`,quote:`with Lacedaemonian enthusiasm`},{id:`governmentalism`,term:`Governmentalism`,kind:`word`,chapter:310,text:`Hugo's mock-coinage for the party of order under the July Monarchy: those who backed the government against every disturbance. His point is that the side of order was itself disorderly, with National Guard captains attacking on their own initiative.`,quote:`governmentalism (the barbarous name of the correct party)`},{id:`fannicot-and-henri-fonfrede`,term:`Fannicot and Henri Fonfrède`,kind:`history`,chapter:310,text:`Fannicot is the National Guard captain whose company, Hugo says, both shot Jean Prouvaire and threw itself at the barricade without orders; he cites the judicial inquiry of 1832 for it. Henri Fonfrède was a real Bordeaux journalist, a fierce defender of the government with his pen.`,quote:`some of the sword, like Fannicot, others of the pen, like Henri Fonfrède`},{id:`lynch-law`,term:`Lynch law`,kind:`word`,chapter:310,text:`Summary punishment without trial, named by most accounts after Charles Lynch, a Virginia justice of the American Revolution. Hugo applies it to National Guard platoons that made themselves “a private council of war” and shot prisoners in five minutes, as they shot Prouvaire.`,quote:`Fierce Lynch law`},{id:`saint-simonians-and-the-duc-de-saint-simon`,term:`Saint-Simonians and the Duc de Saint-Simon`,kind:`history`,chapter:310,text:`The Saint-Simonians were followers of the social reformer Henri de Saint-Simon, preaching an industrial religion of the poor, and much mocked in 1832. The Duc de Saint-Simon, a different man, wrote famous memoirs of the court of Louis XIV. A National Guard who could not tell them apart nearly killed a poet for carrying the wrong book.`,quote:`There’s another of those Saint-Simonians!`},{id:`place-royale`,term:`Place Royale`,kind:`place`,chapter:310,text:`The arcaded square in the Marais built under Henri IV, now the Place des Vosges. No. 6, where the hunted poet takes refuge under the carriage entrance, is the house where Hugo himself lived from 1832 to 1848.`,quote:`the porte-cochère of No. 6`},{id:`fry-in-its-own-fat`,term:`“Fry in its own fat”`,kind:`word`,chapter:310,text:`A general's phrase for letting the insurrection exhaust itself before attacking: leave the barricades to burn up their men and cartridges. It is the tactic of the whole morning, which Fannicot's charge spoils.`,quote:`“the insurrection to fry in its own fat,”`},{id:`vincennes`,term:`Vincennes`,kind:`place`,chapter:310,text:`The fortress east of Paris that served as the army's great arsenal and artillery depot. Repression “does not count its shots” because it has Vincennes behind it; the barricade has only what is in its cartridge-boxes.`,quote:`as it has Vincennes, it does not count its shots`},{id:`quid-divinum-the-10th-of-august-and-the-29th-of-july`,term:`Quid divinum; the 10th of August and the 29th of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:310,text:`Quid divinum, Latin for “something divine”, is the moment an uprising becomes a revolution. The 10th of August 1792 was the storming of the Tuileries that ended the monarchy; the 29th of July 1830 was the last of the three days that drove out Charles X. Hugo's two examples of a riot that the whole city joined.`,quote:`a 10th of August is in the air, a 29th of July is in the air`},{id:`marshal-soult-and-suchet-at-saragossa`,term:`Marshal Soult and Suchet at Saragossa`,kind:`history`,chapter:311,text:`Soult, one of Napoleon's marshals, was Louis-Philippe's minister of war in 1832. In the Peninsular War the French took Saragossa in 1809 only after weeks of house-to-house fighting against the whole population; the remark Hugo gives to Marshal Suchet, about old women emptying chamber-pots on the soldiers, is the old campaigner's knowledge that a city is lost once its households join in.`,quote:`We are lost when the old women empty their pots de chambre on our heads.`},{id:`the-rue-planche-mibray`,term:`The Rue Planche-Mibray`,kind:`place`,chapter:311,text:`A short, crowded old street running up from the Pont Notre-Dame towards the Hôtel de Ville, one of the poorest corners of the centre. Pottery thrown from its roofs at the troops was the sign Soult feared: the households joining in.`,quote:`In the Rue Planche-Mibray, they threw old pieces of pottery`},{id:`the-porte-saint-martin-and-the-cuirassiers`,term:`The Porte Saint-Martin and the cuirassiers`,kind:`place`,chapter:311,text:`The Porte Saint-Martin is a triumphal arch of Louis XIV on the northern boulevards, a usual place for crowds and trouble. Cuirassiers were heavy cavalry in steel breastplates; the army used them to clear the open boulevards, where barricades could not hold.`,quote:`In front of the Porte Saint-Martin, a young man, armed with a rifle, attacked alone a squadron of cavalry`},{id:`madame-scarron`,term:`Madame Scarron`,kind:`reference`,chapter:312,text:`Françoise d'Aubigné, wife of the poor comic poet Scarron and later, as Madame de Maintenon, the secret wife of Louis XIV. The story went that at her dinners, when there was no roast, a servant whispered “Madame, one more story”, and her wit took the place of the meat.`,quote:`like Madame Scarron, replaced nourishment with pleasantry`},{id:`roland-and-angelique`,term:`Roland and Angélique`,kind:`reference`,chapter:312,text:`In Ariosto's Orlando Furioso the paladin Roland goes mad for love of the princess Angelica. Bossuet's theory is that all heroism comes from women, which makes Enjolras, who loves no one, a puzzle.`,quote:`Roland gets himself killed for Angélique`},{id:`patria`,term:`Patria`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:312,text:`Latin: the fatherland. It is Enjolras's whole answer to Bossuet's question of whom he loves, and the “mistress” of the chapter's title.`,quote:`“Patria.”`},{id:`c-est-la-faute-a-voltaire-c-est-la-faute-a-rousseau`,term:`“C’est la faute à Voltaire … c’est la faute à Rousseau”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:313,text:`“It’s Voltaire’s fault … it’s Rousseau’s fault.” A satirical refrain of the Restoration, mocking the clergy and royalists who blamed every ill of France on the two philosophers of the Enlightenment. Gavroche improvises new couplets on it under fire (“I am not a notary … I am a little bird … misery is my trousseau”). Nanterre and Palaiseau are just villages near Paris that rhyme.`,quote:`C’est la faute à Voltaire`},{id:`antaeus`,term:`Antaeus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:313,text:`The giant of Greek myth who drew new strength each time he touched his mother, the Earth; Hercules beat him by holding him in the air. For the street boy, Hugo says, the pavement is his earth.`,quote:`there was something of Antaeus in that pygmy`},{id:`instanter-fichtre`,term:`Instanter; Fichtre`,kind:`word`,chapter:313,text:`Gavroche's vocabulary at its most mixed. “Instanter” is lawyer's Latin for “at once”, picked up on the street; “Fichtre!” is a mild French oath, something like “Blimey!”.`,quote:`“Instanter,” said Gavroche.`},{id:`barriere-d-enfer-and-the-esplanade-de-l-observatoire`,term:`Barrière d’Enfer and the Esplanade de l’Observatoire`,kind:`place`,chapter:314,text:`The streets just south of the Luxembourg garden: the Rue d’Enfer ran to the Barrière d’Enfer, a toll gate in the customs wall, and the Observatory stood at the end of the long avenue below the garden. Showmen and their booths gathered on such open ground at the edge of the city.`,quote:`at the Barrière d’Enfer; or on the Esplanade de l’Observatoire`},{id:`invenerunt-parvulum-pannis-involutum`,term:`“Invenerunt parvulum pannis involutum”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:314,text:`Latin from the Christmas gospel: “They found the infant wrapped in swaddling clothes” (Luke 2). It was carved over the door of the Paris foundling hospital in the Rue d’Enfer, where abandoned babies were taken in. Hugo sets the lost boys within sight of it.`,quote:`Invenerunt parvulum pannis involutum`},{id:`horace-goethe-la-fontaine`,term:`Horace, Goethe, La Fontaine`,kind:`reference`,chapter:314,text:`Hugo's examples of “magnificent egoists of the infinite”: the Roman poet of contented retirement; Goethe, the Olympian of Weimar, accused of looking coolly on the suffering of his age; and perhaps La Fontaine, the dreamy fabulist. Great minds, he says, who see the stars and not the hungry child.`,quote:`Horace was one of them; so was Goethe. La Fontaine perhaps`},{id:`vulcan`,term:`Vulcan`,kind:`reference`,chapter:314,text:`The Roman god of fire and the forge, lame from being thrown out of heaven by Jupiter. Hugo's proof that one may be immortal and still limp.`,quote:`One may be immortal and yet limp: witness Vulcan.`},{id:`solem-quis-dicere-falsum-audeat`,term:`“Solem quis dicere falsum audeat?”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:314,text:`Virgil, Georgics I: “Who would dare to call the sun false?” In Virgil the sun's signs never deceive; Hugo turns the line on the sunlike geniuses he has just criticised, and answers that above the sun there is God.`,quote:`Solem quis dicere falsum audeat?`},{id:`quincunx`,term:`Quincunx`,kind:`word`,chapter:314,text:`Trees planted in a repeating pattern of five, four at the corners of a square and one in the middle, so that they form straight alleys from every angle. The formal groves of the Luxembourg were planted so.`,quote:`The quincunxes and flowerbeds shed forth balm`},{id:`the-crows-of-marie-de-medici`,term:`The crows of Marie de Medici`,kind:`history`,chapter:314,text:`The Luxembourg palace and garden were built in the 1610s for Marie de Medici, widow of Henri IV and mother of Louis XIII. Hugo pretends the old crows in the tall trees are hers.`,quote:`The old crows of Marie de Medici were amorous in the tall trees.`},{id:`the-swans-hutch-and-the-great-basin`,term:`The swans' hutch and the great basin`,kind:`place`,chapter:314,text:`The large octagonal pool in front of the Luxembourg palace, with a little wooden house for its swans. It is the same basin where Marius once overheard a bourgeois giving his son advice; Hugo suggests it is the same man.`,quote:`they kept behind the swans’ hutch`},{id:`it-is-the-saturnalia`,term:`“It is the Saturnalia”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:314,text:`The Roman winter festival when slaves were licensed to play masters. For the comfortable bourgeois the insurrection is simply the rabble on holiday, and two ragged children by the basin are “Anarchy entering this garden”.`,quote:`“It is the Saturnalia.”`},{id:`palmipeds`,term:`Palmipeds`,kind:`word`,chapter:314,text:`Web-footed birds, a naturalist's term. The bourgeois uses it of the swans to sound learned while he teaches his son kindness to animals by throwing them the cake.`,quote:`“Throw it to those palmipeds.”`},{id:`from-the-tuileries-to-the-luxembourg`,term:`“From the Tuileries to the Luxembourg”`,kind:`history`,chapter:314,text:`The Tuileries palace was the king's residence; the Luxembourg palace housed the Chamber of Peers, the upper house. Hence the bourgeois's line about the distance between royalty and the peerage. “The younger branch” is the house of Orléans, Louis-Philippe's line, cousins of the deposed elder Bourbons; he fears it is doomed.`,quote:`From the Tuileries to the Luxembourg, there is but the distance which separates Royalty from the peerage`},{id:`keys-to-the-luxembourg`,term:`Keys to the Luxembourg`,kind:`custom`,chapter:314,text:`Hugo notes that certain houses on the Rue Madame and the Rue d’Enfer, whose backs gave onto the garden, held keys that let their lodgers in after the gates were shut. The well-off can enter when the gates are closed; the poor children are there only by accident.`,quote:`had keys to the Luxembourg garden`},{id:`mortuus-pater-filium-moriturum-expectat`,term:`Mortuus pater filium moriturum expectat`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:315,text:`The chapter's Latin title: “The dead father awaits the son who is about to die.” It closes on Marius wondering what his dead father, Colonel Pontmercy, will say to him when they meet.`,quote:`Marius was disturbed with regard to what his father was about to say to him.`},{id:`epidotas`,term:`Epidotas`,kind:`reference`,chapter:315,text:`Hugo's name for a sombre deity of the Spartans, to whom Enjolras is compared “sacrificing his naked sword”. The Epidotes were obscure gods of Sparta; the learned allusion sets Enjolras among the austere heroes of Lacedaemon.`,quote:`the sombre genius, Epidotas`},{id:`dunkerque`,term:`Dunkerque`,kind:`word`,chapter:315,text:`A set of little open shelves for displaying knick-knacks and curios, a “whatnot”, named after the port of Dunkirk, through which such trinkets came. Courfeyrac lays out his sword-cane, gun, pistols and cudgel with the same fond care.`,quote:`a young girl setting a small dunkerque in order`},{id:`sappers-and-miners`,term:`Sappers and miners`,kind:`word`,chapter:316,text:`Engineer troops, armed with axes, whose work was to cut through obstacles; they led an assault column to break open the barricade for the infantry behind. Their appearance tells Enjolras the final attack is coming.`,quote:`A squad of sappers and miners, axe on shoulder`},{id:`m-clermont-tonnerre-s-tug-of-war`,term:`M. Clermont-Tonnerre's “tug of war”`,kind:`history`,chapter:316,text:`The Marquis de Clermont-Tonnerre was Louis XVIII's minister of war from 1823 (minister of the navy in 1822). Hugo credits him with the phrase for the moment when the fighting comes to grips.`,quote:`M. Clermont-Tonnerre, in 1822, called “the tug of war.”`},{id:`clasp-knife`,term:`Clasp-knife`,kind:`word`,chapter:317,text:`A folding pocket knife, the ordinary tool of a working man or a convict. Javert reads it as fitting: a convict's revenge should be done with a convict's knife.`,quote:`“A clasp-knife!” exclaimed Javert, “you are right. That suits you better.”`},{id:`a-charter-granted-or-a-legal-country`,term:`A charter granted or a legal country`,kind:`history`,chapter:318,text:`The Charter was the constitution “granted” by Louis XVIII in 1814 and revised in 1830. The “legal country” (pays légal) was the tiny electorate of men rich enough to pay the tax that qualified them to vote, some two hundred thousand in a nation of thirty-two million. Hugo's phrase names a regime of law that left the people outside.`,quote:`with a charter granted or with a legal country`},{id:`the-enraged-moderates`,term:`“The enraged moderates”`,kind:`word`,chapter:318,text:`A paradox from the Revolution (les modérés enragés): frightened men of order can turn more savage than radicals. Hugo uses it for the householders behind their shutters, whose fear hardens into hatred of the men dying outside.`,quote:`“The enraged moderates.”`},{id:`from-minerva-to-pallas`,term:`From Minerva to Pallas`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`Two names of one goddess: Minerva as the Roman goddess of wisdom, Pallas Athena as the armed warrior with spear and helmet. Utopia turns into revolution when thought takes up arms.`,quote:`from Minerva turns to Pallas`},{id:`gerard-de-nerval`,term:`Gérard de Nerval`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`Romantic poet and friend of Hugo's youth, who hanged himself in 1855. His remark “God is dead, perhaps” reads, Hugo says, as despair at a halt in progress.`,quote:`“God is dead, perhaps,” said Gerard de Nerval`},{id:`john-brown-and-washington-pisacane-and-garibaldi`,term:`John Brown and Washington; Pisacane and Garibaldi`,kind:`history`,chapter:318,text:`Hugo's pairs of the failed martyr and the successful hero. John Brown raided Harper's Ferry in 1859 to start a slave rising and was hanged; Hugo had pleaded publicly for his life. Carlo Pisacane landed in southern Italy in 1857 to raise revolt and was killed; Garibaldi succeeded in 1860. These are events of Hugo's exile, years after 1832.`,quote:`John Brown is greater than Washington, and Pisacane is greater than Garibaldi.`},{id:`pavements-of-hell-and-the-bear-s-paving-stone`,term:`Pavements of hell and the bear's paving-stone`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`Two proverbs. The road to hell is paved with good intentions, so the barricade is “made of good intentions”. In La Fontaine's fable the bear, meaning to kill a fly on his sleeping friend's face, crushes his head with a paving-stone; “the bear's paving-stone” (le pavé de l'ours) is a kindness that kills.`,quote:`when we behold the pavement, we think of the bear`},{id:`a-black-ball`,term:`A black ball`,kind:`custom`,chapter:318,text:`In secret ballots, clubs and juries voted with white balls for yes and black for no. An insurrection, Hugo says, is an idea passing its examination before the people; if they drop in a black ball, it is only a riot.`,quote:`If the people lets fall a black ball`},{id:`leonidas-and-don-quixote`,term:`Leonidas and Don Quixote`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`Leonidas led the three hundred Spartans who died holding the pass of Thermopylae against the Persians; Don Quixote is Cervantes's knight who tilts at windmills. The insurgents, Hugo says, think of the first and not of the second.`,quote:`We do not think of Don Quixote but of Leonidas.`},{id:`vitae-lampada-tradunt`,term:`“Vitae lampada tradunt”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:318,text:`From Lucretius: “they hand on the torch of life”, like runners in a torch race. Hugo's torch of civilisation passes from Greece to Italy to France. (The text runs the words together.)`,quote:`Vitaelampada tradunt`},{id:`corinth-yes-sybaris-no`,term:`Corinth, yes; Sybaris, no`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`Corinth was the rich and artistic Greek city; Sybaris in southern Italy was so luxurious that “sybarite” came to mean a pampered voluptuary. A civilising people should be artistic, Hugo says, but not soft.`,quote:`Corinth, yes; Sybaris, no.`},{id:`a-missouri-or-a-south-carolina`,term:`A Missouri or a South Carolina`,kind:`history`,chapter:318,text:`Two American slave states, writing in the early 1860s as the Civil War began. When France falls into materialism, Hugo says, her ideas shrink to the size of slaveholders' politics.`,quote:`of the dimensions of a Missouri or a South Carolina`},{id:`socrates-for-falstaff`,term:`Socrates for Falstaff`,kind:`reference`,chapter:318,text:`The philosopher who died for the truth, set against Shakespeare's fat, cowardly, witty knight who lives for sack and comfort. A nation that “bites the mire and finds it good” has traded the one for the other.`,quote:`it has abandoned Socrates for Falstaff`},{id:`zouave`,term:`Zouave`,kind:`word`,chapter:319,text:`Light infantry raised in Algeria from 1830, at first from Kabyle tribesmen and later from Frenchmen in their Arab-style dress, famous for dash in the attack. Hugo's phrase means the citizen-soldiers of 1832 fought like crack colonial troops.`,quote:`a National Guardsman fought like a Zouave`},{id:`generals-who-promised-to-join`,term:`Generals who promised to join`,kind:`history`,chapter:319,text:`The republicans of 1832 had counted on officers of the old imperial army, friends of the dead General Lamarque, to bring troops over to them. None came. Hugo leaves Feuilly's “celebrated names” in brackets, unsaid.`,quote:`who are our generals, and who abandon us`},{id:`homer-s-roll-of-the-slain`,term:`Homer's roll of the slain`,kind:`reference`,chapter:319,text:`The long quotation is a battle catalogue from the Iliad (Book VI), where each Greek hero kills a named Trojan with a line of family history. Hugo sets it beside the medieval romances (Esplandian, a hero of the Amadis cycle, against the giant Swantibore) and the painted dukes of Brittany and Bourbon, then insists that a raw recruit and a medical student killing each other in a Paris alley are as great as any of them.`,quote:`Diomedes cuts the throat of Axylus`},{id:`francois-i-at-marignan`,term:`François I at Marignan`,kind:`history`,chapter:319,text:`At Marignano in 1515 the young King François I fought all day against the Swiss pikemen, and legend had him break three swords, or end with stumps of them in his hands. Enjolras ends with four.`,quote:`one more than François I at Marignan`},{id:`the-forest-of-swords`,term:`The Forest of Swords`,kind:`reference`,chapter:319,text:`One of the hells of Hindu scripture, Asipatravana, where the damned are cut by trees whose leaves are blades. Hugo's comparison for the hand-to-hand fighting on the barricade.`,quote:`the Forest of Swords`},{id:`carrefour-boucherat-and-planche-mibray`,term:`Carrefour Boucherat and Planche-Mibray`,kind:`place`,chapter:319,text:`Two humble spots of old Paris: a crossroads in the Marais and a narrow street by the Seine near the Hôtel de Ville. Hugo's point is that any ordinary corner of the city can become an epic battlefield.`,quote:`the Carrefour Boucherat or in the blind alley Planche-Mibray`},{id:`a-covered-rose`,term:`A covered rose`,kind:`word`,chapter:320,text:`A defensive move in singlestick or quarterstaff fencing, whirling the stick round the head to make a guard on every side. Enjolras uses his rifle like a stick to beat back the bayonets as he covers the door.`,quote:`what singlestick players call a “covered rose” round his head`},{id:`palafox-the-war-with-knives`,term:`Palafox: “the war with knives”`,kind:`history`,chapter:320,text:`José de Palafox, defender of Saragossa against the French in 1808. Summoned to surrender, he is said to have replied “War to the knife.” The wine-shop, Hugo says, fought like a house of Saragossa, room by room.`,quote:`“After the war with cannon, the war with knives.”`},{id:`aquafortis`,term:`Aquafortis`,kind:`word`,chapter:320,text:`Latin for “strong water”: nitric acid, used by engravers and metalworkers. These are the fifteen sealed bottles from the cellar that Enjolras kept back. Thrown down the stairwell they burn and blind; Hugo pleads that the besieged turn everything into a weapon.`,quote:`They were bottles of aquafortis.`},{id:`greek-fire-and-bayard-s-boiling-pitch`,term:`Greek fire and Bayard's boiling pitch`,kind:`reference`,chapter:320,text:`Greek fire was the Byzantine incendiary that burned even on water (Hugo links it loosely with Archimedes, who defended Syracuse with war-machines); the Chevalier Bayard, the “knight without fear and without reproach”, was the model of chivalry and a famous defender of besieged towns. Neither, Hugo says, was dishonoured by using terrible means.`,quote:`Greek fire did not disgrace Archimedes, boiling pitch did not disgrace Bayard`},{id:`do-you-wish-to-have-your-eyes-bandaged`,term:`“Do you wish to have your eyes bandaged?”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:321,text:`The courtesy of the firing squad: a condemned man was offered a blindfold, and refusing it was a mark of courage. The officer's question turns a massacre for a moment into an execution.`,quote:`Do you wish to have your eyes bandaged?`},{id:`there-was-an-insurgent-whom-i-heard-called-apollo`,term:`“There was an insurgent whom I heard called Apollo”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:321,text:`Apollo is the beautiful young god of light. Hugo says such words were spoken before the council of war that tried the insurgents of 1832, and lets the reader decide that they meant Enjolras.`,quote:`There was an insurgent whom I heard called Apollo.`},{id:`the-sewer-grating`,term:`The sewer grating`,kind:`lore`,chapter:322,text:`An iron grille about two feet square, set flat in the pavement over a shaft to the drain below, of the kind that let street water run down into the sewers. With the paving torn up for the barricade it lies loose. The fugitive's old “art of escape” sees in it the one way out that is neither over the barricade nor through the soldiers.`,quote:`an iron grating, placed flat and on a level with the soil`},{id:`twenty-five-millions-into-the-water`,term:`Twenty-five millions into the water`,kind:`money`,chapter:323,text:`Twenty-five million francs a year: Hugo's estimate of what the manure of Paris would be worth as fertiliser if it went to the fields instead of the Seine. For scale, a labourer earned perhaps five or six hundred francs a year; the half-milliard he gives for all France is five hundred million, which he says would pay a quarter of the national budget.`,quote:`Paris casts twenty-five millions yearly into the water.`},{id:`guano`,term:`Guano`,kind:`word`,chapter:323,text:`Seabird droppings accumulated over centuries on rainless islands, above all off Peru, and shipped to Europe in huge quantities from the 1840s as the wonder fertiliser of the age. Hugo's point is the absurdity of sending fleets across the world for bird dung while pouring the city's own into the sea.`,quote:`There is no guano comparable in fertility with the detritus of a capital.`},{id:`liebig`,term:`Liebig`,kind:`reference`,chapter:323,text:`Justus von Liebig, the German chemist who founded agricultural chemistry and argued that a civilisation which does not return its waste to the soil exhausts it. Hugo borrows his claim that the sewers of Rome swallowed the prosperity of the Roman peasant.`,quote:`“The sewers of Rome,” says Liebig`},{id:`the-thames-is-poisoning-london`,term:`“The Thames is poisoning London”`,kind:`history`,chapter:323,text:`Written in the years of the “Great Stink” of 1858, when the Thames, full of London's sewage, smelled so foul that Parliament could hardly sit; cholera had killed tens of thousands. Hugo, in exile in the Channel Islands, followed the English drainage debates closely.`,quote:`the Thames is poisoning London`},{id:`nets-at-saint-cloud`,term:`Nets at Saint-Cloud`,kind:`place`,chapter:323,text:`Below Paris, at Saint-Cloud, nets were stretched across the Seine, and it was there that the bodies of the drowned were caught and recovered. Hugo's grim joke: the public fortune floats away the same road.`,quote:`There should be nets at Saint-Cloud for the public fortune.`},{id:`beaujon-folly`,term:`Beaujon folly`,kind:`history`,chapter:323,text:`Nicolas Beaujon, the immensely rich financier of Louis XV's day, spent fortunes on a pleasure-house and gardens west of Paris, the Folie Beaujon. Here a byword for lavish waste.`,quote:`its Beaujon folly`},{id:`lutetia-the-city-of-mud`,term:`Lutetia, the city of mud`,kind:`history`,chapter:323,text:`Lutetia was the Roman name of Paris, usually derived from the Latin lutum, mud, because the first town stood among the marshes of the Seine. Hugo sets it beside the Athens, Tyre and Sparta that Paris also contains.`,quote:`it also contains Lutetia, the city of mud`},{id:`machiavelli-bacon-and-mirabeau`,term:`Machiavelli, Bacon and Mirabeau`,kind:`reference`,chapter:323,text:`Three great minds with stained characters: the Florentine theorist of ruthless politics; Francis Bacon, philosopher and Lord Chancellor convicted of taking bribes; Mirabeau, orator of 1789, debauched and secretly in the king's pay. Hugo's phrase for them, “grandiose vileness”, is also his phrase for the sewer.`,quote:`Macchiavelli, Bacon and Mirabeau, grandiose vileness`},{id:`madrepore`,term:`Madrepore`,kind:`word`,chapter:323,text:`A reef-building coral, honeycombed with channels. Hugo's image for the ground under Paris: riddled with the catacombs (the old quarries where the bones of emptied cemeteries were stacked), gas pipes, water mains feeding the street fountains, and the sewers.`,quote:`a colossal madrepore`},{id:`the-belt-sewer`,term:`The belt sewer`,kind:`lore`,chapter:324,text:`The great sewer that ran in an arc along the right bank, following the line of an old stream bed round the north of the old city, into which the smaller drains of the right bank emptied. It is the trunk of Hugo's “branch grafted on the river”.`,quote:`the belt sewer will form the trunk of this branch`},{id:`john-of-leyden-and-mokanna`,term:`John of Leyden and Mokanna`,kind:`history`,chapter:324,text:`Two false prophets with stage effects. John of Leyden ruled the Anabaptist kingdom of Münster in 1534–35 and was executed; Mokanna, the veiled prophet of Khorasan in the eighth century, was said to make a false moon rise from a well. (Hugo swaps their tricks.) Moore's poem Lalla Rookh made Mokanna famous.`,quote:`Mokanna, the veiled prophet of Khorassan`},{id:`the-gemoniae`,term:`The Gemoniae`,kind:`history`,chapter:324,text:`The Gemonian Stairs in Rome, where the bodies of executed criminals were thrown and left before being dragged to the Tiber. Hugo treats them as Rome's sewer; the point is that the city's refuse tells its history.`,quote:`The Germoniae narrated Rome.`},{id:`maillotins-tire-laine-huguenots-morin-s-illuminated-chauffeurs`,term:`Maillotins, tire-laine, Huguenots, Morin's illuminated, chauffeurs`,kind:`history`,chapter:324,text:`Hugo's procession of those who hid underground, one for each century. The Maillotins were Paris rioters of 1382 armed with lead mallets; the tire-laine (“cloak-snatchers”) were night thieves; the Huguenots, persecuted Protestants; Simon Morin, a visionary burned for heresy in 1663, and his followers; the chauffeurs, bandits of the 1790s who roasted their victims' feet to make them reveal their money.`,quote:`the maillotins in the fourteenth century, the tire-laine of the fifteenth`},{id:`cour-des-miracles`,term:`Cour des Miracles`,kind:`place`,chapter:324,text:`The beggars' enclaves of old Paris, where cripples and blind men were “miraculously” cured each night when they came home from begging. Hugo had made the most famous of them a setting in Notre-Dame de Paris; here the sewer is its annexe.`,quote:`the adjunct of the Cour des Miracles`},{id:`vide-gousset-and-coupe-gorge`,term:`Vide-Gousset and Coupe-Gorge`,kind:`place`,chapter:324,text:`Real old street names of Paris: Vide-Gousset means “Empty-Purse”, Coupe-Gorge “Cut-Throat”. The thieves who worked them by day slept in the drains by night.`,quote:`the blind-alley Vide-Gousset, or the Rue Coupe-Gorge`},{id:`villon-and-rabelais`,term:`Villon and Rabelais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:324,text:`François Villon, the fifteenth-century poet who was a thief and nearly hanged, and Rabelais, the sixteenth-century comic genius of gross humour. Through the sewer's air-holes the underworld poet talks with the laughing one above.`,quote:`Villon within converses with Rabelais without`},{id:`the-mask-of-basil-and-scapin-s-false-nose`,term:`The mask of Basil and Scapin's false nose`,kind:`reference`,chapter:324,text:`Stage hypocrites: Basile is the slandering music-master of Beaumarchais's Barber of Seville, Scapin the scheming valet of Molière's comedy. In the sewer their masks show the cardboard and strings: everything there is what it really is.`,quote:`The mask of Basil is to be found there`},{id:`historic-murderers-sponging-out-their-work`,term:`Historic murderers sponging out their work`,kind:`history`,chapter:324,text:`Hugo's roll of royal and revolutionary killers and their instruments: Louis XI and his executioner-provost Tristan; François I and his chancellor Duprat; Charles IX and his mother Catherine de Medici, authors of the Saint Bartholomew massacre of Protestants in 1572; Richelieu and Louis XIII; Louvois and Le Tellier, ministers of Louis XIV's persecutions; Hébert and Maillard of the Terror.`,quote:`Louis XI is there with Tristan`},{id:`messalina`,term:`Messalina`,kind:`reference`,chapter:324,text:`Wife of the Emperor Claudius, notorious in Roman satire for slipping out at night to a brothel. Philosophy, Hugo says, can read even her elbow-mark on the cloaks of Rome's porters.`,quote:`the mark of Messalina’s elbowing`},{id:`mercier`,term:`Mercier`,kind:`reference`,chapter:325,text:`Louis-Sébastien Mercier, whose Tableau de Paris (1781–88) described the city street by street, its dirt included. Hugo cites him as his witness that the sewer was then left to itself.`,quote:`Mercier attests the fact`},{id:`daedalus-backed-up-babel`,term:`Daedalus backed up Babel`,kind:`reference`,chapter:325,text:`Daedalus built the Labyrinth of Crete; Babel is the tower of confused tongues. Old Paris was Babel above, its streets and authorities unintelligible, and a labyrinth below.`,quote:`Daedalus backed up Babel`},{id:`the-inundation-of-1802`,term:`The inundation of 1802`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`When the Seine rose in 1802 it drove back up the sewers and the filth burst out into the streets, over the Place des Victoires with its statue of Louis XIV and across both banks. Hugo's street-by-street list and measurements read like an official report, which is the effect he wants.`,quote:`The inundation of 1802`},{id:`sainte-foix-and-the-marquis-de-crequi`,term:`Sainte-Foix and the Marquis de Créqui`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`An eighteenth-century anecdote: Saint-Foix, a playwright famous for his quarrels and duels, and the great nobleman Créqui hobnobbing atop a scavenger's cart. Hugo uses it to recall that in that age the carts of street refuse emptied straight into the sewer mouths.`,quote:`Sainte-Foix fraternized with the Marquis de Créqui`},{id:`the-polypus-hole`,term:`The Polypus-Hole`,kind:`lore`,chapter:325,text:`The Paris nickname for the sewer, in French the Trou Punais, “the stinking hole”; the English renders it after the polyp, a word also used of a foul growth in the nose. Rome had dignified its refuse with a name; Paris insulted its own.`,quote:`Paris insulted hers, and entitled it the Polypus-Hole`},{id:`the-marmousets-and-fagon`,term:`The Marmousets and Fagon`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`Legends of the sewer. The Marmousets were the low-born counsellors of Charles VI, overthrown in 1392; Hugo repeats the tale that their corpses were thrown into a drain. Fagon, Louis XIV's physician, blamed a deadly fever of 1685 on the open sewer of the Marais, a mouth in the Rue Saint-Louis that stayed open until 1833.`,quote:`the corpses of the Marmousets had been cast into the sewer de la Barillerie`},{id:`some-decres-or-cretet-or-other`,term:`“Some Decrès or Crétet or other”`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`Hugo's deliberately offhand naming of Napoleon's minister: Crétet was Minister of the Interior from 1807, Decrès Minister of the Navy. The vagueness puts the great man in his place beside the sewerman.`,quote:`some Decrès or Crétet or other`},{id:`the-veterans-at-the-levee`,term:`The veterans at the levee`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`A roll-call of the armies of the Republic and the Empire before Austerlitz (December 1805): the generals Hoche, Kléber, Desaix and Marceau; the balloonists who observed at Fleurus in 1794; the bridge of Lodi and siege of Mantua in the Italian campaign; the French cavalry that captured the Dutch fleet frozen in the ice in 1795. Hugo heaps up glory in order to set Bruneseau above it.`,quote:`the aérostiers of Fleurus`},{id:`bruneseau`,term:`Bruneseau`,kind:`history`,chapter:325,text:`Pierre-Emmanuel Bruneseau, inspector of sanitation under Napoleon, who between 1805 and 1812 led the first systematic survey of the Paris sewers, mapping and cleaning galleries no one had entered for generations. Hugo makes him an explorer of the underworld, the Columbus of the cesspool.`,quote:`This man existed and his name was Bruneseau.`},{id:`fourcroy`,term:`Fourcroy`,kind:`history`,chapter:326,text:`Antoine-François Fourcroy, the chemist and colleague of Lavoisier, a public-health adviser under Napoleon. His remedy for the sewer's poisonous air was fire: cages of resin-soaked tow burned to purify the galleries.`,quote:`On the advice of Fourcroy`},{id:`philibert-delorme`,term:`Philibert Delorme`,kind:`history`,chapter:326,text:`The great Renaissance architect of Henri II and Catherine de Medici, builder of the Tuileries. Bruneseau found a stone dated 1550 marking how far Delorme's inspection of the drains had gone: the high-water mark of the sixteenth century.`,quote:`Philibert Delorme, charged by Henri II with visiting the subterranean drains of Paris`},{id:`the-brook-of-menilmontant`,term:`The brook of Ménilmontant`,kind:`lore`,chapter:326,text:`A stream that once ran from the heights of Ménilmontant round the north of old Paris to the Seine. From 1412 it was walled and made the Grand Sewer, the belt sewer of the right bank: a country brook promoted to royal service, as if a peasant (Gros-Jean) became Lebel, Louis XV's first valet.`,quote:`something like Gros-Jean transformed into Lebel`},{id:`the-orang-utan-and-the-devil-of-the-rue-des-bernardins`,term:`The orang-utan and the devil of the Rue des Bernardins`,kind:`lore`,chapter:326,text:`Among Bruneseau's finds, the skeleton of an ape escaped from the Jardin des Plantes zoo in 1800. Hugo connects it, straight-faced, with a famous sighting of “the devil” in a nearby street that year.`,quote:`the skeleton of an orangutan, who had disappeared from the Jardin des Plantes in 1800`},{id:`the-arche-marion`,term:`The Arche-Marion`,kind:`place`,chapter:326,text:`An old vaulted outfall of the sewer on the Seine, near the Quai de la Mégisserie on the right bank, where a long drain ended. It gave its name to the spot; the sewermen's finds there (here, a ragpicker's basket preserved whole) were the stuff of legend.`,quote:`this long, arched drain which terminated at the Arche-Marion`},{id:`the-huguenot-medal`,term:`The Huguenot medal`,kind:`history`,chapter:326,text:`Protestant satire of the Wars of Religion: a copper medal showing a pig in a cardinal's hat and a wolf wearing the papal tiara, mocking the Catholic hierarchy as greedy and predatory. Owning one could be dangerous; someone dropped it down a drain.`,quote:`the pig hooded with a cardinal’s hat`},{id:`marat-s-shroud`,term:`Marat's shroud`,kind:`history`,chapter:326,text:`Jean-Paul Marat, “the Friend of the People”, the most violent journalist of the Revolution, was stabbed in his bath in 1793. Before the Revolution he had been a physician in the household of the Comte d'Artois. His body was placed in the Panthéon in 1794 and thrown out a few months later. Hugo's story of the embroidered sheet from a marquise (LAVBESP, Laubespine) carries him from the bed of a great lady to the rats.`,quote:`a morsel of the shroud of Marat`},{id:`batiste`,term:`Batiste`,kind:`word`,chapter:326,text:`A very fine, soft linen or cotton cloth, used for handkerchiefs, shirts and the best bed-linen, named after a legendary weaver of Cambrai. A scrap of it on a hinge in the sewer was an aristocratic object in the wrong place.`,quote:`It was of very fine batiste`},{id:`watteau-and-dante`,term:`Watteau and Dante`,kind:`reference`,chapter:326,text:`Watteau painted the silks and amorous parties of the early eighteenth century; Dante descended into Hell. The rag of Marat's shroud begins as the one and ends as the other.`,quote:`This chamber rag, of which Watteau would formerly have joyfully sketched every fold`},{id:`respectable-and-the-councillor-of-state`,term:`“Respectable” and the councillor of state`,kind:`word`,chapter:327,text:`Hugo uses the English word in quotation marks: to a French ear it summed up English propriety. The modern sewer is like a tradesman made councillor of state (a member of the Conseil d'État, the government's highest administrative body): risen, clean and dull.`,quote:`what is understood in England by the word “respectable.”`},{id:`the-rectilinear-alexandrine-and-the-rue-de-rivoli`,term:`The rectilinear alexandrine and the Rue de Rivoli`,kind:`reference`,chapter:327,text:`The alexandrine, the twelve-syllable line of French classical tragedy, which the Romantics (Hugo first among them) broke up; the Rue de Rivoli, Napoleon's long straight arcaded street. The new sewer is as regular as both: classicism banished from poetry has gone underground.`,quote:`the classical rectilinear alexandrine`},{id:`tartuffe-after-confession`,term:`Tartuffe after confession`,kind:`reference`,chapter:327,text:`Molière's Tartuffe is the religious hypocrite who wears piety as a mask. The clean modern sewer, Hugo says, still gives off a suspicious smell like him fresh from confession: more hypocritical than irreproachable.`,quote:`like Tartuffe after confession`},{id:`the-augean-stables`,term:`The Augean stables`,kind:`reference`,chapter:327,text:`The filthiest place in Greek myth: King Augeas's stables, uncleaned for thirty years, which Hercules washed out in a day by turning two rivers through them. Even Tartuffe's conscience is an improvement on them.`,quote:`Tartuffe’s conscience is a progress over the Augean stables`},{id:`sixty-leagues-of-sewers`,term:`Sixty leagues of sewers`,kind:`history`,chapter:328,text:`Hugo's figures, reign by reign, show the network growing from about twenty-three kilometres under the old monarchy to some two hundred and twenty-six by the early 1860s, ten times over, most of it built under Louis-Philippe and then Napoleon III (“the present government”) as Haussmann rebuilt Paris. A league is about four kilometres.`,quote:`sixty leagues of sewers`},{id:`moutardes`,term:`Moutardes`,kind:`word`,chapter:328,text:`French for “mustards”: the sewer-diggers' name for the soft, yellowish, liquid clays under Paris that swallowed tools and men. One of several hazards of a soil Hugo calls “rebellious to the pick”.`,quote:`which special science calls moutardes`},{id:`the-bievre-and-the-ourcq`,term:`The Bièvre and the Ourcq`,kind:`place`,chapter:328,text:`Two waters of Paris. The Bièvre, a small river of the Left Bank fouled by tanneries and dye-works, was vaulted over and turned into a sewer. The Ourcq canal, dug under Napoleon, brought drinking water to the city from the north-east.`,quote:`vaulted the Bièvre from the Boulevard de l’Hôpital, as far as the Seine`},{id:`monnot-and-duleau`,term:`Monnot and Duleau`,kind:`history`,chapter:328,text:`A works superintendent and an engineer of the Paris sewers who, Hugo says, died of the work, after cutting galleries through quicksand and putrid ground. He names them as soldiers of a war that issues no bulletins.`,quote:`Duleau the engineer died`},{id:`the-cholera-and-the-sewers`,term:`The cholera and the sewers`,kind:`history`,chapter:328,text:`The cholera epidemic of spring 1832 killed some eighteen thousand Parisians. Only fear of it, Hugo says, produced the rebuilding of the sewers that Bruneseau had begun; as late as 1821 a stretch of the belt sewer still lay open to the sky like a canal.`,quote:`the cholera was required to bring about the vast reconstruction`},{id:`cassis`,term:`Cassis`,kind:`word`,chapter:328,text:`The road engineers' name for the big barred gratings at the bottom of a sunken street, where gutters met, worn slippery by feet and dangerous to horses. Many Paris streets of 1832 dipped in the middle to drain into them, instead of rising in the middle as they later did.`,quote:`the expressive name of Cassis`},{id:`the-well-of-grenelle`,term:`The well of Grenelle`,kind:`place`,chapter:328,text:`An artesian well bored at Grenelle, on the Left Bank, between 1833 and 1841, more than five hundred metres down to the water-bearing sands beneath Paris. Its water was thought to gather the rivers of half of France, which is Hugo's “one drinks the Seine … and the Loire in a glass”.`,quote:`the well of Grenelle`},{id:`sewerman-and-knacker`,term:`Sewerman and knacker`,kind:`custom`,chapter:328,text:`The sewerman's trade was as despised and feared as the knacker's (the man who slaughtered worn-out horses and rendered carcasses), long left to the executioner's family. High wages were needed to get men down, and the proverb said that to go into the sewer was to enter the grave.`,quote:`to descend into the sewer is to enter the grave`},{id:`the-slope-as-the-clue`,term:`The slope as the clue`,kind:`lore`,chapter:329,text:`Every Paris sewer runs downhill to the Seine, so the slope underfoot is the thread through the labyrinth: follow the water and you reach the river. That is exactly why Valjean climbs against it, since the nearest outlets would put him in daylight at the busiest spot in the city.`,quote:`To follow to the slope is to arrive at the river.`},{id:`between-the-pont-au-change-and-the-pont-neuf`,term:`Between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf`,kind:`place`,chapter:329,text:`The stretch of right-bank quay facing the Île de la Cité, at the heart of Paris, crowded with traffic, markets and guard posts. An outlet there was the worst place to come up with a bleeding insurgent on one's back.`,quote:`between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf`},{id:`branches`,term:`Branches`,kind:`word`,chapter:329,text:`The sewermen's word for a blind side-passage that leads nowhere. Valjean has landed in one, walled behind him, with only the faint light from the shaft he came down.`,quote:`one of those blind alleys, which the special jargon terms branches`},{id:`the-post-office-and-the-rotunda-of-the-wheat-market`,term:`The Post-Office and the rotunda of the Wheat Market`,kind:`place`,chapter:329,text:`Landmarks above the sewers west of the Halles: the central Post-Office in the Rue Jean-Jacques-Rousseau and the round domed Halle au Blé, the grain exchange. Hugo names the streets above each gallery so that the reader can follow the route on a map, while Valjean cannot.`,quote:`under the Post-Office and under the rotunda of the Wheat Market`},{id:`eight-hundred-livres-the-fathom-petits-materiaux`,term:`Eight hundred livres the fathom; petits matériaux`,kind:`money`,chapter:329,text:`Two scales of cost and quality. The royal sewer under the Rue Saint-Denis was granite and mortar at eight hundred livres the fathom (toise, about two metres); the new work of Valjean's gallery was porous stone and concrete at two hundred francs the metre, in “small stuff”, cheap bourgeois masonry. Hugo slips a social comparison into the stonework.`,quote:`the bourgeoise masonry known as à petits matériaux`},{id:`like-the-prophet-he-was-in-the-belly-of-the-monster`,term:`“Like the prophet, he was in the belly of the monster”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:329,text:`Jonah, swallowed by the great fish and kept three days in its belly before being cast up alive. The image fits the book's title for this section, the intestine of Paris.`,quote:`Like the prophet, he was in the belly of the monster.`},{id:`the-watershed-of-the-right-bank`,term:`The watershed of the right bank`,kind:`lore`,chapter:329,text:`A low ridge under the right bank divides the drainage: one side runs straight down to the Seine, the other north into the Grand Sewer, which carries its water round to come out far downstream. When the water starts running with him, Valjean has crossed the crest.`,quote:`The ridge which the soil of Paris forms on its right bank empties one of its watersheds into the Seine and the other into the Grand Sewer.`},{id:`battue`,term:`Battue`,kind:`word`,chapter:330,text:`A hunting term: beaters drive game out of cover towards the guns. On 6 June the police beat the sewers for fugitive insurgents while the army swept the streets.`,quote:`a battue of the sewers had been ordered`},{id:`gisquet-and-bugeaud`,term:`Gisquet and Bugeaud`,kind:`history`,chapter:330,text:`Henri Gisquet was Prefect of Police of Paris in 1832, the official whose signature was on Javert's card. Thomas Bugeaud, a general of the July Monarchy and later the conqueror of Algeria, is Hugo's commander for the troops in the streets. One searches “occult Paris”, the other “public Paris”.`,quote:`Prefect Gisquet was to search occult Paris while General Bugeaud swept public Paris`},{id:`bousingot`,term:`Bousingot`,kind:`word`,chapter:330,text:`Slang of the early 1830s for a young republican hothead, from the glazed sailor's hat (bousingot) such youths affected. Hugo notes it filled the gap between “Jacobin”, gone stale, and “demagogue”, which came later.`,quote:`any “bousingot” to track out`},{id:`spinning`,term:`“Spinning”`,kind:`word`,chapter:331,text:`Police slang, from the French filer, to tail a suspect without arresting him, in the hope that he will lead the way to his accomplices. The agent in this chapter does it by the book, with a hackney-coach following on the quay above in case it is needed.`,quote:`This delicate operation is called “spinning.”`},{id:`blue-and-red-clothed-by-the-state`,term:`Blue and red: clothed by the state`,kind:`custom`,chapter:331,text:`Hugo's sour joke on two uniforms the state hands out. The agent wears blue, the colour of soldiers and officials; the galley convict wore a red jacket. “There is a purple from below”: the red of the prison is the dark twin of royal purple.`,quote:`To be dressed in blue is glorious; to be dressed in red is disagreeable.`},{id:`the-hackney-coach-rule`,term:`The hackney-coach rule`,kind:`custom`,chapter:331,text:`Hugo quotes the secret instructions of the Paris police: “Always have on hand a hackney-coach.” Fiacres plied for hire everywhere, and drivers knew the agents; one signal and the empty coach turns and keeps pace above the quay.`,quote:`“Always have on hand a hackney-coach, in case of emergency.”`},{id:`the-watering-slope-and-the-shore-below-the-quay`,term:`The watering slope and the shore below the quay`,kind:`place`,chapter:331,text:`In 1832 the right bank below the Champs-Élysées, between the Pont des Invalides and the Pont de Jéna, was still a strip of open shore under the quay wall, with a ramp down which cab-drivers coming from Passy brought their horses to drink. The later embankments did away with both, as Hugo complains.`,quote:`permitted cabdrivers arriving from Passy to come to the river and water their horses`},{id:`the-house-of-francois-i`,term:`The house of François I`,kind:`place`,chapter:331,text:`A Renaissance house front taken down at Moret-sur-Loing and rebuilt on the Cours-la-Reine in 1824 by Colonel Brack, as a fashionable antique. Hugo uses it as a landmark for the deserted stretch of shore.`,quote:`the house brought to Paris from Moret in 1824, by Colonel Brack`},{id:`a-government-key`,term:`“A government key!”`,kind:`lore`,chapter:331,text:`The river outlets of the sewers were closed with locked iron gratings, and only the authorities held keys. The pursuer's outrage is that the man has not picked the lock with a hook but opened it with an official key, which he should not have.`,quote:`“That is too much! A government key!”`},{id:`the-nosebag`,term:`The nosebag`,kind:`custom`,chapter:331,text:`The canvas bag of oats strapped over a cab-horse's muzzle while it waits. Hugo's parenthesis that the Government sometimes applies it to Parisians means it muzzles them with a ration.`,quote:`encased his horses’ muzzles in the bag of oats`},{id:`the-grand-sewer`,term:`The Grand Sewer`,kind:`lore`,chapter:332,text:`The old walled brook of Ménilmontant, the main collector of the right bank: eight feet wide and seven high, about two leagues long, curving round the north of the city to its outlet far downstream. Despite the name “belt”, Hugo stresses, it is not a ring: upstream it ends in a dead end at the foot of Ménilmontant, so turning left, downstream, was the only right choice.`,quote:`The Grand Sewer is, in fact, eight feet wide and seven feet high.`},{id:`banquette`,term:`Banquette`,kind:`word`,chapter:332,text:`The raised ledge or footway running along the side of a sewer gallery above the channel of running water. Valjean lays Marius on it under the light of an air-hole to bind his wounds.`,quote:`deposited Marius on the banquette of the sewer`},{id:`the-isle-louviers-and-the-arsenal`,term:`The Isle Louviers and the Arsenal`,kind:`place`,chapter:332,text:`At the eastern end of old Paris, near the Bastille: the Île Louviers was a small island of timber-yards in the Seine, joined to the right bank in 1843, and the Arsenal was the old royal powder and artillery works beside it. Hugo traces the route through the sewers by which Valjean might have reached an outlet there, and which he could not have known.`,quote:`above the ancient Isle Louviers`},{id:`fontis`,term:`Fontis`,kind:`lore`,chapter:333,text:`The sewermen's word for a spot where water had seeped under the floor of a gallery and turned the subsoil to liquid mud, so that the pavement sagged or gave way into a slough. It might be three feet deep or bottomless, solid enough to hold a child or loose enough to swallow a man in minutes. Hugo calls it “the beach of Mont Saint-Michel in a sewer”, and says such slips were common before the rebuilding begun in 1833.`,quote:`was called a fontis, in the special tongue`},{id:`quicksands-of-brittany-and-mont-saint-michel`,term:`Quicksands of Brittany and Mont Saint-Michel`,kind:`place`,chapter:333,text:`The bay round Mont Saint-Michel, between Normandy and Brittany, has vast tidal flats where the sea retreats for miles and the sand, soaked from beneath, can liquefy under a walker's feet. Pilgrims and fishermen drowned there, and the danger was notorious. Hugo knew such coasts from his years in the Channel Islands.`,quote:`it is the beach of Mont Saint-Michel in a sewer`},{id:`birdlime`,term:`Birdlime`,kind:`word`,chapter:333,text:`A sticky paste smeared on twigs to trap small birds by the feet. The wet sand of the quicksand holds the walker the same way.`,quote:`it is no longer sand, it is birdlime`},{id:`clarence-and-the-butt-of-malvoisie`,term:`Clarence and the butt of Malvoisie`,kind:`history`,chapter:333,text:`George, Duke of Clarence, brother of Edward IV, was put to death in the Tower in 1478 and, by tradition, drowned in a cask of Malmsey (Malvoisie), a sweet Greek wine; Shakespeare uses the story in Richard III. Hugo grants that death a certain dignity, against death in a scavenger's ditch.`,quote:`To die in a butt of Malvoisie, like Clarence, is permissible`},{id:`escoubleau-and-the-duchesse-de-sourdis`,term:`Escoubleau and the Duchesse de Sourdis`,kind:`history`,chapter:333,text:`Hugo's tale of a young officer of the house of Escoubleau, surprised with his cousin the Duchesse de Sourdis, who hid in a drain to escape the duke and drowned in its mire. At the siege of Lérida in Catalonia (1647) Condé's officers were said to have gone to the assault in silk stockings with violins playing; the dandy hero dies a dandy's opposite.`,quote:`Vicomte d’Escoubleau`},{id:`the-charnier-des-innocents`,term:`The Charnier des Innocents`,kind:`place`,chapter:333,text:`The Cemetery of the Holy Innocents beside the Halles, for centuries the great common burial ground of Paris, with its charnel-house galleries of bones. It was closed in 1780 as a danger to health and emptied into the catacombs in 1785–87; the market and the Fountain of the Innocents took its place.`,quote:`the cemetery called the Charnier des Innocents, in 1785`},{id:`the-rue-careme-prenant`,term:`The Rue Carême-Prenant`,kind:`place`,chapter:333,text:`A street in the north-east of Paris whose name means Shrovetide, the days of feasting before Lent. Its manhole is where the sewerman Blaise Poutrain was swallowed in a fontis.`,quote:`the manhole of the Rue Carême-Prenant`},{id:`the-heaping-up-of-the-parthenon`,term:`“The heaping up of the Parthenon”`,kind:`place`,chapter:333,text:`Read Panthéon: the huge domed church of Sainte-Geneviève on the Left Bank hill, built from 1758 and made a temple for great men in 1791. Its weight crushed old underground vaults beneath it. The English edition misprints the name.`,quote:`the heaping up of the Parthenon`},{id:`hero-and-leander-pyramus-and-thisbe`,term:`Hero and Leander; Pyramus and Thisbe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:333,text:`Two pairs of lovers from Greek and Roman legend whose love outlasts death: Hero mourned Leander drowned swimming the Hellespont to her, and Thisbe killed herself over Pyramus's body. In the sewer, Hugo says, love does not hold: Hero will not wash the body, Thisbe holds her nose.`,quote:`Hero refuses to wash the body of Leander.`},{id:`sulfuretted-hydrogen`,term:`Sulfuretted hydrogen`,kind:`word`,chapter:333,text:`Hydrogen sulphide, the rotten-egg gas given off by sewage, poisonous in quantity. It was the sewermen's great danger before ventilation, and its stench is what Hugo sets in place of a sea wind.`,quote:`sulfuretted hydrogen in place of the hurricane`},{id:`the-subsoil-of-the-champs-elysees`,term:`The subsoil of the Champs-Élysées`,kind:`place`,chapter:334,text:`The western end of the right bank, towards the Seine, lies on loose river sand, as do the Saint-Georges and Martyrs quarters to the north on their gassy clay. Rebuilding the sewer under the Faubourg Saint-Honoré in 1836 took six months for that reason. It tells the reader where Valjean now is: under the rich new west of Paris, of mansions (hôtels) and carriages.`,quote:`the quicksand, which forms the subsoil of the Champs-Élysées as far as the Seine`},{id:`old-paintings-of-the-deluge`,term:`Old paintings of the Deluge`,kind:`reference`,chapter:334,text:`Scenes of Noah's Flood were a favourite subject of painters, from Poussin's Winter to Girodet's Scene of a Deluge (1806), and often showed a figure in the rising water lifting a child above it. Hugo gives Valjean that pose.`,quote:`In the old paintings of the deluge there is a mother holding her child thus.`},{id:`gehenna`,term:`Gehenna`,kind:`reference`,chapter:335,text:`The valley outside Jerusalem where refuse burned, which became a Jewish and Christian name for hell. Hugo's damned soul sees its way out: daylight at the end of the gallery.`,quote:`the outlet of Gehenna`},{id:`the-outlet-facing-the-gros-caillou`,term:`The outlet facing the Gros-Caillou`,kind:`place`,chapter:335,text:`The sewer's mouth on the right bank between the Pont de Jéna downstream and the Pont des Invalides upstream, looking across the Seine to the Gros-Caillou, the quarter by the Champ de Mars. In 1832 this was the empty western edge of the city, “one of the most solitary points in Paris”. The text spells it “Grand-Caillou”.`,quote:`the shore which faces the Grand-Caillou`},{id:`funnel-shaped-outlet`,term:`Funnel-shaped outlet`,kind:`lore`,chapter:335,text:`The gallery narrows and lowers towards its grating like the wicket of a prison, a design copied, Hugo says, from penitentiaries and later corrected. The double-locked grating with its rusty bolt is exactly what the convict knows best.`,quote:`imitated from the wickets of penitentiaries`},{id:`half-shares`,term:`“Half shares”`,kind:`word`,chapter:336,text:`Thieves' bargain: split the take. The man with the key assumes he is dealing with a murderer who has robbed his victim, and offers the exit for half the money, a rope and a stone to sink the body thrown in.`,quote:`“Half shares.”`},{id:`remained-stupid`,term:`“Remained stupid”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:336,text:`Hugo points to Pierre Corneille, “the elder”, whose tragedies use stupide in its old sense: struck dumb, stunned motionless with astonishment, not foolish.`,quote:`“remained stupid”—the expression belongs to the elder Corneille`},{id:`the-quarter-of-an-hour-before-the-examining-magistrate`,term:`The quarter of an hour before the examining magistrate`,kind:`custom`,chapter:336,text:`The juge d'instruction questioned suspects in private, with no lawyer present, before any trial; for a criminal it was the dangerous moment, where one careless word could hang him. Silence, Thénardier says approvingly, is good practice for it.`,quote:`that cursed quarter of an hour before the examining magistrate`},{id:`thirty-francs`,term:`Thirty francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:336,text:`All Valjean has in his fob: one louis d'or (twenty francs), two five-franc pieces and a few big copper sous. About two weeks' wages for a labourer, and a joke as the price of a murder: “You cut folks’ throats too cheap altogether.”`,quote:`one louis d’or, two five-franc pieces, and five or six large sous`},{id:`it-s-like-the-fair-you-pay-when-you-go-out`,term:`“It’s like the fair, you pay when you go out”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:336,text:`At the fairground booths and shows of Paris one sometimes paid not at the door but on leaving. Thénardier's joke turns the sewer into a sideshow and himself into its showman.`,quote:`It’s like the fair here, you pay when you go out.`},{id:`bludgeon-with-a-leaden-head`,term:`Bludgeon with a leaden head`,kind:`word`,chapter:337,text:`A short weighted club, the casse-tête (“head-breaker”), carried by plain-clothes agents of the police as a concealed weapon; the lead in its knob made it deadly. It identifies the tall figure before he speaks.`,quote:`a bludgeon of which the leaden head was visible`},{id:`knocker-and-carriage-gate`,term:`Knocker and carriage gate`,kind:`custom`,chapter:338,text:`Old Marais houses opened onto the street by a great carriage gate (porte-cochère) with a heavy knocker; the porter in his lodge inside let visitors in. This one, wrought with a goat and a satyr, belongs to the old style of the eighteenth century, like its owner.`,quote:`raising the heavy knocker of beaten iron, embellished in the old style`},{id:`linen-presses`,term:`Linen-presses`,kind:`word`,chapter:338,text:`Tall cupboards for household linen, the pride of a French household. Nicolette opens them to find sheets and cloth for bandages.`,quote:`Nicolette opened the linen-presses`},{id:`pulling-the-cord`,term:`Pulling the cord`,kind:`custom`,chapter:339,text:`In Paris apartment houses the front door was opened at night by the porter, who pulled a cord from his bed in the lodge to lift the latch; the visitor called out his name as he passed. “Cordon, s'il vous plaît!” was the nightly formula.`,quote:`called to the porter who was in bed and who had pulled the cord from his couch`},{id:`four-napoleons`,term:`Four napoleons`,kind:`money`,chapter:339,text:`A napoleon was the gold twenty-franc coin, so four make the eighty francs the coachman asks for seven hours and his ruined velvet cushions. It is a large sum, and Javert pays it from his own pocket at once, without the official certificate the man asks him to sign.`,quote:`Javert drew four napoleons from his pocket`},{id:`the-posts-of-the-blancs-manteaux-and-the-archives`,term:`The posts of the Blancs-Manteaux and the Archives`,kind:`place`,chapter:339,text:`Guard posts near the Rue de l’Homme Armé, named after the Blancs-Manteaux, the old monastery and street of the “White Cloaks” friars, and after the Archives at the end of the street. Valjean assumes Javert will lock him up in one of them.`,quote:`the post of the Blancs-Manteaux or to the post of the Archives`},{id:`all-sorrowful-roads-have-their-stations`,term:`“All sorrowful roads have their stations”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:339,text:`The Stations of the Cross, the fourteen halts of Christ's road to Calvary, at which the faithful pause in prayer. Valjean's pause on the first-floor landing is one.`,quote:`All sorrowful roads have their stations.`},{id:`wadding`,term:`Wadding`,kind:`word`,chapter:340,text:`Loose sheets of raw cotton, used for padding clothes. With no lint in the house, the doctor packs Marius's wounds with it to stop the bleeding, while Basque and Nicolette tear the household linen into bandages.`,quote:`the doctor, for the time being, arrested the bleeding with layers of wadding`},{id:`telling-her-beads`,term:`Telling her beads`,kind:`custom`,chapter:340,text:`Praying the rosary, counting the prayers on a string of beads. Mademoiselle Gillenormand, too prudish to stay while her nephew is undressed, takes refuge in devotion.`,quote:`She set herself to telling her beads in her own chamber.`},{id:`i-witnessed-the-death-of-louis-xvi`,term:`“I witnessed the death of Louis XVI”`,kind:`history`,chapter:340,text:`Louis XVI was guillotined on the Place de la Révolution on 21 January 1793. M. Gillenormand, born in the reign of Louis XV and over ninety, has lived through the whole of the Revolution and its sequels, which is why he calls himself a hundred years old.`,quote:`I witnessed the death of Louis XVI`},{id:`lafayette-benjamin-constant-tirecuir-de-corcelles`,term:`Lafayette, Benjamin Constant, Tirecuir de Corcelles`,kind:`history`,chapter:340,text:`Three liberal deputies of the opposition, the grandfather's villains: Lafayette, the old general of 1789 and 1830; Benjamin Constant, the liberal writer and orator, dead in 1830; and Claude Tircuy de Corcelles, a deputy of the left whose name M. Gillenormand mangles into “Tirecuir” (“Pull-leather”). He blames their speeches for Marius's wounds.`,quote:`your Lafayettes, your Benjamin Constants, and your Tirecuir de Corcelles`},{id:`clubbist-and-septembrist`,term:`Clubbist and Septembrist`,kind:`history`,chapter:340,text:`Insults from the Revolution: a clubbist was a member of the political clubs such as the Jacobins; the Septembrists were the mobs who massacred prisoners in the Paris jails in September 1792. To M. Gillenormand every young republican is one of them.`,quote:`Ah! clubbist! Ah! wretch! Ah! Septembrist!`},{id:`ammonia`,term:`Ammonia`,kind:`word`,chapter:340,text:`Smelling-salts: a phial of ammonia or sal volatile held under the nose to bring someone round from a faint. The grandfather calls it a waste of the doctor's time on a dead boy.`,quote:`What is the good of making him inhale ammonia and all that parcel of drugs?`},{id:`two-funerals-under-the-same-carriage-gate`,term:`Two funerals under the same carriage gate`,kind:`custom`,chapter:340,text:`In Paris a funeral began at the door of the house, where the coffin lay in state under the carriage entrance hung with black drapery, before the procession left for the church. The grandfather expects to go out the same way as Marius.`,quote:`That will make two funerals under the same carriage gate.`},{id:`napoleon-s-attitudes`,term:`Napoleon's attitudes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:341,text:`Popular prints fixed two poses of the Emperor: arms folded across the chest, the man of decision, and hands clasped behind the back, the man pondering. Javert had only ever used the first.`,quote:`Javert had borrowed from Napoleon’s attitudes`},{id:`the-place-du-chatelet-and-its-post`,term:`The Place du Châtelet and its post`,kind:`place`,chapter:341,text:`The square on the right bank opposite the Île de la Cité, on the site of the Grand Châtelet, the medieval fortress that had housed the royal court and prison of Paris until it was demolished in 1802. In 1832 it held a police station-house, marked by its lantern, at the meeting of the quays and the bridges to the Cité.`,quote:`the post of the Place du Châtelet`},{id:`the-rapid-by-the-pont-notre-dame`,term:`The rapid by the Pont Notre-Dame`,kind:`place`,chapter:341,text:`Between the Pont Notre-Dame and the Pont au Change, with the Quai de la Mégisserie and the Quai aux Fleurs on either hand, the river ran fast and broken through the arches, worse in 1832 for the piles of a mill built on the bridge. Boatmen dreaded it, and Hugo says the best swimmers drowned there. The river had been swollen by the recent rains.`,quote:`This point of the Seine is dreaded by mariners.`},{id:`the-parapet-of-the-quay`,term:`The parapet of the quay`,kind:`place`,chapter:341,text:`The stone wall, about chest high, that tops the embankment along the Seine, with the drop to the water below. Paris walkers lean their elbows on it to look at the river; it is where Javert stands to think, and where he comes back to.`,quote:`Javert leaned both elbows on the parapet`},{id:`the-basin-of-pontius-pilate`,term:`The basin of Pontius Pilate`,kind:`reference`,chapter:341,text:`The Roman governor who handed Jesus over to be crucified and washed his hands before the crowd to show himself innocent of the blood. The voice in Javert's head tells him that arresting the man who saved him would be the same betrayal, with clean hands.`,quote:`Then have the basin of Pontius Pilate brought and wash your claws.`},{id:`fampoux-and-the-iron-horse`,term:`Fampoux and the iron horse`,kind:`history`,chapter:341,text:`At Fampoux, near Arras, a train on the new Northern Railway left the rails in July 1846 and its carriages fell into a marsh, killing more than a dozen: one of the first great railway disasters in France. Hugo turns Javert into a locomotive running on its rigid line until it leaves the track; the original book title is “Javert Derailed”.`,quote:`the Fampoux of a rectilinear conscience`},{id:`a-road-to-damascus`,term:`A road to Damascus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:341,text:`Saul of Tarsus, persecutor of Christians, was struck blind by a light from heaven on the road to Damascus and rose as the apostle Paul (Acts 9). Hugo asks whether a locomotive can have such a conversion.`,quote:`that there should exist for the locomotive a road to Damascus`},{id:`the-station-house-table`,term:`The station-house table`,kind:`custom`,chapter:341,text:`Every police post kept the same furniture for writing reports: a straw-seated chair, a pen, a lead inkstand, a boxwood saucer of sawdust to blot wet ink, and a cardboard box of red wafers to seal the folded sheet. Hugo calls it the lowest stage of official style, where “the literature of the State has its beginning”.`,quote:`a boxwood saucer filled with sawdust and a wafer box of cardboard filled with red wafers`},{id:`a-few-observations-for-the-good-of-the-service`,term:`“A few observations for the good of the service”`,kind:`lore`,chapter:341,text:`Javert's last report is a list of small abuses in the Paris prisons and the Prefecture: prisoners made to stand barefoot while searched, a chair forbidden at the Madelonettes, bribes to the “barkers” who call names in the visiting room, a contractor's fines in the prison weaving shop, gendarmes gossiping about interrogations. Precise, humane in its details, and sealed like a letter.`,quote:`A few observations for the good of the service`},{id:`barkers`,term:`Barkers`,kind:`word`,chapter:341,text:`Prisoners given the job of shouting out the names of inmates who had visitors waiting in the parlour. Javert notes that they extorted two sous from each man to call his name clearly.`,quote:`the prisoners called barkers`},{id:`la-force-and-sainte-marie-l-egyptienne`,term:`La Force and Sainte-Marie-l’Égyptienne`,kind:`place`,chapter:341,text:`La Force was the prison in the Marais, in an old ducal mansion, divided into courts named after saints; Sainte-Marie-l’Égyptienne was one of them, used as the visiting parlour. Visitors had to cross the boys' court to reach it.`,quote:`the parlor of Sainte-Marie-l’Égyptienne`},{id:`the-mousetrap-of-the-secret-cells`,term:`The mousetrap of the secret cells`,kind:`word`,chapter:341,text:`The souricière, police slang for the holding cells under the Palais de Justice where prisoners waited to be questioned by the magistrates, kept “in secret”, cut off from all contact. Javert objects that a woman, the canteen-keeper Mme Henry, holds the wicket of so important a place in the Conciergerie.`,quote:`the wicket to the mousetrap of the secret cells`},{id:`inspector-of-the-1st-class`,term:`Inspector of the 1st class`,kind:`title`,chapter:341,text:`Javert's exact rank, which he gives with his signature, place and hour like any official minute. The police inspectors of Paris were graded in classes, the first being the most senior.`,quote:`Inspector of the 1st class.`},{id:`nolle-prosequi`,term:`Nolle prosequi`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:342,text:`Latin legal phrase for a decision not to prosecute; in French procedure, a non-lieu, the examining magistrate's finding that there is no case to answer. Boulatruelle's drunkenness on the night of the Gorbeau ambush left it unclear whether he was a robber or a victim, so he went free.`,quote:`An order of nolle prosequi`},{id:`road-mender-under-administrative-supervision`,term:`Road-mender under administrative supervision`,kind:`custom`,chapter:342,text:`Breaking stones for the roads was the work given to ex-convicts and paupers, paid by the state. Boulatruelle, a released galley-convict, lives on the Gagny–Lagny road east of Paris under police surveillance, in a turf-roofed hut.`,quote:`under administrative supervision, broken stone for the good of the state`},{id:`the-air-of-guillery`,term:`The air of Guillery`,kind:`reference`,chapter:342,text:`“Le Capitaine Guillery”, an old popular song about a brigand chief of the early seventeenth century, still whistled by country people. A huntsman's tune in the forest of Montfermeil.`,quote:`whistling the air of Guillery`},{id:`worthy-of-tityrus`,term:`Worthy of Tityrus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:342,text:`Virgil's first Eclogue opens on the shepherd Tityrus lying “beneath the shade of a spreading beech”. Hugo's mock-pastoral joke puts the thieving road-mender up the same kind of tree.`,quote:`a beech-tree of great size, worthy of Tityrus and of Boulatruelle`},{id:`the-blaru-bottom`,term:`The Blaru-bottom`,kind:`place`,chapter:342,text:`An old name for a glade in a remote, thorny part of the forest of Montfermeil, known to Boulatruelle by its heap of porous stones and a sick chestnut tree bandaged with a sheet of zinc nailed to the bark. Hugo's title calls it “the tree with the zinc plaster”.`,quote:`This glade was the one which was formerly called the Blaru-bottom.`},{id:`the-wolves-rue-de-rivoli`,term:`“The wolves’ Rue de Rivoli”`,kind:`word`,chapter:342,text:`Boulatruelle's joke for a straight line through the thicket, after Napoleon's famously straight Paris street. The straight way through brambles costs him forty minutes.`,quote:`Let’s take to the wolves’ Rue de Rivoli`},{id:`chloruretted-lotions-and-nitrate-of-silver`,term:`Chloruretted lotions and nitrate of silver`,kind:`word`,chapter:343,text:`The antiseptics of the 1830s, long before germs were understood. Chlorinated lotions (Labarraque's solution) were used to clean wounds and check rot, and nitrate of silver, lunar caustic, to burn away proud or gangrenous flesh. Marius's healing takes four months.`,quote:`the chloruretted lotions and the nitrate of silver overcame the gangrene`},{id:`cerecloths`,term:`Cerecloths`,kind:`word`,chapter:343,text:`Cloth coated with wax or plaster, used to hold dressings and bandages firmly in place; Hugo notes such fixed dressings were not yet invented in 1832. Without them every dressing had to be bound anew.`,quote:`the fixation of apparatus and bandages by cerecloths not having been invented as yet`},{id:`the-gisquet-order`,term:`The Gisquet order`,kind:`history`,chapter:343,text:`After the June rising the Prefect of Police, Gisquet, ordered doctors and hospitals to report every gunshot wound they treated. The order caused an outcry among doctors and public alike, Hugo says the king's included, and it was not enforced, which protected wounded insurgents like Marius.`,quote:`the inexcusable Gisquet order, which enjoined doctors to lodge information against the wounded`},{id:`gavotte`,term:`Gavotte`,kind:`custom`,chapter:343,text:`A lively old French court dance in duple time, already old-fashioned in 1832. The ninety-year-old dances it alone with finger castanets, singing a gallant song about Jeanne from Fougères in Brittany.`,quote:`he danced a gavotte, using his thumb and forefinger as castanets`},{id:`m-le-baron`,term:`“M. le Baron”`,kind:`title`,chapter:343,text:`Colonel Pontmercy's barony, given by Napoleon on the field of Waterloo and never recognised by the Bourbons, passed in Marius's eyes to his son. The royalist grandfather had always scorned it; calling Marius by it now, like shouting “Long live the Republic!”, shows how far joy has carried him.`,quote:`He called Marius, “M. le Baron.”`},{id:`the-men-of-93-were-giants`,term:`“The men of ’93 were giants”`,kind:`history`,chapter:343,text:`The National Convention of 1792–95, which proclaimed the Republic, executed Louis XVI and ran the Terror; Danton, Saint-Just and Robespierre were its leaders. Marius's reply to the royalist harangue is a deliberate challenge, “feeling the ground” before the real battle.`,quote:`“The men of ’93 were giants,” said Marius with severity.`},{id:`dorante-turned-geronte`,term:`Dorante turned Géronte`,kind:`reference`,chapter:344,text:`Stock names of French comedy: Dorante is the young gallant, Géronte the old father who stands in the lovers' way. M. Gillenormand, mocking himself, imagines Marius calling him a former beau grown into the obstacle, a mummy “of the Regency and of the Directory”, two famously loose periods of French manners.`,quote:`that Dorante turned Géronte`},{id:`taking-the-cockchafer-by-the-horns`,term:`Taking the cockchafer by the horns`,kind:`word`,chapter:344,text:`The grandfather's comic twist on “taking the bull by the horns”: the cockchafer, or May-beetle, is a clumsy flying beetle children played with. Marius's grand assault turns out to be against a very small beast.`,quote:`you take the cockchafer by the horns`},{id:`by-the-hundred-thousand-javottes-of-the-devil`,term:`“By the hundred thousand Javottes of the devil”`,kind:`word`,chapter:344,text:`One of M. Gillenormand's invented oaths; Javotte is a typical servant-girl's name. He vents on Basque, polishing boots in the anteroom, the fury he has swallowed in front of Marius.`,quote:`By the hundred thousand Javottes of the devil`},{id:`monsieur-boulard`,term:`Monsieur Boulard`,kind:`history`,chapter:345,text:`Antoine-Marie-Henri Boulard, a Paris notary and the most obsessive book-collector of the age, who filled several houses with hundreds of thousands of volumes and was said never to go out without one. The grandfather's example of a respectable man with a book always under his arm.`,quote:`Monsieur Boulard, one of my acquaintances, never walked out without a book under his arm`},{id:`monsieur-tranchelevent`,term:`“Monsieur Tranchelevent”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:345,text:`M. Gillenormand gets “Fauchelevent” wrong, here and in his other variants. Hugo calls it an aristocratic habit: a great gentleman of the old school did not trouble to remember the names of commoners.`,quote:`inattention to proper names was an aristocratic habit of his`},{id:`asking-for-the-hand`,term:`Asking for the hand`,kind:`custom`,chapter:345,text:`The formal request of marriage, made by the head of the young man's family to the girl's father. Under the Civil Code a son under twenty-five could not marry without the consent of his parents or, failing them, his grandparents, which is why the grandfather's word decides everything. He makes the request with full ceremony, title and all.`,quote:`I have the honor of asking you, on behalf of my grandson, Baron Marius Pontmercy, for the hand of Mademoiselle.`},{id:`saint-denis-du-saint-sacrement-and-saint-paul`,term:`Saint-Denis du Saint-Sacrement and Saint-Paul`,kind:`place`,chapter:345,text:`A Catholic married in his own parish church; Saint-Denis du Saint-Sacrement, in the Rue de Turenne, was the Gillenormands'. A dispensation from the parish priest let a wedding take place elsewhere, and the grandfather wants the grander Jesuit church of Saint-Paul-Saint-Louis on the Rue Saint-Antoine, near the old fountain named after Cardinal de Birague.`,quote:`Our parish is Saint-Denis du Saint Sacrament, but I will get a dispensation so that you can be married at Saint-Paul.`},{id:`coiffing-sainte-catherine`,term:`Coiffing Sainte-Catherine`,kind:`custom`,chapter:345,text:`Unmarried women of twenty-five or more were said to “coiffer Sainte-Catherine”, to put a cap on the saint's statue on her feast day, 25 November; it meant being an old maid. The grandfather's joke at his spinster daughter's expense, along with the Society of the Virgin, the parish association of unmarried women.`,quote:`There is a certain Sainte-Catherine whom I should always like to see uncoiffed.`},{id:`turris-eburnea`,term:`Turris Eburnea`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:345,text:`“Tower of ivory”, one of the titles of the Virgin Mary in the Litany of Loreto, sung at vespers. For the grandfather it stands for the chill devotions of spinsters.`,quote:`chanting Turris Eburnea`},{id:`alcippe-il-est-donc-vrai-dans-peu-tu-te-maries`,term:`“Alcippe, il est donc vrai, dans peu tu te maries”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:345,text:`“So, putting an end to your daydreams, Alcippe, it is true that you will soon be married.” The opening of Boileau's tenth Satire (1694), which goes on to a long attack on women and marriage; the old man quotes the cheerful start and leaves out the rest.`,quote:`Alcippe, il est donc vrai, dans peu tu te maries.`},{id:`a-greuze`,term:`A Greuze`,kind:`reference`,chapter:345,text:`Jean-Baptiste Greuze, eighteenth-century painter of sentimental scenes and of pretty, innocent-looking girls with soft eyes and parted lips. Praise in the language of M. Gillenormand's own youth.`,quote:`She’s a Greuze.`},{id:`annuity`,term:`Annuity`,kind:`money`,chapter:345,text:`A life annuity (rente viagère): an old man sold most of his capital in return for a fixed income until his death, after which nothing was left to inherit. That is why the grandfather fears Marius and Cosette will have “not a sou” and will be “pulling the devil by the tail”, the French phrase for scraping a living.`,quote:`More than half of what I possess is swallowed up in an annuity`},{id:`six-hundred-thousand-francs`,term:`Six hundred thousand francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:345,text:`A great fortune: invested in government stock at the usual five percent it would bring some thirty thousand francs a year, enough for a household with carriage and servants, at a time when a working man earned perhaps six or seven hundred a year. The banknotes, five hundred of a thousand francs and the rest of five hundred, were large denominations few people ever saw.`,quote:`Mademoiselle Euphrasie Fauchelevent possesses six hundred thousand francs.`},{id:`baroness-and-marquise`,term:`Baroness and Marquise`,kind:`title`,chapter:345,text:`In the old French hierarchy a marquis ranked well above a baron, and a barony from Napoleon counted for little with an old-regime nobleman. The grandfather's compliment is that Cosette looks born to a higher title than the one Marius can give her.`,quote:`She will only be a Baroness, which is a come down for her; she was born a Marquise.`},{id:`cherubino-and-rothschild`,term:`Cherubino and Rothschild`,kind:`reference`,chapter:345,text:`Chérubin is the lovesick page boy of Beaumarchais's Marriage of Figaro; the Rothschilds were the great banking family of Europe, James de Rothschild the richest man in Paris. Love, says the grandfather, has proved a better financier than the banks.`,quote:`Cherubino works better than Rothschild.`},{id:`chestnut-shavings`,term:`Chestnut shavings`,kind:`word`,chapter:346,text:`Shavings of chestnut wood, which resists rot and insects, used as packing to keep the box of banknotes dry in the ground for ten years.`,quote:`he had placed it in a coffer filled with chestnut shavings`},{id:`the-laundresses-boat`,term:`The laundresses' boat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:346,text:`Washerwomen of Paris worked on bateaux-lavoirs, big covered barges moored along the quays where they beat linen in the river. The body was found under one, between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf, downstream from where it fell.`,quote:`found drowned under a boat belonging to some laundresses, between the Pont au Change and the Pont-Neuf`},{id:`civil-status-and-the-acte-de-notoriete`,term:`Civil status and the acte de notoriété`,kind:`custom`,chapter:347,text:`To marry in France one had to produce one's état civil, the official record of birth and parentage from the town hall. A person without papers could replace it with an acte de notoriété, a sworn statement before a magistrate by witnesses who vouched for who she was. Valjean, an ex-mayor, knows the procedure; the nuns of Petit-Picpus supply the witnesses.`,quote:`An acte de notoriété was drawn up.`},{id:`guardian-and-supervising-guardian`,term:`Guardian and supervising guardian`,kind:`custom`,chapter:347,text:`Under the Civil Code an orphaned minor had a tuteur, who managed her person and property, and a subrogé tuteur, who watched over the guardian's accounts. Valjean, as “Fauchelevent”, takes the first role and M. Gillenormand the second; the legacy is to be handed over at her majority (twenty-one) or her marriage.`,quote:`he was appointed, under the name of Fauchelevent, as Cosette’s guardian, with M. Gillenormand as supervising guardian over him`},{id:`the-wedding-basket`,term:`The wedding basket`,kind:`custom`,chapter:347,text:`The corbeille de mariage: the gifts of clothes, lace, jewels and trinkets that the bridegroom's family presented to the bride before the wedding, often displayed to visitors. M. Gillenormand fills Cosette's from the drawers of three generations of women.`,quote:`M. Gillenormand was superintending the basket of wedding gifts`},{id:`binche-guipure-genoa-and-alencon-point`,term:`Binche guipure, Genoa and Alençon point`,kind:`word`,chapter:347,text:`Famous laces: guipure is a heavy lace without a net ground, made at Binche in Hainaut; Genoa and Alençon point were needle-laces, Alençon the “queen of French lace”. Old lace was handed down as family treasure, and it was coming back into fashion.`,quote:`a robe of Binche guipure which had descended to him from his own grandmother`},{id:`pekins-lampas-moires-gros-de-tours`,term:`Pekins, lampas, moires, gros de Tours`,kind:`word`,chapter:347,text:`Eighteenth-century silks from the grandfather's drawers: pekin, a striped silk; lampas, a heavily figured silk; moire, watered silk with a rippling sheen; gros de Tours, a ribbed silk from the looms of Tours; dauphines, a light reversible silk. Coromandel lacquer, from the Indian coast, is the black-and-gold lacquer of his chests.`,quote:`Pekins, damasks, lampas, painted moires, robes of shot gros de Tours`},{id:`moire-antique`,term:`“Moire antique!”`,kind:`word`,chapter:347,text:`Moire antique was a fashionable heavy watered silk. Marius says the men of the Revolution are like an “antique memory”; his grandfather, who will not argue politics now, hears only the name of a dress fabric and orders one.`,quote:`“Moire antique!” exclaimed the old gentleman.`},{id:`cato-and-phocion`,term:`Cato and Phocion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:347,text:`Models of stern republican virtue from antiquity: Cato of Utica, who killed himself rather than live under Caesar, and Phocion, the incorruptible Athenian general put to death by his city. Marius places the men of the Revolution beside them.`,quote:`like Cato and like Phocion`},{id:`the-clock-of-strasbourg`,term:`The clock of Strasbourg`,kind:`place`,chapter:347,text:`The astronomical clock in Strasbourg cathedral, a tower of dials, planets and moving figures that paraded at noon. Among the figures M. Gillenormand remembers is “Éponine and Sabinus”: the Gaulish rebel Sabinus and his faithful wife Eponina, who hid him in a cave for nine years after a failed rising against Rome. He prefers the clock's useless splendour to the plain Black Forest cuckoo clock.`,quote:`the Emperor Charles the Fifth, and Éponine, and Sabinus`},{id:`the-duc-de-rohan-in-a-tapecu`,term:`The Duc de Rohan in a tapecu`,kind:`history`,chapter:347,text:`Longchamp was the fashionable promenade where the great showed off their carriages in Holy Week. A tapecu (“bump-bottom”) was a small, plain, jolting cart. For the grandfather, a duke and peer with a string of titles driving there in one, in 1787, signalled the end of the old world.`,quote:`go to Longchamps in a tapecu`},{id:`cujas-and-gamacho`,term:`Cujas and Gamacho`,kind:`reference`,chapter:347,text:`Jacques Cujas, the great sixteenth-century jurist, stands for the marriage contract; Camacho is the rich farmer of Don Quixote whose wedding feast, with whole oxen roasting, is the most famous banquet in literature. First the notary, then the feast.`,quote:`As soon as Cujas had taken his departure, Gamacho entered.`},{id:`the-bride-s-garter`,term:`The bride's garter`,kind:`custom`,chapter:347,text:`An old wedding custom: at the feast the young men contrived to take the bride's garter, which was cut into pieces of ribbon and handed round as favours. The grandfather mourns its passing and makes it the cause of the Trojan War.`,quote:`I regret the bride’s garter.`},{id:`rigadoons-and-m-royer-collard`,term:`Rigadoons and M. Royer-Collard`,kind:`history`,chapter:347,text:`The rigadoon was a lively old dance. Pierre-Paul Royer-Collard was the grave philosopher-politician of the Doctrinaires, the earnest liberal party of the Restoration, famous for his solemnity. “Your rigadoons are doctrinarian”: even dancing has become serious.`,quote:`The ideal of an urchin of twenty when he marries, is to resemble M. Royer-Collard.`},{id:`from-rheims-to-the-pagoda-of-chanteloup`,term:`From Rheims to the pagoda of Chanteloup`,kind:`place`,chapter:347,text:`Rheims cathedral was where the kings of France were crowned; the pagoda of Chanteloup, near Amboise, is a Chinese-style folly tower built by the Duc de Choiseul. A wedding, says the grandfather, should run from the sacred to the fanciful.`,quote:`from the cathedral of Rheims to the pagoda of Chanteloup`},{id:`the-nuptials-of-amphitrite`,term:`The nuptials of Amphitrite`,kind:`reference`,chapter:347,text:`Amphitrite, sea-goddess and bride of Neptune, was painted riding in a shell-chariot drawn by sea-monsters, with Tritons blowing conch shells: the favourite wedding allegory of eighteenth-century ceilings. The couplet he quotes, about Triton's conch “ravishing whoever heard it”, is a jingle on rhyme alone.`,quote:`The nuptials of Amphitrite`},{id:`a-study-required-by-the-council-of-the-order`,term:`A study required by the council of the order`,kind:`custom`,chapter:347,text:`The Order of Barristers of Paris, governed by its council, required a member who wished to practise to have a proper study (cabinet) at his home. Marius had needed one to keep his place at the Bar; his grandfather's library becomes it.`,quote:`a study, it will be remembered, being required by the council of the order`},{id:`chaperon`,term:`Chaperon`,kind:`custom`,chapter:348,text:`A respectable young woman never went into society, still less to her fiancé's house, without an older relative or protector. Custom also had the suitor call on the bride's family, not the reverse, which is why the aunt grumbles that things are upside down.`,quote:`Every girl needs a chaperon.`},{id:`a-quittance-from-the-past`,term:`A quittance from the past`,kind:`word`,chapter:349,text:`A quittance is a receipt showing a debt paid in full. Marius wants to settle two debts of gratitude before he marries: his father's to the sergeant of Waterloo, and his own to the stranger who brought him home.`,quote:`he wished, before entering joyously into the future, to obtain a quittance from the past`},{id:`condemned-by-contumacy`,term:`Condemned by contumacy`,kind:`custom`,chapter:349,text:`A defendant who failed to appear could be tried and sentenced in his absence, par contumace. The sentence stood until he was caught, when he would be retried. The escaped ringleaders of the Gorbeau ambush got hard labour for life, the chief death; only two minor accomplices, present in the dock, got ten years.`,quote:`Thénardier, the head and leader, had been, through contumacy, likewise condemned to death.`},{id:`died-in-prison-pending-the-trial`,term:`Died in prison pending the trial`,kind:`custom`,chapter:349,text:`Accused persons could be held for many months in preventive detention before the Assizes sat, and prisons such as Saint-Lazare and La Force were unhealthy places. Death before trial ended the case against that defendant.`,quote:`Madame Thénardier had died in prison pending the trial.`},{id:`inquiries-at-the-prefecture`,term:`Inquiries at the Prefecture`,kind:`custom`,chapter:349,text:`The Prefecture of Police kept registers of every arrest and the agents' written reports. Since no report of an arrest at the sewer outlet on 6 June was ever filed, the Prefecture treats the coachman's story as a fable invented in the hope of a tip. Marius has to inquire discreetly, being himself a former insurgent.`,quote:`They had no knowledge of any arrest having been made on the 6th of June at the mouth of the Grand Sewer.`},{id:`the-honeymoon-departure`,term:`The honeymoon departure`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`Writing in the 1860s, Hugo mocks the newly imported English fashion of the couple leaving straight from church in a hired post-chaise for a wedding journey and a night at an inn, with the crack of the postilion's whip (the “clic-clacs”). In 1833 a French couple still married and spent the wedding night at home.`,quote:`France had not yet borrowed from England that supreme delicacy of carrying off one’s wife`},{id:`the-postilion-de-lonjumeau`,term:`The Postilion de Lonjumeau`,kind:`reference`,chapter:350,text:`Adolphe Adam's comic opera of 1836, whose hero is a dashing coach-driver in blue jacket, leather breeches, powdered hair and tall boots. Hugo's sneer: modern weddings need a postilion as well as the mayor and the priest.`,quote:`the Postilion de Lonjumeau`},{id:`old-shoes-after-the-bridal-carriage`,term:`Old shoes after the bridal carriage`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`The English custom of throwing old shoes and slippers after a newly married couple for luck. Hugo attaches a story to it about John Churchill, later Duke of Marlborough (the “Malbrouck” of the French nursery song), pelted on his wedding day by an angry aunt.`,quote:`a hail storm of slippers trodden down at heel and of worn-out shoes`},{id:`banns-mayoralty-and-church`,term:`Banns, mayoralty and church`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`Since 1792 French marriage had been a civil act: banns posted at the town hall, papers checked, and the marriage pronounced by the mayor in his tricolour scarf. A Catholic couple then went to church for the religious ceremony, which had no legal force by itself. Hence the delay until 16 February.`,quote:`the banns to publish, the papers to be drawn up, the mayoralty, and the church`},{id:`mardi-gras-of-1833`,term:`Mardi Gras of 1833`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`Shrove Tuesday (Mardi Gras, “fat Tuesday”), the last day of Carnival before the fast of Lent, fell on 16 February in 1833. It was the great day of masks in Paris: carriages and wagons of masqueraders paraded along the boulevards between the Madeleine and the Bastille, and crowds lined the way. A wedding on that day was unusual, and Aunt Gillenormand has scruples.`,quote:`it chanced that the 16th fell on Shrove Tuesday`},{id:`mariage-un-mardi-gras-n-aura-point-enfants-ingrats`,term:`“Mariage un Mardi gras n’aura point enfants ingrats”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:350,text:`“A marriage on Shrove Tuesday will have no ungrateful children.” A rhyming country proverb, which the grandfather produces at once to overrule his daughter.`,quote:`Mariage un Mardi gras`},{id:`community-of-property`,term:`Community of property`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`The default marriage regime of the Civil Code: the couple's goods and income formed one common fund managed by the husband. With no special marriage contract needed, “the papers had been simple”, and the money became, in law, Marius's to administer.`,quote:`As the marriage was taking place under the regime of community of property, the papers had been simple.`},{id:`the-signature`,term:`The signature`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`The guardian of a minor bride had to sign the marriage register at the town hall and the deed handing over her property. With his right hand bandaged in a sling, “M. Fauchelevent” cannot sign; M. Gillenormand, as supervising guardian, signs in his place.`,quote:`had prevented his signing`},{id:`merry-andrew-pantaloon-and-clown`,term:`Merry-Andrew, Pantaloon and Clown`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`Stock characters of the Carnival, from Italian comedy and the fair: Merry-Andrew is the buffoon, Pantaloon the old Venetian merchant, Harlequin and Columbine the lovers, Cassandre the duped old father, Janot the simpleton of the boulevard theatres. Parisians dressed as them in the streets, children too.`,quote:`Merry-Andrew, Pantaloon and Clown persisted`},{id:`citadines-tapissieres-carioles`,term:`Citadines, tapissières, carioles`,kind:`word`,chapter:350,text:`Kinds of Paris carriage: the citadine was a light hired cab, the tapissière an open-sided delivery van used for outings, the cariole a small country cart. On Shrove Tuesday they all joined the slow procession up and down the boulevards, kept in two files by the police.`,quote:`citadines, tapissières, carioles, cabriolets marching in order`},{id:`the-boeuf-gras`,term:`The Boeuf Gras`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`The “fat ox”: a prize ox, garlanded and ridden by a child dressed as Cupid, paraded through Paris by the butchers at Carnival with music and a procession before it went to slaughter. It had the right of way in the middle of the boulevard with the carriages of peers and ambassadors.`,quote:`notably that of the Boeuf Gras`},{id:`lord-seymour`,term:`Lord Seymour`,kind:`history`,chapter:350,text:`Lord Henry Seymour, a rich Anglo-French sportsman and founder of the Jockey Club in Paris, notorious for his Carnival extravagances. The crowd's nickname for him was “Milord l'Arsouille”, Lord Rowdy.`,quote:`Lord Seymour’s post-chaise, harassed by a nickname from the populace, passed with great noise`},{id:`the-pont-aux-choux`,term:`The Pont-aux-Choux`,kind:`place`,chapter:350,text:`The point on the boulevard near the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire where an old bridge over the Marais drain had once stood, at the end of the Rue du Pont-aux-Choux. It is where the two files of carriages stop side by side.`,quote:`At the top of the Pont-aux-Choux, there was a stoppage.`},{id:`the-chariot-of-thespis-and-the-hackney-coach-of-vade`,term:`The chariot of Thespis and the hackney-coach of Vadé`,kind:`reference`,chapter:350,text:`Thespis, the legendary first actor of Greece, was said to tour with his players in a cart; Vadé was the eighteenth-century poet of the fishmarket, inventor of the “poissard” style in the slang of the Halles. Hugo's pair: Greek tragedy began on a wagon, Paris low comedy rides in a cab.`,quote:`Greece stood in need of the chariot of Thespis, France stands in need of the hackney-coach of Vadé.`},{id:`loup`,term:`Loup`,kind:`word`,chapter:350,text:`French for “wolf”: a black half-mask of velvet or satin covering the eyes and upper face, the usual disguise at masked balls. The young fishwife wears one with her low-necked gown in the February rain.`,quote:`masked with a loup`},{id:`i-owe-my-fishwife-day-to-the-prefecture`,term:`“I owe my fishwife day to the prefecture”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`Hugo's charge is that the Prefecture of Police hired people, many from the shady margins of Paris, to ride in the masqueraders' wagons and keep up the Carnival. The girl is paid for the day, watched by inspectors, and cannot leave the cart without risking arrest.`,quote:`I owe my fishwife day to the prefecture.`},{id:`ash-wednesday`,term:`Ash Wednesday`,kind:`custom`,chapter:350,text:`The first day of Lent, when Carnival ends and the faithful receive ashes on the forehead. After Shrove Tuesday masks disappear from the streets, so a man who goes about only in disguise loses his cover.`,quote:`It’s Ash Wednesday.`},{id:`the-cadran-bleu-and-la-rapee`,term:`The Cadran-Bleu and la Rapée`,kind:`place`,chapter:350,text:`Where Paris weddings went to feast: the Cadran-Bleu was a famous restaurant on the Boulevard du Temple, and the Quai de la Rapée, up the river by the wine depots, was lined with taverns serving fried fish. The girl's guesses are the obvious ones.`,quote:`To the Cadran-Bleu.`},{id:`the-bridal-toilette`,term:`The bridal toilette`,kind:`custom`,chapter:351,text:`The white wedding dress was still fairly new as a custom in 1833, a fashion of the Restoration. Cosette's is the full set: white taffeta petticoat, the heirloom Binche guipure, a veil of English point lace, pearls, and the wreath of orange blossom, the emblem of a virgin bride.`,quote:`a wreath of orange flowers`},{id:`the-epoch-of-barras`,term:`The epoch of Barras`,kind:`history`,chapter:351,text:`Paul Barras, the most powerful of the five Directors who governed France from 1795 to 1799, presided over a society of gamblers, speculators and extravagantly dressed dandies. The grandfather's finery belongs to that age.`,quote:`all the elegances of the epoch of Barras`},{id:`the-pall-of-white-moire-and-the-suisse`,term:`The pall of white moire and the suisse`,kind:`custom`,chapter:351,text:`During the nuptial blessing a square of cloth, the poêle, was held over the kneeling couple. The suisse was the church's ceremonial officer, originally a Swiss guard, in a uniform with epaulets, a cocked hat and a halberd, who led processions and kept order, tapping the pavement as he walked.`,quote:`preceded by the suisse, with the epaulets of a colonel, tapping the pavement with his halberd`},{id:`thirty-thousand-livres-income`,term:`Thirty thousand livres income`,kind:`money`,chapter:351,text:`What the fortune yields at about five percent, which the old man still counts in livres (by then the same as francs). It was the income of a rich household with carriage and servants, many times what a government clerk earned in a year.`,quote:`with an income of thirty thousand livres`},{id:`i-am-madame-thou`,term:`“I am Madame Thou”`,kind:`word`,chapter:351,text:`Cosette plays on tu and vous. Lovers and married couples said tu, “thou”, to each other in private; in public it was still vous. Now that they are married, she is “Madame Tu”.`,quote:`I am Madame Thou.`},{id:`venetian-lustre-and-girandoles`,term:`Venetian lustre and girandoles`,kind:`word`,chapter:351,text:`A lustre is a chandelier; a Venetian one is of Murano glass, here with little coloured glass birds among the candles. Girandoles are branched candlesticks, often with mirror backs or hanging prisms. Hundreds of candles made the dining room “as brilliant as the daylight”.`,quote:`a Venetian lustre with flat plates`},{id:`estelle-and-nemorin`,term:`Estelle and Némorin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:351,text:`The shepherd lovers of Florian's pastoral romance Estelle et Némorin (1788), sweetness itself. The grandfather mocks the philosophers who would tell even such lovers to be moderate.`,quote:`Take care, Estelle, thou art too pretty! Have a care, Nemorin, thou art too handsome!`},{id:`the-sancy-diamond`,term:`The Sancy diamond`,kind:`history`,chapter:351,text:`A famous diamond of the French crown jewels, named after Nicolas de Harlay de Sancy, who owned it under Henri III and Henri IV. Hugo's French puns on its weight, “cent six” carats sounding like Sancy; the English “six hundred carats” misses the joke (the real stone weighs about fifty-five).`,quote:`Is the Sancy diamond called the Sancy because it belonged to Harley de Sancy, or because it weighs six hundred carats?`},{id:`trip-gallant-and-cholera-morbus-pourree-and-cachuca`,term:`Trip-gallant and cholera-morbus; pourrée and cachuca`,kind:`word`,chapter:351,text:`The grandfather says the young think they have changed the world only by renaming things. “Trousse-galant” was the old popular name for a deadly summer fever, and the modern one is cholera; the bourrée was an old country dance, and its successor the Spanish cachucha, the rage in Paris in the 1830s.`,quote:`your trip-gallant is called the cholera-morbus, and because your pourrée is called the cachuca`},{id:`celimene-and-alceste`,term:`Célimène and Alceste`,kind:`reference`,chapter:351,text:`The flirtatious young widow and her gruff lover in Molière's The Misanthrope. The grandfather makes the planet Venus the coquette and the stormy ocean the misanthrope who smiles in spite of himself.`,quote:`the Célimène of the ocean`},{id:`daphnis-and-chloe-philemon-and-baucis`,term:`Daphnis and Chloe, Philemon and Baucis`,kind:`reference`,chapter:351,text:`Two pairs of classical lovers: Daphnis and Chloe, the young shepherd sweethearts of Longus's Greek romance, and Philemon and Baucis, the old couple in Ovid who are granted their wish to die at the same moment and become two trees. Young love first, faithful old age afterwards.`,quote:`Be Daphnis and Chloe, while waiting to become Philemon and Baucis.`},{id:`the-great-prize-in-the-lottery`,term:`The great prize in the lottery`,kind:`custom`,chapter:351,text:`The state lottery, with its weekly draws and huge prizes, was a passion of the Parisian poor until it was abolished in 1836. The grandfather tells the couple they have drawn the winning number.`,quote:`You have filched the winning number in the lottery`},{id:`ventre-saint-gris-and-the-oaths-of-the-grandfather`,term:`Ventre-saint-gris and the oaths of the grandfather`,kind:`word`,chapter:351,text:`Ventre-saint-gris (“by the grey saint's belly”) was Henri IV's favourite oath, and the good-living king was remembered for food, wine and women. Saperlotte, sarpejou, ventregoulette and vertu-bamboche are old-fashioned mock oaths of the kind M. Gillenormand delights in.`,quote:`Ventre-saint-gris!`},{id:`goodman-days-of-yore`,term:`Goodman Days of Yore`,kind:`reference`,chapter:351,text:`Le Bonhomme Jadis, a one-act comedy by Henri Murger (1852) about a kindly old man who brings two young people together. Hugo says the play's old-fashioned spirit was present at the wedding in M. Gillenormand.`,quote:`Goodman Days of Yore might have been invited to it.`},{id:`basque-s-dress`,term:`Basque's dress`,kind:`custom`,chapter:352,text:`Black coat, knee-breeches, white stockings and white gloves: the full dress of a manservant waiting at a grand dinner, in the old style that M. Gillenormand keeps up in his household.`,quote:`Basque, in a black coat, knee-breeches, white stockings and white gloves`},{id:`fustian-and-ticking`,term:`Fustian and ticking`,kind:`word`,chapter:352,text:`Fustian is a thick, hard-wearing twilled cloth of cotton and linen, used for working clothes and children's garments; ticking is the striped cotton that covers mattresses and pillows. A bare pillow in its ticking, without a case, marks a bed stripped and left empty.`,quote:`the fustian bodice, which was very thick`},{id:`mourning-for-a-child`,term:`Mourning for a child`,kind:`custom`,chapter:352,text:`Even small children were dressed in black for a dead parent, for a year or more. The black dress, fichu, shoes and stockings Valjean brought to Montfermeil in 1823 were mourning clothes for her dead mother.`,quote:`to clothe herself in these mourning habiliments`},{id:`jacob-and-the-angel`,term:`Jacob and the angel`,kind:`reference`,chapter:353,text:`In Genesis 32 Jacob wrestles all night with a mysterious stranger, is lamed at the hip, and will not let go until he is blessed. Hugo's point: Jacob fought once; Valjean has fought his conscience many nights.`,quote:`Jacob struggled with the angel but one night.`},{id:`the-bottomless-tun`,term:`The bottomless tun`,kind:`reference`,chapter:353,text:`In Greek myth the Danaids, who murdered their husbands, are condemned in the underworld to fill a jar that has a hole in the bottom; Sisyphus forever rolls his stone uphill. Hugo's images of a conscience that asks for everything and is never full.`,quote:`Somewhere in the fog of the ancient hells, there is a tun like that.`},{id:`corrosive-sublimation`,term:`Corrosive sublimation`,kind:`word`,chapter:353,text:`A pun from chemistry: sublimation is turning a solid into vapour by heat, and “corrosive sublimate” was mercuric chloride, a deadly poison. Martyrdom, Hugo says, both raises a man up and eats him away.`,quote:`Martyrdom is sublimation, corrosive sublimation.`},{id:`the-throne-and-crown-of-red-hot-iron`,term:`The throne and crown of red-hot iron`,kind:`history`,chapter:353,text:`A torture of legend and history: the Hungarian peasant rebel György Dózsa, captured in 1514, was set on a red-hot iron throne and crowned with a red-hot crown in mockery of his claim to be king. Hugo extends it to sceptre, orb and a mantle of flame.`,quote:`one seats oneself on the throne of glowing iron`},{id:`bespattered-with-the-title`,term:`“Bespattered with the title”`,kind:`title`,chapter:354,text:`Servants of a titled house took pride in announcing “Monsieur le Baron”, Hugo says, because some of the title splashed onto them. The barony is Napoleon's grant to Colonel Pontmercy; the republican Marius uses it only because his father's last wish told him to, while the royalist grandfather now clings to it.`,quote:`they are what a philosopher would call, bespattered with the title`},{id:`the-voyages-of-cook-and-vancouver`,term:`The voyages of Cook and Vancouver`,kind:`reference`,chapter:354,text:`The narratives of Captain James Cook's Pacific voyages and of George Vancouver's survey of the north-west coast of America, popular reading in French translation. They are among the few books on M. Fauchelevent's shelf, books of distant journeys for a man who never travels.`,quote:`your Voyages of Captain Cook and the other—Vancouver’s`},{id:`whist`,term:`Whist`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`The English card game for four, ancestor of bridge, the fashionable evening game of elderly gentlemen in the salons of the 1830s. Marius offers it as the way to delight his grandfather.`,quote:`Do you play whist?`},{id:`the-days-when-i-am-at-the-courts`,term:`“The days when I am at the courts”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`Marius means to practise as a barrister at the Palais de Justice, pleading cases before the courts, now that he has a study at home and a family. A barrister of the Paris Bar went daily to the Palais in his gown.`,quote:`on the days when I am at the courts`},{id:`committing-a-forgery`,term:`Committing a forgery`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`To sign the marriage register or the guardianship deeds with a name not one's own was a forgery in a public document (faux en écriture publique), a serious crime that could also have made the marriage papers open to challenge. Valjean's bandaged thumb was invented so that only true names went into the record.`,quote:`I invented this injury in order that I might not commit a forgery, that I might not introduce a flaw into the marriage documents`},{id:`our-oath-to-others-may-not-be-received-in-law`,term:`“Our oath to others may not be received in law”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`A man sentenced to hard labour suffered civil degradation: among other losses of rights, he could never again give sworn testimony in a court, only “information”. Valjean asks Marius to believe a word that the law would not accept.`,quote:`although our oath to others may not be received in law`},{id:`condemned-for-life-for-a-second-offence`,term:`Condemned for life for a second offence`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`The penal code punished repeat offenders far more heavily. A released convict who broke his ban (left the town assigned to him with his yellow passport) and stole again could be sent back to the galleys for life, which is what Valjean now tells Marius he is under.`,quote:`Then, I was condemned for life for theft, for a second offence. At the present moment, I have broken my ban.`},{id:`attenuating-circumstance`,term:`Attenuating circumstance`,kind:`custom`,chapter:354,text:`A phrase from the criminal courts: the reform of the penal code in 1832 allowed juries to find “extenuating circumstances” and so lower a sentence. Valjean speaks of his care for Cosette in the language of a man in the dock.`,quote:`Register this attenuating circumstance.`},{id:`the-diligences-of-the-rue-bouloy`,term:`The diligences of the Rue Bouloy`,kind:`place`,chapter:354,text:`The Rue du Bouloi, near the Palais-Royal, held the offices of the great stage-coach companies, from which the diligences left for the provinces. To go away for good, Valjean need only walk there.`,quote:`there are diligences in the Rue Bouloy`},{id:`sympathetic-ink`,term:`Sympathetic ink`,kind:`word`,chapter:355,text:`Invisible ink, made from lemon juice, milk or certain chemicals, whose writing appears only when the paper is warmed. Hugo's image for the memory of the barricade surfacing in Marius under the heat of emotion.`,quote:`like sympathetic ink at the application of heat`},{id:`the-corsican-vendetta`,term:`The Corsican vendetta`,kind:`custom`,chapter:355,text:`The blood feud of Corsica, where a family's honour required killing whoever had killed or wronged one of its own, generation after generation. Mérimée's story Colomba (1840) made it famous in France. Marius explains to himself what he heard behind the Mondétour barricade as a convict's vengeance of this kind.`,quote:`The Corsican vendetta has penetrated to certain lower strata and has become the law there`},{id:`law-and-right-social-damnation`,term:`Law and right; social damnation`,kind:`history`,chapter:355,text:`French distinguishes la loi, the written statute, from le droit, right or justice. In the penal thinking of the day a convict suffered civil degradation and, for life sentences, “civil death”: he lost his legal existence, could not inherit, make a will or validly marry. Marius, a democrat in politics, still accepts this as civilisation.`,quote:`He had not yet come to distinguish between that which is written by man and that which is written by God, between law and right.`},{id:`vade-retro`,term:`Vade retro`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:355,text:`Latin, “Get thee behind me”, from Christ's rebuke in the Gospels (“Vade retro, Satana”) and the formula of exorcism. Marius's final inward gesture towards the convict.`,quote:`Vade retro.`},{id:`the-pope-s-head-brush`,term:`The pope's head brush`,kind:`word`,chapter:356,text:`A round brush on a long pole for sweeping cobwebs from ceilings and high corners; in French a tête-de-loup, “wolf's head”, rendered here as “pope's head”. The room Valjean chooses is one that never sees it.`,quote:`the pope’s head brush`},{id:`madame-and-you`,term:`“Madame” and “you”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:356,text:`A father said tu, “thou”, to his daughter, and she called him “father”. By saying vous and “Madame”, and asking to be called “Monsieur Jean”, Valjean speaks to Cosette as a servant or a stranger would speak to a married lady. When he slips back into “thou”, she hears it at once.`,quote:`“You know, madame, that I am peculiar, I have my freaks.”`},{id:`rosewood-toilet-table`,term:`Rosewood toilet table`,kind:`word`,chapter:356,text:`A dressing table with mirror and drawers, veneered in the dark, red-streaked tropical wood fashionable under Charles X and Louis-Philippe, often with pale inlay. Valjean, offered love, answers with shopping advice.`,quote:`A very neat toilet table in the reigning style. What you call rosewood, I think.`},{id:`the-marquis-de-canaples`,term:`The Marquis de Canaples`,kind:`reference`,chapter:357,text:`A nobleman of anecdote whose eccentricity, as the grandfather tells it, was to buy a palace and live in its garret. For M. Gillenormand such “originals” are common, and M. Fauchelevent's insistence on the cellar room is one more harmless whim.`,quote:`The Marquis de Canaples was still worse. He bought a palace that he might lodge in the garret.`},{id:`one-s-candle-at-the-porter-s-lodge`,term:`One's candle at the porter's lodge`,kind:`custom`,chapter:357,text:`Tenants of a Paris house left their bedroom candlestick and key with the porter and collected them on coming home at night to light their way up the stairs. A rich man who does so, and comes in by the side door, is living like a poor lodger: one of Hugo's “fugitive folds on the surface”.`,quote:`having one’s key in one’s pocket, and one’s candle at the porter’s lodge, however rich one may be`},{id:`arguing-a-case-here-and-there`,term:`Arguing a case here and there`,kind:`custom`,chapter:357,text:`Marius has begun to practise at the Bar, taking a brief now and then. A young barrister without connections pleaded small cases, or was assigned the defence of the poor; with a fortune behind him he need not chase work.`,quote:`Marius argued a case here and there`},{id:`held-on-a-lease`,term:`Held on a lease`,kind:`custom`,chapter:358,text:`The house in the Rue Plumet had been rented, not bought, on a lease that still ran, in Cosette's name. So the couple can still go back to its garden.`,quote:`The house in the Rue Plumet being held on a lease, still belonged to Cosette.`},{id:`a-coupe-a-maid-and-a-box-at-the-theatre`,term:`A coupé, a maid and a box at the theatre`,kind:`money`,chapter:358,text:`The marks of a rich young household in 1833: a coupé (a closed two-seat carriage) with horses and coachman, a lady's maid, a house of one's own and a box for the season at the Opéra or the Théâtre-Italien. Valjean, who has given them a fortune, sees them walking and taking hackney cabs and living with the grandfather on one servant.`,quote:`Why do you not have a carriage of your own?`},{id:`twenty-seven-and-three`,term:`Twenty-seven and three`,kind:`money`,chapter:358,text:`Marius's sum: twenty-seven thousand livres a year from Cosette's fortune and three thousand from his grandfather's allowance. Three thousand francs was a modest middle-class income, enough for a small flat and a maid but no carriage. His question whether she could live on it alone tells Valjean what Marius thinks of the money.`,quote:`Twenty-seven that you own, and three that my grandfather gives me.`},{id:`gillyflowers-and-snapdragons`,term:`Gillyflowers and snapdragons`,kind:`word`,chapter:358,text:`Wallflowers (giroflées) and snapdragons, the plants that seed themselves in the cracks of old walls and bloom in early spring. Hugo fills the old garden walls of the Marais with them in April.`,quote:`a jewelled garniture of gillyflowers spread over the ancient walls`},{id:`from-the-rue-de-l-homme-arme-to-the-rue-des-filles-du-calvaire`,term:`From the Rue de l’Homme Armé to the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire`,kind:`place`,chapter:359,text:`A walk of a quarter of an hour north through the Marais: past the Blancs-Manteaux, up the Rue Culture-Sainte-Catherine, left at the Rue de l’Écharpe into the long Rue Saint-Louis, which runs to the corner of the Filles-du-Calvaire. Hugo names each street as the walk shrinks: the Rue Pavée, the Rue des Trois-Pavillons, and at last no farther than the Blancs-Manteaux.`,quote:`passed in front of the Blancs Manteaux, gained the Rue Culture-Sainte-Catherine`},{id:`he-is-an-innocent`,term:`“He is an innocent”`,kind:`word`,chapter:359,text:`In the old sense, a simpleton or harmless half-wit, “God's innocent”. The women of the quarter take the old man who stops at a corner and weeps for one, and the children laugh at him.`,quote:`The good women of the quarter said: “He is an innocent.”`},{id:`the-portress`,term:`The portress`,kind:`custom`,chapter:361,text:`The porter's wife, who in many Paris houses cooked and cleaned for single lodgers for a few sous, besides keeping the door and the stairs. She is the only person Valjean sees. Her cabbage and potatoes with bacon, and her pride in her “ladyfinger” potatoes (a long, waxy variety), are poor people's fare.`,quote:`His portress, who prepared his scanty repasts, a few cabbages or potatoes with bacon`},{id:`or-at-trinity-day`,term:`“Or at Trinity day”`,kind:`word`,chapter:361,text:`A French way of saying “never”, from the nursery song of Malbrough, who “will come back at Easter, or at Trinity”, and never does. The portress means she does not believe his promise to eat tomorrow.`,quote:`“Or at Trinity day. Why not today?`},{id:`that-gibbet-is-always-good-to-look-at`,term:`“That gibbet is always good to look at”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:361,text:`Hugo's name for the crucifix: the cross was the gallows of the Roman world, and Christ died on it between two thieves. A man condemned by the law hangs one opposite his bed.`,quote:`That gibbet is always good to look at.`},{id:`candles-in-broad-daylight`,term:`Candles in broad daylight`,kind:`custom`,chapter:362,text:`In a house of mourning, candles were kept burning beside a body lying in its room, day and night, until the burial. Hugo points out the custom himself: lighting the Bishop's candlesticks in summer daylight is a deathbed sign.`,quote:`In the same way candles are to be seen lighted in broad daylight in chambers where there is a corpse.`},{id:`writing-with-the-back-of-the-pen`,term:`Writing with the back of the pen`,kind:`custom`,chapter:362,text:`A quill pen left unused dried and its split nib curled; the ink in the pot dried to a crust. With no strength to mend the quill with a penknife, Valjean thins the ink with water and writes with the back of the nib.`,quote:`he was compelled to write with the back of the pen`},{id:`gum-shellac-and-the-jet-wax`,term:`Gum shellac and the jet wax`,kind:`lore`,chapter:362,text:`The secret of the fortune, set down in figures: imitation jet and black-glass beads were fixed to their metal settings with a wax that had been made of resin and lampblack at four livres the pound. Replacing it with shellac (a resin from an Indian insect) and turpentine at thirty sous, a seventh of the price and better, is the invention that made the black-bead trade of Montreuil-sur-Mer rich.`,quote:`I invented a way of making it with gum shellac and turpentine.`},{id:`spirits-of-wine`,term:`Spirits of wine`,kind:`word`,chapter:362,text:`Distilled alcohol, burned in a small lamp to give a clean, steady flame. The bead-maker needs one to soften the wax as he works at his tiny anvil.`,quote:`a lamp burning spirits of wine to soften the wax`},{id:`baron-thenard-member-of-the-institute`,term:`Baron Thénard, member of the Institute`,kind:`history`,chapter:363,text:`Louis-Jacques Thénard, the eminent chemist (discoverer of hydrogen peroxide and of “Thénard's blue”), made a baron by Charles X and a member of the Académie des Sciences, part of the Institut de France. The letter-writer borrows the famous name, his own “a trifle abridged”.`,quote:`I might have been baron Thénard, member of the Institute`},{id:`at-his-hotel`,term:`“At his hotel”`,kind:`word`,chapter:363,text:`Hôtel in French also means a private town mansion, as in hôtel particulier. Addressing a letter to a baron “at his hotel” was the grand style; the spelling “Pommerci” and the coarse paper give the writer away.`,quote:`“To Monsieur, Monsieur le Baron Pommerci. At his hotel.”`},{id:`the-changer`,term:`The Changer`,kind:`lore`,chapter:363,text:`Hugo's account of an old-clothes man near the Arsenal who hired out respectable costumes to thieves at thirty sous a day: a magistrate's suit, a priest's, a banker's, a retired officer's, and the black “ex-ambassador” outfit with wig, green spectacles, watch-seals and two little quills to change the shape of the nose. The clothes always came back, and never fitted.`,quote:`This costumer was called “the Changer;”`},{id:`pitt-and-castelcicala`,term:`Pitt and Castelcicala`,kind:`reference`,chapter:363,text:`William Pitt the Younger, the famously thin British prime minister, and the Prince of Castelcicala, the Neapolitan ambassador in Paris, apparently notable for his bulk. The statesman's suit fits neither, and certainly not the man wearing it.`,quote:`too large for Pitt and too small for Castelcicala`},{id:`princesse-bagration-vicomte-dambray-chateaubriand`,term:`Princesse Bagration, Vicomte Dambray, Chateaubriand`,kind:`history`,chapter:363,text:`Grand names to drop: Princess Bagration, widow of a Russian general, kept a famous salon in Paris; Dambray had been Chancellor of France under the Restoration; Chateaubriand was the greatest writer and royalist statesman of the age. The visitor claims to have met Marius in all their drawing-rooms.`,quote:`at the house of Madame la Princesse Bagration and in the drawing-rooms of his Lordship the Vicomte Dambray`},{id:`la-joya`,term:`La Joya`,kind:`place`,chapter:363,text:`The visitor's tale of a fortified village of adobe near Panama, a single terraced block entered by ladders, in gold country. Panama was then a province of New Granada (Colombia), a crossing-place between the oceans. It is the destination he names for the money he wants.`,quote:`There is in America, in a district near Panama, a village called la Joya.`},{id:`limitation-before-the-law`,term:`Limitation before the law`,kind:`custom`,chapter:363,text:`Prescription: under French criminal law, prosecution for a crime lapsed after ten years, and an unexecuted sentence after twenty, so that very old deeds could no longer be punished. The visitor stresses that his “secret” concerns crimes too recent to be covered.`,quote:`deeds of the past which have lapsed, which can be effaced by limitation before the law and by repentance before God`},{id:`an-honest-rustler`,term:`“An honest rustler”`,kind:`word`,chapter:363,text:`The visitor's slang for the five-hundred-franc banknote flung in his face, which rustles as it is handled; the joke is that it is the only honest thing in the room.`,quote:`“An honest rustler.”`},{id:`a-protested-bill-and-the-debtors-prison`,term:`A protested bill and the debtors' prison`,kind:`money`,chapter:363,text:`A letter of change (bill of exchange) was a written order to pay; when the debtor failed to pay, the creditor had it formally “protested” before a notary, and could have him jailed in the debtors' prison, at Paris the prison of Clichy. Marius sees his father's dying wish as a bill drawn on him that he has not yet honoured.`,quote:`the letter of change drawn from the depths of the tomb by his father upon him, Marius, had been protested up to that day`},{id:`the-moniteur-notice-of-15-june-1832`,term:`The Moniteur notice of 15 June 1832`,kind:`history`,chapter:363,text:`The Moniteur universel was the official government newspaper; communications from the Prefecture of Police were printed in it as fact. That is why Marius cannot doubt what it reports of the inspector's death and of the verbal report he made to the Prefect.`,quote:`the note printed in the Moniteur had been an administrative communication from the Prefecture of Police`},{id:`a-draft-on-new-york`,term:`A draft on New York`,kind:`money`,chapter:363,text:`A banker's draft, a written order payable by a correspondent bank in New York. Paying the twenty thousand francs that way meant the money could be collected only after the man had sailed: Marius's way of making sure he goes.`,quote:`furnished with a draft on New York for twenty thousand francs`},{id:`slave-dealer-in-america`,term:`Slave-dealer in America`,kind:`history`,chapter:363,text:`The Atlantic slave trade had been outlawed by Britain and the United States, but slavery remained legal in the American South, in Brazil and in Cuba until the 1860s and after, and smuggling and the internal trade went on. Hugo, a fierce abolitionist, gives that trade to his worst villain.`,quote:`With Marius’ money, Thénardier set up as a slave-dealer.`},{id:`berlin-jewellery-and-a-gross`,term:`Berlin jewellery and a gross`,kind:`money`,chapter:364,text:`Berlin iron jewellery, delicate cast-iron necklaces and bracelets, was famous after Prussian women gave their gold for the war of 1813 and wore iron instead. A gross is twelve dozen, 144 pieces. The dying man's figures (buckles costing ten francs the gross and selling for sixty) are his proof that the fortune was honestly made.`,quote:`We rivalled what is called Berlin jewellery.`},{id:`would-you-like-a-priest`,term:`“Would you like a priest?”`,kind:`custom`,chapter:364,text:`A dying Catholic was expected to receive the last sacraments, confession and extreme unction, from a priest, and the portress offers to fetch one as any neighbour would. The answer points elsewhere.`,quote:`“Would you like a priest?”`},{id:`a-stone-with-no-name`,term:`A stone with no name`,kind:`custom`,chapter:364,text:`Graves of the comfortable bore inscribed names and dates; the poor went into the common trench or under an unmarked stone. To ask for the first plot of earth found, and no name, is to ask to be buried as a poor man.`,quote:`No name on the stone.`},{id:`the-common-grave-at-pere-lachaise`,term:`The common grave at Père-Lachaise`,kind:`place`,chapter:365,text:`Père-Lachaise, the great cemetery on the eastern hill of Paris, had its fashionable quarter of chapels and monuments and, at its edges, the fosse commune, the trenches where the poor were buried free and their bones later cleared. The plain stone lies near it, under a yew, off any path.`,quote:`in the vicinity of the common grave`},{id:`il-dort-quoique-le-sort-fut-pour-lui-bien-etrange`,term:`“Il dort. Quoique le sort fût pour lui bien étrange”`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:365,text:`The four pencilled lines: “He sleeps. Although fate was very strange for him, he lived. He died when he had his angel no more. It came about simply, of itself, as night falls when day goes.” Alexandrines, the classic verse of French epitaphs, written by an unknown hand and already washed away.`,quote:`Il dort. Quoique le sort fût pour lui bien étrange`}],insights:[{id:`1-1`,chapter:1,kind:`craft`,title:`A slow, sideways opening`,text:`Hugo opens a novel called Les Misérables with a contented old bishop and admits that his first details have 'no connection whatever with the real substance' of the story. He is setting a moral standard first. Book One is a measuring stick, held up before anyone else arrives.`,themes:[]},{id:`1-2`,chapter:1,kind:`context`,title:`A good man and a great man`,text:`The meeting with Napoleon is set in 1804, the year he crowned himself emperor. Myriel's reply turns the hierarchy upside down: the Emperor is merely great, the priest is good, and each may profit from the other. Goodness against greatness: the novel's first scene sets the two side by side.`,themes:[]},{id:`1-3`,chapter:1,kind:`question`,title:`The missing years`,text:`Myriel married, lived in society, lost his wife in exile and came back a priest. Hugo refuses to explain what changed him. Does the gap make him more believable, or less?`,themes:[]},{id:`2-1`,chapter:2,kind:`theme`,title:`Counting beds`,text:`The Bishop does arithmetic out loud: twenty-six patients in a few cramped rooms, three people in a palace. His charity starts with numbers, not feelings. He treats the mismatch as a simple 'mistake' to be corrected, which makes the exchange sound obvious rather than heroic.`,themes:[]},{id:`2-2`,chapter:2,kind:`motif`,title:`Monseigneur Welcome`,text:`The poor choose his name for him, picking out of his baptismal names the one that means something. 'Bienvenu makes up for the Monseigneur,' he says. A title given from above matters less to him than a name given from below.`,themes:[]},{id:`2-3`,chapter:2,kind:`craft`,title:`The budget as portrait`,text:`Hugo prints the Bishop's household budget line by line, seminaries, prisons, debtors, foundlings, with one thousand livres left for himself. A list of expenses becomes a character sketch. You learn what he values by seeing where the money goes.`,themes:[]},{id:`3-1`,chapter:3,kind:`craft`,title:`Preaching by example`,text:`Instead of scolding a stingy canton, the Bishop tells it about a generous one nearby. Each village hears about its neighbour's virtue. Hugo shows a man who persuades by stories rather than threats, and the method suits his gentleness.`,themes:[]},{id:`3-2`,chapter:3,kind:`context`,title:`A diocese of mountains`,text:`Digne lies in the Alps of Haute-Provence, poor, steep and nearly roadless in 1815. The Bishop's donkey is not a picturesque detail. It is the only practical way to reach the hundreds of chapels in his charge.`,themes:[]},{id:`4-1`,chapter:4,kind:`theme`,title:`The road the fault has travelled`,text:`'Examine the road over which the fault has passed.' The Bishop judges a wrong by how a person was brought to it. If the weak and the poor go wrong, he says, the guilt lies with the strong who left them in darkness. Hugo offers it as a measure for every judgement a reader is asked to make.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`4-2`,chapter:4,kind:`motif`,title:`The scaffold`,text:`The guillotine shakes the serene Bishop more than anything else in these pages. He mutters that death belongs to God alone. Hugo campaigned all his life against the death penalty, and here he lets his gentlest character feel its horror at close range.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`4-3`,chapter:4,kind:`craft`,title:`Wit with a point`,text:`'Give them to me,' he says to the marquis who claims to have poor of his own. The Bishop's jokes are short and always land on money and the poor. Hugo keeps him from being a plaster saint by giving him a quick, teasing tongue.`,themes:[]},{id:`5-1`,chapter:5,kind:`motif`,title:`The purple cloak`,text:`He wears a warm cloak even in summer so that nobody sees how worn his cassocks are. It is a small vanity in the service of humility. The detail makes his poverty private, not a show.`,themes:[]},{id:`5-2`,chapter:5,kind:`theme`,title:`Compassion as God's name`,text:`Among the names Scripture gives God, the Bishop's margin note picks Compassion as the most beautiful. In a chapter about rye bread and boiled vegetables, this is the one line of theology, and it explains everything around it.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`6-1`,chapter:6,kind:`craft`,title:`An inventory`,text:`Hugo walks through the house room by room and stops to count the silver: six forks and spoons, a soup ladle, two massive candlesticks. You can feel the narrator wanting you to know exactly what is in that cupboard, and that its key stays in the lock.`,themes:[]},{id:`6-2`,chapter:6,kind:`motif`,title:`A door with no bolt`,text:`The Bishop has the bolts taken off his street door. A latch anyone can lift is all that closes it. 'One should not ask the name of a man who asks for shelter.' The open door is his whole philosophy made into carpentry.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`6-3`,chapter:6,kind:`theme`,title:`Useful flowers`,text:`He defends his one flower bed as being as useful as the vegetables, 'more so, perhaps'. For a man who gives nearly everything away, this is a quiet claim that beauty is also a need of the soul.`,themes:[]},{id:`7-1`,chapter:7,kind:`theme`,title:`No precaution against one's fellow man`,text:`The Bishop crosses bandit country without an escort because shepherds and brigands alike need to hear of God. The real robbers, he says, are prejudices and the real murderers are vices. He treats danger as something that lives inside people, not on the road.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`7-2`,chapter:7,kind:`craft`,title:`God, or the Devil?`,text:`The chest of stolen vestments arrives with no explanation but a note. The curé wavers; the Bishop says 'God!' Hugo leaves the brigand's motive unexplained and even refuses to say where the treasure went, which keeps the episode a parable instead of a neat moral.`,themes:[]},{id:`8-1`,chapter:8,kind:`theme`,title:`A philosophy for the rich`,text:`The senator's materialism, enjoy everything and fear nothing after death, is witty and comfortable. The Bishop's answer is to pretend to admire it and point out that only the rich can afford it. Unbelief, here, is a luxury good, and religion is left to the poor like a cheap substitute dish.`,themes:[]},{id:`8-2`,chapter:8,kind:`craft`,title:`Letting the opponent talk`,text:`Hugo gives the senator a long, lively speech and the Bishop a short reply. He trusts the reader to hear the hollowness in the senator's confidence. Irony does the arguing.`,themes:[]},{id:`9-1`,chapter:9,kind:`craft`,title:`A second point of view`,text:`For the first time we see the Bishop through someone else's eyes: his sister's chatty letter, full of frescoes, genealogy and fear. Her worry makes his calm look braver. A saint described by a nervous sister is easier to believe than one described by a narrator.`,themes:[]},{id:`9-2`,chapter:9,kind:`theme`,title:`Two shadows`,text:`Hugo says the women never interfere when the Bishop acts as a bishop; they simply 'confide him to God'. Their love takes the form of not standing in his way. It is a quiet model of devotion that asks for nothing back.`,themes:[]},{id:`10-1`,chapter:10,kind:`context`,title:`The Convention and 1793`,text:`The Convention was the assembly that abolished the monarchy and tried Louis XVI in 1792–93. Its members were hated by royalists after 1815. G— did not vote for the king's death, but Digne treats him as nearly a regicide, an outcast in his own valley.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`10-2`,chapter:10,kind:`theme`,title:`The bishop who kneels`,text:`The Bishop arrives cold and judging and ends on his knees asking the dying man's blessing. The argument over the Revolution is never settled point by point. What changes is the Bishop himself, who discovers a goodness he did not expect in a man he was taught to despise.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`10-3`,chapter:10,kind:`craft`,title:`An argument with two strong sides`,text:`Hugo lets G— land real blows: the crimes of the monarchy against the crimes of the Terror, the child Louis XVII against Cartouche's brother. He does not make the revolutionary a straw man. The chapter's title calls him an 'unknown light'.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`11-1`,chapter:11,kind:`theme`,title:`The narrator disagrees`,text:`Hugo openly criticises his own saint: it would have been better if the Bishop had not been a royalist, and to applaud a great man's fall is unworthy. Admitting a flaw makes the portrait more trustworthy and shows that Hugo has his own politics.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`11-2`,chapter:11,kind:`context`,title:`Napoleon's shadow`,text:`The Hundred Days were Napoleon's brief return from Elba in 1815, ended at Waterloo. The old guardsman who refuses a cross without the Emperor's face shows how fiercely ordinary men still loved him. The Bishop disapproves of the politics and finds the man a job anyway.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`12-1`,chapter:12,kind:`theme`,title:`The worship of success`,text:`Hugo attacks the way the world mistakes luck for merit, calling the rich great and the lucky clever. People confuse the stars of heaven with the stars that ducks' feet make in a puddle. The Bishop is lonely because goodness offers no career.`,themes:[]},{id:`12-2`,chapter:12,kind:`craft`,title:`Incurable poverty`,text:`Ambitious young priests avoid the Bishop as if his poverty were catching. Hugo's sarcasm is sharp: a saint is a 'dangerous neighbour'. The joke shows how a church can drift from what it preaches.`,themes:[]},{id:`13-1`,chapter:13,kind:`motif`,title:`The garden at night`,text:`After the women go to bed, the Bishop sits under his stunted fruit trees and looks at the stars, dazzled by God rather than studying him. 'A little garden to walk in, and immensity to dream in.' Hugo pairs the smallest space with the largest one.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`13-2`,chapter:13,kind:`theme`,title:`Love spilling over`,text:`'Poor beast! It is not its fault!' he says of an ugly spider, and he sprains an ankle avoiding an ant. Hugo describes his faith as an excess of love. It reaches even creatures nobody else would pity.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`13-3`,chapter:13,kind:`craft`,title:`Gentleness as something earned`,text:`Hints that the young Myriel was passionate, even violent, make his calm a conquest rather than a temperament. Hugo compares it to a hollow worn by dripping water. Goodness, in this book, can be made slowly.`,themes:[]},{id:`14-1`,chapter:14,kind:`theme`,title:`Dressing the wound`,text:`The universe looks to the Bishop like an immense illness. He does not try to solve it. He tries to bandage it. 'Love each other' is his whole doctrine. Hugo is clear that his hero is not a thinker but a practitioner.`,themes:[]},{id:`14-2`,chapter:14,kind:`craft`,title:`A coda before the story`,text:`This short chapter closes Book One by guarding against a misreading: the Bishop is no mystic or system-builder. Fourteen chapters of portrait end here, and the narrative is about to begin. Hugo wants the Bishop's measure fixed before the trial comes.`,themes:[]},{id:`15-1`,chapter:15,kind:`craft`,title:`A man seen from outside`,text:`For a whole chapter the traveller is only described: his shaved head, his huge stick, his purse. Hugo withholds his inner life and shows him the way Digne sees him. We learn his name only when an innkeeper whispers it as an accusation.`,themes:[]},{id:`15-2`,chapter:15,kind:`theme`,title:`Every door`,text:`Two inns, the prison, a peasant's cottage, even a dog's kennel: the rejections pile up until 'I am not even a dog!' Hugo makes society's verdict physical. Paying money is not enough. A record follows him from door to door.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`15-3`,chapter:15,kind:`motif`,title:`Knock there`,text:`The old lady's last words point to the one house that has no bolt. The reader already knows that door. Hugo has spent fourteen chapters preparing it, and the rejected man has not.`,themes:[]},{id:`16-1`,chapter:16,kind:`craft`,title:`Two scenes at once`,text:`While Valjean wanders the streets, Madame Magloire relays the gossip about him to the Bishop. The reader knows exactly who 'the vagabond' is, and the household does not. The suspense comes from our knowledge, not theirs.`,themes:[]},{id:`16-2`,chapter:16,kind:`motif`,title:`Come in`,text:`She asks for new locks because Monseigneur says 'come in' to anybody. The knock comes, and he says it again. The chapter is built so that the Bishop's principle is tested at the very moment it is questioned.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`17-1`,chapter:17,kind:`theme`,title:`A name thrown like a challenge`,text:`Valjean announces himself before anyone asks: his name, his sentence, his yellow passport. He expects rejection and gets ahead of it. The Bishop answers that he already knew his name: 'my brother'.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`17-2`,chapter:17,kind:`motif`,title:`Candlesticks and silver`,text:`The Bishop remarks that the lamp gives poor light and sends for the two silver candlesticks. Then the full silver is laid, as for any guest. Light and silver come out together, in honour of a convict.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`17-3`,chapter:17,kind:`craft`,title:`'Sir'`,text:`What stuns Valjean most is a word: 'sir'. 'You call me sir!' After nineteen years of numbers and insults, courtesy is the shock. Hugo shows dignity being given back in the smallest unit of speech.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`18-1`,chapter:18,kind:`craft`,title:`Tact as a form of love`,text:`The Bishop talks about Pontarlier's cheese co-operatives and never once mentions the galleys. Baptistine notices that he stops short of a phrase that might wound. Hugo shows charity working through what is left unsaid.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`18-2`,chapter:18,kind:`context`,title:`The fruitières`,text:`The cheese-dairies of the Jura were real village co-operatives where poor peasants pooled their milk. The Bishop dwells on them because they offer a man without a past an honest trade and a community. It is advice disguised as small talk.`,themes:[]},{id:`19-1`,chapter:19,kind:`theme`,title:`That is God's concern`,text:`'How do you know I am not an assassin?' The Bishop's answer refuses fear without denying risk. He has chosen to trust, and he leaves the outcome to God. Hugo makes the line short so that its calm stands out against the convict's laugh.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`19-2`,chapter:19,kind:`motif`,title:`The cupboard by the bed`,text:`Valjean is led through the Bishop's own bedroom just as Madame Magloire is locking the silver into the cupboard at the head of the bed. Hugo makes sure the guest sees where it goes. The reader feels the tension the household does not.`,themes:[]},{id:`20-1`,chapter:20,kind:`theme`,title:`A loaf of bread`,text:`Nineteen years 'for a pane of glass and a loaf of bread'. Hugo gives the numbers coldly: five years for the theft, fourteen more for escapes. The punishment grows on itself, and hunger, the real cause, appears nowhere in the sentence.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`20-2`,chapter:20,kind:`motif`,title:`Seven heads`,text:`At Bicêtre Valjean can only say he was a tree-pruner of Faverolles and raises his hand seven times as if touching seven children's heads. The gesture says more than a speech. The family then disappears from his life entirely.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`20-3`,chapter:20,kind:`theme`,title:`A man becomes a number`,text:`At Toulon he becomes 24,601. Hugo strips Valjean's identity layer by layer: family, place, name. The chapter ends with a question about what happened inside that soul.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`21-1`,chapter:21,kind:`theme`,title:`A court inside one man`,text:`Valjean puts himself on trial and finds himself guilty, then puts society on trial and finds it more guilty: crushed first by its lack of foresight, then by its pitiless foresight. Then he condemns Providence too. Conscience here works like a courtroom, and its verdict hardens him.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`21-2`,chapter:21,kind:`motif`,title:`Light on one side, darkness on the other`,text:`Hugo says light entered Valjean's soul from one side and darkness from the other. Learning to read at forty sharpens his mind and his hatred together. Education here is not automatically good; it depends on what it feeds.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`21-3`,chapter:21,kind:`craft`,title:`Jean the Jackscrew`,text:`Hugo spends real care on Valjean's body: worth four men at the capstan, able to climb a wall's corner with back and heels, nicknamed after holding up a balcony's caryatid. The galleys have made him a formidable body as well as a hardened mind.`,themes:[]},{id:`21-4`,chapter:21,kind:`question`,title:`Is there an incorruptible spark?`,text:`Hugo asks outright whether misfortune can deform a soul completely, or whether some spark always survives. He does not answer. What would count as evidence either way in the chapters that follow?`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`22-1`,chapter:22,kind:`craft`,title:`A prose poem in the middle of a story`,text:`Hugo stops the narrative for a page of pure image: a man overboard, a ship sailing on, birds singing uselessly overhead. Placed between Valjean's past and his night in the Bishop's house, it tells you how to read that past. He is the drowning man, and society is the ship.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`22-2`,chapter:22,kind:`question`,title:`Who shall resuscitate it?`,text:`The chapter ends on a question: who can bring back a soul that has sunk in the social night? Hugo asks it while Valjean lies asleep in the Bishop's house, and leaves it open.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`23-1`,chapter:23,kind:`theme`,title:`Free, but not from the sentence`,text:`His nineteen years of earnings shrink from a hundred and seventy-one francs to a hundred and nine by deductions he does not understand. At Grasse a master pays him half wages and says 'Beware of the prison.' The State robbed him wholesale; now a private man robs him retail.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`23-2`,chapter:23,kind:`craft`,title:`Then came Digne`,text:`This short chapter fills the gap between release and arrival and ends by looping back: 'Then came Digne.' Hugo tells the story out of order on purpose. We met the man first as Digne saw him, and only now learn how he got there.`,themes:[]},{id:`24-1`,chapter:24,kind:`craft`,title:`A clock and a mind`,text:`The cathedral clock strikes two, then three, then the quarter. Hugo times Valjean's temptation by the bells so that the reader feels each slow step. Notice the odd intrusion of the convict Brevet's checked suspender, the way the mind snags on trivia under stress.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`24-2`,chapter:24,kind:`motif`,title:`The silver again`,text:`The thought that drives out all others is the cupboard on the right by the head of the bed. Hugo now pays off his own careful inventory: the ladle alone is worth double what Valjean earned in nineteen years. The arithmetic of poverty makes temptation concrete.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`24-3`,chapter:24,kind:`motif`,title:`A door left ajar`,text:`Everything Valjean meets on his way is open: the window held by a pin, the low wall, the bedroom door the Bishop has not closed. The Bishop's trust has become the burglar's path.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`25-1`,chapter:25,kind:`motif`,title:`Moonlight on the sleeper`,text:`A cloud parts and moonlight falls on the Bishop's face as he sleeps. Valjean, standing in shadow with an iron bar, is frightened by this 'luminous old man'. Hugo stages the moment as light against darkness, with the crucifix stretching its arms over both of them.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`conscience`]},{id:`25-2`,chapter:25,kind:`theme`,title:`Skull or hand`,text:`He seems ready either to crush that skull or to kiss that hand. He does neither, and takes the silver. Hugo makes the soul a battlefield where the two possibilities stand side by side, and lets neither fully win yet.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`25-3`,chapter:25,kind:`craft`,title:`The shrieking hinge`,text:`A rusty hinge screams 'like the trumpet of Judgment', and nothing happens. Hugo uses the noise to make Valjean's terror audible. The silence afterwards is the Bishop's trust again: he sleeps through it.`,themes:[]},{id:`26-1`,chapter:26,kind:`theme`,title:`Was the silver ever ours?`,text:`The Bishop's first response to the theft is to ask whether he had any right to the silver in the first place. It belonged to the poor, and the man was poor. He reframes the crime before the gendarmes even arrive.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`26-2`,chapter:26,kind:`theme`,title:`Mercy that binds`,text:`'I gave you the candlesticks too.' The Bishop's lie saves Valjean from the galleys, and his gift adds to the loot. Then he claims a promise Valjean never made and says he has bought his soul for God. This is not leniency. It lays an obligation on the man.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`26-3`,chapter:26,kind:`craft`,title:`So he is not the curé?`,text:`Valjean learns only now that his host is a bishop, when the brigadier says 'Monseigneur'. Hugo makes the revelation small and dazed. The man of high rank has been playing a humble priest the whole time.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`27-1`,chapter:27,kind:`theme`,title:`The beast acting by habit`,text:`Valjean steals a child's forty sous almost without knowing it, and then is horrified. Hugo explains that it was the old convict acting from habit, not the man he is becoming. The theft shows him what he must now leave behind.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`27-2`,chapter:27,kind:`motif`,title:`The coin like an open eye`,text:`After the sun sets Valjean sees the coin shining in the earth and recoils 'as if from an open eye'. An object becomes a witness. His search across the dark plain, shouting 'Little Gervais!', is the first thing he does for someone else.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`conscience`]},{id:`27-3`,chapter:27,kind:`craft`,title:`Rise higher or fall lower`,text:`Hugo spells out the stakes: after the Bishop's pardon, Valjean must either rise higher than the Bishop or sink lower than the convict. There is no middle ground left. The chapter ends not on a resolution but on a glimpse, a man kneeling in the dark before a door.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`27-4`,chapter:27,kind:`question`,title:`What he did afterwards no one knows`,text:`Hugo deliberately turns away from Valjean at the most important moment and gives us only a carter's sighting at three in the morning. Why might he refuse to show the conversion directly?`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`28-1`,chapter:28,kind:`craft`,title:`A year made of gossip`,text:`After Valjean's night of crisis, Hugo jumps to Paris and gives us a list: fashions, scandals, songs, a steamboat called a toy, Byron as 'a certain Lord Baron'. The abrupt change of scale is deliberate. Private drama is set against the noise of public life that ignores it.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`28-2`,chapter:28,kind:`context`,title:`The Restoration`,text:`In 1817 the Bourbons had been back for two years. Louis XVIII dated his reign from 1795, as if Napoleon had never happened. The fleurs-de-lys painted on hairdressers' shops and the hounding of Bonapartists show a regime trying to erase twenty-five years.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`28-3`,chapter:28,kind:`theme`,title:`No trivial facts`,text:`Hugo defends his catalogue: there are no small facts in humanity, and the face of a century is made of the faces of its years. It is a theory of history from below, and a defence of the novelist who stops the story to describe a year.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`29-1`,chapter:29,kind:`craft`,title:`Four and four`,text:`The students come as a set, Tholomyès, Listolier, Fameuil, Blachevelle, and so do their mistresses. Hugo introduces them like a chorus. Only Fantine is singled out: the youngest, still in her first illusions, the only one none of the men addresses as 'thou'.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`29-2`,chapter:29,kind:`theme`,title:`A girl with no name of her own`,text:`Fantine never knew her parents; a passer-by gave her her name. Hugo notes she had no family name at all. In a book about names, she enters with almost none.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`29-3`,chapter:29,kind:`theme`,title:`An amour for him, a passion for her`,text:`Hugo puts the imbalance in one phrase. Tholomyès is thirty, rich, balding and amused by everything; Fantine loves for the first and only time. The book's first love story is lopsided from the start.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`30-1`,chapter:30,kind:`context`,title:`Lost outskirts`,text:`Hugo notes how much the edges of Paris have changed since 1817. Saint-Cloud, Sèvres and the country inns were Sunday escapes for working Parisians. He writes like someone recording a city that has since vanished.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`30-2`,chapter:30,kind:`craft`,title:`Psyche, not Venus`,text:`Hugo spends pages on Fantine's beauty, then insists that what sets her apart is her modesty and chaste astonishment. The others wear low necklines openly; her muslin hides and reveals at once. He wants the reader to see her innocence clearly before anything else happens to her.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`31-1`,chapter:31,kind:`motif`,title:`The swing`,text:`Tholomyès pushes each girl on the swing at Issy and sings a Spanish ballad. Fantine alone will not swing, and Favorite mutters about her airs. A small refusal marks her off from the game the others are playing.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`31-2`,chapter:31,kind:`craft`,title:`Patience`,text:`Favorite keeps demanding the surprise, and Tholomyès keeps saying 'Patience'. Hugo strings the promise through the whole day so that the reader starts to wonder about it too.`,themes:[]},{id:`32-1`,chapter:32,kind:`theme`,title:`The cat that can become a lion`,text:`Hugo quotes a police chief assuring the King that the people of the faubourgs are harmless, and answers him: given a pike or a gun, the little man of Paris made the 10th of August and Austerlitz. In the middle of a picnic, he reminds the reader that the crowd has a history.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`32-2`,chapter:32,kind:`motif`,title:`A room with a bed`,text:`The idyll ends in an ugly room with an alcove and a bed, littered with bouquets and the remains of dinner. Hugo marks the time: from five in the morning to half past four in the afternoon. The day is spending itself.`,themes:[]},{id:`33-1`,chapter:33,kind:`craft`,title:`Two voices at once`,text:`Favorite tells Blachevelle she adores him, then whispers to Dahlia that she detests him and loves a poor actor across the street. 'How I lie!' The short scene shows how love talk in this circle is a performance.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`33-2`,chapter:33,kind:`context`,title:`Grisettes`,text:`A grisette was a young working woman, often a seamstress, who might keep a student lover. The arrangement was a known feature of Latin Quarter life. It was understood by most parties, as Favorite shows, to be temporary.`,themes:[`love`,`paris`]},{id:`34-1`,chapter:34,kind:`craft`,title:`A speech that says nothing`,text:`Tholomyès holds forth on puns, wine, women, sugar and marriage. Hugo lets him ramble because the rambling is the portrait: clever, cynical and empty. Notice that he advises everyone to know when to 'effect his own arrest'.`,themes:[]},{id:`34-2`,chapter:34,kind:`theme`,title:`She does not even hear me`,text:`Tholomyès himself sees that Fantine is a dreamer gazing into the blue: 'I am an illusion; but she does not even hear me.' He understands exactly what she feels and is unmoved by it. Then he tries to kiss her and kisses Favorite by mistake.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`35-1`,chapter:35,kind:`motif`,title:`The fallen horse`,text:`An old mare collapses under a heavy cart outside the window and never rises. Tholomyès makes a joke-poem of it; Fantine alone says 'Poor horse!' Hugo sets a worn-out creature dying of overwork in front of a table of idle diners.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`35-2`,chapter:35,kind:`craft`,title:`The kiss on the brow`,text:`The surprise begins with a solemn kiss on each girl's forehead and the men filing out with fingers to their lips. The gesture feels like a farewell before anyone has said goodbye. 'Don't be too long,' Fantine murmurs.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`36-1`,chapter:36,kind:`theme`,title:`Respectability`,text:`The men's letter calls the girls 'the abyss' and themselves virtuous sons returning to duty, to become prefects and fathers. The postscript adds that dinner is paid. Hugo lets the letter condemn its writers in their own polite words.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`36-2`,chapter:36,kind:`craft`,title:`Laughter, then the turn`,text:`The girls laugh, and Fantine laughs with them. Then, an hour afterwards, alone, she weeps, and in the last line Hugo tells us she has a child. The whole long, sunny Book Three has been leading to this one sentence.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`36-3`,chapter:36,kind:`motif`,title:`The coach that halts`,text:`While they wait, the girls watch the diligences thunder past, and one stops briefly before galloping off. Hugo shows the departure right in front of them before the letter explains it.`,themes:[]},{id:`37-1`,chapter:37,kind:`theme`,title:`One mother meets another`,text:`Fantine trusts Madame Thénardier because her little girls look happy on a swing. A mother's love for her own children is mistaken for goodness in general. The title invites the comparison.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`37-2`,chapter:37,kind:`motif`,title:`A swing made of a chain`,text:`The girls swing in the loop of a rusted chain hung under a wrecked cart. It is a charming picture built from heavy iron. Fantine sees only the charm.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`37-3`,chapter:37,kind:`craft`,title:`A voice from inside`,text:`Madame Thénardier is the face of the bargain, but a man's voice from inside the shop sets the price. Hugo keeps Thénardier offstage and lets money do the talking. The last exchange, about a 'mousetrap', tells you everything Fantine does not hear.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`37-4`,chapter:37,kind:`context`,title:`An unmarried mother`,text:`Fantine cannot find work in Paris and dares not be known as an unmarried mother. In 1818 that status could close every respectable door. Leaving the child is the only way she sees to earn a living for them both.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`38-1`,chapter:38,kind:`theme`,title:`The half-class`,text:`Hugo places the Thénardiers between the bourgeois and the poor, with the vices of both: coarse people who have risen a little, clever people who have sunk. Their wickedness is a social type as well as a personal one.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`38-2`,chapter:38,kind:`motif`,title:`The sign of the Sergeant of Waterloo`,text:`Thénardier says he was a sergeant in 1815 and painted the inn sign himself to celebrate a feat of arms. The narrator pointedly declines to vouch for it. A man who advertises his own heroism has introduced himself.`,themes:[]},{id:`38-3`,chapter:38,kind:`context`,title:`Names from cheap novels`,text:`Madame Thénardier names her daughters Éponine and Azelma from the sentimental romances she reads. Hugo calls this mix-up of names, where ploughboys get Arthurs and viscounts get Jacques, one small eddy of the Revolution's levelling. Even naming has become democratic.`,themes:[`names`,`revolution`]},{id:`39-1`,chapter:39,kind:`theme`,title:`Under the table`,text:`Cosette eats from a wooden bowl under the table with the dog and cat. Her fine clothes are pawned and her mother is told she is thriving. To be poor here means first of all to be unprotected.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`39-2`,chapter:39,kind:`motif`,title:`The Lark`,text:`The village calls her the Lark because she is small, trembling and up before anyone else. Hugo adds that this lark never sings. The nickname is affectionate, and the people who use it do nothing for her.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`39-3`,chapter:39,kind:`craft`,title:`Love that needs a victim`,text:`Madame Thénardier hates the stranger because she loves her own daughters, and Cosette takes the blows that might fall on them. Hugo makes a mother's love the cause of the cruelty, not a softening of it.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`40-1`,chapter:40,kind:`craft`,title:`A man without a past`,text:`A stranger arrives in December 1815 with a knapsack and a thorn stick, saves two children from a fire, and so is never asked for his passport. Hugo gives his arrival in a few lines and lets the details sit. The reader may draw their own conclusions; the narrator draws none.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`40-2`,chapter:40,kind:`context`,title:`Jet and black glass`,text:`Imitation jet beads and black glass trinkets were cheap ornaments, popular in mourning jewellery. The stranger's two small technical changes cut costs and raised wages at once. Hugo, a believer in progress, likes a hero who improves an industry.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`41-1`,chapter:41,kind:`theme`,title:`Work for anyone willing to be honest`,text:`Madeleine's factory asks only goodwill of the men, pure morals of the women and honesty of all. Slack seasons and misery vanish from the district. He treats employment itself as charity.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`41-2`,chapter:41,kind:`motif`,title:`Father, Monsieur, Monsieur le Maire`,text:`The town's name for him rises with his fortunes: Father Madeleine, then Monsieur Madeleine, then Monsieur le Maire. He refuses the cross and the mayoralty before giving in. He is known only by what he has done, and no one asks where he came from.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`41-3`,chapter:41,kind:`craft`,title:`The gossips' theories`,text:`The town explains him as a fortune-hunter, then an ambitious man, then an adventurer. Hugo lists the guesses with mild contempt. None of them can account for a rich man who keeps refusing honours.`,themes:[]},{id:`42-1`,chapter:42,kind:`motif`,title:`Two candlesticks on the mantel`,text:`Rumour imagines the mayor's room as a hermit's cave of skulls. The real room is plain mahogany and cheap wallpaper, with one thing worth noticing: two antique candlesticks that appear to be silver. Hugo lets the visitors see them and say nothing.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`42-2`,chapter:42,kind:`theme`,title:`No bad plants, only bad cultivators`,text:`Madeleine shows villagers the uses of the nettle they are uprooting and ends: there are no bad plants or bad men, only bad cultivators. It is the Bishop's road of the fault, put in a farmer's terms.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`42-3`,chapter:42,kind:`craft`,title:`Strength and stealth`,text:`He lifts horses, frees stuck wheels, shoots perfectly but never at a harmless creature, and forces poor men's doors at night to leave gold behind. Hugo gives his benefactor the skills of a very different kind of man.`,themes:[]},{id:`43-1`,chapter:43,kind:`theme`,title:`The happiness of the blind`,text:`Hugo pauses on a detail the papers left out: the Bishop was blind in his last years and content, with his sister beside him. His meditation on being loved without seeing, hearing a step, feeling a hand, is one of the tenderest passages so far.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`43-2`,chapter:43,kind:`craft`,title:`Mourning without explanation`,text:`Madeleine appears in full black the day after the notice. Asked if he was the Bishop's cousin, he says he served in the family in his youth. Then he stops every Savoyard chimney-sweep boy, asks his name and gives him money. Hugo offers these facts without comment.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`43-3`,chapter:43,kind:`question`,title:`Why the Savoyard boys?`,text:`The mayor asks each little Savoyard his name before giving him money. What could he be looking for? Hugo does not say, and the town does not wonder.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`44-1`,chapter:44,kind:`theme`,title:`The law in human form`,text:`Javert worships authority and hates rebellion, and files every crime under rebellion. The functionary can never be wrong; the convict can never be redeemed. He has no malice and no vices, only a rule with no exceptions.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`44-2`,chapter:44,kind:`craft`,title:`A dog born among wolves`,text:`Hugo explains Javert through an animal: the dog born in a litter of wolves. Born in prison to a fortune-teller and a convict, he could attack society or guard it, and chose to guard. His severity grows out of his origins.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`44-3`,chapter:44,kind:`craft`,title:`I think I have him`,text:`Javert stares after the mayor with a look that says 'I have seen that man somewhere'. He mutters 'I think I have him!' and then goes silent for three days. Hugo builds suspicion without confirming anything, and the chapter ends on 'until one day something happens'.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`45-1`,chapter:45,kind:`theme`,title:`Saving an enemy`,text:`Fauchelevent hated Madeleine and tried to harm him. Madeleine offers twenty louis for someone to lift the cart, and when nobody moves he crawls under it himself. The act is generous to a man who did nothing to earn it.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`45-2`,chapter:45,kind:`craft`,title:`The sentence that changes the scene`,text:`Javert says he has known only one man who could lift such a weight, a convict at Toulon. From then on the rescue has two audiences. Madeleine looks at the dying man and at Javert and understands what lifting the cart would tell the inspector. He smiles sadly and does it.`,themes:[`conscience`,`names`]},{id:`45-3`,chapter:45,kind:`motif`,title:`The jackscrew`,text:`The jackscrew is a quarter of an hour away, and the old man has five minutes. Javert's phrase is that he knew one man 'who could take the place of a screw'. In his mouth a man and a tool are interchangeable.`,themes:[]},{id:`46-1`,chapter:46,kind:`craft`,title:`I purchase your horse and cart`,text:`The horse is dead and the cart is wrecked, and Madeleine still 'buys' them for a thousand francs. The polite fiction lets a proud old enemy accept charity without being humiliated. It is the Bishop's tact again, in a businessman's form.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`46-2`,chapter:46,kind:`motif`,title:`A wolf in its master's clothes`,text:`When Javert first sees Madeleine wearing the mayor's scarf, he shudders like a watchdog that smells a wolf dressed as its master. Hugo returns to the animal image from Javert's introduction. The dog now has to bow to the man he suspects.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`46-3`,chapter:46,kind:`craft`,title:`Fantine comes home`,text:`The book's two threads meet at the factory door, which Hugo says is like a friend's face to Fantine. She learns a new trade and earns enough to live. The chapter ends on the plainest happiness: 'She is making her living.'`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`47-1`,chapter:47,kind:`theme`,title:`The itch of knowing`,text:`Hugo digresses on idle people who spy on their neighbours at their own expense, purely to know. Madame Victurnien spends thirty-five francs on a trip to Montfermeil. A woman's ruin starts with a stranger's curiosity, paid for out of her own pocket.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`47-2`,chapter:47,kind:`context`,title:`A public letter-writer`,text:`Fantine can sign her name but not write, so she pays a public scribe. In a town of her class, her letters pass through a stranger's hands. Illiteracy makes her secret everyone's business.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`47-3`,chapter:47,kind:`theme`,title:`Good because he gave, just because he dismissed`,text:`Fantine assumes the mayor gave her fifty francs because he is kind and dismissed her because he is just. She does not think to appeal. Her humility makes her accept a verdict nobody gave her a chance to answer.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`48-1`,chapter:48,kind:`theme`,title:`Charity that gives but does not understand`,text:`The superintendent has 'the charity that gives but not the charity that understands and forgives'. She judges and expels Fantine in perfect good conscience. Hugo shows how virtue without mercy can do the work of cruelty.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`48-2`,chapter:48,kind:`theme`,title:`The art of poverty`,text:`Old Marguerite teaches Fantine how to live on nothing: no fire in winter, a coverlet turned into a petticoat and back, supper by the light of a neighbour's window. Hugo describes poverty as a skill to be learned. Twelve sous a day earned, ten owed for the child.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`48-3`,chapter:48,kind:`motif`,title:`Her hair`,text:`With everything else gone, combing her long hair in the morning still gives Fantine a moment of pleasure. Hugo makes a point of it at the end of the chapter. It is the last thing that is wholly hers.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`49-1`,chapter:49,kind:`theme`,title:`Selling herself by pieces`,text:`First her hair, for ten francs, then two front teeth, for forty, then herself. Each step down is taken for her child, and each is answered by a lie from Montfermeil. Hugo makes the market for a poor woman's body literal, piece by piece.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`49-2`,chapter:49,kind:`craft`,title:`Two stories at once`,text:`Hugo tells us at once that the petticoat went to Éponine and that Cosette was never ill. The reader knows the letters are lies; Fantine does not. Our knowledge turns her sacrifices into something hard to watch.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`49-3`,chapter:49,kind:`theme`,title:`Hatred aimed at the wrong man`,text:`Fantine comes to blame Father Madeleine for everything and sings when she passes the factory. Her hatred is understandable and mistaken. Hugo lets the reader see the misdirection, since we know the mayor never knew.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`50-1`,chapter:50,kind:`theme`,title:`Slavery has not left Europe`,text:`In three hundred words Hugo names Fantine's story: society buying a slave from hunger and cold, a soul for a morsel of bread. Slavery, he says, survives in Europe; it falls on women and is called prostitution. The novel's commentary breaks through the story like a sermon.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`50-2`,chapter:50,kind:`craft`,title:`No bottom`,text:`Fantine believes she has reached the bottom, and Hugo says that is an error: fate is never exhausted. He places this tiny essay between her fall and the next scene. It makes the reader dread what comes next.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`50-3`,chapter:50,kind:`context`,title:`Christus nos liberavit`,text:`The Latin title means 'Christ has freed us'. Hugo uses it bitterly: Christ's law governs civilisation, he says, but does not yet permeate it. A Christian society still sells women.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`51-1`,chapter:51,kind:`context`,title:`Hats as politics`,text:`Hugo notes that under the Restoration royalists wore narrow-brimmed hats called morillos and liberals wore wide bolivars. A dandy's costume was a political badge. Bamatabois wears the royalist kind.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`51-2`,chapter:51,kind:`craft`,title:`A Tholomyès in the provinces`,text:`Hugo says Tholomyès, had he stayed home, would have become one of these idle young men. The link makes Bamatabois a type, not just a villain. The same careless class that abandoned Fantine now torments her in the street.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`51-3`,chapter:51,kind:`motif`,title:`Snow down a bare back`,text:`Snow forced between bare shoulders is a small, cruel joke for him and a shock for her. Fantine's ball-dress in winter is a working costume, not a choice. Hugo makes the cold itself part of the insult.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`52-1`,chapter:52,kind:`theme`,title:`Two men fight over a woman's life`,text:`Javert sees a prostitute who attacked a citizen and elector. The mayor sees a woman driven onto the street and says the highest law is conscience. Fantine watches the two men who hold her life argue, and the one she hated turns out to be her rescuer.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`52-2`,chapter:52,kind:`craft`,title:`The spit and the wiping`,text:`She spits in the mayor's face, and he wipes it away and orders her freed. Hugo lets the insult and the mercy follow without a pause. He does not ask for an apology.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`52-3`,chapter:52,kind:`theme`,title:`Law against law`,text:`Madeleine wins by citing the code: municipal police matters are the mayor's to judge, and detention without cause is forbidden. Javert bows like a soldier. Mercy here defeats the law by using the law.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`52-4`,chapter:52,kind:`craft`,title:`A comparison that flickers`,text:`For an instant, Hugo says, a hideous comparison between what this woman is and what this mayor might be passes through Javert's mind. It is the inspector's suspicion surfacing at the worst moment. Hugo shows it and moves on.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`53-1`,chapter:53,kind:`motif`,title:`Two martyrs`,text:`Fantine wakes to find the mayor praying before the crucifix, to the martyr on high, he says, and adds to himself: for the martyr here below. He sees her suffering as sacred. It is the first time anyone in the book has looked at her that way.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`53-2`,chapter:53,kind:`theme`,title:`A lark becomes a milch cow`,text:`Thénardier pads his bills with the doctors' fees for his own daughters and keeps Cosette because her mother's protector is rich. Hugo gives him one perfect phrase: the lark is turning into a milch cow. The child is an investment.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`53-3`,chapter:53,kind:`craft`,title:`A letter posted by hand`,text:`Javert writes to Paris the same night and posts the letter himself. The town assumes he is resigning. Hugo does not say what it contains, and lets the reader worry.`,themes:[]},{id:`54-1`,chapter:54,kind:`theme`,title:`Severe with himself`,text:`Javert asks to be dismissed, not allowed to resign, because he denounced a magistrate wrongly. He has been severe with others all his life and would be a blackguard to spare himself. His rigidity is ugly and also honest; he applies the rule to himself first.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`54-2`,chapter:54,kind:`craft`,title:`The mayor keeps his back turned`,text:`Through most of Javert's confession, Madeleine stays bent over his papers. When the name 'Jean Valjean' comes, he turns livid; when the real Valjean is said to be found, the paper drops from his hand. Hugo makes us read the scene through small physical reactions.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`54-3`,chapter:54,kind:`motif`,title:`How Jean becomes Champ`,text:`Jean Mathieu, said in an Auvergne accent, becomes Chan Mathieu, then Champmathieu. The title makes a joke of the slide. An old man is being convicted partly on the sound of a word.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`54-4`,chapter:54,kind:`theme`,title:`False kindness`,text:`Javert rejects the mayor's offered hand: the kindness that supports a street-walker against a citizen, he says, disorganises society. In his view mercy is a kind of disorder. That is a coherent philosophy, and Hugo lets him state it plainly.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`55-1`,chapter:55,kind:`craft`,title:`A woman who has never lied`,text:`Hugo introduces Sister Simplice through one trait: she has never told a lie, not even the small social lies everyone allows. To her Satan has two names, Satan and Lying. In a house where the mayor is hiding a woman's sickness from her, that is a strong trait to place at the bedside.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`55-2`,chapter:55,kind:`craft`,title:`A singular tone`,text:`Madeleine commends Fantine to the sister in a tone she recalls afterwards. He stays an hour instead of half an hour, then studies a road map and pencils figures. Hugo gives us only what witnesses saw, and keeps us outside the mayor's head.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`55-3`,chapter:55,kind:`context`,title:`Not all of this was known`,text:`Book VII opens by warning that not all of what follows was known at Montreuil-sur-Mer. Hugo poses as a historian reconstructing events from witnesses. The device lets him move from public facts to private ones.`,themes:[]},{id:`56-1`,chapter:56,kind:`motif`,title:`The knocker set down`,text:`Madeleine lifts the priest's knocker, holds it, and sets it down without knocking. Whatever he wanted to confess or ask, he decides not to. Then he takes the long way home to avoid passing that door again.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`56-2`,chapter:56,kind:`craft`,title:`A detective in a horse-hirer`,text:`Master Scaufflaire works out the destination from the figures 5, 6 and 8½ left on his mantel: Hesdin, Saint-Pol, Arras. Hugo lets a comic minor character solve the mystery the mayor is trying to keep. The reader knows what is at Arras.`,themes:[]},{id:`56-3`,chapter:56,kind:`craft`,title:`Footsteps overhead`,text:`We see the night only from below: the cashier hears pacing, a cupboard, furniture moved, and sees firelight through a window open in the bitter cold. Hugo then announces that he will tell us what happened in that room. The external view builds pressure for the internal one.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`57-1`,chapter:57,kind:`theme`,title:`A tempest in a skull`,text:`Every argument for silence is a good one: a whole town depends on him, Fantine and Cosette depend on him, and the man at Arras is a thief anyway. Hugo makes the wrong choice sound like self-sacrifice. 'I am Madeleine, and Madeleine I remain.' Conscience here has to see through its own best reasoning.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`57-2`,chapter:57,kind:`theme`,title:`Two rules that nearly always agree`,text:`Since Digne he has lived by two rules: to hide his name and to sanctify his life. Usually they agree. Now they collide. The Bishop's candlesticks, the mourning, the questions to the Savoyards were all moments when goodness was chosen over safety.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`57-3`,chapter:57,kind:`motif`,title:`Into the fire`,text:`He burns the blouse, the knapsack and the thorn cudgel of 1815, and the forty-sou piece glints in the ashes. Then he takes the candlesticks to melt them, and a voice shouts 'Jean Valjean!' He puts them back. The two kinds of object, the convict's relics and the Bishop's gift, cannot be destroyed in the same way.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`57-4`,chapter:57,kind:`context`,title:`Dante and Gethsemane`,text:`Hugo asks us to enter this conscience as Dante entered the gate of hell. He ends by comparing Valjean to another man who pushed away a cup in a garden of olive trees, that is, Christ's night in Gethsemane before the Passion. The references say how serious he thinks this ordinary-looking choice is.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`57-5`,chapter:57,kind:`question`,title:`Paradise and demon, hell and angel`,text:`'Remain in paradise and become a demon, or return to hell and become an angel?' The chapter ends with no decision. Which of his arguments for staying silent did you find hardest to dismiss?`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`58-1`,chapter:58,kind:`motif`,title:`The dream of the dead town`,text:`He dreams of an empty village where silent, earth-coloured men stand behind every door, and the first one tells him he has been dead a long time. Hugo says Valjean later wrote the dream down. It turns his inner crisis into an image of a living death, a man among the buried.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`58-2`,chapter:58,kind:`craft`,title:`Lanterns like fallen stars`,text:`He wakes and takes the tilbury's two red lanterns for stars fallen to earth. The decision he could not make has been made for him by an order he gave the day before. The portress's 'It is five o'clock' sounds like a summons.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`conscience`]},{id:`59-1`,chapter:59,kind:`theme`,title:`Providence as an excuse`,text:`The broken wheel at Hesdin fills him with immense joy: Providence has stopped him, he has honestly done everything, he can turn back. Then a boy fetches an old woman with a cart. Hugo shows how eagerly a conscience accepts any obstacle as permission.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`59-2`,chapter:59,kind:`craft`,title:`A road of hindrances`,text:`Broken wheel, bad cart, closed road, broken whiffletree, a missed fact about the hour of the trial. Hugo piles up delays so the reader feels the journey as Valjean does, each one a chance to give up. The tension is not whether he arrives but whether he wants to.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`59-3`,chapter:59,kind:`motif`,title:`Another night on the plain`,text:`Cold and starving, he remembers his other night journey on the plain near Digne eight years before. Hugo links this drive to the night after Petit-Gervais. Both are journeys away from and towards himself.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`60-1`,chapter:60,kind:`theme`,title:`The first lie refused`,text:`The maid suggests a small white lie: say the mayor is busy with the council. Sister Simplice blushes, weighs the harm the truth might do, and says only that he has gone away. Fantine fills the silence with hope. The truth, told plainly, does more good than the lie would have.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`60-2`,chapter:60,kind:`motif`,title:`A lullaby forgotten for five years`,text:`Waiting for the mayor, Fantine sings in a whisper an old cradle song she sang to Cosette and had not remembered since. The nun weeps. Hugo lets one song carry all the years of separation.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`60-3`,chapter:60,kind:`craft`,title:`Joy as medicine`,text:`The doctor finds her pulse stronger and says great joy has been known to halt even advanced disease. Hugo cuts between Arras and the sickroom so that hope in one place hangs on a decision in the other. The reader carries both.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`61-1`,chapter:61,kind:`craft`,title:`A return ticket`,text:`His first act in Arras is to book a seat on the one o'clock mail back home. The chapter title is wry: on arrival he takes precautions for departure. He still has not decided, and part of him is planning to leave.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`61-2`,chapter:61,kind:`craft`,title:`Ice and fire`,text:`A lawyer says the case is over, a woman named Limosin convicted for life, and for a moment Valjean thinks he is too late. Then the lights turn out to be for a second case. Hugo uses a mistaken identity of cases to give us a jolt like the one he feels.`,themes:[]},{id:`61-3`,chapter:61,kind:`motif`,title:`Writing his name`,text:`He tears a leaf from his notebook and writes by a street lantern: M. Madeleine, Mayor of Montreuil-sur-Mer. The name that has protected him for eight years becomes the key that opens the courtroom door.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`62-1`,chapter:62,kind:`theme`,title:`A reputation as a pass`,text:`The usher who turned him away now bows to the ground. The President admits him at once because of what Madeleine has done. He crushes the deferential note as though it tasted bitter. Being honoured feels to him like an accusation.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`62-2`,chapter:62,kind:`motif`,title:`The brass door-handle`,text:`Only a copper knob separates him from the courtroom. It shines at him 'like a terrible star'. He flees down the corridor, stands a quarter of an hour in the dark, and comes back as though someone were leading him. Hugo makes the whole crisis turn on a door handle.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`62-3`,chapter:62,kind:`context`,title:`Pache's letter`,text:`He stares at a framed letter by Pache, mayor of Paris in Year II of the Revolution, and cannot read it. One mayor's relic in front of another mayor who is about to lose his office. Hugo leaves the detail unexplained.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`63-1`,chapter:63,kind:`theme`,title:`Shall I become like that again?`,text:`The man on the bench looks like Valjean as he entered Digne: wild-eyed, in a blouse, full of hatred. Seeing him, Valjean sees what he was. The trial of another man becomes a mirror held up to his own past.`,themes:[`conscience`,`names`]},{id:`63-2`,chapter:63,kind:`motif`,title:`A crucifix in the courtroom`,text:`The scene is his own trial of twenty-seven years ago come back to life, with one difference: a crucifix now hangs above the President. Hugo marks how the Restoration put religion back into the courts. Whether it changed the justice is another question.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`revolution`]},{id:`63-3`,chapter:63,kind:`craft`,title:`Satire of the bar`,text:`The defence lawyer concedes most of the case in pompous provincial rhetoric, and the prosecutor turns those concessions into proof. He even blames the crime on romantic literature, a joke at Hugo's own expense. The machinery of justice runs smoothly toward the wrong result.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`63-4`,chapter:63,kind:`question`,title:`Is Champmathieu innocent?`,text:`Nobody saw him climb the wall; he says he found the branch on the ground. Yet he may have taken it. Does it matter, for the choice in front of Valjean, whether the old man is a thief?`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`64-1`,chapter:64,kind:`theme`,title:`Defended by his own poverty`,text:`Champmathieu's rambling speech about M. Baloup and his daughter in the cold wash-house makes the court laugh. It is, in fact, a precise account of how labour wears out the poor. Nobody in the room is listening for that.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`64-2`,chapter:64,kind:`craft`,title:`Famous!`,text:`As each convict swears he is Jean Valjean, the prisoner mutters 'Good!' and 'Famous!' with growing satisfaction. His comic incomprehension is also helplessness. The more certain the witnesses, the less he can defend himself.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`64-3`,chapter:64,kind:`craft`,title:`A voice behind the judges`,text:`The chapter ends on three names cried out, Brevet! Chenildieu! Cochepaille!, and twenty voices answering with a fourth: M. Madeleine! Hugo cuts at the instant of recognition, so the reader hears the name before the man speaks.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`65-1`,chapter:65,kind:`theme`,title:`I am Jean Valjean`,text:`He takes his name back in open court, and with it the galleys. Doing right costs him everything he built. Hugo has made us feel every argument against it, so the plain sentence lands with full weight.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`65-2`,chapter:65,kind:`theme`,title:`The galleys make the convict`,text:`He tells the court that he went in a stupid peasant and came out a firebrand; kindness saved him as severity had ruined him. It is the argument of the whole volume, put in the mouth of the man it concerns.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`65-3`,chapter:65,kind:`craft`,title:`Proof by memory`,text:`When the court suggests a doctor, he proves his identity with details only a fellow convict could know: Brevet's checked suspenders, Chenildieu's burn, Cochepaille's tattooed date. The galleys he hid from are his evidence. The hair turned white in an hour is Hugo's outward sign of the night's cost.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`65-4`,chapter:65,kind:`question`,title:`To be envied`,text:`He asks the court to pity him, then says that when he thinks of what he nearly did, he counts himself to be envied. What does he think he has gained?`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`66-1`,chapter:66,kind:`motif`,title:`The doctor's mirror`,text:`Sister Simplice hands him the little mirror used to test whether a patient still breathes. He sees his white hair and says only 'Well!' Hugo chooses a mirror meant for the dying.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`66-2`,chapter:66,kind:`theme`,title:`Can they tell a lie?`,text:`The nun murmurs that they cannot lie to Fantine. He says 'God will inspire us.' Two people committed to truth face a dying woman who believes her child is coming. The chapter ends on her first word: 'And Cosette?'`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`66-3`,chapter:66,kind:`craft`,title:`Then and now`,text:`Two months earlier, by the same bed, her hair was grey and his was brown. Now hers is grey and his is white. Hugo marks how much has happened to him with a single colour.`,themes:[]},{id:`67-1`,chapter:67,kind:`craft`,title:`The doctor's evasion`,text:`The doctor says the child is here but Fantine must be calm before she sees her. It is a medical lie, and Valjean does not contradict it. Hugo shows how quickly an honest silence becomes a kind of deceit.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`67-2`,chapter:67,kind:`motif`,title:`A child singing in the yard`,text:`A little girl sings outside and Fantine is certain it is Cosette. Then come plans for a garden, butterflies, letters, a white veil for first communion. Hugo lets her build a whole future in a few sentences, which makes the end of the chapter brutal.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`67-3`,chapter:67,kind:`craft`,title:`Look behind you`,text:`Fantine sees Javert first and can only sign to Valjean to turn around. Hugo ends the chapter on her terror and his turning.`,themes:[]},{id:`68-1`,chapter:68,kind:`motif`,title:`A buckle under the ear`,text:`Javert looks as correct as ever, except that his stock's buckle sits under his left ear instead of at the back. For a man with no wrinkle in his uniform or his duty, this is an earthquake. Hugo shows inner turmoil through one displaced detail.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`68-2`,chapter:68,kind:`theme`,title:`The evil of the good`,text:`Javert in triumph is a ferocious archangel, a Saint Michael with his heel on crime. Hugo insists there is nothing base in him: probity and duty, wrongly aimed, remain grand even when hideous. He is to be pitied as every ignorant man who triumphs is pitied.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`68-3`,chapter:68,kind:`context`,title:`'The Emperor', not 'Bonaparte'`,text:`The presiding judge is offended that Madeleine said 'the Emperor' rather than 'Bonaparte' when pointing to Cochepaille's tattoo. Under the Restoration the word you chose marked your politics. A slip of vocabulary helps get the warrant signed.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`69-1`,chapter:69,kind:`theme`,title:`Right on every point`,text:`Javert has a warrant, and he refuses three days' grace because a convict would run. Every step is legal and defensible. The result is a woman dead of shock. Hugo puts the law's correctness and its cost side by side.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`69-2`,chapter:69,kind:`motif`,title:`'Thou'`,text:`Javert addresses the mayor as 'thou', the form used to convicts and dogs. Fantine hears it and does not understand. The shift in grammar strips away his title before anyone explains anything.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`69-3`,chapter:69,kind:`craft`,title:`No other name from now on`,text:`The narrator says he will call him Jean Valjean and nothing else from now on. Javert shouts that there is no Madeleine and no mayor. Narrator and policeman agree on the name for opposite reasons.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`69-4`,chapter:69,kind:`question`,title:`What did he whisper?`,text:`Valjean bends and whispers something to the dead woman that no one hears, and Sister Simplice always said she saw a smile come to the lips. Hugo keeps the words secret. What do you imagine he promised?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`70-1`,chapter:70,kind:`theme`,title:`Two hours to forget`,text:`Within two hours the town has forgotten everything the mayor did. People who found him too good now say they always suspected. Only the old portress, without thinking, hangs his key and sets out his candle. Hugo measures a reputation against a name.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`70-2`,chapter:70,kind:`theme`,title:`Sister Simplice's lie`,text:`The woman who has never lied tells two lies to Javert, and he believes her because she has never lied. Hugo prays that this lie be counted to her credit in paradise. Conscience here overrules a rule held for a lifetime.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`70-3`,chapter:70,kind:`motif`,title:`The candlesticks go with him`,text:`He leaves his money for the poor and the trial costs, labels the cudgel tips and the forty-sou piece as evidence, and takes only the Bishop's candlesticks, wrapped in strips of shirt. What he keeps tells you what he believes he owes.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`conscience`]},{id:`70-4`,chapter:70,kind:`craft`,title:`Her grave resembled her bed`,text:`The curé decides a convict and a prostitute deserve no more and gives Fantine the cheapest burial, in the common pit. Hugo ends Volume One on one flat sentence. The book's first volume closes not with the hero but with the poor woman thrown away.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`71-1`,chapter:71,kind:`craft`,title:`A walk instead of a sequel`,text:`Volume One ended on a pauper's grave and a fugitive on the road to Paris. Volume Two opens with a stroll in Belgium in May 1861, ducks on a pond, a girl weeding. Hugo deliberately leaves the reader hanging and approaches his next subject on foot, the way a tourist would.`,themes:[]},{id:`71-2`,chapter:71,kind:`craft`,title:`The name comes last`,text:`The narrator sees a gate, a hole in the stone, a mound with a lion on it, and only then hears the name: Hougomont. Hugo withholds 'Waterloo' to the end of the chapter, so the reader discovers the battlefield as he did, by accident.`,themes:[]},{id:`71-3`,chapter:71,kind:`context`,title:`The narrator in person`,text:`'The person who is telling this story' steps out as a real traveller. Hugo did visit Waterloo in 1861, while finishing the novel in exile. The present-day walk gives the battle a witness who stands in the same fields decades afterwards.`,themes:[]},{id:`72-1`,chapter:72,kind:`motif`,title:`The well with no bucket`,text:`One well in the yard has no bucket because it is full of skeletons; three hundred bodies were thrown in, and legend says voices called from it that night. Hugo makes a domestic object carry the horror. A farm's ordinary things become relics.`,themes:[]},{id:`72-2`,chapter:72,kind:`craft`,title:`Ruins as evidence`,text:`Hugo reads Hougomont like a detective: the patched door, the cut-away stair, the bullet in every apple tree, the burned feet of a wooden Christ. The battle is told through what it left on walls and wood. History, here, is something you can touch.`,themes:[]},{id:`72-3`,chapter:72,kind:`theme`,title:`Three francs`,text:`Three thousand men died in this one enclosure so that a peasant can now offer to explain Waterloo for three francs. Hugo ends on the guide's patter. Great sacrifices shrink into tourism, and the poor make a living from them.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`73-1`,chapter:73,kind:`theme`,title:`A few drops of water`,text:`Hugo's opening thesis is that the night's rain lost Napoleon the battle: soft ground delayed his guns until half-past eleven and gave Blücher time to arrive. The fate of Europe turns on the weather. History, in this view, is made of accidents.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`73-2`,chapter:73,kind:`craft`,title:`A passer-by on the plain`,text:`Hugo disclaims any military authority. One scene of his story is connected with the battle, he says, and he judges 'as the populace does'. He presents himself as a bystander with a moral eye, not a strategist.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`73-3`,chapter:73,kind:`context`,title:`Turning the clock back`,text:`Waterloo was fought on 18 June 1815. Hugo notes that this is a little before the events of the first part of his book, so the story jumps backwards, to the summer before Valjean reached Digne. He claims 'the storyteller's right' to do so.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`74-1`,chapter:74,kind:`craft`,title:`A battle shaped like a letter`,text:`Hugo asks the reader to picture a capital A laid on the ground: two roads for legs, a sunken lane for the crossbar, Wellington at the top, Napoleon at the bottom right. It is a teacher's device, and it works. With the A in mind, the movements of the day become easy to picture.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`74-2`,chapter:74,kind:`motif`,title:`The night that bears his form`,text:`History's pitiless light now throws shadow where people once saw only rays, Hugo says; it is a misfortune for a man to leave behind him a night shaped like himself. Napoleon is drawn in light and darkness at once.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`74-3`,chapter:74,kind:`theme`,title:`Above and below`,text:`On 18 June Wellington had the good position, above, and Napoleon the bad one, below. Two wrestlers, Hugo says, clutch at every bush and fold of ground. A chance hollow can stop an army. Geography is already a character.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`75-1`,chapter:75,kind:`craft`,title:`Rembrandt, not the map-maker`,text:`From noon to four the battle becomes a fog of uniforms and noise that no historian can map. Hugo says Rembrandt would paint it better than a draughtsman. He admits the limits of narrative, and then tells us the battle 'came to a point'.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`75-2`,chapter:75,kind:`context`,title:`11:35`,text:`An English officer noted the first cannon shot at thirty-five minutes past eleven. Hugo grounds his argument in that small fact. Napoleon was waiting for a sun that never came to dry the ground.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`76-1`,chapter:76,kind:`theme`,title:`To do like me`,text:`Asked for orders in case he is killed, Wellington says 'To do like me'. Under his elm he is impassive while his aide falls beside him. Hugo gives the enemy general cold heroism and short, memorable lines.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`76-2`,chapter:76,kind:`craft`,title:`Hidden things`,text:`Guns hidden in the brushwood, a battalion lying in the tall wheat, embrasures cut into hedges so well that a French engineer saw no obstacle. The English position is full of concealment. When the line draws back behind the crest, Napoleon cries 'The beginning of retreat!' The reader has learned to distrust what can be seen from below.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`77-1`,chapter:77,kind:`theme`,title:`Ninety chances in a hundred`,text:`Ill and sleepless, Napoleon has never been in better humour: jokes at breakfast, 'Magnificent!' at the deployment, 'four and twenty handsome maids' for his guns. Hugo dwells on his gaiety. Confidence becomes something to watch.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`77-2`,chapter:77,kind:`motif`,title:`The hollow road`,text:`The chapter ends on a feature invisible from a distance: a sunken lane twelve feet deep along the crest, marked by stone crosses for old accidents. Hugo describes it and says only that on the day of battle it was 'terrible'. He puts it on our map now; from the French side it cannot be seen.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`77-3`,chapter:77,kind:`context`,title:`They have altered my field`,text:`To build the Lion Mound, workers carted away the slope of the ground. Wellington, revisiting, complained that they had altered his battlefield. Hugo warns the modern visitor that the land no longer shows what happened.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`78-1`,chapter:78,kind:`craft`,title:`One enormous sentence`,text:`Hugo lists the morning's setbacks in a single breathless sentence: Hougomont, Bauduin, Foy, the hidden wall, mired guns, shells sinking into mud, Grouchy's delay. Napoleon, he says, never added up the details, only the total. The sentence makes us add them up for him.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`78-2`,chapter:78,kind:`motif`,title:`A shake of the head`,text:`Napoleon asks his peasant guide Lacoste something in a low voice, and Lacoste shakes his head, 'probably perfidiously'. Hugo does not say what was asked. The great man's plan pauses on a peasant's answer.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`78-3`,chapter:78,kind:`question`,title:`Treating destiny as an equal`,text:`Hugo says a man with the Bérésina and Leipzig behind him might have distrusted Waterloo. Is Napoleon's confidence here a sign of genius or of blindness?`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`79-1`,chapter:79,kind:`theme`,title:`The ravine fills`,text:`The cuirassiers burst over the crest shouting 'Vive l'Empereur!' and find the hollow road under their horses' feet. Rank pushes rank in until the ravine is full of living men, and the rest ride over them. 'This began the loss of the battle.'`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`79-2`,chapter:79,kind:`theme`,title:`He embarrassed God`,text:`Could Napoleon have won? Hugo answers no, because of God: his weight in human destiny upset the balance, and the cemeteries had pleaded against him. Waterloo is not a battle but a change of front by the universe. This is history as Providence.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`79-3`,chapter:79,kind:`craft`,title:`Centaurs storming Olympus`,text:`Hugo makes the charge mythic: giants on colossal horses, two steel serpents climbing through grapeshot. The grandeur is built up so that the fall into the sunken road is all the more sudden and horrible.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`80-1`,chapter:80,kind:`craft`,title:`A piper's arm`,text:`Amid the slaughter Hugo picks out a Highland piper playing on a drum until a sabre cuts off his arm and his song together. Single images like this keep the mass of numbers human.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`80-2`,chapter:80,kind:`theme`,title:`Blücher, or night`,text:`Two exhausted armies hold one plateau; nobody can win it. Wellington has no reserves and tells a general to let himself be killed. At five he looks at his watch and murmurs 'Blücher, or night!' Hugo makes rescue a matter of time.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`80-3`,chapter:80,kind:`context`,title:`The squares`,text:`Infantry formed in hollow squares, bayonets outward, could resist cavalry if they did not break. The front rank knelt to take the horses on bayonets; the second rank fired. Seven of thirteen English squares were destroyed and the rest held.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`81-1`,chapter:81,kind:`theme`,title:`Two guides`,text:`A peasant guide may have misled Napoleon; a shepherd boy led Bülow out of the forest by the one route that could bring guns to Plancenoit. Hugo sets the two side by side. The fate of Europe passes through two anonymous country people.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`81-2`,chapter:81,kind:`motif`,title:`A cloud on the horizon`,text:`At noon Napoleon saw something toward Chapelle-Saint-Lambert. Soult said it was Grouchy; others said trees. It did not move. Hugo makes the Prussian army appear first as an ambiguous smudge, read according to hope.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`82-1`,chapter:82,kind:`theme`,title:`Shouting for the Emperor as they die`,text:`The Guard advances knowing it will die and cries 'Vive l'Empereur!' Hugo says history records nothing more moving than that agony bursting into acclamation. He admires the men even while his argument is against their cause.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`82-2`,chapter:82,kind:`motif`,title:`The sun of Austerlitz`,text:`At eight o'clock the clouds part and the setting sun shines on the Guard, the same sun they saw rise at Austerlitz, Napoleon's great victory of 1805. Hugo uses light to close a whole era.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`82-3`,chapter:82,kind:`context`,title:`Reserved for French bullets`,text:`Ney begs the English bullets to find him and they do not. Hugo adds that he was reserved for French bullets. Marshal Ney was shot by a French firing squad in December 1815 for having rejoined Napoleon; Hugo's readers knew it.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`83-1`,chapter:83,kind:`theme`,title:`Lions converted into goats`,text:`The Grand Army dissolves at once on every side, men trampling the living and the dead. 'And who was fleeing in that manner? The Grand Army.' Hugo makes the collapse total to show that something larger than courage was at work.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`83-2`,chapter:83,kind:`theme`,title:`God has passed by`,text:`Waterloo, Hugo says, is the hinge of the nineteenth century: the great man had to vanish for the great century to arrive. He condemns Blücher's slaughter of fugitives in the same breath. Providence does not excuse cruelty.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`83-3`,chapter:83,kind:`motif`,title:`Walking back alone`,text:`At nightfall two officers catch hold of a haggard man walking his horse back toward Waterloo: Napoleon, 'the sleepwalker of a shattered dream'. The chapter's last image is the Emperor reduced to one man on foot.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`84-1`,chapter:84,kind:`craft`,title:`The square shrinks`,text:`At each discharge the last square draws in its four walls and fires back; distant fugitives stop to listen to the thunder growing smaller. Hugo tells the end through sound and shape. When the English guns fall silent out of something like awe, the stage is set for one word.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`84-2`,chapter:84,kind:`theme`,title:`An obscure officer`,text:`After pages of emperors and dukes, the climax belongs to a man Hugo calls 'an obscure officer named Cambronne'. The book has been arguing for unknown people all along, and here the argument enters the battle.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`85-1`,chapter:85,kind:`theme`,title:`The real victor`,text:`Hugo crowns Cambronne the winner of Waterloo: not Napoleon, who fled, nor Wellington, who despaired, nor Blücher, who did not fight. To answer the lightning that kills you with scorn is to conquer. The great men shrink and the unknown man looms.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`85-2`,chapter:85,kind:`craft`,title:`Defending a dirty word`,text:`Hugo knows 'Merde!' will shock his readers and repeats it 'at his own risk'. He calls it Rabelais answering Leonidas, carnival at the end of an epic. Mixing the lowest and the highest is a deliberate principle of his style.`,themes:[]},{id:`85-3`,chapter:85,kind:`motif`,title:`Joseph the postman`,text:`The chapter ends where the Guard died, with Joseph the postman from Nivelles passing at four in the morning, whistling and whipping up his horse. Ordinary life closes over the grave. Hugo returns to the present of his walk.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`86-1`,chapter:86,kind:`theme`,title:`The winning number`,text:`What is Waterloo? Not a victory, Hugo says, but the winning number in a lottery, a stake won by Europe and paid by France. 'It was not worth putting a lion there.' Chance, not merit, decided it.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`86-2`,chapter:86,kind:`craft`,title:`Calculation against genius`,text:`Hugo sets the two generals up as opposites: Wellington the Barême, a famous book of tables, of war; Napoleon its Michelangelo. Genius was beaten by calculation. The comparison is partisan and he knows it, but it is also a portrait of two kinds of mind.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`86-3`,chapter:86,kind:`theme`,title:`Glory to the army, not the captain`,text:`Hugo gives the honour to the English soldiers, Highlanders, Greys and raw recruits, not to their duke, who called them a 'detestable army'. A column, he says, should bear the statue of a people. History belongs to the many.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`87-1`,chapter:87,kind:`theme`,title:`Not glad of Waterloo`,text:`Some liberals do not hate Waterloo; Hugo does. It was the kings of Europe against France and the Revolution, carrying divine right with them. Yet the restored monarchy was forced to grant a charter and be liberal in spite of itself.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`87-2`,chapter:87,kind:`theme`,title:`Tomorrow has no bad tools`,text:`Call revolution Progress, and progress Tomorrow, Hugo says: it uses whatever comes to hand, Bonaparte overturning thrones, Louis XVIII signing a charter. 'The slashers had finished, it was the thinkers' turn.' He reads history as moving forward whatever the generals intend.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`87-3`,chapter:87,kind:`motif`,title:`The mounted Robespierre`,text:`On 18 June 1815, Hugo writes, 'the mounted Robespierre was hurled from his saddle'. He sees Napoleon as the Revolution on horseback, despotic and revolutionary at once. The phrase holds his whole ambivalence.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`88-1`,chapter:88,kind:`context`,title:`The Restoration's furniture`,text:`The white flag and cockade, the Arc du Carrousel hiding its Napoleonic victories behind a royal statue, marble over the pauper's grave of Louis XVI, the Holy Alliance. Hugo lists the props of 1815 the way he listed the gossip of 1817 in Book Three. Both catalogues describe the world Valjean and Fantine live in.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`88-2`,chapter:88,kind:`theme`,title:`Go this way, and not that`,text:`All of it, the throne, the treaties, the caged Emperor, because a shepherd said to a Prussian, 'Go this way, and not that!' Hugo keeps returning to the smallest cause. Then he asks what any of it matters to the Infinite, for whom a grub equals an eagle.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`88-3`,chapter:88,kind:`motif`,title:`You don't know him`,text:`Told that Napoleon is dead, an old soldier answers: 'You don't know him.' The defeated man grows larger in exile. The kings reign ill at ease with Saint Helena on the horizon.`,themes:[`history`,`names`]},{id:`89-1`,chapter:89,kind:`craft`,title:`Why the battle was here`,text:`After seventeen chapters of strategy, Hugo returns 'of necessity' to the field at night. This is the scene of his story he said was connected with Waterloo. The vast digression narrows to two men in the dark of the hollow road.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`89-2`,chapter:89,kind:`theme`,title:`A rescue that is a robbery`,text:`The prowler takes the officer's ring, cross, watch and purse, and in doing so drags him alive from under the dead. The officer thanks him as his saviour. The great day ends in a small private lie, and Hugo calls it the prowler's 'succour', in quotation marks.`,themes:[`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`89-3`,chapter:89,kind:`motif`,title:`Names exchanged`,text:`'Thénardier.' 'I shall not forget that name. My name is Pontmercy.' The marauder gives his name and his invented rank of sergeant; the wounded man promises to remember. A name is offered in gratitude for a theft.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`89-4`,chapter:89,kind:`context`,title:`The rearguard of every army`,text:`Hugo describes the stragglers who followed armies: sutlers, sham invalids, marauders who stripped corpses and changed sides with the victor. Wellington ordered such men shot, and the dead were robbed anyway. The sutler's cart waiting by the Nivelles road fits the type.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`89-5`,chapter:89,kind:`question`,title:`A ghost or a gendarme`,text:`When the hand closes on his coat, the prowler laughs that he prefers a ghost to a gendarme, and digs the man out from curiosity. Why does he bother to save him at all?`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`90-1`,chapter:90,kind:`craft`,title:`News from the papers`,text:`Hugo skips Valjean's capture, trial and sentence and gives us two newspaper clippings instead. The royalist paper and the Paris paper tell it in their own flat styles. We learn the hero's fate secondhand, the way the public did.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`90-2`,chapter:90,kind:`motif`,title:`From 24,601 to 9,430`,text:`The title is a whole biography: a man becomes a number, a name, a mayor, and a number again. The newspapers call him 'the man known as M. Madeleine'. Every identity he built has been filed away.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`90-3`,chapter:90,kind:`theme`,title:`What he foresaw came true`,text:`With Madeleine gone, the factory scatters, wages fall and the poor are left with nothing; tax collection costs double within four years. Hugo confirms that the arguments of his night of hesitation were real. Doing right had a price, and the town paid it.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`90-4`,chapter:90,kind:`context`,title:`Montfermeil coaches`,text:`The Journal de Paris reports he was retaken boarding a little coach to Montfermeil. The reader knows who lives at Montfermeil. The paper does not.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`91-1`,chapter:91,kind:`craft`,title:`Folklore as cover`,text:`Hugo opens with a Montfermeil legend about the devil burying treasure and a sorcerer-monk's Latin couplet: dig, and you may find a coin, a stone, a corpse, a phantom, or nothing. The supernatural story lets the villagers explain away something more ordinary.`,themes:[]},{id:`91-2`,chapter:91,kind:`theme`,title:`No one knows who has been in the galleys`,text:`Thénardier shrugs that nobody knows who has been in the galleys or who will be. It is a cynic's remark, and a true one in this book. He then plies Boulatruelle with wine to find a fortune.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`91-3`,chapter:91,kind:`question`,title:`A small square chest`,text:`Boulatruelle saw a strong man he knew 'from the galleys' carry something square into the thicket by moonlight and come out with only a pick and shovel. Why does Hugo hand this story to a drunken road-mender, of all witnesses?`,themes:[]},{id:`92-1`,chapter:92,kind:`context`,title:`The Spanish war of 1823`,text:`In 1823 France sent an army under the Duc d'Angoulême to restore the absolute King of Spain against Spanish liberals. Hugo calls it a family affair of the Bourbons with little blood and no honour. He says the revolution of 1830 had its germ in it.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`92-2`,chapter:92,kind:`theme`,title:`Shots fired for nothing`,text:`Hugo counts the useless salute guns of the civilised world, a hundred and fifty thousand shots a day, while the poor starve. It is one of his abrupt swerves from a ship's arrival into a sermon on waste.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`92-3`,chapter:92,kind:`craft`,title:`A rescue seen from the quay`,text:`The crowd watches a sailor dangle from a footrope and then a white-haired convict run up the rigging like a cat. Hugo tells the whole scene from the onlookers' viewpoint, as he did with the cart in Montreuil. A gust takes the convict's cap and shows his white hair.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`92-4`,chapter:92,kind:`motif`,title:`Pardon for that man`,text:`The crowd cries 'Pardon for that man!' and old prison sergeants weep. Then he falls between the ships and is not found. The newspaper closes the matter with a number and a name, as the papers closed his trial two chapters earlier.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`93-1`,chapter:93,kind:`craft`,title:`A village and its water`,text:`Hugo opens Cosette's story with a practical problem: the church end of Montfermeil has only one spring, a quarter of an hour away, and the water-carrier stops at dusk. Geography becomes a child's terror. Notice how the whole book's suffering is built from such small, concrete facts.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`93-2`,chapter:93,kind:`motif`,title:`Under the table again`,text:`Christmas Eve: carters drink, a roast turns, the Thénardier girls laugh in the next room, and Cosette knits their stockings on the crossbar under the table, a whip on a nail by the chimney. Hugo arranges the room so that warmth and cruelty sit side by side.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`93-3`,chapter:93,kind:`theme`,title:`A mother who loves some of her children`,text:`From the dark comes the crying of a three-year-old boy whom Madame Thénardier nursed but does not love. Her motherhood stops at her daughters. Hugo adds one more child to the house to show the limits of her love.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`94-1`,chapter:94,kind:`craft`,title:`Two portraits side by side`,text:`She is huge, bearded and violent; he is small, sickly-looking and polite. Hugo builds them as a pair of opposites, an elephant and a polecat. Everyone thinks the wife is the master, but a word from the frail husband rules her.`,themes:[]},{id:`94-2`,chapter:94,kind:`theme`,title:`A trifle exaggerated`,text:`Thénardier tells customers he saved a wounded general at Waterloo under grapeshot. Hugo says the reader already knows what that prowess was, and may notice he exaggerates 'a trifle'. The inn's sign is built on a robbery retold as heroism.`,themes:[`history`,`names`]},{id:`94-3`,chapter:94,kind:`motif`,title:`A fly in a spider's web`,text:`The tavern is a web and Cosette lies trembling in it, ground in a mill and torn with pincers. The wife's method is blows; the husband's is to let her go barefoot in winter. The child says nothing.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`95-1`,chapter:95,kind:`craft`,title:`A child's private prayer`,text:`Cosette reflects 'with the gloomy air of an old woman' and prays silently that nobody will need water. When the cistern shows only a trickle she stops breathing. Hugo puts us inside an eight-year-old's arithmetic of dread.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`95-2`,chapter:95,kind:`motif`,title:`Mademoiselle Dog-lack-name`,text:`Madame Thénardier calls her 'that other beast', 'Mademoiselle Dog-lack-name', 'Mam'selle Toad'. Cosette is never addressed by her name in this chapter, as if she had no right to one.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`95-3`,chapter:95,kind:`motif`,title:`A bucket she could sit in`,text:`The empty bucket is bigger than she is; she could have sat in it. Hugo gives the burden a size before she lifts it, so the reader feels the walk ahead.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`96-1`,chapter:96,kind:`motif`,title:`The lady`,text:`Opposite the tavern door sits a two-foot doll in pink crepe with real hair, which Cosette calls 'the lady'. She measures the abyss between herself and it and decides one must be a queen to own such a thing. The doll is the measure of everything she lacks.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`96-2`,chapter:96,kind:`craft`,title:`Lights with no stars`,text:`The booths blaze for midnight mass, and Hugo notes there is not a star in the sky. All the light in the chapter is man-made and for others. Cosette steps out of it into the dark with her pail.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`97-1`,chapter:97,kind:`theme`,title:`Forests are apocalypses`,text:`Hugo describes the dread of a forest at night: grasses like eels, nettles like clawed arms, a red setting planet like a wound. For a child it is worse than fear. Darkness here is pure terror.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`97-2`,chapter:97,kind:`craft`,title:`Counting to ten`,text:`Paralysed by the night, Cosette counts aloud to ten, over and over, and it brings her back to herself. It is a small, exact detail of how children cope. Hugo trusts it more than any description of courage.`,themes:[]},{id:`97-3`,chapter:97,kind:`motif`,title:`The coin in the spring`,text:`Her apron pocket empties into the water and the fifteen-sou piece sinks without her noticing. Hugo tells us and does not tell her. We carry the dread of what will happen when she reaches home.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`97-4`,chapter:97,kind:`theme`,title:`A hand lifts the weight`,text:`'O my God! my God!' At that moment the bucket weighs nothing; an enormous hand has taken the handle. Hugo says there are instincts for all the encounters of life. 'The child is not afraid.' The chapter ends on the first help she has ever received.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`parenthood`]},{id:`98-1`,chapter:98,kind:`craft`,title:`Back up the same afternoon`,text:`Hugo leaves Cosette with the stranger and rewinds to that afternoon in Paris to follow the man's day. We see the stranger from outside, through what witnesses noticed.`,themes:[]},{id:`98-2`,chapter:98,kind:`context`,title:`The government in a carriage`,text:`Louis XVIII drove to Choisy almost every day at two, and the poor women of the quarter set their clocks by him. 'That big fellow yonder is the government.' A fat king who could not walk loved to gallop. The stranger hides from the cavalcade.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`98-3`,chapter:98,kind:`motif`,title:`Stones and a zinc band`,text:`In the forest the stranger finds a heap of white stones, touches the warts on an ash and a band of zinc on a chestnut, and treads the ground to check it has not been dug. Hugo describes the gestures exactly and explains none of them. The chapter's title points back to Boulatruelle.`,themes:[]},{id:`98-4`,chapter:98,kind:`question`,title:`Who is the man in the yellow coat?`,text:`Poor but scrupulously clean, white-haired but strong in his stride, avoiding a king's carriage and shaking off a policeman, reading a poster for a play called The Two Convicts. Hugo gives many clues and no name. What do you make of him?`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`99-1`,chapter:99,kind:`craft`,title:`Questions in the dark`,text:`The stranger asks short questions and Cosette answers simply: eight years old, no mother, works all day. When she says her name he 'seems to receive an electric shock'. Hugo keeps his face hidden in the dark and shows only that the name means something to him.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`99-2`,chapter:99,kind:`theme`,title:`Other people have mothers`,text:`'Other people have mothers. I have none.' She thinks she never had one. A child who has been told nothing about Fantine has filled the gap with nothing at all.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`99-3`,chapter:99,kind:`motif`,title:`A lead sword`,text:`Her only toy is a little lead sword no longer than her finger, which cuts salad and the heads of flies. Set against Ponine and Zelma's dolls 'full of gold', it says everything about who is loved in that house.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`100-1`,chapter:100,kind:`theme`,title:`A poor man who may be rich`,text:`The Thénardiers read the stranger's coat and charge him double. Then he pays five francs for stockings, twenty sous for a lost coin, and buys the grandest doll in the village. Their contempt turns to greed and then to hatred. Money is the only language in which they can see him.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`100-2`,chapter:100,kind:`theme`,title:`Now I own your work; play`,text:`He buys the stockings she is knitting so that she has the right to stop. 'Now I own your work; play, my child.' He frees her by paying for her time, the only thing this household would recognise.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`100-3`,chapter:100,kind:`motif`,title:`Catherine`,text:`Cosette retreats from the doll 'as if from the sun', asks permission twice, and names it Catherine. Hugo calls the doll the first form of motherhood. Earlier she swaddled a lead sword; now she has something to love.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`100-4`,chapter:100,kind:`motif`,title:`The wooden shoe`,text:`On the hearth sit the daughters' dainty shoes, each with ten sous from their mother, and in the darkest corner Cosette's muddy sabot, placed there 'with the trust of a child who has never been anything but disappointed'. The stranger puts a gold louis in it. The Christmas custom becomes a quiet act of justice.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`100-5`,chapter:100,kind:`craft`,title:`'My mother is dead!'`,text:`Madame Thénardier tells the stranger the mother 'didn't amount to much' and must be dead. Under the table Cosette rocks her sword-baby and sings 'My mother is dead!' Hugo lets the child turn the slander into a lullaby without understanding it.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`101-1`,chapter:101,kind:`craft`,title:`A bill as a character sketch`,text:`Supper three francs, room ten, candle five, fire four, 'servisse' one. Thénardier's invented bill, with its spelling mistake, is a portrait in numbers. His wife watches him write it 'in religious admiration'.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`101-2`,chapter:101,kind:`theme`,title:`Selling a child`,text:`The wife would give Cosette away for nothing. The husband suddenly 'adores' her, mentions four hundred francs of medicine and the need for a passport, and then names his price: fifteen hundred francs. The scene is a market, and the stranger refuses to haggle.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`101-3`,chapter:101,kind:`motif`,title:`Mourning clothes`,text:`The stranger's bundle holds a complete outfit for a girl of seven, all in black. Cosette walks out of Montfermeil in mourning with a pink doll in her arms. Hugo leaves it to the reader to wonder for whom she is in mourning, and how he knew to bring it.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`101-4`,chapter:101,kind:`theme`,title:`Not alone`,text:`For the first time in the five years she can remember, Cosette feels that she is not alone. She walks gravely, looking at the sky, and feels as if she were beside the good God. Hugo keeps her joy quiet and serious.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`102-1`,chapter:102,kind:`craft`,title:`Is that all?`,text:`For the first time in their marriage his wife criticises Thénardier, and the greed she stirs sends him running after the pair. The chapter title is a proverb: he who seeks to better himself may make things worse. Comedy and menace run together.`,themes:[]},{id:`102-2`,chapter:102,kind:`motif`,title:`Fantine's note`,text:`Thénardier demands a paper signed by the mother, and the stranger hands him exactly that: Fantine's note ordering Cosette delivered to the bearer. Thénardier mutters that the signature is 'fairly well imitated' and lets it go.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`102-3`,chapter:102,kind:`craft`,title:`The accounts recited`,text:`The stranger reckons Thénardier's dealings to the sou: a hundred and twenty owed, three hundred and three hundred paid, nine months at fifteen francs. Thénardier has been overpaid. The man's exact knowledge is the trap's 'steel jaw', and Thénardier cannot imagine how he has it.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`102-4`,chapter:102,kind:`theme`,title:`A shopkeeper with a taint of the monster`,text:`Hugo calls Thénardier a double nature: in calm times a respectable tradesman, given the right shock a blackguard. He regrets twice not bringing his gun. What stops him is the size of a cudgel and a look. His wickedness is real, and it is also cowardly.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`103-1`,chapter:103,kind:`craft`,title:`Jean Valjean was not dead`,text:`Hugo opens with the flat statement and then compresses months of flight into a paragraph: a swim underwater, a moored boat, a country house that clothes escaped convicts, a 'mole's track' through southern France. After chapters of 'the stranger', the name returns in the first line.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`103-2`,chapter:103,kind:`theme`,title:`As if he really had died`,text:`Reading his own drowning in a Paris paper, he feels reassured and almost at peace, as if he had really died. Being officially dead is a new kind of mask. His old name now belongs to a corpse in the harbour.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`103-3`,chapter:103,kind:`motif`,title:`Won in the lottery`,text:`The title says Cosette 'wins' number 9,430 in the lottery, echoing Hugo's description of Waterloo as a winning number. The chapter ends with the child asleep on his back, still holding Catherine. The convict's number has become a little girl's good luck.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`104-1`,chapter:104,kind:`context`,title:`The edge of Paris`,text:`The Gorbeau house stands where the city frays into fields, near the Salpêtrière hospital and Bicêtre prison, on the road the condemned once took to the scaffold. Hugo places Valjean's refuge between a hospital, a prison and an execution ground. Grass grows in the ruts.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`104-2`,chapter:104,kind:`theme`,title:`The Raven and the Fox`,text:`Two attorneys named Corbeau and Renard were so mocked with La Fontaine jokes that the king let them change their names to Gorbeau and Prenard. Hugo tells the story of the building's name with relish. Even a slum's name has been altered to escape ridicule.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`104-3`,chapter:104,kind:`craft`,title:`A building that hides its size`,text:`From the road it looks like a hovel; inside it is enormous, a long corridor of rooms like stalls. Only one door and one window face the street. Hugo describes a house built for not being seen.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`105-1`,chapter:105,kind:`motif`,title:`Two kissed hands`,text:`He kisses the sleeping child's hand, as nine months earlier he kissed the hand of her sleeping mother. Hugo makes the link plainly. The gesture of farewell to Fantine becomes a gesture of welcome to Cosette.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`105-2`,chapter:105,kind:`craft`,title:`Yes, madame, here I am!`,text:`Woken by a passing cart, Cosette cries 'Yes, madame, here I am!' and gropes for her broom. Then she sees him smile. One reflex sums up five years of servitude, and one question undoes it: must she sweep? No; she must play.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`105-3`,chapter:105,kind:`theme`,title:`A frightful hole that is pretty`,text:`She declares the bare attic pretty because she feels free. Hugo's chapter title calls them an owl and a warbler sharing one nest. Happiness here has nothing to do with comfort.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`106-1`,chapter:106,kind:`theme`,title:`The dawn of love`,text:`At fifty-five Valjean has never been a father, a lover, a husband or a friend. All his dammed-up affection rushes towards the child. Hugo says the Bishop brought him the dawn of virtue and Cosette brings the dawn of love. She calls him father and knows no other name for him.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`106-2`,chapter:106,kind:`theme`,title:`Reading, for a better reason`,text:`He teaches Cosette to read, remembering that he learned in prison with evil intentions. The skill sharpened for hatred now ends in teaching a child. Hugo marks the reversal quietly.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`106-3`,chapter:106,kind:`question`,title:`Who supports whom?`,text:`Hugo suggests Valjean, sent back to prison for doing right, may have needed this love to keep going in virtue: he is her support and she is his prop. Is his goodness now resting on her?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`107-1`,chapter:107,kind:`motif`,title:`The beggar who gives alms`,text:`In his old yellow coat he is taken for a poor man and accepts a sou with a bow, then slips silver to the next beggar. The neighbourhood's name for him captures the contradiction. His disguise is so good that it attracts attention.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`107-2`,chapter:107,kind:`craft`,title:`The portress at the crack`,text:`The deaf, talkative old woman spies through a crack in the door and fingers the coat on its nail: thicknesses of paper, needles, a big knife, several wigs. Hugo lets a minor busybody gather evidence like a detective. Gossip is how this book's secrets leak.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`108-1`,chapter:108,kind:`craft`,title:`A face under a lamp`,text:`The old beggar by the well looks up under a newly lit street lamp, and for an instant Valjean sees a terrible face. The next evening it is only the ex-beadle again. Hugo gives us the same doubt Valjean has, and lets him laugh it off.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`108-2`,chapter:108,kind:`craft`,title:`A light at the keyhole`,text:`A heavy step on the stairs, then a candle at the keyhole, then the light retreating with no sound of shoes. At dawn a tall back at the top of the stairs. Hugo builds the recognition from sound, light and silhouette, never a face.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`108-3`,chapter:108,kind:`motif`,title:`A five-franc piece rolls`,text:`Valjean rolls up his money, and a coin slips and rattles across the floor. The title makes the small noise the event of the chapter. In a house with someone listening, a dropped coin is loud.`,themes:[]},{id:`109-1`,chapter:109,kind:`theme`,title:`A city that no longer exists`,text:`Hugo asks leave to describe the Paris of his youth in the present tense, though much of it has been rebuilt during his long exile. The chase that follows runs through streets that have vanished. He maps them from memory, like a lost city.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`109-2`,chapter:109,kind:`craft`,title:`The dark side of the street`,text:`Under a full moon Valjean keeps to the shadowed side and watches the lit side, doubling back like a hunted stag. Light is danger now; darkness is safety. The moon that lit the Bishop's face now exposes the fugitive.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`109-3`,chapter:109,kind:`motif`,title:`Cosette asks nothing`,text:`She walks on without a question. Hugo says her early suffering has made her passive, and also that she feels safe with him. Both are true, and the chase is quieter for it.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`110-1`,chapter:110,kind:`craft`,title:`A toll of two sous`,text:`At the Pont d'Austerlitz the toll-keeper demands two sous because the child he carries can walk. Valjean pays, vexed at being noticed. Hugo makes the escape hinge on a petty regulation and a fugitive's horror of being remembered.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`110-2`,chapter:110,kind:`context`,title:`A bridge named for a victory`,text:`The bridge bears the name of Napoleon's victory of 1805. The chapter title jokes that it was lucky it carried carriages, since Valjean crosses hidden in a cart's shadow. Paris is full of the Empire's names, worn into everyday streets.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`]},{id:`111-1`,chapter:111,kind:`context`,title:`The plan of 1727`,text:`The Petit-Picpus quarter, Hugo says, survives only on an old map: walls, gardens, convents, timber-yards, more like a Spanish monastery town than a suburb. He lays out its streets as a Y and then a trap. He is describing a maze that has since vanished.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`111-2`,chapter:111,kind:`motif`,title:`A black statue at the corner`,text:`A blind alley on one side, a motionless man barring the other. The net tightens with geometry. Valjean looks up at the sky in despair. Hugo has turned a street map into a cage.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`112-1`,chapter:112,kind:`craft`,title:`A door that is not a door`,text:`The huge rotten door with its rusty bands looks easy to break, but behind the planks is solid stone. It is a wooden facing on a wall. Hugo spends a chapter testing every way out and closing it. The reader feels the trap through its details.`,themes:[]},{id:`112-2`,chapter:112,kind:`motif`,title:`The niche`,text:`A deep niche hides anyone inside it from both streets. It is the one safe spot in the moonlit square. Hugo makes the hiding place as exact as the danger.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`113-1`,chapter:113,kind:`craft`,title:`The lantern rope`,text:`In those days street lanterns were raised and lowered on ropes kept in locked iron boxes, and on moonlit nights they were not lit. Valjean forces a box and takes the rope. The chapter title notes that this would be impossible with gas lamps. Hugo turns a forgotten piece of city equipment into a means of escape.`,themes:[`paris`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`113-2`,chapter:113,kind:`theme`,title:`It is Madame Thénardier`,text:`To keep Cosette silent he tells her the people coming are Madame Thénardier. It is a lie that works because her terror is real. The worst fear in her life becomes the tool that saves it.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`113-3`,chapter:113,kind:`craft`,title:`Old skills`,text:`Hugo reminds us that Valjean learned at Toulon to climb the inner angle of two walls with back and knees. The convict's craft carries the child over. The galleys trained the hands that now rescue her.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`114-1`,chapter:114,kind:`motif`,title:`A hymn in an empty house`,text:`From the dark, barred building rises a hymn sung by women's voices. Valjean and Cosette, not knowing where they are, kneel. While it lasts he sees not night but a blue sky. Sound replaces light as the sign of grace.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`114-2`,chapter:114,kind:`craft`,title:`Outside and inside`,text:`On one side of the wall: gunstocks on stones and Javert calling his spies. On the other: moss, melon frames and a silence so deep the noise hardly matters. Hugo builds the garden as the opposite of the street.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`115-1`,chapter:115,kind:`motif`,title:`A form on the floor`,text:`Through a lit window he sees a shape in a winding-sheet lying face down, arms stretched in a cross, with what looks like a rope at its neck. It never moves. Hugo refuses to explain. The house that sang like angels now shows 'the gates of the tomb'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`115-2`,chapter:115,kind:`question`,title:`What is this house?`,text:`Angels' voices, barred windows, a shrouded figure, no light anywhere else. Valjean touches the stones to be sure he is not dreaming. What kind of place do these clues suggest to you?`,themes:[]},{id:`116-1`,chapter:116,kind:`theme`,title:`Nothing but her`,text:`Sitting by the sleeping child, Valjean sees that while she is with him he needs nothing and fears nothing except for her. He does not notice the cold without his coat. Fear for her replaces fear for himself.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`116-2`,chapter:116,kind:`motif`,title:`A man belled like a ram`,text:`A limping figure moves among the melon frames, and a bell sounds with every step. Hugo makes the man strange before making him known. Hugo leaves the bell unexplained.`,themes:[]},{id:`116-3`,chapter:116,kind:`craft`,title:`The cold that kills`,text:`Her hands are icy and she will not wake. All his fears vanish into one: she must be by a fire within a quarter of an hour. Hugo makes the real danger a winter night, not the police.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`117-1`,chapter:117,kind:`theme`,title:`Mercy returned`,text:`'Father Fauchelevent, I saved your life.' The old man he pulled from under a cart, and found work for here, now offers him shelter. Two acts of kindness in Montreuil come back as a hiding place in Paris. Fauchelevent agrees to ask nothing.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`117-2`,chapter:117,kind:`motif`,title:`Why the bell`,text:`The bell on the gardener's knee is so that he may be avoided: the house holds only women and girls, and when he comes they go. The mysterious sound turns out to be a rule of the place. The garden is a convent, the Petit-Picpus.`,themes:[]},{id:`117-3`,chapter:117,kind:`motif`,title:`Father Madeleine`,text:`The first words the gardener says are 'So it is you, Father Madeleine!' A name Valjean thought buried at Montreuil is spoken in a Paris garden. In this book old names find their way back.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`118-1`,chapter:118,kind:`craft`,title:`The same chase, from the other side`,text:`Hugo retells the pursuit from Javert's point of view: the newspaper, the report from Montfermeil, Thénardier's invented 'grandfather', the ex-beadle's rags. We learn the hunter was doubting all along. The double telling turns suspense into understanding.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`118-2`,chapter:118,kind:`theme`,title:`Scrupulous to a fault`,text:`Valjean's death was official, so Javert would not arrest on a doubt. He also wanted to unveil his masterpiece suddenly, and he enjoyed letting the fly run. Hugo lists his blunders: hesitation, pride, and playing cat and mouse. The law's servant has an artist's vanity.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`118-3`,chapter:118,kind:`motif`,title:`A pinch of snuff`,text:`Sure of his prey, Javert takes a pinch of snuff, the indulgence he allows himself when pleased with himself. A few minutes afterwards the net is empty. Hugo uses the small habit from Javert's first portrait to mark his overconfidence.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`118-4`,chapter:118,kind:`context`,title:`Thénardier's 'grandfather'`,text:`Wary of drawing the law's eye to his own affairs, Thénardier silences his wife and invents a respectable farmer called Guillaume Lambert. A liar protects the fugitive purely to protect himself. Javert goes home calling himself a ninny.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`119-1`,chapter:119,kind:`craft`,title:`Leaving the hero over the wall`,text:`Valjean and Cosette are hidden in a gardener's hut, and Hugo walks away from them to come back in by the front door as a visitor would. Book Fourteen is a guided tour of the house they have fallen into. He makes us learn the convent the slow way, password by password.`,themes:[]},{id:`119-2`,chapter:119,kind:`motif`,title:`A voice behind shutters`,text:`Grating, tin, a letter slot, then shutters that never open, and a voice saying 'I am here. What do you wish with me?' At best you see a mouth and chin under a veil. The parlour has a window on the world's side and none on the convent's. Everything is built for not being seen.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`119-3`,chapter:119,kind:`craft`,title:`Things never described`,text:`Hugo announces that he will take the reader inside and tell what storytellers have never told. He presents the digression as a scoop. The reader, who has just heard the hymn and seen the figure on the floor, now gets the explanation the hero lacks.`,themes:[]},{id:`120-1`,chapter:120,kind:`context`,title:`The post`,text:`Each nun in turn makes 'reparation': twelve hours on her knees before the Holy Sacrament with a rope around her neck, lying in a cross when exhausted, praying for the sins of the world. Because there is a post with a candle, they call it 'being at the post'. The reader now knows what Valjean saw through the window.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`120-2`,chapter:120,kind:`theme`,title:`A rule harsher than the galleys'`,text:`Fasting all year, rising in the night, straw beds, no fire, weekly scourging, silence, a fever-giving drugget shirt for half the year. Hugo lists the rule as coolly as he listed Valjean's sentences. Between 1825 and 1830 three of these nuns went mad.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`120-3`,chapter:120,kind:`motif`,title:`Our, never my`,text:`The nuns never say 'my', only 'our'. Greetings are answered with 'Forever'. Mothers take names like Nativity or Passion. A whole language has been rebuilt to erase the self.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`121-1`,chapter:121,kind:`motif`,title:`A funeral for the living`,text:`At her profession the novice, crowned with white roses, lies down under a black veil while the office of the dead is sung. One file chants 'Our sister is dead', the other 'Our sister is alive in Jesus Christ'. Entering the convent is staged as a death.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`121-2`,chapter:121,kind:`theme`,title:`Mothers behind bars`,text:`The boarding-school girls see even their mothers only through the parlour grating. A child weeps to kiss her three-year-old sister and is refused even the little hand through the bars. Hugo lets one small story stand for the cost of the rule to families.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`122-1`,chapter:122,kind:`craft`,title:`Collected sayings`,text:`Hugo fills a chapter with children's remarks he gathered: a five-year-old glad to have 'only nine years and ten months' left, a seven-year-old confessing to adultery, a foundling saying her mother 'was not there when she was born'. The gloomy house is suddenly full of voices.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`122-2`,chapter:122,kind:`theme`,title:`Severe only to themselves`,text:`The nuns watch the laughter from a distance and are gentle with the children, severe only toward themselves. Hugo says Homer would have come there to laugh with Perrault. Childhood survives inside the strictest walls.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`122-3`,chapter:122,kind:`motif`,title:`Spiders and woodlice`,text:`The refectory's four corners are Spider, Caterpillar, Woodlouse and Cricket, and each girl belongs to the 'nation' of her corner. When the Archbishop asks who a pretty girl is, he is told she is a spider. The children make their own map of the house.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`123-1`,chapter:123,kind:`craft`,title:`Anecdotes that seem to go nowhere`,text:`A wager with the Archbishop, a madwoman who cries 'Ah! Auguste!' at a sermon, a flute that turns out to be a blind old émigré. Hugo even says Madame Albertine is unconnected with his story. He is building the convent as a world with its own gossip and mysteries.`,themes:[]},{id:`123-2`,chapter:123,kind:`motif`,title:`The flute in the Rue Droit-Mur`,text:`For months the girls fall in love with an unseen flute player, and two climb on a roof at the risk of their lives to see him. He is old, blind and penniless. Desire inside the walls invents its object from a sound.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`123-3`,chapter:123,kind:`theme`,title:`Passing for dead`,text:`Madame Albertine is said to 'pass for dead' in the world, her supposed death serving some great marriage settlement. In this convent a living woman can be officially dead. Hugo lets the idea hang without comment.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`124-1`,chapter:124,kind:`context`,title:`Refugees of the Revolution`,text:`The Little Convent shelters old nuns of orders the Revolution destroyed, each keeping her own rule in black, grey or white. The last nun of Sainte-Aure, too poor to wear her habit, dresses a doll in it. The doll outlives the order.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`124-2`,chapter:124,kind:`craft`,title:`Madame de Genlis`,text:`The famous novelist Madame de Genlis is let in after declaring that she detests novels, and leaves complaining the garden has no shade. Hugo enjoys the joke against a fellow writer. Real people mixed with invented ones make the convent feel documented.`,themes:[]},{id:`124-3`,chapter:124,kind:`motif`,title:`A church that hides its choir`,text:`The public may enter the church, but the nuns sit in a dark hall folded back behind a curtain. Outsiders know they are present only by the clatter of the stall seats. Even shared space is arranged so no face is seen.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`125-1`,chapter:125,kind:`theme`,title:`Speech passes to things`,text:`Because the people are silent, the bells speak: one-and-one for the prioress, six-five for lessons, nine strokes for the Archbishop, and the gardener's bell so he can be avoided. Hugo describes a society that communicates in code. Notice that Valjean has already heard two of these bells without understanding them.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`125-2`,chapter:125,kind:`craft`,title:`A merry prioress`,text:`Mother Innocente sings 'like a cracked pot', knows Latin, Greek and Hebrew, and is the only cheerful woman in the house, adored for it. Hugo gives the place's authority a warm, learned face. A character named in passing among silhouettes stands out.`,themes:[]},{id:`125-3`,chapter:125,kind:`motif`,title:`Carefully chosen men`,text:`Besides the archbishop and the gardener, the girls see two men: an old ugly chaplain and a 'frightful old hunchback' of a drawing-master. Hugo adds dryly that all these men were carefully chosen.`,themes:[]},{id:`126-1`,chapter:126,kind:`motif`,title:`A gibbet laid flat`,text:`Seen from above, the main building traces a gibbet lying on its side. The garden radiates from a central fir like a cross on a wheel. Hugo gives the convent's plan the shapes of execution and faith together.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`126-2`,chapter:126,kind:`context`,title:`Eleven thousand devils`,text:`The convent stands on the site of a medieval tennis-ground nicknamed 'the eleven thousand devils', and the Rue Droit-Mur was once the Rue des Églantiers, Wild Rose Street. 'God opened flowers before man cut stones.' Paris is layers of older names.`,themes:[`paris`,`names`]},{id:`126-3`,chapter:126,kind:`craft`,title:`Walls higher inside`,text:`The garden is sunk below the street, so the walls are far higher from inside than outside. The reader now understands the geography of Valjean's climb and leap. Hugo supplies the architecture after the escape.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`127-1`,chapter:127,kind:`craft`,title:`One more digression`,text:`Hugo begs leave for yet another digression to show the cloister has its originals: a hundred-year-old nun who drops pre-Revolution names and describes the 'four wines' of drunkenness. He is enjoying himself, and he lets the reader see it.`,themes:[]},{id:`127-2`,chapter:127,kind:`motif`,title:`The locked treasure`,text:`The old nun keeps something in a locked cupboard and contemplates it alone; the convent imagines relics. After her death they find a comic Faenza platter of Cupids chased by apothecary boys with syringes. 'Love conquered by the colic.' The holy secret is a bawdy joke.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`127-3`,chapter:127,kind:`question`,title:`The parlour is too gloomy`,text:`She refused all visitors because 'the parlour is too gloomy'. Is she a comic figure, or the one person in the house who kept her own sense of the world?`,themes:[]},{id:`128-1`,chapter:128,kind:`context`,title:`Two orders, one devotion`,text:`Hugo distinguishes the Benedictines of the Perpetual Adoration, founded after church sacrileges in 1649, from the Bernardines of the Petit-Picpus, who were allowed the same adoration in 1657. The precision is almost pedantic. It shows him writing as a historian of institutions as well as a novelist.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`128-2`,chapter:128,kind:`craft`,title:`A friendlier parlour`,text:`At the Temple convent the shutters are brown curtains and the parlour has muslin, bouquets and even the head of a Turk. Hugo uses the comparison to say the Petit-Picpus's darkness is a local trait, not the whole Church.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`129-1`,chapter:129,kind:`theme`,title:`A burden that does not shrink`,text:`A hundred nuns forty years earlier, twenty-eight fifteen years earlier, a handful by 1845: the rule stays the same for the few as for the many, and it crushes them. Two died in Hugo's Paris years, aged twenty-five and twenty-three. Decline is described without triumph.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`129-2`,chapter:129,kind:`theme`,title:`Respect without belief`,text:`Hugo says he has entered this house 'for the profit, perhaps, of the melancholy history of Jean Valjean', understanding not everything and insulting nothing. He places himself between Joseph de Maistre, who sanctified the executioner, and Voltaire, who sneered at the cross. The digression is announced as part of the hero's story.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`129-3`,chapter:129,kind:`motif`,title:`The past in a mask`,text:`The past counterfeits itself and calls itself the future, Hugo warns. Convents pose a problem: civilisation condemns them, liberty protects them. He leaves the question in a single tension.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`130-1`,chapter:130,kind:`theme`,title:`The Infinite as the main character`,text:`In a hundred and seventy-eight words Hugo makes a startling claim: this book is a drama whose chief personage is the Infinite, and man the second. That is why he must stop at a convent. Every place of worship is an instrument for looking at the Infinite.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`130-2`,chapter:130,kind:`craft`,title:`Why a Parenthesis here`,text:`Book Fifteen is titled 'Parenthesis'. Hugo pauses the story with the hero hidden in a convent garden, a natural moment to ask what a convent is. He signals clearly that this is commentary, so a reader can tell digression from narrative.`,themes:[]},{id:`131-1`,chapter:131,kind:`theme`,title:`Mistletoe on an oak`,text:`Judged by history, monasticism is a parasite: useful when it tamed brutal ages, detestable in a grown nation, gnawing Italy and Spain to the bone. Hugo, who has just described the Petit-Picpus gently, now condemns the institution in general.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`131-2`,chapter:131,kind:`context`,title:`The in pace at Villers`,text:`To those who call such accounts 'declamation', Hugo answers with a place he saw himself near Brussels: the abbey dungeons with an iron collar on the wall and a stone box too short to lie in and too low to stand in. 'This exists; it can be touched.' Facts, as at Hougomont, are his weapon.`,themes:[`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`132-1`,chapter:132,kind:`theme`,title:`Respect the past if it consents to be dead`,text:`Hugo states his rule: respect and spare the past where it is willing to be dead; fight it when it insists on living. A convent in the Paris of 1789, 1830 and 1848 is a college of owls facing the light. In ordinary times one need only make it say its date.`,themes:[`revolution`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`132-2`,chapter:132,kind:`craft`,title:`A chorus of old things`,text:`Hugo imagines the old garment, the stale fish, the faded perfume and the corpse each pleading 'I served you once', and answers 'In former days'. The comic list does the work of an argument. Dead institutions are made to sound like leftovers.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`132-3`,chapter:132,kind:`theme`,title:`Truth never exaggerates`,text:`Even while calling for war on the past, Hugo insists on distinctions: some things must be destroyed and some only examined. He ends by asking leave to look at the religious question 'fixedly'. The fighter checks himself.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`133-1`,chapter:133,kind:`theme`,title:`Equality and fraternity in a cloister`,text:`Considered in principle, a monastery realises what the Revolution preached: the rich man becomes poor, lord equals peasant, all share the same bread and straw, and they elect those they obey. Hugo says liberty would turn it into a republic. The convent becomes, for a moment, a model commune.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`133-2`,chapter:133,kind:`question`,title:`What are they doing?`,text:`The chapter ends on a question: these people behind four walls gaze on the darkness, kneel and clasp their hands. What does this signify? Hugo poses it as a genuine puzzle before answering it himself.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`134-1`,chapter:134,kind:`theme`,title:`Two infinities`,text:`Hugo's answer: there is an infinite above us and an infinite within us, and if both are intelligent, the one below is the soul and the one above is God. To bring them into contact by thought is to pray. It is a philosopher's definition of prayer, not a priest's.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`conscience`]},{id:`134-2`,chapter:134,kind:`motif`,title:`Clearing God of caterpillars`,text:`Crush fanaticism, venerate the infinite, strip superstition from religion: 'clear God of caterpillars'. Hugo treats belief like a garden to be weeded. The image fits a book set, at this moment, in a gardener's hut.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`134-3`,chapter:134,kind:`theme`,title:`The right of the soul`,text:`Beside the rights of man, Hugo says, stands the right of the soul. Democracy's greatness is to deny nothing of humanity, including its reaching toward the unknown. The revolutionary and the believer in him are made to agree.`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`]},{id:`135-1`,chapter:135,kind:`theme`,title:`Every sincere prayer is good`,text:`'Turn your book upside down and be in the infinite.' Hugo's religion is generous about forms and strict about sincerity. He compares a philosophy that denies the infinite to one that denies the sun.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`135-2`,chapter:135,kind:`craft`,title:`Arguing with nihilism`,text:`The nihilist doubts even the person he is talking to, Hugo says, yet by saying the word 'mind' admits what he denies. To 'No' the only reply is 'Yes.' Man lives by affirmation even more than by bread. The argument is quick and rhetorical, not technical.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`135-3`,chapter:135,kind:`theme`,title:`Faith and love as engines`,text:`Philosophy should improve man's condition, turning Eden into a school and wisdom into communion. Faith and love are the two motors of progress, and the ideal is God. Hugo ties the convent question back to his belief in progress.`,themes:[`revolution`,`love`]},{id:`136-1`,chapter:136,kind:`theme`,title:`Frown, but never sneer`,text:`A convent is a contradiction: supreme egoism yielding supreme self-sacrifice, a bill drawn on death. Mockery is not allowed on such a subject. The just man may frown; he may not sneer. Hugo sets a rule for his own tone.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`136-2`,chapter:136,kind:`craft`,title:`A very short chapter`,text:`Two hundred and thirty-four words weigh the right to live apart against its abuses. After pages of attack and defence, Hugo compresses the dilemma into paradoxes. Brevity here is a sign of balance.`,themes:[]},{id:`137-1`,chapter:137,kind:`theme`,title:`Some must pray for those who never pray`,text:`Contemplation is labour, Hugo says; folded arms toil and clasped hands work. There must be some who pray constantly for those who never pray at all. He is for religion against religions, and for sacrifice even when wrongly aimed.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`137-2`,chapter:137,kind:`theme`,title:`Women's protest`,text:`In this century, Hugo says, woman suffers most, and her cloistered exile is a kind of protest. The line links the nuns to Fantine's story without naming her. Suffering in his book falls hardest on women.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`137-3`,chapter:137,kind:`craft`,title:`Tender, religious terror`,text:`Hugo ends the Parenthesis in the first person: he does not believe what these women believe, but has never thought of them without tender, religious terror and envious pity. The digression closes on feeling rather than verdict, ready to return to the story.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`138-1`,chapter:138,kind:`craft`,title:`The enigma explained`,text:`The hymn was matins, the hall was the chapel, the shrouded form was a sister at the post, the bell was on Fauchelevent's knee. Hugo sums up in a line what Book Fourteen taught at length. The supernatural mystery of Book Thirteen dissolves into a rule of life.`,themes:[]},{id:`138-2`,chapter:138,kind:`theme`,title:`The most dangerous place is the safest`,text:`A man found inside a convent is one step from prison; yet no one would look for him in an impossible place. Valjean wants to stay. He sees a further reason when he hears there are pupils: Cosette's education provided.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`138-3`,chapter:138,kind:`theme`,title:`Saint, thief or assassin`,text:`Fauchelevent does not understand how M. Madeleine got here and guesses at bankruptcy or politics. One thing is certain: this man crawled under the cart without stopping to think. He decides to save him whatever he is. Gratitude asks no questions.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`138-4`,chapter:138,kind:`motif`,title:`Father Madeleine is buried`,text:`'Mother Crucifixion is dead, and Father Madeleine—' 'Is buried,' says Valjean, smiling sadly. He describes his own state in the convent's language of death. The joke carries a whole history of lost names.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`139-1`,chapter:139,kind:`craft`,title:`A peasant's harangue`,text:`Fauchelevent talks at length about his age, his knee and the melons before asking for his 'brother'. The prioress makes one movement at 'brother' and a reassured one at 'no longer young'. Hugo writes the scene as comedy of cunning, with the old notary's tricks intact.`,themes:[]},{id:`139-2`,chapter:139,kind:`motif`,title:`An iron bar`,text:`Instead of answering, the prioress asks whether he can find a stout iron bar by evening, 'to serve as a lever'. She leaves him there. Both of them have a request, and each is hiding its purpose.`,themes:[]},{id:`139-3`,chapter:139,kind:`context`,title:`Thought stupid, therefore valued`,text:`The convent thinks Fauchelevent stupid, which Hugo says is a great merit in religion. He knows all its bells and secrets and conceals everything. His real knowledge is protected by his reputation for having none.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`140-1`,chapter:140,kind:`theme`,title:`Forbidden by men, enjoined by God`,text:`Mother Crucifixion wished to be buried in her own coffin under the altar, which the law forbids. The prioress answers every objection with a saint or a council. Here the nuns break the law out of conscience, and Hugo lets them do it.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`140-2`,chapter:140,kind:`craft`,title:`Amen`,text:`Fauchelevent says 'Amen' whenever Latin or piety flows, and when the prioress's long-dammed silence bursts into a torrent about Saint Bernard and the police, he says it again. Hugo plays the scene as a comic duet between a learned nun and a wily peasant.`,themes:[]},{id:`140-3`,chapter:140,kind:`motif`,title:`Earth, the same thing as man`,text:`He promises to fill the empty official coffin with earth. 'Earth, that is the same thing as man,' she says, satisfied. A pious commonplace solves a practical problem. The coffin question is now on the table.`,themes:[]},{id:`141-1`,chapter:141,kind:`craft`,title:`Everything arranged, and nothing`,text:`The prioress will admit the brother and niece, but the brother must first be outside, and he cannot get out. Then the empty coffin needs filling. Hugo poses two problems and lets Valjean solve them with one word: 'Me!'`,themes:[]},{id:`141-2`,chapter:141,kind:`theme`,title:`A convict's talent`,text:`Hugo notes that enduring a box is one of Valjean's gloomy convict skills, and that a monk wrote of Charles V using the same device. A man making his escape, Valjean says, does not cough or sneeze. The galleys trained him for this.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`141-3`,chapter:141,kind:`motif`,title:`Buried in white`,text:`The basket he asked for will be of pine, and the cover a cloth, white, since nuns are buried in white. Valjean plans his own burial calmly. To enter the house he must first pass for dead.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`142-1`,chapter:142,kind:`craft`,title:`A fine farce`,text:`Fauchelevent follows the hearse rubbing his hands: 'Here's a fine farce!' Then a stranger says Mestienne is dead. 'After Napoleon, Louis XVIII. After Mestienne, Gribier.' By the end of the chapter the same words come out in consternation. Hugo turns the phrase inside out.`,themes:[]},{id:`142-2`,chapter:142,kind:`theme`,title:`Their hunger is the enemy of my thirst`,text:`Gribier was a student, writes love letters by day at a public stall and digs graves by night. He cannot drink because he has seven children who must eat. Poverty has made him sober, and his sobriety is now the danger.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`142-3`,chapter:142,kind:`context`,title:`A fault that resembles a duty`,text:`Hugo remarks that he finds the nuns' illegal burial a venial offence, 'a fault that resembles a duty'. He takes sides openly with conscience against regulation, as he did with Sister Simplice's lie.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`143-1`,chapter:143,kind:`craft`,title:`Inside the coffin`,text:`Hugo puts us in the box with Valjean: pavement then earth road, the hollow sound of the Pont d'Austerlitz, the tilt into the grave, Latin over him, holy water like rain. He hears his own funeral. Then the shovelfuls fall.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`143-2`,chapter:143,kind:`theme`,title:`Security of conscience`,text:`Valjean is perfectly calm, and Hugo says security of conscience gives security in everything else. Something of the repose of the dead enters him. His calm is a moral state, not only nerve.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`143-3`,chapter:143,kind:`motif`,title:`Requiescat in pace`,text:`'Rest in peace', 'Amen', footsteps, and 'I am alone.' Then earth on the lid like thunder, and one air hole stopped. A man with a convict's name is buried under a nun's prayer. There are things too strong for the strongest man.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`144-1`,chapter:144,kind:`craft`,title:`The card`,text:`Fauchelevent sees something white in Gribier's pocket and steals it. Then he 'kindly' points out that the gate closes in five minutes and the fine is fifteen francs. The chapter title promises the origin of a saying. Hugo's comic invention turns on a scrap of paper.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`144-2`,chapter:144,kind:`theme`,title:`Grief at the graveside`,text:`Fauchelevent opens the coffin and finds Valjean pale and still. He sobs, rails at Mestienne for dying and at himself, and wonders what will become of the little girl. Then the eyes open: 'I fell asleep.' Hugo gives the old man real grief before the relief.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`144-3`,chapter:144,kind:`motif`,title:`Returning the tools`,text:`Fauchelevent climbs to Gribier's garret, where the wife is crying and the children have been searching, and returns the shovel and the card. 'Thanks, villager! The next time I will pay.' The trick ends in a kindness to a poor family.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`145-1`,chapter:145,kind:`motif`,title:`Ultime Fauchelevent`,text:`He enters the convent under the name of Fauchelevent's dead brother, Ultime, 'the last'. Valjean does not say a word; Fauchelevent answers every question for him. Another name, borrowed from the old man he once saved from under a cart.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`145-2`,chapter:145,kind:`craft`,title:`She will grow up ugly`,text:`The prioress studies Cosette and says half aloud: 'She will grow up ugly.' Hugo says this verdict decided her admission, since plain girls are likelier to become nuns. A small, misjudging remark settles a child's future.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`145-3`,chapter:145,kind:`theme`,title:`No one keeps a secret like a child`,text:`Left with the fruit-seller, Cosette neither eats nor sleeps and answers a hundred questions with the same look. She has understood that she must 'be good' and say nothing. Her cry of joy on seeing him seems to come from an abyss.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`145-4`,chapter:145,kind:`motif`,title:`Two bells`,text:`The prioress orders a second kneecap with a bell. Next day two bells sound in the garden and the nuns lift their veils to watch two men digging. The device meant to keep the nuns away from a man now marks the safety of a hunted one.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`146-1`,chapter:146,kind:`theme`,title:`Two places of captivity`,text:`Valjean compares the convent with the galleys point by point: rising before dawn, straw beds, no fire, coarse clothes, lost names. The convicts had stolen and murdered; the nuns had done nothing. Both expiate, but the nuns expiate for others, 'the most divine of human generosities'.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`146-2`,chapter:146,kind:`theme`,title:`Humility after pride`,text:`Hugo says Valjean had begun comparing himself with ordinary men instead of with the Bishop, and pride was growing. The convent stops it. Cosette leads him back to the Bishop's lessons through love, the convent through humility.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`146-3`,chapter:146,kind:`motif`,title:`The valise that smells so good`,text:`Valjean locks the little mourning dress Cosette wore out of Montfermeil in a valise with camphor and keeps the key on him. 'Father, what is there in that box which smells so good?' He keeps the relic of their beginning as he kept the candlesticks.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`146-4`,chapter:146,kind:`craft`,title:`Two houses of God`,text:`Two houses of God have taken him in at the two crises of his life: the Bishop's when every door was shut, and this one when society hunted him again. Hugo makes the end of Volume Two rhyme with the beginning of Volume One. Then years pass, and Cosette grows up.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`146-5`,chapter:146,kind:`motif`,title:`Grafting wild trees`,text:`Once a tree-pruner at Faverolles, he gardens again and grafts the wild orchard trees until they bear good fruit. The image fits the man himself. His first trade returns at the moment he finds peace.`,themes:[`conscience`,`poverty`]},{id:`147-1`,chapter:147,kind:`craft`,title:`A type before a person`,text:`Volume III opens not with a character but with a species. Hugo spends a whole Book describing 'the gamin' in general, the way a naturalist describes the sparrow, before any particular boy appears. The pause tells you the new volume is about Paris itself and the people it breeds, not only about the characters you already know.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`147-2`,chapter:147,kind:`theme`,title:`A pearl in the mud`,text:`The gamin swears, steals a little and sings obscene songs, and yet 'has no evil in his heart'. Hugo's image is a pearl that mud cannot dissolve. It is the book's old argument in a new body: what a person is inside is not the same as the dirt society throws on him.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`148-1`,chapter:148,kind:`motif`,title:`A world under the paving-stones`,text:`The gamin's currency of scrap copper, his insects, his imaginary monster 'the deaf thing' living in old wells: Hugo builds a whole secret economy and natural history for children nobody looks at. Notice how much of it lies underneath, in cracks, gutters and under lifted stones. The street child's Paris is the city seen from below.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`148-2`,chapter:148,kind:`craft`,title:`Jokes as evidence`,text:`Hugo drops in two street-boy jokes, the doctor 'carrying home his own work' and 'Search me!'. He does not explain them. The jokes are his proof that the gamin has a mind as quick as Talleyrand's and is, as he says, more honest.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`149-1`,chapter:149,kind:`motif`,title:`Paradise in the gallery`,text:`The cheap top gallery of the theatre, 'narrow, dark, fetid', is called Paradise because the children pack it with joy. It is a small instance of a pattern in the book: the lowest, darkest place turned into something radiant by the people in it.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`149-2`,chapter:149,kind:`theme`,title:`The useless without the necessary`,text:`'Bestow on an individual the useless and deprive him of the necessary, and you have the gamin.' He has the theatre and no bread. In one line Hugo gives you the shape of urban poverty as he sees it: not only want, but a strange mix of want and freedom.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`150-1`,chapter:150,kind:`theme`,title:`Beware of the gaping gamin`,text:`Hugo lists Prejudice, Oppression, Tyranny and the rest, and warns them all to watch this child. The gamin is the whole of anarchy, as the idle lounger is the whole of monarchy. The street boy is presented as a political force, a witness who is quietly taking notes.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`150-2`,chapter:150,kind:`question`,title:`Jug or amphora?`,text:`'The little fellow will grow up.' Hugo says chance and the spirit of Paris decide whether this lump of common clay becomes something great or something low. How much does he believe in chance here, and how much in what society chooses to do with such a child?`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`151-1`,chapter:151,kind:`craft`,title:`The narrator steps out`,text:`'He who writes these lines has long been a prowler about the barriers of Paris.' Hugo writes in his own person about the wastelands at the city's edge, the quarries, the walls, the mills. The confession gives the description a lived-in feel, and it tells you he knows these 'limbos of Paris' at first hand.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`151-2`,chapter:151,kind:`motif`,title:`The edge of the city`,text:`The banlieue is 'end of the grass, beginning of the pavements', neither country nor town. The reader has been here before: the Gorbeau house sat in just such a half-world. Hugo's children crowned with cornflowers belong to this in-between zone, and for them 'the universe ends there'.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`152-1`,chapter:152,kind:`context`,title:`Galley-slaves wanted`,text:`Under Louis XIV, Hugo says, the courts manufactured convicts to row the new galleys, and a homeless boy of fifteen could be sent to them. Under Louis XV children vanished from the streets altogether. The history lesson is pointed: the state has a long record of treating poor children as raw material.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`,`poverty`]},{id:`152-2`,chapter:152,kind:`theme`,title:`All crimes begin in the child`,text:`'All crimes of the man begin in the vagabondage of the child.' Hugo then makes an exception for Paris, whose air 'preserves the soul'. Hugo is asking where criminals come from, and his answer is: from neglected children. The book has already shown one child, the little Savoyard Petit-Gervais, alone on a road with a coin.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`,`paris`]},{id:`153-1`,chapter:153,kind:`context`,title:`Hugo coins a word`,text:`Hugo claims the word 'gamin' first reached print in 1834 in 'Claude Gueux', which happens to be his own short story about a poor man driven to crime and execution. It is a sly bit of self-reference: he presents himself as the writer who gave this child a name.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`153-2`,chapter:153,kind:`theme`,title:`Laughing at the guillotine`,text:`The gamin treats executions as festivals, gives the guillotine pet names and knows what every condemned man wore. Hugo does not moralise much; he lets the reader feel how strange it is that a society teaches its children to see death by law as entertainment.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`paris`]},{id:`154-1`,chapter:154,kind:`context`,title:`The pear`,text:`From 1830 the gamin 'scrawled pears on the walls'. The pear was the famous caricature of King Louis-Philippe, whose heavy-jowled face cartoonists turned into a pear. Hugo's anecdote of the king himself helping a boy finish one shows the good-natured monarch and the mockery that surrounded him in a single scene.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`154-2`,chapter:154,kind:`theme`,title:`Two things he can never get`,text:`The gamin longs 'to overthrow the government, and to get his trousers sewed up again'. The joke pairs revolution with mending. Both are out of reach for him, and Hugo makes the political and the domestic need sit side by side.`,themes:[`revolution`,`poverty`]},{id:`154-3`,chapter:154,kind:`craft`,title:`He knows every policeman`,text:`The gamin can name every officer in Paris and has 'special notes on each one'. Hugo has shown us a great deal of the police in this book so far, mostly from above. Here the watchers are watched, from below, by a child.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`paris`]},{id:`155-1`,chapter:155,kind:`context`,title:`From Molière to Barra`,text:`Hugo enlists famous Parisians in the gamin's family: Molière (born Poquelin, near the market), Beaumarchais, Camille Desmoulins, and Barra, the boy drummer killed in the Revolutionary wars and made a republican martyr. The list moves from comedy to heroism, which is exactly the range he claims for the street child.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`155-2`,chapter:155,kind:`theme`,title:`He amuses himself because he is unhappy`,text:`The Book has been funny for several chapters. Then the summing-up turns the joke inside out: 'the gamin is a being who amuses himself, because he is unhappy.' Read the earlier jokes again in that light.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`156-1`,chapter:156,kind:`theme`,title:`Cured by light`,text:`The gamin is 'a grace to the nation, and at the same time a disease'. The cure Hugo prescribes is light, which here means schooling: 'Make men, make men.' Light in this book has meant mercy and conscience; now it also means universal education.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`]},{id:`156-2`,chapter:156,kind:`craft`,title:`A catalogue as an argument`,text:`The long, dizzying list pairing ancient Rome and Greece with modern Paris is meant to overwhelm. Its point is simple: 'Seek something that Paris has not.' Paris is the whole of human history in miniature. The final sting, that Paris would miss its guillotine, brings the praise back down to earth.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`157-1`,chapter:157,kind:`theme`,title:`To dare`,text:`'To dare; that is the price of progress.' It is not enough for philosophers to foresee a revolution; 'it is necessary that Danton should dare it.' The chapter is a hymn to audacity, and it ends by linking Prometheus's torch with Cambronne's pipe from Waterloo.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`157-2`,chapter:157,kind:`context`,title:`A roll-call of liberty`,text:`Washington, Kosciusko, Bolivar, Byron at Missolonghi, John Brown at Harper's Ferry, Garibaldi: Hugo, writing in the early 1860s, ranges well beyond the novel's time. He presents Paris, with its 14th of July and its night of 4 August 1789, as the source of the struggles for freedom that followed.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`158-1`,chapter:158,kind:`motif`,title:`Sand into crystal`,text:`The rabble that Cicero and Burke despised is 'vile sand' which, thrown into the furnace, becomes 'a splendid crystal' through which Galileo and Newton see the stars. Hugo keeps returning to the image of something dark becoming transparent. Here it is the poor themselves.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`158-2`,chapter:158,kind:`craft`,title:`Why the whole Book`,text:`'To paint the child is to paint the city.' Hugo now tells you why he spent a dozen chapters on a type: the people of Paris, even grown up, are the gamin. The sparrow was a way of studying the eagle.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`159-1`,chapter:159,kind:`craft`,title:`From type to person`,text:`After the general portrait, one boy steps out of it, and he does not fit. He has the gamin's laugh but 'a heart absolutely sombre and empty'. Hugo built the type so that you would feel exactly how this child differs from it.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`159-2`,chapter:159,kind:`theme`,title:`Orphans with a father and mother`,text:`'His father did not think of him, and his mother did not love him.' The street is softer to him than his mother's heart, and yet 'his mother loved his sisters'. The book has already shown children loved and unloved under one roof. Here is another family that chooses which child to love.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`159-3`,chapter:159,kind:`motif`,title:`Back to the Gorbeau house`,text:`The boy's visits home lead to 'that double number 50–52 with which the reader is acquainted'. Hugo returns us to the hovel where Jean Valjean and Cosette once hid, now full of strangers, with a new old portress. Places in this book are reused, and they carry their memories with them.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`159-4`,chapter:159,kind:`theme`,title:`A name that breaks the thread`,text:`The father calls himself Jondrette and asks the portress to say he is a Pole, an Italian or a Spaniard if anyone asks. The son is called Gavroche. 'It seems to be the instinct of certain wretched families to break the thread.' Names here are not inherited but shed. Why would a man want to be anybody but himself?`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`160-1`,chapter:160,kind:`craft`,title:`A man out of his century`,text:`Marius was promised at the end of the last chapter; Hugo gives us his grandfather instead. M. Gillenormand is over ninety in 1831, with all his teeth, and he 'formerly resembled everybody, and now resembles nobody'. The detour is deliberate: before we meet the young man, we meet the world he comes out of.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`160-2`,chapter:160,kind:`theme`,title:`Too poor, not too old`,text:`He has renounced women, he says, not because he is too old but because he is too poor. His dream is an inheritance to spend on mistresses. Hugo enjoys the old man's vitality, but notice what money means to him: not bread, as it does at the Gorbeau house, but pleasure.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`161-1`,chapter:161,kind:`motif`,title:`Fleurs-de-lys sewn by convicts`,text:`Gillenormand's beloved boudoir is hung with straw tapestry made 'on the galleys of Louis XIV' by convicts, for a nobleman's mistress. Hugo slips it in without comment, a chapter after he told us how Louis XIV filled those galleys. The elegance of the old regime rests, quite literally, on the labour of the condemned.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`]},{id:`161-2`,chapter:161,kind:`theme`,title:`A heap of blackguards`,text:`'The French Revolution is a heap of blackguards.' Gillenormand dresses like a dandy of the Directory, owns a portrait 'of no one knows whom', and despises 1789. He is the eighteenth century surviving into the nineteenth, and his opinions are as fixed as his furniture.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`162-1`,chapter:162,kind:`context`,title:`The dancers of Voltaire's day`,text:`La Camargo, Mlle Sallé and La Guimard were star dancers of the Paris Opéra in the eighteenth century, celebrated by Voltaire. Gillenormand's memories are a roll-call of a vanished world of pleasure. When he sneers at Restoration ministers like Corbière and Casimir Périer as vulgar names, he is measuring the present against that lost glitter.`,themes:[`history`,`names`]},{id:`162-2`,chapter:162,kind:`motif`,title:`Luc-Esprit`,text:`His godfather named him Luc-Esprit, 'Light-Wit', expecting a genius. The chapter title holds the joke: the name is a prophecy he half fulfilled. In a book full of chosen and discarded names, here is one handed down as a hope.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`163-1`,chapter:163,kind:`theme`,title:`Not seeing ninety-three twice`,text:`Gillenormand tells and retells how he dodged the guillotine in the Terror, and jokes that he hopes not to 'see ninety-three twice', meaning both the year 1793 and his own age. His whole political life is a reaction against one terrible year. Hugo returns to 1793 again and again in this book; here it is a private wound and a pun.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`163-2`,chapter:163,kind:`craft`,title:`Comedy with an edge`,text:`The elixir of gold, a quack cure 'for the catastrophes of love', turns out to be perchloride of iron. Hugo plays Gillenormand's credulity for laughs, but the laughter carries a judgement: this is a mind that has stopped learning.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`164-1`,chapter:164,kind:`theme`,title:`Renaming the servants`,text:`Every manservant is called after his province, every maid is Nicolette. The cook Olympie gets higher wages on condition she gives up her name. Gillenormand's household is a small court where the master decides who people are called. Here names are not lost or hidden but simply confiscated.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`164-2`,chapter:164,kind:`craft`,title:`A theory that became a history`,text:`Gillenormand's theory of marriage, let the neglected wife handle the money, 'had become his history': his second wife spent his fortune. Hugo likes this compression: a man's opinion and his fate turn out to be the same sentence.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`165-1`,chapter:165,kind:`theme`,title:`Generous in his own way`,text:`Presented at eighty-four with a baby he cannot be the father of, Gillenormand is flattered rather than angry. When a second arrives, he pays the mother, Magnon, eighty francs a month and insists the boys be treated well. Vanity and decency are hard to pull apart in him. Hugo calls him 'kindly, abrupt, charitable'.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`165-2`,chapter:165,kind:`question`,title:`The disgrace of the family`,text:`One daughter married 'through love, or chance, or otherwise' a soldier of the Republic and the Empire who won the cross at Austerlitz and was made colonel at Waterloo. Her father calls him 'the disgrace of my family'. What does it tell you about Gillenormand that a hero of those wars is, for him, a shame?`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`166-1`,chapter:166,kind:`motif`,title:`Open only after dark`,text:`'The day is vulgar,' says Gillenormand, and keeps his door shut until evening, when 'fashionable people light up their minds'. Light and darkness carry heavy meanings in this book. Here they are turned into a matter of fashion: the old world prefers candlelight to daylight.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`166-2`,chapter:166,kind:`craft`,title:`Frivolous and great`,text:`Hugo sums him up in four words: 'frivolous and great', like his century. After five chapters of comic detail, the verdict is generous. Gillenormand is not a villain; he is a period piece with a heart.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`167-1`,chapter:167,kind:`theme`,title:`Angel and goose`,text:`The two daughters both had wings, 'the one like an angel, the other like a goose'. The younger married her dream of a heroic figure and died; the elder never married and became 'a twilight soul'. Hugo sets two kinds of longing side by side, romantic love and social ambition, and neither ends happily.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`167-2`,chapter:167,kind:`theme`,title:`He idolized him`,text:`Gillenormand speaks to his small grandson only with a raised cane and a list of insults. Then comes the turn: 'He idolized him.' Love in this house speaks in the wrong voice. Compare the Jondrette home a few chapters back, where there are no insults and no love at all.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`167-3`,chapter:167,kind:`motif`,title:`Old man and spinster`,text:`Hugo points out the parallel himself: Gillenormand keeps his daughter beside him as Monseigneur Bienvenu kept his sister. 'Two weaknesses leaning on each other.' The echo invites comparison between the two households, the saint's and the worldly old man's.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`168-1`,chapter:168,kind:`context`,title:`An ultra-royalist salon`,text:`Madame de T.'s salon is a nest of 'ultras', royalists more royal than the king, who think Louis XVIII a Jacobin and pin their hopes on his brother, the future Charles X. They sing a version of the revolutionary song 'Ça ira' with the Bonapartists hanging from lamp-posts in place of the aristocrats. 'Songs are like the guillotine.'`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`168-2`,chapter:168,kind:`theme`,title:`A thief is admitted, provided he be a god`,text:`The Comte de Lamothe, connected with the scandal of Marie-Antoinette's diamond necklace, is honoured in this salon because his name is Valois. High society, Hugo says, admits a thief if he has the right name. Set that beside the yellow passport that shut every door on a convict.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`168-3`,chapter:168,kind:`question`,title:`Poor child`,text:`The handsome boy of seven hears the ladies murmur 'What a pity! Poor child!' because his father is 'a brigand of the Loire', one of Napoleon's officers. What does it do to a child to be pitied, in his own hearing, for who his father is?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`history`]},{id:`169-1`,chapter:169,kind:`craft`,title:`The gardener, then the soldier`,text:`Hugo shows us a shy old man growing tulips at Vernon, gentle enough that 'a child could make him give way'. Only then does he unroll the campaigns: Lodi, Austerlitz, Eylau, Moscow, Waterloo. The order matters. We meet the goodness before the glory, so the 'brigand of the Loire' is a gardener before he is a legend.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`169-2`,chapter:169,kind:`context`,title:`Hugo's uncle in the cemetery`,text:`At Eylau, Pontmercy is 'in the cemetery where ... Captain Louis Hugo, the uncle of the author of this book' held out with eighty-three men. Hugo's father was himself a Napoleonic general. The aside tells you this chapter is family history as well as fiction: the Bonapartist father is a figure Hugo knew from the inside.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`169-3`,chapter:169,kind:`theme`,title:`Am I permitted to wear my scar?`,text:`The Restoration refuses to recognise the rank and title Napoleon gave him at Waterloo, and threatens to prosecute him for wearing his rosette. He sends back letters addressed to 'Commandant'. The quarrel is over a name and a title, and the colonel's question to the Crown Attorney turns it into a question about his body: can the state disown his wounds too?`,themes:[`names`,`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`169-4`,chapter:169,kind:`theme`,title:`Behind a pillar at Saint-Sulpice`,text:`The colonel gave up his son so the boy would inherit, and now slips into Paris every few months 'like a criminal breaking his ban' just to watch him at mass. The scarred veteran is 'afraid of that old spinster'. The book has shown a parent sacrificing everything for a child before, and the picture of the hidden, watching father is close to unbearable.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`169-5`,chapter:169,kind:`motif`,title:`Letters read and unread`,text:`Twice a year Marius sends dictated letters; his father answers 'with very tender letters which the grandfather thrust into his pocket unread'. Hugo makes the whole tragedy of the family visible in one gesture. Words of love exist, and they are stopped in a pocket.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`169-6`,chapter:169,kind:`context`,title:`Waterloo, from the other side`,text:`Hugo now confirms what the Waterloo chapters showed us: the officer pulled from the sunken road of Ohain was Georges Pontmercy, and he is Marius's father. The reader saw what happened in that ravine at night, and who was there. Marius has never been told any of it.`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`170-1`,chapter:170,kind:`motif`,title:`Phantoms round a dying fire`,text:`To the small boy, the old ladies of the salon, with Biblical-sounding names and gowns of no colour, are 'phantoms', and the room lets in 'more night than day'. Hugo calls it 'a mummified society'. The child who arrived 'all joy and light' becomes grave in this dimness.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`170-2`,chapter:170,kind:`context`,title:`What 'ultra' meant`,text:`Hugo explains the word: to be ultra is to find the Pope not papist enough and the King not royal enough, 'to be so strongly for, as to be against'. The ultras dominated the early Restoration (1814–1820). Then came the 'doctrinarians', moderate royalists who argued for accepting all of French history, fleurs-de-lys and Napoleonic N's alike.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`170-3`,chapter:170,kind:`craft`,title:`Affection for what he mocks`,text:`Hugo says he writes of this world 'without any bitter or derisive idea', because it touches his mother, who was a royalist. It is a rare personal admission. He can satirise the salon and still insist it 'had a grandeur of its own'.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`170-4`,chapter:170,kind:`theme`,title:`Cold and ardent`,text:`Marius grows up a fanatical royalist, unloving to his grandfather and 'gloomy' about his father. Hugo's portrait is a list of contradictions: 'cold and ardent, noble, generous, proud'. Notice that his opinions are the air he was given to breathe. Hugo is showing how a young man's politics are made for him.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`revolution`]},{id:`171-1`,chapter:171,kind:`theme`,title:`He no longer expects you`,text:`Marius takes the slow coach. Hugo notes that a night diligence would have brought him in time, and that 'neither Marius nor M. Gillenormand thought of making inquiries'. The colonel dies running to meet a son who is still on the road. The tear on the dead man's cheek 'was his son's delay'.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`171-2`,chapter:171,kind:`craft`,title:`A dropped hat`,text:`Marius, ashamed of feeling so little, drops his hat to look overcome. Hugo does not condemn him outright: 'was it his fault?' The small, false gesture is more painful than any show of grief would be, because it is true to a boy who was taught not to love this man.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`171-3`,chapter:171,kind:`motif`,title:`The note`,text:`The colonel's last words to his son are a title and a debt. Marius is to bear the barony, and to do Thénardier 'all the good he can' for saving his father's life at Waterloo. The reader remembers what Thénardier was actually doing on that battlefield. Marius keeps the paper out of 'vague respect for death'.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`,`history`]},{id:`171-4`,chapter:171,kind:`motif`,title:`The garden goes to nettles`,text:`After the burial the sword and uniform are sold to an old-clothes dealer, the neighbours pillage the rare flowers, and the rest 'turned to nettles and weeds'. The colonel's two lives, the soldier's and the gardener's, are scattered within days. 'In three forgotten.'`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`172-1`,chapter:172,kind:`craft`,title:`The same pillar`,text:`Marius kneels 'behind a pillar' in the very chapel where his father used to hide to watch him. Hugo lets the place do the work: the son stands, without knowing it, where the father stood. A chair with a name on it sets the whole revelation going.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`172-2`,chapter:172,kind:`theme`,title:`A father who loved you dearly`,text:`In a few sentences a stranger gives Marius what his family withheld for ten years: proof that his father adored him. 'A man is not a monster because he was at Waterloo.' The old warden is no political thinker, and that is the point: plain decency sees through what opinion has hidden.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`172-3`,chapter:172,kind:`craft`,title:`Some love affair`,text:`Marius invents a hunting party and Gillenormand winks: 'Some love affair!' The old man reads every absence as gallantry. The comedy is sharpened by our knowing that the passion is for a dead father.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`173-1`,chapter:173,kind:`theme`,title:`Conversion by reading`,text:`Marius changes his politics in a library, reading the Moniteur, memoirs and bulletins until his father's name gives him a week's fever. Hugo traces the stages carefully because, he says, it is 'the history of many minds of our day'. A young royalist becomes a Bonapartist and a republican by meeting history in documents.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`173-2`,chapter:173,kind:`motif`,title:`Stars and a sun`,text:`Where Marius expected 'a guillotine in the twilight' and 'a sword in the night', he sees stars, Mirabeau, Danton, Robespierre, and a sun, Napoleon. The scene of conversion is a night window with Jupiter blazing. Hugo writes the change as a passage from darkness into dazzling light, and warns that dazzlement is not yet clear sight.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`173-3`,chapter:173,kind:`craft`,title:`He went too far`,text:`Hugo openly criticises his hero: like 'all new converts', Marius admits 'force' alongside 'genius' and 'neglected the attenuating circumstances'. The narrator lets us share Marius's thrill and keeps a cooler head himself. 'Progress is not accomplished in one stage.'`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`173-4`,chapter:173,kind:`motif`,title:`A hundred visiting cards`,text:`Marius orders a hundred cards reading 'Le Baron Marius Pontmercy', and then, knowing nobody, keeps them in his pocket. The title his father bled for becomes the visible sign of the son's new self. It is touching and a little absurd, as Hugo means it to be.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`174-1`,chapter:174,kind:`craft`,title:`A comic spy`,text:`Aunt Gillenormand sends the lancer Théodule to follow Marius and find his 'lass'. Argus snores all night. Hugo builds a light farce out of the family's assumption that a young man's secret must be a woman, and the farce is there to make the last line land.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`174-2`,chapter:174,kind:`theme`,title:`The lass was a grave`,text:`Marius buys the finest flowers from a peasant girl, skirts the church and kneels on a grave marked 'Colonel Baron Pontmercy'. The chapter's last sentence turns everything the family assumed upside down. The love Marius has been hiding is for a father he met only as a corpse.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`174-3`,chapter:174,kind:`theme`,title:`Names chosen and endured`,text:`'Marius! what an ugly name!' says the aunt; 'I would rather be called Alfred,' says Théodule. A trivial exchange, in a chapter where the name on a cross is what matters most. Names in this family are a field of battle, and the dead colonel has won this one.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`175-1`,chapter:175,kind:`motif`,title:`The portrait that was a testament`,text:`Gillenormand seizes the black shagreen case expecting a mistress's portrait and finds the colonel's note: 'my son shall take it and bear it.' The old man and his daughter sit with their backs to each other for an hour. The dead man has spoken in their own house.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`175-2`,chapter:175,kind:`theme`,title:`I am your father`,text:`'It means that I am the son of my father.' 'I am your father.' The whole quarrel of the generations is in these two lines. Two men who love the same boy claim him, and the boy has to choose between 'a sacred grave' and 'hoary locks'.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`history`]},{id:`175-3`,chapter:175,kind:`craft`,title:`A man four years dead`,text:`Marius, unable to insult his grandfather or leave his father unavenged, shouts 'Down with the Bourbons, and that great hog of a Louis XVIII!' Hugo deflates it at once: Louis XVIII 'had been dead for four years; but it was all the same to him.' The outburst is political in form and purely personal in content.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`175-4`,chapter:175,kind:`question`,title:`What happened to the paper?`,text:`Nicolette drops the medallion on the dark stairs and it is never found. Marius is sure Gillenormand burned 'his father's testament'. Hugo tells us it was probably an accident. What does it say about Marius that he assumes the worst, and about how quarrels grow on 'petty fatalities'?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`176-1`,chapter:176,kind:`context`,title:`Secret societies of the Restoration`,text:`Hugo mentions the German Tugendbund and the Italian Carbonari, real secret societies that plotted against the post-Napoleonic order. The Friends of the A.B.C. are his invention, but they are modelled on the student and worker clubs of 1820s and 1830s Paris. The pun is the key: 'A.B.C.' sounds like 'abaissé', the abased, meaning the people.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`176-2`,chapter:176,kind:`craft`,title:`A group portrait`,text:`Hugo introduces nine young men one after another, each with a role: Enjolras the logic of the Revolution, Combeferre its philosophy, Courfeyrac the centre, Feuilly the outside world, Grantaire the doubter. It reads like a painted group portrait. Each figure is defined by what he believes and how he believes it.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`176-3`,chapter:176,kind:`theme`,title:`Conflagration or dawn`,text:`Enjolras would follow Saint-Just; Combeferre prefers Condorcet. Combeferre asks why light must come from a fire: 'A conflagration can create an aurora, no doubt, but why not await the dawn?' The two friends hold the book's old question about progress: whether it must come through violence or can come through education.`,themes:[`revolution`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`176-4`,chapter:176,kind:`motif`,title:`Names worn and shed`,text:`Courfeyrac drops his 'de' because the liberals of his day thought the particle unfashionable. Lesgle's family name came from 'Lesgueules' and became 'l'Aigle', the eagle; his friends call him Bossuet because he is 'de Meaux', like the great preacher. Grantaire signs himself with a rebus: R (in French, 'grand R' sounds like Grantaire). These students play with names as freely as Hugo does.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`176-5`,chapter:176,kind:`theme`,title:`The doubter and the believer`,text:`Grantaire believes in nothing except his glass and Enjolras. 'No one loves the light like the blind man.' Enjolras scorns him, and Grantaire still says 'What fine marble!' Hugo makes their friendship a study in opposites: faith without warmth on one side, warmth without faith on the other.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`176-6`,chapter:176,kind:`context`,title:`Poland, 1772`,text:`Feuilly, the self-taught orphan fan-maker, rages over the partition of Poland among Russia, Prussia and Austria in 1772, which he calls the model for the crimes against nations that came after it. Poland's cause was a passion of French liberals throughout the 1830s. The orphan who 'adopted the peoples' turns a private loss into a political love.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`,`parenthood`]},{id:`177-1`,chapter:177,kind:`craft`,title:`A name answers for a name`,text:`Bossuet answers 'Present!' for Marius at roll call, is caught when his own name, Laigle, is called, and gets struck off. 'If you are Pontmercy, you are not Laigle.' Hugo's comic set-piece turns on the same thing as the darker plots of the book: one man answering to another man's name.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`177-2`,chapter:177,kind:`theme`,title:`Chance as a host`,text:`Marius has left his grandfather's with thirty francs and no idea where to go; a name sewn on his bag and a lounger in a café doorway give him a home and friends by evening. Hugo often lets chance shape lives. Here it is kindly, and it brings Marius to the very group the previous chapter described.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`178-1`,chapter:178,kind:`motif`,title:`Jean-Jacques and his children`,text:`Walking down the Rue Jean-Jacques Rousseau, Courfeyrac jokes that Rousseau sent his own babies to the foundling hospital. Enjolras rebukes him: 'He denied his own children, that may be; but he adopted the people.' In a book full of abandoned and adopted children, the remark lands with weight. Is it a defence or an accusation?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`revolution`]},{id:`178-2`,chapter:178,kind:`theme`,title:`Bonaparte, not the Emperor`,text:`Marius has just become a 'democrat-Bonapartist', which Courfeyrac calls 'the gray hue of a reassured rat'. Among these republicans nobody says 'the Emperor', and Enjolras says 'Buonaparte' like a royalist. Marius thought his conversion had settled him. Now the ground shifts again.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`179-1`,chapter:179,kind:`craft`,title:`A room full of conversations`,text:`Hugo moves round the back room corner by corner: Grantaire's drunken tirade, a vaudeville being plotted, a duel discussed, dominoes and love, pagan gods, and finally politics. The effect is of standing in a noisy café and catching fragments. Serious and silly talk share the same tables, as they do among the young.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`]},{id:`179-2`,chapter:179,kind:`theme`,title:`Grantaire's despair`,text:`Under the jokes, Grantaire's speech is a sweeping 'All is vanity': every virtue borders a vice, history repeats itself, victory is stupid, 'man is a failure'. It is the most bleakly sceptical voice we have heard in the book. Hugo puts it in the mouth of a drunk, but he gives it real wit and real learning.`,themes:[`history`,`conscience`]},{id:`179-3`,chapter:179,kind:`context`,title:`Burning the Charter`,text:`The Charter of 1814 was the constitution Louis XVIII 'granted' to France. Its Article 14 let the king rule by decree in emergencies, and in 1830 Charles X used it to suspend liberties, which set off the July Revolution. Courfeyrac's point is that a constitution handed down by a king can be taken back by him: 'By the side of the hand which gives there is the claw which snatches back.'`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`180-1`,chapter:180,kind:`theme`,title:`To be free`,text:`Marius's long, dazzling hymn to Napoleon, codes, bulletins, Austerlitz, 'the archangel of war', ends with 'what greater thing is there?' Combeferre answers in three words: 'To be free.' Hugo lets the cold, simple reply cut through the epic. Glory and liberty are not the same thing.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`180-2`,chapter:180,kind:`motif`,title:`My mother is the Republic`,text:`Combeferre goes down the stairs singing a song about choosing one's mother over Caesar's glory. Marius, who never knew his mother, repeats 'My mother?' Then Enjolras: 'Citizen, my mother is the Republic.' The book keeps mixing family love with political love. Here a cause is offered in place of a parent.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`revolution`,`love`]},{id:`180-3`,chapter:180,kind:`craft`,title:`Napoleon's numbers`,text:`Courfeyrac plays with 18: the 18th Brumaire (Napoleon's coup in 1799), the 18th of June (Waterloo), Louis XVIII. Enjolras turns the game into a verdict: 'the crime and the expiation'. The word 'crime' is what brings Marius to his feet.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`181-1`,chapter:181,kind:`theme`,title:`Doubly isolated`,text:`Too daring for his grandfather, too old-fashioned for his friends, Marius stands 'between two religions'. Hugo compares him to earth torn open by the plough: he feels only the wound for now. He also fears that moving forward may take him further from his father, whose cause he has only just embraced.`,themes:[`conscience`,`revolution`,`parenthood`]},{id:`181-2`,chapter:181,kind:`theme`,title:`Never`,text:`Courfeyrac offers a loan: 'Never.' The aunt sends six hundred francs in gold and Marius sends it back with three francs to his name. Pride is his whole fortune. The scene is funny (he forgets the hotel bill) and also a little alarming. Hugo lets you admire the refusal and doubt its wisdom.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`182-1`,chapter:182,kind:`theme`,title:`The crucible`,text:`Poverty is 'a crucible into which destiny casts a man, whenever it desires a scoundrel or a demigod'. Hugo has shown poverty breaking people in this book. Now he shows it forging one, and insists on the 'obscure heroes' of daily privation who never get a trumpet blast.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`182-2`,chapter:182,kind:`motif`,title:`Clothed with the night`,text:`Courfeyrac's old coat, turned, comes out green, so Marius goes out only after dark, when it looks black. 'As he wished always to appear in mourning, he clothed himself with the night.' One small, exact detail holds grief for his father, poverty and pride together.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`182-3`,chapter:182,kind:`craft`,title:`A cutlet for three days`,text:`Meat on the first day, fat on the second, bone on the third. Hugo makes poverty concrete with a menu. The young man bowing to the butcher's wife with his books under his arm is comic and dignified at once.`,themes:[`poverty`,`paris`]},{id:`182-4`,chapter:182,kind:`question`,title:`A baron and a lawyer`,text:`Gillenormand tears up Marius's letter and is overheard talking to himself: 'one cannot be a baron and a lawyer at the same time.' He is still thinking about his grandson, and still in his own terms. Is he angry, or hurt, or both?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`183-1`,chapter:183,kind:`craft`,title:`Poverty as a budget`,text:`Breakfast four sous, dinner sixteen, rent thirty francs, three shirts in rotation: Hugo does the sums. Seven hundred francs a year, six hundred and fifty spent, and 'he was rich'. The accounting makes Marius's dignity measurable. It also shows how thin the line is between getting by and going under.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`183-2`,chapter:183,kind:`motif`,title:`Back at the Gorbeau house`,text:`Marius's 'den minus a fireplace' is in the Gorbeau house, the same hovel where the Jondrettes live at the end of the corridor. Hugo told us so in passing when Gavroche went home to visit. Now we see the young man settled there. The building keeps gathering lives.`,themes:[`poverty`,`paris`]},{id:`183-3`,chapter:183,kind:`theme`,title:`A creditor is worse than a master`,text:`Marius never owes a sou. 'A debt was, to him, the beginning of slavery': a master owns your body, a creditor your dignity. His pride is a kind of armour against what poverty does to the soul, and Hugo admires it while noting its stiffness: he was 'timid even to rudeness'.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`183-4`,chapter:183,kind:`theme`,title:`The lesser altar`,text:`Marius keeps a shrine in his heart with two altars, the great one for his father and a smaller one for Thénardier, the sergeant he believes carried the colonel out of the grapeshot. He spends three years and his savings searching for him. The reader has seen what Thénardier did that night at Waterloo. The gap between what Marius reveres and what we know is painful to watch.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`,`names`]},{id:`184-1`,chapter:184,kind:`theme`,title:`No grandfather who does not adore`,text:`Hugo now shows us what Marius cannot see. Gillenormand idolizes him, misses him, asks himself whether he would turn him out again, and his 'aged head ... replied sadly no'. His grief is like a furnace 'which consumes its own smoke'. Two proud men are each waiting for the other to move.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`184-2`,chapter:184,kind:`theme`,title:`Work which makes him free`,text:`Poverty in youth, Hugo says, strips life bare and drives the soul toward the ideal. The poor young man thanks God for 'work, which makes him free; and thought, which makes him dignified'. It is one of Hugo's great set-pieces, and it is also a portrait of Hugo's own romantic ideal of the poor student.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`184-3`,chapter:184,kind:`craft`,title:`A gentle criticism`,text:`Hugo does not let Marius off. Contemplation 'ends by becoming one of the forms of idleness'; Marius stopped too soon, refused a good post to keep his liberty, and spends whole days in reverie. The narrator likes his hero and still marks his weakness.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`184-4`,chapter:184,kind:`motif`,title:`The candle, not the someone`,text:`Mabeuf 'operated on me for a cataract', says Marius. Hugo corrects him: the old warden enlightened him 'as does a candle which someone brings; he had been the candle and not the someone'. Light in this book often comes through people who do not know they carry it.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`185-1`,chapter:185,kind:`theme`,title:`A bouquinist`,text:`Mabeuf is neither royalist nor Bonapartist nor anarchist; he is a 'bouquinist', a lover of old books. He cannot see why men hate each other over charters when there are mosses to look at. After chapters of fierce opinions, Hugo gives us a man with none, and treats him tenderly.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`185-2`,chapter:185,kind:`theme`,title:`The slow slide`,text:`His brother dies, a notary fails, the July Revolution kills the market for his Flora, and Mabeuf moves from house to cheaper house, selling his prints and furniture. Hugo describes poverty arriving gradually in a man too absorbed to notice: 'we are the stake, and we look on at the game with indifference'.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`185-3`,chapter:185,kind:`motif`,title:`Indigo and the dragon`,text:`Mabeuf's dream is to grow indigo in France; on moving day he cheers his servant with 'We have the indigo!' Then he mishears 'dragoon' as 'dragon' and drifts into the legend of Buddha converting a fire-breathing dragon. His innocent pleasures are made of plants and books, and of the hope that monsters can be tamed by gentleness.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`185-4`,chapter:185,kind:`context`,title:`A house called Austerlitz`,text:`Mabeuf ends up in a cottage in the village of Austerlitz near the Salpêtrière, 'a brawling name which was ... extremely disagreeable to him'. The district was named after Napoleon's victory. Hugo enjoys the joke: the man who hates guns lives under the name of the Emperor's greatest battle.`,themes:[`names`,`history`]},{id:`186-1`,chapter:186,kind:`theme`,title:`Your conscience? No, your boots`,text:`Marius visits his father's old generals only on freezing nights, so that his boots will shine. 'In a drawing-room you may be soiled everywhere except on your shoes.' A light remark, but it states a truth the book has been driving at: society judges the surface.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`186-2`,chapter:186,kind:`theme`,title:`The party of humanity`,text:`After 1830 Marius has 'no longer any opinions, he had sympathies'. Out of humanity he chooses France, out of France the people, out of the people the woman. His politics have become a kind of pity. Hugo also says a person is best judged by what he dreams, since 'our chimaeras are the things which the most resemble us'.`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`186-3`,chapter:186,kind:`theme`,title:`Do not tell them it was I`,text:`Hearing that the Jondrettes are being evicted for twenty francs, Marius, who has thirty, pays their rent and gives them five francs more, on condition they are not told who paid. He hardly knew he had neighbours. Secret charity has been the mark of goodness in this book from its first pages.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`187-1`,chapter:187,kind:`craft`,title:`For Marius, read Théodule`,text:`The aunt's plan is to replace one young man with another, like correcting a misprint. The scheme fails in the most comic way: Gillenormand forgets the lancer is in the room and rants at the absent Marius instead. Théodule agrees with every word, and gets called a fool for it.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`187-2`,chapter:187,kind:`context`,title:`Students deliberate`,text:`The newspaper reports students meeting at the Panthéon about the National Guard's cannon in the Louvre courtyard. After July 1830 the citizen militia and the new government quarrelled over arms, and student gatherings were constant. Gillenormand's tirade lumps them with Hernani, Hugo's own scandalous 1830 play: 'Antitheses! Abominations which are not even written in French!'`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`187-3`,chapter:187,kind:`theme`,title:`Ranting at an empty chair`,text:`Every insult Gillenormand throws is aimed at Marius: 'Ah! Marius! Ah! you blackguard!' He cannot stop thinking of him. The comedy is real, but so is the grief underneath it. The yes-man Théodule is useless precisely because he is no substitute for the boy who argued back.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`188-1`,chapter:188,kind:`craft`,title:`A misunderstanding with every girl`,text:`Marius thinks girls turn to laugh at his clothes; in fact they turn to look at him. Hugo makes his shyness a comedy of misreading. Courfeyrac calls him 'Monsieur l'Abbé'. The young man who can read a whole history of the Empire cannot read a glance.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`188-2`,chapter:188,kind:`motif`,title:`Mademoiselle Lanoire and Monsieur Leblanc`,text:`Courfeyrac nicknames the pair on the bench from their clothes and hair: Miss Black for the girl's merino gown, Mr White for the man's white hair. 'This nickname became a law in the default of any other name.' Hugo adopts it too. It is an alias invented by a joker and accepted by everyone, including the narrator.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`188-3`,chapter:188,kind:`question`,title:`A tidy widower`,text:`Marius sees a man of about sixty with the air of a retired soldier, dressed with odd care, who never meets anyone's eye and looks at the girl with 'ineffable paternity'. Hugo tells us almost nothing else. What do you make of him?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`189-1`,chapter:189,kind:`motif`,title:`Her April had arrived`,text:`In six months the thin, awkward girl has become beautiful. Hugo compares it to three days of April covering a tree with blossom, and to a poor man suddenly coming into an income. Notice he describes her almost entirely through Marius's eyes, feature by feature, the way a young man would look.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`189-2`,chapter:189,kind:`craft`,title:`Not yet`,text:`She looks at him 'indifferently', as she would look at a marble vase, and he 'thought about something else'. Hugo builds the beauty up and then refuses the spark. The delay is deliberate: he is about to show us love starting, and he wants us to see the moment before.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`190-1`,chapter:190,kind:`motif`,title:`A glance like dawn`,text:`The two glances meet, and the girl's eye is no longer a child's but 'a mysterious gulf which had half opened'. Hugo calls that first look 'like the dawn in the sky'. Then he adds that love is 'that sombre flower, impregnated with perfume and with poison'. Light and shadow arrive together.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`190-2`,chapter:190,kind:`craft`,title:`From glance to wardrobe`,text:`The chapter's climax is a glance; its last paragraph is Marius inspecting his worn knees and pale elbows. Hugo cuts from the sublime to the ridiculous on purpose. The first sign of love in a poor young man is shame about his clothes.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`191-1`,chapter:191,kind:`craft`,title:`Hannibal marched on Rome`,text:`Marius 'marched on the bench' in his new coat like Hannibal on Rome, and retreated before reaching it. Hugo writes the whole approach as a military campaign, with hesitations, advances and a dash 'under the cannon of the place'. The mock-heroic tone lets us laugh at the lover and still feel his terror.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`191-2`,chapter:191,kind:`theme`,title:`Irreverence in a nickname`,text:`For the first time, Marius feels it is rude to call the gentleman 'M. Leblanc', even in his head. A name invented as a student joke now seems disrespectful because the man has become someone who matters to him. Hugo keeps showing how names change weight with feeling.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`191-3`,chapter:191,kind:`motif`,title:`A vague blue light`,text:`The girl seems to fill the end of the alley 'with a vague blue light'; soon it is 'that bench which was surrounded by an aureole'. Marius does not look at her directly. He sees a radiance. Love in Hugo is described in the vocabulary of light.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`192-1`,chapter:192,kind:`craft`,title:`Ma'am Bougon gives chase`,text:`The portress is staggered that Marius wears his best coat three days running and tries to follow him, 'a hippopotamus undertaking the pursuit of a chamois'. Hugo uses the outside world's view to measure how strange Marius has become. Courfeyrac even renames her: her real name is Burgon.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`192-2`,chapter:192,kind:`question`,title:`Melancholy eyes, merry smile`,text:`Hugo's only criticism of her beauty is that her gaze is melancholy while her smile is merry, which gives her face 'a rather wild effect'. Why give a portrait seen through a lover's eyes this one flaw?`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`193-1`,chapter:193,kind:`theme`,title:`A glance is a spark`,text:`'When the mine is charged, when the conflagration is ready, nothing is more simple. A glance is a spark.' Hugo compares a woman's glance to gears that seize you and carry you 'from torture to torture', ending either 'disfigured with shame, or transfigured by passion'. He takes love as seriously as revolution, and uses the same images of mines and fire.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`193-2`,chapter:193,kind:`craft`,title:`The symptoms of love`,text:`Marius treats Courfeyrac to dinner, tips six sous, praises a political speech, laughs uproariously, embraces a stranger from the provinces, and announces that 'it is very agreeable, all the same to have the cross!' His friends notice. 'That's queer!' 'No, that's serious.' Hugo shows the inner state through behaviour that makes no sense to anyone else.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`193-3`,chapter:193,kind:`motif`,title:`Dust on his boots`,text:`In the middle of his ecstasy, Marius is 'horribly vexed because there was dust on his boots', and sure she noticed. The boots have been a measure of dignity since the generals' parties. Now they are a measure of love.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`194-1`,chapter:194,kind:`theme`,title:`Something was required in the foreground`,text:`Hugo explains that Marius's worship of his father had retreated into the depths of his soul, like all religions, and left the foreground empty. 'Love came.' The young man who lived for a dead father now lives for a living girl. Hugo presents it as a natural succession.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`194-2`,chapter:194,kind:`motif`,title:`U. F.`,text:`Marius finds a white handkerchief marked U. F. and decides she is called Ursule. He sleeps with it under his lips. Hugo then tells us flatly that it 'belonged to the old gentleman'. The lover's whole edifice stands on a misreading. Names, once again, are guessed rather than known.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`194-3`,chapter:194,kind:`craft`,title:`Three blunders`,text:`Hugo counts Marius's mistakes like a strategist: falling for the 'ambush' of the bench by the Gladiator, leaving when the father came alone, and soon a third. M. Leblanc changes benches and stops bringing his daughter. Something is being tested, and Marius does not see it.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`195-1`,chapter:195,kind:`craft`,title:`Jealous of the wind`,text:`A gust lifts her dress and Marius is furious with her, although 'there had been but one culprit, the wind'. Then he wants to exterminate an old one-armed, wooden-legged veteran who winks at him. Hugo is laughing at his hero, and at the 'Bartholo who exists in Cherubin', the jealous old guardian inside the young lover.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`195-2`,chapter:195,kind:`question`,title:`Their first quarrel`,text:`The 'quarrel' is one sullen look from Marius and one raised eyebrow from her. They have never spoken. How much of this love affair is happening between two people, and how much inside Marius's head?`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`196-1`,chapter:196,kind:`theme`,title:`Are you a police spy?`,text:`Marius follows them home and questions the porter, who asks if he is a police spy. Seen from outside, the lover's devotion looks exactly like surveillance. The man on the third floor is 'a very kind man who does good to the unfortunate, though not rich himself'.`,themes:[`love`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`196-2`,chapter:196,kind:`motif`,title:`A window without a light`,text:`For a week Marius stands under the window watching the red lamp and the passing shadows. Then one night there is no light, and the next day the porter says they have moved, leaving no address. Hugo ends Book VI on a dark window. Someone has taken pains to disappear.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`196-3`,chapter:196,kind:`question`,title:`Why move?`,text:`M. Leblanc stared at Marius at the gate; the next day they stopped coming, and within a week they were gone. What would make a quiet, kindly man of modest means flee his home because a young man followed his daughter?`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`197-1`,chapter:197,kind:`craft`,title:`A new Book, a new depth`,text:`Book VII opens with a digression. Hugo takes a theatre term, the 'third lower floor' beneath the stage, and applies it to society. Above are the 'mines' of religion, philosophy and revolution, where people dig for the future. The digression sets the scale for what comes next: we are going down.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`]},{id:`197-2`,chapter:197,kind:`motif`,title:`The starry eye and the shadowy eye`,text:`All the diggers of the upper mines, from Huss and Luther down to Marat and Babeuf, share one trait: disinterestedness. 'Venerate the man ... who has this sign—the starry eye.' Below them lies a mine where light goes out. Hugo divides people by what their eyes seek, the infinite or nothing at all.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`revolution`]},{id:`197-3`,chapter:197,kind:`theme`,title:`Darkness that holds light`,text:`'In the sacred shadows there lies latent light.' The catacombs where the first Christians prayed were 'the vaults of the world'. Not every darkness in this book is evil. Some of it is where light is being prepared.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`198-1`,chapter:198,kind:`theme`,title:`The only social peril is darkness`,text:`In the lowest mine, each one is for himself, and the 'I' 'howls, seeks, fumbles, and gnaws'. Its two stepmothers are ignorance and misery. Hugo's cure is the one he named for the gamin: 'Destroy the cavern Ignorance and you destroy the lair Crime.' The argument of the whole novel is compressed into these pages.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`198-2`,chapter:198,kind:`theme`,title:`The same clay`,text:`'Humanity is identity. All men are made of the same clay.' Hugo refuses the idea of born criminals. What turns a man evil is the blackness of ignorance working into him. The book has already shown a man hardened in prison and then turned by a single act of mercy.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`198-3`,chapter:198,kind:`context`,title:`Lacenaire`,text:`'From that vault Lacenaire emerges.' Pierre-François Lacenaire was a real murderer, guillotined in 1836, who became a celebrity by posing as a poet and a philosopher of crime. Hugo mentioned him as a gamin earlier. He stands for the criminal who is all appetite and no ideal.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`]},{id:`199-1`,chapter:199,kind:`craft`,title:`Four portraits of the underworld`,text:`Gueulemer is all muscle, Babet all talk, Claquesous all darkness, Montparnasse all vanity. Hugo paints each as a type, as he did with the gamin and the students. The four together make a counter-portrait to the Friends of the A.B.C.: another group of young and not-so-young men, organised for the opposite end.`,themes:[`paris`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`199-2`,chapter:199,kind:`theme`,title:`A Cain from an Abel`,text:`Montparnasse, not yet twenty, handsome, with spring in his eyes, became a murderer through the wish to be well dressed: 'the height of elegance is idleness; idleness in a poor man means crime.' Compare Marius, also poor and handsome, who chose hunger over debt. Hugo sets two young men on either side of the same choice.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`199-3`,chapter:199,kind:`motif`,title:`Claquesous is night`,text:`He appears only after dark, speaks with his back turned, puts on a mask if a candle comes near, and throws his voice like a ventriloquist. Nobody knows if he has a name, a voice or a face. He is the darkness of Book VII given human form, a man made entirely of hiding.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`names`]},{id:`199-4`,chapter:199,kind:`theme`,title:`Lost like a handkerchief`,text:`Babet 'had been married and had had children. He did not know what had become of his wife and children. He had lost them as one loses his handkerchief.' The joke is savage. It joins a list of fathers in this book who do not think of their children.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`200-1`,chapter:200,kind:`context`,title:`Patron-Minette`,text:`The gang's name is old slang for the early morning, the hour when night creatures part. Vidocq, mentioned here, was the real ex-convict who became head of the Paris detective police. Hugo blends fact and fiction: Lacenaire's 'Perhaps it was Patron-Minette' gives his invented gang a place in the true crime record of the 1830s.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`200-2`,chapter:200,kind:`motif`,title:`A roll-call of aliases`,text:`Printanier, Bigrenaille, Mardisoir, Les-pieds-en-l'Air, and Boulatruelle, 'the road-mender already introduced' at Montfermeil. 'These names have faces attached. They do not express merely beings, but species.' The underworld is a place where names are worn, swapped and lent like disguises.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`200-3`,chapter:200,kind:`theme`,title:`Light up society from below`,text:`Kill the individuals and 'the tribe subsists'; these men are born again from 'the social ooze' as long as society stays as it is. Hugo's remedy is not the police but light: 'Not a single bat can resist the dawn.' He is arguing that crime is a social product, which a social cure can end.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`201-1`,chapter:201,kind:`theme`,title:`A lost dog`,text:`With the girl gone, Marius is no longer the bold defier of fate but 'a lost dog'. Nature, once full of lights and voices, 'now lay empty'. To every thought he answers 'What is the use?' Hugo shows how love has taken over the place where his ideas and plans used to be.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`201-2`,chapter:201,kind:`craft`,title:`A white-haired workman`,text:`Marius glimpses a man in workman's clothes and cap, with very white hair and M. Leblanc's profile, walking as if in painful thought. 'What signified that disguise?' He decides it was only a resemblance, and Hugo lets the doubt stand.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`201-3`,chapter:201,kind:`craft`,title:`Where lost women are found`,text:`At the ball at Sceaux, Grantaire mutters that this is 'where all lost women are found'. The pun is cruel and exact: Marius is looking for a lost girl among the 'lost' women of popular Paris. He walks home alone through the night, as he did after the Café Musain.`,themes:[`love`,`paris`]},{id:`202-1`,chapter:202,kind:`motif`,title:`Candlemas`,text:`It is the 2nd of February, Candlemas, the feast of light, whose 'treacherous sun' promises six more weeks of cold. Hugo quotes the old almanac rhyme about the bear going back into its cave. Marius, emerging from his own 'cave' into the dusk, is about to have his darkness interrupted.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`202-2`,chapter:202,kind:`theme`,title:`Angels and ghouls`,text:`Two ragged girls fleeing the police jostle Marius and drop a packet. Then he passes a child's coffin lit by a candle and thinks: 'There is one thing sadder than to see one's children die; it is to see them leading an evil life.' His last thought sets the Luxembourg girl against these: 'formerly they were angels and now they are ghouls'. Is that fair to them?`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`203-1`,chapter:203,kind:`craft`,title:`Four letters, one hand`,text:`Don Alvarès the Spanish royalist, Mistress Balizard the abandoned mother of six, Genflot the playwright, Fabantou the actor: four begging letters tuned to four kinds of donor, each claiming the cause the reader is likely to pity. Hugo reproduces the spelling mistakes with relish. Then Marius notices the same paper, the same tobacco smell, the same errors.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`203-2`,chapter:203,kind:`theme`,title:`Charity as a market`,text:`The letters show a man who studies the rich the way a salesman studies customers: legitimism for the marquise, motherhood for the countess, flattery for the merchant who fancies himself a patron of letters, philosophy for the churchgoer. Poverty here has become a trade in identities. 'P.S. Even if it is only forty sous.'`,themes:[`poverty`,`names`]},{id:`204-1`,chapter:204,kind:`theme`,title:`Fifty years mingled with fifteen`,text:`The elder Jondrette girl stands in the doorway in a chemise and petticoat, with the body of a girl and 'the look of a corrupt old woman'. Hugo insists that she 'had not come into the world to be homely'. Poverty has aged her, and the traces of what she might have been make it worse.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`204-2`,chapter:204,kind:`theme`,title:`A father who uses his daughters as the stake`,text:`Jondrette sends his girls with the letters 'at their risk and peril'; 'he was playing a game with fate, and he used them as the stake'. Marius sees 'impure and innocent monsters produced by misery'. The book has shown a mother sell everything for a child. Here a father spends his children.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`204-3`,chapter:204,kind:`craft`,title:`I know how to read, I do!`,text:`The girl reads a line about Hougomont, writes 'The bobbies are here' with no spelling mistakes, and says 'We have not always been as we are now. We were not made—' before she breaks off with 'Bah!' Hugo lets her pride in her schooling stand as a sign of a better past she cannot finish describing.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`204-4`,chapter:204,kind:`theme`,title:`You are a very handsome fellow`,text:`She knows his name, follows his walks to Mabeuf's, and likes his tumbled hair. Marius steps back and answers with 'cool gravity'. He is so absorbed in a girl he has never spoken to that he hardly sees the one who has just told him she watches him.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`204-5`,chapter:204,kind:`motif`,title:`The sun is shining`,text:`Marius gives her five of his last francs and keeps sixteen sous for his dinner. 'Good! the sun is shining!' she cries. Then she seizes a mouldy crust: 'it breaks my teeth!' Her account of nights under the bridges, stars like smoking lamps and the river that looks too cold to drown in, is one of the most frightening descriptions of hunger in the book.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`205-1`,chapter:205,kind:`theme`,title:`The misery of the child`,text:`'He who has seen only the misery of woman has seen nothing; he must see the misery of the child.' Marius, poor for five years, realises he has never seen real misery. Hugo builds a ladder of suffering, and puts children on the bottom rung, which is where the novel began its account of Cosette at Montfermeil.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`205-2`,chapter:205,kind:`theme`,title:`Only a wall`,text:`Marius scolds himself: he lived beside these people, heard them through the plaster, and gave them nothing but one mechanical payment while he dreamed of 'loves in the air'. 'Whose fault is this?' Hugo turns his romantic hero's absorption into a moral failure, and asks the reader the same question about their own neighbours.`,themes:[`conscience`,`poverty`]},{id:`205-3`,chapter:205,kind:`motif`,title:`A peephole`,text:`A triangle of missing plaster near the ceiling becomes a window into the next room. 'It is permissible to gaze at misfortune like a traitor in order to succor it.' Hugo justifies the spying with pity, but notice the word 'traitor'. Looking unseen is a kind of power, however kindly meant.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`206-1`,chapter:206,kind:`craft`,title:`Caverns are better than hovels`,text:`Hugo opens with a comparison: the forest's lair is savage and grand, the city's is petty and ugly. Then he describes the Jondrette room inch by inch, the leprous walls, the constellation of old shoes, the nooks where 'monstrous human beings' might hide. The description is an argument: this is what society makes of people.`,themes:[`poverty`,`paris`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`206-2`,chapter:206,kind:`motif`,title:`The Dream`,text:`On the wall hangs a cheap print called 'The Dream': Napoleon in glory over a column misspelled 'Maringo Austerlits Iena Wagramme Elot'. Beside it stands a panel turned to face the wall. The household's one decoration is Bonapartist kitsch, and next to it a picture nobody is allowed to see.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`206-3`,chapter:206,kind:`theme`,title:`Even the dead are unequal`,text:`Jondrette rages that at Père-Lachaise the rich lie in the paved acacia alley and the poor in mud up to the knees. 'Oh! I could eat the whole world!' His grievance is real, and Hugo has made similar points himself. But here it comes from a man forging begging letters, and the anger has turned to appetite.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`206-4`,chapter:206,kind:`theme`,title:`Ashes of affection`,text:`'Bodies press close to each other in misery, as in cold, but hearts draw apart.' The mother still says 'my dear, my little friend' while her heart is silent. Hugo shows what poverty does to love inside a family: the words survive the feeling.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`206-5`,chapter:206,kind:`craft`,title:`A face for Lavater`,text:`Lavater was the eighteenth-century writer who claimed to read character in faces. Hugo says he would have seen in Jondrette 'the vulture mingled with the attorney'. The narrator, who has insisted that we are all made of the same clay, here judges a face at first sight. Ask yourself why he allows himself that.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`207-1`,chapter:207,kind:`craft`,title:`Staging poverty`,text:`When the 'philanthropist' is announced, Jondrette puts out the fire, kicks through the chair, makes his daughter break a window and drapes himself in a torn chemise. Hugo describes him as 'a general making the final preparation' before battle. Real misery is dressed up to look more miserable, which is the strangest thing in the scene.`,themes:[`poverty`,`names`]},{id:`207-2`,chapter:207,kind:`theme`,title:`So much the better`,text:`The younger girl cuts her fist on the glass. 'So much the better! I foresaw that.' Her father bandages her with his shirt and admires the effect. Of all the parents in this book so far, none has used a child's body so coldly.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`208-1`,chapter:208,kind:`craft`,title:`She`,text:`'It was She!' The girl from the Luxembourg walks into the worst room in Paris. Hugo gives the moment the full romantic treatment, 'that star which had beamed upon him for six months', and sets it against the hovel he has just described. The light he lost has reappeared in the darkest place he knows.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`208-2`,chapter:208,kind:`theme`,title:`Pack of rascals`,text:`Just before the knock, Jondrette pours out his hatred of the charitable rich: they bring old clothes and bread when what he wants is money, and they must have stolen to get rich. 'Society ought to be grasped by the four corners of the cloth and tossed into the air.' Then the door opens and he is all bows. Hugo shows the hatred and the flattery come from the same mouth.`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`208-3`,chapter:208,kind:`motif`,title:`Sombre eyes`,text:`The elder Jondrette girl steps back behind the door and stares at the velvet bonnet, the satin, 'that charming, happy face'. Hugo gives her one line. Two girls of about the same age stand in the same room, one in silk boots, one in borrowed men's shoes.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`209-1`,chapter:209,kind:`theme`,title:`Talent of a juggler`,text:`Jondrette checks which alias he used on the letter, answers to 'Fabantou', claims to be a pupil of Talma and a friend of Mlle Mars, and pinches his daughter's wound to make her cry on cue. He even lies about the rent. Hugo lets us watch every trick from behind the wall, so the visitors' kindness looks all the more exposed.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`209-2`,chapter:209,kind:`question`,title:`Take a look at that man`,text:`While the visitors bend over the hurt child, Jondrette studies the gentleman 'as though seeking to summon up his recollections', and twice tells his wife to look at him. What has he seen?`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`209-3`,chapter:209,kind:`motif`,title:`The greatcoat`,text:`M. Leblanc leaves his big brown greatcoat on purpose, and tells Jondrette to put it on before going out in the cold. It is the kind of quiet, practical charity the book has praised from its opening pages. Jondrette wears it straight out into the snow to meet someone else.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`210-1`,chapter:210,kind:`craft`,title:`A hummingbird among toads`,text:`Marius saw the whole scene 'and yet, in reality, had seen nothing'. He watched only her, and could not even hear her voice through Jondrette's noise. Hugo is careful to tell us that the one witness to the tricks missed most of them, because love had narrowed his eyes to a single point.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`210-2`,chapter:210,kind:`theme`,title:`For lack of twenty-four sous`,text:`The cab wants forty sous in advance; Marius has sixteen. He thinks bitterly of the five francs he gave the Jondrette girl that morning. It is a small, sharp lesson in what poverty means. Charity cost him the chance he wanted most.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`210-3`,chapter:210,kind:`motif`,title:`A prowler in the snow`,text:`Coming home, Marius sees Jondrette in the philanthropist's coat talking with a man who looks like Panchaud, alias Printanier, alias Bigrenaille, one of the names on the Patron-Minette list. Hugo has just introduced that underworld in general. Now one of its members stands across the road from Marius's door.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`211-1`,chapter:211,kind:`theme`,title:`Call me thou`,text:`The Jondrette girl offers to help Marius, unasked: 'I do not want you to be grieved.' When he speaks, she begs him to use the intimate 'thou'. He does, and asks her to find another girl's address. Hugo marks the moment 'them' becomes 'her' in her mouth, 'something indescribably significant and bitter'.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`211-2`,chapter:211,kind:`question`,title:`What will you give me?`,text:`'Anything you like.' 'Anything I like?' She takes the commission and shuts the door. Marius does not stop to wonder what she might ask. What does she want from him, and does he have any idea?`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`211-3`,chapter:211,kind:`craft`,title:`Was the veil about to be rent?`,text:`Hugo throws Marius from despair back to the peephole with one overheard sentence: 'I recognized him.' The run of breathless questions puts us inside his hope. Notice that his reason for listening is still love, not suspicion.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`212-1`,chapter:212,kind:`theme`,title:`I recognize him`,text:`'Eight years have passed! But I recognize him!' Jondrette is certain he knows the philanthropist from long ago, and that the girl is someone he and his wife once knew too. The reader is not told who. Hugo lets Marius, and us, overhear only fragments, with the key name whispered out of earshot.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`212-2`,chapter:212,kind:`theme`,title:`A sow with the look of a tigress`,text:`The mother, who has lain stupefied all day, comes alive with rage at the girl's satin and velvet: 'my daughters are going barefoot'. Her hatred is jealousy on her daughters' behalf, the only tenderness she shows, turned to savagery. 'I should like to kick her stomach in for her!'`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`212-3`,chapter:212,kind:`theme`,title:`My fortune is made`,text:`Jondrette's speech is the cry of a man who has had enough of misery, 'my share and other people's share', and wants to eat, drink, sleep and do nothing 'like other people'. Hugo gives him a real grievance and then shows where it leads: a trap, a brazier, a trip to the ironmonger's.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`212-4`,chapter:212,kind:`motif`,title:`The coat that made it possible`,text:`'He did a devilish good thing in leaving it for me, the old scoundrel! If it hadn't been for that, I couldn't have gone out.' The greatcoat given in kindness lets Jondrette go and recruit his accomplices. 'What small points things hang on, anyway!' Charity has been turned into a tool against the giver.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`212-5`,chapter:212,kind:`craft`,title:`The neighbour doesn't listen`,text:`'Our neighbor? ... he doesn't listen, the big booby.' Hugo has spent pages establishing that Marius never paid attention to the Jondrettes. Now that indifference has become the plotters' blind spot. The falling snow, which deadens the traffic, lets Marius hear every word.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`213-1`,chapter:213,kind:`theme`,title:`Pity for a toad, a boot for a viper`,text:`Marius 'had the kindliness of a brahmin, and the severity of a judge'. A moment ago he was reproaching himself for not helping these people; now he says 'These wretches must be stamped upon.' Hugo shows how quickly compassion can flip into judgement when the poor turn predators.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`213-2`,chapter:213,kind:`theme`,title:`Rendering justice to Providence`,text:`On the way to the police, Marius realises that if he had not given away his five francs he would have followed the carriage and never heard the plot. The charity he regretted that afternoon now looks providential. Hugo often lets small acts of goodness pay out in ways nobody could plan.`,themes:[`conscience`,`poverty`]},{id:`213-3`,chapter:213,kind:`motif`,title:`Voices behind a wall`,text:`Two men sitting in the snow behind a low wall mention Patron-Minette and 'Father What's-his-name's team', then chat about a melodrama at the Gaîté. Crime and theatre sit side by side again, as they did in the begging letters. Marius learns things through walls all day.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`214-1`,chapter:214,kind:`craft`,title:`A look that searched`,text:`A tall man by the stove, square face, ferocious whiskers, a glance that 'searched'. He never says 'monsieur'. He knows the Gorbeau house, the room at the end of the corridor and every name in Patron-Minette. Hugo saves his name for the very last line, though the reader may well have recognised him first: 'You will ask for Inspector Javert.'`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`214-2`,chapter:214,kind:`motif`,title:`The dog and the wolf`,text:`'The dog is, at times, no less terrible to meet than the wolf.' Hugo has used this image for Javert before. Here the hunter and the hunted are set side by side again, police and criminal equally fierce, distinguished only by which side of the law they serve.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`214-3`,chapter:214,kind:`theme`,title:`Not too soon`,text:`Javert gives Marius two pistols and one instruction: wait until the crime has begun, then fire. 'You are a lawyer; you know the proper point.' The law needs the crime to happen before it can act. Marius is now an agent of the police, watching through his peephole on their behalf.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`214-4`,chapter:214,kind:`context`,title:`Former property of Gorbeau`,text:`Javert says 'I know that barrack' and names its old owner. The reader remembers why he knows it: years ago he hunted a man and a child out of this very house by night. Marius has no idea of that history.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`paris`]},{id:`215-1`,chapter:215,kind:`craft`,title:`A man following a man`,text:`Courfeyrac and Bossuet see Marius in the snow and assume he is chasing a girl. Then they notice he is following a man in a coat 'too large for him' and ragged trousers. 'He's a poet,' says Courfeyrac. The friends' comic misreading lets us see Marius from outside, turned detective.`,themes:[`paris`,`love`]},{id:`215-2`,chapter:215,kind:`motif`,title:`A cold chisel`,text:`Jondrette visits a hovel in the Rue Gracieuse, buys a huge cold chisel at the ironmonger's and hides it under the philanthropist's coat. The five-franc piece left in charity has bought a brazier and a tool. Hugo lists the purchases without comment.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`215-3`,chapter:215,kind:`motif`,title:`Four heads in the dark`,text:`Night falls; the moon rises red behind the dome of the Salpêtrière. In an empty attic along the corridor Marius glimpses 'the motionless heads of four men'. Then Ma'am Bougon locks the house behind her. Hugo shuts every door on the scene before it begins.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`216-1`,chapter:216,kind:`motif`,title:`A bloody glow`,text:`The moon on the snow fills Marius's room with twilight; through the hole in the partition comes a reddish light 'which seemed bloody to him', and no sound at all. Hugo stages the waiting like the moment before a curtain rises: crime advancing on one side, justice on the other, and Marius between them in his stockinged feet.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`216-2`,chapter:216,kind:`craft`,title:`The mirror`,text:`Sent to check that Marius's room is empty, the elder Jondrette girl walks straight to his bed, and past it, to the mirror. She smooths her hair and sings a love song: 'Our loves lasted a whole week.' Hugo turns a moment of pure suspense into a glimpse of a girl wanting to look pretty, in the one room where someone might look at her.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`216-3`,chapter:216,kind:`question`,title:`There's no one here`,text:`'I am looking under the bed and the furniture,' she says, while looking only in the mirror. Did she simply not look, or did she choose not to see?`,themes:[`love`,`conscience`]},{id:`216-4`,chapter:216,kind:`theme`,title:`Green silk boots`,text:`'The idea of standing watch in the snow barefoot!' 'Tomorrow you shall have some dainty little green silk boots!' Jondrette sends his daughters out as lookouts for his crime and pays them in promises. The children are part of the machinery.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`217-1`,chapter:217,kind:`craft`,title:`Forge or mouth of hell`,text:`Brazier, chisel heating in the coals, a heap of rope that turns out to be a rope ladder, burglars' tools, a kitchen knife tested on a thumbnail. Hugo lights the room by the brazier and lets the objects speak. 'One felt that there existed in it the anticipation of something terrible.'`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`217-2`,chapter:217,kind:`motif`,title:`Moonlight in the crimson room`,text:`The white moonlight entering the red garret seems to Marius 'like a thought of heaven mingled with the misshapen reveries of earth'. Hugo notes wryly that he is 'dreamy even in the moment of action'. The light that shows him the scene is also the shadow that hides him when the Jondrette woman comes for his chairs.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`217-3`,chapter:217,kind:`theme`,title:`To sit on`,text:`'Two chairs will be needed here.' 'What for?' 'To sit on.' The mildness of the answer chills Marius more than the knife. Jondrette even pays for the cab with Marius's own five francs. Everything Marius gave in kindness is being turned to use.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`217-4`,chapter:217,kind:`craft`,title:`The partition cracking`,text:`Marius cocks his pistol; it clicks; Jondrette starts, then laughs: 'It's the partition cracking!' Hugo gives us a near-miss in the dark that tightens everything. The wall that has let Marius hear everything nearly gives him away.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`218-1`,chapter:218,kind:`craft`,title:`Picture it yourself`,text:`Hugo stops the action to ask the reader to build the scene: the snow like winding-sheets, the black elms, the single candle, two men at a table, the 'female wolf' in the corner and Marius behind the wall, pistol in hand. It is a tableau, the way a stage play would freeze before the climax.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`218-2`,chapter:218,kind:`theme`,title:`I shall be able to stop that wretch`,text:`Marius feels horror but no fear. He holds the pistol, believes the police are near, and hopes the clash will at last tell him who the girl and her father are. Hugo marks the mix of motives honestly: the rescuer is also still the lover, waiting for information.`,themes:[`love`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`218-3`,chapter:218,kind:`motif`,title:`Four louis`,text:`M. Leblanc arrives exactly at six and lays four gold louis on the table, 'for your rent and your most pressing necessities. We will attend to the rest hereafter.' He has kept his word to the letter. His serenity, Hugo says, makes him 'singularly venerable'.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`219-1`,chapter:219,kind:`motif`,title:`Fabantou, alias Jondrette`,text:`The wife slips and calls her husband Jondrette; M. Leblanc notices; 'An artistic sobriquet!' The aliases from the begging letters collide in one room. A man with too many names has trouble keeping them straight.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`219-2`,chapter:219,kind:`craft`,title:`Men with black faces`,text:`One by one, silently, men with blackened faces and bare tattooed arms slip in and sit on the beds. 'Chimney-builders,' says Jondrette. Hugo slows the count, one, then two, then four, so the trap closes on the reader at the same pace it closes on M. Leblanc.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`names`]},{id:`219-3`,chapter:219,kind:`theme`,title:`Four sous a day`,text:`Jondrette's long complaint about the paper-box trade, fourteen hours a day, thirteen handlings per box, for four sous, is a stalling tactic. It is also true. Hugo puts an accurate account of sweated labour in the mouth of a man setting a trap. Real misery and its exploitation are tangled together.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`219-4`,chapter:219,kind:`motif`,title:`A signboard for a tavern`,text:`The panel that stood face to the wall is turned round: a crude daub with one main figure. Jondrette calls it a masterpiece he loves like his daughters and asks a thousand crowns. M. Leblanc says it is 'some signboard for a tavern, and is worth about three francs'. Marius cannot see it at all.`,themes:[`names`,`history`]},{id:`219-5`,chapter:219,kind:`question`,title:`Do you know me?`,text:`The whining stops; the little man becomes 'terrible' and shouts the question. The chapter ends on it. Who is Jondrette, and why does he need M. Leblanc to recognise him?`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`220-1`,chapter:220,kind:`theme`,title:`My name is Thénardier`,text:`'My name is not Fabantou, my name is not Jondrette, my name is Thénardier.' With one sentence the alias falls away, and Hugo announces he will 'henceforth call by no other name' the innkeeper of Montfermeil. The reader has known this man since he bought a child's labour for a few francs a month. Now we watch him try to cash in on her a second time.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`220-2`,chapter:220,kind:`theme`,title:`The man who took the Lark`,text:`Thénardier's tirade gives the game away: Christmas Eve 1823, the yellow greatcoat, the doll, 'that Fantine's child', the cudgel in the forest. The reader saw all of it. M. Leblanc is Jean Valjean, and the girl of the Luxembourg is Cosette, grown up. Marius hears every word and understands almost none of it.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`220-3`,chapter:220,kind:`theme`,title:`Two voices in one conscience`,text:`The name Thénardier knocks the pistol out of Marius's hand. His father's will says 'do him all the good you can'; the scene in front of him says this man is about to commit murder. Hugo gives Marius a full storm in a skull, like the one Valjean once had over Champmathieu: two sacred duties, no way to reconcile them, and seconds to choose.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`220-4`,chapter:220,kind:`motif`,title:`The signboard`,text:`The 'painting by David' turns out to be the inn sign: a man carrying another through the smoke of Waterloo. Thénardier claims it shows him saving a general whose name he never caught. Marius sees his father rise from the panel 'like a phantom'. The reader knows what really happened in that sunken road, which makes the sign a lie painted in oils.`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`220-5`,chapter:220,kind:`theme`,title:`We are the thermometers`,text:`Called a villain, Thénardier explodes: the rich eat asparagus in January and read the cold in the newspapers; 'We, it is we who are thermometers.' It is one of the most powerful speeches about poverty in the book, and Hugo gives it to a torturer. He calls the outburst 'as hideous as evil, and as heartrending as the truth'.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`220-6`,chapter:220,kind:`craft`,title:`Why didn't he cry out?`,text:`Thénardier notices that his prisoner has not shouted once, and concludes he has his own reasons to avoid the police. The reader can see why a man with Valjean's past would choose silence even when bound among nine ruffians. Marius, who knows nothing, finds the silence deeply troubling.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`220-7`,chapter:220,kind:`motif`,title:`Urbain Fabre`,text:`Asked to sign, the prisoner gives a name to match the U. F. on his handkerchief, Urbain Fabre, and a false address that buys time. So the initials Marius kissed were never 'Ursule'. In Hugo's book of names, even the lover's private name for his beloved was built on a borrowed alias.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`220-8`,chapter:220,kind:`motif`,title:`The convict's sou`,text:`Hugo steps out of the scene to describe a sou hollowed into a box holding a watch-spring saw, a 'marvel of industry' made by galley-slaves. Found under the bed afterwards, it explains the cut ropes. The prisoner's past, the patience learned in chains, is what saves him tonight.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`]},{id:`220-9`,chapter:220,kind:`theme`,title:`Have no more fear of me than I have for you`,text:`Freed, Valjean seizes the white-hot chisel and presses it into his own arm, without a flinch, then throws it into the snow. He will not be made to speak by pain. Hugo says suffering 'vanished in serene majesty'. The man who once stole from a child has become someone torture cannot touch.`,themes:[`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`220-10`,chapter:220,kind:`craft`,title:`The bobbies are here`,text:`Marius's solution comes by moonlight: the line the elder daughter scrawled that morning to show off her spelling. Wrapped round plaster and tossed through the hole, it lets him save the victim without betraying his father's saviour. A girl's boast becomes a warning, and a wall becomes a way to speak.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`220-11`,chapter:220,kind:`craft`,title:`Would you like my hat?`,text:`The thieves squabble over who goes down the ladder first and Thénardier mocks the idea of drawing names from a hat. A voice from the doorway offers one. Javert's entrance, hat in hand and smiling, ends the longest chapter of the book on a joke.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`221-1`,chapter:221,kind:`motif`,title:`Two names for two sisters`,text:`Javert catches the younger girl on watch, Azelma, but not the elder, Éponine, who 'was not at her post'. After nearly twenty chapters of 'the elder Jondrette girl', Hugo hands her a name in the middle of a police report. Where she was instead, he does not say. The warning that saved the prisoner was in her handwriting.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`221-2`,chapter:221,kind:`craft`,title:`You'll miss fire`,text:`'Come now, don't fire. You'll miss fire.' The pistol misfires. Javert's calm is so complete it seems to command the gun. Hugo plays him almost as comedy here: the 'emperor of the fiends' grants Bigrenaille his tobacco and greets each masked man by name as if taking a roll call.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`221-3`,chapter:221,kind:`theme`,title:`My daughters!`,text:`Mme Thénardier holds off the police with a paving-stone, shielding her husband with her body. When she is handcuffed, her first cry is 'My daughters!' 'They are in the jug,' says Javert. Whatever else she is, her love for her girls is real. It is the same love the reader saw at Montfermeil, and it still has room for only two.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`221-4`,chapter:221,kind:`theme`,title:`The most valuable of the lot`,text:`While Javert writes his report, the victim slips out of the window and down the ruffians' own ladder. 'He must have been the most valuable of the lot.' The policeman's instinct is right for reasons he cannot guess. Notice the title: one should always begin by arresting the victims.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`221-5`,chapter:221,kind:`question`,title:`Who has he missed?`,text:`Javert never saw the prisoner's face clearly, and the man fled rather than give evidence. Marius still does not know who 'M. Leblanc' is, only that he answers to Urbain Fabre and that a criminal called his daughter the Lark. What does Valjean's flight from his rescuers tell you about the life he has been leading since Montfermeil?`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`222-1`,chapter:222,kind:`craft`,title:`A title that points back`,text:`'The Little One Who Was Crying in Volume Two': the title recalls a scene from Montfermeil, when Mme Thénardier wanted nothing to do with a crying child. Hugo now makes the inference that the trap forced. Gavroche, the son of 'Jondrette', is a Thénardier, brother to Éponine and Azelma.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`222-2`,chapter:222,kind:`theme`,title:`I have come to see my ancestors`,text:`Gavroche kicks the door of the Gorbeau house and learns in three lines that his father is in La Force, his mother in Saint-Lazare, his sisters in the Madelonettes. He says 'Ah!', spins on his heel and goes off singing. The whole family is in prison, and the child they kicked into the street is the only one free.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`222-3`,chapter:222,kind:`motif`,title:`A mask of Decrepitude`,text:`Gavroche mocks old Ma'am Bougon as 'an enormous, enormous dog' and tells her she lacks the style of beauty that pleases him. Her face, lit red by a street lantern, floats in the dark 'like a mask of Decrepitude'. The scene is cruel and funny, the gamin's wit from Book I in action.`,themes:[`paris`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`223-1`,chapter:223,kind:`craft`,title:`A new volume, a history lesson`,text:`Volume IV opens not with Marius or the man who escaped the trap, but with an essay on 1830 and 1831. Hugo steps back from the Gorbeau house to the whole of France. As at Waterloo and the convent, he wants the reader to understand the ground before the story moves across it.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`223-2`,chapter:223,kind:`context`,title:`The July Revolution in brief`,text:`In July 1830 Charles X issued ordinances suspending press freedom and dissolving the Chamber, using the Charter's emergency article. Paris rose for three days, the 'Three Glorious Days', and the Bourbons went into exile. Hugo's summary: 'What it termed its concessions were our conquests; what it termed our encroachments were our rights.'`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`223-3`,chapter:223,kind:`theme`,title:`Right against fact`,text:`Hugo's key idea is the duel of 'right' and 'fact'. A fact, a regime or an arrangement that merely exists, decays over time if it holds no justice; right stays 'eternally beautiful and pure'. The July Revolution was 'right overthrowing the fact', which is why it could be gentle. Notice how the same contrast could describe a law that punishes a man for a loaf of bread.`,themes:[`revolution`,`mercy-law`,`history`]},{id:`223-4`,chapter:223,kind:`theme`,title:`Fair to the Bourbons`,text:`Hugo does not simply cheer their fall. Under the Restoration, he says, France learned calm debate, liberty of the press and conscience; it was 'the turn of intelligence to have the word'. The Bourbons 'carried away with them respect, but not regret'. This is the same narrator who mocked the ultra salon and still found it touching.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`224-1`,chapter:224,kind:`theme`,title:`The skilful and the sages`,text:`Once a revolution reaches shore, 'the skilful make haste to prepare the shipwreck'. Hugo's contempt is for the 'statesmen' who wrapped the giant-people in flannel and put it to bed. 1830, he says, was 'a revolution arrested midway', half progress, half right.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`224-2`,chapter:224,kind:`motif`,title:`A chair is not a caste`,text:`The bourgeoisie, Hugo insists, is not a class but 'the contented portion of the people', the man 'who now has time to sit down'. The danger is sitting down too soon. Compare Gillenormand in his armchair and Marius on his feet, and Thénardier, who boasted of having been 'a bourgeois' himself.`,themes:[`revolution`,`poverty`]},{id:`224-3`,chapter:224,kind:`context`,title:`The 221 and the best of republics`,text:`'The 221' were the deputies who in March 1830 voted an address of protest against Charles X; after July they offered the crown to Louis-Philippe of Orléans. Lafayette embraced him on the balcony of the Hôtel de Ville and supposedly called the new monarchy 'the best of republics'. Hugo's last line: absolute right protested, then 'retired into the darkness'.`,themes:[`history`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`225-1`,chapter:225,kind:`craft`,title:`A portrait in contradictions`,text:`Hugo's sketch of Louis-Philippe is one enormous sentence of balanced opposites: 'something of Charlemagne and something of an attorney', 'a gentleman, but not a chevalier', bold abroad and timid at home. The style itself argues that the king was a compromise made flesh: 'Louis Philippe is 1830 made man.'`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`225-2`,chapter:225,kind:`theme`,title:`I won seven last night`,text:`The detail Hugo values most is that the king sat up at night going through death sentences to pardon as many as he could. 'What a pity that I was not wounded! Then I might have pardoned!' In a book that began with a bishop at a guillotine and a convict's sentence, a king who fights the scaffold earns Hugo's warmth.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`225-3`,chapter:225,kind:`context`,title:`Two tombs in exile`,text:`Hugo knew Louis-Philippe and was made a peer of France under him. The king died in exile in England in 1850; Hugo wrote this chapter in exile on Guernsey after opposing Napoleon III. Hence the closing line: no one will say of 'two tombs in exile' that one flattered the other.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`225-4`,chapter:225,kind:`question`,title:`Kindness or greatness?`,text:`'In history, where kindness is the rarest of pearls, the man who is kindly almost takes precedence of the man who is great.' Marius, a few Books ago, worshipped Napoleon's greatness. Which does this book, so far, seem to value more?`,themes:[`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`226-1`,chapter:226,kind:`theme`,title:`Nobody to blame`,text:`Hugo insists the coming conflicts were nobody's crime: the king defends royalty in good faith, democracy attacks in good faith, 'a blind man is no more a criminal than a Vendean is a ruffian'. It is a generous position, and a deliberate one: he wants us to watch the struggle without simple villains.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`,`conscience`]},{id:`226-2`,chapter:226,kind:`context`,title:`To produce wealth and to share it`,text:`Hugo reduces the socialist thinkers of the 1830s, Saint-Simon, Fourier and others, to two problems: producing wealth and distributing it. England, he says, solves the first and fails the second; communism kills production to divide it. His own answer is to make property 'universal' and education free and compulsory.`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`226-3`,chapter:226,kind:`motif`,title:`Piles of shadows`,text:`The chapter ends in weather: shadows gathering on the horizon, leaves trembling 'at the approach of a storm', cholera, risings at Lyon, the Duchesse de Berry stirring the Vendée. Hugo has used storm imagery for the inner life of his characters. Now the whole country has a tempest in its skull.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`227-1`,chapter:227,kind:`craft`,title:`History from police files`,text:`Hugo builds this chapter out of overheard remarks, secret reports, torn letters, a recipe for gunpowder found in a drawer. It reads like a dossier. The method makes the insurrection feel like something that grew out of a thousand small acts by ordinary people, not a plan drawn up by a few.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`227-2`,chapter:227,kind:`motif`,title:`How is your wife?`,text:`Shopkeepers ask workmen 'How is the rising coming along?' in the tone of 'How is your wife?' A furniture dealer offers his gun. The strangest thing Hugo records is how public and calm it all was: an insurrection preparing 'in the face of the government'.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`227-3`,chapter:227,kind:`theme`,title:`The barbarians of civilization`,text:`Hugo defends the ragged crowds of 1793 from the Faubourg Saint-Antoine: they 'demanded light with the mask of night'. Between them and the gilded men 'glorifying ... the sword, the stake, and the scaffold', he would choose the barbarians. Then he adds a third way: 'progress with a gentle slope'.`,themes:[`revolution`,`light-darkness`,`poverty`]},{id:`227-4`,chapter:227,kind:`context`,title:`The Faubourg Saint-Antoine`,text:`The working-class district east of the Bastille had supplied the crowds of 1789 and 1793 and was the traditional heart of Parisian revolt. Hugo calls it 'that powder-house of suffering and ideas'. The Friends of the A.B.C. appear here too, briefly, as one small cell in a much larger network.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`228-1`,chapter:228,kind:`craft`,title:`A census of the willing`,text:`Enjolras sends each friend to a different corner of Paris, the polytechnic students, the masons, the medical school, the law courts, and keeps the Cougourde for himself. Hugo uses the roll-call to show the reach of the movement and to remind us who each young man is. Marius, 'that abstracted Marius', is the one name missing.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`228-2`,chapter:228,kind:`theme`,title:`I believe in you`,text:`'You do not believe in everything.' 'I believe in you.' Grantaire volunteers for the Barrière du Maine, puts on a red Robespierre waistcoat and whispers 'Be easy.' Enjolras finds him playing dominoes. The joke is sharp, but Grantaire's one faith, in a person rather than a principle, is plain.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`228-3`,chapter:228,kind:`motif`,title:`Fanning the ashes`,text:`The workmen of the Barrière du Maine are 'becoming extinguished'; 'those ashes must be fanned into a glow'. Enjolras pictures his friends as a 'powder train' and an 'electric spark', and sees 'a luminous uplifting beneath the gloomy skirts of the future'. For him, revolution is fire and light.`,themes:[`revolution`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`229-1`,chapter:229,kind:`theme`,title:`The wicked poor man`,text:`Marius flees the Gorbeau house partly from horror at what he saw there, 'a social deformity which is, perhaps, even more terrible than the wicked rich man, the wicked poor man'. After all of Hugo's pleading for the poor, the phrase is a hard one. It admits that misery can produce monsters without excusing the society that made them.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`229-2`,chapter:229,kind:`theme`,title:`Five francs every Monday`,text:`Marius borrows money for the first time in his life, from Courfeyrac, to send five francs a week to Thénardier in La Force. 'Whence can this come to me?' Thénardier wonders. The son honours his father's debt to the man he has just watched try to murder a bound prisoner. Is this loyalty, or a refusal to look at what he knows?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`229-3`,chapter:229,kind:`theme`,title:`Thought and reverie`,text:`'Thought is the toil of the intelligence, reverie its voluptuousness. To replace thought with reverie is to confound a poison with a food.' Hugo warns that a poor man who is generous and does not work slides down a slope that ends in 'suicide or crime'. It is a sober passage about a hero the narrator plainly loves.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`229-4`,chapter:229,kind:`motif`,title:`Writing to her`,text:`Marius fills a notebook with his purest reveries and calls it 'writing to her'. He does not even know her name: not Ursule, and 'the Lark' is only a nickname he heard from a criminal. Love, Hugo says, 'kindles in the darkness of the heart; the more it has disappeared, the more it beams'.`,themes:[`love`,`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`229-5`,chapter:229,kind:`motif`,title:`The Lark's meadow`,text:`A passer-by names a pretty field by the Bièvre 'the Lark's meadow' and adds that Ulbach murdered a shepherdess there, a real crime of 1827. Marius hears only the first word. 'It was absurd, but irresistible.' A place-name becomes a shrine, much as a handkerchief's initials did before.`,themes:[`names`,`love`,`paris`]},{id:`229-6`,chapter:229,kind:`question`,title:`His heroic and his equivocal sides`,text:`Marius now thinks the white-haired workman near the Invalides was M. Leblanc in disguise. Why did he not cry out? Why did he flee the police? Is he the girl's father at all? The reader can answer some of these questions. Marius cannot, and they do not diminish his love.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`230-1`,chapter:230,kind:`craft`,title:`Loose ends`,text:`Hugo lists what Javert's triumph missed: the victim fled, Montparnasse slipped away with Éponine, and Claquesous vanished from a police carriage between the Gorbeau house and La Force. The chapter is an accounting of escapes. The underworld leaks out of every net the law throws.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`230-2`,chapter:230,kind:`theme`,title:`Paws in crime and paws in authority`,text:`Did Claquesous melt into the shadows, or did some police agent let him go because 'such a villain ... might make a very good agent'? Hugo raises the possibility that order and disorder are 'on such intimate juggling terms with the night'. Vidocq, remember, was a convict before he was a policeman. The line between hunter and hunted is not as clean as Javert believes.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`230-3`,chapter:230,kind:`motif`,title:`Brujon, 1811`,text:`A fortress carved with a nail on the wall of La Force, signed 'Brujon, 1811', by the father of the Brujon of 1832. Crime here runs in families like a trade. Hugo has spent this volume on fathers and sons; this is the darkest version of the inheritance.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`230-4`,chapter:230,kind:`context`,title:`A postilion into Ireland`,text:`Hugo enjoys the prison slang: a 'postilion' is a pellet of bread with a message inside, thrown over the roofs from one yard to another, 'into Ireland'. A 'biscuit' means 'nothing to be done'. The note passes from Brujon to Babet to a woman in the Salpêtrière to Magnon to Éponine, a whole postal service of the underworld.`,themes:[`paris`,`names`]},{id:`230-5`,chapter:230,kind:`question`,title:`A gate on a garden`,text:`'Babet. There is an affair in the Rue Plumet. A gate on a garden.' Éponine goes to look, watches the house for days, and sends back a biscuit: nothing to be done. Hugo does not say why. What do you make of her verdict?`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`230-6`,chapter:230,kind:`craft`,title:`Knotting another thread`,text:`'Often when we think we are knotting one thread, we are tying quite another.' Hugo ends on a proverb about unintended consequences. The book has been full of small acts, a coin given, a coat left behind, a note tossed through a wall, that change things in ways nobody planned.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`231-1`,chapter:231,kind:`theme`,title:`When misery looses bonds`,text:`Marius and Mabeuf pass on the boulevard with a melancholy nod and do not speak. 'There comes a moment when misery looses bonds! Two men who have been friends become two chance passersby.' Poverty isolates. Mabeuf, nearly eighty, now eats one egg a day and gives the other to his unpaid servant.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`231-2`,chapter:231,kind:`motif`,title:`A tear of dew`,text:`Too weak to draw water from his well, Mabeuf looks up at a sky full of stars and begs for 'A tear of dew! A little pity!' The stars are beautiful and useless. Then a voice in the hedge offers help. Hugo's light imagery turns practical here: what the old man needs is not radiance but rain.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`]},{id:`231-3`,chapter:231,kind:`theme`,title:`Angel or devil`,text:`Éponine waters the whole garden, barefoot, looking like a bat among the flowerbeds. 'You are an angel since you take care of the flowers.' 'No. I am the devil, but that's all the same to me.' Mabeuf blesses her; she calls herself damned. Which of them is right?`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`231-4`,chapter:231,kind:`craft`,title:`A goblin of the Bièvre`,text:`Mabeuf has been reading old books on demons and the goblins said to haunt his very garden. Falling asleep, he wonders whether his visitor was one. Hugo lets the old bookman's superstition frame the scene, so Éponine, who appears and vanishes in the twilight, has something of a spirit about her.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`232-1`,chapter:232,kind:`theme`,title:`Poorer and prettier`,text:`Éponine has grown 'poorer and prettier, two steps which it had not seemed within her power to take', 'towards the light and towards distress'. Straw in her hair from a stable loft, prison in her face, and yet 'she was beautiful'. Hugo refuses to let us see her only as the girl of the Gorbeau garret.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`232-2`,chapter:232,kind:`theme`,title:`You have called me Éponine!`,text:`Marius uses her name to make her swear she will not tell her father the address. She hardly hears the oath. 'That's nice! You have called me Éponine!' To him the name is a tool; to her it is a gift. Few moments in the book show so clearly the gap between what each of them wants.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`232-3`,chapter:232,kind:`motif`,title:`I don't want your money`,text:`He fumbles out his only coin, the five francs meant for her father in prison, and puts it in her hand. She lets it fall to the ground. She has told us she would sew his shirt, and asked to see him smile. The one thing she refuses is payment. What does she want instead?`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`232-4`,chapter:232,kind:`craft`,title:`A woman like me`,text:`'A nice young man like you must not be seen with a woman like me.' Hugo pauses on the word 'woman' in the mouth of a child of not quite sixteen: 'No tongue can express all that lay in that word.' Marius, in ecstasy, notices nothing. The reader is made to notice everything he misses.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`233-1`,chapter:233,kind:`motif`,title:`A house built to hide`,text:`The Rue Plumet house was made by an eighteenth-century judge to conceal a mistress: a back lodge to hide a child and nurse, a secret door, a walled lane winding a quarter of a league to the Rue de Babylone. Only the birds saw it. Hugo describes its architecture in detail because its whole design is concealment, and its new tenant has a great deal to conceal.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`233-2`,chapter:233,kind:`craft`,title:`The narrator says it at last`,text:`'This unobtrusive tenant was Jean Valjean, the young girl was Cosette.' Hugo confirms in his own voice what Thénardier shouted in the garret, and adds that the reader 'has, doubtless, been no less prompt than Thénardier' to see it. He kept the secret from Marius for a whole Book, but never really from us.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`233-3`,chapter:233,kind:`theme`,title:`Happiness that might be theft`,text:`Valjean left the convent because he was too happy there. He saw that keeping Cosette among the nuns, letting a vocation 'germinate' in her before she knew life, would be 'to rob a human creature of its nature and to lie to God'. The man once condemned for theft now examines his own love for any trace of stealing. His conscience is still the sharpest thing about him.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`233-4`,chapter:233,kind:`motif`,title:`The inseparable`,text:`Valjean carries a small valise with an 'odor of embalming' that he never lets out of his sight and never opens before others. Cosette laughs and says she is jealous of it. Hugo states the fact and offers no explanation.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`233-5`,chapter:233,kind:`theme`,title:`Three addresses`,text:`Besides the Rue Plumet house, Valjean rents two poor rooms in distant quarters, the Rue de l'Ouest and the Rue de l'Homme Armé, so he can vanish at a moment's warning. 'This lofty virtue had three domiciles in Paris for the sake of escaping from the police.' The reader now understands the flat Marius found empty, and the sudden flights.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`234-1`,chapter:234,kind:`theme`,title:`Better than the master`,text:`Cosette has the pavilion, the tapestries, the heated rooms; Valjean lives in the porter's lodge with a folding bed, no fire, and black bread. 'It is the young lady who is the mistress of this house.' 'And you, monsieur?' 'I am a much better thing than the master, I am the father.' Hugo shows love as self-denial in the smallest household details.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`234-2`,chapter:234,kind:`context`,title:`A convict in the National Guard`,text:`The census of 1831 swept 'M. Fauchelevent' into the National Guard, the citizen militia of the July Monarchy. Valjean mounts guard willingly: the uniform is 'a correct disguise which mixed him with everyone'. The escaped galley-slave now patrols for public order. 'Within, the angel, without, the bourgeois.'`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`,`history`]},{id:`234-3`,chapter:234,kind:`motif`,title:`Two costumes`,text:`With Cosette he dresses like a retired officer; alone, at night, he wears a workman's blouse and a cap over his face. 'Was this precaution or humility? Both.' The reader now knows what Marius glimpsed near the Invalides. Toussaint calls him a saint; her butcher calls him 'a queer fish'.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`234-4`,chapter:234,kind:`context`,title:`The benevolent gentleman`,text:`Valjean takes Cosette to Saint-Jacques-du-Haut-Pas because it is far away, gives generously to the poor of that district, and so draws Thénardier's begging letter. Hugo ties the threads together: his charity is what led him into the trap. Goodness in this book is never safe.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`235-1`,chapter:235,kind:`motif`,title:`The garden that belongs to itself`,text:`Left alone for half a century, the garden has become a thicket 'as impenetrable as a forest, as peopled as a city ... sombre like a cathedral'. Branches and weeds 'married' in 'the holy mystery of fraternity, symbol of the human fraternity'. Hugo makes the wild garden an image of what society might be if nothing blocked 'the sacred effort of things towards life'.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`235-2`,chapter:235,kind:`theme`,title:`Nothing is small`,text:`The digression widens from a Paris garden to the whole cosmos: 'the radiation of the star profits the rose', a nebula is 'an anthill of stars', and the universe is 'a machine made of mind ... the prime motor of which is the gnat'. It is Hugo's mystical side. Notice how it echoes the book's moral claim that no person is too small to matter.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`236-1`,chapter:236,kind:`theme`,title:`Paphos made over into Eden`,text:`A garden built for illicit love has 'returned to virginity and modesty' and is now 'arranged for love' of a purer kind. And 'there was, also, in this solitude, a heart which was quite ready'. Hugo is plainly setting a stage, and he says so.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`236-2`,chapter:236,kind:`theme`,title:`Many mothers, no mother`,text:`'All the nuns in the world are not worth as much as one mother in the formation of a young girl's soul.' Cosette had 'many mothers, in the plural', and an old man who 'knew nothing at all'. Hugo warns that convent ignorance leaves the heart to build its romances in the dark. It is one of his clearest statements about what a parent gives.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`236-3`,chapter:236,kind:`motif`,title:`Black bread`,text:`'If you eat it, I will eat it too.' So Valjean eats white bread to stop Cosette eating black. She scolds him for his cold room and threatens to visit until he lights a fire. The man who went to the galleys over a loaf now gives up his bread of penance for the girl he loves.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`236-4`,chapter:236,kind:`theme`,title:`Perhaps this man is my mother`,text:`Cosette prays for a mother she never knew and does not even know her name; whenever she asks, Valjean is silent, and once the smile ends in a tear. 'This silence ... covered Fantine with darkness.' Cosette imagines her mother's soul has passed into the old man. The reader knows the name, and the story, that he cannot bring himself to say.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`,`love`]},{id:`236-5`,chapter:236,kind:`question`,title:`Through martyrdom`,text:`Cosette dreams of her mother with great wings and says she must have been 'almost a saint'. Valjean answers: 'Through martyrdom.' Is his silence about Fantine a kindness to Cosette, or a debt he is not paying?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`237-1`,chapter:237,kind:`craft`,title:`The same story, from her side`,text:`Hugo now rewinds to the Luxembourg and tells it again through Cosette. We saw the thin girl become beautiful through Marius's eyes; now we see her discover it in a mirror. The chapter ends exactly where Marius's story began: 'It was at this epoch that Marius ... saw her once more at the Luxembourg.' The two halves click together.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`237-2`,chapter:237,kind:`motif`,title:`She had just dazzled herself`,text:`Cosette, who was told she was ugly all her childhood, looks in the mirror and cries out. Her beauty arrives 'like the sudden advent of daylight'. The image picks up the light Marius saw around her bench. Notice the mirror: the Jondrette girl looked into one in Marius's room too.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`237-3`,chapter:237,kind:`theme`,title:`What is to become of me?`,text:`'How beautiful she is! What is to become of me?' Valjean watches her beauty with terror, because it means change in a life so happy he dares not move. Hugo names the difference plainly: 'What he beheld with anguish, a mother would have gazed upon with joy.' His love is a father's and also something more fearful.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`237-4`,chapter:237,kind:`theme`,title:`He would lose by heaven`,text:`Valjean, still a fugitive whom 'the law had not released from its grasp', asks one thing of Providence, the law and the world: that Cosette go on loving him. Asked if he wanted heaven, 'he would have replied: I should lose by it.' The saintly man has one attachment he would not give up even for God.`,themes:[`love`,`conscience`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`237-5`,chapter:237,kind:`motif`,title:`That disguise`,text:`Valjean asks her to wear her old schoolgirl merino again. 'That disguise! ... I shall never put on those horrors again.' To him the plain clothes were safety; to her they were a costume she has outgrown. He himself has lived in disguises for years. Hugo lets the word land on him.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`238-1`,chapter:238,kind:`theme`,title:`Two clouds laden with lightning`,text:`Hugo defends the much-mocked 'glance' of romance: 'That is the way people do fall in love, nevertheless, and the only way.' Cosette had noticed Marius long before he noticed her, and thought him handsome while he still thought her plain. Love here is mutual from the first, though neither knows it.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`238-2`,chapter:238,kind:`craft`,title:`Timid boy, bold girl`,text:`'The first symptom of true love in a young man is timidity; in a young girl it is boldness.' It was Cosette who led her father along the walk toward Marius's bench. The reader saw the moment from Marius's side as a miracle. From hers, it was a decision.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`238-3`,chapter:238,kind:`theme`,title:`Love without a name`,text:`In the convent's song-books 'amour' was replaced by 'tambour' or 'pandour', so Cosette has no word for what she feels. 'Is anyone the less ill because one does not know the name of one's malady?' She loves without being able to name either the feeling or the man.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`238-4`,chapter:238,kind:`motif`,title:`Stars separated by millions of leagues`,text:`'They did not address each other ... they saw each other; and like stars of heaven ... they lived by gazing at each other.' The whole affair is conducted with the eyes. To Cosette, Marius is 'a vision', 'charming, luminous, and impossible'.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`239-1`,chapter:239,kind:`theme`,title:`A dog scanning a thief`,text:`Valjean, who thought himself past malice, feels 'the old depths of his soul' open against Marius, 'craters forming in his bosom'. His long inward speech, sixty years on his knees, forgiven every evil, and now 'a great booby' at the Luxembourg, is self-pity and grief together. The convict's old wrath is not dead, only sleeping.`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`239-2`,chapter:239,kind:`craft`,title:`The traps Marius fell into`,text:`Now we learn what was behind the changed benches, the forgotten handkerchief, the visits alone: 'Jean Valjean laid a host of ambushes for him', and Marius 'answered yes' to every one. The 'three blunders' Hugo counted earlier were tests set by a jealous father. Cosette, meanwhile, gave nothing away.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`239-3`,chapter:239,kind:`theme`,title:`What have I done?`,text:`Alone at night on his pallet, Valjean looks back at the convent as an Eden he left 'voluntarily and madly' and asks 'What have I done?' The choice he made out of conscience, to give Cosette the world, has brought the world to her gate. Self-sacrifice has turned on him.`,themes:[`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`239-4`,chapter:239,kind:`theme`,title:`Suffering side by side, with a smile`,text:`'What is the matter with you?' 'There is nothing the matter with me.' 'And you, father?' 'With me? Nothing.' Two people who love each other completely now hide their pain from each other. Hugo makes the sadness lie in the kindness: neither will hurt the other by speaking.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`239-5`,chapter:239,kind:`question`,title:`Would you like to come to the Luxembourg?`,text:`Once, Cosette says yes and Marius is not there; the next day she says no. Her silence is a strategy her father cannot read. Is Valjean's ignorance of 'the only miseries which are charming' a failing, or simply the cost of a life with no youth in it?`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`240-1`,chapter:240,kind:`craft`,title:`A vision at daybreak`,text:`Hugo builds the chain-gang out of the dark like an apparition: a confused shape, wheels, the clank of something like chains, then faces turned to 'the faces of corpses' by the first light. We see it before we understand it, exactly as Cosette does. Then the sun rises and sets 'fire to all those ferocious heads'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`240-2`,chapter:240,kind:`theme`,title:`Twenty-four men to a chain`,text:`Iron collars, one chain for all, guards with cudgels flogging even the sick, children running behind 'screaming with laughter', an old woman telling her son 'let that be a warning to you!' Hugo calls it Dante's seven circles on the march. This is what the law does with the men Book VII called the products of ignorance and misery.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`240-3`,chapter:240,kind:`theme`,title:`Five and thirty years before`,text:`Valjean cannot move his feet. He remembers that this is the usual route, chosen to keep the convoy out of the king's sight, and that he himself passed this barrier thirty-five years ago. The reader saw him leave Toulon. Here, for the first time in years, his past drives by in daylight with the woman he loves beside him.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`,`history`]},{id:`240-4`,chapter:240,kind:`theme`,title:`Are they still men?`,text:`'Father, are they still men?' 'Sometimes,' answers the unhappy man. That night Cosette says that if she met one of those men she would die at the sight. She has no idea what she is saying, or to whom. The single word 'sometimes' carries everything Valjean has lived.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`,`parenthood`]},{id:`240-5`,chapter:240,kind:`motif`,title:`Shelter in a uniform`,text:`To take Cosette's mind off the convicts, Valjean takes her to the public fêtes in his National Guard uniform, 'with the vague inward feeling of a man who is betaking himself to shelter'. Earlier he envied a general's gold lace. A uniform is the opposite of a convict's red blouse, and he hides inside it.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`240-6`,chapter:240,kind:`craft`,title:`What are the galleys like?`,text:`Days later, on a sunny morning, Cosette pulls a daisy apart, 'without a suspicion that to pluck a daisy apart is to do the same by a heart', and asks over her shoulder: 'Father, what are the galleys like?' Hugo ends the chapter on the question, without an answer.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`love`]},{id:`241-1`,chapter:241,kind:`craft`,title:`The burn, seen from home`,text:`The morning after the Gorbeau trap, Valjean appears 'calm as was his wont' with a burned arm he explains 'in some way or other', and refuses a doctor. The reader knows exactly how he got it. Hugo makes us see the heroic scene from the other side: at home it looks like a minor accident kept secret.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`241-2`,chapter:241,kind:`theme`,title:`What a good misfortune!`,text:`Nursing him, Cosette returns to his lodge and reads to him, and his fears about the young man dissolve. 'Oh! what a kindly wound!' He even feels grateful to the Thénardiers. The torture they meant for him has given him back his daughter for a month.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`241-3`,chapter:241,kind:`motif`,title:`Light falls into cellars`,text:`'In spring, sad souls grow light, as light falls into cellars at midday.' April brings Cosette back to life without her noticing. Hugo ties the inner weather to the season, as he did when Marius first saw her change on a summer morning.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`242-1`,chapter:242,kind:`theme`,title:`There is the sun`,text:`Mabeuf owes three quarters' rent, has no wood, no meat, no bread on credit. 'What will you warm yourself with this winter?' 'There is the sun.' His serenity is almost saintly and almost foolish. Gavroche, listening under the hedge, decides not to steal his apples.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`242-2`,chapter:242,kind:`theme`,title:`The hardest of all work is thieving`,text:`Valjean pins Montparnasse to the ground and delivers a long sermon on idleness: the rolling-mill of crime, the rope of torn sheets, the hollowed sou with its watch-spring saw, twenty years in and out at fifty 'broken, bent ... with white locks'. He is describing his own life. The reader has seen the sou; Montparnasse has no idea who is speaking.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`,`poverty`]},{id:`242-3`,chapter:242,kind:`motif`,title:`My purse? Here it is`,text:`The sermon ends with Valjean handing over his purse to the young thief, as the Bishop once handed over his candlesticks. 'The blockhead!' says Montparnasse. Hugo has shown us the gesture before. The question is whether it can work twice, on a different kind of man.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`242-4`,chapter:242,kind:`theme`,title:`Fallen from heaven`,text:`Gavroche picks the purse from Montparnasse's pocket and throws it over the hedge to Mabeuf. The money passes from a saint to a thief to a street boy to a starving old man in a few minutes. 'That has fallen from heaven,' says Mother Plutarque. Providence in this book often works through the most unlikely hands.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`243-1`,chapter:243,kind:`theme`,title:`Passion calmed or lulled to sleep`,text:`Spring heals Cosette faster than Marius. 'Why! I no longer think of him.' Hugo is blunt about the difference: Marius buries himself in sorrow, Cosette plunges in and comes out again. 'Whose fault was it? No one's.' It is an unusually unromantic observation from a romantic narrator.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`243-2`,chapter:243,kind:`craft`,title:`The lancer again`,text:`The officer passing the gate every day, with waxed moustache and cigar, is Théodule Gillenormand, Marius's cousin, the aunt's favourite and the comic spy of Book III. Hugo enjoys the coincidence. Théodule, for his part, cannot be bothered to look at every girl who looks at him.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`243-3`,chapter:243,kind:`motif`,title:`Marble column or wine-shop post`,text:`An orphan girl's heart is like a vine tendril that clings to whatever is nearest, 'the capital of a marble column or ... the post of a wine-shop'. Hugo warns that a poor unknown young man may be the column and a polished man of the world the post. The image asks the reader to judge suitors from the inside, as he asked us to judge Marius.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`244-1`,chapter:244,kind:`motif`,title:`A shadow with a round hat`,text:`After footsteps in the night, Cosette sees beside her own moonlit shadow 'another shadow ... which had a round hat', and when she turns there is no one. 'Phantoms do not wear round hats.' Hugo stages the scene like the garret, by moonlight and silhouettes, but this time the watcher hides from the watched.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`244-2`,chapter:244,kind:`craft`,title:`The chimney-pot`,text:`Valjean, armed with a club, guards the garden three nights, then laughs and shows Cosette the shadow of a chimney-pipe on a neighbouring roof. She accepts it and forgets to ask why a chimney's shadow should flee when looked at. Hugo lets the reader notice the hole in the explanation that the girl, relieved, does not.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`parenthood`]},{id:`244-3`,chapter:244,kind:`theme`,title:`More lark than dove`,text:`'There flowed in her veins some of the blood of the bohemian and the adventuress who runs barefoot.' Hugo reminds us that Cosette is Fantine's daughter and the barefoot child of Montfermeil as well as a convent pupil. Her courage in searching the dark thicket alone comes from that rougher past.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`245-1`,chapter:245,kind:`motif`,title:`A stone on the bench`,text:`An arm through the railing leaves a stone where Cosette was sitting. By night it becomes 'as large as a mountain and full of caverns'; by morning, 'that which is terror by night is curiosity by day'. Hugo traces how fear and light trade places, and how a young girl's imagination turns a stone into a monster and back.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`245-2`,chapter:245,kind:`craft`,title:`Toussaint's melodrama`,text:`Toussaint cheerfully imagines men entering at night and cutting their throats with bad knives. Hugo also announces he will stop reproducing her stammer: 'The musical notation of an infirmity is repugnant to us.' A small moment where the narrator shows tact toward a minor character.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`245-3`,chapter:245,kind:`question`,title:`Perhaps Fantine was within that shadow`,text:`Sitting alone at dusk, Cosette feels an 'objectless sadness', and Hugo adds a single line: 'Perhaps Fantine was within that shadow.' The narrator believes the dead may watch over the living. What does it add to the scene that the mother Cosette cannot name may be present at the moment love arrives?`,themes:[`parenthood`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`246-1`,chapter:246,kind:`craft`,title:`Drops of soul`,text:`The whole chapter is the notebook found under the stone: aphorisms on love, God and death, with no name or address. Hugo drops the story entirely and gives us the text itself, so the reader reads it as Cosette does. It is the notebook Marius was 'writing to her' in his despair.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`246-2`,chapter:246,kind:`motif`,title:`To love a being is to render that being transparent`,text:`'Things are black, creatures are opaque. To love a being is to render that being transparent.' The notebook is full of light: stars, sun, a woman who 'emits light as she walks'. 'If there did not exist someone who loved, the sun would become extinct.' Love is presented as the force that lets light through the world.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`246-3`,chapter:246,kind:`theme`,title:`The stars through his soul`,text:`'I encountered in the street, a very poor young man who was in love. His hat was old ... water trickled through his shoes, and the stars through his soul.' The writer has slipped a self-portrait into his meditations. Poverty and love, the two forces of Marius's life, share one sentence.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`246-4`,chapter:246,kind:`theme`,title:`Try to love souls`,text:`'Woe ... to him who shall have loved only bodies, forms, appearances! Death will deprive him of all. Try to love souls, you will find them again.' The notebook ends its thoughts on love with death and the hope of reunion. It sounds like Marius, but it also sounds like Hugo, whose own beliefs about death run through the book.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`247-1`,chapter:247,kind:`craft`,title:`The officer passes again`,text:`Hugo times Théodule's daily ride past the gate twice in this chapter: once as Cosette finishes the notebook, when she finds him hideous, and once on the third reading, when he is 'insipid, silly, stupid ... and extremely ugly'. The comedy measures the change in her heart. One page of writing undoes weeks of glances.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`247-2`,chapter:247,kind:`motif`,title:`A pellet of bread over La Force`,text:`Cosette believes 'an intervention of the angels' has given him back to her. Hugo corrects her: that celestial chance 'was a pellet of bread tossed by one thief to another thief ... over the roofs of La Force'. Brujon's message sent Éponine to the Rue Plumet; Éponine brought Marius. The sublime is delivered by the underworld.`,themes:[`love`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`247-3`,chapter:247,kind:`theme`,title:`The firebrand and the flame`,text:`Her education had 'talked to her of the soul, and never of love, very much as one might talk of the firebrand and not of the flame'. The notebook is 'a spark which had fallen from that other soul into hers'. Hugo returns to his argument that keeping girls ignorant does not protect them; it leaves them open to the first strong light.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`248-1`,chapter:248,kind:`theme`,title:`Oh! my mother!`,text:`When Marius finally speaks, Cosette's first words are 'Oh! my mother!' and she nearly faints. She has never known her mother, and once wondered if Valjean was her mother. In the moment of love she calls for the parent she never had.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`248-2`,chapter:248,kind:`craft`,title:`A kiss, and that was all`,text:`After a long, breathless speech from Marius, Hugo turns brief: 'How did it come to pass that their lips met? How comes it to pass that the birds sing, that snow melts?' The kiss is placed alongside the seasons and the dawn. Then: 'A kiss, and that was all.'`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`248-3`,chapter:248,kind:`theme`,title:`My name is Cosette`,text:`They tell each other everything, dreams, despair, how they adored each other from afar, and only at the very end ask each other's names. 'My name is Marius.' 'My name is Cosette.' After 'Ursule', 'the Lark', 'Mlle Lanoire' and the rest, the two finally exchange true names, last of all.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`248-4`,chapter:248,kind:`motif`,title:`Not yet a ghost`,text:`Marius appears bare-headed, thin and pale, lit by the dying day, 'not yet a ghost, and ... no longer a man'. Hugo surrounds the first meeting with images of death and night. The young man who has been sinking toward despair arrives looking as if he has already half left the world.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`249-1`,chapter:249,kind:`theme`,title:`A mother to her daughters only`,text:`Hugo now tells us that the Thénardiers had five children, two girls and three boys, and that the mother's love stopped at the girls: 'Her hatred of the human race began with her own sons.' Why? 'Because.' The reader saw her neglect the crying boy at Montfermeil and turn Gavroche out. Now we learn there were two more.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`249-2`,chapter:249,kind:`theme`,title:`Children on loan`,text:`When Magnon's two boys, paid for by M. Gillenormand, died of croup on the same day, the Thénardiers lent her their two youngest for ten francs a month. 'The little Thénardiers became little Magnons.' Gillenormand visits twice a year and is told they look like him. Children here are a source of income, swapped like coins.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`,`names`]},{id:`249-3`,chapter:249,kind:`motif`,title:`Jean-Jacques did even better`,text:`When his wife feels a scruple about abandoning their children, Thénardier silences her: 'Jean Jacques Rousseau did even better!' It echoes the student debate on the Rue Jean-Jacques Rousseau in Book IV. Enjolras defended the philosopher because he 'adopted the people'. Thénardier uses him as an excuse.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`revolution`]},{id:`249-4`,chapter:249,kind:`theme`,title:`Identity broken between two streets`,text:`'In Paris, the identity which binds an individual to himself is broken between one street and another.' Magnon moves from the Quai des Célestins to the Rue Clocheperce and her children's names change with the move. In this book, a whole life can be swapped by changing an address.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`249-5`,chapter:249,kind:`motif`,title:`The malicious playfulness of the wind`,text:`After the police raid, a cobbler gives the two little boys a paper with M. Barge's address. A gust tears it from the elder's numb fingers at the corner of the street, and night falls. A whole fate turns on a scrap of paper and a puff of wind. The chapter title makes the wind the villain.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`250-1`,chapter:250,kind:`motif`,title:`The door of the sepulchre`,text:`Spring 1832 brings icy north winds 'like the door of winter' left ajar, and Hugo names what came through it: cholera, the epidemic that killed thousands in Paris that year. He adds that these winds came with strong electric tension and frequent storms. The weather of the book is turning ominous.`,themes:[`history`,`paris`]},{id:`250-2`,chapter:250,kind:`theme`,title:`Brothers without knowing it`,text:`Gavroche takes in two lost little boys, feeds them his last sou of bread and puts them to bed in his elephant. The reader knows, from the chapter before, that they are his own brothers. Gavroche never asks their names. A child thrown out by his parents mothers the children his parents gave away.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`250-3`,chapter:250,kind:`motif`,title:`White bread`,text:`Offered black bread, Gavroche snorts 'Keksekça?' and demands white: 'I'm standing treat.' He gives the biggest piece to the elder boy and keeps the smallest for himself. The book has made bread a sacred object since a loaf sent a man to the galleys. A ragged boy shares it with the generosity of a lord.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`250-4`,chapter:250,kind:`theme`,title:`Shivering more than Saint Martin`,text:`Gavroche gives his only shawl to a half-naked beggar girl and shivers, 'more than Saint Martin, for the latter retained one-half of his cloak'. 'The wicked skies punish good deeds,' Hugo notes as the rain redoubles. The gamin of Book I, all jokes and mockery, turns out to have the reflexes of a saint.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`250-5`,chapter:250,kind:`context`,title:`The elephant of the Bastille`,text:`Napoleon planned a vast bronze elephant fountain on the site of the Bastille; only a full-size plaster model was built, and it stood rotting there until 1846. The July Column, the 'stove' Hugo mocks, replaced the fortress as a monument to 1830. Hugo also notes a real court case of a child found sleeping inside the elephant.`,themes:[`history`,`paris`]},{id:`250-6`,chapter:250,kind:`theme`,title:`God had done a grander thing with it`,text:`'The Emperor had had the dream of a genius ... he wished to incarnate the people. God had done a grander thing with it, he had lodged a child there.' Hugo's hymn to 'the unforeseen utility of the useless' turns a forgotten imperial monument into a cradle. Glory is less than shelter; Marius's Napoleon is less than this.`,themes:[`history`,`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`250-7`,chapter:250,kind:`craft`,title:`Nobody says police, they say bobbies`,text:`Gavroche keeps correcting the little boys' French into slang: 'crib', 'darkmans', 'nut', 'blaze the crib'. It is comic, and it is also a lesson in how to survive the street. Hugo lets a child teach the language of the street.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`250-8`,chapter:250,kind:`motif`,title:`Since the good God is lighting his candle`,text:`Inside the elephant Gavroche's 'cellar rat' wick gives more smoke than light; when thunder and lightning come, he laughs: 'Since the good God is lighting his candle, I can blow out mine.' Then come the rats, which ate his cat, held off by a brass net from the zoo and by his hand across the dark.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`250-9`,chapter:250,kind:`question`,title:`We need you`,text:`Before dawn Montparnasse calls Gavroche out with a parakeet cry: 'We need you. Come, lend us a hand.' 'I'm with you.' Gavroche asks no questions and leaves the little boys asleep. What does it tell you about Gavroche that he mothers two strangers and runs errands for a murderer on the same night?`,themes:[`conscience`,`poverty`]},{id:`251-1`,chapter:251,kind:`craft`,title:`That same night`,text:`Hugo steps back in time to tell 'what had taken place that same night at La Force'. The storm that rattled the elephant and the lightning that frightened the little boys also covered the noise of Brujon and Gueulemer breaking through a chimney. Two stories of the same night run in parallel until a parakeet cry joins them.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`251-2`,chapter:251,kind:`context`,title:`Inside La Force`,text:`La Force was a prison in the Marais built from an old ducal mansion; Hugo describes its crumbling 'New Building', where stones fell on sleeping prisoners, and the cages of the 'Fine-Air' under the roof. He notes drily that the dungeons were renamed 'chambers of punishment' with exactly the same contents. The reform was in the word only.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`,`names`]},{id:`251-3`,chapter:251,kind:`theme`,title:`Something of the star and of the lightning`,text:`Nobody ever learned how Thénardier crossed the prison walls. Hugo calls the escaping man 'inspired' and compares the thief's leap to the flight of genius. It is a startling comparison for a man we have watched try to murder a bound prisoner. The drive for freedom, in Hugo, belongs to everyone, even the worst.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`251-4`,chapter:251,kind:`theme`,title:`Hullo! it's my father!`,text:`At dawn Gavroche sees the face on the wall and recognises it. 'Hullo! it's my father! Oh, that won't hinder.' He climbs the flue and saves him. Then he waits on a fence-post 'thinking that perhaps his father would turn towards him'. Thénardier never does. Hugo gives the moment no commentary at all.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`251-5`,chapter:251,kind:`motif`,title:`Now, whom are we to eat?`,text:`Safe on the pavement, Thénardier's first words are 'Now, whom are we to eat?' He came down from the wall the way he climbed up at Waterloo and in the Gorbeau house: hungry. The appetite Book VII described as the mark of the lowest mine is speaking.`,themes:[`poverty`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`251-6`,chapter:251,kind:`theme`,title:`It must be seen to`,text:`Brujon raises the Rue Plumet job again: a deserted street, an old gate, 'lone women'. Éponine looked and sent back a biscuit. 'The girl's no fool,' says Thénardier. 'Still, it must be seen to.' The reader now knows who lives behind that gate. The underworld's interest in the house has not ended with one refusal.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`251-7`,chapter:251,kind:`craft`,title:`Do you think so?`,text:`'It strikes me that it was your son.' 'Bah! do you think so?' The chapter ends on Thénardier's shrug. Hugo has just shown three brothers who do not know each other and a father who does not recognise the child who saved him. The family has dissolved so completely that its members are strangers.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`names`]},{id:`251-8`,chapter:251,kind:`motif`,title:`Icigo and icicaille`,text:`Thénardier, stranded on the wall, identifies his rescuers in the dark by their slang: 'icigo' from the barriers means Brujon, 'icicaille' from the Temple means Babet, plain French means Montparnasse. Each kind of 'here' is a signature.`,themes:[`names`,`paris`]},{id:`252-1`,chapter:252,kind:`craft`,title:`A digression in defence of a digression`,text:`Book VII opens by defending itself. When Hugo put a thief speaking argot into 'The Last Day of a Condemned Man' in 1829, readers were scandalised. He answers: 'When has horror ever excluded study? Since when has malady banished medicine?' The digression explains why the novel goes down into the gutter at all.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`252-2`,chapter:252,kind:`theme`,title:`The language of wretchedness`,text:`Hugo grants that every trade has its jargon, sailors, stockbrokers, academicians who call the sea 'Neptune'. But true argot is 'the tongue of wretchedness', a language of combat invented by misery against society. It is one more piece of evidence for his argument that crime is born of poverty.`,themes:[`poverty`,`names`]},{id:`252-3`,chapter:252,kind:`theme`,title:`Two kinds of historian`,text:`One historian records battles, kings and revolutions in daylight; the other descends into 'the depths, the people who toil, suffer, wait'. Hugo insists the two are one: 'Do we really know the mountain well when we are not acquainted with the cavern?' It is a statement of method for the whole novel.`,themes:[`history`,`poverty`]},{id:`252-4`,chapter:252,kind:`motif`,title:`A dressing-room for the tongue`,text:`'Slang is nothing but a dressing-room where the tongue having some bad action to perform, disguises itself.' Argot is French in a mask, as the characters of this book go about in aliases. Hugo extends his theme of disguise from people to words.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`252-5`,chapter:252,kind:`theme`,title:`The luminous and the shady`,text:`'In this world ... there are no fortunate.' The real division is between 'the luminous and the shady', and the aim is to increase the luminous: 'To teach reading, means to light the fire; every syllable spelled out sparkles.' And yet light is not joy: 'The luminous weep, if only over those in darkness.'`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`252-6`,chapter:252,kind:`question`,title:`Who knows whether man is not a recaptured offender?`,text:`'Let us have compassion on the chastised. Alas! Who are we ourselves?' Hugo suggests the earth itself resembles a jail and every human life feels like a sentence. If everyone is in some sense a prisoner, what follows for how we treat the ones in actual chains?`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`253-1`,chapter:253,kind:`motif`,title:`Words branded like shoulders`,text:`Argot words appear 'wrinkled and shrivelled ... beneath the hot iron of the executioner'; a phrase is like 'the shoulder of a thief branded with the fleur-de-lys'. Hugo describes the language in the same terms as the convict's body. The punishment society inflicts on men has marked their speech as well.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`253-2`,chapter:253,kind:`context`,title:`Villon's snows of yesteryear`,text:`Hugo claims that Villon's famous line 'Mais où sont les neiges d'antan?' (But where are the snows of yesteryear?) contains a word of thieves' slang, 'antan'. François Villon was a fifteenth-century poet and a convicted thief who narrowly escaped the gallows. The point: some of France's finest poetry has roots in the criminal underworld.`,themes:[`history`,`names`]},{id:`253-3`,chapter:253,kind:`theme`,title:`A subterranean edifice built by all the miserable`,text:`Dig into argot like a geologist and you find layers of Spanish, Italian, English, German, Latin, Basque and Celtic. 'Each accursed race has deposited its layer, each suffering has dropped its stone there.' Hugo turns a vocabulary into an archive of human misery across centuries, a 'third lower floor' made of words.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`253-4`,chapter:253,kind:`motif`,title:`Man is a derivative of the night`,text:`In argot the night is 'la sorgue' and man 'l'orgue'; an arrested man is 'sick', a condemned man 'dead', a prison is 'the college'. Hugo reads each metaphor as a lesson: these men think of society as an atmosphere that kills them. 'A whole penitentiary system can be evolved from that word.'`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`253-5`,chapter:253,kind:`theme`,title:`It was the rhymes that kept me up`,text:`Hugo describes the cellar of the Châtelet, eight feet below the Seine, where men bound for the galleys hung by neck-chains in mud, sometimes for a year, 'for stealing a hare from the king'. There most of the galley songs were born. 'Song lingers where there is no longer any hope.' Even here, he adds, one song is tender: love survives.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`love`,`history`]},{id:`253-6`,chapter:253,kind:`motif`,title:`A sombre Andromeda`,text:`The chapter ends with the human soul chained to 'the rock of night' like Andromeda, waiting for a knight of light to save it from the monster. Hugo's question is whether anyone will come. The image restates his whole argument as myth: ignorance is the monster, enlightenment the rescuer.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`254-1`,chapter:254,kind:`theme`,title:`From the sigh to the laugh`,text:`The old songs of the underworld were humble and plaintive; the pègre was 'the hare in hiding'. In the eighteenth century they turn insolent and jovial, sung 'while cutting a man's throat'. Hugo reads this as a sign that the criminal poor were losing their sense of guilt, and that something larger was coming.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`,`conscience`]},{id:`254-2`,chapter:254,kind:`context`,title:`Philosophers and sophists`,text:`Hugo defends the Enlightenment: Diderot toward the beautiful, Turgot the useful, Voltaire the true, Rousseau the just. Beside them grew 'hemlock in the virgin forest', writers such as Restif de La Bretonne whose works spread a cruder message among the miserable. Schiller's play The Robbers (1781), about a noble outlaw, is his German example.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`254-3`,chapter:254,kind:`theme`,title:`Revolution is the vaccine of Jacquerie`,text:`A 'jacquerie' was a blind peasant uprising, 'the revolt of discomfort against comfort'. Hugo argues that the French Revolution, 'that immense act of probity', prevented one by giving the poor rights. He cites the ragpickers who guarded the crown jewels in 1848: 'Rags mounted guard over the treasure.' For him, rights make men honest.`,themes:[`revolution`,`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`254-4`,chapter:254,kind:`craft`,title:`The scarecrow scares no longer`,text:`Hugo, writing in the 1860s, mocks those who still use fear of a red uprising in politics: 'The principal spring of the red spectre is broken.' This is the narrator in his own time speaking over the heads of his characters. The digression is as much about France under Napoleon III as about 1832.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`255-1`,chapter:255,kind:`theme`,title:`Social phthisis`,text:`There will be no more sudden apoplexy of revolt, Hugo says, but society can still die of consumption, and 'social phthisis is called misery'. His programme follows: schools for every aptitude, work for every arm, 'a limit to poverty without setting a limit to wealth'. The novel stops to state its politics outright.`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`255-2`,chapter:255,kind:`theme`,title:`A soul dying of hunger for the light`,text:`'If there is anything more heartbreaking than a body perishing for lack of bread, it is a soul which is dying from hunger for the light.' Bread and light, the two things the poor of this book have lacked from the start, are joined in one sentence.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`poverty`]},{id:`255-3`,chapter:255,kind:`craft`,title:`An austere interlude in a mournful drama`,text:`Hugo names his own digression: 'an austere interlude in a mournful drama'. He insists on hope, 'it will be saved', and then admits that even the most logical have hours of weakness. The chapter ends not with certainty but with an image: the ideal, small and bright, 'no more in danger than a star in the maw of the clouds'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`revolution`]},{id:`255-4`,chapter:255,kind:`question`,title:`To watch and to hope`,text:`The Book's title names two duties. Hugo has spent three chapters watching the underworld and its language; now he asks us to hope. Having just seen Thénardier step off a prison wall asking whom to eat, how convincing do you find his confidence that misery can be abolished 'by a simple elevation of level'?`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`,`revolution`]},{id:`256-1`,chapter:256,kind:`craft`,title:`An easier gate than Romeo's`,text:`Hugo invokes Romeo entering Juliet's garden and then deflates it at once: Marius has no wall to scale, only a rusty bar that wobbles 'after the fashion of old people's teeth'. The joke keeps the idyll on the ground. This is a real garden with a broken railing, and the lovers are two very young people, not a legend.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`256-2`,chapter:256,kind:`theme`,title:`Love that ruins or saves`,text:`'Love has no middle course; it either ruins or it saves.' Hugo stops to say Cosette, generous and unguarded, 'would have been lost' with a careless lover. A reader who remembers Fantine and Tholomyès hears whose daughter is being spoken of. The mother met the love that ruins. Hugo goes out of his way to tell us the daughter has met the other kind.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`256-3`,chapter:256,kind:`motif`,title:`Euphrasie`,text:`Cosette confides that her real name is Euphrasie, which Marius has never heard. The reader has known it since Fantine's chapters: Cosette was her mother's pet name for her. She decides she prefers Cosette because he does. Here is a true name shared in the dark, and then gladly set aside for the name that love uses.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`256-4`,chapter:256,kind:`motif`,title:`Full light, at night`,text:`The chapter is called 'Full Light', yet every scene in it happens after dark, under trees, with the planets turning overhead. For Hugo the light is inward. Two people 'nearer to the archangels than to mankind' make their own daylight in a neglected garden that no one else can see into.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`257-1`,chapter:257,kind:`craft`,title:`Everything except everything`,text:`'They had told each other everything except everything.' Marius gives his name, his father's rank and his quarrel with his grandfather. He leaves out the Gorbeau garret, Thénardier and her father's strange flight. The reader holds all the facts the two lovers have not shared, and feels the gap between their innocence and what is known.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`257-2`,chapter:257,kind:`context`,title:`Blind to the cholera`,text:`The cholera that the lovers fail to notice was real. It reached Paris in late March 1832 and killed some eighteen thousand Parisians that year, including the prime minister, Casimir Perier. Hugo sets the purest love in his book against the city's worst epidemic. Happiness, he suggests, is also a kind of deafness.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`]},{id:`257-3`,chapter:257,kind:`question`,title:`No yesterday, no tomorrow`,text:`Marius 'hardly remembers the morning' by the evening, and neither lover asks where any of this is leading: they 'consider that they have already arrived'. Is this suspension the purest part of their love, or something that leaves them unready for the world? Hugo describes it with delight and gives no verdict.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`258-1`,chapter:258,kind:`craft`,title:`Whitewash on her back`,text:`Valjean, who has outwitted Javert and climbed convent walls, notices only that Cosette has whitewash on her back. Hugo plays it as gentle comedy: the watchful father defeated by a girl who agrees to everything. The man who has spent his life on guard has stopped thinking that Marius exists.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`258-2`,chapter:258,kind:`theme`,title:`The debt Marius does not see`,text:`Éponine's 'Good evening, Monsieur Marius' embarrasses him. He owes her his happiness and has not thought of her once. Hugo adds that Marius has not even registered that she is a Thénardier, a name his father's will told him to honour. Notice that she speaks with downcast eyes and leaves abruptly. Marius sees none of it.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`258-3`,chapter:258,kind:`motif`,title:`From light to shadow`,text:`'Full Light', 'The Bewilderment of Perfect Happiness', 'The Beginning of Shadow': the chapter titles of this Book follow a day from noon to dusk. Hugo marks the turn not with a disaster but with a greeting on a boulevard that Marius answers awkwardly.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`259-1`,chapter:259,kind:`theme`,title:`The daughter of a wolf`,text:`Six armed men, her own father among them, are stopped by one starving girl sitting on a kerb. Éponine guards the house of the girl Marius loves against the family she belongs to. When Thénardier pleads that they must eat, she answers 'Burst!' Notice how fully the parent-child relation is reversed: the daughter threatens the father with the police.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`259-2`,chapter:259,kind:`motif`,title:`She no longer talks slang`,text:`'It is remarkable that Éponine did not talk slang.' The chapter title promises slang, and the gang speaks it. Hugo has just spent a whole Book on argot as the language of crime and misery. He makes Éponine's dropped slang a sign of what Marius has meant to her: love has changed the very words she uses.`,themes:[`names`,`poverty`]},{id:`259-3`,chapter:259,kind:`context`,title:`The nets at Saint-Cloud`,text:`Éponine says she does not care whether she is found dead on this pavement tomorrow 'or in the nets at Saint-Cloud' a year hence. Downstream from Paris, nets across the Seine at Saint-Cloud caught what the river carried, including the bodies of the drowned. Her bravado is the bravado of someone who already knows where the city's poor end up.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`259-4`,chapter:259,kind:`craft`,title:`Stopped by omens`,text:`It is not the law that saves the house in the Rue Plumet. It is a girl's threat and Brujon's superstition: two sparrows fighting that morning, a quarrelsome woman jostled that evening. Hugo gives the underworld its own rules and prophets. These men fear bad luck more than the police.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`260-1`,chapter:260,kind:`craft`,title:`A prose poem in place of a scene`,text:`This one-paragraph chapter steps out of the street and into a forest. Ruffians become beasts of prey, and the girl at the gate becomes a spectral figure in a shroud. 'The ferocious fear the sinister; wolves recoil when they encounter a ghoul.' Hugo gives the image its own chapter so that it can sink in before the story moves on.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`260-2`,chapter:260,kind:`theme`,title:`The power of having nothing`,text:`What stops six armed men? Hugo's answer is that Éponine has passed beyond fear: thin, coughing, indifferent to death, she belongs to 'that which emerges from the cemetery'. Poverty has worn her down to something the predators cannot grip. It is a frightening kind of strength, and Hugo does not pretend it is a healthy one.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`261-1`,chapter:261,kind:`theme`,title:`Boots that let in water`,text:`For six weeks love has floated free of money. One sentence from Cosette brings it down: her father may take her to England. Marius's answer is an inventory of poverty: debts to Courfeyrac, a three-franc hat, boots that let in water, no money for a passport. The pride that made him refuse help now stands like a wall between him and her.`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`261-2`,chapter:261,kind:`motif`,title:`An address on the wall`,text:`Marius scratches '16 Rue de la Verrerie' into the plaster with his penknife, so that Cosette can always find him. Notice the change. For weeks the garden has been a place without writing or witnesses. Now it carries words cut into its wall, and words on a wall can be read by anyone who passes that way.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`261-3`,chapter:261,kind:`craft`,title:`A word of honour`,text:`Marius gives his word of honour for the first time in his life, 'feeling his father beside him': if she goes, he will die. Then he speaks darkly of a 'he' who receives only in the evening, and Cosette does not understand. Hugo lets a desperate plan form offstage, with Marius himself calling it senseless.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`262-1`,chapter:262,kind:`theme`,title:`What have you come here for?`,text:`For four years M. Gillenormand has longed for the bell to ring. When it does, joy comes out of his mouth as harshness. Hugo writes two proud men who love each other and cannot say so. Each wants the other to yield first. Notice how the old man summons and dismisses his daughter just to make Marius wait.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`262-2`,chapter:262,kind:`craft`,title:`The word 'Father'`,text:`Marius, who fought to claim the father his grandfather erased, now calls the old man 'father' in his plea, and that single word undoes him. Gillenormand hauls him into a chair, presses a hundred louis on him and tells him to chatter about his love. Hugo shows how near reconciliation came.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`262-3`,chapter:262,kind:`context`,title:`Pamela and the lancer's gossip`,text:`At 'Rue Plumet' the old man recalls Théodule's tale of a 'Pamela' behind a railing, a pretty girl in the eighteenth-century sense of Richardson's heroine. Gillenormand is a man of Louis XV's century, for whom such girls are for amusement, not marriage. 'Make her your mistress' is his idea of generosity. To Marius it is a sword.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`262-4`,chapter:262,kind:`theme`,title:`Father and wife`,text:`'Five years ago you insulted my father; today you have insulted my wife.' Marius sets the two insults side by side: the dead father he has made his religion, and a girl he has not married. The old man runs to the window calling his name four times. Hugo leaves him with 'nothing left in his eyes'.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`263-1`,chapter:263,kind:`craft`,title:`The same words, read from the other side`,text:`The address Marius scratched for Cosette is found by her father, who reads it as a signal to enemies or a warning to himself. The reader knows exactly who wrote it and why. Hugo uses the same twenty letters to show two worlds that never meet: a lover's tenderness and a fugitive's dread.`,themes:[`love`,`names`]},{id:`263-2`,chapter:263,kind:`context`,title:`Pépin and Morey`,text:`Valjean fears that police hunting 'political men like Pépin or Morey' might stumble on him. Pépin, a grocer of the Faubourg Saint-Antoine, and Morey, a saddler, were real republican conspirators of the period. Valjean's private danger is now tied to public events. When Paris grows restless, a hidden man is less safe.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`263-3`,chapter:263,kind:`motif`,title:`A message without a face`,text:`'Move away from your house.' A folded paper drops from nowhere, and a small figure in a grey blouse vanishes over a parapet. Valjean's life has been shaped by documents: a yellow passport, a convict's number, borrowed names. Now the warning comes unsigned, and he cannot tell whether it is from a friend or an enemy.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`264-1`,chapter:264,kind:`theme`,title:`Lamarque's funeral sounds like Chinese`,text:`'Are you coming to General Lamarque's funeral?' Courfeyrac asks, and to Marius the words mean nothing. Hugo makes history arrive at the moment a young man has lost interest in everything. The revolt his friends have been planning for chapters reaches him only as distant noise from the boulevards.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`264-2`,chapter:264,kind:`motif`,title:`Javert's pistols`,text:`Marius pockets the two loaded pistols Javert gave him for the Gorbeau ambush, 'without knowing why'. They were handed to him in the service of the police. He now carries them, unthinking, through a city at war with its government. Objects in this book keep passing from one side of the law to the other.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`264-3`,chapter:264,kind:`craft`,title:`A voice through the trees`,text:`Hugo withholds a face at the climax of the chapter. Marius has decided to die, and the call to the barricade comes as a voice from the dark street, hoarse and half familiar. He sees only someone who looks like a young man running off. The summons that sends him into the revolt comes from someone he cannot see.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`264-4`,chapter:264,kind:`question`,title:`Why go to the barricade?`,text:`Marius goes to the Rue de la Chanvrerie because Cosette is gone and dying seems all that remains, not because he believes in the republic. Is that a lesser reason to take up arms, or simply a more honest one? Hugo has given his friends their convictions; he gives Marius only grief.`,themes:[`revolution`,`love`]},{id:`265-1`,chapter:265,kind:`theme`,title:`Choosing which child to sacrifice`,text:`Mabeuf looks over his books 'like a father choosing which child to sacrifice'. Hugo keeps an exact ledger of the decline: twenty sous for a book that cost twenty francs, a Robert Estienne sold to buy an Aldus, an Elzevir sold for the cab fare to a dinner where no one speaks to him. Poverty strips a man who has harmed no one, page by page.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`265-2`,chapter:265,kind:`motif`,title:`Honesty that does not pay`,text:`Mabeuf handed the purse that fell into his garden to the police as lost property, since he could not believe a star had coined itself into louis. No one claimed it. Hugo gives us one more honest act that changes nothing. Virtue in this book is often its own and only reward.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`265-3`,chapter:265,kind:`motif`,title:`The missing book`,text:`The chapter ends on a gesture: Mabeuf takes his hat, reaches for a book to put under his arm, finds none and says 'Ah! truly!' His whole life is in that habit. With the last volume sold for Mother Plutarque, he walks out towards the sound of firing, not knowing what else to do with his hands.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`266-1`,chapter:266,kind:`craft`,title:`Giving the other side the floor`,text:`Hugo opens his history of the rising by letting the opposition speak. The 'golden mean' school says riots shut shops, depress the funds and cost twenty, forty, sixty millions a day. Hugo states their case fairly, even generously, before rejecting it. This digression teaches the reader how to judge what is about to happen in the streets.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`266-2`,chapter:266,kind:`theme`,title:`Effects and causes`,text:`The bourgeois argument counts effects in francs. Hugo answers that if July 1830 cost a hundred and twenty millions, putting Philip V on the Spanish throne cost two milliards, and he 'would still prefer the 14th of July'. His real question is not what revolt costs but why people rise at all.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`266-3`,chapter:266,kind:`context`,title:`Two years of riots`,text:`Hugo writes of the riots that followed 1830 as though everyone remembers them. The July Revolution put Louis-Philippe on the throne but left most people without a vote. The early 1830s brought repeated unrest, including the silk-workers' rising at Lyons in November 1831. June 1832 is one wave in a stormy sea.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`267-1`,chapter:267,kind:`theme`,title:`Insurrection and revolt`,text:`The chapter gives the book a key word pair. Insurrection is the whole people reclaiming its rights from a usurping fraction. Revolt is a fraction attacking the whole. 'There is no insurrection except in a forward direction.' The same cannon can be wrong one day and right another. Hugo asks us to judge every rising by its direction, not by its violence.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`267-2`,chapter:267,kind:`context`,title:`The vote as cure`,text:`Hugo argues that universal suffrage dissolves riots at birth by giving insurrection the ballot. In 1832 the French electorate was tiny: under the July Monarchy fewer than two hundred thousand men could vote, in a nation of over thirty million. Hugo was writing in 1862, after manhood suffrage had arrived in 1848.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`267-3`,chapter:267,kind:`craft`,title:`'We have seen this'`,text:`Hugo promises to tell one small, little-known episode of 5 and 6 June, changing a few names. He presents himself as an eyewitness: 'We have seen this.' The novel now claims the authority of testimony. What follows is offered as the foliage of history, the part the historians leave out.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`268-1`,chapter:268,kind:`context`,title:`General Lamarque`,text:`Jean Maximilien Lamarque was a real general of the Empire and a liberal deputy, hugely popular with the people. He died of cholera on 1 June 1832. His funeral on 5 June, with Lafayette speaking at the Austerlitz bridge, became the spark of the rising. Hugo follows the historical record closely here.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`268-2`,chapter:268,kind:`craft`,title:`Nobody knows what happened in that minute`,text:`Dragoons and crowd touch, three shots ring out, and Hugo admits that 'no one knows' who began: a trumpet, a child's dagger-blow? He writes history as a crowd lives it, full of rumour, red flags, a man with a red beard pointed out. The first dead include an officer and an old deaf woman closing her window.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`268-3`,chapter:268,kind:`motif`,title:`A burial and a birth`,text:`The title calls the funeral 'an occasion to be born again'. Lamarque, who kept the sadness of Waterloo for seventeen years, is carried through Paris with the sword of the Hundred Days. The dead general becomes a rallying point for the living. In Hugo's Paris, funerals are where revolutions begin.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`269-1`,chapter:269,kind:`craft`,title:`Riot as inventory`,text:`Two hundred and thirty guns, sixty-four swords, eighty-three pistols; twenty-seven barricades in the Halles quarter alone. Hugo's lists turn chaos into a register. The style tells you he is writing history as well as fiction: precise, street by street, as if mapping a battlefield like Waterloo.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`269-2`,chapter:269,kind:`context`,title:`The author under fire`,text:`'The author of this book' sheltered for half an hour behind a half-column in the Passage du Saumon. Hugo really was caught in the fighting of 5 June 1832, and he puts himself in the story as a witness. The novel's narrator steps briefly into the street he is describing.`,themes:[`history`,`paris`]},{id:`269-3`,chapter:269,kind:`theme`,title:`The calm at the centre`,text:`A third of Paris is in the insurgents' hands. Around the Tuileries 'solitude forms', and Louis-Philippe is 'perfectly serene'. Hugo sets the boiling streets against the stillness of power. The centre of Paris has become a citadel where, he notes, no fighting has yet begun.`,themes:[`revolution`,`paris`]},{id:`270-1`,chapter:270,kind:`theme`,title:`Billiards beside grapeshot`,text:`In ordinary riots Paris barely notices: a few streets from the firing, billiard balls click and theatres play vaudeville. In 1831 a fusillade paused to let a wedding pass; in 1839 a cocoa-seller served both sides. Hugo loves this nonchalance as a sign of the city's size and gaiety, the city of Voltaire and Napoleon.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`270-2`,chapter:270,kind:`theme`,title:`This time Paris is afraid`,text:`Then the tone changes. Shutters close in distant quarters, eight hundred are arrested by nine o'clock, and wives and mothers ask why he has not come home. Hugo brings the rising into private houses. History is now something families wait for behind bolted doors.`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`270-3`,chapter:270,kind:`motif`,title:`The tocsin of Saint-Merry`,text:`Through these chapters one sound recurs: the alarm bell of Saint-Merry, ringing over the city. Hugo builds the night around listening, to cavalry, trumpets and firing, and above all to that 'lamentable' peal. The reader, like the households of Paris, hears the rising before seeing it.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`271-1`,chapter:271,kind:`theme`,title:`Where are my two children?`,text:`Gavroche still wonders 'where the devil his two children are'. The reader knows they are his own brothers, though he never learned it. He spent a night with his brothers and the dawn with his father without knowing either bond. A child of eleven or so talks like a parent about the only family he chose.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`271-2`,chapter:271,kind:`motif`,title:`A pistol with no trigger`,text:`Gavroche 'borrows' a holster-pistol from a junk shop and marches off to the wars, only to find it has no trigger. His revolution is courage and theatre without means. Hugo enjoys the joke and lets it stand for something larger: the very poor go to war with whatever they can find.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`271-3`,chapter:271,kind:`craft`,title:`A gamin of letters`,text:`Who wrote Gavroche's songs? Perhaps Gavroche himself. Hugo makes him a folk poet who mixes the birds' repertory with the workshops'. He once spent three months with a printer and ran an errand for an Academician. The street child is given an authorship of his own, and Paris itself seems to sing through him.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`272-1`,chapter:272,kind:`theme`,title:`Helping the enemy up`,text:`On his way to fight the government, Gavroche puts down his pistol to help a fallen National Guard lancer, and then the horse. Hugo slips the act in without comment. Gavroche wants to overturn the authorities, but he will not leave a man or a horse lying in the street.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`revolution`]},{id:`272-2`,chapter:272,kind:`craft`,title:`Four corners of old age`,text:`Three portresses and a ragpicker gossip in a doorway, and Hugo notes the hierarchy even here: the ragpicker defers to the portresses, the humble rank of the refuse heap. Their chatter about cats, meat prices and the Duc de Bordeaux is the voice of the Marais, where order is a habit. To them Gavroche is just 'a bastard'.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`272-3`,chapter:272,kind:`motif`,title:`A gaunt poodle`,text:`Gavroche, insulted as a beggar's brat, pauses to pity a starving poodle 'whose hoops all show'. The street child recognises his own kind. Like his kindness to the lancer's horse, it is a tiny gesture, and it tells us who Gavroche is more surely than his swagger does.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`273-1`,chapter:273,kind:`craft`,title:`A cannonball that is a pebble`,text:`The barber declares he would rather die of a cannonball in the belly than slowly in bed. A crash, he turns pale, 'a cannonball!', and the old soldier picks up a pebble. Hugo sets the veteran's list of wounds from Marengo to Waterloo beside the barber's bluster, and the comedy deflates both bravado and fear.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`273-2`,chapter:273,kind:`theme`,title:`What has anyone done to that gamin?`,text:`The hairdresser is outraged: what has anyone done to that gamin? The reader knows. This is the shop where the two little boys were turned out into the rain. Gavroche has not forgotten. His stone is a rough, street-level justice for children nobody else defended.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`parenthood`]},{id:`273-3`,chapter:273,kind:`context`,title:`The Emperor's wounds`,text:`The veteran's catalogue of battles, Marengo, Austerlitz, Jena, Friedland, the Moskowa, Lutzen, Waterloo, runs through Napoleon's whole career. In 1832 such men were still everywhere in Paris, and their memories fed the crowd that buried Lamarque. The Empire is still alive in a barber's chair.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`274-1`,chapter:274,kind:`theme`,title:`Not firing out of the ranks`,text:`Bahorel tears down the Archbishop's Lenten notice, and Gavroche is won over. Enjolras rebukes him: the Archbishop is not the enemy, and one must not 'fire out of the ranks with the soul' any more than with a gun. Notice how Enjolras wants anger aimed. His revolution has no room for spite.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`274-2`,chapter:274,kind:`craft`,title:`Latin for a gamin`,text:`'Hercle!' 'Para bellum!' Gavroche asks what the Latin means and approves once he understands. Hugo enjoys the meeting of the students' classroom culture and the street child's quickness. The band is a mixture of social worlds, and Gavroche picks up their language as fast as he once picked up slang.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`274-3`,chapter:274,kind:`motif`,title:`An old man, unarmed`,text:`The chapter closes on a figure among the clubs and bayonets: an extremely old man, unarmed, hurrying so as not to be left behind. 'Keksekça?' asks Gavroche. Hugo saves the name for the last line: M. Mabeuf. The book-lover who had nothing left to sell is marching with the insurgents.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`275-1`,chapter:275,kind:`theme`,title:`'That is good'`,text:`Fighting, cannon, flinging the government to the earth: to each, Mabeuf answers 'That is good.' The man who once cared only for plants and books now walks with a firm step and 'the face of a man asleep'. Hugo does not give him politics. He gives him the calm of someone who has nothing left to lose.`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`275-2`,chapter:275,kind:`motif`,title:`An old regicide`,text:`The students decide the silent old man must be a former member of the Convention, a regicide. A legend forms around Mabeuf in minutes, because a revolt needs ancestors. Hugo has already shown us one real Conventionist, the dying G. at Digne. This one is only a timid churchwarden whom grief has made fearless.`,themes:[`names`,`revolution`]},{id:`275-3`,chapter:275,kind:`craft`,title:`A nonsense ballad as marching song`,text:`Gavroche marches ahead 'like a trumpet', singing of Charlot and Charlotte and two drunken sparrows, with refrains for Chatou, Passy, Meudon and Pantin. No Marseillaise here. Hugo leads the insurgents towards Saint-Merry to the tune of a street song, the city's own music, cheeky and light.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`]},{id:`276-1`,chapter:276,kind:`craft`,title:`Strangers in the ranks`,text:`Two newcomers join the band, and Hugo names neither. One is a tall, grey-haired man of bold bearing whom none of the leaders knows. The other is a pale, slight young workman who has waited an hour at Courfeyrac's lodge asking for Marius. A crowd on the march takes in anyone. Hugo lets us see them arrive, and does not explain them.`,themes:[`names`,`revolution`]},{id:`276-2`,chapter:276,kind:`motif`,title:`Monsieur de Courfeyrac`,text:`'If you call me Monsieur de Courfeyrac again, I shall call you Mother de Veuvain.' Courfeyrac's father really was M. de Courfeyrac, but the republican son has dropped the noble particle. On the way to a barricade he will not be given a title even by his portress. In this book names are always a statement of where one stands.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`276-3`,chapter:276,kind:`craft`,title:`A mob does not go where it intends`,text:`The band means to reach Saint-Merry, overshoots it and ends up 'without quite knowing how' in the Rue Saint-Denis. Hugo keeps showing chance at work in history. The great events of a rising are placed by wrong turnings, a forgotten hat and a coffer fetched from under dirty linen.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`277-1`,chapter:277,kind:`craft`,title:`A letter for a map`,text:`As he drew Waterloo as a capital A, Hugo now draws the Halles quarter as an N, with the Rue de la Chanvrerie as one upright. The digression is doing a practical job: it gives the reader a map of a battlefield before the battle. It also says that this tangle of streets is a real place with a three-hundred-year history.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`]},{id:`277-2`,chapter:277,kind:`context`,title:`A vanished quarter`,text:`'All of it vanished' when the Mondétour labyrinth was opened up in 1847, and the Rue Rambuteau 'devoured' the old streets. Hugo, writing in exile in the early 1860s, describes a Paris that no longer existed. Like the convent of the Petit-Picpus, the Corinthe is preserved only in his book.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`]},{id:`277-3`,chapter:277,kind:`motif`,title:`Carpe ho ras`,text:`Father Hucheloup advertises his stuffed carps, 'Carpes au gras', in his own spelling. Rain washes out two letters and leaves 'Carpe ho ras', so that 'knowing no French, he had equalled Horace'. Hugo loves accidents that turn the ordinary into Latin. The wall of a cheap wine-shop now tells its customers to seize the hour.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`277-4`,chapter:277,kind:`theme`,title:`Out of pity`,text:`After Hucheloup's death the cooking turns execrable and the wine worse, and the friends keep coming 'out of pity', as Bossuet says. They eat on credit, pay badly and are always welcome. The Corinthe is a picture of loyalty among poor people: the students and the bearded widow keep each other going.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`278-1`,chapter:278,kind:`theme`,title:`A sceptic among believers`,text:`While Paris boils, Grantaire breakfasts on oysters and harangues: creation is bankrupt, God patches events like a tradesman with an empty till, and the one reality is drink. He is the voice of doubt inside the ABC. Hugo gives him wit and some truth, and lets him drink himself away from the cause his friends serve.`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`]},{id:`278-2`,chapter:278,kind:`craft`,title:`Make it here!`,text:`Bossuet, drunk on a windowsill with his back in the rain, sees the band pass the end of the street and bellows to Courfeyrac: 'This is a good place! Make it here!' That is how the barricade of the Rue de la Chanvrerie is placed. Hugo, who has just lectured on the causes of revolt, shows how its sites are chosen, by chance and by friendship.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`278-3`,chapter:278,kind:`question`,title:`Sent to Bossuet, not to me`,text:`Enjolras sends his warning to Bossuet, and Grantaire mutters that Enjolras disdains him. Among all his jokes this is the one remark said without a joke. What does Grantaire want from Enjolras, and why does it matter more to him than the funeral or the riot?`,themes:[`revolution`,`love`]},{id:`279-1`,chapter:279,kind:`craft`,title:`A city turned into a wall`,text:`Iron bars from the wine-shop grating, paving-stones, a lime-dealer's dray, the widow's empty casks, beams from house-fronts, a passing omnibus: in minutes the street is barred higher than a man. Hugo's catalogue shows the barricade as Paris itself, rearranged. Everyday things of the city become a fortification.`,themes:[`paris`,`revolution`]},{id:`279-2`,chapter:279,kind:`context`,title:`Non licet omnibus`,text:`Bossuet hands down 'the ladies', takes the omnibus by the bridle and declares in Latin that not everyone may go to Corinth: 'Non licet omnibus adire Corinthum.' The old proverb, from Horace, meant that not everyone can afford the pleasures of Corinth. Here 'omnibus', 'for everyone', is also the vehicle. Even building a barricade, these students make Latin puns.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`279-3`,chapter:279,kind:`theme`,title:`'Let me sleep here'`,text:`Enjolras orders the drunken Grantaire off the barricade, and the rebuke sobers him at once. He looks at Enjolras 'with indescribable gentleness' and asks to sleep there. Enjolras tells him he is incapable of believing, thinking, willing, living and dying. 'You will see,' Grantaire answers, and falls asleep. Grantaire believes in nothing except Enjolras, and Enjolras cannot see it.`,themes:[`revolution`,`love`]},{id:`279-4`,chapter:279,kind:`theme`,title:`Jesus with Rothschild's fortune`,text:`In his drunken dithyramb Grantaire says that if kind hearts had fat purses there would be no more poor, and imagines Jesus Christ with Rothschild's fortune. It is a joke and also the novel's question in the mouth of its scoffer. Goodness without means has been shown again and again: the bishop, Mabeuf, Marius.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`280-1`,chapter:280,kind:`craft`,title:`Consolation, Arab style`,text:`Courfeyrac 'consoles' Mother Hucheloup by telling her that wrecking her shop avenges a fine she once paid, and Hugo caps it with the tale of an Arab father who evened a slap by slapping his daughter's other cheek. Even as the bullets are cast, Hugo keeps the comedy going. These young men laugh at the edge of danger, and the book laughs with them.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`280-2`,chapter:280,kind:`motif`,title:`Spoons into bullets`,text:`The establishment's pewter mugs, spoons and forks go into the brazier and come out as bullets; dishcloths become lint for wounds. Notice how the tavern's ordinary life is converted, object by object, into war. Hugo lists the volunteers the same way: a breastplate of grey paper, an awl, a cartridge-box marked 'Public Order'.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`280-3`,chapter:280,kind:`theme`,title:`Brothers without names`,text:`Pale young men and bronzed longshoremen work side by side, 'brothers who do not know each other's names'. Hugo makes the barricade a fraternity of strangers, built on a shared hope of help at three in the morning, a sure regiment and Paris rising. The word fraternity on the Republic's banner is lived here for an evening.`,themes:[`revolution`,`names`]},{id:`280-4`,chapter:280,kind:`theme`,title:`A gun for the children`,text:`Gavroche goes from man to man demanding a real gun; he had one in 1830, he says. Enjolras answers that when there are enough for the men they will give some to the children. 'Gamin!' 'Greenhorn!' The exchange is comic, but it raises a real question the book has carried since the Thénardier children: what place does a child have in the grown-ups' wars?`,themes:[`revolution`,`parenthood`]},{id:`281-1`,chapter:281,kind:`craft`,title:`Correcting the newspapers`,text:`The papers called the barricade nearly impregnable and as high as a first floor. Hugo corrects them: six or seven feet, with steps inside, a gap at one end, an omnibus pole for a flagstaff. The novelist writes like a military historian checking the record. His precision lets the reader stand inside the redoubt and see its weak points.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`281-2`,chapter:281,kind:`motif`,title:`The coffer opened`,text:`The square coffer Courfeyrac fetched from under his linen is opened on a table: cartridges. 'A tremor and a silence' pass through the bravest. Up to now the day has been flags, songs and furniture. Thirty cartridges to each man turn the street game into something final.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`281-3`,chapter:281,kind:`motif`,title:`Waiting in the dusk`,text:`The alarm beating through Paris becomes a noise they no longer hear. They load without haste, Enjolras posts three sentinels, and the twilight thickens among houses 'that seem dead'. Hugo slows the prose to the rhythm of waiting. Darkness is coming in from outside the barricade, and so is something unseen.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`282-1`,chapter:282,kind:`theme`,title:`Love verses with loaded carbines`,text:`Hugo insists on telling what they did while waiting, 'since it is history': they sat together as in their student days and recited love poems. Prouvaire's poem remembers a garret, a burnt muff, a Dante used as a table for chestnuts. These young men are not only revolutionaries. Their politics come from the same young hearts that wrote poems about love.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`282-2`,chapter:282,kind:`craft`,title:`A poem inside the novel`,text:`Hugo stops his prose for verses, a whole poem of youth in the Latin Quarter. Its lightness, against 'the funereal calm of the deserted streets', gives it what he calls a pathetic charm. The poem looks back at a love that has passed, while the men around it wait for the attack.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`282-3`,chapter:282,kind:`motif`,title:`A torch for the flag`,text:`The wax torch is set in a cage of paving-stones so that all its light falls on the red flag and none on the men. Street and barricade sink into gloom; the flag glows, 'its scarlet deepened to a terrible purple'. Hugo stages the barricade like a theatre at night. Only the idea is lit, and the men stay in the dark.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`283-1`,chapter:283,kind:`craft`,title:`Recognition on a child's face`,text:`Before any word is spoken, Gavroche's face runs through 'every grimace' of disbelief, uncertainty and conviction as he circles the stranger on tiptoe. Hugo stages the unmasking first as mime. Then the questions, and the name: 'Javert.' The tall recruit from the Rue des Billettes has been the hunter all along, and the chapter makes us wait for the word.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`283-2`,chapter:283,kind:`theme`,title:`The mouse who has caught the cat`,text:`'It's the mouse who has caught the cat,' says Gavroche. Javert, the law in person, is bound to the post in the middle of a wine-shop by rebels. He does not cry out, and raises his head 'with the intrepid serenity of the man who has never lied'. Hugo grants him dignity even in defeat: he is wrong, perhaps, but he is honest.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`283-3`,chapter:283,kind:`theme`,title:`Judges, not assassins`,text:`'Then finish the business with a blow from a knife.' 'Spy, we are judges and not assassins.' Enjolras claims for the barricade the very thing Javert serves: lawful judgement. Each side calls itself the law. Javert is to be shot ten minutes before the barricade falls, and both men speak of it calmly, as a matter of procedure.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`revolution`]},{id:`283-4`,chapter:283,kind:`context`,title:`Prefect Gisquet`,text:`The card found on Javert bears the signature of 'M. Gisquet', the real Prefect of Police of Paris from 1831 to 1836. Hugo threads real names through his invented ones, as he did with Lamarque and Lafayette. A fictional inspector carries a real man's orders, which makes the fiction feel documented.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`284-1`,chapter:284,kind:`theme`,title:`He killed, therefore I killed him`,text:`Enjolras executes Le Cabuc for murdering the old porter, then tells the barricade: 'I have judged myself also.' The reader has seen this rigour before, in Javert asking Madeleine to dismiss him for a mistaken denunciation. Hugo sets the revolutionary and the policeman as mirror images. Both are pure, both are merciless, and both apply their law to themselves.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`284-2`,chapter:284,kind:`theme`,title:`Fatality and Fraternity`,text:`Enjolras calls necessity 'a monster of the old world named Fatality', and says the law of progress is for Fatality to vanish before Fraternity. He uses death but hates it, he says. Hugo calls him 'executioner and priest', and the speech is both a creed and a confession. His friends watch him with admiration mixed with compassion.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`284-3`,chapter:284,kind:`craft`,title:`The porter who said no`,text:`The old porter at his window cannot even see who is aiming at him. He says 'That cannot be, gentlemen' and is shot under the chin. Hugo ends the image with a head on the sill and a little white smoke. Like the deaf old woman at the Austerlitz bridge, the first innocent dead of the barricade is someone who only wanted to keep his door shut.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`284-4`,chapter:284,kind:`context`,title:`Le Cabuc and Claquesous`,text:`Hugo offers, as 'a tradition of the police, strange but probably well founded', that Le Cabuc was Claquesous, the masked member of Patron-Minette who slipped away after the Gorbeau arrests. A police card was found on the body; Hugo says he held the 1832 report in 1848. A man whose life was 'all shadows' ends as a riddle, on the edge of both the underworld and the police.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`285-1`,chapter:285,kind:`craft`,title:`A descent through Paris`,text:`Marius walks from the Rue de Rivoli, with its gaslight and ices, through shut shops with chatting merchants, then a silent crowd, then the army's bivouacs, into streets 'like a cellar'. Hugo turns the walk into a cross-section of the city, layer by layer, 'like a descent of black steps'. Each stage is darker and quieter than the one before.`,themes:[`paris`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`285-2`,chapter:285,kind:`motif`,title:`Two white horses and a shaving dish`,text:`In the black streets Marius meets the two omnibus horses Bossuet unharnessed that morning, still wandering 'with the patience of brutes'. A stray shot pierces a barber's brass dish, 'still to be seen there in 1848'. Hugo uses small physical leftovers of the day, and his own memory of them, to make the night real.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`285-3`,chapter:285,kind:`theme`,title:`The key to the tomb`,text:`To Marius the voice through the twilight sounds like destiny: he wished to die, and 'a hand in the darkness offers the key to the tomb'. Notice that he goes to the barricade as a lover in despair, armed by chance with Javert's pistols, not as a convert to his friends' politics.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`286-1`,chapter:286,kind:`context`,title:`Paris from the air, again`,text:`'An owl's view of Paris' answers a famous chapter in Hugo's Notre-Dame de Paris (1831), 'A Bird's-Eye View of Paris'. There he rebuilt the medieval city from the towers of the cathedral. Here, from above, the old Halles quarter is a black hole in the centre of Paris, with only Saint-Jacques and Saint-Merry rising out of it.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`286-2`,chapter:286,kind:`motif`,title:`Order means night`,text:`'The insurrection's invisible police keep order, that is, night': every window that showed a candle at dusk received a shot. Hugo turns his central image pair upside down. Here darkness is the rebels' protection, and the light belongs to the bayonets tightening in a ring around them.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`286-3`,chapter:286,kind:`theme`,title:`The voice from below`,text:`Hugo sets the political battle, the schools against the middle classes, inside a deeper sound. In 'wretched old Paris beneath the splendour of happy Paris', the people roar, a voice 'from below like the lion and from on high like the thunder'. Behind the students' principles he wants us to hear the misery the whole book has described.`,themes:[`revolution`,`poverty`]},{id:`287-1`,chapter:287,kind:`theme`,title:`The sword that would not come`,text:`Marius thinks of his father's sword, sold by his grandfather, and decides it did well not to come: a sword from Marengo would not go to the Rue de la Chanvrerie. Civil war feels like desecrating Colonel Pontmercy. The dead father has become Marius's conscience. Notice that he argues with his father's ghost before he argues with himself.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`287-2`,chapter:287,kind:`question`,title:`Reason or rationalisation?`,text:`Marius works his way to a 'splendid rectification': there is only just and unjust war, and the monarchy is itself a foreigner. Hugo remarks on the soul's strange aptitude for cold reasoning in extremity, 'the thread of the syllogism floating unbroken in the storm'. Has Marius found a conviction, or given grief the reasons it wanted?`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`]},{id:`287-3`,chapter:287,kind:`motif`,title:`A dead man at the window`,text:`When Marius looks up, a spectator above the barricade seems 'singularly attentive': it is the porter killed by Le Cabuc, staring, mouth open, blood trailing down the wall. Hugo makes the dead a witness. Marius came here to look death in the face, and this is the first face he sees.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`288-1`,chapter:288,kind:`context`,title:`The Commander's tread`,text:`The troops approach with a measured, heavy step, 'like the stone tread of the Commander's statue multiplied into a legion'. Hugo borrows from the Don Juan story, in which the statue of a murdered man walks to dinner and drags the sinner down to hell. The army arrives as something inhuman and inevitable, a law of stone walking through the dark.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`288-2`,chapter:288,kind:`theme`,title:`Two words each`,text:`'Who goes there?' 'The French Revolution!' 'Fire!' The whole conflict is reduced to a challenge and an answer. Enjolras does not give his name or his cause; he gives an epoch. Hugo makes the exchange sound like history speaking to itself across a dark street.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`288-3`,chapter:288,kind:`craft`,title:`An unanswered question`,text:`The volley cuts the flagstaff, and Enjolras asks who will plant the flag again. No one answers; even he feels a thrill. 'Does no one volunteer?' Hugo ends the chapter on that silence, and the title, 'Act First', tells us the barricade is being staged as a drama with a second act to come.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`289-1`,chapter:289,kind:`theme`,title:`A churchwarden becomes '93`,text:`The insurgents cry 'It is the member of the Convention!' as Mabeuf climbs, and in the torchlight they think they see 'the spectre of '93'. Courfeyrac knows better: a 'brave blockhead', a gentle botanist. Enjolras answers: 'The head of a blockhead and the heart of a Brutus.' The barricade needs a legend, and it makes one out of a timid old man.`,themes:[`revolution`,`names`]},{id:`289-2`,chapter:289,kind:`motif`,title:`'This is our flag now'`,text:`Enjolras kisses the dead man's brow, lifts off his coat and holds up its bloody holes: 'This is our flag now.' The red cloth has fallen and a man's body takes its place. He tells his friends to defend this dead old man as they would a living father. The barricade has found its symbol in a person.`,themes:[`revolution`,`parenthood`]},{id:`289-3`,chapter:289,kind:`question`,title:`Why does Mabeuf climb?`,text:`Hugo describes Mabeuf's eyes as lit 'with the mournful flame of aberration'. He sat for hours with his head thrust forward 'as if gazing over a precipice', and he cries 'Death!' along with 'Republic!'. Did he die for the Republic, or of the slow loss of everything he loved? Is it possible to separate the two?`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`290-1`,chapter:290,kind:`craft`,title:`A title that warns`,text:`'Gavroche Would Have Done Better to Accept Enjolras' Carbine.' Hugo announces the mistake before we see it, then delivers it inside the chapter: the giant guardsman, the trigger, nothing. The title turns the scene into suspense. We know the gun will fail; we do not know what will happen to the boy when it does.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`290-2`,chapter:290,kind:`motif`,title:`Javert's weapons`,text:`Gavroche's borrowed gun, taken from Javert, is not loaded. The shots that save him come from Javert's two pistols in Marius's hands. The policeman's weapons, scattered by chance, fail and save on the rebels' side. Meanwhile Enjolras tells the bound Javert, as Mabeuf's body passes: 'It will be your turn presently!'`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`290-3`,chapter:290,kind:`theme`,title:`Bahorel falls`,text:`Bahorel, the scarlet-waistcoated joker who loved a riot 'like a fish in water', kills the first guard over the barricade and is killed by the second. Hugo gives his death one line, in the middle of the scramble. On a barricade death does not wait for a speech, and the tone of the book changes with it.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`291-1`,chapter:291,kind:`craft`,title:`What one misses in battle`,text:`As a soldier aims at Marius, a hand is laid over the muzzle; the young workman in velvet trousers falls. Marius 'barely registers' the gun, the hand or the shot. Hugo notes that 'in such moments everything vacillates and one pauses for nothing'. The narration shows us something that its hero does not see.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`291-2`,chapter:291,kind:`theme`,title:`The power of a man who wants to die`,text:`Marius stands with a torch over a broken powder barrel: 'Blow up the barricade, and yourself with it!' 'And myself also.' The troops flee. He wins because he no longer fears death. Hugo calls him 'the young revolution after the apparition of the old', following Mabeuf onto the stones.`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`]},{id:`291-3`,chapter:291,kind:`craft`,title:`Close enough to talk`,text:`After the first volley, the leaders stand unsheltered with their backs to the houses, facing soldiers 'so close they could talk without raising their voices'. 'Lay down your arms!' 'Fire!' Hugo strips the fight to a few yards of street and a cloud of smoke. When it clears, both sides reload in silence.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`292-1`,chapter:292,kind:`theme`,title:`'You are he!'`,text:`Marius asks who the chief is, and Enjolras answers: 'You are he!' Two months ago he was a lover in a garden; now he leads an insurrection. Hugo calls it a nightmare he must make an effort to believe real. History has picked up a young man who came only to die and has made him a leader.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`292-2`,chapter:292,kind:`theme`,title:`Your friends have just shot you`,text:`Combeferre proposes exchanging Javert for the captured Prouvaire. Then a voice at the end of the street cries 'Long live France! Long live the future!', a shot, silence. Enjolras turns to Javert: 'Your friends have just shot you.' Each side now holds the other's justice in its hands, and the logic of exchange becomes the logic of reprisal.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`revolution`]},{id:`292-3`,chapter:292,kind:`motif`,title:`The end of the verses`,text:`The chapter title calls Prouvaire's death 'the end of the verses'. A few hours ago the gentle poet was murmuring love poems in the dusk. His last lines are a shout for France and the future. Notice that Hugo lets us hear only his voice; we never see him die.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`292-4`,chapter:292,kind:`craft`,title:`Not even seen`,text:`In his daze Marius does not recognise Javert, bound to his post, and in fact 'has not even seen him'. Javert has watched the whole assault 'with the resignation of a martyr and the majesty of a judge'. Hugo keeps the two men in one room without letting them meet, and the reader is the only one who sees them both.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`293-1`,chapter:293,kind:`theme`,title:`To die together`,text:`Éponine led Marius to the barricade so that they would die together, and then put her hand over the gun aimed at him because she wanted to die first. Both things are true at once. Hugo does not tidy her love into pure sacrifice or pure jealousy. 'You thought me ugly, didn't you?' is the question of a girl who has never been looked at.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`293-2`,chapter:293,kind:`motif`,title:`A girl in men's clothes`,text:`The young workman in velvet trousers was Éponine. The disguise makes literal what Marius has always done: looked at her without seeing her. She has been beside him in the garret, at the gate, in the street, and he has never quite known who was there. Only now, with her head on his knees, does he say her name.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`293-3`,chapter:293,kind:`theme`,title:`'My brother is here'`,text:`Hearing Gavroche sing, she whispers: 'My brother is here. He must not see me. He would scold me.' Marius has just been thinking bitterly of the duty to the Thénardiers that his father left him. Now he learns the singing boy is Thénardier's son. The debt in the colonel's note has turned into a child on this barricade.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`293-4`,chapter:293,kind:`motif`,title:`The things she remembers`,text:`Dying, Éponine recalls looking into his mirror, the day by the washerwomen when the birds sang, the hundred-sou piece she refused and hopes he picked up. He had forgotten all of it. Notice how much of her love is made of these small scraps of his attention. 'I believe that I was a little bit in love with you.'`,themes:[`poverty`,`love`]},{id:`293-5`,chapter:293,kind:`craft`,title:`The agony of life`,text:`The title sets two agonies side by side: the agony of death after 'the agony of life'. 'Oh! How happy I am! Everyone is going to die.' For Éponine, who has known hunger, cold and her father's crimes, death beside Marius is the first moment that is hers. Hugo lets the line be terrible and tender together.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`294-1`,chapter:294,kind:`craft`,title:`The pieces assemble`,text:`Now Hugo explains: Éponine threw the warning on the Champ-de-Mars, was the 'young workman' Cosette gave her letter to, waited at Courfeyrac's lodge, and was the voice at the gate. Scenes the reader saw from outside fall into place. Hugo keeps the explanation short; he has trusted us to carry the separate moments until now.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`294-2`,chapter:294,kind:`motif`,title:`He did not know her handwriting`,text:`'Such was the innocence of their love that Marius did not even know her handwriting.' Two months of nights in the garden, and not one letter. The first words Cosette ever writes to him reach him through the hands of the girl who kept them back, and he reads them by a wine-shop candle on a barricade.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`294-3`,chapter:294,kind:`theme`,title:`Carry my body to my grandfather`,text:`Marius writes in his pocketbook: 'My name is Marius Pontmercy. Carry my body to my grandfather.' The young man who walked out of the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire twice, insulted, still names the old man as his home. And he sends Gavroche away with the letter partly to save him: Thénardier's son is the one debt of his father's he can still pay.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`294-4`,chapter:294,kind:`craft`,title:`A profound calculator of distances`,text:`Marius says the barricade will not be attacked before daybreak; Gavroche hesitates, snatches the letter and runs. His private plan: it is barely midnight, the Rue de l'Homme Armé is close, he can deliver it now and be back in time. Hugo's ironic title praises a child's arithmetic. Gavroche wants to keep both his errand and his place in the fight.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`295-1`,chapter:295,kind:`motif`,title:`A mirror that rights the letters`,text:`Cosette's blotting-book lies open by the sideboard, and the slanting mirror rights the reversed imprint of her note. Valjean reads it twice and does not believe it, then understands. Chance brings the truth to him backwards, by reflection. 'The light of the world is eclipsed.'`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`love`]},{id:`295-2`,chapter:295,kind:`theme`,title:`Every kind of love at once`,text:`Hugo says Valjean loves Cosette as a father, but a father made of grandfather, son, brother and husband, 'with even a mother included'. A lonely man has put every love he never had into one. That is why the discovery is the most terrible of his trials. Another person is the goal of her heart, and he is only her father.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`295-3`,chapter:295,kind:`theme`,title:`The spectre called Hate`,text:`The man who 'laboured so long to turn all misery into love' looks into himself and sees Hate. Hugo has shown Valjean's conscience at war before, in the long night of 'A Tempest in a Skull'. This time the enemy is not fear of the galleys but jealousy, and he has fallen to the bottom of the precipice 'without perceiving it'.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`295-4`,chapter:295,kind:`motif`,title:`Three men on three posts`,text:`Valjean goes out bareheaded and sits on the stone post at his door. Mabeuf sat on his overturned post in the rain; Marius sat on a post at the edge of the barricade and thought of his father. Hugo keeps placing his people on these little stone markers at the moment of decision, motionless while Paris fights around them.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`296-1`,chapter:296,kind:`theme`,title:`A coin for a child`,text:`Valjean lays a five-franc piece in a hungry boy's hand. The reader remembers another coin, forty sous, that Valjean once put his foot on and stole from little Petit-Gervais. Gavroche refuses the bribe, then takes it 'for his mother'. Neither knows that the mother is Madame Thénardier, the woman who worked the child Cosette like a servant.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`296-2`,chapter:296,kind:`craft`,title:`An enemy of light`,text:`Gavroche smashes the lantern opposite No. 7 because 'they still have their lanterns here, which is disorderly'. Behind the curtains people cry that '93 has come again. In this night darkness serves the barricades. Hugo adds that while the tocsin rings and Saint-Paul strikes eleven, 'the tocsin is man and the hour is God'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`296-3`,chapter:296,kind:`theme`,title:`'I am the person'`,text:`Valjean, who gave himself up in court rather than let another man bear his name, claims to be the person the letter is for. 'You? You are not a woman.' It is a small lie, told on impulse, and it puts a letter meant for Cosette into his hands. Hugo does not comment. He lets us watch it happen.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`297-1`,chapter:297,kind:`theme`,title:`A frightful joy`,text:`'I die. When thou readest this, my soul will be near thee.' Valjean seizes on those two lines and feels 'a frightful cry of inward joy'. He need only keep the note in his pocket. Hugo does not hide or soften the feeling. Opening the paper 'smacks of theft', and the hero is shown wishing a young man dead.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`297-2`,chapter:297,kind:`craft`,title:`The missing debate`,text:`In 'A Tempest in a Skull' Hugo gave a whole night to Valjean's struggle. Here he writes: 'Having said all this to himself, he becomes gloomy. Then he goes downstairs and wakes the porter.' The struggle happens off the page, and we see only what he does next. The reader is left to guess what passed between the joy and the uniform.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`297-3`,chapter:297,kind:`motif`,title:`In the uniform of order`,text:`An hour later Valjean goes out in the full costume of a National Guard, with a loaded gun and a box of cartridges, towards the markets. The former convict wears the uniform of the citizens who keep order. In this book clothes keep changing a man's place in the world: a yellow coat, a mayor's sash, a gardener's knee-bell, now this.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`297-4`,chapter:297,kind:`question`,title:`Where is he going?`,text:`Valjean has the letter, the joy, and a uniform. He has said nothing to Cosette and nothing to us. What does he mean to do at the markets, and which part of him has decided it? Hugo has given us all his reasons for wishing Marius dead and none for what he does next.`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`298-1`,chapter:298,kind:`craft`,title:`Received thy cart`,text:`Gavroche tips a drunken Auvergnat out of his handcart and leaves a receipt: 'French Republic. Received thy cart. Gavroche.' The insurgents have been chalking receipts on doors for requisitioned guns all day. The street boy copies the forms of revolution exactly, and makes them comic by doing so.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`298-2`,chapter:298,kind:`theme`,title:`How history records a prank`,text:`A boy with a cart and a song sets off a quarter-hour of blind musketry that 'kills several panes of glass'. The Auvergnat is later hauled before the councils of war as an accomplice, and the Marais remembers 'The nocturnal attack by the post of the Royal Printing Establishment'. Hugo shows the comic side of official history.`,themes:[`history`,`paris`]},{id:`298-3`,chapter:298,kind:`motif`,title:`Où vont les belles filles`,text:`Gavroche sings his way through the empty Marais, listing girls' names, Atala, Agnès, Paméla, Stella, with grimaces nobody sees in the dark. His song runs right through the chapter and picks up new verses after the fusillade. In a city of shut shutters, a child's voice is the only cheerful thing on the streets.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`299-1`,chapter:299,kind:`craft`,title:`A digression that leaps sixteen years`,text:`Volume V opens in June 1848, outside the story's time. Hugo describes two giant barricades so that we can measure the small one of 1832 against them. The digression gives scale: the Rue de la Chanvrerie is one cell in a long history of Paris rising. It also warns us not to read the next chapters as a simple fable of good rebels and bad soldiers.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`299-2`,chapter:299,kind:`context`,title:`Hugo in June 1848`,text:`In the June Days of 1848 the Paris workers rose against the new Republic after it closed the National Workshops, and the army crushed them. Hugo, then an elected representative, stood on the side of order. Here he speaks of 'the rabble' with 'pain and respect': the honest man must resist the suffering crowd 'with a sad conscience'. It is a confession as much as a history.`,themes:[`history`,`poverty`]},{id:`299-3`,chapter:299,kind:`motif`,title:`The dragon and the sphinx`,text:`The Saint-Antoine barricade is a three-storey heap of houses, rags and noise, 'a heap of filth and a Sinai'. The Temple barricade is a straight, silent wall where every passer-by falls to an unseen marksman. Hugo sets fury against cold method, and gives them two builders: a stormy naval officer and a pale former galley-slave who had killed a policeman at seventeen.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`revolution`]},{id:`300-1`,chapter:300,kind:`motif`,title:`A cross in the candlelight`,text:`In the taproom, the 'hall of the dead', Javert stands bound upright to his post and Mabeuf lies under his black cloth. Hugo notes that the prisoner and the corpse 'made a kind of cross'. Law and the old man's despair are laid across each other in one image, lit by candles, in the middle of a wine-shop.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`300-2`,chapter:300,kind:`motif`,title:`Words cut into walls`,text:`Feuilly, the orphan fan-painter, spends his rest carving 'Long live the peoples!' on the wall with a nail. Courfeyrac chalked a joke on the Corinthe door; Marius scratched his address into a garden wall. Writing on walls runs through these chapters: messages left by people who may not be there to say them.`,themes:[`revolution`,`names`]},{id:`300-3`,chapter:300,kind:`theme`,title:`Remorse in a liberating killing`,text:`At dawn Combeferre talks of the dead, Prouvaire, Bahorel, Mabeuf, even Le Cabuc, and says that even a killing that frees leaves more remorse than joy. Then he drifts to the Georgics, Caesar and Brutus. Hugo's title asks what one does in the abyss if one does not converse. These students face death the way they faced exams, by talking.`,themes:[`conscience`,`revolution`]},{id:`301-1`,chapter:301,kind:`theme`,title:`'You are abandoned'`,text:`The barricade expects a regiment to come over at six and Paris to rise at noon. Enjolras returns from scouting and tells the truth: a third of the army is coming, the people are not stirring, 'You are abandoned.' Hugo gives the leader the courage of plain speech. The chapter title, 'Light and Shadow', is the passage from hope to that sentence.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`301-2`,chapter:301,kind:`craft`,title:`The anonymous hero`,text:`A voice from the darkest part of the crowd answers: raise the barricade, stay, offer 'the protest of corpses'. No one ever knew who spoke. Hugo honours a man in a blouse who says the decisive word and vanishes. The bravest sentence on the barricade belongs to no one.`,themes:[`names`,`revolution`]},{id:`302-1`,chapter:302,kind:`theme`,title:`Mothers at the windows`,text:`Combeferre's appeal is about those left behind: mothers watching at candlelit windows, daughters who, left without bread, 'would be driven to the street and the police', a child dead in the Necker Hospital with ashes in his teeth. The reader has seen that road to the street in Fantine's story. 'To die was fine; to make others die was murder.'`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`302-2`,chapter:302,kind:`craft`,title:`Minus five, plus one`,text:`Five men are chosen to live, and there are only four uniforms. Then a fifth uniform falls on the pile. Hugo makes Valjean's entrance an act of arithmetic and of giving: before he says a word, he has saved a stranger. 'Who is he?' 'A man who saves others.' Marius, astonished, says only: 'I know him.'`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`302-3`,chapter:302,kind:`question`,title:`Why has he come?`,text:`Hours ago Valjean felt 'a frightful cry of inward joy' at the thought of Marius's death. Now he is inside the barricade, silent, helping the fifth man into his uniform. Hugo gives no reason. What has happened between the letter and this moment, and what does Valjean mean to do here?`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`302-4`,chapter:302,kind:`craft`,title:`Forgetting his own mother`,text:`Hugo adds a quiet sting to Combeferre's speech: Combeferre, who was not an orphan, 'remembered other men's mothers and forgot his own'. The noble appeal is also blind. Hugo admires his idealists and still notices what they leave out.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`303-1`,chapter:303,kind:`theme`,title:`The twentieth century will be happy`,text:`Enjolras imagines cities flooded with light, nations as sisters, free and compulsory schooling, 'the right to the alphabet', and no more famine, prostitution or scaffold. 'The nineteenth century is great, but the twentieth will be happy.' The speech is Hugo's own creed of progress, given to a young man who expects to die before morning.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`303-2`,chapter:303,kind:`context`,title:`Saint-Just and Cloots`,text:`Hugo says Enjolras had 'too much of Saint-Just and not enough of Anacharsis Cloots'. Saint-Just was the young, austere theorist of the Terror; Cloots, a Prussian baron turned revolutionary, called himself the 'orator of the human race'. Enjolras has the pure, severe republic in him. Combeferre has been teaching him a gentler, worldwide one.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`303-3`,chapter:303,kind:`motif`,title:`A tomb flooded with dawn`,text:`'Here misery met the ideal, the day embraced the night.' The barricade, Enjolras says, is made of two heaps, of ideas and of woes, and whoever dies on it enters 'a tomb all flooded with dawn'. Hugo's long contest of light and darkness becomes the whole argument of the speech. There is no applause, only a whisper like leaves.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`303-4`,chapter:303,kind:`question`,title:`Hearing the prophecy now`,text:`Hugo wrote Enjolras's speech in the 1860s, and France made primary schooling free and compulsory in the 1880s. A reader today knows what the twentieth century brought. How should we hear 'the twentieth will be happy'? As naive, as a promise betrayed, or as a hope worth holding all the same?`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`304-1`,chapter:304,kind:`craft`,title:`'It is perfectly simple'`,text:`Bound on the table, Javert notices a shadow, looks up and recognises Jean Valjean. He does not start. He lowers his eyelids proudly and says: 'It is perfectly simple.' After hundreds of pages of pursuit, Hugo makes the recognition almost silent. To Javert the world is still in order: the convict is here among the criminals, where he belongs.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`304-2`,chapter:304,kind:`theme`,title:`A glass of water`,text:`Enjolras, who has sentenced Javert to death, holds a glass of water to his lips himself, and moves him from the post to the table at his request. Mercy and execution sit side by side in the same man. Javert asks for no favour except to lie down 'like that other man', the dead Mabeuf.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`304-3`,chapter:304,kind:`theme`,title:`Everyone comes here to die`,text:`Marius does not ask why M. Fauchelevent is here. In his despair 'it seemed natural that the whole world should come there to die'. Fauchelevent does not look at him, which suits Marius, who has always found that man impossible to address. Grief has made Marius incurious, and Hugo shows how close that is to blindness.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`305-1`,chapter:305,kind:`craft`,title:`A fortress and a rat hole`,text:`At dawn the Mondétour lane is walled up, and the redoubt has three fronts and no exit: 'a fortress but a rat hole too,' laughs Courfeyrac. Each man picks his place 'as at the theatre', and Hugo notes that peril produces order. The only sound when Enjolras takes his post is the cocking of guns.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`305-2`,chapter:305,kind:`motif`,title:`Christ over Napoleon`,text:`Combeferre lectures on the brass eight-pounder and ends with a sum: a cannonball travels six hundred leagues an hour, light seventy thousand a second, 'the superiority of Christ over Napoleon'. Even under artillery fire these young men argue in images of light. Hugo lets the joke carry his conviction: ideas outrun force.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`history`]},{id:`305-3`,chapter:305,kind:`craft`,title:`Present!`,text:`The cannonball and Gavroche arrive at the same instant, and the boy makes 'a greater sensation than the cannonball'. The barricade bursts out laughing. Hugo times the return like stage comedy. The first shot of the artillery is upstaged by a child shouting 'Present!' as if at school roll-call.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`306-1`,chapter:306,kind:`theme`,title:`He might be your brother`,text:`Combeferre begs Enjolras to look at the young blond sergeant: he has a family, he is probably in love, 'he might be your brother'. 'He is.' 'He is mine too, let us not kill him.' 'Let me alone. It must be done.' A tear runs down Enjolras's marble cheek as he fires. Civil war, as Marius thought on the edge of the barricade, is war between brothers.`,themes:[`revolution`,`conscience`]},{id:`306-2`,chapter:306,kind:`craft`,title:`A letter in the wrong hands`,text:`Gavroche lies that he gave the letter to the porter. Marius wonders whether the letter and M. Fauchelevent's arrival are connected, then decides perhaps Fauchelevent is simply a republican. The reader knows exactly where the letter went. Hugo lets us watch Marius come close to the truth and turn away from it.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`306-3`,chapter:306,kind:`craft`,title:`The grapeshot`,text:`Infantry builds a low wall behind the gun; the muzzle shifts slightly left; grapeshot hits the cut and rebounds, leaving two dead and three wounded. Hugo follows the artillery's method with a soldier's eye. The battle is now a technical problem, and the barricade is losing it.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`307-1`,chapter:307,kind:`theme`,title:`The marksmanship that condemned him`,text:`The long title recalls 1796, when Valjean's skill as a poacher counted against him at his trial for stealing bread. Now that same 'infallible marksmanship' cuts two ropes against the sky and brings down a mattress. The talent the law once used against him becomes the thing that saves a barricade full of strangers.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`307-2`,chapter:307,kind:`motif`,title:`Glory to the thing that yields`,text:`The old woman's mattress, hung at her attic window back when the barricade was built, now stops the grapeshot. Bossuet laughs at the 'immorality' of a mattress that annuls a cannon: 'glory to the thing that yields'. It is a joke that fits the book. Softness and endurance defeat force more often than force defeats them.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`307-3`,chapter:307,kind:`theme`,title:`'Citizen, the Republic thanks you'`,text:`Valjean walks out through the bullets, carries the mattress back on his shoulders and fixes it in the breach. 'Citizen, the Republic thanks you.' The escaped convict is honoured by the barricade's young lawgiver without being known. Notice that he protects the barricade without killing anyone.`,themes:[`names`,`revolution`]},{id:`308-1`,chapter:308,kind:`craft`,title:`Carriage gates in the distance`,text:`Cosette wakes smiling, sure Marius has her letter and will come today. She hears sharp shocks far off and thinks people are opening and shutting carriage gates early. It is the cannon. Hugo cuts from grapeshot to a girl's bedroom and lets the reader hear what she cannot.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`308-2`,chapter:308,kind:`motif`,title:`A martins' nest`,text:`Below her window a mother martin spreads over her brood while the father flies to and fro with food. Cosette gazes at 'that small family' with a trouble she cannot name. Hugo sets a complete, ordinary family in the cornice of a house in a city full of broken ones. It is the life she wants and cannot yet picture.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`308-3`,chapter:308,kind:`craft`,title:`A chamber not described`,text:`Hugo refuses to describe a young girl rising: 'one may show a nuptial chamber but not a virginal one.' He keeps the camera outside her door. The tact matters, because Cosette is all expectation. She looks at the sky instead of the walled court, 'as if he might come from there'.`,themes:[`love`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`309-1`,chapter:309,kind:`theme`,title:`Missing nothing, killing no one`,text:`A fireman and then an officer look down into the barricade from a roof. Valjean knocks each one's helmet off with a single bullet, and no one appears there again. The title states the paradox. He is the best shot on the barricade and he will not kill. When Bossuet asks why, 'Jean Valjean made no reply.'`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`309-2`,chapter:309,kind:`craft`,title:`The silence of the barricade`,text:`Hugo explains the besiegers' tactic: fire for hours so the insurgents waste powder, then assault when their fire slackens. Enjolras does not fall for it, and the barricade stays silent. Meanwhile Gavroche puffs his cheek at each volley and tells the soldiers to rip up more cloth if they want lint.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`310-1`,chapter:310,kind:`theme`,title:`Disorder on the side of order`,text:`The title is a paradox, and Hugo means it. The suburban National Guard fought for order with no order at all: captains attacked on inspiration, and platoons set themselves up as courts and shot a prisoner in five minutes. That is how Jean Prouvaire died. The law's defenders could be as lawless as the barricade.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`310-2`,chapter:310,kind:`craft`,title:`Killed by order`,text:`Captain Fannicot, furious at Mabeuf's coat, which he takes for a black flag, throws his company at the barricade without orders. He loses fifteen men and then dies under his own side's cannon, which 'had received no order to stop'. Hugo's dry phrase, 'killed by order', carries the whole irony of the scene.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`310-3`,chapter:310,kind:`theme`,title:`One against a hundred`,text:`Enjolras is angry at the wasted shots, and Hugo calls him 'the true general of insurrection'. A rising has a fixed number of men and cartridges; repression has the army and the arsenal of Vincennes. The barricade always loses, Hugo says, unless revolution itself rises and the army, 'that lion', finds France, 'that prophet', standing in its way.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`310-4`,chapter:310,kind:`motif`,title:`Good by gunshots`,text:`'He has not answered my question,' Bossuet mutters, and Combeferre replies that the silent marksman is 'a man who does good by gunshots'. The insurgents have no name for Valjean except what they see him do. To them he is a puzzle, a crack shot who aims at helmets.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`311-1`,chapter:311,kind:`motif`,title:`Lightning without thunder`,text:`For an hour on the morning of the 6th Paris seems to wake: barricades begun, a young man killing a cavalry officer, crockery raining from roofs. Then it is gone, 'a flash of lightning without thunder'. Hugo lets hope cross the barricade like weather, and then lets it pass.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`311-2`,chapter:311,kind:`context`,title:`Old women and chamber-pots`,text:`When crockery rains on the troops from the roofs of the Rue Planche-Mibray, Marshal Soult recalls Suchet's saying at Saragossa: 'We are lost when the old women empty their pots de chambre on our heads.' Saragossa had resisted Napoleon's armies house by house in 1808 and 1809. Hugo's real incidents of the 6th of June show a city resisting in small, scattered ways.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`311-3`,chapter:311,kind:`theme`,title:`Die without anything to eat?`,text:`An insurgent asks whether they are really going to die like this, without eating. Enjolras nods without taking his eyes off the street. Hunger has been the book's subject since Valjean's loaf of bread. Now it is simply one more fact of the barricade, accepted in silence.`,themes:[`poverty`,`revolution`]},{id:`312-1`,chapter:312,kind:`theme`,title:`Patria`,text:`Bossuet theorises that all heroism comes from women, and that a man without a woman is 'a pistol without a trigger', like Gavroche's pistol. How can Enjolras, with no mistress, be so cold and so bold? Enjolras seems not to listen, then murmurs: 'Patria.' The title promised his mistress's name, and this is it.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`312-2`,chapter:312,kind:`craft`,title:`Jokes in place of wine`,text:`With no food and the brandy rationed, Courfeyrac and Bossuet 'poured out gaiety in place of wine'. The cannon's grapeshot is 'only a cough'; Courfeyrac announces a second gun like an usher: 'My name is Eight-Pounder.' Hugo keeps the friends' wit alive to the end. It is a form of courage, and of friendship.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`312-3`,chapter:312,kind:`craft`,title:`Success that ruins`,text:`The barricade's volleys cut down two-thirds of the gunners. 'Success,' says Bossuet. Enjolras shakes his head: another quarter-hour of such success and there will be no cartridges left. The last line is very short: 'Gavroche, it appears, overheard him.' Hugo ends the chapter there.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`313-1`,chapter:313,kind:`context`,title:`The fault of Voltaire`,text:`Gavroche's song, 'it's the fault of Voltaire... the fault of Rousseau', mocks a familiar line of the Restoration, when the Church and the royalists blamed the philosophers of the Enlightenment for the Revolution and every evil since. The street child turns their complaint into a joke and throws it back at the soldiers with every bullet.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`313-2`,chapter:313,kind:`theme`,title:`Joy his character, misery his trousseau`,text:`'Joy was his character and misery his trousseau.' Hugo began Volume III by describing the gamin of Paris as a species, a pearl in the mud. Here the type becomes one boy, singing in the street, collecting cartridges among the dead, 'the invulnerable dwarf of the fray'. The soldiers laugh as they aim.`,themes:[`poverty`,`paris`]},{id:`313-3`,chapter:313,kind:`craft`,title:`An unfinished line`,text:`Hit once, Gavroche sits up, blood on his face, and sings again: '...the fault of...' He did not finish. A second bullet stops him. Hugo breaks the song off in the middle of a word, so that the line is never completed. 'That grand little soul had taken its flight.'`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`313-4`,chapter:313,kind:`motif`,title:`The little bird`,text:`'He was not a notary, he was a little bird.' From the first pages about the gamin, Hugo has likened him to the sparrow of the streets. He sings like a bird, hops between bodies like one, and in the last sentence his soul takes flight. The image runs through the whole portrait of the street child.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`314-1`,chapter:314,kind:`theme`,title:`From a brother to a father`,text:`Gavroche once took the two lost boys in, fed them and gave them a bed in the elephant. Now, alone in the Luxembourg, the elder fishes a wet brioche out of the swans' basin and keeps the small half, giving the large one to his brother. The title names what has happened. A child has become a parent, as Gavroche did, without knowing anyone taught him.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`poverty`]},{id:`314-2`,chapter:314,kind:`craft`,title:`A lesson in compassion for animals`,text:`A comfortable bourgeois, possibly the one Marius once heard counselling his son against excess, throws his son's stale brioche to the swans 'to teach compassion for animals', while two ragged children watch from behind the hutch. He calls the firing 'the Saturnalia' and the boys 'anarchy'. Hugo's satire is quiet and exact.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`314-3`,chapter:314,kind:`theme`,title:`Thinkers who forget to love`,text:`Hugo pauses on 'magnificent egoists' like Horace and Goethe, so filled with the zodiac that they do not see the weeping child. They are great and pitiable at once. The garden is all flowers, bees and breakfasting birds, 'not a beast's stomach empty', and two hungry children walk through it unseen.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`314-4`,chapter:314,kind:`craft`,title:`The garden at eleven`,text:`Hugo turns from the barricade to the Luxembourg, closed by police order and empty, at the same hour. The cut is abrupt and deliberate. Events of world importance are happening a mile away, and here the drama is a cake in a pond.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`315-1`,chapter:315,kind:`context`,title:`The dead father waits`,text:`The Latin title means 'The dead father awaits the son who is about to die'. It gathers up Marius's thought on the edge of the barricade, when he argued with Colonel Pontmercy's ghost. At the chapter's end, among the jokes and the cartridge-making, Marius is 'troubled about what his father was going to say to him'.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`315-2`,chapter:315,kind:`theme`,title:`What Thénardier did for my father`,text:`Marius carries Gavroche's body back, thinking that what Thénardier did for his father he is now doing for Thénardier's son. But Thénardier brought the colonel back alive, and Marius brings the child back dead. The reader knows that the 'rescue' at Waterloo was a robbery. The son repays a debt that was never owed.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`315-3`,chapter:315,kind:`motif`,title:`One shawl for two`,text:`They lay Gavroche on Mabeuf's table and spread the widow's black shawl over both: 'there was enough of it for the old man and the child.' The eighty-year-old who sold his last book and the boy who had nothing to sell lie under one cloth. Hugo needs only one sentence for it.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`315-4`,chapter:315,kind:`theme`,title:`Defending without fighting`,text:`Valjean shakes his head at his share of cartridges. Combeferre calls him an eccentric who has found a way of not fighting in a barricade; Enjolras answers that this does not stop him defending it. Hugo lets the insurgents describe, without understanding it, a new kind of courage.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`316-1`,chapter:316,kind:`theme`,title:`'That is just'`,text:`Valjean asks, as his reward, to be the one who shoots the spy. Javert raises his head, sees who it is, and says: 'That is just.' In Javert's world this is the natural order: the convict kills the policeman when he can. He expects nothing else, and in his eyes the request only confirms what Valjean is.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`316-2`,chapter:316,kind:`craft`,title:`Death as a masterpiece`,text:`Enjolras walls the windows, plans the loopholes, nails up the kitchen door so the wounded will not be splashed, and counts twenty-six men and thirty-four guns. Hugo says he felt that since such men were to die, 'their death should be a masterpiece'. Asked who will drink the fifteen bottles, he answers: 'They.'`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`316-3`,chapter:316,kind:`craft`,title:`Blood under the nails`,text:`Hugo opens with a remark about memory: one comes out of civil war as out of a dream, with blood under one's nails and no clear recollection. The barricade is calm inside and still a vision to those in it. He is telling us how unreliable the survivors' accounts of such days are.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`317-1`,chapter:317,kind:`theme`,title:`Revenge as freedom`,text:`The title promises revenge, and Javert asks for it: 'Take your revenge.' Valjean takes out a knife and cuts the cords. 'You are free.' The reader remembers the bishop handing a thief his candlesticks. Valjean now does to his pursuer what was once done to him, and repays a lifetime of hounding with release.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`317-2`,chapter:317,kind:`craft`,title:`Giving his enemy the address`,text:`Valjean adds that if he leaves alive, he lives as Fauchelevent at No. 7, Rue de l'Homme Armé. He hands the policeman his name and his door. Javert repeats the number, snarls 'Have a care', and walks off. A man who has spent years hiding chooses, here, to hide nothing.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`317-3`,chapter:317,kind:`motif`,title:`The lost 'thou'`,text:`'You annoy me. Kill me, rather,' Javert shouts, 'not noticing that he had stopped addressing Valjean as thou.' In French the familiar 'tu' is used to inferiors and convicts. Javert's grammar shifts before his mind does. Something in his view of Valjean has moved, and he has not noticed it himself.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`317-4`,chapter:317,kind:`craft`,title:`Two shots, two meanings`,text:`Valjean fires the pistol into the air and returns: 'It is done.' At that moment Marius has just learned the prisoner's name and recognised the inspector of the Rue de Pontoise. To him the shot means an execution. The reader knows it was a release. The same sound tells the truth to us and a lie to Marius.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`318-1`,chapter:318,kind:`craft`,title:`A pause before the end`,text:`Just before the final assault, Hugo stops the battle to judge it. The title gives his verdict: the dead are in the right and the living are not in the wrong. He defends the insurgents and the people who would not open their doors. The digression asks the reader to hold both sympathies at once.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`318-2`,chapter:318,kind:`theme`,title:`Every blade has two edges`,text:`'An insurrection is an idea sitting its examination before the people.' Utopia that takes up arms almost always comes too soon. And it borrows battle 'from the lie of yesterday': it shoots spies and executes traitors. Hugo, who has just shown Le Cabuc's execution and Javert's sentence, does not exempt his heroes.`,themes:[`revolution`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`318-3`,chapter:318,kind:`theme`,title:`The book names its subject`,text:`Hugo says what the whole novel is about: the march 'from evil to good, from night to day, from appetite to conscience, from nothingness to God'. Matter at the start, the soul at the end; 'the hydra at the beginning, the angel at the end'. It is a key to the light and darkness he has used since the bishop's candlesticks.`,themes:[`conscience`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`318-4`,chapter:318,kind:`context`,title:`John Brown before Washington`,text:`Hugo says he venerates the 'confessors of Utopia' most in defeat, preferring martyrdom to success, 'John Brown to Washington'. John Brown, the American abolitionist, was hanged in December 1859 after his raid on Harpers Ferry. Hugo had publicly pleaded for his life, so the name was fresh when he wrote this.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`319-1`,chapter:319,kind:`craft`,title:`The roll of the slain`,text:`'Bossuet was killed; Feuilly was killed; Courfeyrac was killed.' After the long build-up, Hugo gives each friend half a line. The flat repetition is the epic catalogue cut down to its bare form, as in Homer's lists of the fallen. A peasant recruit and a pale student, he insists, cast a shadow as long as any Ajax.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`319-2`,chapter:319,kind:`theme`,title:`Combeferre lifts a soldier`,text:`Combeferre, the gentle philosopher who begged Enjolras to spare the artillery sergeant, dies pierced by three bayonets 'as he lifted a wounded soldier'. He dies helping one of the enemy. Hugo gives him a glance towards heaven and nothing more.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`revolution`]},{id:`319-3`,chapter:319,kind:`theme`,title:`Sheltered and exposed`,text:`Enjolras, who holds the whole defence in his head, shelters himself and is untouched. Marius fights with half his body above the breastwork, 'a phantom firing a gun', his face vanishing under blood. One man fights to win; the other came to die. Hugo puts them at the two ends of the barricade.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`320-1`,chapter:320,kind:`motif`,title:`A door that does not open`,text:`Hemmed in, some of the defenders hammer at the door of the tall house at the back, begging to be let in. No one opens. From the third-floor window the murdered porter's head looks down on them. The chapter before argued that a city disowns a rising by shutting its doors. Here the argument becomes one door and some men beating on it.`,themes:[`paris`,`poverty`]},{id:`320-2`,chapter:320,kind:`theme`,title:`The only two kisses of his life`,text:`Before the last fight Enjolras bends over Mabeuf and Gavroche and kisses the old man's hanging hand, as he kissed his brow the night before: 'the only two kisses of his life'. The man whose mistress is Patria gives his tenderness to an old man he hardly knew.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`320-3`,chapter:320,kind:`craft`,title:`Heroism become monstrous`,text:`The barricade fought like a gate of Thebes, the wine-shop like a house of Saragossa. Then the last cartridges are gone, and the men on the first floor fight with bottles of aquafortis. Hugo says it resembled 'Milton and Dante rather than Homer': demons attacking, spectres resisting. He refuses to make the end glorious.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`321-1`,chapter:321,kind:`context`,title:`Orestes and Pylades`,text:`In Greek legend Pylades was the friend who stood by Orestes through exile and madness, ready to die in his place. Hugo's title makes Enjolras the fasting Orestes and Grantaire the drunken Pylades. The pair of the austere believer and the sceptic is given the most famous friendship of antiquity.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`321-2`,chapter:321,kind:`theme`,title:`'Do you permit it?'`,text:`Grantaire wakes in the silence, understands, and walks to the wall: 'Long live the Republic! I'm one of them.' Then, gently: 'Do you permit it?' Enjolras, who called him incapable of believing or dying, presses his hand with a smile. 'You will see,' Grantaire once said. He believed in nothing but Enjolras, and here he acts on it.`,themes:[`love`,`conscience`]},{id:`321-3`,chapter:321,kind:`craft`,title:`Shooting a flower`,text:`Enjolras, unwounded after twenty-four hours, 'fresh and rosy', compels his killers to kill him respectfully; a witness spoke of an insurgent they called Apollo, and a guardsman says he feels he is about to shoot a flower. Pierced by eight bullets, he stays upright 'as though nailed there'. Hugo makes his death almost a statue.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`321-4`,chapter:321,kind:`craft`,title:`Silence wakes a drunkard`,text:`Grantaire has snored through fusillade, cannon and assault, so like the corpses that no one noticed him. 'Noise does not rouse a drunkard, silence does.' Hugo's small observation sets up the scene's turn: the moment the fighting stops is the moment the doubter wakes.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`322-1`,chapter:322,kind:`craft`,title:`He never took his eyes off him`,text:`'The hand that gripped him as he fell was Jean Valjean's.' Hugo now tells us what the barricade did not see: Valjean 'had seemed not to see Marius and had never taken his eyes off him'. He fought only by carrying the wounded. The man he wished dead the night before is the one he watched over all day.`,themes:[`love`,`conscience`]},{id:`322-2`,chapter:322,kind:`motif`,title:`Once Cosette, now Marius`,text:`Trapped with no way out, Valjean finds a loose grating and drops into the dark with Marius on his back. He remembers the wall of the convent garden, eight years ago, when what he carried was Cosette. 'What he carried now was not Cosette but Marius.' Hugo repeats an old escape with a different burden.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`322-3`,chapter:322,kind:`motif`,title:`Below the noon`,text:`Above, the June sun, the assault, the tocsin. Three metres down, 'profound peace, absolute silence and night'. The noise of the taken wine-shop reaches Valjean only as a murmur. Hugo moves his hero from the most public place in Paris into the most hidden one in a single paragraph.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`323-1`,chapter:323,kind:`craft`,title:`A sewer book that starts with money`,text:`Hugo opens his history of the sewers like an accountant: Paris throws twenty-five million francs a year into the river as human manure that could feed the fields. The digression is not a detour from his themes. The city wastes what could end hunger, and Hugo says better drainage would 'lighten the problem of misery'. The sewer becomes another chapter on poverty.`,themes:[`poverty`,`paris`]},{id:`323-2`,chapter:323,kind:`motif`,title:`Lutetia beneath Paris`,text:`Beneath the city of light lies another city, 'Lutetia, the city of mud', with its own streets, crossroads and blind alleys. Lutetia was the Roman name of Paris. Hugo makes the underground a double of the surface: the oldest, dirtiest self of the capital, still there under the boulevards.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`paris`]},{id:`323-3`,chapter:323,kind:`context`,title:`Liebig and the petrels`,text:`Hugo cites Justus von Liebig, the German chemist who founded agricultural chemistry and warned that cities were robbing the soil. The 'fleets that go to the South Pole for the dung of petrels' are the guano trade: in the mid-nineteenth century Europe imported vast quantities of bird droppings from Pacific islands as fertiliser.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`324-1`,chapter:324,kind:`theme`,title:`The conscience of the city`,text:`'The sewer is the conscience of the city.' Everything ends there and nothing lies: the bottle-end confesses drunkenness, the apple-core 'that had literary opinions' becomes an apple-core again, everything rouged is washed clean. In a book full of masks and false names, Hugo finds one place where the masks come off.`,themes:[`conscience`,`names`]},{id:`324-2`,chapter:324,kind:`theme`,title:`Asylum for the hunted`,text:`The sewer has hidden everything the law persecuted: medieval rebels, Huguenots, bandits, thieves and free thinkers. 'Villon within converses with Rabelais without.' Hugo lists outlaws and martyrs together without sorting them. Like the convent and the garret, the sewer is one more refuge for people society hunts.`,themes:[`history`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`324-3`,chapter:324,kind:`craft`,title:`Murderers scrubbing in the dark`,text:`Hugo imagines the great killers of French history, Louis XI, Charles IX and his mother, Richelieu, kneeling in the sewer's gloom 'with a scrap of shroud for an apron, sponging out their work'. The Saint Bartholomew massacre filters through its stones drop by drop. The image makes the sewer the place where official history is cleaned up.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`325-1`,chapter:325,kind:`theme`,title:`Filth that comes back`,text:`Old Paris could 'no more sweep out filth than abuses'. Sometimes the sewer overflowed and the city tasted its own dirt, 'a resemblance to remorse that served as a warning badly received'. Hugo makes the sewer a picture of a buried conscience. What a city refuses to face rises up through the gratings.`,themes:[`conscience`,`paris`]},{id:`325-2`,chapter:325,kind:`context`,title:`Bruneseau`,text:`Pierre-Emmanuel Bruneseau was a real inspector of works who surveyed and cleared the sewers of Paris from 1805. Hugo stages his introduction to Napoleon at the height of the Empire, between Marengo and Austerlitz: the most intrepid man in the Empire is not a soldier but a man who wants to go down into the sewer.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`325-3`,chapter:325,kind:`motif`,title:`A Columbus of the cesspool`,text:`The sewer was the lower world, 'Daedalus backing up Babel'; not even the police explored it. 'Yet the cesspool found its Christopher Columbus.' Hugo frames the exploration of darkness as a voyage of discovery, the same heroic language he gives the barricade, turned now towards the city's underside.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`326-1`,chapter:326,kind:`motif`,title:`Marat's shroud`,text:`On a hinge at the mouth of the Grand Sewer hangs a rag of fine batiste with a marquis's coronet: a piece of Marat's shroud, the only fine linen in his house, kept from a youthful love affair with a great lady. From the Pantheon to the rats. Bruneseau's men leave it hanging 'from scorn or respect, both of which Marat deserved'.`,themes:[`revolution`,`history`]},{id:`326-2`,chapter:326,kind:`craft`,title:`An inventory of the buried`,text:`Dated stones from 1550, dungeons with an iron collar still hanging, a Huguenot medal of a pig in a cardinal's hat, an orangutan escaped from the Jardin des Plantes. Hugo lists the finds like an archaeologist. The sewer keeps what the city above forgot, and each object is a small piece of history.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`326-3`,chapter:326,kind:`theme`,title:`A night battle`,text:`Eight of the twenty labourers refuse to go on; men faint in the foul air, one vanishes into a bottomless hole. Hugo's sources are the details 'Bruneseau left out of his official report'. As with the barricade, he goes looking for the unrecorded courage of ordinary workmen.`,themes:[`poverty`,`history`]},{id:`327-1`,chapter:327,kind:`craft`,title:`A tradesman turned councillor of state`,text:`The modern sewer is clean, straight and correct, 'like a tradesman who has become a councillor of state'. A gut is now a gallery; the classical alexandrine seems to have taken refuge in its vault. It still smells faintly, 'like Tartuffe after confession'. Hugo's comedy is aimed at respectability, which he finds an improvement and still a little suspect.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`327-2`,chapter:327,kind:`theme`,title:`The man everyone forgets`,text:`'It is more than progress; it is transmutation.' Hugo credits the change to Bruneseau, 'the man everyone forgets'. Throughout this book history's real benefactors are obscure: a bishop in a small diocese, a manufacturer in a provincial town, a sewer inspector. The famous names are elsewhere.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`328-1`,chapter:328,kind:`theme`,title:`Bravery without bulletins`,text:`Monnot built a sewer in four months of day-and-night work and died of it; Duleau vaulted three thousand metres and died. Quicksands swallow men at Belleville, typhus soaks into the workmen. 'There are no bulletins for such bravery, more useful than that of the battlefield.' Coming straight after the barricade, the comparison is pointed.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`328-2`,chapter:328,kind:`context`,title:`Paris rebuilt underground`,text:`Hugo writes in the 1860s, when the engineer Eugène Belgrand was rebuilding the Paris sewers on a vast scale under Napoleon III and Haussmann. He stresses that the sewers of 1832 were 'far from today's': the old gothic sewer still gaped open in many streets. The reader who has just watched Valjean go down a grating knows which sewer he is in.`,themes:[`paris`,`history`]},{id:`328-3`,chapter:328,kind:`theme`,title:`Health and misery`,text:`Hugo ends where he began: wash the sewer back onto the fields and the city will 'gain in health and lose in misery'. For ten centuries, he says, the cesspool has been the disease of Paris. His sewer chapters are part of the same argument as the rest of the book: misery is a social problem, with causes that can be changed.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`329-1`,chapter:329,kind:`craft`,title:`The map he does not have`,text:`Hugo names every gallery Valjean passes, the Montmartre sewer, the Plâtrière, the Rue du Cadran, and then tells us Valjean knows none of them. The reader walks with a map and the hero walks blind. The suspense comes from that gap: we can see the dead ends he is trying to feel his way past.`,themes:[`paris`]},{id:`329-2`,chapter:329,kind:`motif`,title:`In the belly of the monster`,text:`'Like the prophet, he was in the belly of the monster.' Valjean, carrying a man who may be dead, walks in the dark like Jonah in the whale. Then he sees his own shadow thrown ahead of him: a light behind. Here light is the danger, 'the gloomy star of the police rising in the sewer'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`329-3`,chapter:329,kind:`theme`,title:`From the barricade to the living city`,text:`A rumble overhead tells Valjean he is passing from the Paris 'petrified by the uprising' into the living city. He chose to climb instead of following the slope to the river, so as not to come out with two bleeding men in the busiest spot in Paris. Notice that his old convict's instincts, avoiding the crowd, keeping to the walls, are what guide him now.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`330-1`,chapter:330,kind:`motif`,title:`The lantern saw him badly`,text:`'If he saw the lantern well, the lantern saw him badly: it was light and he was shadow.' Hugo turns his oldest image pair around once more. The police carry the light, and the darkness keeps the fugitive. Valjean stops, and the sound stops. Everything hangs 'on that thread'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`330-2`,chapter:330,kind:`craft`,title:`A shot for his conscience`,text:`Before leaving, the sergeant fires one shot into the dark 'to satisfy his policeman's conscience'. The ball strikes the vault above Valjean's head. Hugo's irony is light: a duty done blindly, by a man who believes there is no one there. The law shoots in the dark and misses.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`331-1`,chapter:331,kind:`craft`,title:`Two men at a distance`,text:`Two men walk along the right bank, one trying to get away without seeming to, the other following without seeming to hurry, 'like a game of checkers played at a distance'. Hugo says the reader might recognise them at closer range, and does not bring us closer. He is teasing us, and the pleasure of the chapter is in the guessing.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`331-2`,chapter:331,kind:`context`,title:`Red rather than blue`,text:`The pursuer's aim, Hugo says, was to dress the ragged man more warmly, 'in red rather than blue'. Convicts in the galleys wore red jackets; the blue is the workman's blouse. Notice the police's steady habit: while a revolution may be breaking out across Paris, an agent is still patiently 'spinning' a thief.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`331-3`,chapter:331,kind:`question`,title:`A government key`,text:`The fugitive vanishes through an iron sewer gate on the shore and locks it behind him. 'That is too much! A government key!' How does a ragged thief come to hold a key to the city's underworld, and what is it used for? The pursuer settles behind the rubbish to wait.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`332-1`,chapter:332,kind:`craft`,title:`Bandages and hatred`,text:`Valjean tears up his own shirt to bind Marius's wounds, checks that the heart still beats, then gazes at him 'with inexpressible hatred'. Hugo puts the two feelings in the same paragraph without choosing between them. The title, 'He Also Bears His Cross', tells us which of them controls what he does.`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`332-2`,chapter:332,kind:`motif`,title:`The forgotten roll`,text:`In Marius's pocket Valjean finds a roll 'forgotten there the evening before', the penny roll Marius bought and forgot during his day of despair. It keeps Valjean alive. With it is the pocketbook: 'Carry my body to my grandfather.' The small things a young man forgot when he wanted to die become the means of saving him.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`332-3`,chapter:332,kind:`motif`,title:`'In the night'`,text:`Asked where he was, Hugo says, Valjean would have answered: 'In the night.' The reader has street names to light the march; Valjean has only fading light at the air-holes and a thinning rumble of carriages above. 'Suddenly the darkness became terrible.' Hugo ends the chapter at that sentence.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`333-1`,chapter:333,kind:`craft`,title:`A digression at the worst moment`,text:`Valjean feels the pavement turn to mud beneath him, and Hugo stops to describe the quicksands of Brittany and Scotland. The traveller sinks slowly, sees villages, sails and birds, until a hand waves above the sand and disappears. The pause stretches the suspense almost past bearing, and it shows us exactly what Valjean is walking into.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`333-2`,chapter:333,kind:`theme`,title:`A deformed death`,text:`Death on a pyre or in a shipwreck can be great, Hugo says. Death in mire is 'filthy and humiliating'; everywhere else the grave is sinister, 'here it is deformed'. The book has always cared about dignity at the end of a life. Here it imagines an end with no dignity at all.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`333-3`,chapter:333,kind:`theme`,title:`The sewer extinguishes love`,text:`The young Vicomte d'Escoubleau, fleeing a jealous duke, hid in a sewer and was swallowed; his mistress called for her smelling-bottle 'and forgot to weep'. 'Hero refuses to wash Leander, and Thisbe stops her nose at Pyramus.' Hugo's sour joke about lovers is set against the man in the dark carrying his rival on his back.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`334-1`,chapter:334,kind:`motif`,title:`A mother in the Flood`,text:`Up to his armpits, then to his chin, Valjean holds Marius above the water 'like the mother holding up her child in the old paintings of the Deluge'. Hugo has told us Valjean's love for Cosette held every kind of love, 'even a mother included'. Here that love is given, physically, to the young man who is taking her away.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`334-2`,chapter:334,kind:`theme`,title:`Mud that bears one man`,text:`'Mud dense enough to bear one man could not bear two; singly, each might have got out.' Valjean could save himself by letting go. He goes on, holding up the dying man. Hugo states the choice in one plain sentence and does not stop to praise it.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`334-3`,chapter:334,kind:`motif`,title:`A strange light`,text:`His foot finds the far edge of the sunken pavement. He climbs out, falls on his knees, and 'reflecting that this was only just', prays. He rises shivering, foul-smelling, bent under Marius, 'with his soul filled with a strange light'. Out of the deepest mire in the book Hugo brings his hero up lit from within.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`335-1`,chapter:335,kind:`craft`,title:`An outlet that is a prison`,text:`After the fontis, white daylight at the end of the vault: the way out. Valjean runs, and finds a heavy double-locked grating, 'one of the prison locks old Paris loved', in an arch built like a prison wicket. Flies pass through the bars. He has escaped from the barricade 'only into a prison'. The title warns us of the disappointment before it happens.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`335-2`,chapter:335,kind:`theme`,title:`Thinking of Cosette`,text:`Collapsed beside Marius, at the last drop of anguish, 'he was thinking neither of himself nor of Marius. He was thinking of Cosette.' After all his struggle, the chapter's last thought is for the person he has done all this for, who does not know where either man is.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`336-1`,chapter:336,kind:`craft`,title:`Valjean veiled, Thénardier unmasked`,text:`'Half shares.' Thénardier stands in the light of the grating; Valjean, mud-covered and bloody, has his back to it. 'The duel began between Valjean veiled and Thénardier unmasked.' Hugo arranges the light so that one man is recognised and the other is not, and Valjean keeps his back turned through the whole scene.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`names`]},{id:`336-2`,chapter:336,kind:`theme`,title:`Waterloo again`,text:`Thénardier takes Valjean for a murderer, searches both men's pockets, takes every sou, and opens the gate. At Waterloo he robbed a man he thought dead and was thanked as a rescuer. Here, for his own reasons, he again saves the living while robbing them. 'Providence in horrible guise', Hugo calls it: the good angel rising from the earth in Thénardier's shape.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`history`]},{id:`336-3`,chapter:336,kind:`motif`,title:`A strip of cloth`,text:`With a pickpocket's skill Thénardier tears a strip from Marius's coat and hides it under his blouse, and Valjean does not notice. Hugo shows us a theft that the man beside him does not see. He also tells us the oiled hinges show the gate opens 'more often than anyone supposed'.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`336-4`,chapter:336,kind:`theme`,title:`Paris the killer`,text:`Thénardier gives the 'idiot' advice: a rope, a stone, the river. 'The river is the true grave, Paris the killer, and justice asks no questions.' It is the voice of the underworld, sure that a body in the Seine is no one's business. Hugo lets him talk, and the speech tells us how he sees the city.`,themes:[`paris`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`337-1`,chapter:337,kind:`motif`,title:`The river with the sound of a kiss`,text:`Out of the miasma, the pure evening air, the river flowing 'with the sound of a kiss', birds saying good night in the elms, the first stars. For a few seconds Valjean is bathed in prayer. Hugo gives his hero and the reader one breath of beauty before the next blow falls.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`337-2`,chapter:337,kind:`theme`,title:`'Jean Valjean'`,text:`Javert does not recognise him. 'Who is I?' 'Jean Valjean.' The man who has hidden for years behind the names Madeleine and Fauchelevent gives his true name to the one person who can send him back to the galleys. He asks only to carry Marius home first. He has considered himself Javert's prisoner, he says, since the morning.`,themes:[`names`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`337-3`,chapter:337,kind:`craft`,title:`A face that contracts`,text:`Javert's face 'contracted as it always did when anyone thought him capable of a concession', but he does not say no. He wets his handkerchief and wipes Marius's brow; he still does not say 'thou'; he does not ask how the man came from the barricade. Hugo shows us the cracks through small actions while Javert says nothing.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`337-4`,chapter:337,kind:`motif`,title:`The corpse, the spectre and the statue`,text:`In the hackney-coach the lamplight flashes at intervals on three figures: 'the corpse, the spectre and the statue'. Marius unconscious, Valjean grey with mud, Javert rigid. They drive towards the Marais 'in a glacial silence'. Hugo reduces the three men to emblems, as if they were already figures in a legend.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`338-1`,chapter:338,kind:`craft`,title:`A drawer for every incident`,text:`Javert tells the porter that M. Gillenormand's son has been brought back dead and that there will be a funeral tomorrow. Behind him Valjean shakes his head, and the porter understands neither man. 'For Javert every incident of the public highway had its drawer: uproar, revolt, carnival, funeral.' Hugo shows a mind that files things, even now.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`338-2`,chapter:338,kind:`context`,title:`The prodigal son`,text:`The title plays on the parable of the prodigal son, who comes home after wasting his inheritance and is met by his father with open arms. Marius has been 'prodigal of his life', not his money, and the household lets the grandfather sleep, thinking he will learn soon enough. The parable has its homecoming; here it has not happened yet.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`338-3`,chapter:338,kind:`theme`,title:`A second favour`,text:`Valjean asks to go home for a moment, after which Javert may do as he likes with him. Javert is silent, chin in his collar, then lowers the front glass and tells the driver: 'Rue de l'Homme Armé, No. 7.' A man who has never made a concession makes another one. Hugo does not tell us what Javert is thinking.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`339-1`,chapter:339,kind:`motif`,title:`The cat and the mouse`,text:`On the barricade Gavroche crowed that the mouse had caught the cat. Now Valjean assumes Javert is showing him 'the haughty confidence of a cat that lets the mouse go to the length of its claws'. When he looks out from the landing a moment later, the street is empty. The old image of hunter and hunted is used once more, and then it no longer fits.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`339-2`,chapter:339,kind:`craft`,title:`'I will wait for you here'`,text:`Javert says it 'with a strange expression and as though it cost him an effort'. Then he is gone, and Hugo shows us only the absence: the open window, the street lit from end to end by the lantern, no one there. The title, 'Concussion in the Absolute', tells us something has been shaken in a man who has never doubted.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`339-3`,chapter:339,kind:`theme`,title:`Since the Bishop`,text:`Valjean does not think of escape or of ending his life. Since the Bishop, Hugo says, there has been in him 'a profound hesitation before any violence, even against himself'. He has been seized and has not resisted; he wants only to warn Cosette and settle his affairs. His surrender is calm, almost practical.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`340-1`,chapter:340,kind:`theme`,title:`'He is dead! Ah! The rascal!'`,text:`Gillenormand's first words over the bleeding boy are fury: he got himself killed to spite his grandfather, to avenge a remark about the Duc de Berry. Grief comes out as rage, as joy came out as harshness when Marius rang the bell. Then the memories: the little shovel in the Tuileries, the boy who could not say his Ds. 'Children take hold of you and never let go.'`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`340-2`,chapter:340,kind:`motif`,title:`The pocketbook that stopped a bullet`,text:`The doctor finds that a bullet was 'deadened by the pocketbook' and tore round the ribs without entering. It is the same pocketbook in which Marius wrote 'Carry my body to my grandfather', the one Valjean read in the sewer and Javert read on the shore. A young man's last message has done more than carry his address.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`340-3`,chapter:340,kind:`craft`,title:`My well-beloved son`,text:`Gillenormand blames the newspapers, progress and the rights of man; he has 'seen Louis XVI die' and is perfectly composed. Hugo lets him rave comically and painfully. When Marius opens his eyes, the old man cries 'My child! My well-beloved son!' and faints. A grandfather, at the edge of losing him, calls him son.`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`341-1`,chapter:341,kind:`theme`,title:`A superior above the Prefect`,text:`Javert owes his life to a convict and has repaid it by letting the convict go. To arrest him is wrong; to leave him free is wrong. He discovers that there is something beyond duty, 'a superior above the Prefect, God', and does not know how to hand in his resignation to that superior. The law, for the first time, has not told him what to do.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`341-2`,chapter:341,kind:`motif`,title:`Madeleine behind Valjean`,text:`On the parapet, M. Madeleine reappears behind Jean Valjean, and 'the two figures fuse into one venerable figure'. For years Javert kept them apart: the mayor he respected, the convict he hunted. Now they are one man, and Javert finds himself admiring a galley-slave. The categories that made up his world have collapsed.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`341-3`,chapter:341,kind:`craft`,title:`Observations for the good of the service`,text:`In his calmest hand Javert writes ten numbered complaints: barefoot prisoners catching cold, chairs forbidden to visitors, a woman keeping the wicket to the secret cells. He signs it with the date and the hour. Even here he serves the institution, and the small reforms he asks for are mostly kindnesses to prisoners.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`341-4`,chapter:341,kind:`motif`,title:`Only the darkness knows`,text:`Javert walks with his head bowed and his hands behind his back, 'two things he has never done in his life'. At last he sets his hat on the quay; a tall black figure stands on the parapet and falls into the shadows. 'Only the darkness knows what became of the shape.' Hugo, who usually tells us everything, ends Javert's chapter in the dark.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`342-1`,chapter:342,kind:`craft`,title:`An old thread picked up`,text:`Hugo returns to Boulatruelle, the road-mender of Montfermeil who dreamed of treasure in the forest, a figure from long ago in the book. Again he sees a man he half recognises walking into the woods at dawn, and again he loses him. The chapter reopens an old mystery and leaves it open: freshly turned earth, a pickaxe, a hole.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`342-2`,chapter:342,kind:`motif`,title:`The tree with the zinc plaster`,text:`The title points to a small, odd landmark: an ailing chestnut-tree bandaged with a sheet of zinc nailed to its bark, beside a heap of porous stones in the Blaru-bottom glade. Hugo trusts the reader to remember it. His long book is held together by such objects, which turn up again hundreds of pages later.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`342-3`,chapter:342,kind:`theme`,title:`'Thief!'`,text:`Boulatruelle, a part-time thief himself, shrieks 'Thief!' at the horizon over an empty hole. He was saved at the Gorbeau trial only by his drunkenness, and his taste for theft has 'somewhat cooled'. Hugo gives the comic minor rogue his moment of outrage at being robbed of a treasure he never had.`,themes:[`poverty`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`343-1`,chapter:343,kind:`theme`,title:`The grandson of his grandson`,text:`When Marius is declared out of danger, Gillenormand gives the porter three louis, dances a gavotte, sings a bawdy song and is found kneeling in prayer, he who never believed in God. He even shouts 'Long live the Republic!' Hugo says he becomes 'the grandson of his grandson'. Love has done what argument never could, and the old royalist's politics give way.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`343-2`,chapter:343,kind:`theme`,title:`Marius arms himself`,text:`Marius, meanwhile, distrusts the tenderness as a ploy, never once calls him 'father', and remembers his father's wrongs. He tests the old man by praising the men of '93 and resolves to tear off his bandages and refuse food if he is refused Cosette. Hugo shows the young man preparing for war in a house that has already surrendered.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`conscience`]},{id:`343-3`,chapter:343,kind:`context`,title:`Doctors told to inform`,text:`After June 1832 the Prefecture ordered doctors to report the wounded they treated, so that insurgents could be traced. The order caused public outrage, and Hugo says even the King shared it. In the end the councils of war left alone the insurgents who were not taken in arms. Marius's long illness protects him.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`344-1`,chapter:344,kind:`craft`,title:`Marius attacked`,text:`Marius sits up, fixes his grandfather with a terrible look, and declares: 'I wish to marry.' 'Agreed.' The title is a joke: the attack is the old man's surrender. He has already found Cosette, already learned the lancer's story was false. 'Take her. Such is my ferocity.' Hugo turns Marius's planned battle into comedy in a single word.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`344-2`,chapter:344,kind:`theme`,title:`'Father!'`,text:`In 262 Marius's word 'father' nearly reconciled them before everything went wrong. Now he cries 'Father!' again, and the old man answers, 'Ah, so you love me!' He allows Cosette to come today instead of tomorrow, since Marius has said 'father' three times. Notice how much this one word weighs in the book, between a dead father and a living grandfather.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`344-3`,chapter:344,kind:`context`,title:`André Chénier`,text:`The grandfather quotes André Chénier, the poet guillotined in July 1794 days before Robespierre fell, then blunders into calling the men of '93 'ruffians', tries to retract, and ends by shouting at Basque that 'those ruffians did assassinate him!' The old royalist cannot help himself. Hugo gives the reconciliation its comic coda, with Basque bewildered in the anteroom.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`345-1`,chapter:345,kind:`motif`,title:`Mademoiselle Euphrasie Fauchelevent`,text:`'Mademoiselle Euphrasie Fauchelevent possesses six hundred thousand francs.' 'I am she,' says Cosette. The real first name she confided in the garden is now joined to a borrowed surname and a fortune. Gillenormand, careless with names, has just called her guardian 'Monsieur Tranchelevent'. The name that opens doors is half true and half invented.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`345-2`,chapter:345,kind:`craft`,title:`'That is good'`,text:`Asked about his close friend, Marius names Courfeyrac and says he is dead. 'That is good,' says the grandfather absently, thinking of something else. The reader hears Mabeuf, who answered 'That is good' to every danger on his way to the barricade. Hugo slips a reminder of the dead into the happy scene, and the old man does not notice.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`345-3`,chapter:345,kind:`motif`,title:`Calluses from lint`,text:`Cosette shows Marius the callus on her fingers from four months of making lint. On the barricade the widow and her maids tore dishcloths into lint while the men cast bullets. Here the same humble work has been love's way of keeping watch. Notice how small her part in the drama has been, and how faithful.`,themes:[`love`,`revolution`]},{id:`346-1`,chapter:346,kind:`theme`,title:`Money and candlesticks in one hole`,text:`Hugo explains: the fortune is what Valjean earned as M. Madeleine, withdrawn from Laffitte's bank and buried in the forest of Montfermeil, with the Bishop's candlesticks beside it. The honest earnings of his redeemed life and the gift that redeemed it lay in the earth together for ten years. Cosette's dowry comes out of both.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`346-2`,chapter:346,kind:`craft`,title:`Arithmetic of a hidden life`,text:`Six hundred and thirty thousand francs went in; five hundred and eighty-four thousand five hundred came out. The difference is ten years of expenses, the convent years having cost only five thousand. Hugo does the sums like a notary, which suits the title's joke about trusting a forest over a notary. The figures make the mystery solid and finished.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`346-3`,chapter:346,kind:`theme`,title:`'He must already have been mad'`,text:`Valjean reads in the Moniteur that an inspector named Javert was found drowned, and that his last paper pointed to derangement. His conclusion: since Javert let him go once he had him in his power, he must already have been mad. Even Valjean, who has lived by mercy, cannot imagine mercy in Javert. Hugo leaves the misreading uncorrected.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`346-4`,chapter:346,kind:`context`,title:`Laffitte's bank`,text:`Jacques Laffitte was a real Paris banker, one of the richest men in France and a leading liberal; he helped put Louis-Philippe on the throne in 1830 and briefly headed his government. Hugo places Madeleine's fortune in a real bank, which helps the story feel documented.`,themes:[`history`]},{id:`347-1`,chapter:347,kind:`theme`,title:`A family of dead people`,text:`Valjean, who 'knows how' from his years as a mayor, invents Cosette's civil status: she is the daughter not of himself but of 'the other Fauchelevent', the dead gardener, and her money is a legacy from an unknown benefactor. On paper he gives her up as his daughter. Cosette learns he is only a kinsman, and goes on calling him father.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`347-2`,chapter:347,kind:`theme`,title:`Dry happiness is dry bread`,text:`Gillenormand rifles his lacquered chests for lace, guipure and antique jewels and preaches the philosophy of the superfluous: happiness is not only the necessary; 'dry happiness is dry bread'. The ancien-régime grandfather and the austere Valjean love Cosette in opposite styles, each 'after his own fashion', as the title says.`,themes:[`love`,`poverty`]},{id:`347-3`,chapter:347,kind:`craft`,title:`Blinded by love and money`,text:`'The oddities go unnoticed, one party blinded by love and the others by the money.' And the aunt, who would probably have left a poor nephew poor, decides to leave her fortune to young people who no longer need it. Hugo's dry asides show how money flows towards money, even in a house full of goodwill.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`348-1`,chapter:348,kind:`theme`,title:`Black in, white out`,text:`Marius's memory has a black hole of four months. Mabeuf falling, Gavroche singing, Éponine's cold brow, his friends rising and dispersing like smoke: were they dreams? He 'entered a tomb black and came out white while the others stayed inside'. Hugo gives the survivor's sense of unreality, close to guilt, without naming it.`,themes:[`history`,`revolution`]},{id:`348-2`,chapter:348,kind:`craft`,title:`The name of the street`,text:`'Of course you know that street?' 'I have no idea of the name of that street.' Hugo points out that the answer 'bears on the name and not the street'. It strikes Marius as conclusive, and he decides he was dreaming. The reader notices that Valjean has avoided the question without telling a lie.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`348-3`,chapter:348,kind:`motif`,title:`Free and compulsory schooling`,text:`Marius and Fauchelevent edge towards conversation only on politics, and once, on free and compulsory education, they nearly agree. It was Enjolras's dream on the barricade: 'the right to the alphabet'. The idea survives the men who died for it, and turns up as small talk in a drawing-room.`,themes:[`revolution`]},{id:`349-1`,chapter:349,kind:`theme`,title:`Two debts`,text:`Marius seeks two men: Thénardier, who 'saved' his father at Waterloo, and the unknown man who carried him through the sewer. The reader knows the first debt is false and the second true, and knows who sits across the room. 'If Cosette's six hundred thousand francs were mine...' 'They are yours.' 'I would give them all to find that man again.' Jean Valjean remains silent.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`349-2`,chapter:349,kind:`motif`,title:`The coat with a torn skirt`,text:`Marius keeps his bloodstained clothes in case they help; one skirt of the coat has a piece torn away. The reader saw the tear made, in the dark at the sewer gate, by a hand Valjean did not notice. Hugo states the detail plainly and moves on.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`349-3`,chapter:349,kind:`context`,title:`What became of the Thénardiers`,text:`Hugo settles the Gorbeau case: Madame Thénardier has died in prison awaiting trial; two minor accomplices got ten years in the galleys; Thénardier, condemned to death in his absence, has sunk with Azelma into hiding. The family that tormented Cosette and fathered Éponine and Gavroche has been scattered by the law.`,themes:[`history`,`parenthood`]},{id:`350-1`,chapter:350,kind:`context`,title:`A wedding on Shrove Tuesday`,text:`The wedding falls on Mardi Gras, 16 February 1833, the last day of Carnival, when Paris filled with masked revellers in carts. Hugo digresses on the old tradition and on the 'suspicious, government-sponsored laughter' of the Carnival, with masks hired by the prefecture. The marriage procession has to pass through the masquerade.`,themes:[`paris`,`names`]},{id:`350-2`,chapter:350,kind:`motif`,title:`A man who can only go out masked`,text:`In a cart of maskers, an old 'Spaniard' with a huge nose recognises the man with his arm in a sling and tells the girl beside him to find out where the wedding goes. 'You understand me, Azelma.' He can go out only in a mask and must return to his hole on Ash Wednesday. Carnival is the one day a hunted man can show himself.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`350-3`,chapter:350,kind:`craft`,title:`An arm in a sling`,text:`A few days before the wedding Valjean crushed the thumb of his right hand, let no one see it, and has worn his arm in a sling since, so that the grandfather signs the papers in his place. Hugo mentions it in passing, among the banns and the carriages. Notice how carefully the injury keeps one man's signature off the wedding documents.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`351-1`,chapter:351,kind:`motif`,title:`An empty armchair`,text:`Two armchairs flank the bride, one for the grandfather and one for Jean Valjean. The second stays empty: his hand is paining him, Basque reports, and he has gone. The chill lasts a moment; then Marius takes the seat, 'Fortunatus beside Fortunata', and within five minutes the table is laughing 'with the animation of forgetfulness'. Hugo lets the gap close almost at once.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`351-2`,chapter:351,kind:`motif`,title:`'I am Madame Thou'`,text:`In the carriage Cosette whispers: 'My name is Marius. I am Madame Thou.' She takes his name, and the familiar 'thou' becomes her new title. Through the book Cosette's names have been given to her by others: Euphrasie, the Lark, Ursule, Mademoiselle Fauchelevent. This one she chooses, and she makes a joke of it.`,themes:[`names`,`love`]},{id:`351-3`,chapter:351,kind:`theme`,title:`'Well, then, laugh'`,text:`Before dinner Cosette finds Valjean half hidden behind the drawing-room door, curtsies in her wedding dress, and asks, 'Father, are you satisfied?' 'Yes, I am content.' 'Well, then, laugh.' And Jean Valjean laughs. He obeys her as he always has. The reader sees him laughing because she told him to, and the scene is as sad as it is sweet.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`351-4`,chapter:351,kind:`context`,title:`Daphnis and Chloe, Philemon and Baucis`,text:`Gillenormand's toast tells the couple to be Daphnis and Chloe while waiting to become Philemon and Baucis. The first are the young lovers of a Greek pastoral romance; the second the old couple in Ovid who lived poor and devoted and asked to die at the same hour. The old libertine's sermon on love moves from youth to old age.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`352-1`,chapter:352,kind:`motif`,title:`The inseparable`,text:`At last the valise Cosette was jealous of is opened: inside are the little black dress, fichu, woollen stockings and coarse shoes he brought to Montfermeil for her ten years ago, mourning for a mother she never knew. She was jealous of her own childhood. Valjean lays the clothes out on the bed, and 'that stoical old heart breaks'.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`352-2`,chapter:352,kind:`craft`,title:`The same room, eight months on`,text:`Valjean slips out through the antechamber he entered eight months ago black with mud and blood, carrying the grandson to the grandfather. Now the musicians sit on the sofa where Marius was laid. Hugo uses the same room twice to measure what has changed: the dying boy is a bridegroom, and the man who carried him leaves unnoticed.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`352-3`,chapter:352,kind:`motif`,title:`Listening under the windows`,text:`He stands for several minutes in the dark beneath the lit dining-room, hearing the violins, the plates and, through all of it, Cosette's voice. Then he walks home by the longer way, the road she used to pass along. Hugo gives Valjean's grief no speech, only these small habits, an empty flat and a sling taken off.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`353-1`,chapter:353,kind:`context`,title:`The immortal liver`,text:`The title alludes to Prometheus, chained to a rock for giving fire to men, whose liver an eagle tore out every day and which grew back every night. Valjean's conscience is the same torment: wounded, healed, wounded again. Hugo reminds us how many times the Bishop's spark has seized him in the dark.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`353-2`,chapter:353,kind:`theme`,title:`The raft of his shipwreck`,text:`The question is simple: should he stay in the life he made for Cosette and Marius, sitting 'veiled at that bright fireside' with the shadow of the law at his feet, or let go? 'Cosette is the raft of his shipwreck.' Hugo pleads for him: can perpetual self-sacrifice be exacted? Who would blame Sisyphus for saying 'It is enough'?`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`353-3`,chapter:353,kind:`craft`,title:`What he resolves the chapter does not say`,text:`For twelve hours of a winter night he lies over the little clothes, arms flung out 'like a man unnailed from a cross'. Once he kisses the garments, 'and so one sees he is alive'. Hugo deliberately withholds the decision. 'Who could see? No one was there, except the One who is in the shadows.'`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`354-1`,chapter:354,kind:`context`,title:`Hell and heaven in one room`,text:`In Dante's Inferno the seventh circle holds the violent; in his Paradiso the eighth heaven is the sphere of the fixed stars. Hugo's title sets them side by side: the morning after the wedding, Marius is radiant with the happiest night of his life, and Valjean has come straight from the worst night of his to say 'I am an ex-convict.'`,themes:[`conscience`,`love`]},{id:`354-2`,chapter:354,kind:`theme`,title:`He will not steal a name`,text:`'A name is an I.' Fauchelevent could lend him his name, but he could not take it. 'In days gone by he stole a loaf to live; today, to live, he will not steal a name.' In a book where names have been masks for hundreds of pages, Valjean's last fight is to own his true one, even when it costs him the person he loves.`,themes:[`names`,`conscience`]},{id:`354-3`,chapter:354,kind:`theme`,title:`Tracked by himself`,text:`Why confess when no one pursues him? 'I am tracked. By whom? By myself.' Conscience is 'a grasp like a fist in his own collar'. Javert is gone, and Valjean has become his own pursuer. The law hunted him from outside for years; now the hunt goes on inside him, and it is harder on him than Javert ever was.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`354-4`,chapter:354,kind:`craft`,title:`Happiness escorts despair to the door`,text:`Marius, cooling, agrees it would be better if Valjean did not see Cosette. Valjean turns back at the door: it was in order to stay near her that he had to tell the truth. 'You shall come every evening.' Hugo sums up the scene in one line: 'Happiness escorts despair to the door.' The two men stand on either side of the same doorway.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`355-1`,chapter:355,kind:`theme`,title:`Lower than the lowest rung`,text:`Marius admits the evidence: the returned fortune, the confession nobody forced. 'Whatever he is, he is a conscience awakening.' Yet on penal questions this democrat holds that a convict is lower than the lowest rung, and his last gesture is to turn his head away. Hugo shows that a generous young man can carry the old prejudice of the law without seeing it in himself.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`355-2`,chapter:355,kind:`craft`,title:`Afraid of the answers`,text:`Marius reasons that Valjean fled the Gorbeau garret because he was a fugitive, and went to the barricade to kill Javert. He remembers the pistol shot behind the corner. The reader saw where that shot went. Hugo adds that Marius did not ask the decisive questions 'because he was afraid of the answers'. Love has made him incurious once again.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`355-3`,chapter:355,kind:`motif`,title:`The nettle and the lily`,text:`How did the wolf come to protect the lamb for nine years? Marius sees in it 'Jean Valjean's secret and also God's': the workman horrible, the work admirable; 'the dung-heap has helped the spring make the rose'. Questioning Cosette about her childhood, he grows convinced 'the sinister nettle had loved and protected the lily'. Hugo's images are of plants growing out of dirt.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`356-1`,chapter:356,kind:`motif`,title:`Madame and Monsieur Jean`,text:`In the damp ground-floor room Valjean will not kiss her, calls her 'you' and 'Madame', and asks to be called 'Monsieur Jean'. He takes their family apart word by word. Then her question breaks him: 'Are you angry with me because I am happy?' 'Cosette, thou art happy; my day is over.' 'Ah, you have said thou to me!'`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`356-2`,chapter:356,kind:`craft`,title:`A fire already lit`,text:`Basque is waiting as though on orders to ask whether monsieur will go up or stay below; in the cobwebbed room 'a fire has already been lit'. His answer was foreseen. Hugo lets the household's quiet arrangements tell us that Marius has prepared this. Nothing unkind is said; a room is simply made ready.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`356-3`,chapter:356,kind:`theme`,title:`'You no longer need a father'`,text:`Cosette begs him to live with them and be her father. 'You no longer need a father, you have a husband.' Then he changes the subject to a rosewood toilet table in a shop window, and she blows at him 'like an angry cat'. Hugo shows a girl who has never had to understand him and a man who will not let her try.`,themes:[`love`,`parenthood`]},{id:`357-1`,chapter:357,kind:`motif`,title:`Who are you?`,text:`'You were my father, then my uncle, then Monsieur Fauchelevent, now Jean; who are you?' Cosette's question sums up a whole life lived under borrowed names. 'If I did not know how good you are, I should be afraid of you.' Notice that she asks it laughing, and that he has no answer he is willing to give.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`357-2`,chapter:357,kind:`craft`,title:`A marsh with a hydra in it`,text:`No one sees the 'sinister foundation'. Hugo compares such a man to an Indian marsh that ripples without wind because a hydra moves on the bottom. The side door, the deserted street, the candle left with the porter: the small oddities of solitary people, he says, often come from a formidable depth. The household calls him 'an original'.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`357-3`,chapter:357,kind:`theme`,title:`'Say Jean'`,text:`Once she calls him 'father' by chance. 'A flash of joy lights his face', and at once he corrects her: 'Say Jean.' 'Ah! truly, Monsieur Jean.' 'That is right,' he says, and turns away to wipe his eyes. He is teaching her, gently and deliberately, to need him less, and it is working.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`358-1`,chapter:358,kind:`motif`,title:`A whole day in her past`,text:`Marius and Cosette fly off 'like two swallows' to their old garden in the Rue Plumet, and Valjean waits an hour in the lower room in vain. Next day Cosette talks of nothing but having 'lived a whole day in her past', and does not notice she missed him. The garden belongs to their love, and he has no place in it.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`358-2`,chapter:358,kind:`craft`,title:`Furniture that disappears`,text:`First the fire is not lit; then the armchairs are moved to the door; then they are gone. Valjean covers each time, pretending he ordered it himself. Hugo stages the eviction entirely through objects, with no word spoken. 'This time he has understood.'`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`358-3`,chapter:358,kind:`theme`,title:`Three thousand livres`,text:`Marius asks Cosette whether she would have the courage to live on his three thousand livres and not her twenty-seven. She says yes, on nothing, with him. Valjean takes it as proof that Marius thinks the money unclean and will not touch it. The reader knows the fortune was earned honestly at Montreuil. Here a scruple meant to be honest does a man an injustice.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`359-1`,chapter:359,kind:`motif`,title:`A pendulum running down`,text:`Every evening an old man in black walks towards the corner of the Rue des Filles-du-Calvaire 'like a magnetic needle trembling towards the pole', looks round the last house, and turns back with a tear on his lip. Day by day the walk grows shorter: halfway, then the Rue Pavée, then the Blancs-Manteaux. Hugo turns a decline into geometry: 'a pendulum no longer wound up'.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`359-2`,chapter:359,kind:`craft`,title:`Seen from the street`,text:`Hugo shows this chapter through the eyes of the Marais: shopkeepers and loungers notice an old man; the good women call him an innocent; children follow him and laugh. The man who lifted a cart on his back is now a local oddity with an umbrella he never opens. The outside view, with no access to his thoughts, is the saddest view of all.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`360-1`,chapter:360,kind:`theme`,title:`A terrible thing to be happy`,text:`'It is a terrible thing to be happy: in possessing happiness one forgets duty.' Hugo asks the reader not to blame Marius, who has slowly placed himself between Cosette and 'Monsieur Jean' until 'the effacement became an eclipse', nor Cosette, who conforms without knowing why. The title's two halves, pity and indulgence, are his instruction to us.`,themes:[`love`,`conscience`]},{id:`360-2`,chapter:360,kind:`theme`,title:`Two graves, two fathers`,text:`The young couple have been away, to Vernon, where Marius took Cosette to his father's grave. He honours one father with a pilgrimage while the other sits alone in the Rue de l'Homme Armé. Hugo does not underline it. He lets the visit stand beside the shortening walk of the chapter before.`,themes:[`parenthood`]},{id:`360-3`,chapter:360,kind:`motif`,title:`The ingratitude of nature`,text:`'The young turn towards the light and the old towards the shadows.' Branches grow away from the trunk without breaking from it. Hugo calls it 'the ingratitude of nature' and refuses to call it a crime. 'Let us not blame these poor children.' His image of light and darkness is used here without judging anyone.`,themes:[`light-darkness`,`parenthood`]},{id:`360-4`,chapter:360,kind:`craft`,title:`Searching for the owner`,text:`From a former Laffitte clerk Marius has picked up 'mysterious information', and he now believes he must restore the six hundred thousand francs to someone he is quietly seeking. He will not touch the money meanwhile. The reader learned where the fortune came from in the forest of Montfermeil. Marius is looking for the money's owner while keeping that owner out of his house.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`361-1`,chapter:361,kind:`theme`,title:`People die of that`,text:`The doctor comes down and tells the portress her lodger is very ill. With what? 'Everything and nothing. He is a man who, to all appearances, has lost some person who is dear to him. People die of that.' And then: 'Someone else besides must come.' Hugo puts the diagnosis in plain words from a stranger.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`361-2`,chapter:361,kind:`motif`,title:`A copper crucifix`,text:`While he still went out, Valjean bought a little copper crucifix for a few sous and hung it opposite his bed. Alone, unvisited, in a street where no one passes, he has the cheapest emblem of suffering for company. Notice how Hugo strips the scene to essentials: a bed, a plate of potatoes, a jug, a cross.`,themes:[`conscience`]},{id:`361-3`,chapter:361,kind:`craft`,title:`The porter's philosophy`,text:`'If he has a doctor he will die, and if he has none he will die,' pronounces the porter 'with marital sovereignty', while his wife scrapes grass from the pavement and calls the lodger 'white as a chicken'. Hugo keeps the comic voices of ordinary Paris around the sickbed. Their chatter makes the solitude upstairs louder.`,themes:[`poverty`]},{id:`362-1`,chapter:362,kind:`motif`,title:`The man who lifted the cart`,text:`The title recalls Montreuil-sur-Mer, when M. Madeleine crawled under a fallen cart and lifted it off old Fauchelevent, and Javert saw a convict's strength in the mayor. Now a pen is too heavy for that man. He must moisten dried ink with water and write with the back of a curled nib. Hugo measures the collapse against his hero's most famous act of strength.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`names`]},{id:`362-2`,chapter:362,kind:`motif`,title:`Candles in daylight`,text:`He puts two wax candles in the Bishop's candlesticks and lights them in broad summer daylight, 'as candles are lit by day in rooms where there is a corpse'. The silver given to a thief in Digne has followed him through every hiding place. Hugo's image is solemn and exact, and Valjean has arranged it himself.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`362-3`,chapter:362,kind:`theme`,title:`A recipe for honesty`,text:`His letter to Cosette turns, astonishingly, into a manufacturing manual: white jet from Norway, black jet from England, a resin-and-lampblack wax at thirty sous instead of four livres, buckles of violet glass on black iron. He needs her to know the money is clean. The dying man's love comes out as the trade secrets of the Madeleine factory.`,themes:[`poverty`,`conscience`]},{id:`362-4`,chapter:362,kind:`craft`,title:`Then a knock`,text:`'It is nothing to die; what is frightful is to die without seeing her.' The pen falls, a sob comes up 'from the depths of his being', all is over. 'At that moment there is a knock at the door.' Hugo ends the chapter there, at the height of despair.`,themes:[`love`]},{id:`363-1`,chapter:363,kind:`craft`,title:`Ink that whitens`,text:`The title is the chapter's irony: Thénardier comes with a bottle of ink to blacken Valjean and only succeeds in whitening him. Trying to prove a thief, he proves Valjean was M. Madeleine. Trying to prove a murderer, he proves Javert killed himself. Trying to sell a secret, he produces the strip of coat that names Marius's saviour.`,themes:[`names`,`light-darkness`]},{id:`363-2`,chapter:363,kind:`motif`,title:`Costumes for rent`,text:`Thénardier arrives in green spectacles and a suit too big for him, the 'statesman, ex-ambassador' outfit hired from 'the Changer', who rents respectable costumes to rogues at thirty sous a day. Marius strips the disguise with a list: Thénardier, Jondrette, Fabantou, Genflot, Don Alvarès, Mistress Balizard. Even respectability can be hired by the day.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`363-3`,chapter:363,kind:`theme`,title:`'The young man was myself'`,text:`Thénardier tells his sewer story, holds up the black strip, and Marius fumbles in a cupboard: 'The young man was myself, and here is the coat!' The strip fits the tear exactly. Hugo stages the moment of truth as physical proof, cloth fitted to cloth, and in Marius's eyes the convict he despised becomes 'a hero, a saint'.`,themes:[`mercy-law`]},{id:`363-4`,chapter:363,kind:`theme`,title:`Waterloo protects him`,text:`Marius pays the debt his father bequeathed him: thousands of francs and a passage to America, 'Waterloo protects him'. Hugo's last word on Thénardier is curt: with Marius's money he sets up in America as a slave-dealer. Every kindness in this book has been offered to him, and he has turned each one into profit.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`poverty`]},{id:`364-1`,chapter:364,kind:`motif`,title:`She was called Fantine`,text:`'Cosette, the moment has come to tell thee the name of thy mother. She was called Fantine. Kneel whenever thou utterest it. She suffered much. She loved thee dearly.' For hundreds of pages Cosette has not known her mother's name. The reader has carried Fantine's story all this time, and at the end it is passed to her daughter.`,themes:[`names`,`parenthood`]},{id:`364-2`,chapter:364,kind:`theme`,title:`The candlesticks`,text:`He leaves Cosette the two candlesticks, 'silver but to him gold and diamonds', and says he does not know whether the one who gave them is pleased with him. The novel began with a bishop handing them to a thief. Asked if he wants a priest, Valjean answers 'I have had one' and points above, and Hugo thinks it probable the Bishop was present.`,themes:[`mercy-law`,`conscience`]},{id:`364-3`,chapter:364,kind:`theme`,title:`There is nothing else`,text:`'Love each other well and always, there is nothing else in the world.' And of the people who tormented her as a child: the Thénardiers were wicked, and she must forgive them. Valjean's last counsel joins the two lessons he learned from the Bishop, love and pardon. He gives them as simply as household advice, beside instructions about jet buckles.`,themes:[`love`,`mercy-law`]},{id:`364-4`,chapter:364,kind:`craft`,title:`A night behind which there is day`,text:`The candles light his white face turned to heaven; the night outside is starless; 'no doubt some immense angel stands in the gloom with wings outspread'. The title promises that this night has a day behind it. Hugo's long contest between darkness and light ends in a dark room lit by the Bishop's candles.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]},{id:`364-5`,chapter:364,kind:`theme`,title:`Hands on their heads`,text:`'Give me your dear and well-beloved heads, so that I may lay my hands upon them.' Cosette and Marius kneel under his hands, which no longer move. Marius calls him 'My father'. The man who belonged to no family dies giving a blessing, like the patriarchs of the Bible blessing their children.`,themes:[`parenthood`,`love`]},{id:`365-1`,chapter:365,kind:`motif`,title:`A stone with no name`,text:`He asked for a grave 'with no name on it', and that is what he has. Just before, he refused to steal a name because 'a name is an I'. Now the man of many names lies under none. Even the four lines someone pencilled on the stone have been washed away by rain and dust.`,themes:[`names`]},{id:`365-2`,chapter:365,kind:`theme`,title:`Near the common grave`,text:`The stone lies in a deserted corner of Père-Lachaise, near the common grave, far from the fashionable tombs. Fantine was thrown into the common pit at Montreuil-sur-Mer. Hugo places her daughter's protector at the edge of the place the poor are buried, among dandelions, moss and lizards.`,themes:[`poverty`,`parenthood`]},{id:`365-3`,chapter:365,kind:`craft`,title:`As night falls when the day departs`,text:`The book ends not with a death but with a place, years later, and four effaced lines: he lived, though fate was strange to him; he died when he no longer had his angel; it happened simply, 'as night falls when the day departs'. After hundreds of pages, Hugo closes on grass, rain and a sentence that can no longer be read.`,themes:[`light-darkness`]}]};export{e as default};